          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fe211e15633917b1c9260080\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He could not tell whether the voice belonged to a man or a woman. He could not understand the words, but believed the language was Spanish. This witness described the condition of the room and the bodies as we described it yesterday.\\n\\n\u201c_Henri Duval_, a neighbor and silversmith, states that he was among the group that first entered the house. He generally confirms Mus\u00e8t\u2019s testimony. As soon as they forced their way in, they shut the door again to keep out the crowd, which gathered very quickly despite the late hour. This witness believed the shrill voice was that of an Italian. He was certain it was not French. He could not tell whether it was a man\u2019s voice; it might have been a woman\u2019s. He did not know Italian. He could not make out the words, but was convinced by the speaker\u2019s tone that it was an Italian. He knew Madame L. and her daughter and had often spoken with both. He was certain the shrill voice belonged to neither of the dead women.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014_Odenheimer, restaurateur._ This witness offered his testimony voluntarily. Since he did not speak French, he was questioned through an interpreter. He was born in Amsterdam. He was passing the house when the screams began. They continued for several minutes\u2014probably ten. They were long, loud, terrible, and distressing. He was one of those who entered the building. He confirmed the earlier testimony in every respect except one. He was certain the shrill voice belonged to a man\u2014a Frenchman. He could not understand the words. They were loud, rapid, and uneven, apparently spoken in fear as well as anger. The voice was harsh\u2014not truly shrill. He could not describe it as a shrill voice. The rough voice repeatedly said \u2018_sacr\u00e9_,\u2019 \u2018_diable_,\u2019 and once \u2018_mon Dieu._\u2019\\n\\n\u201c_Jules Mignaud_, banker with the firm of Mignaud et Fils, Rue Deloraine. He is the elder Mignaud. Madame L\u2019Espanaye had some property. She opened an account with his bank in the spring of the year\u2014eight years earlier. She made frequent deposits of small amounts. She wrote no checks until three days before her death, when she personally withdrew 4000 francs. The money was paid in gold, and a clerk accompanied it to her home.\\n\\n\u201c_Adolphe Le Bon_, clerk to Mignaud et Fils, states that on the day in question, around noon, he accompanied Madame L\u2019Espanaye to her residence with the 4000 francs, packed in two bags. When the door opened, Mademoiselle L. appeared and took one bag from his hands, while the old woman took the other.\u201d\"}"
