          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1a71348d9c85f9de2cf3971b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I thanked him and apologised. He said, \u201cNot at all,\u201d and continued.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham was now an heiress, so you may imagine that people considered her a very desirable match. Her half-brother had plenty of money again, but debts and fresh madness soon wasted most of it once more. The differences between him and her were even greater than those between him and their father, and it was suspected that he held a deep and deadly grudge against her because he believed she had turned their father against him. Now I come to the cruel part of the story\u2014pausing only, my dear Handel, to point out that a dinner napkin will not fit inside a tumbler.\u201d\\n\\nWhy I was trying to force mine into my tumbler, I cannot say at all. I only know that, with persistence better suited to a more worthwhile task, I made the greatest efforts to squeeze it into that small space. Once again I thanked him and apologised, and once again he cheerfully said, \u201cNot at all, I\u2019m sure!\u201d Then he continued.\\n\\n\u201cThere appeared on the scene\u2014at the races, perhaps, or at public balls, or anywhere else you like\u2014a certain man who began courting Miss Havisham. I never saw him, since this happened twenty-five years ago, before you and I were born, Handel, but I heard my father say that he was flashy and exactly the sort of man for the purpose. Still, my father strongly insists that no one should mistake him for a gentleman, unless ignorance or prejudice caused the mistake. My father\u2019s principle is that no man who was not a true gentleman at heart has ever been a true gentleman in manner. He says that no varnish can hide the grain of wood; the more varnish you apply, the more clearly the grain shows. Well! This man pursued Miss Havisham closely and claimed to be devoted to her. I believe she had shown little feeling for anyone before then, but all the feeling she possessed came out at that time, and she loved him passionately. There is no doubt that she worshipped him. He deliberately exploited her love and obtained large sums of money from her. He also persuaded her to buy her brother\u2019s share in the brewery\u2014which their father had foolishly left him\u2014for an enormous price, claiming that, once he became her husband, he would have to own and manage the whole business. Your guardian was not yet involved in Miss Havisham\u2019s affairs, and she was too proud and too deeply in love to accept advice from anyone.\u201d\"}"
        }
