          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a82cab878c9acdb759d4d432\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She would be such a delightful companion for Emma.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Woodhouse agreed, but added,\\n\\n\u201cOur little friend Harriet Smith, however, is another very pretty and pleasant young person. You will like Harriet. Emma could not have a better companion than Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am very glad to hear it\u2014but Jane Fairfax is the only one known to be so accomplished and superior!\u2014and she is exactly Emma\u2019s age.\u201d\\n\\nThey discussed this subject very happily, and then moved on to others of similar importance, all passing with the same good feeling. But the evening did not end without a small return of uneasiness. The gruel was brought in, giving them plenty to discuss: much praise and many remarks, firm agreement that it was healthy for every constitution, and rather sharp criticism of the many houses where it was never made properly. Unfortunately, among the failures the daughter mentioned, the most recent and therefore the most memorable was her own temporary cook at South End, a young woman who had never understood what she meant by a basin of smooth, pleasant gruel, thin but not too thin. However often she had asked for it, she had never received anything tolerable. This was a dangerous subject.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and looking at her with tender concern. To Emma, the exclamation meant: \u201cAh! There is no end to the sad results of your going to South End. It cannot bear discussion.\u201d For a while she hoped he would say no more, and that quietly thinking about it might restore his enjoyment of his own smooth gruel. After several minutes, however, he began:\\n\\n\u201cI shall always be very sorry that you went to the seaside this autumn instead of coming here.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut why should you be sorry, sir? I assure you, it did the children a great deal of good.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd besides, if you had to go to the seaside, you should not have gone to South End. South End is an unhealthy place. Perry was surprised to hear that you had chosen South End.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know many people think that, but it is quite a mistake, sir. We were all perfectly healthy there and never suffered the least inconvenience from the mud. Mr. Wingfield says it is entirely wrong to suppose the place unhealthy, and I am sure he can be trusted, because he thoroughly understands the nature of the air. His own brother and family have been there repeatedly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere. Perry once spent a week at Cromer, and he considers it the best of all the seaside resorts.\u201d\"}"
        }
