          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"59edefba0b486a711a300c6b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself time to think, and scarcely knowing what she felt, Elizabeth, after finishing this letter, immediately seized the other and opened it with the greatest impatience. It read as follows; it had been written a day after the first letter ended.\\n\\n\u201cBy now, my dearest sister, you will have received my hurried letter. I hope this one may be clearer, but although I am not pressed for time, my mind is so confused that I cannot promise to be coherent. Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know what to write, but I have bad news for you, and it cannot wait. Imprudent as a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now desperate to know that it has taken place, because there is only too much reason to fear that they have not gone to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday, having left Brighton the day before, only a few hours after the express. Although Lydia\u2019s brief letter to Mrs. F. led them to believe that they were going to Gretna Green, Denny let something slip, saying that he believed W. had never intended to go there, or to marry Lydia at all. This was repeated to Colonel F., who immediately became alarmed and set out from B., intending to follow their route. He traced them easily as far as Clapham, but no farther; on entering that place, they changed into a hackney coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. All that is known afterward is that they were seen continuing along the London road. I do not know what to think. After making every possible inquiry in that part of London, Colonel F. came on to Hertfordshire, anxiously repeating his questions at every turnpike and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but without success\u2014no such people had been seen passing through. With the greatest kindness and concern, he came to Longbourn and told us his fears in a way that did him the highest credit. I am truly sorry for him and Mrs. F.; but no one can blame them. Our distress, my dear Lizzy, is very great. My father and mother believe the worst, but I cannot think so badly of him. Many circumstances might make it preferable for them to marry privately in town rather than follow their original plan; and even if _he_ could form such a scheme against a young woman of Lydia\u2019s connections, which is unlikely, can I imagine her so completely lost to all sense of propriety? Impossible! I am distressed to find, however, that Colonel F. is unwilling to rely on their marriage: when I expressed my hopes, he shook his head and said he feared W. was not a man to be trusted.\u201d\"}"
