          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6e636d4d1473b85727f407c3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet despite all this, the boy grew increasingly melancholy, pale, and dejected. She added hot baths, sitz baths, showers, and plunges. The boy remained as gloomy as a hearse. She began supporting the water treatment with a sparse oatmeal diet and blister plasters. She measured his capacity as if he were a jug and filled him each day with patent remedies.\\n\\nBy then Tom had become indifferent to persecution. This stage filled the old lady\u2019s heart with alarm. His indifference had to be broken at any cost. Then she heard of Pain-killer for the first time. She ordered a large supply at once. She tasted it and felt profoundly grateful. It was simply fire in liquid form. She abandoned the water treatment and everything else, placing all her faith in Pain-killer. She gave Tom a teaspoonful and watched anxiously for the result. Her troubles immediately vanished and her soul was at peace again, because his \u201cindifference\u201d had been shattered. The boy could not have shown a wilder or more vigorous interest if she had built a fire beneath him.\\n\\nTom decided it was time to wake up; this kind of life might be romantic enough in his ruined condition, but it was becoming too lacking in sentiment and too full of distracting variety. He considered several ways to obtain relief and finally decided to pretend that he liked Pain-killer. He asked for it so often that he became a nuisance, and his aunt finally told him to help himself and stop bothering her. If it had been Sid, she would have enjoyed the matter without any doubts; but because it was Tom, she secretly watched the bottle. She saw that the medicine really was being used up, but it never occurred to her that the boy was repairing a crack in the sitting-room floor with it.\\n\\nOne day Tom was pouring medicine into the crack when his aunt\u2019s yellow cat came over, purring, eyeing the teaspoon greedily, and begging for a taste. Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t ask for it unless you want it, Peter.\u201d\\n\\nBut Peter made it clear that he did want it.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019d better be sure.\u201d\\n\\nPeter was sure.\\n\\n\u201cNow that you\u2019ve asked for it, I\u2019ll give it to you, because there\u2019s nothing mean about me; but if you discover that you don\u2019t like it, you must blame no one but yourself.\u201d\\n\\nPeter agreed. So Tom pried open his mouth and poured in the Pain-killer. Peter leaped several feet into the air, then gave a war whoop and raced around the room, crashing into furniture, knocking over flowerpots, and creating general havoc.\"}"
