          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2147b74575f3275c4616e37d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Moreover, this is a knot that few people besides sailors can tie, and it is especially associated with the Maltese. I found the ribbon at the foot of the lightning rod. It could not have belonged to either of the dead men. Now, even if my conclusion from this ribbon\u2014that the Frenchman was a sailor from a Maltese vessel\u2014is wrong, I can have done no harm by saying what I did in the advertisement. If I am mistaken, he will simply think that some circumstance misled me, and he will not bother to investigate it. But if I am right, we have gained a great advantage. Though innocent of the murder, the Frenchman knows about it, and will naturally hesitate before answering the advertisement and claiming the Ourang-Outang. He will reason in this way: \u201cI am innocent and poor. My Ourang-Outang is extremely valuable\u2014to someone in my position, it is a fortune. Why should I give it up because of vague fears of danger? It is here, within my reach. It was found in the Bois de Boulogne, far from the place where the murders occurred. How could anyone suspect that a wild animal committed the crime? The police have failed; they have not found the slightest clue. Even if they trace the animal, they could never prove that I knew about the murder or connect me with it merely because I had that knowledge. Above all, I am known. The person who placed the advertisement identifies me as the animal\u2019s owner. I do not know how much else he may know. If I refuse to claim property of such great value, which everyone knows I possess, I will make the animal itself suspicious. It is not in my interest to draw attention either to myself or to the beast. I will answer the advertisement, recover the Ourang-Outang, and keep it hidden until this affair has passed.\u201d\u201d\\n\\nAt that moment, we heard footsteps on the stairs.\\n\\n\u201cBe ready,\u201d said Dupin, \u201cwith your pistols, but do not use them or show them until I give you a signal.\u201d\\n\\nThe house\u2019s front door had been left open, so the visitor entered without knocking and climbed several steps. Then he seemed to hesitate. A moment later, we heard him going back down. Dupin was hurrying toward the door when we heard the man coming up again. This time he did not turn back. He climbed firmly and knocked on the door of our room.\\n\\n\u201cCome in,\u201d said Dupin in a cheerful, welcoming voice.\\n\\nA man entered. He was clearly a sailor\u2014a tall, broad, muscular man with a bold, reckless expression that was not entirely unpleasant.\"}"
