          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7a7995b70ee4d014eaa5b6fc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma Woodhouse\u2014handsome, clever, and wealthy, with a comfortable home and a cheerful nature\u2014seemed to possess many of life\u2019s finest blessings. She had lived almost twenty-one years with very little to trouble or upset her.\\n\\nShe was the younger of two daughters born to a deeply affectionate and indulgent father. After her sister married, Emma had become mistress of his household at a very early age. Her mother had died too long before for Emma to retain more than a vague memory of her caresses, and her place had been filled by an excellent governess whose affection had come close to that of a mother.\\n\\nMiss Taylor had been part of Mr. Woodhouse\u2019s family for sixteen years, less as a governess than as a friend. She loved both daughters, but Emma especially. Between them, the relationship was more like that of sisters. Even before Miss Taylor formally gave up her position as governess, her gentle nature had scarcely allowed her to impose any real discipline; and now that all trace of authority had long disappeared, they had lived together as two mutually devoted friends, with Emma doing exactly as she pleased. She valued Miss Taylor\u2019s judgment highly, but was guided chiefly by her own.\\n\\nThe true disadvantages of Emma\u2019s situation were her ability to have almost everything her own way and her tendency to think rather too highly of herself. These were the flaws that threatened to diminish her many pleasures. Yet the danger was still so unnoticed that she did not regard them as misfortunes at all.\\n\\nSorrow came\u2014a gentle sorrow\u2014but not in the form of any unpleasant self-awareness. Miss Taylor married. It was the loss of Miss Taylor that first brought Emma real grief. On the wedding day of this beloved friend, Emma first sat in prolonged, mournful reflection. Once the wedding was over and the newlyweds had departed, she and her father were