          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8f7005a574fe4a46fadfb484\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The two ladies were delighted to see their dear friend again. They said it seemed an age since they had met and repeatedly asked what she had been doing since they parted. They paid little attention to the rest of the family: they avoided Mrs. Bennet as much as possible, said little to Elizabeth, and nothing at all to the others. Soon they left again, rising so quickly that their brother was surprised, and hurrying away as if eager to escape Mrs. Bennet\u2019s polite attentions.\\n\\nThe thought of the Netherfield ball pleased every woman in the family. Mrs. Bennet chose to believe it was being held as a compliment to her eldest daughter, and she was especially flattered that Mr. Bingley himself had sent the invitation instead of a formal card. Jane imagined a happy evening with her two friends and their brother\u2019s attention. Elizabeth looked forward to dancing often with Mr. Wickham and seeing proof of everything she believed in Mr. Darcy\u2019s expression and behavior. Catherine and Lydia\u2019s expected happiness depended less on one event or one person. Although each of them, like Elizabeth, planned to dance half the evening with Mr. Wickham, he was far from the only partner who could please them, and a ball was, in any case, a ball. Even Mary assured her family that she had no objection to attending.\\n\\n\u201cWhile I can have my mornings to myself,\u201d she said, \u201cthat is enough. I do not think it is a sacrifice to take part occasionally in evening activities. Society makes demands on us all, and I consider periods of recreation and amusement desirable for everyone.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth was in such high spirits that, although she did not often speak unnecessarily to Mr. Collins, she could not help asking whether he intended to accept Mr. Bingley\u2019s invitation and, if so, whether he thought it proper to join the evening\u2019s entertainment. She was rather surprised to learn that he had no hesitation about it and was far from fearing a reprimand, either from the Archbishop or Lady Catherine de Bourgh, for daring to dance.\\n\\n\u201cI am certainly not of the opinion,\u201d he said, \u201cthat a ball of this kind, given by a respectable young man to respectable people, can have any harmful effect. Far from objecting to dancing myself, I hope to be honored with the hands of all my fair cousins during the evening. I would also like to ask for yours, Miss Elizabeth, especially for the first two dances\u2014a preference which I trust my cousin Jane will understand correctly, and not take as any disrespect toward her.\u201d\"}"
