          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dc200414687f56db2d21f036\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While these events were unfolding in the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the old den\u2014the same room from which the girl had taken Oliver\u2014brooding over a dull, smoky fire. He held a pair of bellows across his knees, apparently trying to stir it into a livelier blaze; but he had fallen into deep thought. With his arms folded over them and his chin resting on his thumbs, he stared absently at the rusty bars.\\n\\nAt a table behind him sat the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling, all absorbed in a game of whist; the Artful playing dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. The first-named gentleman\u2019s face, always remarkably intelligent, gained still greater interest from his close attention to the game and his careful study of Mr. Chitling\u2019s hand. Whenever the opportunity arose, he cast it a series of serious glances, wisely directing his own play according to what he observed in his neighbour\u2019s cards. Since it was a cold night, the Dodger wore his hat, as he often did indoors. He also kept a clay pipe between his teeth, removing it only briefly when he found it necessary to seek refreshment from a quart pot on the table, already filled with gin and water for the company.\\n\\nMaster Bates was attentive to the game as well; but, being more excitable than his accomplished friend, he applied himself more often to the gin and water and made numerous jokes and irrelevant comments, all thoroughly improper during a serious rubber. Indeed, relying on their close friendship, the Artful more than once took the opportunity to lecture his companion solemnly on these offenses. Master Bates accepted every remonstrance in excellent spirits, merely telling his friend to be \u201cblowed,\u201d or to put his head in a sack, or answering with some other neatly phrased joke of the same kind. The cleverness of these replies greatly impressed Mr. Chitling. It was remarkable that he and his partner invariably lost, and that this, far from angering Master Bates, seemed to amuse him intensely; for at the end of every deal he laughed uproariously and declared that he had never seen such a jolly game in all his life.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s two doubles and the rubber,\u201d said Mr. Chitling, looking very miserable as he drew half a crown from his waistcoat pocket. \u201cI never saw such a fellow as you, Jack; you win everything.\u201d\"}"
