{"request_id":"5340b6a1438aa414d7e932ad","rewritten_text":"They were her whole life; dearer than any place hallowed by love, dearer than everything else the earth possessed. The boys listened with childish delight to the news of our departure: Clara asked whether we were going to Athens. “It is possible,” I answered; and her face shone with pleasure. There she would see her parents’ tomb, and the land crowded with memories of her father’s glory. In silence, yet without pause, she had dwelt upon these scenes. Their remembrance had changed her infant merriment into seriousness, and filled her with lofty, restless thoughts.

There were many beloved friends we could not leave behind, humble as they might be. There was the spirited, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter; there was Alfred’s dog, and a tame eagle whose sight had grown dim with age. Yet this list of favourites to accompany us could not be made without sorrow over our grievous losses, and a profound sigh for the many things we were forced to leave. Tears sprang into Idris’s eyes, while Alfred and Evelyn brought first a cherished rose tree, then a beautifully carved marble vase, declaring that these too must go, and lamenting that we could not carry the castle and forest, the deer and birds, and every familiar, treasured object with us. “Fond, foolish ones,” I said, “we have forever lost treasures much more precious than these; and we abandon them in order to preserve treasures which, beside them, are nothing. Let us never for an instant lose sight of our purpose and our hope; they will make an unyielding barrier against the overflowing of our grief for trifles.”

The children were readily diverted, and soon returned to imagining the pleasures ahead. Idris had vanished. She had gone to conceal her weakness; leaving the castle, she had descended into the little park and sought a secluded place where she might give way to her tears. I found her clasping an ancient oak, pressing its rough trunk with her rosy lips, while tears streamed down her face and her sobs and broken cries could no longer be restrained. With overwhelming grief I saw this beloved one of my heart surrendered to sorrow. I drew her toward me; and when she felt my kisses upon her eyelids and my arms enfolding her, she returned to awareness of what still remained. “You are very kind not to reproach me,” she said. “I weep, and a bitter, unbearable anguish rends my heart. Yet I am happy; mothers mourn their children, wives lose their husbands, while you and my children remain to me.”"}

