          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"44b9a7321c597dd50878297b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But enough of this. I will begin my tale: it is my final task, and I hope I possess strength enough to complete it. I record no crimes; my faults may readily be forgiven, for they arose not from evil intention but from lack of judgement; and I believe few would claim that, through different conduct and greater wisdom, they could have escaped the misfortunes of which I am the victim. My fate was ruled by necessity, a dreadful necessity. It demanded hands stronger than mine\u2014stronger, I believe, than any human power\u2014to break the thick, adamantine chain that bound me, once breathing only joy and filled with warm love and delight in goodness, to misery that could end only, and is now about to end, in death. But I lose myself; my tale remains untold. I will pause for a few moments, wipe my dim eyes, and try to replace the present obscure yet heavy unhappiness with the sharper emotions of the past.\\n\\nI was born in England. My father was a man of rank: he had lost his own father early, and was raised by a weak mother who granted him all the indulgence she believed proper for a wealthy nobleman. He was sent to Eton and afterward to college, and from childhood was permitted the free use of large sums of money; thus enjoying from his earliest years the independence that a boy with such advantages always gains at a public school.\\n\\nUnder the influence of these circumstances, his passions found rich soil in which to take root and flourish, as flowers or weeds according to their nature. Having always been allowed to act for himself, his character became strongly and early defined, displaying a varied surface upon which a keen observer might discern the seeds of virtues and misfortunes. His careless extravagance, which led him to squander immense sums satisfying passing whims\u2014whims whose apparent force he dignified with the name of passions\u2014often revealed itself as boundless generosity. Yet while he occupied himself earnestly with the needs of others, his own desires were indulged to their fullest extent. He gave his money, but sacrificed none of his own wishes to his gifts; he gave his time, which he did not value, and his affections, which he was glad to have awakened in any way.\\n\\nI do not say that, had his desires been set against those of others, he would have shown excessive selfishness, but that test was never made. He was raised in prosperity and surrounded by all its advantages; everyone loved him and wished to gratify him.\"}"
