          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2fb5fb4ee79a5d3749faa5ec\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Having driven a peg with great precision into the ground at the exact place where the beetle had fallen, my friend drew a tape measure from his pocket. He secured one end to that point on the trunk of the tree nearest the peg, unrolled it to the peg, and then continued unrolling it in the direction already fixed by the tree and peg, for fifty feet\u2014Jupiter cutting through the brambles with his scythe. At the place reached, a second peg was driven, and around it, as a centre, a rough circle some four feet across was marked. Legrand then took a spade himself, handed one to Jupiter and another to me, and urged us to begin digging as quickly as we could.\\n\\nTo tell the truth, I had never had any particular taste for such work, and at that moment would gladly have refused it; night was approaching, and I was already greatly wearied by the exercise. Yet I saw no way to escape, and feared that a refusal might upset my poor friend\u2019s composure. Had I been able to rely upon Jupiter\u2019s assistance, I would not have hesitated to try carrying the madman home by force; but I knew the old negro\u2019s character too well to expect him, in any circumstances, to aid me in a physical struggle with his master. I had little doubt that Legrand had fallen prey to one of the countless Southern superstitions concerning buried treasure, and that his fancy had been strengthened by finding the _scarab\u00e6us_, or perhaps by Jupiter\u2019s stubborn insistence that it was \u201ca bug of real gold.\u201d A mind inclined toward madness could easily be carried away by such notions\u2014particularly when they agreed with cherished preconceived ideas\u2014and I remembered then the poor fellow\u2019s remark that the beetle was \u201cthe index of his fortune.\u201d On the whole, I was deeply troubled and perplexed; but finally I resolved to make a virtue of necessity, dig willingly, and thereby convince 