          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1b7685dcd24583f9c0472e32\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He shuddered and went back, closing the window behind him.\\n\\nWhen he reached the door, he turned the key and opened it. He did not even look at the murdered man. He felt that the secret was not to acknowledge what had happened. The friend who had painted the fatal portrait, the source of all his misery, had vanished from his life. That was enough.\\n\\nThen he remembered the lamp. It was a rather unusual Moorish piece, made of dull silver inlaid with arabesques of burnished steel and set with rough turquoise stones. His servant might notice it was missing, and questions might follow. He hesitated briefly, then turned back and took it from the table. He could not avoid seeing the dead thing. How motionless it was! How horribly white the long hands appeared! It looked like a terrible wax figure.\\n\\nAfter locking the door behind him, he crept quietly downstairs. The woodwork creaked and seemed to cry out in pain. Several times he stopped and waited. No: everything was silent. It was only the sound of his own footsteps.\\n\\nWhen he reached the library, he saw the bag and coat in the corner. They had to be hidden somewhere. He unlocked a secret cupboard in the paneling, where he kept his own strange disguises, and placed them inside. He could burn them easily later. Then he took out his watch. It was twenty minutes to two.\\n\\nHe sat down and began to think. Every year\u2014almost every month\u2014men were hanged in England for what he had done. There had been a murderous madness in the air. Some red star had drawn too near the earth.... And yet, what evidence was there against him? Basil Hallward had left the house at eleven. No one had seen him return. Most of the servants were at Selby Royal. His valet had gone to bed.... Paris! Yes. Basil had gone to Paris, on the midnight train, as he had planned. Given his peculiar, secretive habits, it would be months before anyone grew suspicious. Months! Everything could be destroyed long before then.\\n\\nA sudden idea came to him. He put on his fur coat and hat and went into the hall. There he stopped, hearing the policeman\u2019s slow, heavy footsteps on the pavement outside and seeing the flash of the bull\u2019s-eye reflected in the window. He waited, holding his breath.\\n\\nAfter a few moments, he lifted the latch and slipped out, closing the door very gently behind him. Then he began ringing the bell.\"}"
