          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"07b3aec810c2127d077f277d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Moreover, this is a knot that few except sailors can tie, and it is peculiar to the Maltese. I picked up the ribbon at the foot of the lightning rod. It could not have belonged to either of the dead men. Now, even if my deduction from this ribbon\u2014that the Frenchman was a sailor serving on a Maltese vessel\u2014is wrong, I can have done no harm by saying what I did in the advertisement. If I am mistaken, he will merely assume that some circumstance misled me, and will not trouble himself to investigate it. But if I am right, a great deal is gained. Though aware of the murder and innocent of it, the Frenchman will naturally hesitate to answer the advertisement\u2014to claim the Ourang-Outang. He will reason as follows: \u2018I am innocent; I am poor; my Ourang-Outang is extremely valuable\u2014to a man in my circumstances, it is a fortune in itself. Why should I lose it through vague fears of danger? Here it is, within my reach. It was found in the Bois de Boulogne, far from the scene of that slaughter. How could anyone suspect that a brute animal committed the deed? The police are mistaken; they have not obtained the slightest clue. Even if they traced the animal, it would be impossible to prove that I knew of the murder, or to implicate me in it because of that knowledge. Above all, I am known. The advertiser identifies me as the animal\u2019s owner. I do not know how far his knowledge may extend. If I avoid claiming property of such great value, which is known to belong to me, I will at least make the animal suspicious. It is not in my interest to draw attention either to myself or to the beast. I will answer the advertisement, recover the Ourang-Outang, and keep it hidden until this affair has blown over.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAt that moment we heard footsteps on the stairs.\\n\\n\u201cBe ready,\u201d said Dupin, \u201cwith your pistols, but do not use them or reveal them until I give a signal.\u201d\\n\\nThe house\u2019s front door had been left open, and the visitor had entered without ringing and climbed several steps. Now, however, he seemed to hesitate. Soon we heard him going back down. Dupin was hurrying toward the door when we heard him coming up again. This time he did not turn back, but climbed decisively and knocked at the door of our room.\\n\\n\u201cCome in,\u201d said Dupin in a cheerful, welcoming voice.\\n\\nA man entered. He was plainly a sailor\u2014a tall, broad, muscular-looking man with a certain reckless expression that was not entirely unattractive.\"}"
