          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8040bbe161b20042a1153e96\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how mysterious they were! The soul could be animal-like, while the body sometimes seemed spiritual. The senses could become refined, and the intellect could sink into vulgarity. Who could say where physical desire ended and mental desire began? How limited were the neat definitions of ordinary psychologists! And yet how hard it was to choose between the claims of the different schools. Was the soul a shadow sitting in the house of sin? Or was the body truly contained within the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? Separating spirit from matter was a mystery, and joining spirit to matter was a mystery too.\\n\\nHe began to wonder whether psychology could ever become such an exact science that every small force behind life would be exposed. As things stood, we always misunderstood ourselves and seldom understood anyone else. Experience had no moral value. It was simply the name people gave to their mistakes. Moralists usually treated it as a warning, claiming that it helped shape character, praising it as something that taught us what to pursue and what to avoid. But experience had no power to make us act. It was no more an active cause than conscience itself. All it truly showed was that our future would resemble our past, and that a sin we had committed once, with disgust, we would commit many times, with pleasure.\\n\\nIt was clear to him that experiment was the only way to reach a scientific understanding of the passions; and Dorian Gray was a subject ready to hand, promising rich and useful results. His sudden, passionate love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological event of great interest. Curiosity and the desire for new experiences had certainly played a large part in it, but it was not a simple passion. It was highly complex. Whatever came from the purely sensual instinct of youth had been reshaped by the imagination into something that seemed, to the boy himself, far removed from the senses\u2014and was therefore more dangerous. The passions whose origins we misunderstood were the ones that ruled us most strongly. Our weakest motives were those whose nature we understood. Often, when we believed we were experimenting on others, we were actually experimenting on ourselves.\\n\\nAs Lord Henry sat thinking about these matters, someone knocked at the door. His valet entered and reminded him that it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
