          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ffdb7e6f45b8186e08f06d6e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With ordinary resources unavailable, the analyst puts himself in his opponent\u2019s place and identifies with him. In this way, he often sees at once the only ways\u2014sometimes absurdly simple ones\u2014to lead him into error or rush him into a wrong calculation.\\n\\nWhist has long been known for its effect on what is called the calculating faculty. Men of the highest intelligence have taken an apparently inexplicable pleasure in it while dismissing chess as frivolous. Certainly, no similar game puts the power of analysis under such strain. The finest chess player in the world may be little more than a fine chess player; but skill at whist suggests an ability to succeed in any more important undertaking in which one mind contends with another. By skill, I mean perfection in the game, including an understanding of every legitimate source of advantage. These sources are not only numerous but varied, and often lie in areas of thought completely beyond the ordinary mind. Careful observation leads to clear memory, so the focused chess player will do well enough at whist; and Hoyle\u2019s rules, based on the game\u2019s simple mechanics, are generally easy to understand. Thus a strong memory and playing \u201cby the book\u201d are commonly considered the whole of good play. But the analyst\u2019s skill appears in matters beyond mere rules. Silently, he makes countless observations and deductions. His companions may do the same; the difference lies less in the soundness of their deductions than in the quality of their observations. The essential knowledge is knowing what to observe. Our player limits himself in no way. Because the game is his object, he does not ignore clues outside it. He studies his partner\u2019s face, carefully comparing it with each opponent\u2019s. He considers how the cards are arranged in every hand, often counting trumps and honors by watching the glances their holders give them. As play continues, he notices every change of expression, drawing information from signs of certainty, surprise, triumph, or disappointment. From the way a trick is gathered, he judges whether the player who took it can win another in that suit. He recognizes a feint by the manner in which the card is placed on the table. A careless word; the accidental dropping or turning of a card, together with the concern or indifference shown about hiding it; the counting of tricks and their arrangement; embarrassment, hesitation, eagerness, or fear\u2014all provide his seemingly instinctive perception with clues to the true situation.\"}"
