          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1dad29634957c7bb8fa12e22\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell,\u201d I said, \u201cI needed the watermelon.\u201d But he said I didn\u2019t need it to help me escape from prison; that was the difference. He said if I had wanted it to hide a knife in and smuggle it to Jim so he could kill the seneschal, then it would have been all right. So I let the matter drop, though I couldn\u2019t see the advantage of pretending to be a prisoner if I had to sit down and think through a lot of fine distinctions like that every time I saw a chance to grab a watermelon.\\n\\nWell, as I was saying, that morning we waited until everyone had settled down to work and no one was in sight around the yard. Then Tom carried the sack into the lean-to while I stood some distance away to keep watch. After a while he came out, and we sat on the woodpile to talk. He said:\\n\\n\u201cEverything\u2019s all right now except the tools, and that\u2019s easy to fix.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTools?\u201d I said.\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTools for what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, to dig with. We aren\u2019t going to _gnaw_ him out, are we?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAren\u2019t those old broken picks and things in there good enough to dig a black man out with?\u201d I said.\\n\\nHe turned toward me with such a pitying look that it could make a person cry, and said:\\n\\n\u201cHuck Finn, have you _ever_ heard of a prisoner having picks and shovels, along with all the modern conveniences, in his wardrobe to dig himself out with? Now I want to ask you\u2014if you have any sense at all\u2014what kind of chance would _that_ give him to be a hero? Why, they might as well lend him the key and be done with it. Picks and shovels\u2014why, they wouldn\u2019t provide them even for a king.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, then,\u201d I said, \u201cif we don\u2019t want picks and shovels, what do we want?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA couple of case-knives.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo dig out the foundations under that cabin?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDarn it, Tom, that\u2019s foolish.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt doesn\u2019t matter how foolish it is; it\u2019s the _right_ way\u2014and the usual way. And there isn\u2019t any _other_ way that I\u2019ve ever heard of, and I\u2019ve read all the books that give information about these things. They always dig out with a case-knife\u2014and not through dirt, mind you; usually it\u2019s through solid rock. And it takes them weeks and weeks and weeks, forever and ever. Why, look at one of those prisoners in the bottom dungeon of the Castle Deef, in the harbor of Marseilles, who dug himself out that way. How long do you suppose _he_ worked at it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, guess.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. A month and a half.\u201d\"}"
