          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c344b4f326efa85536637985\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Tom woke in the morning, he wondered where he was. He sat up, rubbed his eyes, and looked around. Then he remembered. It was the cool gray dawn, and a deep, peaceful calm filled the woods. Not a leaf moved; no sound disturbed Nature\u2019s quiet thoughts. Dewdrops covered the leaves and grass. A white layer of ash covered the fire, and a thin blue thread of smoke rose straight into the air. Joe and Huck were still asleep.\\n\\nFar away in the woods, one bird called, and another answered. Soon the tapping of a woodpecker could be heard. Slowly, the cool, dim gray morning grew lighter, and sounds multiplied as life began to stir. The wonder of Nature waking and going to work unfolded before the thoughtful boy. A little green worm crawled across a wet leaf, raising two-thirds of its body into the air from time to time and \u201csniffing around,\u201d then moving on again\u2014for Tom said it was measuring. When the worm came near him, Tom sat as still as a stone, his hopes rising and falling as it approached or seemed ready to turn away. At last, after a painful pause with its curved body raised, it came firmly down onto Tom\u2019s leg and began crawling over him. Tom\u2019s heart filled with joy, because this surely meant he would get a new suit of clothes\u2014a bright pirate uniform, without a doubt. Then a line of ants appeared from nowhere and went about their work. One struggled along carrying a dead spider five times its size and dragged it straight up a tree trunk. A brown-spotted ladybug climbed the dizzy height of a blade of grass. Tom leaned close and said, \u201cLadybug, ladybug, fly away home, your house is on fire, your children\u2019s alone.\u201d The insect flew off to check, which did not surprise Tom, because he had long known that ladybugs believed easily in fires and had fooled them more than once. Next came a tumblebug, pushing hard against its ball. Tom touched it, and it pulled its legs against its body and pretended to be dead. By then the birds were making a great uproar. A catbird, the Northern mockingbird, landed in a tree above Tom and sang imitations of its neighbors with great delight. Then a sharp-voiced jay flashed down like blue fire and stopped on a twig almost within reach. It tilted its head and stared at the strangers with intense curiosity. A gray squirrel and a large fox squirrel came hurrying by, stopping now and then to sit up, inspect the boys, and chatter at them. The wild creatures had probably never seen a human being before and hardly knew whether they should be afraid.\"}"
