{"request_id":"d3a4cb9546f7e957aa2e5552","rewritten_text":"Summer advanced, and, crowned with the sun’s powerful rays, the plague sent its unerring arrows across the earth. The nations beneath their influence bowed their heads and died. The corn that had sprung up in abundance lay rotting on the ground by autumn, while the melancholy wretch who had gone out to gather bread for his children lay stiff and plague-stricken in the furrow. The green woods waved their branches majestically, while the dying lay scattered beneath their shade, answering the solemn melody with discordant cries. Painted birds flitted through the shadows; the careless deer rested unharmed upon the fern—the oxen and horses strayed from their unguarded stables and grazed among the wheat, for death fell on man alone.

With summer and mortality, our fears grew. My poor love and I looked at each other, and at our babes. “We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now we shall tell them of our fears, which will then have passed with their cause. Even if they alone remain upon the earth, still they shall live; their cheeks shall not grow pale, nor their sweet voices fade.” Our eldest understood, to some degree, the scenes unfolding around him, and at times, with a serious expression, questioned me about the reason for such vast desolation. But he was only ten years old, and the liveliness of youth soon drove unreasonable care from his brow. Evelyn, a laughing cherub, a playful infant without any notion of pain or sorrow, would shake his light curls back from his eyes and make the halls echo with his merriment, drawing our attention to his games in a thousand artless ways. Clara, our lovely, gentle Clara, was our support, our comfort, our delight. She made it her task to tend the sick, comfort the sorrowful, assist the aged, share in the children’s games, and awaken the young to happiness. She moved through the rooms like a good spirit sent from heaven to brighten our dark hour with an otherworldly radiance. Gratitude and praise marked every place her footsteps had touched. Yet when she stood before us in unassuming simplicity, playing with our children or performing small acts of kindness for Idris with girlish devotion, one wondered in what fair feature of her pure beauty, or in what soft note of her thrilling voice, so much heroism, wisdom, and active goodness could reside.

Summer passed slowly, for we trusted that winter would at least check the disease. That it might vanish altogether was a hope too precious—too deeply felt—to express. Whenever such a thought was carelessly spoken, those who heard it, with a rush of tears and passionate sobs, revealed how profound their fears were and how slight their hopes."}

