          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"38a669b0dad4ab9941f9f7a1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Looking at him, one might have said that Nemesis had stolen Pallas\u2019s shield and shown him the Gorgon\u2019s head. He seemed turned to stone, his face marble in its melancholy. He had lived the delicate, luxurious life of a young man born to wealth and position, a life exquisite in its freedom from sordid cares and its beautiful, boyish insouciance; and now, for the first time, he became aware of Destiny\u2019s terrible mystery, of Doom\u2019s dreadful meaning.\\n\\nHow mad and monstrous it all seemed! Could it be that written on his hand, in characters he could not read but another could decipher, was some fearful secret of sin, some blood-red mark of crime? Was there no possible escape? Were we no better than chessmen moved by an unseen power, vessels shaped by the potter\u2019s whim for honour or disgrace? His reason rebelled against it, yet he felt that some tragedy hung over him and that he had suddenly been called upon to bear an intolerable burden. Actors were fortunate. They could choose whether to appear in tragedy or comedy, whether to suffer or celebrate, laugh or weep. But real life was different. Most men and women were forced to play parts for which they had no qualifications. Our Guildensterns played Hamlet for us, and our Hamlets had to jest like Prince Hal. The world was a stage, but the play was badly cast.\\n\\nSuddenly Mr. Podgers entered the room. When he saw Lord Arthur, he started, and his coarse, fat face turned a greenish yellow. Their eyes met, and for a moment there was silence.\\n\\n\u2018The Duchess left one of her gloves here, Lord Arthur, and asked me to bring it to her,\u2019 Mr. Podgers said at last. \u2018Ah, I see it on the sofa! Good evening.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Mr. Podgers, I insist that you give me a straightforward answer to a question I am about to ask.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Another time, Lord Arthur, but the Duchess is anxious. I am afraid I must go.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You shall not go. The Duchess is in no hurry.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Ladies should not be kept waiting, Lord Arthur,\u2019 said Mr. Podgers with his sickly smile. \u2018The fair sex is inclined to be impatient.\u2019\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s finely shaped lips curled in petulant disdain. The poor Duchess seemed of very little importance to him at that moment. He crossed the room to where Mr. Podgers stood and held out his hand.\\n\\n\u2018Tell me what you saw there,\u2019 he said. \u2018Tell me the truth. I must know it. I am not a child.\u2019\"}"
