          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"89f8dbe720fe972cad690559\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her situation was truly one of unusual difficulty and distress. She felt an urgent, burning need to uncover the mystery surrounding Oliver\u2019s past, yet she could not betray the confidence placed in her by the wretched woman she had just spoken with, who had trusted her as a young and innocent girl. The woman\u2019s words and manner had moved Rose Maylie deeply. Alongside her love for Oliver, and almost equal to it in sincerity and strength, was her earnest hope of bringing the outcast back to repentance and hope.\\n\\nThey intended to stay in London for only three days before leaving for several weeks at a distant part of the coast. It was now midnight on the first day. What action could she decide on and carry out within forty-eight hours? And how could she delay their journey without arousing suspicion?\\n\\nMr. Losberne was staying with them and would remain for the next two days. But Rose knew the good doctor\u2019s impulsive nature too well. She could easily imagine the anger with which, in his first burst of outrage, he would view the person responsible for Oliver\u2019s capture. She could not trust him with the secret while no experienced person was available to support her arguments on the girl\u2019s behalf. For the same reasons, she had to be extremely careful and discreet in telling Mrs. Maylie, whose first instinct would certainly be to discuss the matter with the worthy doctor. Seeking legal advice was hardly possible either, even if she had known where to find it. At one point she thought of asking Harry for help; but this brought back the memory of their last parting. It seemed unfair to call him back when\u2014her eyes filled with tears as she followed this thought\u2014he might by now have learned to forget her and might be happier away from her.\\n\\nTroubled by these conflicting thoughts, leaning first toward one plan and then another, and drawing back from each as new objections arose, Rose spent a sleepless and anxious night. After thinking the matter over again the next day, she reached the painful decision to consult Harry.\\n\\n\u201cIf it hurts him to return here,\u201d she thought, \u201chow much more it will hurt me! But perhaps he will not come. He may write, or he may come in person and carefully avoid seeing me\u2014he did so when he left. I scarcely expected it then; but it was better for both of us.\u201d At this point Rose let the pen fall and turned away, as if even the paper that would carry her message must not see her weep.\"}"
