          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d37aafa88f602cb214e9e1e7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They were bringing along a very fine-looking old gentleman and a good-looking younger man with his right arm in a sling. My goodness, how the people yelled and laughed, and kept it up. But I couldn\u2019t see anything funny about it, and I figured the duke and the king would have trouble finding anything funny either. I expected them to turn pale. But no, not a bit did they turn pale. The duke never showed that he suspected anything. He just went around making foolish happy noises, as pleased as a jug bubbling out buttermilk. As for the king, he stared sadly at the newcomers, as if the thought that there could be such frauds and rascals in the world gave him a deep stomachache. Oh, he acted wonderfully. Many of the leading people gathered around him, to show that they were on his side. The old gentleman who had just arrived looked completely confused. Soon he began to speak, and I immediately saw that he pronounced words like an Englishman\u2014not in the king\u2019s way, though the king\u2019s imitation was pretty good. I can\u2019t repeat the old gentleman\u2019s exact words or imitate his speech, but he turned to the crowd and said something like this:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a surprise I wasn\u2019t expecting, and I\u2019ll admit, honestly and openly, that I\u2019m not well prepared to face it or answer it. My brother and I have had bad luck. He has broken his arm, and our baggage was mistakenly unloaded at a town upriver sometime last night. I am Peter Wilks\u2019s brother Harvey, and this is his brother William, who can neither hear nor speak\u2014and can\u2019t even make useful signs now that he has only one hand to use. We are who we claim to be; and in a day or two, when I get the baggage, I can prove it. Until then, I won\u2019t say anything more. I\u2019ll go to the hotel and wait.\u201d\\n\\nSo he and the new mute man started off, and the king laughed and blurted out:\\n\\n\u201cBroken his arm\u2014_very_ likely, _isn\u2019t_ it? And very convenient, too, for a fraud who has to make signs but hasn\u2019t learned how. Lost their baggage! That\u2019s _excellent_\u2014and very clever\u2014under the _circumstances!_\u201d\\n\\nThen he laughed again, and everyone else did too, except three or four people, or perhaps half a dozen. One was the doctor. Another was a sharp-looking gentleman carrying an old-fashioned carpetbag made from carpet material. He had just come off the steamboat and was speaking quietly to the doctor, glancing toward the king now and then and nodding. It was Levi Bell, the lawyer who had gone up to Louisville. Another was a big, rough, strong man who had come along, listened to everything the old gentleman said, and was now listening to the king.\"}"
