          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eb70933e88852b361b83a6e2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHe was delivered to you. You see that I know the circumstances?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is certainly true,\u201d said Defarge. His wife had conveyed to him, through an accidental touch of her elbow as she knitted and hummed, that he would do best to answer, but always briefly.\\n\\n\u201cIt was to you,\u201d said the spy, \u201cthat his daughter came; and it was from your care that she took him, accompanied by a neat brown gentleman; what was his name?\u2014in a little wig\u2014Lorry\u2014of Tellson and Company\u2019s bank\u2014and brought him to England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is true,\u201d Defarge repeated.\\n\\n\u201cVery interesting memories!\u201d said the spy. \u201cI have known Doctor Manette and his daughter in England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes?\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cYou don\u2019t hear much about them now?\u201d asked the spy.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cIn fact,\u201d Madame interrupted, looking up from her work and her little song, \u201cwe never hear about them. We received news of their safe arrival, and perhaps one other letter, or perhaps two; but since then, they have gradually gone their separate ways in life\u2014we ours, they theirs\u2014and we have had no correspondence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly so, madame,\u201d replied the spy. \u201cShe is going to be married.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGoing?\u201d Madame echoed. \u201cShe was pretty enough to have been married long ago. You English are cold, it seems to me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! You know I am English.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can tell by your tongue,\u201d Madame replied; \u201cand I suppose the man is like his tongue.\u201d\\n\\nHe did not take this identification as a compliment, but made the best of it and dismissed it with a laugh. After sipping his cognac to the last drop, he added:\\n\\n\u201cYes, Miss Manette is going to be married. But not to an Englishman; to someone who, like herself, was born French. And speaking of Gaspard\u2014ah, poor Gaspard! It was cruel, cruel!\u2014it is a curious thing that she is going to marry the nephew of the Marquis for whom Gaspard was raised to that height of so many feet; in other words, the present Marquis. But he lives unknown in England, where he is no Marquis; he is Mr. Charles Darnay. D\u2019Aulnais is his mother\u2019s family name.\u201d\\n\\nMadame Defarge knitted steadily, but the news had a clear effect on her husband. Whatever he did behind the little counter, striking a match and lighting his pipe, he was troubled, and his hand was unsteady. The spy would not have been a spy if he had failed to notice it or record it in his mind.\\n\\nHaving made at least this one discovery, whatever it might prove worth, and with no customers arriving to give him another opportunity, Mr. Barsad paid for his drink and left. Before departing, he took the opportunity to say politely that he looked forward to the pleasure of seeing Monsieur and Madame Defarge again.\"}"
