          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"623aa83853d82c10e918efce\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, bother my hat! I want Virginia!\u201d cried the little Duke, laughing, as they galloped on to the railway station. There Mr. Otis asked the station-master whether anyone matching Virginia\u2019s description had been seen on the platform, but he learned nothing. The station-master, however, sent messages up and down the line and assured him that everyone would keep a close watch for her. After buying a hat for the little Duke from a linen-draper who was just closing his shop, Mr. Otis rode to Bexley, a village about four miles away. He had been told that it was a favorite place for gypsies, since a large common lay beside it. There they woke the local policeman, but he could give them no information. After riding all over the common, they turned their horses toward home and reached the Chase at about eleven o\u2019clock, exhausted and nearly heartbroken. Washington and the twins were waiting for them at the gatehouse with lanterns, because the avenue was very dark. No trace at all of Virginia had been found. The gypsies had been caught on Brockley Meadows, but Virginia was not with them. They explained that they had left suddenly because they had mistaken the date of Chorton Fair and hurried away for fear of being late. They had been deeply upset to hear that Virginia was missing, since they were very grateful to Mr. Otis for letting them camp in his park, and four of them had stayed behind to help search. The carp pond had been dragged, and the entire Chase had been searched, but nothing had been found. It was clear that, at least for that night, Virginia was lost. In the deepest despair, Mr. Otis and the boys walked up to the house, while the groom followed with the two horses and the pony. In the hall they found a group of frightened servants. Poor Mrs. Otis lay on a sofa in the library, almost out of her mind with fear and worry, while the old housekeeper bathed her for