ause of all these things that I am moved to treat you gently now\u2014yes, Edward Leeford, even now\u2014and to blush at the unworthiness of one who bears his name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat does the name have to do with it?\u201d the other asked, after watching, partly in silence and partly with stubborn wonder, his companion\u2019s agitation. \u201cWhat does the name mean to me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing,\u201d replied Mr. Brownlow. \u201cNothing to you. But it was hers, and even now, after all these years, it brings back to me, an old man, the warmth and excitement I once felt simply hearing it spoken by a stranger. I am very glad you have changed it\u2014very, very glad.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is all very fine,\u201d said Monks, retaining his assumed name, after a long silence. During it, he had jerked himself back and forth in sullen defiance, while Mr. Brownlow sat with his face shaded by one hand.\"}"
