          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aace1711ed3106125174ba9a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot of Kellynch Hall, in Somersetshire, never picked up any book for pleasure except the Baronetage. It gave him something to do in an idle hour and comfort in a troubled one. Reading about the few families with the oldest baronetcy patents filled him with admiration and respect. Any unpleasant feelings caused by family matters naturally turned into pity and contempt as he read through the almost endless new titles created during the previous century. And if nothing else could interest him, he could always read his own history with unfailing pleasure. This was the page where his favourite book always opened:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married, July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, in the county of Gloucester, by which lady (who died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly how the paragraph had come from the printer. But Sir Walter had improved it for his own information and that of his family by adding, after the date of Mary\u2019s birth: \u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq. of Uppercross, in the county of Somerset.\u201d He had also carefully added the exact day of the month on which he had lost his wife.\\n\\nThe family\u2019s history and rise followed, written in the usual style: how it had first settled in Cheshire; how it was mentioned by Dugdale; how one of its members had served as high sheriff and represented a borough in three successive parliaments; its loyal service and the dignity of a baronetcy, granted in the first year of Charles II; and all the Marys and Elizabeths whom its members had married. Altogether, it filled two handsome duodecimo pages and ended with the arms and motto: \u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset.\u201d Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting appeared once more at the end:\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity was the beginning and the end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character: vanity about his appearance and his position. He had been remarkably handsome when young, and at fifty-four he was still a very fine-looking man. Few women cared more about their appearance than he did, and no valet serving a newly made lord could have been more pleased with his place in society. He believed beauty was a blessing second only to a baronetcy; and Sir Walter Elliot, who possessed both, was the constant object of his deepest respect and devotion.\\n\\nHis good looks and rank gave him one reasonable claim to his own admiration, since he must have owed them the wife he had\u2014a woman of far better character than anything he himself deserved.\"}"
