{"request_id":"eae9be30c8f4bf5dd5af19ae","rewritten_text":"It then showed my altitude to be 132,000 feet, or five-and-twenty miles, so that I was at that moment surveying an extent of the earth’s surface equal to no less than the three hundred-and-twentieth part of its whole area. At nine o’clock I had once more lost sight of land toward the east, though not before noticing that the balloon was moving swiftly to the N. N. W. The ocean’s convexity beneath me was exceedingly plain, although my view was frequently broken by the masses of cloud drifting to and fro. I now observed that even the lightest vapors rose no higher than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I attempted an experiment by casting a handful of feathers through the valve. Contrary to my expectation, they did not float, but fell straight downward, like a bullet, all together, and with extraordinary speed—vanishing from sight within a few seconds. At first I could not understand this remarkable occurrence, being unable to suppose that my ascent had suddenly undergone so immense an acceleration. It soon occurred to me, however, that the atmosphere had become too thin to support even feathers; that they had truly fallen, as they seemed to, with great rapidity; and that I had been deceived by the combined velocities of their descent and my own upward motion.

“By ten o’clock I discovered that there was very little to demand my immediate attention. Everything proceeded smoothly, and I believed the balloon to be rising with a speed that increased every moment, though I no longer possessed any means of measuring that increase. I felt no pain or discomfort whatever, and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I occupied myself partly with inspecting my various apparatus, and partly with renewing the air within the chamber. This last task I resolved to perform at intervals of forty minutes, chiefly for the sake of preserving my health, rather than because such frequent renewal was absolutely required. Meanwhile, I could not refrain from forming anticipations. Fancy delighted in the wild, dreamlike regions of the moon. Imagination, finding herself free of restraint for once, wandered at pleasure through the ever-shifting marvels of a dim and unstable land. At one moment there were ancient, hoary forests, jagged precipices, and waterfalls plunging noisily into bottomless abysses. Then I was suddenly transported into quiet noonday solitudes, where no breath of heaven ever entered, and where immense fields of poppies and slender, lily-like flowers extended a weary distance, forever silent and still."}

