          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b93e185a4ea2bae0ca73c61e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Moreover, this is a knot that few but sailors can tie, and it is characteristic of the Maltese. I found the ribbon at the foot of the lightning-rod. It could not have belonged to either of the dead men. Now, even if my deduction from this ribbon\u2014that the Frenchman was a sailor serving on a Maltese vessel\u2014should prove mistaken, I can have done no harm by saying what I did in the advertisement. If I am wrong, he will simply assume that some circumstance has misled me, and will not trouble himself to investigate it. But if I am right, much has been accomplished. Though aware of the murder and innocent of it, the Frenchman will naturally hesitate before answering the advertisement\u2014before claiming the Ourang-Outang. He will argue in this fashion:\u2014\u2018I am innocent; I am poor; my Ourang-Outang is extremely valuable\u2014to a man in my position, a fortune in itself\u2014why should I surrender it because of vague fears of danger? It is here, within my reach. It was discovered in the Bois de Boulogne\u2014far from the place where that slaughter occurred. How could anyone suspect that a mere animal committed the crime? The police are baffled\u2014they have obtained not the smallest clew. Even if they should track down the creature, they could never prove that I knew of the murder, or connect me with the guilt merely because I possessed that knowledge. Above all, _I am known._ The advertiser identifies me as the animal\u2019s owner. I do not know how far his information may go. If I refrain from claiming property of such value, which is known to be mine, I shall at least make the animal appear suspicious. It is not in my interest to draw attention either to myself or to the beast. I will reply to the advertisement, recover the Ourang-Outang, and keep it hidden until this affair has passed.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAt that instant we heard footsteps on the stairs.\\n\\n\u201cBe prepared,\u201d said Dupin, \u201cwith your pistols, but do not use them or reveal them until I give a signal.\u201d\\n\\nThe house\u2019s front door had been left open, and the visitor entered without ringing, proceeding several steps up the staircase. Then, however, he appeared to pause. Soon we heard him going back down. Dupin was hurrying toward the door when once more we heard him ascending. This time he did not retreat, but came up firmly and knocked at the door of our room.\\n\\n\u201cCome in,\u201d said Dupin, in a bright and cordial voice.\\n\\nA man entered. He was plainly a sailor\u2014a tall, broad, powerfully built fellow, with a somewhat reckless expression that was not entirely unattractive.\"}"
