{"request_id":"8f8153d1e74bad3069bace52","rewritten_text":"And Mrs Musgrove seized the first chance of being alone with Anne to say, “Oh! Miss Anne, I cannot help wishing Mrs Charles possessed a little of your way with those children. They are quite different beings under your care! But then, generally speaking, they are so spoilt! It is a pity you cannot teach your sister how to manage them. They are as fine, healthy children as one could ever see, poor little dears! I say that without partiality; but Mrs Charles knows no more how they ought to be treated—! Bless me! how troublesome they can be at times. I assure you, Miss Anne, it keeps me from wishing to have them at our house as often as I otherwise might. I believe Mrs Charles is not altogether pleased that I do not invite them more frequently; but you know it is very disagreeable to have children with one whom one must be correcting every minute; ‘don’t do this,’ and ‘don’t do that;’ or whom one can keep in tolerable order only with more cake than is good for them.”

Mary, moreover, had given her this information. “Mrs Musgrove considers all her servants so dependable that questioning it would be high treason; but I am certain, without exaggeration, that her upper housemaid and laundrymaid, instead of attending to their work, are wandering about the village all day. I encounter them wherever I go; and I declare, I never enter my nursery twice without seeing one of them. If Jemima were not the most trustworthy, steady creature in the world, it would be enough to corrupt her; for she tells me they are always trying to persuade her to walk out with them.” On Mrs Musgrove’s side, it was, “I make it a rule never to interfere in any concern of my daughter-in-law’s, because I know it would not answer; but I shall tell you, Miss Anne, since you may perhaps be able to put matters right, that I have no very favourable opinion of Mrs Charles’s nursery-maid: I hear odd accounts of her; she is always out and about; and from what I myself know, I can say she is so fond of dressing finely that she is enough to spoil every servant she comes near. Mrs Charles certainly swears by her, I know; but I merely give you this warning, that you may keep watch; for if you notice anything wrong, you need not hesitate to mention it.”

Again, Mary complained that Mrs Musgrove was often inclined not to give her the precedence due to her when they dined at the Great House with other families; and she could not see why she should be treated as so much at home that she lost her proper place."}

