          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"997e3d7de3e9a5a912095216\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is remarkable! I must meet Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\nHallward rose and paced back and forth through the garden. After a while he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cDorian Gray is, to me, merely a motive in art. You may see nothing in him. I see everything. He is never more present in my work than when no likeness of him appears. As I have said, he suggests a new manner. I discover him in the curves of certain lines, in the beauty and delicacy of particular colours. That is all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen why do you refuse to exhibit his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked.\\n\\n\u201cBecause, without meaning to, I have placed in it some expression of this strange artistic idolatry, which, naturally, I have never wished to mention to him. He knows nothing of it. He must never know anything of it. Yet the world might suspect it, and I will not expose my soul to their shallow, prying eyes. My heart shall never be placed beneath their microscope. There is far too much of myself in the picture, Harry\u2014far too much!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPoets are less scrupulous than you. They understand how useful passion can be in print. These days, a broken heart may go through many editions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI despise them for that,\u201d Hallward exclaimed. \u201cAn artist ought to make beautiful things, but put none of his own life into them. We live in an age when men regard art as though it were intended to be autobiography. We have lost the abstract feeling for beauty. One day I shall show the world what it is; and for that very reason, the world shall never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe you are mistaken, Basil, but I will not debate it with you. Only the intellectually lost ever argue. Tell me, is Dorian Gray very fond of you?\u201d\\n\\nThe painter reflected for several moments. \u201cHe likes me,\u201d he replied at last; \u201cI know that he does. Naturally, I flatter him terribly. I take a peculiar pleasure in saying things to him that I know I shall regret saying. Usually he is charming toward me, and we sit in the studio discussing a thousand subjects. At times, however, he is cruelly thoughtless, and appears to enjoy causing me pain. Then I feel, Harry, that I have surrendered my entire soul to someone who treats it as though it were a flower to place in his coat, a small decoration to gratify his vanity, an ornament for a summer day.\u201d\"}"
