          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"007f8ff12de46d00c4b39c5a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Col. Grangerford was a gentleman, you see\u2014completely a gentleman, and so was his family. He was well born, as people say, and that matters as much in a man as in a horse, according to the Widow Douglas. No one ever denied that she belonged to the highest class in our town; Pap said it too, though he himself had no more quality than a mudcat. Col. Grangerford was very tall and thin, with a pale, darkish complexion and not a trace of red in it. Every morning he shaved his narrow face clean. He had extremely thin lips and nostrils, a high nose, heavy eyebrows, and the blackest eyes, set so far back that they seemed to look at you from caves. His forehead was high, and his straight black hair fell to his shoulders. His hands were long and thin. Every day he wore a clean shirt and a complete linen suit, white enough to hurt your eyes; on Sundays he wore a blue tailcoat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane with a silver handle. There was nothing frivolous about him, and he was never loud. He was as kind as possible\u2014you could feel it, and that gave you confidence. Sometimes he smiled, and it was good to see. But when he stood straight as a liberty pole and lightning began to flash beneath his eyebrows, you wanted to climb a tree before finding out what was wrong. He never had to tell anyone to behave; everyone had good manners around him. Everyone liked having him there, too. He was usually like sunshine\u2014I mean, he made everything feel like good weather. When he became a cloudbank, it grew terribly dark for half a minute, and that was enough; nothing else went wrong for a week.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came down in the morning, the whole family rose from their chairs and wished them good morning. No one sat again until they had taken their seats. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard, mixed a glass of bitters, and handed it to him. He held it while they mixed their own, and then they bowed and said, \u201cOur duty to you, sir, and madam.\u201d The two elders bowed slightly and thanked them, and all three drank. Bob and Tom then poured a spoonful of water over the sugar and the little bit of whisky or apple brandy at the bottom of their tumblers, gave it to me and Buck, and we drank to the old people too.\"}"
