          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6b40f449e414469054d9a703\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With great care, my friend drove a peg into the ground at the exact spot where the beetle had fallen. He then took a tape measure from his pocket. He fixed one end to the point on the tree nearest the peg, unrolled it to the peg, and then continued unrolling it in the direction already set by the tree and peg, for fifty feet\u2014while Jupiter cleared the brambles with his scythe. At that point, he drove in a second peg and drew a rough circle about four feet across, using the peg as its center. He took a spade, gave one to Jupiter and one to me, and urged us to begin digging as quickly as possible.\\n\\nTo tell the truth, I had never especially enjoyed such work, and at that moment I would gladly have refused. Night was approaching, and I was already very tired from the exercise. But I could see no way out, and I feared that refusing would upset my poor friend. If I could have relied on Jupiter\u2019s help, I would not have hesitated to take the madman home by force. But I knew the old negro too well to expect him to help me in any physical struggle with his master. I was certain Legrand had been influenced by one of the many Southern superstitions about buried treasure, and that finding the scarab\u00e6us had strengthened his belief\u2014or perhaps Jupiter\u2019s stubborn insistence that it was \u201ca bug of real gold\u201d had done so. A mind inclined toward madness could easily be carried away by such ideas, especially when they supported beliefs already held. I also remembered the poor fellow\u2019s claim that the beetle was \u201cthe index of his fortune.\u201d In the end, I was deeply troubled and confused, but decided to accept the situation, dig willingly, and so convince the visionary through clear evidence that his beliefs were mistaken.\\n\\nAfter the lanterns were lit, we all began working with a zeal better suited to a more sensible purpose. As the light shone on us and our tools, I could not help thinking what a picturesque group we made, and how strange and suspicious our work must have seemed to any stranger who happened to come upon us.\\n\\nWe dug steadily for two hours.\"}"
