          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2f6961f95eae16f60741ffd2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And he always pretended that Peter had written the things to him; but that was false: he had got every single one of them from that young flathead we paddled up to the steamboat.\\n\\nThen Mary Jane brought out the letter her father had left, and the king read it aloud and wept over it. It left the house and three thousand dollars in gold to the girls; and it left the tanyard (which was doing a good business), together with some other houses and land (worth around seven thousand), and three thousand dollars in gold to Harvey and William, and said where the six thousand dollars in cash was hidden in the cellar. So the two frauds said they would go bring it up, and make everything fair and above-board; and they told me to come along with a candle. We closed the cellar door behind us, and when they found the bag they poured it onto the floor, and it was a beautiful sight, all those yellow-boys. My, how the king\u2019s eyes shone! He slapped the duke on the shoulder and said:\\n\\n\u201cOh, _this_ ain\u2019t bully or anything! Oh, no, I reckon not! Why, Bilji, it beats the Nonesuch, _don\u2019t_ it?\u201d\\n\\nThe duke agreed that it did. They handled the yellow-boys, sifted them through their fingers, and let them jingle onto the floor; and the king said:\\n\\n\u201cIt ain\u2019t any use talking; being brothers to a rich dead man and representatives of foreign heirs that have been left behind is the line for you and me, Bilge. This here comes from trusting Providence. It\u2019s the best way, in the long run. I\u2019ve tried them all, and there ain\u2019t any better way.\u201d\\n\\nNearly everybody would have been content with the pile and accepted it on trust; but no, they had to count it. So they counted it, and it came out four hundred and fifteen dollars short. Says the king:\\n\\n\u201cDern him, I wonder what he did with that four hundred and fifteen dollars?\u201d\\n\\nThey fretted over it for a while and searched everywhere for it. Then the duke says:\\n\\n\u201cWell, he was a very sick man, and likely made a mistake\u2014I reckon that\u2019s how it was. The best thing is to let it go, and say nothing about it. We can spare it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, shucks, yes, we can _spare_ it. I don\u2019t care anything about that\u2014it\u2019s the _count_ I\u2019m thinking of. We want to be mighty fair and open and above-board here, you know. We want to carry this here money upstairs and count it before everybody\u2014then there ain\u2019t anything suspicious. But when the dead man says there\u2019s six thousand dollars, you know, we don\u2019t want to\u2014\u201d\"}"
