          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5d65430b8c9255ca88ca2c70\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But he had oppressed no one and imprisoned no one. Far from harshly demanding payment of what was owed him, he had voluntarily given it up, cast himself into a world that offered him no favor, secured his own private place there, and earned his own living. Monsieur Gabelle had administered the impoverished and encumbered estate according to written instructions: to spare the people, giving them what little could be given\u2014such fuel as the heavy creditors allowed them in winter, and such produce as could be saved from the same grasp in summer. No doubt he had stated and proved this for his own protection, so that it could not fail to appear now.\\n\\nThis strengthened the desperate resolution Charles Darnay had begun to form: he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. Like the sailor in the old story, the winds and currents had carried him within reach of the Loadstone Rock; it was drawing him toward itself, and he had to go. Everything that came before his mind pushed him onward, faster and faster, more steadily, toward that terrible attraction. His hidden uneasiness had been that evil purposes were being carried out in his unhappy country by evil instruments, while he, who could not help knowing that he was better than they, was not there trying to do something to halt the bloodshed and uphold the claims of mercy and humanity. With this uneasiness half suppressed and half accusing him, he had been led to compare himself with the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so powerful. That comparison, damaging to himself, had been immediately followed by Monseigneur\u2019s sneers, which had wounded him deeply, and by Stryver\u2019s, which above all were coarse and painful for old reasons. Then had come Gabelle\u2019s letter: an innocent prisoner\u2019s appeal, in danger of death, to his justice, honor, and good name.\\n\\nHis decision was made. He had to go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. The Loadstone Rock was drawing him, and he had to sail on until he struck it. He knew of no rock and saw scarcely any danger. The purpose behind what he had done, even though he had left it unfinished, presented the act to him in a form that France would gratefully recognize when he appeared to claim it. Then that glorious vision of doing good\u2014which is so often the hopeful mirage of so many good minds\u2014rose before him, and he even saw himself, in that illusion, possessing some influence with which to guide the raging Revolution that was running so terribly wild.\"}"
