          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b20c1c37fffae7fd8792d421\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental qualities described as analytical are, in themselves, scarcely open to analysis. We recognize them only through their effects. Among other things, we know that, when possessed in excessive degree, they are always a source of the keenest pleasure to their owner. As the strong man takes pride in his bodily power, enjoying exercises that set his muscles in motion, so the analyst delights in that mental activity which _disentangles._ He finds pleasure even in the most insignificant occupations that bring his talent into use. He is partial to enigmas, conundrums, and hieroglyphics, displaying in solving each a degree of _acumen_ that seems to ordinary understanding almost supernatural. His conclusions, produced by the very essence of method, have, in fact, every appearance of intuition.\\n\\nThe faculty of re-solution may perhaps be greatly strengthened by mathematical study, particularly by that highest division of the subject which, unfairly and solely because of its retrograde operations, has been termed, as though _par excellence_, analysis. Yet calculation is not analysis in itself. A chess-player, for instance, performs the former without attempting the latter. It follows that chess is widely misunderstood in its effects upon the mind. I am not composing a treatise now, but merely introducing a somewhat unusual narrative with observations made very much at random; I shall therefore take this opportunity to maintain that the higher and more useful powers of the reflective intellect are exercised more decisively by the quiet game of draughts than by all the elaborate frivolity of chess. In the latter game, where the pieces move in different and _bizarre_ ways and possess varied, shifting values, what is merely complicated is mistaken (as often happens) for what is profound. The _attention_ is intensely engaged. If it falters for even a moment, an oversight occurs, bringing injury or defeat. Since the possible moves are not only numerous but involute, the opportunities for such oversights increase; and in nine games out of ten, it is the more concentrated rather than the more acute player who wins. In draughts, by contrast, where the moves are _unique_ and vary little, the likelihood of inadvertence is reduced, and with mere attention left comparatively idle, whatever advantage either side gains comes from superior _acumen_. To make the point less abstract, imagine a game of draughts in which the pieces have been reduced to four kings, so that, naturally, no oversight need be anticipated. It is clear that here the victory can be determined (assuming the players are otherwise equal) only by some _recherch\u00e9_ move, arising from a powerful exertion of intellect.\"}"
