          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d7fdd30e58d82c217d8deeb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After joining in the general expressions of sorrow over the dreadful consequences of the affair, which Elizabeth regarded as nearly certain, while Miss Bennet could not say they were entirely impossible, the former went on: \u201cBut tell me everything about it that I have not yet heard. Give me more particulars. What did Colonel Forster say? Had they no suspicion of anything before the elopement? They must have seen them together constantly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted that he had often suspected some partiality, particularly on Lydia\u2019s part, but nothing that caused him alarm. I am so sorry for him. His conduct was as attentive and kind as possible. He was on his way to us, intending to assure us of his concern, before he had any notion that they had not gone to Scotland; when that fear became known, it hastened his journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny persuaded that Wickham would not marry? Did he know they meant to run away? Had Colonel Forster seen Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when questioned by him, Denny denied knowing anything of their intentions and would not state his true opinion. He did not repeat his belief that they would not marry, and from that I am inclined to hope he may have been misunderstood earlier.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster came in person, none of you, I suppose, doubted that they were actually married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such a thought have entered our minds? I was somewhat uneasy\u2014a little afraid for my sister\u2019s happiness in marrying him, because I knew his behaviour had not always been entirely proper. My father and mother knew nothing of this; they only saw how imprudent the match must be. Kitty then confessed, with very natural satisfaction at knowing more than the rest of us, that in Lydia\u2019s last letter she had prepared her for such a step. It seems she had known for many weeks that they were in love.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I believe not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think poorly of Wickham himself? Does he know his true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must admit that he did not speak of Wickham as favourably as he once had. He considered him imprudent and extravagant; and since this unhappy affair occurred, it is said that he left Meryton deeply in debt: though I hope that is untrue.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secretive, if we had told what we knew of him, this could never have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it would have been better,\u201d her sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cBut to reveal a person\u2019s former faults without knowing what their present feelings were seemed unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
