          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e62b624181bb3daeebd3df7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Thus I passed two years. Day after day, hundreds upon hundreds slipped by; they brought no outward changes, yet a few slowly worked upon my mind as I moved toward death. I began to study more; to sympathize more with the thoughts of others as expressed in books; to read history, and to lose my individuality among the multitude that had lived before me. Perhaps, as the immediate sensation of suffering faded, I became more human. Solitude also lost some of its appeal: I began once more to long for sympathy\u2014not that I was ever tempted to seek the crowd, but I wished for one friend to love me. You may say that I gradually became fit to return to society. I do not think so. The sympathy I desired had to be so pure, so free from the influence of outward circumstances, that in the world I could not avoid being thwarted by the coarse materials that constantly mingle even with its finest feelings. Believe me, I was then less suited to any communion with my fellow creatures than before. When I left them, they had tormented me as pain and sickness may torment: something external to the mind that irritated it, and that I wished to cast away. But now I would have desired sympathy; I would have wished to bind my soul to another\u2019s, and would have prepared myself for abundant disappointment and suffering; for I was tender as the sensitive plant, all nerve. I did not desire sympathy and aid in ambition or wisdom, but sweet and mutual affection; smiles to encourage me and gentle words of comfort. I wished for one heart into which I could pour my complaints without restraint, and from the heavenly nature of that soil, blessed fruit might spring from such poor seed. Yet how could I find this? The love that is the soul of friendship is a gentle spirit, seldom found except when two amiable beings are joined from early youth, or bound together by shared suffering and pursuits; it comes to some of the chosen unsought and unrecognized; it descends like gentle dew upon favored places, which, however barren before, become under its benign influence fertile with every sweet plant. But when desired, it flees; it mocks the prayers of those who seek it; it will give itself, but cannot be pursued.\\n\\nI knew all this and did not go in search of sympathy; yet there, on my solitary heath, beneath my humble roof, where all around was desert, it came to me like a sunbeam in winter, adorning the landscape even as it helped dissolve the drifted snow. Alas, the sun shone upon blighted fruit; I did not revive beneath its radiance, for I was too utterly ruined to feel its kindly power.\"}"
