{"request_id":"a5affea3cc754f8968c59c04","rewritten_text":"Summer moved onward, and, crowned by the sun’s powerful rays, plague sent her unerring arrows across the earth. The nations beneath their influence bowed their heads and perished. The corn that had sprung up abundantly lay rotting on the ground in autumn, while the melancholy wretch who had gone forth to gather bread for his children lay stiff and plague-stricken in the furrow. The green woods waved their branches majestically, while the dying lay scattered beneath their shade, answering the solemn melody with discordant cries. Painted birds flitted through the shadows; the careless deer rested unharmed upon the fern—the oxen and horses wandered from their unguarded stables and grazed among the wheat, for death had fallen upon man alone.

With summer and mortality, our fears increased. My poor love and I looked at one another, and at our babes.—“We will save them, Idris,” I said, “I will save them. Years hence we shall tell them of our fears, then vanished with their cause. Though they alone should remain upon the earth, still they shall live; their cheeks shall not grow pale, nor their sweet voices languish.” Our eldest understood in some measure the scenes unfolding around him, and sometimes, with grave looks, asked me why such vast desolation had come to pass. But he was only ten years old; and youthful hilarity soon drove unreasonable care from his brow. Evelyn, a laughing cherub, a playful infant without any notion of pain or sorrow, would shake his light curls back from his eyes and make the halls ring with his merriment, drawing our attention in a thousand artless ways to his play. Clara, our lovely, gentle Clara, was our support, our comfort, and our joy. She made it her duty to tend the sick, console the sorrowful, aid the aged, and share the games and awaken the cheerfulness of the young. She moved through the rooms like a good spirit sent from the celestial kingdom to brighten our dark hour with borrowed splendour. Gratitude and praise marked the places her footsteps had touched. Yet when she stood before us in unassuming simplicity, playing with our children, or with girlish diligence performing small kindnesses for Idris, one wondered in what fair feature of her pure beauty, in what soft note of her thrilling voice, so much heroism, wisdom, and active goodness could dwell.

Summer passed slowly, for we trusted that winter would at least restrain the disease. That it might disappear entirely was a hope too precious—too deeply felt—to be spoken. Whenever such a thought was carelessly expressed, those who heard it, with a rush of tears and passionate sobs, showed how profound their fears were and how slight their hopes."}

