{"request_id":"22c1ad74cc5e672630203e0b","rewritten_text":"We endured a few miserable hours until eleven o’clock, when the trial was to begin. Since my father and the rest of the family were required to attend as witnesses, I went with them to the court. Throughout that wretched parody of justice, I suffered a living torture. The question was whether the result of my curiosity and unlawful experiments would bring about the deaths of two fellow human beings: one, a smiling infant filled with innocence and joy; the other, murdered far more terribly, with every added disgrace that could make the crime unforgettable in its horror. Justine, too, was a worthy girl, possessing qualities that promised to make her life happy; now everything was to be buried in a dishonorable grave—and I was the cause! A thousand times I would rather have confessed to the crime attributed to Justine, but I had been absent when it was committed, and such a confession would have been dismissed as the ravings of a madman and would not have freed her, who suffered because of me.

Justine appeared calm. She wore mourning clothes, and her face, always appealing, was made exquisitely beautiful by the solemnity of her feelings. Yet she seemed confident in her innocence and did not tremble, though thousands stared at and cursed her; all the kindness her beauty might otherwise have inspired had been erased from the spectators’ minds by their imagining the enormity of the crime she was believed to have committed. She was composed, but her composure was plainly forced; and since her earlier confusion had been presented as evidence of guilt, she steeled herself to appear courageous. When she entered the courtroom, she looked around and quickly saw where we were sitting. A tear seemed to cloud her eye when she noticed us, but she soon recovered, and an expression of sorrowful affection appeared to confirm her complete innocence.

The trial began, and after the advocate prosecuting her had stated the charge, several witnesses were called. Several strange circumstances combined against her, and they might have shaken anyone who did not possess the certainty of her innocence that I did. She had been away all night on the night of the murder, and near morning a market woman had seen her not far from the place where the murdered child’s body was later found. The woman asked what she was doing there, but Justine looked very strangely and gave only a confused, unintelligible reply. She returned home around eight o’clock, and when someone asked where she had spent the night, she answered that she had been searching for the child and urgently asked whether anything had been heard about him."}

