          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d6306bd51c30140e13702759\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I ran to her, gathered her into my arms, covered her with kisses, and pleaded:\\n\\n\u201cSpeak, darling, speak! What is it?\u201d\\n\\nHer head drooped helplessly against my breast, and she breathed, scarcely audibly:\\n\\n\u201cHELLO-CENTRAL!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuick!\u201d I cried to Clarence. \u201cTelephone the king\u2019s homeopath and tell him to come!\u201d\\n\\nWithin two minutes I was kneeling beside the child\u2019s crib, while Sandy sent servants hurrying in every direction throughout the palace. I understood the case almost at once\u2014membranous croup! Bending over her, I whispered:\\n\\n\u201cWake up, sweetheart! Hello-Central.\u201d\\n\\nShe opened her gentle eyes languidly and managed to murmur:\\n\\n\u201cPapa.\u201d\\n\\nThat reassured me. She was not nearly dead yet. I ordered sulphur preparations, and personally brought out the croup-kettle; when Sandy or the child is ill, I do not sit idle waiting for doctors. I knew how to care for both of them, having had practice. This little creature had spent much of its brief life in my arms, and many times I had quieted its troubles and made it laugh through the tears clinging to its lashes when even its mother could not.\\n\\nSir Launcelot, clad in his finest armor, came striding down the great hall on his way to the stock-board. He was president of that board and held the Siege Perilous, which he had purchased from Sir Galahad; for the stock-board was made up of the Knights of the Round Table, who now used the Round Table for business. Seats there were worth\u2014well, the amount would never be believed, so there is no point in naming it. Sir Launcelot was a bear, had cornered one of the new lines, and was preparing to squeeze the shorts that day; but what did it matter? He remained the same old Launcelot, and when he glanced through the doorway, discovered that his pet was sick, nothing else mattered. Bulls and bears could battle as they pleased; he would come in and stand by little Hello-Central with all his strength. And so he did. He tossed his helmet into a corner, and within half a minute had fitted a fresh wick into the alcohol lamp and was heating the croup-kettle. By then Sandy had made a blanket canopy over the crib, and all was prepared.\\n\\nSir Launcelot raised steam. Together we filled the kettle with unslaked lime and carbolic acid, adding a little lactic acid, then poured in the water and pushed the steam-spout beneath the canopy. Everything was shipshape, and we sat on opposite sides of the crib to keep watch.\"}"
