          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"960bb3a2d207d219644da247\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJoseph!! Joseph!!!\u201d Thereupon he shook his head and tapped it, conveying his opinion of Joseph\u2019s shortcomings.\\n\\n\u201cBut my dear young friend,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, \u201cyou must be hungry, you must be worn out. Sit down. Here is a chicken had round from the Boar, here is a tongue had round from the Boar, and here are one or two little things had round from the Boar, which I hope you may not disdain. But do I,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, rising again the instant after sitting down, \u201csee before me him I once sported with in the happy days of infancy? And may I\u2014_may_ I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nBy this May I, he meant whether he might shake hands. I gave my consent; he did so warmly, and then sat down once more.\\n\\n\u201cHere is wine,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook. \u201cLet us drink, Thanks to Fortune, and may she always select her favourites with equal discernment! And yet I cannot,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, getting up again, \u201csee before me One\u2014and also drink to One\u2014without once more expressing\u2014May I\u2014_may_ I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nI told him he might, and he shook hands with me again, drained his glass, and turned it upside down. I did likewise; and if I had turned myself upside down before drinking, the wine could not have gone more straight to my head.\\n\\nMr. Pumblechook helped me to the liver wing and the finest slice of tongue (none of those remote No Thoroughfares of Pork now), while taking, comparatively speaking, scarcely any care of himself. \u201cAh! poultry, poultry! You little imagined,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, apostrophising the fowl in the dish, \u201cwhen you were a young fledgling, what awaited you. You little imagined you were destined to be refreshment beneath this humble roof for one who\u2014ascribe it to weakness, if you please,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, rising again, \u201cbut may I? _may_ I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nIt was becoming needless to repeat the words telling him he might, so he did it immediately. How he managed to do it so often without injuring himself on my knife, I cannot say.\\n\\n\u201cAnd your sister,\u201d he continued, after eating steadily for a little while, \u201cwho had the honour of bringing you up by hand! It is a sad picter, to think that she is no longer capable of fully appreciating the honour. May\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI saw he was preparing to approach me again, and stopped him.\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll drink her health,\u201d said I.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d cried Mr. Pumblechook, leaning back in his chair, quite limp with admiration, \u201cthat\u2019s the way you know \u2019em, sir!\u201d (I don\u2019t know who Sir was, but he certainly was not I, and there was no third person present); \u201cthat\u2019s the way you know the noble-minded, sir!\u201d\"}"
