{"request_id":"7d9ad52ea32a2261419a1316","rewritten_text":"My father was gone, and his memory had been the life of my life. I might feel gratitude toward another, but I could never again love or hope as I once had; everything was suffering, and even my pleasures were endured rather than enjoyed. I was like a solitary place among mountains, enclosed on every side by steep black precipices, where no warmth could reach and from which there was no path to sunnier fields. Thus, although the spirit of friendship soothed me for a time, it could not restore me. It came like a gentle visitation; it departed, and I scarcely felt the loss. The spirit of existence was dead within me. Do not be surprised, then, that when it came I did not welcome it more gladly, or that when it left I did not mourn more bitterly the best gift of heaven—a friend.

My friend’s name was Woodville. I will briefly tell his history, so that you may judge how cold my heart must have been not to be warmed by his eloquent words and tender sympathy; and how, since he too was deeply unhappy, we were well suited to console one another, had Misery’s Medusa head not hardened me into stone. Woodville’s misfortunes did not lie at the heart of his being, as mine did. His was a natural grief, one that would not destroy but purify the heart, and from which he might, once its shadow had passed over him, emerge brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had received a classical education. He was one of the very few whom Fortune favors from birth, upon whom she bestows every gift of mind and person with boundless generosity, and whom, under her special protection, no imperfection, however slight, or disappointment, however brief, is allowed to touch. She seemed to have formed his mind from a purity that no dross could tarnish, and his understanding was such that no error could corrupt it. His genius was transcendent, and when it rose like a bright star in the east, every eye turned toward it in admiration. He was a poet. That name has been degraded so often that it cannot convey all that he was. He was like a poet of old, crowned by the Muses in his cradle, with bees feeding upon his lips. As he moved among other men, he seemed surrounded by a heavenly halo that set him apart from them and raised him above them. It was his extraordinary beauty, the dazzling fire of his eyes, and his words, whose rich tones wrapped the listener in silent, ecstatic wonder, that made him surpass all others, so that beside him they seemed created only to serve his superior excellence."}

