          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0e09d8cd51a21fdd96fc9957\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If she had known that her sister wanted to tear her away from such hopes and realities, what would she have felt? Only her mother could have understood, for she might have felt almost the same. Lydia\u2019s trip to Brighton was the only thing that comforted her in the sad certainty that her husband never meant to go there himself.\\n\\nBut they knew nothing of what had happened, and their excitement continued, with few breaks, until the very day Lydia left home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now going to see Mr. Wickham for the last time. Since returning, she had often been in his company, and her agitation had mostly faded; her former partiality had disappeared completely. She had even learned to see, in the very gentleness that had first charmed her, an affected manner and a dull sameness that disgusted and tired her. His present behavior toward her gave her another reason for displeasure. He soon showed an inclination to renew the attentions that had marked the beginning of their acquaintance, but after everything that had happened, this could only annoy her. She no longer cared for him, seeing that he had chosen her as the object of such empty, foolish flirtation. Though she firmly discouraged him, she could not help feeling reproached by his belief that, no matter how long his attentions had been withdrawn or for what reason, renewing them at any time would flatter her vanity and win back her preference.\\n\\nOn the very last day the regiment remained in Meryton, Wickham dined at Longbourn with several other officers. Elizabeth was so unwilling to part from him on friendly terms that, when he asked how she had spent her time at Hunsford, she mentioned that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both spent three weeks at Rosings, and asked whether he knew the former.\\n\\nHe looked surprised, displeased, and alarmed; but after a moment\u2019s thought, he smiled again and replied that he had once seen Colonel Fitzwilliam often. After remarking that he was a very gentlemanly man, he asked Elizabeth how she had liked him. Her answer strongly favored the colonel. Soon afterward, with an indifferent air, he added, \u201cHow long did you say he was at Rosings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNearly three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you saw him often?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, almost every day.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis manners are very different from his cousin\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves with acquaintance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed!\u201d cried Wickham, with an expression she did not miss. \u201cAnd may I ask\u2014\u201d But stopping himself, he added in a lighter tone, \u201cIs it in his manner of speaking that he improves?\u201d\"}"
