          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2f13b124082636d7c30032a2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The two ladies were delighted to see their dear friend once more, declared that it seemed an age since they had met, and repeatedly inquired how she had occupied herself since they parted. They gave little attention to the rest of the family; avoiding Mrs. Bennet as much as they could, saying very little to Elizabeth, and nothing whatsoever to the others. They soon departed again, rising with an energy that surprised their brother, and hurrying away as though anxious to escape Mrs. Bennet\u2019s civilities.\\n\\nThe prospect of the Netherfield ball was highly agreeable to every female member of the family. Mrs. Bennet chose to regard it as a compliment to her eldest daughter, and felt especially flattered at receiving the invitation from Mr. Bingley himself rather than a formal card. Jane imagined a delightful evening in the company of her two friends and their brother\u2019s attentions; while Elizabeth looked forward with pleasure to dancing frequently with Mr. Wickham, and to seeing everything confirmed by Mr. Darcy\u2019s expression and conduct. Catherine and Lydia\u2019s expected happiness rested less upon one particular event or person; for although each of them, like Elizabeth, intended to dance half the evening with Mr. Wickham, he was certainly not the only partner capable of pleasing them, and a ball was, in any case, a ball. Even Mary could assure her family that she had no objection to attending.\\n\\n\u201cProvided I may have my mornings to myself,\u201d said she, \u201cit is sufficient. I do not think it any sacrifice to take part occasionally in evening engagements. Society makes demands upon us all; and I count myself among those who regard periods of recreation and amusement as beneficial to everyone.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth\u2019s spirits were so elevated by the occasion that, though she seldom addressed Mr. Collins without necessity, she could not refrain from asking whether he meant to accept Mr. Bingley\u2019s invitation, and, if so, whether he considered it proper to participate in the evening\u2019s entertainment. She was somewhat surprised to discover that he had no hesitation on the subject, and was far from fearing a reprimand, either from the Archbishop or Lady Catherine de Bourgh, for venturing to dance.\\n\\n\u201cI am by no means of the opinion, I assure you,\u201d said he, \u201cthat a ball of this nature, given by a young man of good character for respectable people, can have any harmful tendency; and I am so far from objecting to dancing myself that I hope to be honoured with the hands of all my fair cousins during the evening. I take this opportunity, moreover, of requesting yours, Miss Elizabeth, particularly for the first two dances; a preference which I trust my cousin Jane will ascribe to its proper cause, and not to any disrespect toward her.\u201d\"}"
