          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d2bbd19b5609ed030127e809\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIs that all? That is not much! Many people have fallen out of favor with the Republic and must look out through the little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which one is he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am he. Naturally, since I am the last.\u201d\\n\\nJarvis Lorry has answered all the earlier questions. Jarvis Lorry has climbed down and now stands with one hand on the coach door, answering a group of officials. They slowly walk around the carriage and slowly climb onto the driver\u2019s seat to inspect the small amount of luggage on the roof. The country people gathered there move closer to the coach doors and stare greedily inside. A small child, carried by its mother, stretches out its short arm to touch the wife of an aristocrat who has gone to the Guillotine.\\n\\n\u201cHere are your papers, Jarvis Lorry, properly countersigned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMay one leave, citizen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne may leave. Forward, my postilions! Have a good journey!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI salute you, citizens\u2014and the first danger is past!\u201d\\n\\nJarvis Lorry says these words again as he clasps his hands and looks upward. Terror fills the carriage; there is crying and the heavy breathing of the unconscious traveler.\\n\\n\u201cAre we not going too slowly? Can they not be made to go faster?\u201d Lucie asks, clinging to the old man.\\n\\n\u201cIt would look like flight, my dear. I must not press them too hard; that would arouse suspicion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook back, look back, and see whether we are being followed!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe road is clear, my dearest. So far, no one is following us.\u201d\\n\\nPairs and groups of houses pass by, along with lonely farms, ruined buildings, dye-works, tanneries, and similar places, then open country and rows of leafless trees. The hard, uneven road lies beneath us, with soft, deep mud on both sides. At times we turn into the mud at the edge to avoid stones that clatter against us and shake us; at other times we get stuck in its ruts and bogs. Our impatience then becomes so painful that, in our panic and haste, we want to get out and run, hide, or do anything rather than stop.\\n\\nFrom the open country we pass again among ruined buildings, lonely farms, dye-works, tanneries, and similar places, cottages in pairs and groups, and rows of leafless trees. Have these men tricked us and brought us back by another road? Is this not the same place we have passed twice? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look back, look back, and see whether we are being followed! Hush! The posting-house.\\n\\nSlowly, our four horses are taken out. Slowly, the coach stands in the little street, without horses and with no sign that it will ever move again. Slowly, the new horses appear, one by one. Slowly, the new postilions follow, sucking and braiding the lashes of their whips. Slowly, the old postilions count their money, add it incorrectly, and reach unhappy conclusions.\"}"
