{"request_id":"473f3bc1b2d768451c898453","rewritten_text":"Ye spirits of the storm, take me! ye powers of ruin, spread wide your arms and hold me forever! unless some gentler power has ordained another fate, that after long suffering I may gather my reward, and once more feel my heart beating beside the heart of one like myself.

The Tiber, that road laid down by nature’s own hand as it winds through her continent, lay beneath my feet, and many boats were moored along its banks. With a few books, provisions, and my dog, I would board one of them and drift down the river’s current into the sea; then, keeping close to shore, coast the lovely shores and sunlit headlands of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples and Calabria, and brave the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis; then, with bold purpose—for what had I left to lose?—skim the ocean toward Malta and the farther Cyclades. I would shun Constantinople, whose familiar towers and inlets belonged to a different existence from my own; I would follow the coasts of Asia Minor and Syria, and, passing the seven-mouthed Nile, turn northward once more, until, leaving forgotten Carthage and deserted Libya behind, I reached the pillars of Hercules. And then—anywhere would do—the slimy caves and silent depths of the ocean might become my home, before I completed this long voyage, or disease’s arrow struck my heart as I drifted alone upon the tossing Mediterranean; or, at some place where I landed, I might discover what I sought—a companion. Or, if that could never be—through endless years, bent and grey-haired, with youth already buried beside those I loved—the solitary wanderer would still spread his sail and grasp the tiller, still following heaven’s breezes around one headland after another, anchoring in bay after bay, continuing to plough the empty sea, leaving Europe’s green native shores behind, passing down Africa’s tawny coast, and, after weathering the fierce seas of the Cape, moor my battered skiff in a creek shaded by the spicy groves of the fragrant islands of the distant Indian Ocean.

These are fevered dreams. Yet ever since they came upon me a week ago, as I stood on the height of St. Peter’s, they have governed my imagination. I have chosen my boat and stored its meagre provisions. I have picked out a few books; the chief among them are Homer and Shakespeare—but the libraries of the world stand open to me, and at every port I can replenish my supply. I expect no change for the better; but the dull sameness of the present is unbearable."}

