          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0036f8c340218aa43fd3a55e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThey ought to concern you, Dorian. Every gentleman cares about his reputation. You do not wish people to speak of you as something foul and degraded. Naturally, you have your rank, your fortune, and all the rest of it. But rank and fortune are not everything. Believe me, I do not credit these rumours at all. At least, I cannot believe them when I look at you. Sin is something that marks itself upon a man\u2019s face. It cannot be hidden. People sometimes speak of secret vices. There are no such things. If a miserable man has a vice, it appears in the lines about his mouth, the heaviness of his eyelids, even in the shape of his hands. Someone\u2014I shall not name him, though you know whom I mean\u2014came to me last year to have his portrait painted. I had never seen him before, and at that time had heard nothing about him, though I have heard plenty since. He offered me an extravagant sum. I refused him. There was something in the form of his fingers that repelled me. I know now that my instinct about him was entirely right. His life is terrible. But you, Dorian, with your clear, radiant, innocent face and your wonderfully untroubled youth\u2014I cannot believe anything against you. And yet I see you so rarely, you never come to the studio now, and when you are away from me, hearing all these dreadful things people whisper about you, I do not know what to think. Why is it, Dorian, that a man such as the Duke of Berwick leaves a club room when you enter? Why is it that so many gentlemen in London will neither visit your house nor ask you to theirs? You were once a friend of Lord Staveley. I met him at dinner last week. Your name came up in conversation, in connection with the miniatures you have lent to the exhibition at the Dudley. Staveley curled his lip and said that, however artistic your tastes might be, you were a man no pure-minded girl should be permitted to know, and no chaste woman should sit in the same room with. I reminded him that I was your friend and asked what he meant. He told me. He said it openly, before everyone. It was dreadful! Why is your friendship so ruinous to young men? There was that unfortunate boy in the Guards who took his own life. You were his closest friend. There was Sir Henry Ashton, who had to leave England with his name disgraced.\u201d\"}"
