          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c164ffc687b8da085f22068a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It seemed to be a collection of back lanes, ditches, and small gardens, giving the impression of a rather dull retreat. Wemmick\u2019s house was a little wooden cottage surrounded by garden plots, with its upper part cut out and painted like a battery fitted with guns.\\n\\n\u201cMy own work,\u201d said Wemmick. \u201cLooks good, doesn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nI praised it highly. I think it was the smallest house I had ever seen, with the strangest Gothic windows\u2014most of them sham\u2014and a Gothic door almost too small to enter.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s a real flagstaff, you see,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cand on Sundays I raise a real flag. Now look here. After I\u2019ve crossed this bridge, I hoist it up\u2014like this\u2014and cut off communication.\u201d\\n\\nThe bridge was a plank crossing a chasm about four feet wide and two feet deep. But it was very pleasant to see the pride with which he raised it and secured it, smiling as he did so with genuine enjoyment, not merely out of habit.\\n\\n\u201cAt nine o\u2019clock every night, Greenwich time,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cthe gun fires. There he is, you see! And when you hear him go, I think you\u2019ll say he\u2019s a Stinger.\u201d\\n\\nThe cannon he referred to stood in a separate fortress made of latticework. An ingenious little tarpaulin arrangement, shaped rather like an umbrella, protected it from the weather.\\n\\n\u201cThen, at the back,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cout of sight, so it doesn\u2019t interfere with the idea of fortifications\u2014for it\u2019s a principle of mine that if you have an idea, you should carry it out and maintain it\u2014I don\u2019t know whether you agree\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI said I certainly did.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014At the back, there\u2019s a pig, and there are chickens and rabbits. Then I put together my own little frame, you see, and grow cucumbers; and at supper you\u2019ll judge what sort of salad I can produce. So, sir,\u201d said Wemmick, smiling again, though seriously too, as he shook his head, \u201cif you can imagine the little place under siege, it would hold out for a devil of a long time as far as provisions are concerned.\u201d\\n\\nThen he led me to a bower about a dozen yards away. It was reached by such cleverly twisting paths that it took quite a long time to get there, and in this retreat our glasses had already been set out. Our punch was cooling in an ornamental lake beside which the bower stood. The water, with an island in the middle that might have supplied the supper salad, was circular, and he had built a fountain in it. When you set a small mill in motion and removed a cork from a pipe, it played with such force that it made the back of your hand quite wet.\"}"
