          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a663d62555451bda10805d9b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be car-ri-ed toe the skies, on flow\u2019ry _beds_ of ease,\\n\\nWhilst others fight to win the prize, and sail thro\u2019 _blood_ -y seas?\\n\\nHe was considered a marvelous reader. At church \u201csociables\u201d he was invariably asked to read poetry; and when he had finished, the ladies would raise their hands and drop them helplessly into their laps, and \u201cwall\u201d their eyes, and shake their heads, as if to say, \u201cWords cannot express it; it is too beautiful, TOO beautiful for this mortal earth.\u201d\\n\\nAfter the hymn was sung, the Rev. Mr. Sprague transformed himself into a bulletin-board and read aloud \u201cnotices\u201d of meetings, societies, and other matters until it appeared the catalogue might continue to the crack of doom\u2014a strange custom still maintained in America, even in cities, in this age of plentiful newspapers. Often, the less there is to support a traditional custom, the more difficult it becomes to abandon it.\\n\\nThen the minister prayed. It was a good, liberal prayer, and entered into particulars: it pleaded for the church, and the church\u2019s little children; for the village\u2019s other churches; for the village itself; for the county; for the State; for the State officers; for the United States; for the churches of the United States; for Congress; for the President; for the Government\u2019s officers; for poor sailors tossed upon stormy seas; for the oppressed millions groaning beneath European monarchies and Oriental despotisms; for those who possess the light and good tidings, yet have neither eyes to see nor ears to hear; for the heathen on the distant islands of the sea; and ended by asking that the words he was about to utter might find grace and favor, and prove like seed sown in fertile soil, producing in due season a thankful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nThere was a rustling of dresses, and the congregation, which had been standing, sat down. The boy whose story this book tells did not enjoy the prayer; he merely endured it\u2014if he even did that. He was restless throughout; he unconsciously kept count of the prayer\u2019s details\u2014for he was not listening, but knew the territory well, and the clergyman\u2019s customary path across it\u2014and whenever some small piece of new material was inserted, his ear caught it and his whole nature rebelled; he regarded additions as unfair, and scoundrelly. During the prayer a fly had settled on the back of the pew before him and tormented his spirit by calmly rubbing its hands together, clasping its head with its arms, and polishing it so vigorously that it seemed almost to separate from the body, leaving a slender thread of neck exposed; scraping its wings with its hind legs and smoothing them against its body as though they were coat-tails; performing its entire toilet as peacefully as if it knew itself perfectly secure.\"}"
