          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d2eb2a682410749716cc966b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was at Warwick Castle that I encountered the curious stranger I am about to describe. Three things drew me to him: his frank simplicity, his astonishing knowledge of ancient armor, and the ease of his company\u2014for he did all the talking. We fell in together, as quiet people do, at the rear of the group being conducted through, and he immediately began saying things that interested me. As he continued, softly, pleasantly, and smoothly, he seemed gradually to drift out of the present world and age into some distant era and forgotten land; and little by little he cast such a spell over me that I appeared to wander among the ghosts, shadows, dust, and mildew of a dim antiquity, conversing with one of its relics! Just as I might speak of my closest friends or enemies, or my most familiar neighbors, he spoke of Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Launcelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and all the other famous names of the Round Table\u2014and how ancient, impossibly ancient, faded, dry, musty, and old he came to seem as he went on! Presently he turned to me and said, in the same manner one might mention the weather or any ordinary subject\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou know about transmigration of souls; do you know about transposition of epochs\u2014and bodies?\u201d\\n\\nI said I had never heard of it. He was so unconcerned\u2014just as when people discuss the weather\u2014that he did not seem to notice whether I answered him or not. There was scarcely half a moment of silence before the salaried guide\u2019s droning voice broke in:\\n\\n\u201cAncient hauberk, date of the sixth century, time of King Arthur and the Round Table; said to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramor le Desirous; observe the round hole through the chain-mail in the left breast; cannot be explained; supposed to have been made by a bullet after firearms were invented\u2014perhaps maliciously by Cromwell\u2019s soldiers