{"request_id":"5d03457a9251546e1089d253","rewritten_text":"It is only four o’clock, but it is winter, and the sun has already set. There are no clouds in the clear, frosty sky to catch its slanting rays, yet the air itself is faintly rose-colored, and that tint is reflected once more on the snow covering the ground. I live in a lonely cottage on a broad, deserted heath; no sound of life reaches me. I see the bleak plain spread white, except for a few black patches where the midday sun has melted the snow from the tops of those sharply pointed hillocks. As it fell, the snow slid down them, leaving it thinner there than on the level ground. A few birds peck at the hard ice covering the pools, for the frost has lasted a long time.

I am in a strange state of mind. I am alone—entirely alone—in the world. Misfortune has passed over me like a blight and withered me; I know that I am about to die, and I feel happy—joyful. I feel my pulse; it beats quickly. I place my thin hand against my cheek; it burns. There is a slight, restless spirit within me, now giving off its final sparks. I shall never see the snows of another winter—I truly believe I shall never again feel the life-giving warmth of another summer sun—and with this conviction I begin to write my tragic history. Perhaps a story like mine should die with me, but a feeling I cannot define draws me onward, and I am too weak in body and mind to resist the smallest impulse. While life was still strong in me, I believed there was a sacred horror in my tale that made it unfit to be spoken; now, as I am about to die, I profane its mysterious terrors. It is like the wood of the Eumenides, which none but the dying may enter; and Oedipus is about to die.

What am I writing? I must gather my thoughts. I do not know whether anyone will read these pages except you, my friend, who will receive them after my death. I do not address them only to you because it will give me pleasure to dwell on our friendship in a way that would be unnecessary if you alone were to read what I write. I shall therefore tell my story as though I were writing for strangers. You have often asked what caused my solitary life, my tears, and above all my impenetrable, unkind silence. In life I dared not reveal it; in death I uncover the mystery. Others will skim these pages carelessly; to you, Woodville, kind and affectionate friend, they will be dear—the precious memorials of a brokenhearted girl who, even while dying, is still warmed by gratitude toward you. Your tears will fall upon the words that record my misfortunes; I know they will—and while I live, I thank you for your sympathy."}

