          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dab2871fbbaffac2f0f8b7c3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThe stupor and astonishment caused in my mind by this extraordinary change in the situation was perhaps, after all, the part of the adventure least open to explanation. For the upheaval itself was not only natural and inevitable, but had long been anticipated as something to expect when I reached that precise point in my voyage at which the planet\u2019s attraction would be replaced by that of the satellite\u2014or, more exactly, when the balloon\u2019s gravity toward the earth would become weaker than its gravity toward the moon. Certainly, I had awakened from a deep sleep, all my senses confused, to witness a most startling phenomenon, one that I had expected, though not at that moment. The reversal itself must, of course, have occurred smoothly and gradually, and it is far from certain that, even had I been awake when it happened, I would have detected it through any inward sign of inversion\u2014that is, through any discomfort or disturbance either in my person or in my equipment.\\n\\n\u201cIt is scarcely necessary to say that, once I fully understood my situation and emerged from the terror that had overwhelmed every faculty of my soul, my attention was first directed entirely toward observing the moon\u2019s general physical appearance. It lay beneath me like a map\u2014and although I judged it still to be at a considerable distance, the markings of its surface appeared with a striking and wholly inexplicable clarity. The complete absence of ocean or sea, and indeed of any lake, river, or body of water whatsoever, struck me at first sight as the most extraordinary feature of its geological condition. Yet, strangely, I saw vast level regions of a distinctly alluvial character, while by far the greater part of the visible hemisphere was covered with countless volcanic mountains, conical in form and looking more artificial than naturally formed. The highest of them rises no more than three and three-quarter miles vertically; but a map of the volcanic districts of the Campi Phlegraei would give Your Excellencies a better impression of their general surface than any inadequate description I might attempt. Most of them were plainly erupting, and they made me understand their fury and power with dreadful clarity through the repeated thunder of the so-called meteoric stones, which now shot upward past the balloon with increasingly terrifying frequency.\u201d\"}"
