          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3a20306e1903cb46fab7e6f2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The party was arranged on two adjoining benches: Anne sat among those in front, and Mr Elliot had contrived so successfully, aided by his friend Colonel Wallis, to secure a place beside her. Miss Elliot, surrounded by her cousins and receiving the chief of Colonel Wallis\u2019s attentions, was perfectly satisfied.\\n\\nAnne\u2019s mind was in an especially favourable condition for the evening\u2019s entertainment; it provided just enough occupation. She had feeling for the tender, spirits for the lively, attention for the scientific, and patience for the tedious; and she had never enjoyed a concert more, at least during the first act. Near its conclusion, in the interval after an Italian song, she explained the song\u2019s words to Mr Elliot. A concert programme lay between them.\\n\\n\u201cThis,\u201d said she, \u201cis almost the sense, or rather the meaning of the words\u2014for certainly the sense of an Italian love-song must not be discussed\u2014but it is as close to the meaning as I can make it; for I do not claim to understand the language. I am a very poor Italian scholar.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, I see that you are. I see you know nothing about it. You possess only enough knowledge of the language to translate at sight these inverted, transposed, shortened Italian lines into plain, intelligible, graceful English. You need say no more of your ignorance. Here is decisive evidence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI will not resist such courteous kindness; but I should be sorry to be tested by a genuine proficient.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not had the pleasure of visiting Camden Place for so long,\u201d he replied, \u201cwithout learning something of Miss Anne Elliot; and I consider her a person too modest for the world generally to know half her accomplishments, and too accomplished for modesty to be natural in any other woman.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor shame! For shame! This is excessive flattery. I forget what comes next,\u201d she said, turning to the programme.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps,\u201d said Mr Elliot in a low voice, \u201cI have known your character longer than you suppose.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed! How can that be? You could have known it only since I came to Bath, unless you heard me spoken of beforehand by my own family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI knew you by reputation long before you came to Bath. I had heard you described by people who knew you intimately. I have known you by character for many years. Your appearance, your disposition, accomplishments, and manner\u2014all were familiar to me.\u201d\\n\\nMr Elliot was not disappointed in the interest he had hoped to awaken. No one can resist the attraction of such a mystery.\"}"
