          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"95f9ff6d4a42f604915172ba\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This little explanation with Mr. Knightley gave Emma great pleasure. It became one of the pleasant memories of the ball, which she enjoyed as she walked around the lawn the next morning. She was very glad that they now understood each other so well about the Eltons, and that their opinions of both husband and wife were so similar. His praise of Harriet, and his admission that she had been right, pleased her especially. The Eltons\u2019 rudeness, which for a few minutes had threatened to spoil the rest of her evening, had instead brought her some of its greatest pleasures; and she looked forward to another happy result\u2014the end of Harriet\u2019s infatuation. From the way Harriet had spoken of it before they left the ballroom, Emma had strong hopes. It seemed that Harriet\u2019s eyes had suddenly been opened, allowing her to see that Mr. Elton was not the superior person she had believed him to be. The fever was over, and Emma had little fear that harmful politeness would make it return. She trusted the Eltons\u2019 bad feelings to provide all the pointed neglect that might still be needed. Harriet sensible, Frank Churchill not too deeply in love, and Mr. Knightley unwilling to quarrel with her\u2014how happy a summer lay ahead of her!\\n\\nShe was not going to see Frank Churchill that morning. He had told her that he could not give himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, since he had to be home by midday. She did not mind.\\n\\nHaving considered all these matters, examined them carefully, and set them right in her mind, she was turning toward the house, her spirits renewed for the demands of the two little boys and their grandfather, when the large iron gate opened. Two people entered whom she had never expected to see together: Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning on his arm\u2014Harriet herself! One moment was enough to show her that something unusual had happened. Harriet looked pale and frightened, while he tried to comfort her. The iron gate and the front door were less than twenty yards apart; soon all three were in the hall, and Harriet immediately sank into a chair and fainted.\\n\\nA young lady who faints must be brought round; questions must be answered, and surprises explained. Such events are very interesting, but the suspense cannot last long. Within a few minutes Emma knew the whole story.\\n\\nMiss Smith and Miss Bickerton, another parlour boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s who had also attended the ball, had gone out walking together and taken the Richmond road. Though it seemed public enough to be safe, the road had frightened them. About half a mile beyond Highbury, it made a sudden turn and became deeply shaded by elms on both sides. For quite a distance it was very secluded; and when the young ladies had gone some way along it, they suddenly saw, a short distance ahead, on a wider patch of grass beside the road, a group of gypsies.\"}"
