          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b921ac61028f7c45741c6731\",\"rewritten_text\":\"If not, and you rely upon my forbearance and the mercy of those you have grievously wronged, sit down silently in that chair. It has been waiting for you these two entire days.\u201d\\n\\nMonks muttered several indistinct words, but still remained wavering.\\n\\n\u201cYou will act promptly,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow. \u201cOne word from me, and the alternative will be lost forever.\u201d\\n\\nThe man continued to hesitate.\\n\\n\u201cI have no wish to bargain with you,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, \u201cand, since I am defending the dearest interests of others, I have no right to do so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs there\u2014\u201d Monks asked in an unsteady voice\u2014\u201cis there\u2014no course between the two?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNone.\u201d\\n\\nMonks glanced anxiously at the old gentleman; but, seeing in his face only sternness and resolve, entered the room, shrugged his shoulders, and sat down.\\n\\n\u201cLock the door from outside,\u201d Mr. Brownlow told the attendants, \u201cand return when I ring.\u201d\\n\\nThe men complied, leaving the two alone together.\\n\\n\u201cThis is fine treatment, sir,\u201d said Monks, tossing aside his hat and cloak, \u201cfrom my father\u2019s oldest friend.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is precisely because I was your father\u2019s oldest friend, young man,\u201d replied Mr. Brownlow; \u201cbecause the hopes and desires of youthful, happy years were joined with him, and with that lovely creature of his blood and family who returned to her God in youth, leaving me here a solitary and lonely man; because, while still a boy, he knelt beside me at the death-bed of his only sister, on the morning that would\u2014but Heaven decreed otherwise\u2014have made her my young wife; because my scarred heart remained attached to him from that day onward, through all his trials and mistakes, until his death; because old memories and associations filled my heart, and even looking upon you revives old thoughts of him; it is for all these reasons that I am moved to deal gently with you now\u2014yes, Edward Leeford, even now\u2014and to blush for your unworthiness as the bearer of his name.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat has the name to do with this?\u201d asked the other, after watching, partly in silence and partly with stubborn wonder, his companion\u2019s agitation. \u201cWhat is the name to me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing,\u201d answered Mr. Brownlow, \u201cnothing to you. But it was hers, and even after all these years it brings back to me, an old man, the warmth and thrill I once felt merely hearing it spoken by a stranger. I am very glad you have changed it\u2014very\u2014very.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is all very grand,\u201d said Monks (to preserve his assumed name) after a long silence, during which he had jerked himself sullenly back and forth in defiance, while Mr. Brownlow sat with his face shaded by one hand.\"}"
