          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e9c38697c032a53e0b89e18f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat is it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean? Ten at night?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir. Your honour ordered me to wake you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I recollect. Very well, very well.\u201d\\n\\nAfter several heavy attempts to fall asleep again, which the man skilfully defeated by keeping the fire stirred for five minutes, he rose, put on his hat, and went out. He entered the Temple, and, having refreshed himself by walking twice along King\u2019s Bench-walk and Paper-buildings, turned into the Stryver chambers.\\n\\nThe Stryver clerk, who never took part in these meetings, had gone home, and Stryver himself opened the door. He wore slippers and a loose bed-gown, with his throat uncovered for comfort. Around his eyes was that somewhat wild, strained, weathered look seen in all such free livers, from the portrait of Jeffries onward, and traceable, in different artistic disguises, through the portraits of every Drinking Age.\\n\\n\u201cYou are somewhat late, Memory,\u201d said Stryver.\\n\\n\u201cAbout the ordinary hour; perhaps a quarter of an hour afterward.\u201d\\n\\nThey entered a shabby room, its walls covered with books and its floor strewn with papers, where a bright fire burned. A kettle steamed on the hob, while amid the paper chaos a table gleamed, abundantly supplied with wine, brandy, rum, sugar, and lemons.\\n\\n\u201cYou have had your bottle, I see, Sydney.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTwo tonight, I believe. I dined with the day\u2019s client\u2014or watched him dine; it comes to the same thing!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was an excellent point, Sydney, which you used in establishing the identification. How did you discover it? When did it occur to you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought he was a fairly handsome man, and that I should have been much the same kind of man if fortune had favoured me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Stryver laughed until his prematurely developed paunch shook.\\n\\n\u201cYou and your fortune, Sydney! Set to work, set to work.\u201d\\n\\nThe jackal, in a sufficiently sullen mood, loosened his clothing, went into the next room, and returned with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and a couple of towels. Dipping the towels in the water and partly wringing them, he arranged them on his head in a dreadful fashion, sat at the table, and said, \u201cNow I am ready!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot much reducing to do tonight, Memory,\u201d said Mr. Stryver cheerfully, as he searched through his papers.\\n\\n\u201cHow much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly two batches.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere they are, Sydney. Begin!\u201d\\n\\nThe lion then settled himself on his back upon a sofa at one side of the drinking-table, while the jackal sat at his own proper table, covered with papers, on the opposite side, the bottles and glasses conveniently within reach.\"}"
