          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"aed9a93456abcc415b74590c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He shivered and went back, closing the window behind him.\\n\\nWhen he reached the door, he turned the key and opened it. He did not even look at the murdered man. He felt that the only way to deal with the whole thing was not to face what had happened. The friend who had painted the fatal portrait, the cause of all his misery, was gone from his life. That was enough.\\n\\nThen he remembered the lamp. It was a curious Moorish piece, made of dull silver inlaid with polished steel arabesques and set with rough turquoise stones. His servant might notice it was missing, and questions might follow. He hesitated, then went back and took it from the table. He could not avoid seeing the dead body. How still it was! How horribly white the long hands looked! It resembled a terrible wax figure.\\n\\nAfter locking the door behind him, he crept quietly downstairs. The woodwork creaked and seemed to cry out in pain. Several times he stopped and waited. No: everything was silent. It was only the sound of his own footsteps.\\n\\nWhen he reached the library, he saw the bag and coat in the corner. They had to be hidden somewhere. He unlocked a secret cupboard in the wood paneling, where he kept his strange disguises, and put them inside. He could burn them later without difficulty. Then he took out his watch. It was twenty minutes to two.\\n\\nHe sat down and began to think. Every year\u2014almost every month\u2014men were hanged in England for what he had done. There had been a murderous madness in the air. Some red star had come too near the earth.... And yet, what proof was there against him? Basil Hallward had left the house at eleven. No one had seen him return. Most of the servants were at Selby Royal. His valet had gone to bed.... Paris! Yes. Basil had gone to Paris, on the midnight train as he had planned. Because of his secretive habits, it would be months before anyone became suspicious. Months! Everything could be destroyed long before then.\\n\\nA sudden idea came to him. He put on his fur coat and hat and went into the hall. There he stopped, hearing the policeman\u2019s slow, heavy footsteps on the pavement outside and seeing the flash of the bull\u2019s-eye reflected in the window. He waited and held his breath.\\n\\nAfter a few moments, he lifted the latch and slipped out, closing the door very gently behind him. Then he began ringing the bell.\"}"
