          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e3aa5f10dd8f4cc3a3d812f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNext, we built a wire fence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA wire fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. You suggested it yourself two or three years ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I remember\u2014the first time the Church tested its strength against us, then decided it would be wiser to wait for a more hopeful season. Well, how did you arrange the fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI ran twelve extremely strong bare wires\u2014not insulated\u2014from a large dynamo in the cave. The dynamo has only two brushes, one positive and one negative.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, that\u2019s right.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe wires leave the cave and enclose a circle of level ground a hundred yards across. They form twelve separate fences, ten feet apart\u2014that is, twelve circles inside one another\u2014and their ends return to the cave.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRight. Go on.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fences are attached to heavy oak posts set only three feet apart, and the posts are buried five feet deep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is strong enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. Outside the cave, the wires have no connection to the ground. They leave from the dynamo\u2019s positive brush; the negative brush is grounded. The other ends return to the cave, where each is grounded separately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, no, that won\u2019t do!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy not?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt wastes power and costs too much. You need no ground connection except the one through the negative brush. The other end of every wire must return to the cave and be fastened separately, but without being grounded. Now consider how much that saves. A cavalry charge rushes at the fence. You use no power and spend no money, because there is only one ground connection until the horses strike the wire. The instant they touch it, they create a connection with the negative brush through the ground and fall dead. Don\u2019t you see? You use no energy until it is needed. The lightning is waiting there, ready, like a bullet in a gun, but it costs nothing until you fire it. Yes, the single ground connection\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course! I don\u2019t know how I missed that. It is not only cheaper, but more effective, because if wires break or become tangled, nothing happens.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, especially if we have a warning device in the cave and disconnect any broken wire. Well, go on. The Gatlings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes\u2014that is arranged. In the center of the inner circle, on a broad platform six feet high, I have placed a battery of thirteen Gatling guns and supplied plenty of ammunition.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s it. They cover every approach, and when the Church\u2019s knights arrive, there will be music. The edge of the precipice above the cave\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ve put a wire fence there, along with a Gatling.\u201d\"}"
