          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"517a535fbf9da1b8b6788b57\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, it is\u2014and _I_ could have had him if I\u2019d been big enough; I saw him _first_. Who captured him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt was an old man\u2014a stranger\u2014and he sold his claim on him for forty dollars because he has to go up the river and can\u2019t wait. Just think of that! You bet _I\u2019d_ wait, even if it took seven years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s me, every time,\u201d I said. \u201cBut maybe his claim isn\u2019t worth more than that, if he\u2019ll sell it so cheaply. Maybe something isn\u2019t right about it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it _is_ right\u2014straight as a string. I saw the handbill myself. It tells everything about him exactly\u2014describes him like a picture and names the plantation he came from, below New Orleans. No, sir, there\u2019s no trouble with that deal, you bet. Say, give me a chew of tobacco, will you?\u201d\\n\\nI didn\u2019t have any, so he left. I went to the raft and sat down in the wigwam to think. But I couldn\u2019t figure anything out. I thought until my head hurt, but I couldn\u2019t see any way out of the trouble. After that long journey, and after everything we had done for those scoundrels, it had all come to nothing. Everything was ruined because they had the heart to play such a trick on Jim, making him a slave again for the rest of his life, among strangers too, for forty dirty dollars.\\n\\nAt one point I told myself it would be a thousand times better for Jim to be a slave at home with his family, if he had to be a slave, and that I should write Tom Sawyer and tell him to inform Miss Watson where Jim was. But I soon abandoned that idea for two reasons: she would be angry and disgusted by his wickedness and ingratitude for leaving her, and would sell him straight down the river again; and even if she didn\u2019t, everyone naturally despises an ungrateful Black man, and they would make Jim feel it constantly, leaving him ashamed and miserable. And then, what about _me_? Word would spread that Huck Finn had helped a Black man escape; and if I ever saw anyone from that town again, I would be so ashamed I\u2019d be ready to kneel down and kiss his boots. That\u2019s how it is: a person does something low and then doesn\u2019t want to face the consequences. He thinks that as long as he can hide it, it isn\u2019t disgraceful. That was exactly my situation. The more I thought about it, the more my conscience tormented me, and the more wicked, low, and miserable I felt.\"}"
        }
