          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9916b26ecac056923db184b9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, burglar\u2019s tools. That seems to relieve you considerably. But what made you react that way? What did you expect we had found?\u201d\\n\\nHuck was in a tight spot\u2014the questioning eyes were fixed on him. He would have given anything for material for a believable answer, but nothing came to him. The eyes seemed to bore deeper and deeper. A senseless reply suggested itself; there was no time to consider it, so he blurted it out on a chance, weakly:\\n\\n\u201cSunday-school books, maybe.\u201d\\n\\nPoor Huck was too troubled to smile, but the old man laughed loudly and happily, shaking every part of his body from head to foot. He ended by saying that such laughter was money in a man\u2019s pocket because it reduced the doctor\u2019s bill enormously. Then he added:\\n\\n\u201cPoor old fellow, you\u2019re pale and exhausted\u2014you\u2019re not well at all. No wonder you\u2019re a little confused and unsteady. But you\u2019ll recover. Rest and sleep will set you right, I hope.\u201d\\n\\nHuck was annoyed that he had acted like such a fool and shown such suspicious excitement. He had abandoned the idea that the parcel brought from the tavern was the treasure as soon as he heard the conversation at the widow\u2019s stile. He had only thought it was not the treasure, though\u2014he had not known for certain that it wasn\u2019t\u2014and the suggestion that a bundle had been captured was too much for his self-control. Still, he was glad the little incident had occurred, because now he knew beyond any doubt that that bundle was not the bundle, and his mind was peaceful and completely at ease. Indeed, everything now seemed to be moving in exactly the right direction. The treasure must still be in No. 2; the men would be captured and jailed that day, and he and Tom could take the gold that night without trouble or fear of interruption.\\n\\nJust as breakfast ended, someone knocked at the door. Huck leaped toward a hiding place, since he had no desire to be connected, even indirectly, with the night\u2019s events. The Welshman admitted several ladies and gentlemen, including the Widow Douglas, and noticed groups of citizens climbing the hill to stare at the stile. So the news had spread. The Welshman had to recount the night\u2019s story to the visitors. The widow openly expressed her gratitude for being saved.\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t mention it, madam. There is someone to whom you may owe more than you do to me and my boys, but he won\u2019t let me reveal his name. We would not have been there without him.\u201d\"}"
