          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d088be6bf3dab0e3a2cc93d2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months passed, and winter was well underway. I had been going to school most of the time and could spell, read, and write a little. I could recite the multiplication table as far as six times seven is thirty-five, and I don\u2019t suppose I could ever get beyond that, even if I lived forever. I don\u2019t put much faith in mathematics, anyway.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I got so I could tolerate it. Whenever I became especially tired, I played hooky, and the beating I received the next day did me good and lifted my spirits. So the longer I attended school, the easier it became. I was also getting somewhat used to the widow\u2019s ways, and they didn\u2019t grate on me so much. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed weighed on me pretty heavily most of the time, but before the cold weather came, I used to slip away and sleep in the woods sometimes, and that gave me a rest. I preferred the old ways, but I was beginning to like the new ones a little, too. The widow said I was improving slowly but surely and doing very well. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning I happened to knock over the saltcellar at breakfast. I reached for some salt as quickly as I could to throw over my left shoulder and ward off bad luck, but Miss Watson got there first and stopped me. She said, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; what a mess you\u2019re always making!\u201d The widow spoke up for me, but I knew that wasn\u2019t going to keep the bad luck away. After breakfast I went out feeling anxious and unsteady, wondering where it would strike me and what form it would take. There are ways to ward off some kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of them; so I didn\u2019t try to do anything, but simply trudged along, discouraged and watchful.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile in the high board fence. There was an inch of fresh snow on the ground, and I saw someone\u2019s tracks. They had come up from the quarry, lingered around the stile for a while, and then continued along the garden fence. It was strange that they hadn\u2019t come in after standing there so long. I couldn\u2019t understand it. Somehow, it was very curious. I was about to follow the tracks around, but first I bent down to examine them.\"}"
