          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dfcc3c00abd3dec689647035\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On my return, I found this letter from my father:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\\n\\n\u201cYou have likely been waiting impatiently for a letter settling the date of your return to us; at first I was inclined to write only a few lines, simply naming the day on which I expected you. But that would be a cruel kindness, and I dare not do so. What would your astonishment be, my son, when, expecting a joyful and happy welcome, you were instead met by tears and misery? And how, Victor, can I tell you of our misfortune? Absence cannot have made you indifferent to our joys and sorrows; and how can I bring pain to my son, so long absent? I wish to prepare you for the dreadful news, but I know it cannot be done; even now your eyes hurry across the page, searching for the words that will bring you the terrible tidings.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam is dead!\u2014that beloved child, whose smiles delighted and warmed my heart, who was so gentle, yet so cheerful! Victor, he has been murdered!\\n\\n\u201cI will not try to comfort you; I shall merely relate the circumstances of the event.\\n\\n\u201cLast Thursday (May 7th), my niece, your two brothers, and I went for a walk in Plainpalais. The evening was warm and calm, and we extended our walk farther than usual. It was already dusk before we considered returning; then we discovered that William and Ernest, who had gone ahead, were nowhere to be seen. We therefore sat on a bench until they should come back. Soon Ernest arrived and asked whether we had seen his brother. He said he had been playing with him, that William had run off to hide, and that he had searched for him in vain, then waited a long while, but William had not returned.\\n\\n\u201cThis account caused us some alarm, and we went on looking for him until night came, when Elizabeth supposed that he might have gone back to the house. He was not there. We returned once more with torches; I could not rest, thinking that my dear boy might be lost and exposed to the night\u2019s damp and dew; Elizabeth too endured the greatest anguish. At about five in the morning I found my lovely boy, whom I had seen the previous evening blooming with health and activity, lying on the grass, pale and motionless; the mark of the murderer\u2019s fingers was upon his neck.\\n\\n\u201cHe was carried home, and the grief plainly visible in my face revealed the secret to Elizabeth. She was most anxious to see the body. At first I tried to stop her, but she persisted, and entering the room where it lay, quickly examined the victim\u2019s neck, then clasped her hands and cried, \u2018O God!\"}"
