          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e53e0a44ee47d0e47129f510\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They was bringing along a very fine-looking old gentleman, and a good-looking younger one with his right arm in a sling. And, my souls, how the people yelled and laughed, and kept it going. But I couldn\u2019t see any joke in it, and I reckoned it would take some straining for the duke and king to find one either. I figured they\u2019d turn pale. But no, not a shade did they turn. The duke never let on that he suspected anything, but went goo-gooing about, cheerful and contented, like a jug gurgling out buttermilk; and the king just stared sorrowfully down at the newcomers, as if the thought that such frauds and rascals could exist in the world gave him a stomach-ache clear to the heart. Oh, he played it admirable. A lot of the principal people gathered around the king, to show him they were on his side. The old gentleman who had just arrived looked completely bewildered. Pretty soon he began to speak, and I saw at once that he pronounced like an Englishman\u2014not in the king\u2019s fashion, though the king\u2019s imitation was pretty good. I can\u2019t give the old gentleman\u2019s exact words, nor imitate him; but he turned to the crowd and said, something like this:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a surprise I wasn\u2019t expecting; and I\u2019ll admit, plain and frank, that I\u2019m not very well prepared to meet it or answer it; for my brother and I have had misfortunes. He has broken his arm, and our baggage was put off at a town above here last night, during the night, by mistake. I am Peter Wilks\u2019s brother Harvey, and this is his brother William, who can neither hear nor speak\u2014and can\u2019t even make signs to much purpose, now that he has only one hand to use. We are who we claim to be; and in a day or two, when I get the baggage, I can prove it. But until then I won\u2019t say anything more, but will go to the hotel and wait.\u201d\\n\\nSo he and the new dummy started off; and the king laughed and blustered out:\\n\\n\u201cBroke his arm\u2014_very_ likely, _isn\u2019t_ it?\u2014and very convenient, too, for a fraud who has to make signs and hasn\u2019t learned how. Lost their baggage! That\u2019s _mighty_ good!\u2014and mighty clever\u2014under the _circumstances!_\u201d\\n\\nThen he laughed again, and everybody else did too, except three or four, or maybe half a dozen. One was the doctor; another was a sharp-looking gentleman with an old-fashioned carpetbag made of carpet material, who had just come off the steamboat and was speaking to him in a low voice, glancing toward the king now and then and nodding their heads\u2014it was Levi Bell, the lawyer who had gone up to Louisville; and another was a big, rough, powerful fellow who had come along and listened to everything the old gentleman said, and was listening to the king now.\"}"
