e presence of the magistrates. I say we went, but Pumblechook pushed me along as if I had just picked a pocket or set fire to a haystack. Indeed, everyone in the court seemed to think he had caught me red-handed. As Pumblechook shoved me through the crowd, I heard some people ask, \u201cWhat has he done?\u201d and others say, \u201cHe\u2019s young, too, but he looks bad, doesn\u2019t he?\u201d One mild-looking, kindly person even gave me a religious tract decorated with a woodcut of a wicked young man covered in a complete sausage-shop of chains. It was titled TO BE READ IN MY CELL.\\n\\nThe Hall seemed a strange place to me, with pews higher than those in a church, people leaning over them to watch, and powerful Justices\u2014one with a powdered head\u2014sitting back in chairs with folded arms, taking snuff, falling asleep, writing, or reading newspapers. On the walls hung several shiny black portraits, which my untrained eye saw as a mixture of hard toffee and sticking-plaster.\"}"
