          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"61c817f4aa6cfb5add7f41ea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShall I ask them? Or Miss Bates? She is nearer still.\u2014And I am not sure that Miss Bates may not understand the inclinations of the other people as well as anyone. I think we do need a larger council. Suppose I go and invite Miss Bates to join us?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014if you please,\u201d said Mrs. Weston, somewhat hesitantly, \u201cif you believe she will be of any assistance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will get nothing useful from Miss Bates,\u201d said Emma. \u201cShe will be full of delight and gratitude, but she will tell you nothing. She will not even attend to your questions. I see no benefit in consulting Miss Bates.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut she is so entertaining, so exceedingly entertaining! I am very fond of listening to Miss Bates talk. And I need not bring the entire family, you know.\u201d\\n\\nAt this point Mr. Weston joined them, and, hearing what was suggested, gave it his firm approval.\\n\\n\u201cAye, do, Frank.\u2014Go and fetch Miss Bates, and let us settle the matter at once. She will enjoy the plan, I am certain; and I know no more suitable person to show us how to overcome difficulties. Fetch Miss Bates. We are becoming a little too particular. She is a constant example of how to be happy. But fetch them both. Invite them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBoth, sir! Can the old lady\u2014?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe old lady! No, the young lady, of course. I shall consider you a great blockhead, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I beg your pardon, sir. I did not remember at once. Certainly, if you wish it, I will try to persuade them both.\u201d And off he ran.\\n\\nLong before he returned, bringing the short, neat, brisk-moving aunt and her elegant niece, Mrs. Weston, like a good wife and sweet-tempered woman, had examined the passage once more and found its difficulties far less than she had first imagined\u2014indeed, very slight; and with that, the question of decision was ended. Everything else, at least in theory, went perfectly smoothly. All the lesser arrangements of tables and chairs, lights and music, tea and supper, arranged themselves; or were left as trifles to be settled later between Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes.\u2014Everyone invited was certainly to attend; Frank had already written to Enscombe, suggesting that he remain a few days beyond his fortnight, which could not possibly be denied. And it promised to be a delightful dance.\\n\\nWhen Miss Bates arrived, she most heartily agreed that it must be so. As a counsellor she was unnecessary; but as an approver, a much safer role, she was entirely welcome. Her approval, at once broad and particular, warm and unceasing, could not fail to please; and for another half-hour they all walked back and forth between the various rooms, some proposing, some listening, and everyone happily enjoying the prospect.\"}"
