{"request_id":"a898495571a0facb6ad7631e","rewritten_text":"We were raised together, with less than a year between us in age. I need hardly say that we knew nothing of division or quarrel. Harmony was the soul of our companionship, and the differences and contrasts in our characters brought us closer. Elizabeth had a calmer, more focused nature; but despite all my ardor, I could apply myself more intensely and was more deeply consumed by a thirst for knowledge. She occupied herself with following the poets’ airy creations; and in the majestic, wondrous scenes surrounding our Swiss home—the sublime forms of the mountains, the changing seasons, storm and calm, winter’s silence, and the vitality and turbulence of our Alpine summers—she found abundant reason for admiration and delight. While my companion regarded the magnificent appearances of the world with a serious and contented spirit, I took pleasure in investigating their causes. To me, the world was a secret I longed to understand. Curiosity, earnest efforts to discover nature’s hidden laws, and a joy approaching rapture as those laws were revealed to me are among the earliest sensations I can remember.

When a second son was born, seven years younger than I, my parents abandoned their wandering life entirely and settled in their native country. We had a house in Geneva and a campagne at Belrive, on the lake’s eastern shore, a little more than a league from the city. We lived mainly at the latter, and my parents’ lives were spent in considerable seclusion. It was my nature to avoid crowds and attach myself passionately to a few people. I was therefore indifferent to my schoolfellows in general, but I formed the closest friendship with one of them. Henry Clerval was the son of a Geneva merchant. He was a boy of remarkable talent and imagination. He loved enterprise, hardship, and even danger for their own sake. He had read deeply in books of chivalry and romance. He composed heroic songs and began writing many tales of enchantment and knightly adventure. He tried to make us perform plays and take part in masquerades, with characters drawn from the heroes of Roncesvalles, King Arthur’s Round Table, and the chivalrous company who shed their blood to recover the holy sepulchre from the infidels.

No human being could have had a happier childhood than I did. My parents possessed the very spirit of kindness and indulgence. We felt that they were not tyrants ruling our lives according to their whims, but the agents and creators of all the many pleasures we enjoyed."}

