          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2ef6fc8ea40f4e32738b48a5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No letter of condolence had been sent to Ireland. The neglect had come back upon the sinner; when poor Lady Elliot herself died, no letter of condolence arrived at Kellynch, and there was consequently too much reason to fear that the Dalrymples regarded the relationship as ended. How to put this troubling matter right and be accepted as cousins again was the question; and, in a more sensible way, neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot considered it unimportant. \u201cFamily connections were always worth preserving, and good society always worth pursuing; Lady Dalrymple had taken a house for three months in Laura Place and would be living in style. She had been in Bath the previous year, and Lady Russell had heard her described as a charming woman. It was very desirable that the connection should be renewed, if it could be done without any sacrifice of propriety on the Elliots\u2019 part.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter, however, would choose his own method, and eventually wrote his distinguished cousin a very fine letter full of explanation, regret, and entreaty. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot admired the letter, but it achieved everything required, producing three lines of scrawl from the Dowager Viscountess: \u201cShe was greatly honoured, and would be happy to renew their acquaintance.\u201d The difficult work was finished; the rewards began. They visited at Laura Place, received the cards of Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and the Honourable Miss Carteret, and placed them wherever they would be most noticeable. \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place\u201d\u2014\u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret\u201d\u2014were mentioned to everyone.\\n\\nAnne was ashamed. Even if Lady Dalrymple and her daughter had been especially agreeable, she would still have been ashamed of the excitement they caused; but they were nothing of the kind. They possessed no superiority of manner, accomplishment, or understanding. Lady Dalrymple had earned the reputation of \u201ca charming woman\u201d because she had a smile and a polite reply for everyone. Miss Carteret, with even less to say, was so plain and awkward that she would never have been tolerated in Camden Place except for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell admitted that she had expected better; yet \u201cit was an acquaintance worth having.\u201d When Anne ventured to express her opinion of them to Mr Elliot, he agreed that they were nothing in themselves, but still insisted that, as a family connection, as people of good society, and as those who would gather good society around them, they had value. Anne smiled and said,\\n\\n\u201cMy idea of good company, Mr Elliot, is the company of intelligent, well-informed people who have a great deal to say; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
