{"request_id":"963d13eedf3d05229fb691a1","rewritten_text":"It is only four o’clock, but it is winter and the sun has already set. There are no clouds in the clear, freezing sky to catch its slanting rays, yet the air itself has a faint rosy tint, which is reflected again on the snow covering the ground. I live in a lonely cottage on a broad, empty heath; no sound of life reaches me. I see the bleak plain covered in white, except for a few black patches where the midday sun has melted the snow from the tops of the sharp, pointed hillocks. As the snow fell, it slid down their sides, leaving it thinner there than on the level ground. A few birds peck at the hard ice covering the pools, for the frost has lasted a long time.

I am in a strange state of mind. I am alone—completely alone—in the world. Misfortune has passed over me and withered me; I know I am about to die, and I feel happy—joyful. I feel my pulse; it beats quickly. I place my thin hand against my cheek; it burns. A small, quick spirit still lives within me, now giving off its final sparks. I shall never see the snows of another winter. I truly believe I shall never again feel the life-giving warmth of another summer sun, and with this belief I begin to write my tragic history. Perhaps a story like mine should die with me, but an indefinable feeling urges me onward, and I am too weak in body and mind to resist even the smallest impulse. While I was still strong, I believed there was a sacred horror in my tale that made it unfit to tell; now, as I am about to die, I profane its mysterious terrors. It is like the wood of the Eumenides, which none but the dying may enter; and Oedipus is about to die.

What am I writing? I must gather my thoughts. I do not know whether anyone will read these pages except you, my friend, who will receive them after my death. I do not address them only to you because I want the pleasure of dwelling on our friendship in a way that would be unnecessary if you alone were to read what I write. I shall therefore tell my story as though I were writing for strangers. You have often asked why I live alone, why I weep, and above all why I remain so silent and distant. In life I dared not reveal it; in death I uncover the mystery. Others will quickly skim these pages, but to you, Woodville, my kind and loving friend, they will be precious—the treasured record of a broken-hearted girl who, even while dying, is still warmed by gratitude toward you. Your tears will fall on the words that tell of my misfortunes; I know they will. And while I live, I thank you for your sympathy."}

