          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7891226556b8efa4fc88eafd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself time to reflect, and scarcely understanding what she felt, Elizabeth, as soon as she had finished this letter, immediately took up the other and, opening it with the greatest impatience, read as follows: it had been written a day after the first had ended.\\n\\n\u201cBy this time, my dearest sister, you must have received my hurried letter; I hope this will be clearer, but although I am not pressed for time, my mind is so confused that I cannot promise to be coherent. Dearest Lizzy, I scarcely know what to say, but I have bad news for you, and it must not be postponed. Imprudent as a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now eager to know that it has actually occurred, for there is only too much cause to fear that they have not gone to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday, having left Brighton the previous day, only a few hours after the express. Though Lydia\u2019s brief letter to Mrs. F. led them to believe that they were bound for Gretna Green, Denny let something fall which showed his opinion that W. had never meant to go there, or to marry Lydia at all. This was repeated to Colonel F., who, immediately alarmed, set out from B., intending to follow their path. He traced them without difficulty as far as Clapham, but no farther; for on reaching that place, they changed into a hackney-coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. The only information afterward is that they were seen proceeding along the London road. I do not know what to think. Having made every possible inquiry in that part of London, Colonel F. came on to Hertfordshire, anxiously repeating his search at every turnpike and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but with no success\u2014no such persons had been seen passing through. With the greatest kindness and concern he continued to Longbourn, and revealed his fears to us in a manner that did the utmost credit to his heart. I am truly sorry for him and Mrs. F.; but no blame can be laid upon either of them. Our distress, my dear Lizzy, is extreme. My father and mother believe the worst, but I cannot think so badly of him. Many circumstances might make it preferable for them to marry privately in town rather than follow their original plan; and even if _he_ could conceive such a scheme against a young woman of Lydia\u2019s connections, which is unlikely, can I believe her so utterly lost to every principle? Impossible! I am sorry, however, to discover that Colonel F. is unwilling to rely upon their marriage: when I expressed my hopes, he shook his head and said he feared W. was not a man who could be trusted.\u201d\"}"
