e would boast that most people, as far as he was concerned, had windows in their chests. He would then support this claim with direct and startling proofs of his intimate knowledge of my own thoughts. At these moments his manner became cold and distant, his eyes blank. His voice, usually a rich tenor, rose to a treble that might have sounded childish or irritating if not for the care and complete clarity of his speech.\"}"
