          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9200e040575fbc6ca9814b87\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He led her to understand that Frank admired her greatly\u2014thought her extremely beautiful and charming; and, with so much to recommend him altogether, she felt she could not judge him too severely. As Mrs. Weston remarked, \u201call young people would have their little whims.\u201d\\n\\nThere was one person among his new acquaintances in Surrey who was less indulgent. In general, throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury, he was judged with considerable fairness; generous allowances were made for the minor excesses of so handsome a young man\u2014one who smiled so readily and bowed so gracefully; but there was one spirit among them that censure could not soften, whether by bows or smiles\u2014Mr. Knightley. The matter was told him at Hartfield; for a moment he said nothing, but Emma heard him almost at once afterwards mutter to himself, over the newspaper in his hand, \u201cHum! just the trifling, silly fellow I took him for.\u201d She was half inclined to resent it; but a moment\u2019s observation persuaded her that it had been said merely to ease his own feelings, and was not intended to provoke her; so she allowed it to pass.\\n\\nThough in one respect Mr. and Mrs. Weston\u2019s visit that morning brought unwelcome news, in another it was especially timely. Something happened while they were at Hartfield which made Emma desire their advice; and, still more fortunately, the advice they offered was precisely what she wanted.\\n\\nThe event was this:\u2014The Coles had lived in Highbury for several years, and were very respectable people\u2014friendly, generous, and unassuming; but, on the other hand, they were of humble origin, in trade, and only moderately genteel. When they first came into the country, they had lived according to their income, quietly, receiving little company, and spending little on it; but during the last year or two their means had increased considerably\u2014their business in town had produced larger profits, and fortune generally had favoured them. As their wealth grew, so did their ambitions: they wanted a larger house and more society. They enlarged their house, increased their servants, and raised their expenses in every direction; and by this time, in fortune and manner of living, they were second only to the family at Hartfield. Their fondness for company, together with their new dining-room, made everyone expect them to give dinners; and several parties, chiefly for unmarried men, had already been held. Emma could scarcely imagine that the established and finest families would be invited\u2014not Donwell, Hartfield, or Randalls. Nothing would induce her to attend if they were; and she regretted that her father\u2019s familiar habits would make her refusal seem less significant than she wished.\"}"
