{"request_id":"18f7b5d587ccd9490b5d916c","rewritten_text":"I cannot describe the joyful relief with which I turned from political quarrels at home and the physical troubles of distant countries to my own dear home, that chosen refuge of goodness and love; to peace, and the sharing of every sacred feeling. If I had never left Windsor, these emotions would not have been so strong. But in Greece I had been exposed to fear and terrible change; there, after a time of anxiety and sorrow, I had watched two people depart whose very names stood for greatness and virtue. Such suffering could never enter the family circle that remained to me. Secluded in our beloved forest, we lived in peace. The passing years did bring some small changes, however, and time, as it always does, marked our pleasures and hopes with signs of mortality. Idris, the most loving wife, sister, and friend, was a tender and devoted mother. For her, motherhood was not, as it was for many, a pastime; it was a passion. We had three children; one, the second in age, died while I was in Greece. This had mixed grief and fear with the proud, joyful feelings of motherhood. Before this happened, the little beings born from her, the young heirs of her brief life, had seemed certain to live. Now she feared that the merciless destroyer might take her remaining children, just as it had taken their brother. The slightest illness filled her with terror. She was miserable whenever she had to be away from them. She had gathered all her happiness into their fragile lives and watched constantly, afraid that the hidden thief might again steal these precious treasures. Fortunately, she had little reason to fear. Alfred, now nine years old, was an upright, manly little boy, with a bright forehead, gentle eyes, and a mild yet independent nature. Our youngest was still an infant, but his soft cheek showed the roses of health, and his tireless liveliness filled our halls with innocent laughter.

Clara had passed beyond the age whose silent ignorance had caused Idris such fear. She was dear to her and to everyone. Intelligence joined with innocence, sensitivity with self-control, and seriousness with perfect good humour; her beauty was extraordinary, yet matched by such winning simplicity that she hung like a pearl among our possessions, a treasure of wonder and goodness.

At the beginning of winter, our Alfred, now nine years old, went to school at Eton for the first time. To him, this seemed the first step toward manhood, and he was accordingly delighted. Sharing lessons and amusements with others brought out the best in his character: his steady perseverance, generosity, and well-controlled firmness."}

