          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"45667f52c358670fed729c5d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in these moods, I often reflected on the old philosophy of the Bi-Part Soul, and entertained the notion of a double Dupin\u2014the creative and the analytical.\\n\\nLet no one suppose, from what I have just said, that I am revealing any mystery or writing a romance. What I have described in the Frenchman was merely the product of an excited, or perhaps diseased, mind. But an example of the nature of his remarks at such times will best convey the idea.\\n\\nOne night we were walking down a long, filthy street near the Palais Royal. Apparently lost in thought, neither of us had spoken a word for at least fifteen minutes. Suddenly Dupin broke the silence with these words:\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very small fellow, that\u2019s true, and would be better suited to the _Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s_. \u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere can be no doubt of that,\u201d I replied involuntarily, without at first noticing\u2014so deeply was I absorbed in thought\u2014the extraordinary way in which his words matched my own reflections. A moment later I came to myself, and my astonishment was profound.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said gravely, \u201cthis is beyond my understanding. I do not hesitate to say that I am amazed and can scarcely trust my senses. How could you know I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d I stopped, to determine beyond doubt whether he truly knew whom I had in mind.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 of Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy do you stop? You were saying to yourself that his small stature made him unfit for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly what I had been thinking. Chantilly had once been a cobbler on the Rue St. Denis who, becoming obsessed with the stage, had attempted the role of Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy of that name and had been notoriously mocked for his efforts.\\n\\n\u201cTell me, for Heaven\u2019s sake,\u201d I cried, \u201cthe method\u2014if there is a method\u2014by which you have managed to read my mind in this matter.\u201d In truth, I was even more startled than I would have cared to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruit seller,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to conclude that the sole-mender was not tall enough for Xerxes, and all the rest of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruit seller! You astonish me\u2014I know no fruit seller at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who bumped into you as we entered the street\u2014it may have been fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI now remembered that a fruit seller carrying a large basket of apples had indeed nearly knocked me down by accident as we passed from the Rue C\u2014\u2014 into the street where we stood. But what this had to do with Chantilly, I could not possibly understand.\"}"
        }
