          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c252f078bd615af32ababd57\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I rose early that morning and once again managed to evade my creditors\u2019 watchfulness. I hurried to the bookseller\u2019s stall and spent what little ready money I had on several volumes of Mechanics and Practical Astronomy. After returning home safely with them, I devoted every spare moment to reading, and soon became, as I believed, sufficiently skilled in such studies to carry out my plan. During this time, I also made every effort to reconcile the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble. At last I succeeded\u2014partly by selling enough of my household furniture to satisfy half their claim, and partly by promising to pay the remainder when I completed a small project I told them I had in mind, for which I asked their assistance. By these means\u2014for they were ignorant men\u2014I had little difficulty winning them over to my purpose.\\n\\n\u201cOnce matters were arranged, I secretly and cautiously disposed of the property still remaining to me, with my wife\u2019s help, and borrowed, in small sums and under various pretexts, a considerable amount of ready money, without giving any thought to how I would repay it. With these funds, I gradually obtained very fine cambric muslin, in pieces of twelve yards each; twine; a quantity of caoutchouc varnish; a large, deep wicker basket made to order; and several other articles needed to construct and equip a balloon of extraordinary size. I instructed my wife to assemble it as quickly as possible and gave her all the necessary information about the method to follow. Meanwhile, I made the twine into a net of sufficient dimensions, fitted it with a hoop and the required cords, and purchased a quadrant, a compass, a spyglass, an ordinary barometer with important modifications, and two astronomical instruments less commonly known. I also found opportunities to transport by night to a secluded place east of Rotterdam five iron-bound casks, each holding about fifty gallons, along with one larger cask; six tin tubes, three inches in diameter, properly shaped and ten feet long; a quantity of a particular metallic substance, or semimetal, which I will not name; and twelve demijohns of a very common acid. The gas produced from these last materials is one no person but myself has ever generated\u2014or, at least, ever used for a similar purpose. I would have no objection to revealing the secret, except that it rightfully belongs to a citizen of Nantes, France, who communicated it to me conditionally.\u201d\"}"
