          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cacbfe8efe04fbf31c899530\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Thus I passed two years. Day after day, hundreds of them, went by. They brought no visible changes, but a few slowly changed my mind as I moved toward death. I began to study more and to understand the thoughts of others as expressed in books. I read history and lost my individuality among the crowds who had lived before me. Perhaps, as the feeling of immediate suffering faded, I became more human. Solitude also lost some of its appeal. I began once more to wish for sympathy\u2014not that I was ever tempted to seek society, but I wished for one friend who would love me. You may say that I was gradually becoming fit to return to society. I do not think so. The sympathy I wanted had to be so pure, so free from the influence of outward circumstances, that in the world I could only have been disappointed by the coarse things mixed into even its finest feelings. Believe me, I was then less suited to any connection with my fellow creatures than before. When I left them, they had tormented me as pain and sickness might: something outside the mind that irritated it, and that I wished to throw off. But now I would have wanted sympathy. I would have wished to join my soul to another\u2019s, and would have prepared myself for abundant disappointment and suffering; for I was as tender as the sensitive plant, all nerve. I did not want sympathy and help in ambition or wisdom, but gentle and mutual affection: smiles to encourage me and kind words of comfort. I wanted one heart into which I could pour my complaints without restraint, so that, through the heavenly nature of that soil, blessed fruit might grow from such poor seed. Yet how could I find this? The love that gives friendship its soul is a gentle spirit rarely found except when two kind people are joined from childhood, or bound together by shared suffering and pursuits. It comes to some chosen people without being sought or expected; it falls like gentle dew on favored places, which, however barren before, become fertile under its kindly influence. But when desired, it flees; it mocks the prayers of those who seek it. It will give itself, but it will not be pursued.\\n\\nI knew all this and did not go looking for sympathy. Yet there, on my lonely heath, beneath my humble roof, with desert all around me, it came like winter sunlight, adorning the snowdrifts even as it helped melt them. Alas, the sun shone on blighted fruit. I did not revive beneath its light, for I was too completely ruined to feel its gentle power.\"}"
