{"request_id":"48c386c1d3289977e7cdf5b4","rewritten_text":"Among the other public buildings in a certain town, which for many reasons it will be wise not to name, and to which I shall give no invented name, stands one anciently familiar in most towns, large or small: namely, a workhouse; and in this workhouse was born, on a day and date I need not take the trouble to repeat, since it can have no possible importance to the reader at this point in the proceedings; the mortal item whose name appears at the head of this chapter.

For a considerable time after it had been brought into this world of sorrow and trouble by the parish surgeon, it remained very doubtful whether the child would live long enough to receive a name; in which event it is more than likely that these memoirs would never have been published; or, if they had, being confined to a couple of pages, they would have enjoyed the remarkable distinction of being the briefest and most faithful specimen of biography existing in the literature of any age or nation.

Though I am not inclined to argue that birth in a workhouse is, by itself, the most fortunate or enviable event that can happen to a human being, I do assert that, in this particular case, it was the best possible thing that could have happened to Oliver Twist. The truth was, there was great difficulty in persuading Oliver to assume the duty of respiration,—an inconvenient practice, though custom has made it necessary to our comfortable existence; and for some time he lay gasping upon a small flock mattress, rather unevenly balanced between this world and the next: the advantage being decidedly with the latter. Now, had Oliver during this short interval been surrounded by attentive grandmothers, worried aunts, experienced nurses, and doctors of deep wisdom, he would most certainly and unquestionably have been killed in an instant. Since there was no one present except a pauper old woman, made somewhat hazy by an unusual allowance of beer; and a parish surgeon who handled such matters by contract; Oliver and Nature settled the question between them. The outcome was that, after a few struggles, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and proceeded to inform the workhouse inmates that a new burden had been placed upon the parish, by raising as loud a cry as might reasonably have been expected from a male infant who had not possessed that highly useful appendage, a voice, for considerably longer than three minutes and a quarter."}

