          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"508205372fab8d270398e62c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She and the children might remain very comfortably.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! papa\u2014that is what you have never managed to accomplish, and I do not believe you ever will. Isabella cannot endure staying apart from her husband.\u201d\\n\\nIt was too true to be disputed. Though unwelcome, Mr. Woodhouse could only utter a resigned sigh; and when Emma noticed how much the thought of his daughter\u2019s devotion to her husband had lowered his spirits, she at once turned the conversation toward a subject more likely to cheer him.\\n\\n\u201cHarriet must spend as much time with us as she can while my brother and sister are here. I am sure she will enjoy the children. We are very proud of them, are we not, papa? I wonder which she will consider the handsomer, Henry or John?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I wonder which she will. Poor little dears, how delighted they will be to come. They are very fond of visiting Hartfield, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say they are, sir. I am sure I cannot think of anyone who is not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHenry is a fine boy, but John resembles his mama very much. Henry is the eldest; he was named for me, rather than for his father. John, the second, bears his father\u2019s name. Some people are surprised, I believe, that the eldest was not, but Isabella wished him to be called Henry, which I thought very kind of her. And he is an exceedingly clever boy. They are all remarkably clever, and have so many charming ways. They come and stand beside my chair, saying, \u2018Grandpapa, can you give me a piece of string?\u2019 and once Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him knives were made only for grandpapas. I think their father is often too rough with them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe seems rough to you,\u201d said Emma, \u201cbecause you are so exceptionally gentle yourself; but if you compared him with other papas, you would not call him rough. He wants his boys to be active and hardy; and when they behave badly, he can speak sharply to them now and then. But he is an affectionate father\u2014Mr. John Knightley is certainly an affectionate father. The children are all fond of him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd then their uncle comes in and throws them up to the ceiling in a most alarming manner!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut they enjoy it, papa; there is nothing they like better. They take such pleasure in it that, unless their uncle made them take turns, the one who began would never yield to the other.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I cannot understand it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is true of all of us, papa. Half the world cannot understand the pleasures of the other half.\u201d\"}"
