          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f03c1d99d0260a3ca7f543c2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But that is of little importance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI might as well ask,\u201d she replied, \u201cwhy, with such an obvious intention of offending and insulting me, you chose to tell me that you liked me against your will, against your reason, and even against your character. Was that not some excuse for your rudeness, if I _was_ rude? But I have other reasons for resentment. You know I do. If my own feelings had not already decided against you, if they had been indifferent, or even favourable, do you think anything could persuade me to accept the man who has been responsible for destroying, perhaps forever, the happiness of a dearly loved sister?\u201d\\n\\nAs she spoke these words, Mr. Darcy changed colour; but the emotion was brief, and he listened without trying to interrupt her as she continued\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI have every reason in the world to think badly of you. No motive can excuse the unjust and ungenerous part you played _there_. You cannot deny that you were the chief, if not the only, cause of separating them from each other; of exposing one to the world\u2019s censure for caprice and instability, the other to its ridicule for disappointed hopes, and involving them both in the most painful misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe paused and saw, with considerable indignation, that he was listening in a manner proving himself entirely untouched by remorse. He even looked at her with a smile of affected disbelief.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny that you did it?\u201d she repeated.\\n\\nHe then replied with assumed calm, \u201cI have no intention of denying that I did everything in my power to separate my friend from your sister, or that I am pleased with my success. Toward _him_ I have been kinder than toward myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth refused to appear to notice this courteous remark, but its meaning was not lost on her, nor was it likely to win her over.\\n\\n\u201cBut this is not the only matter,\u201d she continued, \u201con which my dislike is based. My opinion of you had been formed long before it occurred. Your character was revealed in the account I received many months ago from Mr. Wickham. What have you to say about that? In what imaginary act of friendship can you defend yourself here? Or under what false account can you deceive others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou take a keen interest in that gentleman\u2019s affairs,\u201d said Darcy, in a less composed tone, his colour deepening.\\n\\n\u201cWhoever knows what his misfortunes have been could fail to feel an interest in him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis misfortunes!\u201d Darcy repeated contemptuously. \u201cYes, his misfortunes have indeed been great.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd caused by you,\u201d Elizabeth cried energetically. \u201cYou have reduced him to his present condition of poverty\u2014comparative poverty.\u201d\"}"
