bours.\u2014He could see no objection whatever to his, Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there some very fine morning. He thought it very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014very kind and sensible\u2014far cleverer than dining out.\u2014He was not fond of dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was fortunate in receiving everyone\u2019s immediate agreement. The invitation was welcomed so warmly everywhere that it seemed, like Mrs. Elton, they all regarded the plan as a special compliment to themselves.\u2014Emma and Harriet declared that they expected great pleasure from it; and Mr. Weston, without being asked, promised to bring Frank over to join them if possible, a demonstration of approval and gratitude that might have been spared.\u2014Mr. Knightley was therefore obliged to say that he should be glad to see him; and Mr. Weston undertook to write at once and use every argument to persuade him to come.\"}"
