          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bbbadb3bc19b98e94616120a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Colonel Grangerford was a gentleman, you see. He was a gentleman in every way, and so was his family. He was well born, as people say, and that counts for as much in a man as it does in a horse, according to Widow Douglas\u2014and no one ever denied that she belonged to the highest aristocracy in our town. Pap always said it, too, though he had no more quality than a mudcat. Colonel Grangerford was very tall and very thin, with a dark, pale complexion and not a trace of red anywhere. Every morning he shaved his entire thin face clean, and he had the thinnest lips, the narrowest nostrils, a high nose, heavy eyebrows, and the blackest eyes, set so deeply that they seemed to look out at you from caverns, you might say. His forehead was high, and his hair was black, straight, and shoulder-length. His hands were long and thin, and every day of his life he put on a clean shirt and a complete linen suit, from head to foot, so white it hurt your eyes to look at it. On Sundays he wore a blue tailcoat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane with a silver head. There was nothing frivolous about him, not the slightest bit, and he was never loud. He was as kind as he could be\u2014you could feel it, you know, and that gave you confidence. Sometimes he smiled, and it was good to see; but when he straightened himself like a liberty pole and lightning began flickering beneath his eyebrows, you wanted to climb a tree first and find out what was wrong afterward. He never had to tell anyone to mind their manners\u2014everyone behaved properly when he was there. Everyone loved having him around, too; he was almost always sunshine\u2014I mean, he made it feel like good weather. When he turned into a cloudbank, it grew terribly dark for half a minute, and that was enough; nothing would go wrong again for a week.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came down in the morning, the whole family rose from their chairs and wished them good morning, and did not sit again until they had sat down. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard, where the decanter stood, mixed a glass of bitters, and handed it to him. He held it while they mixed their own, and then they bowed and said, \u201cOur duty to you, sir, and madam.\u201d They bowed the slightest bit and said thank you, and all three drank. Then Bob and Tom poured a spoonful of water over the sugar and the little bit of whisky or apple brandy at the bottom of their tumblers, gave it to me and Buck, and we drank to the old people too.\"}"
