{"request_id":"435fb0e99a4924c0debe92b6","rewritten_text":"The stars were still shining brightly when I awoke, and Taurus, high in the southern sky, showed that it was midnight. I had awakened from troubled dreams. I thought I had been invited to Timon’s final feast; I arrived with a sharp appetite, the covers were taken away, and the hot water sent up its disappointing vapours, while I fled from the host’s anger, who had assumed Raymond’s form. To my diseased imagination, the vessels he hurled after me were filled with foul vapour, and my friend’s figure, changed by a thousand distortions, swelled into a gigantic phantom, bearing the mark of pestilence upon its brow. The rising shadow grew and grew, filling, and then seeming to strive to burst beyond, the adamantine vault that arched above, upheld, and enclosed the world. The nightmare became torture; with a powerful effort I cast off sleep and restored reason to her accustomed office. My first thought was Perdita; I must return to her, and support her, drawing from despair whatever nourishment might best sustain her wounded heart, recalling her from grief’s wild excesses through the stern laws of duty and the gentle tenderness of regret.

The stars’ position was my only guide. I turned away from the terrible ruin of the Golden City and, after great exertion, managed to free myself from its enclosure. Outside the walls I encountered a company of soldiers; I borrowed a horse from one of them and hurried to my sister. In that brief interval the plain had changed; the encampment had been broken up, and remnants of the scattered army appeared in little groups here and there. Every face was darkened, and every gesture expressed astonishment and dismay.

With a heavy heart I entered the palace, afraid to move forward, speak, or look. In the centre of the hall sat Perdita on the marble pavement, her head bowed upon her breast, her disordered hair falling around her, and her fingers busily entwined. She was pale as marble, and agony tightened every feature. She noticed me and looked up questioningly; the faint hope in her glance was misery. The words died before I could utter them, and I felt a ghastly smile crease my lips. She understood my gesture; once more her head sank, and once more her fingers moved restlessly. At length I found my voice, but its sound frightened her. The unhappy girl had read my expression, and would not, for all the world, have the story of her grievous misery formed and confirmed by harsh, irrevocable words. Rather, she seemed to wish to turn my thoughts from the subject. She rose from the floor. “Hush!” she whispered; “after much weeping, Clara sleeps; we must not disturb her.”"}

