nd her, and her fingers busily entwined. She was pale as marble, and agony tightened every feature. She noticed me and looked up questioningly; the faint hope in her glance was misery. The words died before I could utter them, and I felt a ghastly smile crease my lips. She understood my gesture; once more her head sank, and once more her fingers moved restlessly. At length I found my voice, but its sound frightened her. The unhappy girl had read my expression, and would not, for all the world, have the story of her grievous misery formed and confirmed by harsh, irrevocable words. Rather, she seemed to wish to turn my thoughts from the subject. She rose from the floor. \u201cHush!\u201d she whispered; \u201cafter much weeping, Clara sleeps; we must not disturb her.\u201d\"}"
