          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"70904253249905a3d65961ad\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Looking at him, one might have thought that Nemesis had taken Pallas\u2019s shield and shown him the Gorgon\u2019s head. He seemed turned to stone, and his face was as pale and sorrowful as marble. He had lived the refined, luxurious life of a young man born to wealth, free from ugly worries and full of carefree charm. Now, for the first time, he understood the terrible mystery of Destiny and the dreadful meaning of Doom.\\n\\nHow insane and monstrous it all seemed! Could it be that his hand carried, in letters he could not read but another person could interpret, some terrible secret of sin, some blood-red mark of crime? Was there truly no escape? Were people no more than chess pieces moved by an unseen power, or clay vessels shaped by a potter for honour or disgrace? His reason rebelled against the idea, yet he felt that some tragedy hung over him and that he had suddenly been forced to carry an unbearable burden. Actors were fortunate. They could choose whether to appear in tragedy or comedy, whether to suffer or celebrate, laugh or cry. Real life was different. Most men and women had to play roles for which they were not suited. Our Guildensterns played Hamlet for us, while our Hamlets had to joke like Prince Hal. The world was a stage, but the play was badly cast.\\n\\nSuddenly Mr. Podgers entered the room. When he saw Lord Arthur, he started, and his coarse, heavy face turned a greenish yellow. Their eyes met, and for a moment neither man spoke.\\n\\n\u2018The Duchess left one of her gloves here, Lord Arthur, and asked me to bring it to her,\u2019 Mr. Podgers said at last. \u2018Ah, I see it on the sofa! Good evening.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Mr. Podgers, I insist that you give me a direct answer to a question I am about to ask you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Another time, Lord Arthur. The Duchess is anxious, and I am afraid I must go.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You will not go. The Duchess is in no hurry.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Ladies should not be kept waiting, Lord Arthur,\u2019 said Mr. Podgers with his sickly smile. \u2018The fair sex can be impatient.\u2019\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s finely shaped lips curled with irritated disdain. At that moment, the poor Duchess seemed almost unimportant to him. He crossed the room to where Mr. Podgers stood and held out his hand.\\n\\n\u2018Tell me what you saw there,\u2019 he said. \u2018Tell me the truth. I must know. I am not a child.\u2019\"}"
