          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1cffd7a3cc118ddf60add4c1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But if it had not been for me, he never would have escaped. Morgan le Fay hated him with all her heart, and she never would have relented toward him. Yet his offense arose more from carelessness than deliberate wickedness. He had said she had red hair. Well, she did; but that was no proper way to describe it. When red-haired people belong to a certain social class, their hair is auburn.\\n\\nConsider this: among these forty-seven captives were five whose names, offenses, and dates of imprisonment were no longer known! One woman and four men\u2014all bent, wrinkled, and mentally extinguished patriarchs. They themselves had forgotten these details long ago; at most, they possessed vague theories about them, nothing definite, and nothing they told twice in the same way. The succession of priests whose duty had been to pray daily with the captives, remind them that God had placed them there for some wise purpose, and teach them that patience, humility, and submission to oppression were what He wished to see in people of an inferior rank, had traditions about these poor human ruins, but nothing more. Those traditions went only a little way, for they concerned merely the length of the imprisonment, not the names of the offenses. Even with the help of tradition, the only fact that could be established was that none of the five had seen daylight for thirty-five years; how much longer they had endured this deprivation could not be guessed. The king and queen knew nothing about these poor creatures except that they were heirlooms, assets inherited with the throne from the previous owners. None of their history had been passed on with them, and so their inheriting masters had considered them worthless and taken no interest in them. I said to the queen:\\n\\n\u201cThen why on earth didn\u2019t you set them free?\u201d\\n\\nThe question baffled her. She did not know why she had not; the thought had never occurred to her. Thus, without realizing it, she was already laying the foundation for the future history of prisoners in the Castle d\u2019If. It seemed clear to me now that, given her upbringing, those inherited prisoners were simply property\u2014nothing more and nothing less. When we inherit property, it does not occur to us to throw it away, even when we place no value on it.\\n\\nWhen I led my procession of human bats into the open air and the harsh afternoon sunlight\u2014first blindfolding them, out of mercy for eyes so long untroubled by light\u2014they were a sight to behold. Skeletons, scarecrows, goblins, pathetic horrors, every one of them; the most authentic children imaginable of Monarchy by the Grace of God and the Established Church.\"}"
