          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"331ae1582da1903d12136d1a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You wouldn\u2019t know about me unless you\u2019ve read a book called The Adventures of Tom Sawyer; but that doesn\u2019t matter. Mr. Mark Twain wrote that book, and he told the truth, mostly. There were things he stretched, but mostly he told the truth. That\u2019s nothing. I never knew anybody who didn\u2019t lie once in a while, unless it was Aunt Polly, or the widow, or maybe Mary. Aunt Polly\u2014Tom\u2019s Aunt Polly, she is\u2014and Mary, and the Widow Douglas are all mentioned in that book, which is mostly true, with some stretchers, as I said before.\\n\\nNow this is how the book ends: Tom and I found the money the robbers had hidden in the cave, and it made us rich. We got six thousand dollars each\u2014all gold. It was a tremendous amount of money when it was piled up. Well, Judge Thatcher took it and put it out at interest, and it brought us a dollar a day apiece all year round\u2014more than a person could figure out what to do with. The Widow Douglas took me as her son and said she would sivilize me; but living in the house all the time was hard, seeing how dreary regular and proper the widow was in everything she did; and when I couldn\u2019t bear it any longer, I took off. I put on my old rags and went back to my sugar-hogshead, and was free and content. But Tom Sawyer tracked me down and said he meant to start a band of robbers, and I could join if I went back to the widow and acted respectable. So I returned.\\n\\nThe widow cried over me and called me a poor lost lamb, and she called me plenty of other names too, but she didn\u2019t mean any harm by it. She put me into those new clothes again, and all I could do was sweat and sweat and feel squeezed up. Well, then the old business started over. The widow rang a bell for supper, and you had to come right away. When you reached the table, you couldn\u2019t begin eating at once; you had to wait while the widow bowed her head and grumbled a little over the victuals, though there wasn\u2019t really anything wrong with them\u2014that is, nothing except that everything had been cooked separately. In a barrel of odds and ends it\u2019s different; the things mix together, the juice sort of trades places, and everything tastes better.\\n\\nAfter supper she brought out her book and taught me about Moses and the Bulrushers, and I was eager to learn all about him; but after a while she let slip that Moses had been dead for a very long time. So then I didn\u2019t care about him anymore, because I don\u2019t take any interest in dead people.\"}"
