          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b95f248667b7528c9942e81c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She suddenly felt a strong urge to go to the outer door; she wanted to see whether it was raining. Why should she suspect herself of another reason? Captain Wentworth must be out of sight. She rose from her seat and decided to go. One half of her should not always be wiser than the other, or forever suspecting the other of being worse than it was. She would see if it rained. But in that moment Captain Wentworth entered, with a group of gentlemen and ladies who were clearly his acquaintances and whom he must have joined just below Milsom Street. The sight of her seemed to strike and confuse him more than she had ever seen before; he turned quite red. For the first time since they had renewed their acquaintance, she felt that she was the more composed of the two. She had had a few moments to prepare herself. The first overwhelming, blinding effects of surprise had already passed. Yet she still felt enough: agitation, pain, pleasure, something between delight and misery.\\n\\nHe spoke to her, then turned away. His manner showed embarrassment. She could not have called it cold or friendly, or described it with any certainty except as embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a short while, however, he came toward her and spoke again. They exchanged a few questions about ordinary matters, though neither was probably much wiser for the answers, and Anne remained fully aware that he was less at ease than before. Because they had spent so much time together, they had learned to speak with apparent calm and indifference; but he could not manage it now. Time had changed him, or Louisa had changed him. He was conscious of something. He looked very well, not like someone who had suffered in health or spirits, and he spoke of Uppercross, the Musgroves, and even Louisa. As he mentioned her, he briefly wore his old, teasingly meaningful expression. Yet he was still Captain Wentworth: uncomfortable, uneasy, and unable to pretend otherwise.\\n\\nIt did not surprise Anne, but it saddened her, to see that Elizabeth would not acknowledge him. She saw that he noticed Elizabeth, and that Elizabeth noticed him; each clearly recognized the other. Anne was sure he was ready and expecting to be acknowledged as an acquaintance, and it pained her to watch her sister turn away with unchanging coldness.\\n\\nLady Dalrymple\u2019s carriage, which Miss Elliot had been waiting for with growing impatience, now arrived. The servant came in to announce it.\"}"
