          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fd6d2546462ee1b4fc055078\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But enough of this. I will begin my story: it is my final task, and I hope I have enough strength to complete it. I record no crimes; my faults can easily be forgiven, for they came not from evil intentions but from poor judgment. I believe few people would claim that, through different actions and greater wisdom, they could have escaped the misfortunes of which I am the victim. My fate was controlled by necessity, a terrible necessity. It required hands stronger than mine\u2014stronger, I believe, than any human power\u2014to break the thick, unyielding chain that bound me, once filled with nothing but joy and a warm love of goodness, to misery that could end only in death, and now is about to end there. But I am forgetting myself; my story has not yet been told. I will pause for a few moments, wipe my clouded eyes, and try to replace the present dull but heavy unhappiness with the sharper emotions of the past.\\n\\nI was born in England. My father was a man of rank. He had lost his own father when he was young and was raised by a weak mother, who gave him all the indulgence she believed proper for a wealthy nobleman. He was sent to Eton and later to college, and from childhood was allowed the free use of large sums of money. Thus, from his earliest years, he enjoyed the independence that a boy with such advantages always gains at a public school.\\n\\nUnder these influences, his passions found rich soil in which to take root and grow, either as flowers or weeds, according to their nature. Because he had always been allowed to act for himself, his character developed early and strongly, showing a varied surface where a sharp-eyed observer might see the seeds of both virtues and misfortunes. His careless extravagance led him to spend enormous sums on passing whims, which, because of their apparent force, he dignified with the name of passions. Yet this extravagance often appeared as boundless generosity. While he earnestly concerned himself with the needs of others, his own desires were satisfied completely. He gave his money, but none of his own wishes were sacrificed by his gifts; he gave his time, which he did not value, and his affections, which he was glad to have awakened in any way.\\n\\nI do not say that, if his desires had been set against those of others, he would have shown excessive selfishness, but he was never put to that test. He was raised in prosperity and surrounded by all its advantages; everyone loved him and wanted to please him.\"}"
