          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eabd257fa894d3e14cea3bfe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He had, indeed, been entirely unaware of his own influence. He had followed her into the shrubbery without any thought of attempting it. He had come, anxious to see how she endured Frank Churchill\u2019s engagement, with no selfish purpose, no purpose at all beyond trying, if she gave him an opportunity, to comfort or advise her.\u2014What followed had been the work of the moment, the immediate consequence of what he heard upon his feelings. The delightful certainty of her complete indifference to Frank Churchill, of her heart being wholly free from him, had awakened the hope that, in time, he might win her affection for himself;\u2014but it had not been an immediate hope\u2014only, in that instant when eagerness overcame judgment, a wish to be told that she did not reject his effort to attach her.\u2014The higher hopes that gradually unfolded were all the more enchanting.\u2014The affection he had asked permission to create, if he could, was already his!\u2014In less than half an hour, he had gone from a state of profound distress to something so nearly perfect happiness that it could be called by no other name.\\n\\n_Her_ change had been equal.\u2014That single half-hour had bestowed on each the same precious certainty of being loved, and had removed from each the same measure of ignorance, jealousy, or mistrust.\u2014On his side, there had been a jealousy of long standing, as old as Frank Churchill\u2019s arrival, or even the expectation of it.\u2014He had loved Emma, and been jealous of Frank Churchill, from nearly the same time, one feeling having perhaps revealed the other to him. It was his jealousy of Frank Churchill that had driven him from the country.\u2014The party at Box Hill had determined him to leave. He would spare himself the sight of such permitted and encouraged attentions again.\u2014He had gone away to learn indifference.\u2014But he had chosen the wrong place. There was too much domestic happiness in his brother\u2019s home; woman appeared in it in too amiable a shape; Isabella was too much like Emma\u2014differing only in those conspicuous deficiencies which always made the other shine more brilliantly before him\u2014for much to have been accomplished, even if his stay had been longer.\u2014Still, he had remained resolutely, day after day\u2014until the morning post brought the account of Jane Fairfax.\u2014Then, with the gladness that must be felt, and which he did not hesitate to feel, since he had never thought Frank Churchill in any way worthy of Emma, there was so much tender concern, so much urgent anxiety for her, that he could remain no longer. He had ridden home through the rain, and had walked up immediately after dinner, to see how this sweetest and best of creatures, faultless despite all her faults, bore the discovery.\"}"
