          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"be965ba5962a59faf3ae1e1e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He rose and left the room with my nurse, and a moment later my father entered.\\n\\nNothing at that moment could have pleased me more than my father\u2019s arrival. I reached out my hand to him and cried,\\n\\n\u201cAre you safe\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d\\n\\nMy father reassured me that they were well and tried, by speaking of matters so dear to my heart, to lift my spirits. But he soon realized that a prison cannot be a cheerful place. \u201cWhat a place this is that you inhabit, my son!\u201d he said, looking sadly at the barred windows and miserable condition of the room. \u201cYou travelled in search of happiness, but some fatality seems to follow you. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThe name of my unfortunate, murdered friend caused an agitation too great for my weakened state; I began to weep.\\n\\n\u201cAlas, yes, my father,\u201d I replied. \u201cSome terrible destiny hangs over me, and I must live to fulfil it; otherwise, surely, I would have died upon Henry\u2019s coffin.\u201d\\n\\nWe were not permitted to speak for long, since the precarious state of my health made every precaution necessary to preserve my tranquillity. Mr. Kirwin entered and insisted that I must not exhaust my strength through excessive exertion. Yet my father\u2019s presence was like that of my good angel, and I gradually regained my health.\\n\\nAs my illness left me, I became consumed by a dark and hopeless melancholy that nothing could dispel. Clerval\u2019s image was always before me, ghastly and murdered. More than once, the agitation caused by these thoughts made my friends fear a dangerous relapse. Alas, why had they preserved such a miserable and detested life? Surely it was so that I might fulfil my destiny, which was now nearing its end. Soon\u2014oh, very soon\u2014death would silence these throbbing pains and free me from the immense weight of anguish that was bearing me to the grave; and, in carrying out the sentence of justice, I too would sink into rest. At that time death still seemed far away, though the desire for it was always in my thoughts; and I often sat for hours without moving or speaking, longing for some great upheaval that might bury both me and my destroyer beneath its ruins.\\n\\nThe assizes drew near. I had already spent three months in prison, and although I remained weak and constantly in danger of relapse, I was forced to travel nearly a hundred miles to the country town where the court was held. Mr. Kirwin took responsibility for every detail of gathering witnesses and preparing my defence.\"}"
