          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3cab4950902297a6099c1aef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He could not help it. And it drove him mad to think that Becky Thatcher had not once noticed that he was even alive. But she had noticed, after all; she knew she was winning their struggle, and she was glad to see him suffer as she had suffered.\\n\\nAmy\u2019s cheerful chatter became unbearable. Tom hinted that he had things to take care of, things that had to be done, and that time was running short. But it was no use\u2014the girl kept talking. Tom thought, \u201cOh, hang her, am I ever going to get rid of her?\u201d At last he really had to attend to those things, and she innocently said she would be \u201caround\u201d when school ended. He hurried away, hating her for saying it.\\n\\n\u201cAny other boy!\u201d Tom thought, grinding his teeth. \u201cAny boy in the whole town except that Saint Louis show-off who thinks he dresses so well and is some kind of aristocrat! Oh, all right, I beat you the first day you ever came to this town, mister, and I\u2019ll beat you again! Just wait until I catch you alone! I\u2019ll just\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe acted out a beating of an imaginary boy, punching the air, kicking, and gouging. \u201cOh, you do, do you? You\u2019re yelling \u2018enough,\u2019 are you? Well, let that teach you!\u201d And he finished the imaginary beating to his satisfaction.\\n\\nTom ran home at noon. His conscience could not stand any more of Amy\u2019s grateful happiness, and his jealousy could endure no more of the other girl\u2019s suffering. Becky went back to looking at pictures with Alfred, but as the minutes passed and Tom did not come to suffer, her triumph began to fade and she lost interest. She became serious and distracted, then sad. Two or three times she listened hopefully when she heard footsteps, but each time she was disappointed; Tom did not come. At last she became completely miserable and wished she had not taken things so far. Poor Alfred saw that he was losing her, though he did not know why, and kept saying, \u201cOh, here\u2019s a funny one! Look at this!\u201d Finally she lost patience and said, \u201cOh, don\u2019t bother me! I don\u2019t care about them!\u201d Then she burst into tears, stood up, and walked away.\\n\\nAlfred followed close behind and was about to comfort her, but she said:\\n\\n\u201cGo away and leave me alone, can\u2019t you? I hate you!\u201d\\n\\nThe boy stopped, wondering what he could have done. She had said she would look at pictures with him throughout the lunch break. She walked on, crying. Alfred then went thoughtfully into the empty schoolhouse. He felt humiliated and angry. It was easy for him to understand the truth: the girl had merely used him to take out her spite on Tom Sawyer.\"}"
