          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"92f036b3dbf1bda4c7f660d3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was Lady Windermere\u2019s last reception before Easter, and Bentinck House was even more crowded than usual. Six Cabinet Ministers had come straight from the Speaker\u2019s Lev\u00e9e, wearing their stars and ribbons. All the fashionable women wore their finest dresses, and at the far end of the picture gallery stood Princess Sophia of Carlsr\u00fche, a large, Tartar-looking woman with tiny black eyes and magnificent emeralds. She spoke bad French at the top of her voice and laughed wildly at everything said to her. It was certainly a remarkable mixture of people. Splendid peeresses chatted pleasantly with fierce Radicals, popular preachers rubbed shoulders with famous sceptics, and a whole group of bishops followed a stout opera singer from room to room. Several Royal Academicians, disguised as artists, stood on the staircase, and it was said that the supper room had, for a while, been packed with geniuses. In fact, it was one of Lady Windermere\u2019s finest evenings, and the Princess stayed until nearly half past eleven.\\n\\nAs soon as she had left, Lady Windermere returned to the picture gallery, where a famous political economist was solemnly explaining the scientific theory of music to an offended Hungarian virtuoso, and began speaking with the Duchess of Paisley. She looked wonderfully beautiful, with her noble ivory throat, her large blue eyes like forget-me-nots, and her thick coils of golden hair. They were truly gold\u2014not the pale straw colour that now wrongly claims that name, but gold like sunlight woven into cloth or hidden in unusual amber. They gave her face something of a saint\u2019s appearance, mixed with no small amount of a sinner\u2019s charm. She was a fascinating psychological study. Early in life, she had discovered the important truth that nothing resembles innocence so closely as an indiscretion. Through a series of reckless adventures, half of them completely harmless, she had gained all the freedoms that came with being an individual. She had changed husbands more than once; indeed, Debrett listed three marriages. But since she had never changed her lover, society had long since stopped gossiping about her. She was now forty, had no children, and possessed that intense love of pleasure which is the secret of staying young.\\n\\nSuddenly she looked eagerly around the room and said in her clear contralto voice, \u2018Where is my cheiromantist?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Your what, Gladys?\u2019 exclaimed the Duchess, starting despite herself.\\n\\n\u2018My cheiromantist, Duchess. I can\u2019t live without him at present.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Dear Gladys! You are always so original,\u2019 murmured the Duchess, trying to remember what a cheiromantist actually was and hoping it was not the same thing as a chiropodist.\\n\\n\u2018He comes to read my hand twice a week, regularly,\u2019 Lady Windermere continued, \u2018and he is most interesting about it.\u2019\"}"
