onfirmed. Rose was in the first stage of a severe and dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must act, Oliver, and not surrender to fruitless sorrow,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing a finger to her lips as she looked steadily into his face; \u201cthis letter must be sent to Mr. Losberne with all possible speed. It must be taken to the market-town: no more than four miles away by the footpath across the fields: and from there sent by a mounted express directly to Chertsey. The people at the inn will undertake this: and I know I can rely on you to see it done.\u201d\\n\\nOliver could not answer, but his anxious expression showed that he wished to leave at once.\"}"
