{"request_id":"58301904916b2e5f51b3f91e","rewritten_text":"Far off, the line of flickering gas-lamps curved into the distance, and outside a small walled house a solitary hansom stood, its driver asleep within. He walked quickly towards Portland Place, glancing back from time to time as if afraid someone was following him. At the corner of Rich Street, two men were reading a little bill pasted on a hoarding. Moved by a strange curiosity, he crossed the road. As he approached, the word ‘Murder,’ printed in black, caught his eye. He started, and a deep flush spread across his cheek. The notice offered a reward for information leading to the capture of a man of medium height, between thirty and forty, wearing a billy-cock hat, a black coat, and check trousers, and bearing a scar on his right cheek. He read it again and again, wondering whether the miserable man would be taken, and how he had received his scar. Perhaps one day his own name might be posted on London’s walls. Perhaps, too, a price would be placed upon his head.

The thought filled him with sickening horror. He turned away and hurried into the night.

He scarcely knew where he went. He dimly remembered wandering among a maze of wretched houses, lost in a vast web of gloomy streets, and it was broad dawn when at last he found himself in Piccadilly Circus. As he walked home towards Belgrave Square, he encountered the great wagons bound for Covent Garden. The carters in white smocks, with kindly sunburnt faces and rough, curly hair, marched sturdily along, cracking their whips and calling occasionally to one another; on the back of a huge grey horse, leader of a clattering team, sat a plump boy with a bunch of primroses in his battered hat, gripping the mane tightly in his small hands and laughing; and the towering heaps of vegetables seemed like masses of jade beneath the morning sky, like green jade against the pink petals of some wondrous rose. Lord Arthur felt strangely moved, though he could not say why. Something in the delicate beauty of dawn appeared to him unbearably pathetic, and he thought of all the days that open in loveliness and close in storm. These countrymen, too, with their rough, good-natured voices and careless manners—what a peculiar London they must see! A London untouched by the sin of night and the smoke of day, a pale, ghostly city, a deserted town of tombs! He wondered what they thought of it, and whether they knew anything of its splendour and its shame, its fierce, flame-coloured pleasures and its dreadful hunger, of all that it creates and destroys from morning until evening."}

