ught to me in a way few are forced to endure. I mourn now, and must always mourn, those few brief months of paradisaical bliss; I had broken no command, eaten no apple, and yet was ruthlessly expelled from it. Alas! my companion had, and I was hurled down in his fall. But I stray from my account\u2014let sorrow arrive in its appointed hour; at this point in my story I may still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this delightful companionship, when my aunt became ill. I spent an entire month nursing her in her chamber, but her illness was fatal, and she died, leaving me for some time inconsolable. Death is terrible to the living; the bonds of habit are so powerful, even when affection does not bind them, that the heart must suffer when they are broken.\"}"
