          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"acb2e5bc3b61819d869420f9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No bringing him up by hand, then. Not at all!\u201d\\n\\nJoe offered me more gravy, which I was afraid to accept.\\n\\n\u201cHe gave you a world of trouble, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Mrs. Hubble sympathetically to my sister.\\n\\n\u201cTrouble?\u201d my sister repeated; \u201ctrouble?\u201d Then she launched into a dreadful list of every illness I had been responsible for, every sleepless night I had caused, every high place from which I had fallen, every low place into which I had tumbled, every injury I had inflicted on myself, and every occasion when she had wished me in my grave, while I had stubbornly refused to go there.\\n\\nI imagine the Romans must have irritated one another greatly with their noses. Perhaps that was why they became such a restless people. At any rate, Mr. Wopsle\u2019s Roman nose annoyed me so intensely during the account of my offences that I could have pulled it until he howled. Yet everything I had suffered so far was nothing beside the terrible feelings that seized me when the silence following my sister\u2019s recital was broken\u2014a silence in which everyone had stared at me (as I was painfully aware) with indignation and disgust.\\n\\n\u201cStill,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, gently guiding the company back to the subject from which they had wandered, \u201cPork\u2014considered as biled\u2014is rich, too; ain\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTake a little brandy, uncle,\u201d said my sister.\\n\\nO Heavens, the moment had come at last! He would discover that it was weak, declare it weak, and I would be ruined! I gripped the table leg beneath the cloth with both hands and awaited my fate.\\n\\nMy sister fetched the stone bottle, returned with it, and poured out his brandy; nobody else took any. The miserable man toyed with his glass\u2014lifted it, examined it against the light, set it down\u2014and lengthened my agony. Meanwhile, Mrs. Joe and Joe were busily clearing the table for the pie and pudding.\\n\\nI could not take my eyes from him. Still clutching the table leg with hands and feet, I watched the wretched creature play with his glass, pick it up, smile, tilt back his head, and swallow the brandy. Immediately afterward, the company was struck with indescribable alarm when he sprang up, spun around several times in a horrifying, spasmodic whooping-cough dance, and rushed through the door. He then appeared beyond the window, plunging and spitting violently, making the most dreadful faces, and seeming completely out of his mind.\\n\\nI held on firmly while Mrs. Joe and Joe ran to him.\"}"
