{"request_id":"510a0a2d915410ed412ad10c","rewritten_text":"During his brief stay this time, Emma had barely seen him; but she had seen enough to feel that their first meeting was over, and to sense that the mixture of resentment and pretension now spread across his manner had not improved him. In fact, she was beginning to wonder how she had ever found him pleasing at all; and the sight of him was so inseparably linked with deeply unpleasant feelings that, except in a moral sense—as a penance, a lesson, and a useful humiliation for her own mind—she would have been grateful to know she would never see him again. She wished him well enough; but he caused her pain, and his welfare twenty miles away would have given her the greatest satisfaction.

The pain of his continued residence in Highbury, however, would certainly be reduced by his marriage. It would prevent many foolish anxieties and smooth over many awkward situations. A Mrs. Elton would provide an excuse for any change in their relations; their former intimacy might fade without comment. It would be almost like beginning their polite acquaintance again.

Emma thought very little of the lady herself. She was no doubt good enough for Mr. Elton; accomplished enough for Highbury, and handsome enough—though probably plain beside Harriet. As for family connections, Emma felt entirely at ease, convinced that, despite all his loudly proclaimed claims and contempt for Harriet, he had achieved nothing. On that point, the truth seemed discoverable. What she was might remain uncertain; but who she was could be found out. And setting aside the £10,000, she did not seem in any way superior to Harriet. She brought no name, no distinguished family, no important connections. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol merchant—he must, of course, be called a merchant; but since the profits of his commercial life appeared so modest, it was fair to suppose that the dignity of his business had been equally modest. She had usually spent part of every winter in Bath; but Bristol was her home, the very center of Bristol. Though her parents had died several years earlier, an uncle remained—working in the legal profession; nothing more specifically honorable was risked about him than that he was in the legal profession—and the daughter had lived with him. Emma guessed that he was some attorney’s drudge, too dull to advance. All the grandeur of the connection seemed to depend on the elder sister, who was very well married to a gentleman in a great way of business near Bristol, and who kept two carriages. That was the conclusion of the story; that was Miss Hawkins’s glory."}

