          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"962b46a86750087c1ebc3205\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He mounted a fiery horse, the same one that had carried him to victory in Greece; he exhausted himself with punishing exercise, losing the anguish of a troubled mind in physical sensation.\\n\\nHe slowly recovered himself; at last, like someone emerging from the effects of poison, he raised his head above the vapors of fever and passion into the still air of calm reflection. He considered what should be done. He was first struck by the length of time that had passed since madness, rather than any rational impulse, had governed his actions. A month had gone by, and during it he had not seen Evadne. Her power, connected with few of the lasting emotions in his heart, had greatly weakened. He was no longer her slave\u2014no longer her lover: he would never see her again, and, by the completeness of his return, earn Perdita\u2019s confidence.\\n\\nYet, as he made this decision, imagination summoned the miserable dwelling of the Greek girl\u2014a dwelling she had refused to exchange for one of greater luxury out of noble and lofty principle. He thought of the splendor of her circumstances and appearance when he had first known her; of her life in Constantinople, surrounded by every feature of Oriental magnificence; of her present poverty, her daily labor, her lonely condition, her faded, famine-stricken cheek. Compassion filled his breast; he would see her once more. He would devise some plan to restore her to society and the enjoyment of her rank; their separation would then follow naturally.\\n\\nAgain he thought of how, during this long month, he had avoided Perdita, fleeing from her as from the stings of his own conscience. But he was awake now; all this would be corrected, and future devotion erase the memory of this single stain upon the serenity of their life. He grew cheerful as he considered it, and calmly and firmly laid out the conduct he would follow. He remembered that he had promised Perdita to attend that very evening\u2014the nineteenth of October, the anniversary of his election as Protector\u2014a festival held in his honor. May this festival prove a good omen for the happiness of future years. First, he would visit Evadne; he would not remain, but he owed her some explanation, some compensation for his long, unannounced absence; then he would go to Perdita, to the forgotten world, to the duties of society, the splendor of rank, and the enjoyment of power.\\n\\nAfter the scene described in the preceding pages, Perdita had expected a complete change in Raymond\u2019s manner and conduct. She anticipated open communication and a return to those habits of affectionate companionship that had made up the happiness of her life.\"}"
