          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fbc73b32b0bf04c580c8a259\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must go, uncertain of what will happen to me; but I shall return here, or follow your party, as soon as I can. A word, a look, will be enough to decide whether I enter your father\u2019s house this evening or never.\u201d\\n\\nA letter like that was not something she could quickly recover from. Half an hour alone with her thoughts might have calmed her; but the mere ten minutes that passed before she was interrupted, with all the constraints of her situation, could do nothing to restore her composure. Every moment instead brought new agitation. It was overwhelming happiness. And before she had moved beyond the first intensity of feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta all entered.\\n\\nThe absolute necessity of appearing like herself immediately set off a struggle; but after a while she could do no more. She began to understand nothing they said and had to plead illness and excuse herself. They could then see that she looked very unwell, were shocked and concerned, and would not leave her for anything. This was dreadful. If only they had gone away and left her in the quiet possession of the room, it would have cured her; but having them all standing or waiting around her was unbearable, and in desperation she said she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cBy all means, my dear,\u201d cried Mrs. Musgrove, \u201cgo home at once and take care of yourself, so that you may be fit for the evening. I wish Sarah were here to treat you, but I am no doctor myself. Charles, ring and order a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut the chair would never do. Worse than everything! To lose the chance of speaking two words to Captain Wentworth during her quiet, solitary walk up the town\u2014and she felt almost certain of meeting him\u2014was impossible to bear. She strongly objected to the chair, and Mrs. Musgrove, thinking only of one kind of illness, asked anxiously whether there had been any fall; whether Anne had recently slipped and struck her head; and, once assured that she was certain no such thing had happened, could let her go cheerfully, confident of finding her better that night.\\n\\nAnxious to leave no possible precaution unattended to, Anne struggled and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid, ma\u2019am, that this has not been fully understood. Please be so kind as to tell the other gentlemen that we hope to see your entire party this evening. I am afraid there has been some mistake; and I particularly wish you to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! my dear, it is perfectly understood, I assure you.\u201d\"}"
