          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7fa232ae5509e0c4d633c0f7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the center of this rift I saw a white spot, but at first could not tell what it was. I adjusted the telescope\u2019s focus, looked again, and now recognized it as a human skull.\\n\\n\u201cUpon making this discovery, I was so confident that I considered the enigma solved; for the phrase \u2018main branch, seventh limb, east side\u2019 could refer only to the skull\u2019s position on the tree, while \u2018shoot from the left eye of the death\u2019s head\u2019 allowed only one interpretation in connection with a search for buried treasure. I understood that the plan was to drop a bullet through the skull\u2019s left eye, and that a bee-line\u2014or, in other words, a straight line\u2014drawn from the nearest point of the trunk through \u2018the shot\u2019 (the place where the bullet fell), and then extended fifty feet farther, would mark a precise point. Beneath that point, I thought it at least possible, a valuable deposit lay hidden.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this,\u201d I said, \u201cis perfectly clear, and, though ingenious, still simple and explicit. When you left the Bishop\u2019s Hotel, what happened next?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHaving carefully taken the tree\u2019s bearings, I turned toward home. The moment I left \u2018the devil\u2019s seat,\u2019 however, the circular rift disappeared; and no matter how I turned, I could not see it again. What seems to me the greatest ingenuity in the whole affair is the fact\u2014for repeated experiments have convinced me that it is a fact\u2014that this circular opening can be seen from no other accessible viewpoint than the narrow ledge on the face of the rock.\\n\\n\u201cOn this expedition to the \u2018Bishop\u2019s Hotel,\u2019 Jupiter had accompanied me. He had evidently noticed my distracted manner for several weeks and took particular care not to leave me alone. But the next day, rising very early, I managed to slip away from him and went into the hills to search for the tree. After much effort, I found it. When I returned that night, my valet offered to flog me. As for the rest of the adventure, I believe you know it as well as I do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI suppose,\u201d said I, \u201cthat you missed the spot on your first attempt at digging because of Jupiter\u2019s stupidity in allowing the bug to fall through the skull\u2019s right eye instead of its left.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly. This mistake shifted the \u2018shot\u2019\u2014that is, the position of the peg nearest the tree\u2014by about two and a half inches. If the treasure had been directly beneath \u2018the shot,\u2019 the error would have mattered little; but \u2018the shot\u2019 and the nearest point of the tree served only as two points from which to establish a line of direction. Naturally, the error, however slight at first, grew as we followed the line, and by the time we had gone fifty feet, it led us completely away from the trail.\u201d\"}"
