          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f84bf48b00efdb2926281ff4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThe stupor and astonishment caused in my mind by this remarkable alteration in the state of affairs was perhaps, in the end, the portion of the adventure most difficult to explain. For the bouleversement itself was not merely natural and unavoidable, but had long been foreseen as something to be expected when I reached that precise stage of my voyage at which the planet\u2019s attraction would be overtaken by that of the satellite\u2014or, more exactly, when the balloon\u2019s gravitation toward the earth became weaker than its gravitation toward the moon. Certainly, I rose from a deep sleep, all my senses bewildered, to witness a most startling phenomenon, one which, though anticipated, had not been expected at that particular instant. The revolution must, naturally, have occurred smoothly and gradually; and it is far from certain that, even had I been awake when it happened, I would have detected it through any inward sign of inversion\u2014that is, through any discomfort or disturbance either in my person or in my apparatus.\\n\\n\u201cIt is scarcely necessary to say that, once I had properly understood my situation and emerged from the terror that had seized every faculty of my soul, my attention was first entirely occupied by the moon\u2019s general physical appearance. It stretched beneath me like a chart\u2014and though I believed it still to be at a considerable distance, the markings of its surface appeared to my sight with a striking and wholly inexplicable sharpness. The complete lack of ocean or sea, and indeed of any lake, river, or water of any kind, struck me at once as the most extraordinary feature of its geological condition. Yet, oddly enough, I saw immense level tracts of a distinctly alluvial character, while much the greater part of the visible hemisphere was covered by countless volcanic mountains, conical in form, and seeming more artificial than naturally formed. The tallest of these rises no more than three and three-quarter miles in perpendicular height; but a map of the volcanic regions of the Campi Phlegraei would give Your Excellencies a more accurate notion of their general surface than any inadequate description I might undertake. Most of them were plainly erupting, and made me dreadfully aware of their violence and strength through the repeated thunder of the misnamed meteoric stones, which now shot upward past the balloon with increasingly alarming frequency.\u201d\"}"
