          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5a8d46ddbbb8eef59d4718b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He couldn\u2019t help it. And it drove him mad to think that Becky Thatcher had not once suspected he was even alive. But she had noticed him after all; she knew she was winning their battle, too, and was pleased to see him suffer as she had suffered.\\n\\nAmy\u2019s cheerful chatter became unbearable. Tom hinted that he had things to attend to, things that had to be done, and that time was running short. But it was useless\u2014the girl kept chirping away. Tom thought, \u201cOh, damn her, am I ever going to get rid of her?\u201d At last he had to go attend to those things\u2014and she said innocently that she would be \u201caround\u201d when school let out. He hurried away, hating her for it.\\n\\n\u201cAny other boy!\u201d Tom thought, grinding his teeth. \u201cAny boy in the whole town except that Saint Louis smart-aleck who thinks he dresses so well and is some kind of aristocrat! Oh, all right, I beat you the first day you ever saw this town, mister, and I\u2019ll beat you again! Just wait until I catch you alone! I\u2019ll just take and\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe went through the motions of beating an imaginary boy\u2014punching the air, kicking, and gouging. \u201cOh, you do, do you? You\u2019re yelling enough, are you? Now then, let that teach you!\u201d And the imaginary beating ended to his satisfaction.\\n\\nTom ran home at noon. His conscience could not endure any more of Amy\u2019s grateful happiness, and his jealousy could bear no more of the other misery. Becky went on examining pictures with Alfred, but as the minutes passed and Tom did not come to suffer, her triumph began to fade and she lost interest; seriousness and distraction followed, then sadness. Two or three times she raised her head at the sound of footsteps, but each time it was a false hope; Tom did not come. At last she became completely miserable and wished she had not carried things so far. When poor Alfred, seeing that he was losing her without knowing why, kept exclaiming, \u201cOh, here\u2019s a funny one! Look at this!\u201d she finally lost patience and said, \u201cOh, don\u2019t bother me! I don\u2019t care about them!\u201d Then she burst into tears, stood up, and walked away.\\n\\nAlfred followed close behind and was about to try to comfort her, but she said:\\n\\n\u201cGo away and leave me alone, can\u2019t you? I hate you!\u201d\\n\\nThe boy stopped, wondering what he could have done\u2014for she had said she would look at pictures throughout the noon break\u2014and she walked on, crying. Then Alfred went thoughtfully into the deserted schoolhouse. He was humiliated and angry. He easily guessed the truth: the girl had simply used him as a means of venting her spite against Tom Sawyer.\"}"
