          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a43563731729edd47f1f17f0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I thanked him and apologised. He replied, \u201cNot at all,\u201d and continued.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham was by this time an heiress, and you may imagine that she was watched over as a splendid match. Her half-brother had once more abundant means, but through debts and fresh extravagance he squandered them terribly again. The disagreements between him and her were even greater than those between him and his father, and it is believed that he bore her a deep and deadly resentment, thinking she had helped provoke their father\u2019s anger. Now I come to the cruel part of the story,\u2014pausing only, my dear Handel, to observe that a dinner-napkin cannot be put into a tumbler.\u201d\\n\\nWhy I was attempting to force mine into my tumbler, I am entirely unable to explain. I only know that I found myself, with perseverance deserving a far better purpose, exerting myself most vigorously to squeeze it into that space. Once more I thanked him and apologised, and once more he answered in the most cheerful fashion, \u201cNot at all, I am sure!\u201d and went on.\\n\\n\u201cThere appeared on the scene\u2014at the races, perhaps, or the public balls, or wherever you choose\u2014a certain man who paid his addresses to Miss Havisham. I never saw him (for this occurred five-and-twenty years ago, before you and I were, Handel), but I have heard my father say that he was a flashy man, and just the sort for the purpose. Yet my father insists most emphatically that he was not, without ignorance or prejudice, to be mistaken for a gentleman; for it is one of his principles that no man who was not a true gentleman at heart has ever, since the beginning of the world, been a true gentleman in manner. He says that no polish can conceal the grain of wood; and that the more polish applied, the more plainly the grain will show. Well! This man followed Miss Havisham closely and declared himself devoted to her. I believe she had displayed little susceptibility before then; but all she possessed certainly appeared at that time, and she loved him passionately. There is no question that she worshipped him completely. He exploited her affection so methodically that he obtained large sums of money from her, and persuaded her to buy her brother\u2019s share in the brewery (which their father had weakly left to him) at an enormous price, claiming that once he became her husband, he would have to possess and manage the whole concern. Your guardian was not then among Miss Havisham\u2019s advisers, and she was too proud and too deeply in love to accept advice from anyone.\u201d\"}"
