          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d05095ede16ccbc7f34288a3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut, in truth, once an ascent has been made to any particular altitude, the quantity of ponderable air passed through in rising still farther is by no means proportional to the extra height gained (as what has already been said clearly shows), but follows a continually diminishing ratio. It is therefore plain that, however high we ascend, we cannot, strictly speaking, reach a boundary beyond which no atmosphere exists. It must be present, I reasoned, though perhaps in a condition of infinite rarefaction.\\n\\n\u201cOn the other hand, I knew that arguments had been advanced to establish the existence of a genuine and fixed limit to the atmosphere, beyond which there is no air at all. Yet one circumstance, overlooked by those who maintain such a boundary, appeared to me, though not an absolute disproof of their belief, nevertheless deserving the most serious examination. When the intervals between Encke\u2019s comet\u2019s successive returns to perihelion are compared, after the disturbances caused by planetary attractions have been allowed for with the greatest precision, it is found that the periods are gradually shortening; in other words, the major axis of the comet\u2019s ellipse is slowly but steadily decreasing. Now this is exactly what should occur if the comet encountered resistance from an exceedingly rare ethereal medium filling the regions of its orbit. Such a medium, by checking the comet\u2019s speed, must plainly increase its centripetal force by diminishing its centrifugal force. Thus the sun\u2019s attraction would continually gain strength, and the comet would be drawn closer at each revolution. Indeed, there is no other explanation for the change in question. But again:\u2014The actual diameter of this comet\u2019s nebulosity is seen to contract rapidly as it nears the sun, and to expand just as rapidly as it travels away toward its aphelion. Was I not justified, with M. Valz, in supposing that this apparent condensation of volume arose from compression by the same ethereal medium mentioned before, which becomes denser in proportion to its nearness to the sun? The lenticular phenomenon, also known as the zodiacal light, likewise deserved attention. This radiance, so conspicuous in the tropics and not to be confused with any meteoric glow, rises obliquely from the horizon and generally follows the direction of the sun\u2019s equator. It seemed to me plainly to be a rare atmosphere extending outward from the sun, at least beyond Venus\u2019s orbit, and, I believed, indefinitely farther. Indeed, I could not imagine this medium restricted to the comet\u2019s elliptical path or to the sun\u2019s immediate vicinity.\u201d\"}"
