{"request_id":"381c9aa7de7794f8e7e52c84","rewritten_text":"But this thought, which sustained me at the beginning of my career, now serves only to drive me deeper into the dust. All my speculations and hopes have come to nothing, and like the archangel who aspired to omnipotence, I am chained in an eternal hell. My imagination was vivid, while my powers of analysis and application were intense; through the union of these qualities, I conceived the idea and carried out the creation of a man. Even now I cannot recall without passion the dreams I had while the work remained unfinished. In my thoughts I walked in heaven, sometimes exulting in my powers, sometimes burning with visions of their effects. From childhood I had been filled with high hopes and lofty ambition; but how far I have fallen! Oh, my friend, if you had known me as I once was, you would not recognise me in this degraded state. Despondency rarely touched my heart; a great destiny seemed to carry me forward, until I fell—never, never to rise again.”

Must I then lose this admirable being? I have longed for a friend; I have sought someone who would understand and love me. Behold, on these deserted seas I have found such a person, but I fear I have gained him only to learn his worth and lose him. I would reconcile him to life, but he rejects the thought.

“I thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your kind intentions toward so miserable a wretch; but when you speak of new bonds and fresh affections, do you think that any can replace those who are gone? Can any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman become another Elizabeth? Even when the affections are not deeply stirred by some superior excellence, the companions of our childhood always hold a certain power over our minds that almost no later friend can acquire. They know our childhood dispositions, which, however they may later be changed, are never entirely erased; and they can judge our actions with greater certainty concerning the honesty of our motives. A sister or brother can never, unless such signs appeared early, suspect the other of fraud or deceit, while another friend, however devoted, may, despite himself, be viewed with suspicion. But I had friends dear to me not only through habit and shared memories, but for their own virtues; and wherever I may be, the gentle voice of my Elizabeth and the conversation of Clerval will always echo in my ears. They are dead, and only one feeling, in such solitude, can persuade me to preserve my life. If I were engaged in some noble undertaking or design, filled with broad usefulness to my fellow creatures, then I could live to complete it."}

