          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0dbb914e9ee180807fc75a55\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And he always pretended that Peter had written the things for him; but that was a lie: he got every single one of them from that young fool we had canoed up to the steamboat.\\n\\nThen Mary Jane brought out the letter her father had left, and the king read it aloud and cried over it. It left the house and three thousand dollars in gold to the girls; and it left the tanyard, which was doing good business, along with some other houses and land worth about seven thousand, and three thousand dollars in gold to Harvey and William, and said where the six thousand in cash was hidden in the cellar. So the two frauds said they would go and bring it up, to make everything proper and aboveboard, and told me to come with a candle. We shut the cellar door behind us, and when they found the bag, they poured it out onto the floor. It was a beautiful sight, all those yellow boys. My, how the king\u2019s eyes shone! He slapped the duke on the shoulder and said:\\n\\n\u201cOh, this is something, isn\u2019t it? Oh, no doubt about it! Why, Bilji, it beats the Nonesuch, doesn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nThe duke agreed that it did. They handled the gold pieces, sifted them through their fingers, and let them jingle onto the floor; and the king said:\\n\\n\u201cThere\u2019s no use talking; being brothers to a rich dead man and representatives of foreign heirs who\u2019ve been left behind is the life for you and me, Bilge. This is what comes of trusting Providence. It\u2019s the best way in the long run. I\u2019ve tried them all, and there isn\u2019t any better way.\u201d\\n\\nMost people would have been satisfied with the pile and accepted it on trust; but no, they had to count it. So they counted it, and it came out four hundred and fifteen dollars short. The king said:\\n\\n\u201cDamn him, I wonder what he did with those four hundred and fifteen dollars?\u201d\\n\\nThey worried over that for a while and searched everywhere for it. Then the duke said:\\n\\n\u201cWell, he was a very sick man, and likely he made a mistake\u2014I reckon that\u2019s how it was. The best thing is to let it go and keep quiet about it. We can afford it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, nonsense, yes, we can afford it. I don\u2019t care anything about that\u2014it\u2019s the count I\u2019m thinking about. We want to be perfectly honest and open and aboveboard here, you know. We want to carry this money upstairs and count it in front of everybody\u2014then there won\u2019t be anything suspicious. But when the dead man says there\u2019s six thousand dollars, you know, we don\u2019t want to\u2014\u201d\"}"
