          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c2ef8d5cfaadef0abb70bd43\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They were bringing along a very fine-looking old gentleman, and a good-looking younger man with his right arm in a sling. And, my goodness, how the people shouted and laughed and kept it up. But I couldn\u2019t see any joke in it, and I figured it would take some effort for the duke and the king to find one either. I expected them to turn pale. But no, not a trace of paleness did they show. The duke never let on that he suspected anything, but simply went around babbling happily and contentedly, like a jug gurgling out buttermilk; and as for the king, he only gazed sorrowfully at the newcomers, as if the thought that there could be such frauds and scoundrels in the world gave him a stomachache right in his heart. Oh, he did it admirably. Many of the leading people gathered around the king, to show him they were on his side. The old gentleman who had just arrived looked completely bewildered. Before long he began to speak, and I saw at once that he pronounced his words like an Englishman\u2014not in the king\u2019s manner, though the king\u2019s imitation was fairly good. I can\u2019t reproduce the old gentleman\u2019s words or his accent, but he turned to the crowd and said, more or less like this:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a surprise I wasn\u2019t expecting; and I\u2019ll admit, honestly and frankly, that I\u2019m not very well prepared to meet it or answer it, because my brother and I have had misfortunes. He has broken his arm, and our baggage was mistakenly put off at a town upriver during the night. I am Peter Wilks\u2019s brother Harvey, and this is his brother William, who can neither hear nor speak\u2014and can\u2019t even make useful signs now, since he has only one hand to make them with. We are who we claim to be; and in a day or two, when I recover the baggage, I can prove it. But until then I won\u2019t say anything more. I\u2019ll go to the hotel and wait.\u201d\\n\\nSo he and the new mute started off; and the king laughed and blurted out:\\n\\n\u201cBroken his arm\u2014_very_ likely, _isn\u2019t_ it?\u2014and very convenient, too, for a fraud who has to make signs and hasn\u2019t learned how. Lost their baggage! That\u2019s _excellent_!\u2014and mighty ingenious\u2014under the _circumstances!_\u201d\\n\\nSo he laughed again, and everybody else did too, except three or four, or perhaps half a dozen. One was the doctor; another was a sharp-looking gentleman with an old-fashioned carpetbag made of carpet material, who had just come off the steamboat and was speaking to him in a low voice, glancing toward the king now and then and nodding their heads\u2014it was Levi Bell, the lawyer who had gone up to Louisville; and another was a large, rough, powerful man who had come along and listened to everything the old gentleman said, and was listening to the king now.\"}"
