          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f45c7204b026edc649c09c24\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGet up! What are you doing?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and looked around, trying to figure out where I was. The sun was already up, and I had been sleeping soundly. Pap was standing over me, looking sour and sick as well. He said:\\n\\n\u201cWhat are you doing with this gun?\u201d\\n\\nI figured he didn\u2019t remember anything about what he had been doing, so I said:\\n\\n\u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was lying in wait for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you wake me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I tried, but I couldn\u2019t; I couldn\u2019t move you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, then. Don\u2019t stand there talking all day. Get out and see whether there\u2019s a fish on the lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll be along in a minute.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I hurried out along the riverbank. I noticed pieces of branches and other things floating downstream, with bits of bark scattered among them, so I knew the river had begun to rise. I figured I would have a fine time now if I were over in town. The June rise had always been lucky for me, because as soon as it began, cordwood came floating down, along with pieces of log rafts\u2014sometimes a dozen logs together. All you had to do was catch them and sell them to the wood yards and the sawmill.\\n\\nI went up the bank, keeping one eye out for Pap and the other on whatever the rising water might bring along. Then, all at once, a canoe came drifting by\u2014a beautiful one, too, about thirteen or fourteen feet long, riding high like a duck. I plunged headfirst from the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and swam toward it. I expected someone would be lying in it, because people often did that to fool others; when a man had pulled a skiff nearly within reach, they would sit up and laugh at him. But it wasn\u2019t so this time. It really was a drift canoe, and I climbed in and paddled it ashore. I thought, the old man will be glad when he sees this\u2014it\u2019s worth ten dollars. But when I reached shore, Pap was still nowhere in sight. As I was pushing it into a little creek like a gully, thickly covered with vines and willows, another idea came to me: I decided to hide it well, and then, instead of taking to the woods when I ran away, I would go downriver about fifty miles and settle in one place for good, so I wouldn\u2019t have such a hard time traveling on foot.\\n\\nIt was fairly close to the shanty, and I thought I heard the old man coming the whole time. But I got it hidden, then stepped out and looked around a clump of willows. There was the old man a little way down the path, taking aim at a bird with his gun.\"}"
