          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d28309f5ca38067e9f30bb70\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These were the events that weighed on Felix\u2019s heart and made him, when I first saw him, the most miserable member of his family. He could have endured poverty, and while it was the reward of his virtue, he even took pride in it. But the Turk\u2019s ingratitude and the loss of his beloved Safie were sorrows far more bitter and impossible to repair. The Arabian\u2019s arrival now brought new life to his spirit.\\n\\n\u201cWhen news reached Leghorn that Felix had lost his wealth and rank, the merchant ordered his daughter to forget her lover and prepare to return to her native country. Safie\u2019s generous nature was outraged by this command. She tried to argue with her father, but he angrily left her, repeating his harsh order.\\n\\n\u201cA few days later, the Turk entered his daughter\u2019s room and hurriedly told her that he had reason to believe his stay in Leghorn had been discovered and that he would soon be handed over to the French government. He had therefore hired a ship to take him to Constantinople, and he would sail within a few hours. He planned to leave his daughter in the care of a trusted servant, while she followed later with most of his property, which had not yet reached Leghorn.\\n\\n\u201cWhen she was alone, Safie considered what she ought to do in this crisis. Living in Turkey was hateful to her; both her religion and her feelings opposed it. From some of her father\u2019s papers that came into her hands, she learned of her lover\u2019s exile and discovered where he was living. She hesitated for some time, but at last made her decision. Taking some jewels that belonged to her and a sum of money, she left Italy with an attendant, a native of Leghorn who understood the common language of Turkey, and set out for Germany.\\n\\n\u201cShe reached a town about twenty leagues from De Lacey\u2019s cottage safely, but there her attendant became dangerously ill. Safie cared for her with complete devotion, but the poor girl died, leaving the Arabian alone, unable to speak the country\u2019s language and entirely unfamiliar with the ways of the world. Still, she came into good hands. The Italian had mentioned the place they were traveling to, and after her death, the woman whose house they had stayed in made sure that Safie reached her lover\u2019s cottage safely.\u201d\"}"
