{"request_id":"387bfc4cb0c1350e0d3bc7b6","rewritten_text":"They were her life—dearer than any place consecrated to love, dearer than everything else the earth contained. The boys heard of our departure with childish delight; Clara asked whether we were going to Athens. “It is possible,” I replied, and her face lit up with pleasure. There she would see her parents’ tomb and the land filled with memories of her father’s glory. In silence, but without pause, she had dwelt on these scenes. Their memory had changed her childish gaiety into seriousness and filled her with lofty, restless thoughts.

There were many dear friends we could not leave behind, humble though they were. There was the spirited, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter; Alfred’s dog; and a pet eagle whose sight had grown dim with age. Yet making this list of favourites to take with us brought grief at our heavy losses and a deep sigh for all we had to leave behind. Tears filled Idris’s eyes, while Alfred and Evelyn brought first a favourite rose tree, then a beautifully carved marble vase, insisting that these must go, and lamenting that we could not take the castle and forest, the deer and birds, and every familiar, cherished object with us. “Fond, foolish ones,” I said, “we have forever lost treasures far more precious than these, and we abandon them to preserve treasures that, beside them, are nothing. Let us never forget our purpose and our hope; they will form an unbreakable barrier against the flood of our grief over trifles.”

The children were easily distracted and soon returned to imagining future amusements. Idris had vanished. She had gone to conceal her weakness; leaving the castle, she had descended into the little park and sought solitude so that she could give way to her tears. I found her clinging to an old oak, pressing its rough trunk with her flushed lips as tears poured down her face, while her sobs and broken cries could not be restrained. With overwhelming grief, I saw this beloved one of my heart lost in sorrow. I drew her toward me, and when she felt my kisses on her eyelids and my arms around her, she returned to an awareness of what remained to her. “You are very kind not to reproach me,” she said. “I weep, and a bitter, unbearable sorrow tears at my heart. And yet I am happy; mothers mourn their children, wives lose their husbands, while you and my children remain to me.”"}

