{"request_id":"0fff7a393a4a4d6696b61d74","rewritten_text":"You see, I had my handkerchief in my helmet, along with a few other things; but it was the kind of helmet you couldn’t remove by yourself. That hadn’t occurred to me when I put it there; in fact, I didn’t know it. I had assumed it would be especially convenient. And now the thought of it being there, so close and handy yet impossible to reach, made everything worse and harder to endure. Yes, what you can’t have is usually what you want most; everyone has noticed that. Well, it took my mind away from everything else—completely away—and fixed it on my helmet; mile after mile, it stayed there, imagining the handkerchief, picturing the handkerchief. It was bitter and maddening to have salty sweat trickling into my eyes while I couldn’t reach it. It sounds like a small thing on paper, but it was no small thing at all; it was the most genuine kind of misery. I wouldn’t say so if it weren’t true. I decided that next time I would carry a reticule, no matter how it looked or what people said. Of course, those iron gentlemen of the Round Table would consider it scandalous and perhaps raise hell over it, but as for me, comfort first and style afterward. So we jogged on, and now and then crossed a stretch of dust that rose in clouds, filled my nose, and made me sneeze and cry; and naturally I said things I shouldn’t have said—I don’t deny it. I’m no better than anyone else.

We couldn’t seem to meet anyone in that lonely Britain, not even an ogre; and in the mood I was in then, that was fortunate for the ogre—an ogre with a handkerchief, that is. Most knights would have thought only of taking his armor; but if I could get his bandanna, he could keep his hardware, as far as I was concerned.

Meanwhile, it was growing hotter and hotter inside. The sun was beating down, warming the iron more and more every moment. When you’re hot like that, every little thing irritates you. At a trot, I rattled like a crate of dishes, and that bothered me; besides, I couldn’t stand the shield slapping and banging, first against my chest, then around my back. If I slowed to a walk, my joints creaked and screeched in the tiresome way a wheelbarrow does; and since we made no breeze at that pace, I was liable to be roasted in that oven. Besides, the more slowly you went, the more heavily the iron settled onto you, and the more tons you seemed to weigh with every passing minute."}

