          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"47e957d2811dc962d34a444e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWell, well, Mr. Bumble,\u201d he said at last, \u201cthere\u2019s no denying that, since the new feeding system was introduced, the coffins are somewhat narrower and shallower than they used to be; but we must make some profit, Mr. Bumble. Properly seasoned timber is expensive, sir; and all the iron handles come by canal from Birmingham.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cevery trade has its disadvantages. A reasonable profit is, of course, permissible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course,\u201d replied the undertaker; \u201cand if I fail to profit on one particular item, why, I make it up over time, you see\u2014he! he! he!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly,\u201d said Mr. Bumble.\\n\\n\u201cThough I must say,\u201d continued the undertaker, returning to the line of thought the beadle had interrupted, \u201cthough I must say, Mr. Bumble, that I face one very serious disadvantage: all the stout people die the quickest. Those who have been better off, and have paid rates for many years, are the first to collapse when they enter the workhouse; and let me tell you, Mr. Bumble, that three or four inches beyond one\u2019s estimate makes a considerable hole in one\u2019s profits\u2014especially when one has a family to support, sir.\u201d\\n\\nAs Mr. Sowerberry said this with the proper indignation of a mistreated man, and as Mr. Bumble felt that it seemed to cast a reflection on the parish\u2019s honour, the latter thought it best to change the subject. Oliver Twist was foremost in his thoughts, so he made him the topic.\\n\\n\u201cBy the way,\u201d said Mr. Bumble, \u201cyou don\u2019t happen to know anyone who wants a boy, do you? A parish apprentice, who is currently a dead weight; a millstone, as I might say, around the parish\u2019s neck? Generous terms, Mr. Sowerberry, generous terms?\u201d As Mr. Bumble spoke, he raised his cane to the notice above him and gave three distinct taps on the words \u201cfive pounds,\u201d printed there in enormous Roman capitals.\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens!\u201d said the undertaker, taking Mr. Bumble by the gilt-edged lapel of his official coat. \u201cThat is exactly what I wanted to speak to you about. You know\u2014dear me, what an elegant button this is, Mr. Bumble! I never noticed it before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I think it is rather handsome,\u201d said the beadle, looking proudly down at the large brass buttons adorning his coat. \u201cThe design is the same as the parish seal\u2014the Good Samaritan tending the sick and injured man. The board gave it to me on New Year\u2019s morning, Mr. Sowerberry. I remember wearing it for the first time when I attended the inquest on that impoverished tradesman who died in a doorway at midnight.\u201d\"}"
