          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"add6fd25b4ac94a140e175a0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 7. I rose early and, to my great joy, finally saw what there could be no doubt was the North Pole itself. It lay there beyond question, directly beneath my feet; but, alas, I had risen to such an enormous height that nothing could be made out with precision. Indeed, judging from the sequence of figures recording my altitude at different times between six in the morning on April 2 and twenty minutes before nine that same morning, when the barometer ran down, it might reasonably be concluded that, at four o\u2019clock on the morning of April 7, the balloon had reached a height of at least 7,254 miles above sea level. This elevation may seem immense, but the estimate on which it is based almost certainly fell far short of the truth. In any event, I clearly saw the entire major diameter of the earth; the whole northern hemisphere lay beneath me like an orthographic map, and the equator itself formed the boundary of my horizon. Your Excellencies may readily imagine, however, that the unexplored regions within the Arctic Circle, though directly below me and therefore seen without any apparent foreshortening, were still too small in themselves and too distant from my viewpoint to permit close examination. Nevertheless, what could be seen was strange and compelling. Northward from that enormous rim mentioned earlier\u2014which, with some qualification, may be called the limit of human discovery in these regions\u2014one continuous, or nearly continuous, sheet of ice extended onward. For the first few degrees, its surface was noticeably flattened; farther on, it sank into a level plane; and at last, becoming distinctly concave, it ended at the Pole itself in a sharply defined circular center. Seen from the balloon, this center subtended an angle of about sixty-five seconds. Its dark color varied in intensity, but it was always darker than any other point on the visible hemisphere, sometimes deepening into complete, impenetrable blackness. Beyond this, little could be determined. By noon, the circular center had greatly diminished in circumference, and by seven in the evening I had lost sight of it altogether. The balloon passed over the western edge of the ice and drifted rapidly away toward the equator.\\n\\n\u201cApril 8. I noticed a clear reduction in the earth\u2019s apparent diameter, along with a significant change in its general color and appearance.\"}"
