{"request_id":"ed93c5e6c3eb7ebbf6bf2895","rewritten_text":"Perdita’s name was not mentioned, yet I could not doubt that his love for her caused the uncertainty he showed. And who was more worthy of love than my noble-minded sister? Who deserved the hand of this self-important king more than she, whose glance belonged to a queen of nations? She loved him as he loved her, although disappointment had cooled her passion, while ambition struggled strongly against his.

We went together to the House that evening. Although Raymond knew that his plans and hopes would be discussed and decided in the expected debate, he was cheerful and careless. As we entered the coffee-room, a hum like that of ten thousand swarming beehives overwhelmed us. Groups of politicians stood together, their brows anxious and their voices loud or grave. The aristocratic party—the richest and most influential men in England—seemed less disturbed than the others, since the question would be debated without their interference. Near the fire stood Ryland and his supporters. Ryland was of obscure birth but possessed immense wealth, inherited from his father, who had been a manufacturer. As a young man, he had witnessed the king’s abdication and the union of the two Houses, Lords and Commons. He had supported these popular advances, and strengthening them had been the work of his life. Since then, the influence of the landed proprietors had grown. At first Ryland was not sorry to see Lord Raymond’s schemes drawing away many of his opponents’ supporters. But matters had now gone too far. The poorer nobles welcomed the return of sovereignty as an event that would restore their lost power and rights. The nearly dead spirit of royalty stirred again in people’s minds, and they, willing slaves and self-appointed subjects, were ready to bow their necks beneath the yoke. A few upright and manly spirits still remained, pillars of the state; but the word republic had become dull to ordinary ears, and many—whether they were the majority, events would show—longed for royalty’s glitter and display. Ryland was stirred to resist. He declared that his patience alone had allowed this party to grow, but the time for tolerance had ended, and with one sweep of his arm he would clear away the webs blinding his countrymen.

When Raymond entered the coffee-room, his friends greeted him almost with a shout. They gathered around him, counted their numbers, and explained why they expected to gain the support of certain members who had not yet declared themselves."}

