          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"355f449a6d7810ac879b2e04\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After I had sent them in, I could not stay away from the places where they were, but felt that they were more hopeful and less desperate when I was near them. In this unreasonable restlessness and distress of mind, I would wander the streets in the evening, passing those offices and houses where I had left the petitions. Even now, the dreary western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night, with their rows of severe, shuttered mansions and their long lines of lamps, seem melancholy to me because of that association.\\n\\nThe daily visits I was allowed to make him were shorter now, and he was watched more closely. Seeing, or imagining, that I was suspected of intending to carry poison to him, I asked to be searched before sitting down at his bedside, and told the officer who was always there that I was willing to do anything that would convince him of the purity of my intentions. No one was harsh with him or with me. There was a duty to perform, and it was performed, but not cruelly. The officer always assured me that he was worse, and some of the other sick prisoners in the room, along with other prisoners who attended them as nurses\u2014criminals, but not incapable of kindness, thank God!\u2014always gave the same report.\\n\\nAs the days passed, I noticed more and more that he would lie peacefully staring at the white ceiling, with no light in his face until some word of mine brightened it for a moment, after which it would fade again. Sometimes he was almost, or entirely, unable to speak; then he would answer me with slight pressures of my hand, and I gradually came to understand him very well.\\n\\nThe days had reached ten when I saw a greater change in him than ever before. His eyes were turned toward the door, and they lit up when I entered.\\n\\n\u201cDear boy,\u201d he said as I sat down beside his bed, \u201cI thought you were late. But I knew you couldn\u2019t be.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is exactly the right time,\u201d I said. \u201cI waited for it at the gate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou always wait at the gate, don\u2019t you, dear boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. So I won\u2019t lose a moment of the time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear boy, thank you. God bless you! You\u2019ve never deserted me, dear boy.\u201d\\n\\nI pressed his hand in silence, for I could not forget that I had once intended to desert him.\\n\\n\u201cAnd best of all,\u201d he said, \u201cyou\u2019ve been more comfortable with me since I was under a dark cloud than when the sun was shining.\u201d\"}"
