          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a5589e60831211e9f13b29eb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During his brief stay this time, Emma had hardly seen him; but she had seen enough to feel that their first meeting was over, and to notice that the mixture of irritation and pride now showing in his manner had not improved him. In fact, she was beginning to wonder how she had ever found him pleasing at all. Seeing him was so closely tied to unpleasant feelings that, except as a moral punishment, a lesson, and a useful humiliation for her own mind, she would have been glad to know she would never see him again. She wished him well; but he caused her pain, and his welfare twenty miles away would have given her the greatest satisfaction.\\n\\nHis continued residence in Highbury, however, would certainly be made less painful by his marriage. It would prevent many foolish worries and smooth over many awkward situations. A Mrs. Elton would excuse any change in their relations; their former intimacy could fade without comment. It would almost be like beginning their polite acquaintance again.\\n\\nEmma thought very little of the woman herself. She was no doubt good enough for Mr. Elton, accomplished enough for Highbury, and handsome enough\u2014though she would probably look plain beside Harriet. As for her family connections, Emma felt completely at ease. After all his proud claims and his contempt for Harriet, he had done nothing remarkable. On that point, the truth seemed possible to discover. What she was might be uncertain, but who she was could be found out; and apart from the \u00a310,000, she did not seem at all superior to Harriet. She had no important name, ancestry, or connections. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol merchant, as he must be called; but since the profits of his business seemed so modest, it was fair to guess that the standing of his trade had been modest too. She had spent part of every winter in Bath, but Bristol was her home\u2014the very center of Bristol. Her parents had died several years earlier, but an uncle remained, in the legal profession; nothing more clearly respectable was said of him than that he was in the legal profession. The daughter had lived with him. Emma guessed that he was some attorney\u2019s overworked clerk, too dull to advance. All the grandeur of the family connection seemed to depend on the older sister, who was very well married to a gentleman in a great way of business near Bristol, and who owned two carriages. That was the end of the story; that was Miss Hawkins\u2019s glory.\"}"
