{"request_id":"7ebcd33f04e12dab7d33c84a","rewritten_text":"“I reasoned as follows, after the fact. The murderers had escaped through one of these windows. If so, they could not have fastened the sashes again from inside, yet that was how they had been found. This obvious point had stopped the police from examining the matter further. Still, the sashes were fastened. They must, therefore, have been able to fasten themselves. There was no other possible conclusion. I went to the unobstructed window, pulled out the nail with some difficulty, and tried to raise the sash. As I had expected, it would not move. There must be a hidden spring. This confirmed my idea and showed that my reasoning was sound, though the mystery of the nails remained. A careful search soon revealed the concealed spring. I pressed it and, satisfied, did not raise the sash.

“I replaced the nail and examined it closely. Someone leaving through this window might have closed it again, allowing the spring to catch, but could not have replaced the nail. The conclusion was clear, and it narrowed my investigation once more. The assassins must have escaped through the other window. If the springs on both sashes were alike, as seemed likely, there had to be some difference between the nails, or at least in the way they were fixed. I climbed onto the bedstead’s sacking and carefully examined the second window above the headboard. Reaching behind the board, I quickly found and pressed the spring. As I had expected, it was identical to the one in the other window. I then examined the nail. It was just as strong as the first and seemed to be fixed in the same way, driven almost to its head.

“You may think I was confused, but if so, you have misunderstood my reasoning. To use a sporting expression, I had not once been ‘at fault.’ I had never lost the trail. Every link in the chain was sound. I had followed the secret to its final result—and that result was the nail. In every way it looked like the nail in the other window; but that fact, however convincing it seemed, meant nothing beside the fact that my clue ended there. ‘There must be something wrong,’ I said, ‘with the nail.’ I touched it, and the head, along with about a quarter of an inch of the shaft, came away in my fingers.”"}

