          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"980cea26bbb7df2a12377247\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut,\u201d I said, handing the slip back to him, \u201cI am as much in the dark as ever. If all the jewels of Golconda were waiting for me to solve this enigma, I am quite certain I could not earn them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet,\u201d said Legrand, \u201cthe solution is nowhere near as difficult as a first hurried examination of the characters might lead you to believe. These characters, as anyone might readily guess, form a cipher\u2014that is, they convey a meaning. But, knowing what we do of Kidd, I could not imagine him capable of constructing one of the more abstruse cryptographs. I immediately decided that this was a simple kind\u2014one that would nevertheless seem, to the sailor\u2019s crude intellect, completely impossible to solve without the key.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you actually solved it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEasily; I have solved others ten thousand times more obscure. Circumstances, along with a certain inclination of mind, have led me to take an interest in such riddles, and it may well be doubted whether human ingenuity can devise an enigma that human ingenuity cannot, with proper application, unravel. In fact, once I had established a sequence of connected, legible characters, I scarcely gave another thought to the mere difficulty of discovering their meaning.\\n\\n\u201cIn this case\u2014as, indeed, in every case of secret writing\u2014the first question concerns the cipher\u2019s language; for the principles of solution, particularly with the simpler ciphers, depend upon and vary according to the character of the particular language. In general, there is no choice but to test, guided by probability, every language known to the person attempting the solution, until the correct one is found. But with the cipher before us, the signature removed all difficulty. The pun on the word \u2018Kidd\u2019 can be understood in no language but English. Without that clue, I would have begun with Spanish and French, the languages in which a secret of this kind would most naturally have been written by a pirate of the Spanish Main. As it was, I assumed the cryptograph was English.\\n\\n\u201cYou will notice that there are no spaces between the words. Had there been spaces, the task would have been comparatively easy. In that case, I would have begun by comparing and analyzing the shorter words; and if a one-letter word had appeared, as is most likely\u2014\u2018a\u2019 or \u2018I,\u2019 for example\u2014I would have considered the solution certain. But since there were no divisions, my first step was to determine the most common letters, as well as the rarest.\u201d\"}"
