          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bd91cf8f85642fecd47f83c4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Tom woke that morning, he wondered where he was. He sat up, rubbed his eyes, and glanced around. Then he understood. It was the cool gray dawn, and a delicious feeling of rest and peace lay in the deep, all-encompassing stillness of the woods. Not a leaf moved; no sound intruded upon great Nature\u2019s meditation. Dewdrops glittered on the leaves and grasses. A white coating of ashes covered the fire, while a thin blue thread of smoke rose straight upward. Joe and Huck were still asleep.\\n\\nNow, far off in the forest, one bird called; another replied; presently the tapping of a woodpecker could be heard. Little by little the cool, dim gray of morning brightened, and sounds gradually increased as life revealed itself. The wonder of Nature casting off sleep and beginning its work opened before the thoughtful boy. A small green worm crawled across a dewy leaf, repeatedly raising two-thirds of its body into the air and \u201csniffing around,\u201d then moving on again\u2014for, Tom declared, it was measuring. When the worm drew near him, he sat motionless as a stone, his hopes rising and sinking by turns as the creature continued toward him or appeared ready to go elsewhere; and when, after a painful pause with its curved body raised, it finally settled firmly upon Tom\u2019s leg and began traveling over him, his whole heart rejoiced\u2014for that meant, beyond any doubt, that he would receive a new suit of clothes, a splendid pirate uniform. Next came a procession of ants, appearing from nowhere in particular and attending to their business; one struggled bravely along, carrying a dead spider five times its own size, and hauled it directly up a tree trunk. A brown-spotted ladybug climbed the dizzy height of a blade of grass. Tom leaned close and said, \u201cLady-bug, lady-bug, fly away home, your house is on fire, your children\u2019s alone,\u201d whereupon she flew off to investigate\u2014which did not surprise the boy, since he had long known that this insect believed readily in fires and had taken advantage of its simplicity more than once. A tumblebug followed, pushing stoutly at its ball, and Tom touched it, watching as it drew its legs against its body and pretended to be dead. By then the birds were fairly celebrating. A catbird, the Northern mockingbird, landed in a tree above Tom\u2019s head and poured out imitations of her neighbors in delighted ecstasy. Then a sharp jay darted down like a flash of blue flame and perched on a twig almost within the boy\u2019s reach, tilting his head and studying the strangers with intense curiosity. A gray squirrel and a large \u201cfox\u201d squirrel came scurrying by, pausing now and then to sit upright, inspect the boys, and chatter at them, for the wild creatures had likely never seen a human being before and hardly knew whether they ought to be frightened.\"}"
