          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3f36972c7990aae704f3b8ab\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now come to my own story. There is little to tell about the early part of my life, so I will be brief. But I must spend some time on my childhood, so it may be understood how, when one hope failed, life became empty; and how, when the only affection I was allowed to cherish was destroyed, my existence seemed to end with it.\\n\\nI have said that my aunt was very different from my father. I believe that, without having the slightest trace of an evil nature, she had the coldest heart that ever beat in a human breast: it was completely incapable of affection. She took responsibility for me because she believed it was her duty; but she had lived alone for so long, undisturbed by the noise and chatter of children, that she could not bear to have me disturb her peace. She had never married, and for the last five years had lived entirely alone on an estate she had inherited through her mother, on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland. In his letters, my father had expressed the wish that she would live with me at his family home, which stood in beautiful countryside near Richmond in Yorkshire. She refused, however, and as soon as she had settled the affairs left in her care by her brother\u2019s departure, she left England and took me with her to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nThe care of me as a baby, and later until I was eight years old, was given to one of my mother\u2019s servants, who had accompanied us into retirement for that purpose. I was kept in a distant part of the house and saw my aunt only at fixed times. This happened twice a day: once around noon, when she came to my nursery, and once after dinner, when I was brought to her. She never showed me affection, and while I remained in the room she seemed constantly afraid that some childish action of mine would annoy her. My good nurse always instructed me very carefully before allowing me to enter the sitting room. The fear inspired by my aunt\u2019s cold looks and few, forced words was so great that I rarely failed to follow my nurse\u2019s lessons or broke the exemplary silence I had been taught to keep during these short visits.\\n\\nUnder my good nurse\u2019s care, I ran freely through our park and the nearby fields. As the child of the deepest love, I showed from my earliest years the greatest sensitivity of feeling. I cannot express how passionately I loved everything, even the lifeless objects around me.\"}"
