          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"15e72fd71adaed176d2c36c6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Thus I passed two years. Day followed day, and hundreds upon hundreds slipped away; they brought no visible alteration, yet a few of them gradually worked upon my mind as I moved onward toward death. I began to study more, to enter more sympathetically into the thoughts of others as revealed in books; I read history, and lost my individuality among the multitude who had lived before me. Perhaps, as the feeling of immediate suffering diminished, I became more human. Solitude, too, surrendered some of its attractions: I again began to long for sympathy; not that I was ever inclined to seek the crowd, but I wished for a single friend who would love me. You may say, perhaps, that I was slowly becoming fit to return to society. I do not believe it. The sympathy I desired had to be so pure, so free from the influence of outward circumstances, that in the world I could not avoid being disappointed by the coarse materials continually mingled even with its finest feelings. Believe me, I was then less prepared for fellowship with my fellow creatures than before. When I had left them, they had tormented me as pain and sickness might torment: something foreign to the mind, irritating it, which I wished to throw off. But now I would have desired sympathy; I would have wished to join my soul to another\u2019s, and would thereby have prepared myself for abundant draughts of disappointment and suffering; for I was tender as the sensitive plant, all nerve. I did not seek sympathy and assistance in ambition or wisdom, but sweet and mutual affection; smiles to encourage me and gentle words of consolation. I wanted one heart into which I could pour my complaints without restraint, so that, through the heavenly nature of the soil, blessed fruit might grow from such poor seed. Yet how could I find this? The love that forms the soul of friendship is a gentle spirit, seldom discovered except when two amiable beings are united from early youth, or joined by shared suffering and pursuits; it comes to some of the chosen without being sought or recognized; it falls like soft dew upon favored places, which, however barren before, become through its gracious influence fertile with every sweet plant. But when desired, it flees; it mocks the prayers of those who worship it; it will give itself, but cannot be pursued.\\n\\nI knew all this and did not go in search of sympathy; yet there, upon my lonely heath, beneath my humble roof, where desert lay all around, it came to me like a sunbeam in winter, adorning the snowdrifts even as it helped to melt them.--Alas, the sun shone upon blighted fruit; I did not revive beneath its radiance, for I was too completely ruined to feel its kindly strength.\"}"
