          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f49fa4fa6c500ca33a29e8ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With my heart pounding and afraid, though I knew not of what, I dismissed the servant, locked my door, and sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were the words it held.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Child\\n\\n\u201cI have betrayed your trust; I have sought to corrupt your mind, and have made your innocent heart familiar with the looks and language of unlawful and monstrous passion. I must atone for these crimes, and must try in some measure to make my punishment answerable to my guilt. You are, I doubt not, prepared for what I am about to declare; we must part and remain separated forever.\\n\\n\u201cI deprive you of your parent and only friend. You are cast shelterless upon the world: your hopes are destroyed; the peace and safety of your pure mind have been ruined; memory will bring before you dreadful images of guilt, and the anguish of innocent love betrayed. Yet I, who bring all this misery upon you; I, who cast you out and without remorse have stamped distrust and agony upon the heart and brow of my own child, who with devilish lightness have tried to take away her loveliness and replace it with the foul deformity of sin; I, in the overflowing torment of my heart, beg you to forgive me.\\n\\n\u201cI do not ask for your pity; you must, and do, abhor me: but pardon me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts pursue me in exile with unrelenting anger. I must never see you again; never again hear your voice; yet the gentle whispers of your forgiveness will reach me and cool the burning of my disordered brain and heart; I am certain I should feel them even in my grave. And I dare strengthen this request by telling how miserably I was betrayed into this snare of fiery anguish, and of all my efforts to free myself: indeed, if your soul were less pure and radiant, I would not try to excuse myself before you; I should fear that, if I led you to regard me with less abhorrence, you might hate vice less: but in speaking to you I feel as though I were appealing to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness, and I must strive to obtain it, or despair. I therefore implore you to hear my words, and if, among the good, guilt may in any degree be lessened by sharp agony and remorse that tears the brain as madness perhaps you may believe, though I dare not, that I have some claim to your compassion.\\n\\n\u201cI beg you to remember our first happy life upon the shores of Loch Lomond. I had returned from a weary wandering of sixteen years, during which, though I had passed through many dangers and misfortunes, my affections had remained entirely blank.\"}"
