          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a0fd9c53674c6a10ff19b94d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must leave, not knowing what my fate may be; but I shall return here, or join your party, as soon as I can. A word, a glance, will suffice to determine whether I enter your father\u2019s house this evening or never.\u201d\\n\\nA letter of this kind was not easily recovered from. Half an hour alone with her thoughts might have calmed her; but the mere ten minutes that now elapsed before she was disturbed, under all the constraints of her position, could do nothing to restore her composure. Each moment instead increased her agitation. Her happiness was overwhelming. And before she had passed through the first full rush of feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta entered together.\\n\\nThe urgent need to appear herself at once brought on a struggle; but after some time she could manage no longer. She began to comprehend nothing they said, and was forced to plead illness and excuse herself. They could plainly see that she looked extremely unwell, and were alarmed and distressed, refusing to leave her for anything. This was terrible. Had they gone away and left her quietly in possession of the room, it would have restored her; but having them all standing or waiting about her was unbearable, and in despair she said she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cBy all means, my dear,\u201d exclaimed Mrs Musgrove, \u201cgo home at once, and look after yourself, so that you may be ready for the evening. I wish Sarah were here to treat you, but I am no doctor. Charles, ring and order a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut the chair would never do. Worse than anything! To lose the chance of exchanging two words with Captain Wentworth during her quiet, solitary walk up the town (and she felt nearly certain of encountering him) was impossible to endure. She earnestly objected to the chair, and Mrs Musgrove, thinking only of one particular kind of illness, had anxiously satisfied herself that there had been no fall; that Anne had not lately slipped and struck her head; that she was entirely certain she had suffered no fall; and could therefore let her depart cheerfully, expecting to find her improved by night.\\n\\nEager to neglect no possible precaution, Anne made an effort and said\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI fear, ma\u2019am, that this has not been fully understood. Pray be kind enough to tell the other gentlemen that we hope to see your entire party this evening. I am afraid there has been some misunderstanding; and I particularly wish you to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! my dear, it is perfectly understood, I assure you.\u201d\"}"
