          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cdbdd2048c43d3b9bbb03466\",\"rewritten_text\":\"One day, while I was occupied with my books and Mr. Pocket, I received a note by post, the mere sight of its outside throwing me into a great flutter; for, although I had never seen the handwriting in which it was addressed, I guessed whose hand it was. It had no formal opening, such as Dear Mr. Pip, Dear Pip, Dear Sir, or Dear Anything, but began in this manner:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am to arrive in London the day after to-morrow by the midday coach. I believe it was arranged that you should meet me? At any rate, Miss Havisham has that impression, and I write in obedience to it. She sends you her regards.\\n\\n\u201cYours, ESTELLA.\u201d\\n\\nHad there been time, I should likely have ordered several new suits for the occasion; but there was not, and I had to be satisfied with those already in my possession. My appetite disappeared at once, and I knew neither peace nor rest until the day came. Nor did its arrival bring me either; for by then I was worse than ever, and began haunting the coach-office in Wood Street, Cheapside, before the coach had even departed from the Blue Boar in our town. Though I knew perfectly well that this was so, I still felt it unsafe to leave the coach-office out of my sight for more than five minutes at a stretch; and in this unreasonable state I had completed the first half-hour of a watch lasting four or five hours, when Wemmick ran into me.\\n\\n\u201cHalloa, Mr. Pip,\u201d he said; \u201chow are you? I should scarcely have imagined this was _your_ beat.\u201d\\n\\nI explained that I was waiting to meet someone arriving by coach, and asked after the Castle and the Aged.\\n\\n\u201cBoth flourishing, thankye,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cand the Aged especially. He\u2019s in wonderful feather. He\u2019ll be eighty-two next birthday. I have an idea of firing eighty-two times, if the neighbourhood doesn\u2019t object and that cannon of mine can withstand the pressure. However, that isn\u2019t London conversation. Where do you suppose I\u2019m going?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo the office?\u201d I said, since he was moving in that direction.\\n\\n\u201cNext door to it, as good as,\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cI\u2019m going to Newgate. We have a banker\u2019s-parcel case on hand just now, and I\u2019ve been down the road to take a look at the scene of action; so I must have a word or two with our client.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid your client commit the robbery?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cBless your soul and body, no,\u201d Wemmick answered, very dryly. \u201cBut he is charged with it. You or I might be charged with it just as easily. Either of us might be accused of it, you know.\u201d\"}"
