          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"71ee1e33e1c61d94cf062fd8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGet up! What are you doing?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and looked around, trying to figure out where I was. The sun was already up, and I had been sleeping soundly. Pap stood over me, looking sour and sick, too. He said:\\n\\n\u201cWhat are you doing with this gun?\u201d\\n\\nI figured he didn\u2019t remember anything about what he had been doing, so I said:\\n\\n\u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was waiting for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you wake me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I tried to, but I couldn\u2019t. I couldn\u2019t move you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, then. Don\u2019t stand there talking all day. Get out and see if there\u2019s a fish on the lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll be along in a minute.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I hurried up the riverbank. I noticed pieces of branches and other things floating downstream, along with bits of bark, so I knew the river had started to rise. I figured I would have a great time now if I were over in town. The June rise had always been lucky for me, because as soon as it began, cordwood came floating down, along with pieces of log rafts\u2014sometimes a dozen logs together. All you had to do was catch them and sell them to the wood yards and sawmill.\\n\\nI walked up the bank, keeping one eye out for Pap and the other for whatever the rising water might bring. Then, all at once, a canoe came floating by\u2014a beautiful one, too, about thirteen or fourteen feet long, riding high like a duck. I jumped headfirst from the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and swam toward it. I expected someone to be lying in it, because people often did that to fool others; when a man had pulled a skiff almost within reach, they would sit up and laugh at him. But this time there was nobody. It really was a drifting canoe, and I climbed in and paddled it to shore. I thought the old man would be glad when he saw it\u2014it was worth ten dollars. But when I reached shore, Pap was still nowhere in sight. As I was pushing the canoe into a little creek like a gully, covered with vines and willows, I had another idea: I would hide it well, and instead of heading into the woods when I ran away, I would go about fifty miles downriver and settle in one place, so I wouldn\u2019t have such a hard time walking.\\n\\nThe creek was quite close to the shanty, and I thought I heard the old man coming the whole time. But I got the canoe hidden, then stepped out and looked around a clump of willows. There was the old man farther down the path, taking aim at a bird with his gun.\"}"
