{"request_id":"2e2a16dc1ed685899cdec0fb","rewritten_text":"They were her whole life, dearer than any place made sacred by love, dearer than everything else on earth. The boys heard about our move with childish delight. Clara asked whether we were going to Athens. “It is possible,” I replied, and her face lit up with joy. There she would see her parents’ tomb and the land filled with memories of her father’s glory. She had thought about these places constantly and in silence. Remembering them had changed her childhood cheerfulness into seriousness and filled her with lofty, restless thoughts.

There were many dear friends we could not bear to leave behind, humble as they were. There was the spirited, obedient horse Lord Raymond had given his daughter; Alfred’s dog; and a pet eagle whose sight had grown dim with age. Yet making this list of favourites to take with us brought sorrow over our great losses and a deep sigh for all we had to leave behind. Tears filled Idris’s eyes, while Alfred and Evelyn brought first a favourite rose tree, then a beautifully carved marble vase, insisting that these must come too. They cried out that it was cruel we could not take the castle and forest, the deer and birds, and every familiar, cherished thing with us. “Dear foolish ones,” I said, “we have lost treasures forever that were far more precious than these, and we are abandoning them to save treasures that, beside them, are nothing. Let us never forget our purpose and our hope; they will build an unbreakable barrier against our grief over such small things.”

The children were easily distracted and soon returned to thoughts of the pleasures ahead. Idris had vanished. She had gone to hide her weakness. Leaving the castle, she had descended into the little park and sought a lonely place where she could give way to her tears. I found her clinging to an old oak, pressing its rough trunk with her rosy lips, while tears poured down and she could no longer hold back her sobs and broken cries. With overwhelming grief, I saw the woman I loved so dearly lost in sorrow. I drew her toward me, and when she felt my kisses on her eyelids and my arms around her, she returned to the awareness of what she still possessed. “You are very kind not to reproach me,” she said. “I weep, and a bitter, unbearable sorrow tears at my heart. And yet I am happy; mothers mourn their children, wives lose their husbands, while you and my children remain with me.”"}

