elings even within my own heart; they pressed upon me with the weight of a mountain, and their excess buried my agony beneath them. Thus I returned home and, entering the house, presented myself to the family. My haggard, wild appearance caused intense alarm, but I answered no questions and scarcely spoke. I felt as though I had been placed under a ban\u2014as though I had no right to claim their sympathy\u2014as though I might never again enjoy their companionship.\"}"
