          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c0fdbbc44f7a8472c116acbb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThis must have been around that period of Mr Elliot\u2019s life,\u201d said Anne, \u201cwhich has always made me especially curious. It must have been about the same time that he became known to my father and sister. I never knew him myself; I only heard about him. But there was something in his behavior toward my father and sister then, and later in the circumstances of his marriage, that I could never fully reconcile with the man we see now. It seemed to show that he was a very different kind of person.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know it all, I know it all,\u201d cried Mrs Smith. \u201cHe had been introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I knew him, but I heard him speak of them constantly. I know he was invited and encouraged, and I know he chose not to go. I may be able to satisfy you about matters you would hardly expect; and as for his marriage, I knew everything about it at the time. I was aware of all the arguments for and against it; I was the friend to whom he confided his hopes and plans. Though I had not known his wife before, since her lower position in society made that impossible, I knew her throughout the rest of her life, or at least until the last two years, and can answer any question you may wish to ask.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI have no particular questions about her. I have always understood that they were not a happy couple. But I should like to know why, at that time in his life, he rejected my father\u2019s acquaintance as he did. My father was certainly ready to treat him with kindness and proper attention. Why did Mr Elliot withdraw?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr Elliot,\u201d replied Mrs Smith, \u201chad one aim at that point in his life: to make his fortune, and by a faster method than the law. He meant to do it through marriage. At least, he was determined not to damage his prospects through an imprudent marriage. I know it was his belief\u2014whether rightly or not, I cannot say\u2014that your father and sister, through their kindness and invitations, were trying to arrange a match between the heir and the young lady. Such a match could never have met his ideas of wealth and independence. That was why he withdrew, I assure you. He told me the whole story. He kept nothing from me. It was curious that, having just left you in Bath, my first and closest acquaintance after my marriage should be your cousin, and that through him I should constantly hear about your father and sister.\"}"
