          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ab15566f8cb796176ce92ebe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"_P.S._\u2014Do tell me about the bows. Jennings insists they are fashionable.\\n\\nLord Arthur looked so serious and unhappy as he read the letter that the Duchess burst into laughter.\\n\\n\u2018My dear Arthur,\u2019 she cried, \u2018I shall never show you a young lady\u2019s letter again! But what shall I say about the clock? I think it is an excellent invention, and I should like one myself.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I don\u2019t think much of them,\u2019 said Lord Arthur with a sad smile. After kissing his mother, he left the room.\\n\\nUpstairs, he threw himself onto a sofa, and his eyes filled with tears. He had done his best to commit the murder, but both times he had failed, through no fault of his own. He had tried to do his duty, yet it seemed that Destiny herself had betrayed him. He felt the emptiness of good intentions and the uselessness of trying to be noble. Perhaps it would be better to end the engagement altogether. Sybil would suffer, it was true, but suffering could not truly damage a nature as noble as hers. As for himself, what did it matter? There was always some war in which a man could die, some cause for which he could give his life; and since life held no pleasure for him, death held no fear. Let Destiny bring about his doom. He would do nothing to help her.\\n\\nAt half-past seven he dressed and went down to the club. Surbiton was there with a group of young men, and Lord Arthur had to dine with them. Their shallow conversation and foolish jokes did not interest him. As soon as coffee was served, he left, making up an engagement to escape. On his way out, the hall porter handed him a letter. It was from Herr Winckelkopf, asking him to come the following evening and see an explosive umbrella that went off as soon as it was opened. It was the newest invention and had just arrived from Geneva. He tore the letter into pieces. He had decided not to attempt any more experiments. Then he wandered down to the Thames Embankment and sat beside the river for hours. The moon looked through a mane of golden-brown clouds like a lion\u2019s eye, while countless stars covered the hollow sky like gold dust scattered across a purple dome. From time to time, a barge swung into the muddy current and drifted away with the tide, while the railway signals changed from green to scarlet as trains screamed across the bridge.\"}"
