{"request_id":"1eee228abface76e77a72108","rewritten_text":"I come from a sea-surrounded corner of the world, a cloud-shadowed land that, when the globe appears to my mind with its shoreless oceans and trackless continents, seems only an insignificant speck in the immense whole; yet, measured by intellectual power, it outweighed countries of greater size and larger populations. So true is it that the human mind alone created everything good or great for humanity, and that Nature herself was merely its first servant. England, lying far to the north in the troubled sea, now visits my dreams in the form of a vast, well-manned ship that mastered the winds and rode proudly over the waves. In my boyhood she was the universe to me. When I stood on my native hills and saw plain and mountain extend to the farthest limits of my sight, dotted with the homes of my countrymen and made fertile by their labour, the very centre of the earth was fixed for me in that place, while the rest of the globe seemed a fable, one I could have forgotten without effort of imagination or understanding.

From the beginning, my fortunes illustrated the power that change can exert over the course of a human life. In my case, this was almost inherited. My father was one of those men to whom nature had lavishly given the coveted gifts of wit and imagination, then left his life’s vessel to be driven by those winds without providing reason as its rudder or judgment as its pilot. His origins were obscure, but circumstances brought him early to public attention, and his small inherited property was soon spent amid the splendid world of fashion and luxury in which he played a part. During the brief years of his careless youth, he was adored by the fashionable idlers of the day, not least by the young sovereign, who escaped from party intrigues and the demanding duties of kingship to find unfailing amusement and renewed spirits in his company. My father’s impulses, never under his own control, constantly led him into difficulties from which only his ingenuity could free him; and the growing burden of debts of honour and commerce, which would have crushed anyone else, he carried with a light heart and unbroken cheerfulness. His presence was so essential at the tables and gatherings of the wealthy that his failings were treated as minor, and he himself was welcomed with intoxicating flattery.

This kind of popularity, like all others, fades; and the difficulties he faced on every side grew at a frightening rate compared with his limited means of escaping them."}

