          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d15f82f76c56046c04c22d8f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIs that everything? It is not much, that! Many are under the Republic\u2019s displeasure, and must peer out through the little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which is he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am he. Necessarily so, being the last.\u201d\\n\\nJarvis Lorry has answered all the questions that came before. Jarvis Lorry has descended and now stands with one hand upon the coach door, replying to a cluster of officials. They stroll at their leisure around the carriage and leisurely climb onto the box, to inspect the scant luggage upon its roof; the country folk loitering nearby press closer to the coach doors and stare greedily inside. A small child, carried by its mother, stretches out its short arm so that it may touch the wife of an aristocrat who has been taken to the Guillotine.\\n\\n\u201cHere are your papers, Jarvis Lorry, countersigned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne may leave, citizen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne may leave. Forward, my postilions! A good journey!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI salute you, citizens.\u2014And the first danger is past!\u201d\\n\\nThese too are Jarvis Lorry\u2019s words, as he clasps his hands and gazes upward. Terror fills the carriage; there is weeping, and the laboured breathing of the unconscious traveller.\\n\\n\u201cAre we not going too slowly? Can they not be persuaded to go faster?\u201d asks Lucie, clinging to the old man.\\n\\n\u201cIt would appear that we were fleeing, my darling. I must not press them too hard; it would awaken suspicion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook back, look back, and see whether we are followed!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe road is clear, my dearest. Thus far, we are not pursued.\u201d\\n\\nHouses in twos and threes go past, along with lonely farms, crumbling buildings, dye-works, tanneries, and the like, open country, and avenues of bare trees. Beneath us lies the hard, uneven pavement; on either side is soft, deep mud. At times we turn into the bordering mud to escape the stones that clatter beneath and jolt us; at others we become stuck in the ruts and mire. Then the torment of our impatience grows so intense that, in our frantic fear and haste, we are ready to get out and run\u2014hide\u2014do anything rather than remain stopped.\\n\\nFrom the open country, back among crumbling buildings, lonely farms, dye-works, tanneries, and the like, cottages in twos and threes, avenues of bare trees. Have these men tricked us and brought us back by another road? Is this not the same place appearing twice? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look back, look back, and see whether we are pursued! Hush! The posting-house.\\n\\nAt their leisure, our four horses are removed; at their leisure, the coach waits in the little street, stripped of horses and apparently never likely to move again; at their leisure, the fresh horses gradually appear, one after another; at their leisure, the new postilions come along, sucking and plaiting their whip-lashes; at their leisure, the old postilions count their money, add incorrectly, and reach displeased conclusions.\"}"
