{"request_id":"45782f9055df48aeaba0585c","rewritten_text":"Many American ladies, on departing from their native country, assume an air of lasting ill-health, believing it to be a mark of European refinement; Mrs. Otis, however, had never made that mistake. She possessed a splendid constitution and an extraordinary supply of animal spirits. In fact, in many ways she was thoroughly English, and offered an excellent illustration of the truth that nowadays we share almost everything with America—except, naturally, the language. Her eldest son, named Washington by his parents in a patriotic moment that he never stopped regretting, was a fair-haired and fairly handsome young man. He had prepared himself for American diplomacy by leading the German at the Newport Casino for three seasons in succession, and even in London was known as a first-rate dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his sole weaknesses; in every other respect he was remarkably sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis was a girl of fifteen, slender and beautiful as a fawn, with a spirited openness in her large blue eyes. She was an accomplished amazon, and had once raced old Lord Bilton on her pony twice around the park, winning by a length and a half just before the statue of Achilles. This greatly delighted the young Duke of Cheshire, who proposed to her at once, only to be sent back to Eton that same night by his guardians, weeping bitterly. After Virginia came the twins, generally known as ‘The Stars and Stripes,’ because they were forever being swished. They were charming boys and, apart from the worthy Minister, the family’s only genuine republicans.

Since Canterville Chase lay seven miles from Ascot, the nearest railway station, Mr. Otis had telegraphed for a waggonette to meet them, and they set off on their drive in excellent spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air was faintly perfumed by the pine woods. From time to time they heard a wood pigeon brooding over its own sweet song, or glimpsed, among the stirring ferns, the pheasant’s polished breast. Little squirrels watched them from the beeches as they passed, while rabbits darted through the undergrowth and across the mossy rises, their white tails held aloft. Yet as they entered the avenue leading to Canterville Chase, the sky suddenly darkened with clouds, an unusual silence seemed to settle over the air, a great flock of rooks flew noiselessly above them, and before they arrived at the house, several large drops of rain had fallen.

An old woman stood on the steps to welcome them, dressed neatly in black silk, with a white cap and apron."}

