          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8f01be38e55b83e30f67b49f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAre you speaking of\u2014Mr. Knightley?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo be sure I am. I never could have meant anybody else\u2014and so I thought you understood. When we spoke of him, it was perfectly clear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot quite,\u201d replied Emma, with forced composure, \u201cfor everything you said then seemed to me to concern another person. I could almost insist that you had _named_ Mr. Frank Churchill. I am certain the service Mr. Frank Churchill had done you, in saving you from the gipsies, was what you mentioned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Miss Woodhouse, how strangely you forget!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Harriet, I remember perfectly the substance of what I said at the time. I told you I did not wonder at your attachment; that, considering the service he had performed for you, it was entirely natural:\u2014and you agreed, speaking very warmly of your gratitude for that service, and even describing what you had felt when you saw him come forward to rescue you.\u2014The whole impression remains strong in my memory.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear,\u201d cried Harriet, \u201cnow I remember what you mean; but I was thinking of something quite different then. It was not the gipsies\u2014it was not Mr. Frank Churchill I meant. No! (with some emphasis) I was thinking of a far more precious circumstance\u2014of Mr. Knightley\u2019s coming to ask me to dance, when Mr. Elton would not dance with me; and when there was no other partner in the room. That was the kind act; that was the noble benevolence and generosity; that was the service which first made me feel how superior he was to every other person on earth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood God!\u201d cried Emma, \u201cthis has been a most unfortunate\u2014most dreadful mistake!\u2014What can be done?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou would not have encouraged me, then, if you had understood me? At least, I cannot be worse off than I would have been if the other had been the person; and now\u2014it _is_ possible\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe stopped for a few moments. Emma was unable to speak.\\n\\n\u201cI do not wonder, Miss Woodhouse,\u201d she continued, \u201cthat you should see a great difference between the two, whether in relation to me or to anybody. You must think one five hundred million times above me compared with the other. But I hope, Miss Woodhouse, that supposing\u2014that if\u2014strange as it may seem\u2014. Yet you know they were your own words, that _more_ extraordinary things had happened, and matches of _greater disparity_ had taken place than one between Mr. Frank Churchill and me; and therefore it seems possible that even such a thing as this may have happened before\u2014and if I should be fortunate beyond expression, as to\u2014if Mr. Knightley should truly\u2014if _he_ does not object to the disparity, I hope, dear Miss Woodhouse, you will not oppose it, or try to place obstacles in the way.\u201d\"}"
