          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0f88b4742b07b4cf3861128c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When I saw him in these moods, I often thought about the old philosophy of the Bi-Part Soul, and entertained the idea of a double Dupin\u2014the creative one and the analytical one.\\n\\nLet no one suppose from this that I am revealing a mystery or writing a romance. What I have described in the Frenchman was simply the result of an excited, or perhaps unhealthy, mind. But an example of the kind of remarks he made at such times will explain it best.\\n\\nOne night we were walking down a long, dirty street near the Palais Royal. We both seemed lost in thought, and neither of us had spoken a word for at least fifteen minutes. Suddenly Dupin said:\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very small man, that is true, and would be better suited to the _Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s_. \u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere can be no doubt of that,\u201d I replied without thinking. I had been so absorbed in my own thoughts that I did not at first notice how strangely his words matched them. A moment later I realized what had happened, and was deeply astonished.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said seriously, \u201cI cannot understand this. I admit that I am amazed and can hardly trust my senses. How could you know that I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d I stopped, wanting to make certain that he really knew whom I had in mind.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy do you stop? You were thinking that his small figure made him unfit for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly what I had been thinking. Chantilly had once been a shoemaker on the Rue St. Denis. After becoming obsessed with the theater, he had tried to play Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy of that name, and had been widely mocked for it.\\n\\n\u201cFor Heaven\u2019s sake, tell me,\u201d I cried, \u201chow you managed to read my thoughts in this matter\u2014if there is any method behind it.\u201d In truth, I was even more startled than I was willing to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruit seller,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to conclude that the shoemaker was not tall enough to play Xerxes, and so on.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruit seller! You amaze me. I do not know any fruit seller at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who bumped into you as we entered this street\u2014it may have been fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI now remembered that a fruit seller carrying a large basket of apples had accidentally nearly knocked me down as we passed from the Rue C\u2014\u2014 into the street where we stood. But how this could have anything to do with Chantilly, I could not understand at all.\"}"
