          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"16351d5e18891be6049edf64\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Knights and horses clad in heavy armor could not move very swiftly. They would make the best speed possible, and with the ground now in good shape, free of snow and mud, they could likely manage seven miles at a stretch; they would need to change horses twice; they would reach us around six, or shortly afterward; there would still be ample daylight; they would spot the white cloth I was to bind around my right arm, and I would assume command. We would surround the prison and have the king out almost immediately. All things considered, it would be sufficiently grand and picturesque, though I would have preferred noon, because the scene would then possess a more theatrical quality.\\n\\nNow, to add more strings to my bow, I decided to seek out some of the people I had once known and make myself recognized. That might get us out of our difficulty without help from the knights. But I had to act carefully, since it was dangerous work. I needed to put on splendid clothing, and it would not do to leap into it all at once. No, I must approach it gradually, purchasing one suit after another in shops far apart, and choosing a slightly finer article each time, until I reached silk and velvet and was prepared for my undertaking. So I set out.\\n\\nBut the plan collapsed like scat! At the very first corner I turned, I ran straight into one of our slaves, prowling about with a watchman. I happened to cough, and he shot me a sudden glance that pierced to my marrow. I supposed he thought he had heard that cough before. I turned at once into a shop and moved down along the counter, pricing goods while keeping watch from the corner of my eye. Those men had halted and were speaking together, peering in through the doorway. I resolved to escape by the rear, if a rear exit existed, and asked the shopwoman whether I might go out there to search for the escaped slave, who was thought to be hiding somewhere behind the shop. I told her I was an officer in disguise, that my partner was at the door with one of the murderers under arrest, and asked whether she would kindly go there and tell him he need not wait, but should immediately proceed to the far end of the back alley and be ready to intercept the man when I drove him out.\\n\\nShe was aflame with eagerness to see one of the already famous murderers, and she set off on the errand without delay.\"}"
