          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6ef7ed5adf1f8a39f0c864e4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He got up and left the room with my nurse, and shortly afterward my father came in.\\n\\nAt that moment, nothing could have pleased me more than my father\u2019s arrival. I reached out my hand to him and exclaimed,\\n\\n\u201cAre you safe, then\u2014and Elizabeth\u2014and Ernest?\u201d\\n\\nMy father reassured me of their safety and tried, by speaking of matters so dear to my heart, to lift my sinking spirits; but he soon perceived that a prison could not be a place of cheerfulness. \u201cWhat a dwelling this is that you occupy, my son!\u201d he said, gazing sadly at the barred windows and miserable state of the room. \u201cYou travelled in search of happiness, yet some fatality appears to follow you. And poor Clerval\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThe mention of my unfortunate, murdered friend caused an agitation too severe for my feeble condition; I burst into tears.\\n\\n\u201cAlas! Yes, my father,\u201d I answered; \u201csome destiny of the most dreadful nature hangs over me, and I must live to accomplish it, or surely I would have died upon Henry\u2019s coffin.\u201d\\n\\nWe were not permitted to speak for long, since the precarious condition of my health made every measure necessary that might preserve tranquillity. Mr. Kirwin entered and insisted that I should not exhaust my strength through excessive exertion. Yet my father\u2019s presence seemed to me like that of my good angel, and I slowly regained my health.\\n\\nAs my illness departed, I became consumed by a dark and gloomy melancholy that nothing could relieve. The image of Clerval, ghastly and murdered, remained forever before me. More than once, the agitation caused by these thoughts made my friends fear a dangerous relapse. Alas! Why had they preserved so wretched and detested a life? Surely it was so that I might accomplish my destiny, which was now nearing its end. Soon\u2014oh, very soon\u2014death would silence these throbbings and free me from the immense burden of anguish that pressed me toward the dust; and, in carrying out the sentence of justice, I too would sink into rest. Death still seemed far away, though the desire for it was constantly in my thoughts; and I often sat for hours without movement or speech, longing for some mighty upheaval that would bury both me and my destroyer beneath its ruins.\\n\\nThe assizes drew near. I had already spent three months in prison, and though I remained weak and continually threatened with relapse, I was compelled to travel nearly a hundred miles to the country town where the court convened. Mr. Kirwin took upon himself every responsibility of gathering witnesses and preparing my defence.\"}"
