{"request_id":"3d00baa85de5723b9f051c77","rewritten_text":"He also had to think of Sybil’s father and mother, who were rather old-fashioned and might object to the marriage if there were anything resembling a scandal. Still, he felt sure that if he told them the full facts, they would be the first to understand and approve his motives. He therefore had every reason to choose poison. It was safe, certain, and quiet, and avoided any need for painful scenes, which, like most Englishmen, he strongly disliked.

He knew absolutely nothing about poisons, however. When the waiter seemed unable to find anything in the library except _Ruff’s Guide_ and _Bailey’s Magazine_, he searched the shelves himself. At last he found a handsomely bound edition of the _Pharmacopoeia_ and a copy of Erskine’s _Toxicology_, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the Buckingham’s oldest members. He had been elected by mistake for someone else, and the Committee had been so angry that, when the real candidate appeared, they unanimously black-balled him. Lord Arthur was greatly puzzled by the technical language in both books and began to regret not having paid more attention to his classics at Oxford. Then, in the second volume of Erskine, he found a clear and complete account of aconitine’s properties. It seemed exactly the poison he needed. Its effect was swift—almost immediate—completely painless, and, when taken in a gelatine capsule as Sir Mathew recommended, not unpleasant. He therefore wrote the amount required for a fatal dose on his shirt cuff, replaced the books, and walked up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the leading chemists. Mr. Pestle, who always served aristocratic customers himself, was rather surprised by the order and deferentially murmured that a medical certificate might be required. But when Lord Arthur explained that the poison was for a large Norwegian mastiff he had to destroy because it showed early signs of rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice in the calf, Mr. Pestle was completely satisfied. He praised Lord Arthur’s remarkable knowledge of toxicology and had the prescription prepared at once.

Lord Arthur placed the capsule in a pretty little silver _bonbonnière_ he had seen in a Bond Street shop, threw away Pestle and Humbey’s ugly pillbox, and drove straight to Lady Clementina’s."}

