          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f710a144ff51b9587266c2e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We both agreed in loving Adrian, though Perdita, still not fully beyond childhood, could not appreciate the extent of his merits as I did, or feel the same sympathy with his pursuits and opinions. I was always with him. There was such sensitivity and sweetness in his nature that it gave our conversations a tender, almost unearthly quality. Yet he could also be as cheerful as a lark singing from its lofty tower, as soaring in thought as an eagle, and as innocent as the gentle-eyed dove. He could dispel Perdita\u2019s seriousness and remove the sting from the tormenting energy of my nature. I looked back on my restless desires and painful struggles with my fellow beings as on a troubled dream, and felt as changed as though I had passed into another form, whose new senses and nervous system had altered the reflection of the visible universe in the mind\u2019s mirror. But I had not changed in that way; I remained the same in strength, in earnest hunger for sympathy, and in my longing for active exertion. My manly virtues had not abandoned me, for the witch Urania spared Samson\u2019s hair while he rested at her feet; yet everything in me had been softened and humanized. Nor did Adrian teach me only the cold facts of history and philosophy. While using them to teach me how to restrain my own reckless and uncultivated spirit, he also opened before me the living page of his own heart and enabled me to feel and understand its wondrous nature.\\n\\nThe former queen of England had, from her son\u2019s earliest infancy, tried to plant bold and ambitious designs in his mind. She saw that he possessed genius and extraordinary talent, and cultivated these qualities so that she might later use them to advance her own purposes. She encouraged his thirst for knowledge and his impetuous courage; she even tolerated his untamed love of freedom, hoping that it would, as so often happens, develop into a passion for command. She tried to raise him with resentment toward, and a desire for revenge upon, those who had helped bring about his father\u2019s abdication. In this she failed. The accounts given him, however distorted, of a great and wise nation asserting its right to govern itself awakened his admiration; from an early age he became a republican on principle. Still, his mother did not give up. To his love of power and proud sense of birthright, she added deliberate ambition, patience, and self-control. She devoted herself to studying his character. Through praise, blame, and exhortation, she tried to discover and strike the right chords; and although the music produced by her touch sounded discordant to her, she based her hopes on his talents and remained certain that she would eventually win him over.\"}"
