          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bec2fa7ac794172260b81ae7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It should be said in the young lady\u2019s favor that she did not flatly declare she would not go; she merely expressed an emphatic and earnest wish to be \u201cblessed\u201d if she did\u2014a polite and delicate way of evading the request, showing that she possessed the natural good manners that cannot bear to inflict on another person the pain of a direct and definite refusal.\\n\\nThe Jew\u2019s face fell. He turned from this young lady, dressed gaily, almost extravagantly, in a red gown, green boots, and yellow curl-papers, to the other woman.\\n\\n\u201cNancy, my dear,\u201d said the Jew soothingly, \u201cwhat do _you_ say?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat it won\u2019t do; so there\u2019s no point trying it on, Fagin,\u201d Nancy replied.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by that?\u201d Mr. Sikes asked, looking up sourly.\\n\\n\u201cWhat I said, Bill,\u201d the woman replied calmly.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, you\u2019re exactly the person for it,\u201d Mr. Sikes argued. \u201cNobody around here knows anything about you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd since I don\u2019t want them to, either,\u201d Nancy replied in the same composed manner, \u201cit\u2019s rather more no than yes with me, Bill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe\u2019ll go, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\n\u201cNo, she won\u2019t, Fagin,\u201d said Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cYes, she will, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\nAnd Mr. Sikes was right. Through a mixture of threats, promises, and bribes, the woman was eventually persuaded to undertake the errand. She was not held back by the same concerns as her agreeable friend; having recently moved to the neighborhood of Field Lane from the distant but respectable suburb of Ratcliffe, she did not fear being recognized by her many acquaintances to the same degree.\\n\\nAccordingly, with a clean white apron tied over her gown and her curl-papers tucked beneath a straw bonnet\u2014both items supplied from the Jew\u2019s inexhaustible stock\u2014Miss Nancy prepared to set out on her errand.\\n\\n\u201cWait a moment, my dear,\u201d said the Jew, producing a small covered basket. \u201cCarry that in one hand. It looks more respectable, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive her a door key to carry in the other, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes. \u201cIt looks real and genuine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, my dear, so it does,\u201d said the Jew, hanging a large street-door key from the forefinger of the young lady\u2019s right hand. \u201cThere; very good! Very good indeed, my dear!\u201d he said, rubbing his hands.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my brother! My poor, dear, sweet, innocent little brother!\u201d Nancy cried, bursting into tears and wringing the little basket and street-door key in an agony of distress. \u201cWhat has become of him? Where have they taken him? Oh, have pity, and tell me what has been done with the dear boy, gentlemen; do, gentlemen, if you please, gentlemen!\u201d\"}"
