          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"949851dee0fd2ee49fac0999\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Looking over his letter once more while answering it had so softening an effect that it was especially necessary to strengthen her with a few firm expressions; and she was so deeply troubled by the thought of making him unhappy, considered so much what his mother and sisters would think and say, and was so eager that they should not imagine her ungrateful, that Emma felt, had the young man appeared before her then, he might have been accepted after all.\\n\\nThe letter was nevertheless written, sealed, and sent. The matter was settled, and Harriet secure. She was somewhat downcast throughout the evening, but Emma could make allowance for her kindly regrets, sometimes easing them by mentioning her own affection, and sometimes by introducing the thought of Mr. Elton.\\n\\n\u201cI shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d she said in a rather mournful voice.\\n\\n\u201cNor, if you were, could I ever endure losing you, my Harriet. You are far too necessary at Hartfield to be spared to Abbey-Mill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I am sure I should never wish to go there; for I am never happy anywhere but at Hartfield.\u201d\\n\\nAfter a while she said, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be greatly surprised if she knew what had occurred. I am sure Miss Nash would\u2014for Miss Nash considers her own sister very respectably married, and she is only a linen-draper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne ought to regret seeing greater pride or refinement in a schoolmistress, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would envy you such a chance of being married. Even this conquest would seem important in her eyes. As for anything better suited to you, I suppose she is entirely ignorant of it. The attentions of a certain person can scarcely yet have entered the gossip of Highbury. So far, I believe you and I are the only ones whose eyes and manners have revealed his meaning.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet blushed and smiled, and said something about being surprised that people should like her so much. The thought of Mr. Elton was certainly encouraging; yet, after some time, her tender feelings returned towards the rejected Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow that he has received my letter,\u201d she said softly. \u201cI wonder what they are all doing\u2014whether his sisters know\u2014if he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope he will not take it too much to heart.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet us think of those absent friends who are more pleasantly occupied,\u201d cried Emma. \u201cAt this very moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is showing your picture to his mother and sisters, saying how much more beautiful the original is, and, after being asked for it five or six times, permitting them to hear your name, your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
