          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"64e46a10b3db8cfc9d08b8d7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He said nothing about it, however, until their visitor had departed for the night; but the instant he was gone, he turned to his daughter and said,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cJane, I congratulate you. You will be a very happy woman.\u201d\\n\\nJane immediately went to him, kissed him, and thanked him for his kindness.\\n\\n\u201cYou are a good girl,\u201d he answered, \u201cand it gives me great pleasure to think that you will be so happily settled. I have no doubt that you will do very well together. Your tempers are not at all unlike. You are both so accommodating that nothing will ever be decided; so trusting that every servant will cheat you; and so liberal that you will always spend beyond your income.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hope not. Carelessness or thoughtlessness with money would be unpardonable in _me_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSpend beyond their income! My dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d cried his wife, \u201cwhat can you mean? Why, he has four or five thousand a year, and probably more.\u201d Then, turning to her daughter, she continued, \u201cOh, my dear, dear Jane, I am so happy! I am certain I shall not sleep a wink tonight. I knew how it would end. I always said it must happen at last. I was sure you could not be so beautiful without attracting someone! I remember, the very first time I saw him, when he came into Hertfordshire last year, I thought it very likely that you would come together. Oh, he is the handsomest young man ever seen!\u201d\\n\\nWickham and Lydia were entirely forgotten. Jane was, beyond all comparison, her favourite child. At that moment she cared for nobody else. Her younger daughters soon began appealing to her for pleasures she might one day be able to provide.\\n\\nMary asked to use the library at Netherfield; and Kitty earnestly begged for a few balls there each winter.\\n\\nFrom this time onward, Bingley was naturally a daily visitor at Longbourn, often arriving before breakfast and always staying until after supper, except when some barbarous neighbour, whom he could not possibly detest enough, had invited him to dinner, an invitation he felt bound to accept.\\n\\nElizabeth now had little opportunity to converse with her sister; for whenever he was present, Jane had attention for no one else. Yet Elizabeth found herself quite useful to them both during the periods of separation that occasionally had to occur. When Jane was absent, he always attached himself to Elizabeth for the pleasure of speaking about her; and when Bingley had gone, Jane regularly sought the same consolation.\\n\\n\u201cHe has made me so happy,\u201d she said one evening, \u201cby telling me that he had been completely unaware of my being in town last spring!\"}"
