          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c456ad424f6337449bcb70a1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But how could I, a poor, bewildered village boy, avoid that wonderful inconsistency into which the best and wisest men fall every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may all be quite true,\u201d I said to Biddy, \u201cbut I admire her terribly.\u201d\\n\\nIn short, when I reached that point, I turned onto my face, seized the hair on either side of my head, and gave it a good wrench. All the while, I knew my heart\u2019s madness was so extreme and misplaced that I was fully aware my face would have deserved it if I had lifted it by my hair and smashed it against the pebbles as punishment for belonging to such an idiot.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and she tried no further to reason with me. She placed her hand\u2014which was comforting, though roughened by work\u2014on my hands one after another, and gently drew them from my hair. Then she softly patted my shoulder to soothe me, while I cried a little with my face against my sleeve, exactly as I had in the brewery yard, and felt vaguely certain that someone, or everyone, was treating me very badly; I couldn\u2019t say which.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m glad of one thing,\u201d said Biddy, \u201cand that is that you felt able to confide in me, Pip. And I\u2019m glad of another: you know, of course, that you can rely on me to keep your confidence and always prove worthy of it. If your first teacher\u2014dear me, such a poor one, and so much in need of teaching herself!\u2014were your teacher now, she thinks she knows what lesson she would give you. But it would be a difficult one to learn, and you have gone beyond her, so it is no use now.\u201d With a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank and said, in a fresh and pleasant tone, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, rising, putting my arm around her neck, and kissing her, \u201cI shall always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you\u2019re a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never shall be, so that means always. Not that I have anything to tell you, because you know everything I know\u2014as I told you at home the other night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Biddy very softly, looking away toward the ships. Then, in the same pleasant tone as before, she repeated, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did. The summer afternoon faded into the summer evening, and it was very beautiful.\"}"
