          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ac6f11ea1b6b8060a9af7699\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She and the children might stay quite comfortably.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! Papa\u2014that is what you have never managed to achieve, and I do not think you ever will. Isabella cannot bear to stay away from her husband.\u201d\\n\\nThis was too true to argue with. Though he disliked it, Mr. Woodhouse could only sigh in submission; and when Emma saw that the thought of his daughter\u2019s devotion to her husband had lowered his spirits, she quickly turned the conversation to something that would cheer him.\\n\\n\u201cHarriet must spend as much time with us as she can while my brother and sister are here. I am sure she will enjoy the children. We are very proud of them, are we not, Papa? I wonder which she will think the handsomer, Henry or John?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I wonder which she will. Poor little dears, how happy they will be to come. They are very fond of visiting Hartfield, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say they are, sir. I am sure I do not know anyone who is not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHenry is a fine boy, but John looks very much like his mother. Henry is the eldest, and he was named after me, not his father. John, the second, was named after his father. I believe some people are surprised that the eldest was not, but Isabella wanted him called Henry, which I thought was very sweet of her. And he is a very clever boy indeed. They are all remarkably clever, and they have so many charming ways. They come and stand beside my chair and say, \u2018Grandpapa, can you give me a piece of string?\u2019 Once Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him knives were made only for grandpapas. I think their father is too rough with them very often.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe seems rough to you,\u201d said Emma, \u201cbecause you are so gentle yourself; but if you compared him with other fathers, you would not think him rough. He wants his boys to be active and strong, and if they misbehave, he may speak sharply to them now and then. But he is an affectionate father\u2014Mr. John Knightley certainly is. The children are all very fond of him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd then their uncle comes in and throws them up to the ceiling in a very frightening way!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut they like it, Papa; there is nothing they enjoy more. They find it so delightful that, if their uncle did not make them take turns, whichever one started would never let the other have a turn.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I cannot understand it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is true of all of us, Papa. Half the world cannot understand the pleasures of the other half.\u201d\"}"
