          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6426dc1f0bf20f283dc1b49f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was finally agreed that we would live together during my stay in the city. Since my financial circumstances were somewhat less strained than his, I was allowed to pay for renting and furnishing, in a style suited to the rather fanciful gloom of our shared temperament, a timeworn and grotesque mansion. It had long stood deserted because of superstitions we never investigated, and was slowly collapsing in a secluded, desolate part of the Faubourg St. Germain.\\n\\nIf the world had known our routine there, we would have been considered madmen\u2014though perhaps harmless ones. Our isolation was complete. We received no visitors. Indeed, I had carefully concealed the location of our retreat from my former associates, while Dupin had not known, or been known by, anyone in Paris for many years. We lived entirely within ourselves.\\n\\nMy friend had a strange fancy (for what else can I call it?) for loving the night for its own sake; and, as with all his other peculiarities, I quietly adopted it, surrendering myself completely to his wild whims. The dark goddess could not always remain with us, but we could imitate her presence. At dawn we closed every shutter of our old house and lit a pair of strongly scented candles, which gave off only the faintest and most ghastly light. By their glow we occupied our minds with dreams\u2014reading, writing, or talking\u2014until the clock announced the arrival of true Darkness. Then we went out arm in arm, continuing the day\u2019s discussions or wandering far and wide until late, seeking among the crowded city\u2019s shifting lights and shadows that boundless mental stimulation which quiet observation can provide.\\n\\nOn such occasions I could not help noticing and admiring\u2014though his powerful imagination had prepared me for it\u2014a remarkable analytical gift in Dupin. He also seemed to take intense pleasure in using it, if not precisely in displaying it, and openly admitted the satisfaction it gave him. With a low, chuckling laugh, he would boast that most men, as far as he was concerned, wore windows in their chests, and would then support the claim with direct and astonishing demonstrations of his intimate knowledge of my own thoughts. At such moments his manner was cold and detached; his eyes were blank; and his voice, ordinarily a rich tenor, rose to a treble that would have sounded petulant were it not for the deliberate precision and complete clarity of his speech.\"}"
