          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b6d5fd06b9a138caf10c0753\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Saturday at noon I went to the well and watched for a while. Merlin was still burning smoke-powders, pawing at the air, and muttering gibberish as energetically as ever. But he looked discouraged, because naturally he had not even started a sweat in that well yet. At last I said:\\n\\n\u201cHow does it look by now, partner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBehold, I am now testing the most powerful enchantment known to the princes of occult magic in the lands of the East. If it fails, nothing can help us. Be silent until I am finished.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he raised so much smoke that it darkened the whole area and must have made things unpleasant for the hermits, since the wind carried it toward them and rolled it over their dens in a thick, billowing fog. He poured out an equal amount of speech and twisted his body, cutting through the air with his hands in the strangest way. After twenty minutes he collapsed, panting and nearly exhausted. Then the abbot arrived with several hundred monks and nuns. Behind them came a crowd of pilgrims and a couple of acres of foundlings, all drawn by the enormous smoke and all wildly excited. The abbot anxiously asked about the results. Merlin said:\\n\\n\u201cIf any work a mortal could perform might break the spell binding these waters, the effort I have just made would have done so. It has failed. Therefore I now know that what I feared is true: the most powerful spirit known to the magicians of the East, whose name no one may speak and live, has cast his spell upon this well. No mortal breathes, nor ever will, who can discover the secret of that spell, and without that secret no one can break it. The water will never flow again, good Father. I have done all a man could do. Let me go.\u201d\\n\\nNaturally, this threw the abbot into great confusion. He turned to me, his face showing his distress, and said:\\n\\n\u201cYe have heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSome of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all, then\u2014not all! Which part is true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has cast his spell upon the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s wounds, then we are ruined!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPossibly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not certainly? Ye mean, not certainly?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen ye also mean that when he says none can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. When he says that, he says something that is not necessarily true. There are conditions under which an attempt to break it might have some chance\u2014that is, some small, even trifling, chance\u2014of succeeding.\u201d\"}"
