{"request_id":"2bfb1f28621543f05c00cc76","rewritten_text":"In fact, he had been completely unaware of his own influence. He had followed her into the shrubbery without any thought of trying it. He had come, anxious to see how she was bearing Frank Churchill’s engagement, with no selfish purpose—indeed, with no purpose at all beyond trying, if she gave him an opening, to comfort or advise her. The rest had been the work of the moment, the immediate effect of what he heard upon his feelings. The delightful certainty of her complete indifference to Frank Churchill, of her heart being entirely free of him, had given rise to the hope that, in time, he might win her affection himself; but it had not been a present hope. In that momentary triumph of eagerness over judgment, he had only dared hope to be told that she would not forbid him from trying to attach her to him. The greater hopes that gradually unfolded were all the more enchanting. The affection he had been asking permission to create, if he could, was already his! Within half an hour, he had passed from a state of deep distress to something so close to perfect happiness that it could be called nothing else.

Her change was equal. That half-hour had given each of them the same precious certainty of being loved, and had removed from each the same measure of ignorance, jealousy, or distrust. On his side, there had been a long-standing jealousy, as old as Frank Churchill’s arrival—or even the expectation of his arrival. He had been in love with Emma, and jealous of Frank Churchill, from nearly the same time; one feeling had probably revealed the other to him. It was his jealousy of Frank Churchill that had driven him away from the country. The Box Hill party had decided him to leave. He would spare himself from witnessing such permitted, encouraged attentions again. He had gone away to learn indifference.

But he had gone to the wrong place. There was too much domestic happiness in his brother’s home; woman appeared there in too amiable a form; and Isabella was too much like Emma—differing only in those striking deficiencies that always made the other appear more brilliant to him—for much to be accomplished, even if his stay had been longer. Still, he had remained, resolutely, day after day, until that very morning’s post brought the story of Jane Fairfax. Then, with the gladness that must be felt—indeed, that he did not hesitate to feel, since he had never believed Frank Churchill at all worthy of Emma—there was so much tender concern and such sharp anxiety for her that he could stay no longer. He had ridden home through the rain and, immediately after dinner, walked up to see how this sweetest and best of all creatures, faultless despite all her faults, was bearing the discovery."}

