{"request_id":"e2576b24f34071600f6917ee","rewritten_text":"“I reasoned as follows—after the fact. The murderers had escaped through one of these windows. If so, they could not have fastened the sashes again from inside, as they had been found fastened; that obvious point had stopped the police from examining this area further. Yet the sashes were fastened. They must, therefore, have been capable of fastening themselves. There was no escaping that conclusion. I went to the unobstructed casement, pulled out the nail with some difficulty, and tried to raise the sash. It resisted every effort, as I had expected. A concealed spring must exist, I now knew; and this confirmation convinced me that my premises were correct, however mysterious the circumstances surrounding the nails remained. A careful search soon revealed the hidden spring. I pressed it and, satisfied by the discovery, did not raise the sash.

“I replaced the nail and examined it closely. Someone passing through this window might have closed it again, allowing the spring to catch—but could not have replaced the nail. The conclusion was clear, and narrowed the field of my investigation once more. The assassins must have escaped through the other window. Assuming, then, that the springs on both sashes were alike, as seemed likely, there must be some difference between the nails, or at least in the way they were fixed. Climbing onto the bedstead’s sacking, I looked carefully over the headboard at the second casement. Reaching my hand behind the board, I quickly found and pressed the spring, which was, as I had supposed, identical to its neighbor. I then examined the nail. It was as stout as the other and apparently fitted in the same way—driven in almost to the head.

“You may say that I was baffled; but if you do, you have misunderstood the nature of my deductions. To use a sporting expression, I had not once been ‘at fault.’ The trail had never been lost for an instant. There was no weakness in any link of the chain. I had followed the secret to its final result—and that result was the nail. It looked in every respect like its counterpart in the other window; but that fact was completely meaningless, however conclusive it might seem, when set against the fact that the clue ended here, at this point. ‘Something must be wrong,’ I said, ‘with the nail.’ I touched it, and the head, with about a quarter inch of the shank, came away in my fingers.”"}

