          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5345729f849e611ec839fcd7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I want to see you permanently well connected, and for that reason it would be best for you to have as few unusual acquaintances as possible. Therefore, I say that if you are still in this country when Mr. Martin marries, I hope your intimacy with his sisters will not lead you to become acquainted with his wife, who will probably be nothing more than a farmer\u2019s daughter, without education.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly. Yes. Not that I think Mr. Martin would ever marry anyone who had not received some education and been very well brought up. Still, I do not mean to set my opinion against yours\u2014and I am sure I shall not seek his wife\u2019s acquaintance. I shall always feel great regard for the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and would be very sorry to give them up, since they are quite as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, then certainly I had better not visit her, if I can avoid it.\u201d\\n\\nEmma watched her throughout these changing remarks and saw no alarming signs of love. The young man had been her first admirer, but Emma trusted that he had no other hold over her, and that Harriet would offer no serious resistance to any friendly arrangement of her own.\\n\\nThey met Mr. Martin the very next day as they were walking along the Donwell road. He was on foot, and after looking at Emma with great respect, turned to her companion with the most genuine pleasure. Emma was not sorry to have such an opportunity to examine him; and, walking a few yards ahead while they spoke together, she soon made her quick eye sufficiently acquainted with Mr. Robert Martin. His appearance was very neat, and he looked like a sensible young man, but he had no other personal advantage. When compared with gentlemen, Emma thought he must lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s affections. Harriet was not indifferent to manners; she had voluntarily remarked upon her father\u2019s gentleness with admiration as well as wonder. Mr. Martin looked as though he had no idea what good manners were.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, since Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting. Harriet then came running to her with a smiling face and a flutter of spirits that Miss Woodhouse hoped to calm very soon.\\n\\n\u201cJust think of our happening to meet him! How very strange! He said it was pure chance that he had not gone around by Randalls. He did not think we ever walked along this road. He thought we went toward Randalls most days.\u201d\"}"
