          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3a83e56de6163d5d4f10690c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNever!\u201d the girl answered.\\n\\n\u201cTell me why?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor one reason,\u201d the girl replied firmly, \u201cone reason the lady knows and will support me in\u2014I know she will, for I have her promise. And for this other reason besides: bad a life as he has led, I have led a bad life too. There are many of us who have followed the same courses together, and I will not turn against them\u2014any of them\u2014who might have turned against me, but did not, bad as they are.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d said the gentleman quickly, as though this were the point he had been trying to reach, \u201cplace Monks in my hands, and leave me to deal with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat if he turns against the others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI promise you that, in that event, if the truth is forced from him, the matter shall end there. There must be circumstances in Oliver\u2019s little history which it would be painful to expose before the public, and once the truth is drawn from him, they shall go free.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if it is not?\u201d the girl suggested.\\n\\n\u201cThen,\u201d continued the gentleman, \u201cthis Fagin shall not be brought to justice without your consent. In such a case, I think I could show you reasons that would persuade you to give it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave I the lady\u2019s promise for that?\u201d the girl asked.\\n\\n\u201cYou have,\u201d Rose replied. \u201cMy true and faithful pledge.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMonks would never discover how you learned what you know?\u201d the girl said, after a brief pause.\\n\\n\u201cNever,\u201d replied the gentleman. \u201cThe information would be used against him in such a way that he could never even guess.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have been a liar, and among liars, since I was a little child,\u201d said the girl after another silence, \u201cbut I will trust your word.\u201d\\n\\nAfter both had assured her that she could safely do so, she began, in a voice so low that the listener could often scarcely make out even the meaning of her words, to describe by name and location the public-house from which she had been followed that night. From the way she sometimes stopped, it seemed that the gentleman was hastily writing down some of the information she gave him. When she had fully explained the place, the best position for watching it without attracting notice, and the night and hour when Monks most commonly visited it, she appeared to pause for a few moments, trying to bring his features and appearance more clearly to mind.\\n\\n\u201cHe is tall,\u201d said the girl, \u201cand strongly built, though not stout; he has a furtive walk, and as he goes along he is always looking over his shoulder, first to one side and then to the other.\u201d\"}"
