          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"42d47189d7964780b8e8221c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It is a strange thing, but everyone who vanishes is supposedly seen in San Francisco. It must be a charming city, with all the attractions of the next world.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you suppose has happened to Basil?\u201d Dorian asked, raising his Burgundy to the light and wondering how he could speak of the matter with such composure.\\n\\n\u201cI haven\u2019t the faintest idea. If Basil elects to conceal himself, that is no concern of mine. If he is dead, I don\u2019t wish to think of him. Death is the one thing that ever frightens me. I detest it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy?\u201d the younger man asked tiredly.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d said Lord Henry, drawing the gilt trellis of an open vinaigrette beneath his nostrils, \u201cnowadays one can endure everything except that. Death and vulgarity are the only two facts in the nineteenth century that cannot be explained away. Let us take our coffee in the music-room, Dorian. You must play Chopin for me. The man my wife ran away with played Chopin beautifully. Poor Victoria! I was very fond of her. The house feels rather lonely without her. Naturally, married life is only a habit, and a bad one. Yet one regrets losing even one\u2019s worst habits. Perhaps those are the habits one regrets most. They form so necessary a part of one\u2019s personality.\u201d\\n\\nDorian made no reply, but got up from the table and went into the adjoining room, where he sat at the piano and let his fingers wander over the keys\u2019 black and white ivory. When the coffee arrived, he stopped, glanced across at Lord Henry, and said, \u201cHarry, has it ever occurred to you that Basil was murdered?\u201d\\n\\nLord Henry yawned. \u201cBasil was extremely popular, and he always carried a Waterbury watch. Why would anyone murder him? He was not intelligent enough to possess enemies. Certainly, he had extraordinary talent as a painter. But a man may paint like Velasquez and still be utterly dull. Basil was, in truth, rather dull. He interested me only once, when, years ago, he told me that he adored you wildly and that you were the ruling inspiration of his art.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI was very fond of Basil,\u201d Dorian said, sadness audible in his voice. \u201cBut don\u2019t people say he was murdered?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, some of the newspapers do. It does not strike me as likely at all. I know Paris contains dreadful places, but Basil was not the kind of man who would have visited them. He had no curiosity. That was his principal defect.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would you say, Harry, if I told you that I had murdered Basil?\u201d the younger man asked.\"}"
