          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3bec15e8b6eb2b92e2011660\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mighty voice\u2014we might call it inorganic, since we could not connect its sound with any mechanism of pipe or key\u2014fell silent, and the girl, turning to help her aged companion, finally noticed us.\\n\\nHe was her father, and since childhood she had guided his sightless steps. They were Germans from Saxony who, having emigrated there only a few years earlier, had formed new bonds with the surrounding villagers. Around the time the pestilence broke out, a young German student had joined them. Their simple history was easy to understand. He, a nobleman, loved the poor musician\u2019s beautiful daughter and followed them in flight from his friends\u2019 persecution; but soon the great leveller came with an unblunted scythe, mowing down the tall flowers of the field along with the grass. The young man was an early victim. She preserved herself for her father\u2019s sake. His blindness allowed her to maintain a deception that had begun by accident\u2014and now, as solitary beings and the only survivors in the land, he remained unaware of the change. He did not know that when he listened to his daughter\u2019s music, the silent mountains, lifeless lake, and unconscious trees were, apart from himself, her only listeners.\\n\\nOn the very day we arrived, she had been struck by the illness\u2019s symptoms. She was paralyzed with horror at the thought of leaving her aged, blind father alone on the empty earth; yet she lacked the courage to reveal the truth, and the sheer force of her desperation drove her to extraordinary effort. At the usual evening hour, she led him to the chapel; and though trembling and weeping for him, she played, without losing the rhythm or missing a note, the hymn written to celebrate the creation of the adorned earth, soon to become her tomb.\\n\\nWe came to her like visitors from heaven itself; her strained courage and barely maintained composure vanished at the sight of relief. With a shriek she rushed toward Adrian, clasped his knees, and, saying only, \u201cO save my father!\u201d opened, amid sobs and hysterical cries, the long-sealed floodgates of her grief.\\n\\nPoor girl! She and her father now lie side by side beneath the tall walnut tree where her lover rests, and which she had pointed out to us in her final moments. Her father, at last aware of his daughter\u2019s danger but unable to see the changes in her beloved face, stubbornly held her hand until death chilled and stiffened it. Even then he neither moved nor spoke, until twelve hours later, when merciful death carried him into unbroken rest. They lie beneath the earth, the tree their monument; the sacred place remains vivid in my memory, framed by the craggy Jura and the distant, immeasurable Alps. The spire of the church they attended still rises among the surrounding trees; and though her hand is cold, I still seem to hear the divine music they loved drifting around them, comforting their gentle ghosts.\"}"
