          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"36c06c5576d0b59391a584cd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I was sorry to hear Jim say that; it made him seem so humble. My conscience stirred me more strongly than ever, until at last I said to it, \u201cLeave me alone\u2014it isn\u2019t too late yet. I\u2019ll paddle ashore at first light and tell.\u201d At once I felt easy, happy, and light as a feather. All my troubles were gone. I began watching carefully for a light and half singing to myself. After a while, one appeared. Jim called out:\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019re safe, Huck, we\u2019re safe! Jump up and click your heels! That\u2019s old Cairo at last, I just know it!\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ll take the canoe and go see, Jim. It might not be, you know.\u201d\\n\\nHe jumped up, prepared the canoe, put his old coat in the bottom for me to sit on, and handed me the paddle. As I pushed off, he said:\\n\\n\u201cPretty soon I\u2019ll be shouting for joy, and I\u2019ll say it was all because of Huck. I\u2019m a free man, and I could never have been free if it hadn\u2019t been for Huck. Huck did it. Jim will never forget you, Huck. You\u2019re the best friend Jim\u2019s ever had, and you\u2019re the only friend old Jim has now.\u201d\\n\\nI paddled away, sweating to tell on him; but when he said this, it seemed to take all the strength out of me. I went more slowly then, and I wasn\u2019t sure whether I was glad I had started or not. When I was fifty yards away, Jim called:\\n\\n\u201cThere you go, faithful Huck\u2014the only white gentleman who ever kept his promise to old Jim.\u201d\\n\\nI felt sick. But I told myself, I have to do it\u2014I can\u2019t escape it. Just then a skiff came along with two men carrying guns. They stopped, and I stopped too. One of them said:\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s that over there?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA piece of a raft,\u201d I said.\\n\\n\u201cDo you belong on it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre there any men on it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly one, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, five escaped slaves ran off tonight up above the bend. Is your man white or black?\u201d\\n\\nI didn\u2019t answer right away. I tried, but the words wouldn\u2019t come. For a second or two I tried to gather my courage and say it, but I wasn\u2019t brave enough\u2014I didn\u2019t have the courage of a rabbit. I could feel myself weakening, so I stopped trying and said:\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019s white.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI suppose we\u2019ll go see for ourselves.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI wish you would,\u201d I said, \u201cbecause my father is there, and maybe you could help me tow the raft ashore where the light is.\u201d\"}"
