          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e420f7886b43b3c5d9498849\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI agree. It\u2019s the best thing. Would you just hold hands and swear that we\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh no, that won\u2019t do for this. That\u2019s good enough for little, worthless common things\u2014especially with girls, because _they_ go back on you anyway and blab if they get angry\u2014but there ought to be writing about a big thing like this. And blood.\u201d\\n\\nTom\u2019s whole being applauded the idea. It was deep, dark, and terrible; the hour, the circumstances, and the surroundings suited it perfectly. He picked up a clean pine shingle lying in the moonlight, took a small piece of \u201cred keel\u201d from his pocket, turned his work toward the moon, and painfully scrawled these lines, emphasizing every slow downward stroke by clamping his tongue between his teeth and easing the pressure on the upward strokes.\\n\\n\u201cHuck Finn and Tom Sawyer swear they will keep quiet about This and They wish They may Drop down dead in Their Tracks if They ever Tell and Rot.\u201d\\n\\nHuckleberry was filled with admiration for Tom\u2019s skill at writing and the grandeur of his language. He immediately took a pin from his lapel and was about to prick his flesh, but Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cWait! Don\u2019t do that. A pin\u2019s brass. It might have verdigris on it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s verdigris?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s poison. That\u2019s what it is. Just swallow some of it once\u2014you\u2019ll see.\u201d\\n\\nSo Tom unwound the thread from one of his needles, and each boy pricked the ball of his thumb and squeezed out a drop of blood. After many squeezes, Tom finally managed to sign his initials, using the ball of his little finger as a pen. Then he showed Huckleberry how to make an H and an F, and the oath was complete. They buried the shingle near the wall with grim ceremonies and incantations, and the fetters binding their tongues were considered locked, with the key thrown away.\\n\\nA figure now crept stealthily through a gap at the other end of the ruined building, but they did not notice it.\\n\\n\u201cTom,\u201d whispered Huckleberry, \u201cdoes this keep us from _ever_ telling\u2014_always_?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course it does. It doesn\u2019t matter _what_ happens; we have to keep quiet. We\u2019d drop dead\u2014don\u2019t _you_ know that?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I reckon that\u2019s true.\u201d\\n\\nThey continued whispering for a little while. Presently, a dog began a long, mournful howl just outside\u2014less than ten feet away. The boys suddenly clasped each other in terror.\\n\\n\u201cWhich of us does he mean?\u201d Huckleberry gasped.\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know\u2014look through the crack. Quick!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, _you_, Tom!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can\u2019t\u2014I can\u2019t _do_ it, Huck!\u201d\"}"
