          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"94b3adb2345fc2949eda936e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShall I ask them? Or Miss Bates? She is closer.\u2014And I do not know whether Miss Bates may not understand the inclinations of the other people as well as anyone. I think we do need a larger council. Suppose I go and invite Miss Bates to join us?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014if you please,\u201d said Mrs. Weston, hesitating a little, \u201cif you think she will be of any use.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will get nothing useful from Miss Bates,\u201d said Emma. \u201cShe will be full of delight and gratitude, but she will tell you nothing. She will not even listen to your questions. I see no advantage in consulting Miss Bates.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut she is so amusing, so extremely amusing! I am very fond of hearing Miss Bates talk. And I need not bring the whole family, you know.\u201d\\n\\nAt this point Mr. Weston joined them, and, hearing what was proposed, gave it his firm approval.\\n\\n\u201cYes, do, Frank.\u2014Go and fetch Miss Bates, and let us settle the matter at once. She will enjoy the plan, I am sure; and I do not know a better person for showing us how to overcome difficulties. Fetch Miss Bates. We are becoming a little too particular. She is a constant lesson in how to be happy. But fetch them both. Invite them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBoth, sir! Can the old lady\u2014?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe old lady! No, the young lady, of course. I shall think you a great fool, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I beg your pardon, sir. I did not remember at once. Certainly, if you wish it, I will try to persuade them both.\u201d And away he ran.\\n\\nLong before he returned, bringing the short, neat, brisk-moving aunt and her elegant niece, Mrs. Weston, like a good-natured woman and a good wife, had examined the passage again and found its problems far less serious than she had first imagined\u2014indeed, very slight; and there the difficulties of deciding ended. Everything else, at least in theory, was perfectly smooth. All the smaller arrangements of tables and chairs, lights and music, tea and supper, seemed to arrange themselves; or were left as mere trifles to be settled at any time between Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes.\u2014Everyone invited was certainly to come; Frank had already written to Enscombe to suggest staying a few days beyond his fortnight, which could not possibly be refused. And it was to be a delightful dance.\\n\\nWhen Miss Bates arrived, she most warmly agreed that it must be. As a counsellor she was unnecessary; but as an approver, a much safer role, she was genuinely welcome. Her approval, at once general and detailed, warm and unending, could not fail to please; and for another half-hour they all walked back and forth between the different rooms, some suggesting, some listening, and all happily enjoying the prospect.\"}"
