{"request_id":"e9326ad9bf391dab299880f7","rewritten_text":"He also had to consider Sybil’s father and mother, who were rather old-fashioned people and might object to the marriage if there were anything resembling a scandal, though he felt sure that, if he told them the full facts, they would be the first to appreciate the motives that had guided him. He therefore had every reason to choose poison. It was safe, certain, and quiet, and eliminated any need for painful scenes, to which, like most Englishmen, he had a deep-rooted objection.

He knew absolutely nothing about the science of poisons, however, and when the waiter seemed unable to find anything in the library except _Ruff’s Guide_ and _Bailey’s Magazine_, he searched the shelves himself. At last he found a handsomely bound edition of the _Pharmacopoeia_ and a copy of Erskine’s _Toxicology_, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the Buckingham’s oldest members. He had been elected by mistake for someone else, an error that so enraged the Committee that, when the real candidate appeared, they black-balled him unanimously. Lord Arthur was thoroughly puzzled by the technical terms in both books and had begun to regret not having paid more attention to his classics at Oxford when, in the second volume of Erskine, he found a very interesting and complete account of aconitine’s properties, written in reasonably clear English. It seemed exactly the poison he needed. Its effect was swift—indeed, almost immediate—entirely painless, and, when taken in a gelatine capsule, as Sir Mathew recommended, not at all unpleasant. He therefore noted on his shirt cuff the amount required for a fatal dose, replaced the books, and strolled up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the leading chemists. Mr. Pestle, who always served the aristocracy personally, was rather surprised by the order and murmured deferentially that a medical certificate would be necessary. But as soon as Lord Arthur explained that it was for a large Norwegian mastiff he was forced to have destroyed because it showed signs of incipient rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice in the calf, he declared himself completely satisfied, complimented Lord Arthur on his remarkable knowledge of toxicology, and had the prescription prepared at once.

Lord Arthur placed the capsule in a pretty little silver _bonbonnière_ he had seen in a Bond Street shop window, discarded Pestle and Humbey’s ugly pillbox, and drove immediately to Lady Clementina’s."}

