          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"88882ab0764c6f332abe552e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Merton was deeply distressed by the marriage being postponed for a second time, and Lady Julia, who had already ordered her wedding dress, did everything she could to persuade Sybil to end the engagement. Sybil loved her mother dearly, but she had placed her entire life in Lord Arthur\u2019s hands, and nothing Lady Julia said could shake her faith. Lord Arthur himself took days to recover from his terrible disappointment, and for a while his nerves were completely shattered. His excellent common sense soon returned, however, and his practical, sensible mind left him in no doubt about what he should do. Since poison had failed completely, dynamite, or some other kind of explosive, was clearly the next thing to try.\\n\\nHe therefore reviewed the list of his friends and relatives once more and, after careful thought, decided to blow up his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean, a man of great culture and learning, was passionately fond of clocks and possessed a remarkable collection of timepieces, dating from the fifteenth century to the present day. Lord Arthur thought this hobby of the good Dean\u2019s offered an excellent opportunity to carry out his plan. Finding an explosive device, of course, was an entirely different matter. The London Directory provided no information, and he felt there was little point in going to Scotland Yard, since they never seemed to know anything about the dynamite faction\u2019s movements until after an explosion, and not much even then.\\n\\nSuddenly he remembered his friend Rouvaloff, a young Russian with strongly revolutionary views, whom he had met at Lady Windermere\u2019s during the winter. Count Rouvaloff was said to be writing a life of Peter the Great and to have come to England to study documents concerning the Tsar\u2019s stay in the country as a ship\u2019s carpenter. It was generally suspected, however, that he was a Nihilist agent, 