          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7344610040bd90b3669a9e2c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But that is of little importance.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI may as well ask,\u201d she replied, \u201cwhy, when you clearly meant to offend and insult me, you chose to tell me that you liked me against your will, your reason, and even your character. Was that not an excuse for rudeness, if I had been rude? But I have other reasons to resent you. You know I do. If my own feelings had not already decided against you, if they had been indifferent, or even favourable, do you think anything could persuade me to accept the man who has helped ruin, perhaps forever, the happiness of a dearly loved sister?\u201d\\n\\nAs she spoke, Mr. Darcy changed colour; but the emotion quickly passed, and he listened without trying to interrupt her as she continued\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI have every reason to think badly of you. Nothing can excuse the unjust and ungenerous part you played there. You cannot deny that you were the chief, if not the only, cause of separating them; of exposing one to the world\u2019s criticism for being fickle and unstable, and the other to its mockery for disappointed hopes, while bringing them both the deepest misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe paused and saw, with great indignation, that he listened in a way that showed no remorse at all. He even looked at her with a smile of false disbelief.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny that you did it?\u201d she repeated.\\n\\nHe then replied with forced calm, \u201cI do not wish to deny that I did everything I could to separate my friend from your sister, or that I am pleased with my success. I have treated him more kindly than I have treated myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth refused to show that she had noticed this polite remark, but she understood its meaning, and it was unlikely to win her over.\\n\\n\u201cBut this is not the only matter on which my dislike is based,\u201d she continued. \u201cMy opinion of you had been settled long before it happened. Your character was revealed in the account Mr. Wickham gave me many months ago. What can you say about that? What imagined act of friendship can you use to defend yourself? Or what false account can you use to deceive others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou take a strong interest in that gentleman\u2019s affairs,\u201d said Darcy, less calmly now, his face growing red.\\n\\n\u201cWhoever knows what misfortunes he has suffered could help feeling concern for him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis misfortunes!\u201d Darcy repeated contemptuously. \u201cYes, his misfortunes have indeed been great.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd caused by you!\u201d Elizabeth cried passionately. \u201cYou have reduced him to his present state of poverty\u2014comparative poverty.\u201d\"}"
