          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f2634f6e414128dcaaf65b75\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was finally settled that we should share lodgings during my stay in the city; and since my worldly means were somewhat less strained than his, I was allowed to bear the cost of renting and furnishing, in a manner suited to the rather fanciful gloom of our united dispositions, a weathered and grotesque mansion. It had long stood empty because of superstitions we did not trouble to investigate, and was sinking toward ruin in a secluded, desolate quarter of the Faubourg St. Germain.\\n\\nIf the world had known the pattern of our existence there, it would have judged us madmen\u2014though perhaps harmless ones. Our isolation was complete. We received no callers. In fact, I had taken care to conceal the place of our retreat from my former companions; and Dupin had not, for years, either known anyone in Paris or been known there himself. We lived solely within ourselves.\\n\\nIt was one of my friend\u2019s whims (for what other name can I give it?) to love the night for its own sake; and, as with all his other oddities, I quietly adopted this one, surrendering myself wholly to his extravagant fancies. The black goddess could not remain with us at all hours; but we could imitate her presence. At the first light of day we shut every untidy shutter of our old house, and lit two candles whose heavy perfume produced only the most ghastly and feeble illumination. By their aid we occupied our minds with dreams\u2014reading, writing, or talking\u2014until the clock announced the arrival of genuine Darkness. Then we went out arm in arm, continuing the day\u2019s discussions or wandering far and wide until late, seeking among the strange lights and shadows of the crowded city that boundless mental stimulation which quiet observation can provide.\\n\\nOn such occasions I could not fail to notice and admire\u2014though his rich imagination had led me to expect it\u2014a remarkable analytical power