{"request_id":"b19b5fdc5d900efa5bdf69e7","rewritten_text":"Few could claim to possess so pure a heart, so much genuine humility of spirit, united with such firm confidence in her own integrity and faith in that of others. From birth she had led a secluded life. Her mother died when she was very young, but her father had devoted himself to her education—He held many unusual opinions, which shaped the system he had followed in bringing her up—She knew the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those of England who had lived several centuries before, while she was almost unaware of the events of the present day: she had read scarcely any writers of the past fifty years, yet apart from this her reading had been remarkably broad. Thus, though she seemed less acquainted with the secrets of life and society than he, her knowledge was more profound and rested upon firmer ground; and even had her beauty and gentleness not enchanted him, her understanding would always have kept his in bondage. He regarded her as his guide, and his devotion was such that he took pleasure in heightening, in his own thoughts, the feeling of inferiority she sometimes inspired in him.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. On that account he left college, and for a time abandoning his former companions, withdrew to the vicinity of his Diana, finding all his comfort in her gentle voice and still dearer embraces. This brief separation from his associates gave him the courage to declare his independence. He felt that, whatever ridicule they might voice concerning his proposed marriage, they would not dare show it once the marriage had occurred; so, having sought and with some difficulty secured the consent of his guardian, and more readily that of his beloved’s father, he told no one else of his purpose, and before reaching his twentieth birthday had become Diana’s husband.

He loved her passionately, and her tenderness possessed a charm that left him unable to think of anything but her. He asked several college friends to visit him, but their frivolity repelled him. Diana had torn away the veil that had previously kept him in childhood: he had become a man, and wondered how he could ever have shared the empty phrases and notions of his fellow students, or for an instant feared the judgment of people such as these. He abandoned his former friendships, not through fickleness, but because they were truly beneath him."}

