{"request_id":"f95aaebb1db683d34037acbe","rewritten_text":"I drilled a hole in the end of the keg above the pitcher and fitted it with a soft-wood plug, cut in a tapering, cone-like shape. I pushed the plug in or pulled it out until, after several tests, it reached exactly the right tightness: the water seeping through the hole and dripping into the pitcher below would fill it to the brim in sixty minutes. This was easy to determine by observing how much of the pitcher filled during a given period. Once this was arranged, the rest of the plan was clear. I had set up my bed on the floor of the car so that, when I lay down, my head was directly beneath the pitcher’s mouth. It was obvious that after an hour, when the pitcher became full, it would overflow through its mouth, which was slightly lower than the rim. It was equally obvious that water falling from a height of more than four feet could only land on my face, and would certainly wake me instantly, even from the deepest sleep.

“It was nearly eleven when I finished these arrangements, and I went straight to bed, fully confident that my invention would work. I was not disappointed. Exactly every sixty minutes, my reliable timekeeper woke me. I emptied the pitcher into the keg’s bung-hole, carried out the duties of the condenser, and then returned to bed. These regular interruptions disturbed my sleep less than I had expected. When I finally rose for the day, it was seven o’clock, and the sun stood many degrees above the edge of my horizon.

“April 3. I found the balloon at a truly immense height, and the earth’s apparent curve had grown noticeably greater. Far below me in the ocean was a group of black specks, which were undoubtedly islands. Far to the north, I saw a thin, white, intensely bright line or streak along the horizon. I had no doubt that it was the southern edge of the ice of the Polar Sea. My curiosity was strongly aroused, because I hoped to travel much farther north and might perhaps, at some point, find myself directly above the Pole. I now regretted that my great height would prevent me from making as accurate a survey as I wished."}

