          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3953de66eb483f5469f4fcd1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You won\u2019t, will you? Oh, you precious flat!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s naughty, ain\u2019t it, Oliver?\u201d asked Charley Bates. \u201cHe\u2019ll get scragged, won\u2019t he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know what that means,\u201d Oliver replied.\\n\\n\u201cSomething like this, old fellow,\u201d said Charley. As he spoke, Master Bates seized one end of his neckerchief and held it upright in the air; then he let his head fall onto his shoulder and jerked a strange sound through his teeth, thus showing by a lively pantomime that scragging and hanging meant the same thing.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s what it means,\u201d said Charley. \u201cLook at the way he stares, Jack! I never saw such prime company as that \u2019ere boy; he\u2019ll be the death of me, I know he will.\u201d Master Charley Bates, after laughing heartily once more, took up his pipe again, tears standing in his eyes.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve been badly brought up,\u201d said the Dodger, examining his boots with great satisfaction after Oliver had polished them. \u201cFagin will make something of you, though, or you\u2019ll be the first he ever had who proved unprofitable. You\u2019d better start at once; you\u2019ll come to the trade much sooner than you imagine, and you\u2019re only wasting time, Oliver.\u201d\\n\\nMaster Bates supported this advice with several moral warnings of his own. When these were exhausted, he and his friend Mr. Dawkins launched into an enthusiastic account of the many pleasures belonging to the life they led, mixed with various hints that Oliver\u2019s best course would be to win Fagin\u2019s favour without delay, by the methods they themselves had used to obtain it.\\n\\n\u201cAnd always remember this, Nolly,\u201d said the Dodger, as they heard the Jew unlocking the door above, \u201cif you don\u2019t take fogels and tickers\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the use of talking like that?\u201d interrupted Master Bates; \u201che doesn\u2019t know what you mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf you don\u2019t take pocket-handkerchiefs and watches,\u201d said the Dodger, bringing his speech down to Oliver\u2019s level, \u201csome other fellow will; then the fellows who lose them will be worse off, and you\u2019ll be worse off too, while nobody will be half a ha\u2019p\u2019orth better except the chaps who get them\u2014and you\u2019ve as good a right to them as they have.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly, certainly!\u201d said the Jew, who had entered without Oliver noticing. \u201cIt all lies in a nutshell, my dear; in a nutshell\u2014take the Dodger\u2019s word for it. Ha! ha! ha! He knows the catechism of his trade.\u201d\\n\\nThe old man rubbed his hands together gleefully as he confirmed the Dodger\u2019s reasoning in these words, and chuckled with pleasure at his pupil\u2019s skill.\\n\\nThe conversation went no further then, for the Jew had come home with Miss Betsy and a gentleman Oliver had never seen before. The Dodger addressed him as Tom Chitling; and, after lingering on the stairs to exchange a few gallantries with the lady, he now appeared.\"}"
