          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"25c3fc37f8d9649678ff42d1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was invitation enough.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, my dear, you must know that Mrs. Long says Netherfield has been rented by a wealthy young man from the north of England. He arrived on Monday in a carriage drawn by four horses to see the house, liked it so much that he immediately made an agreement with Mr. Morris, and will move in before Michaelmas. Some of his servants are to be in the house by the end of next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat is his name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBingley.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he married or single?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, single, my dear, certainly! A single man with a large fortune\u2014four or five thousand a year. What a wonderful thing for our girls!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow so? How could it affect them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d his wife replied, \u201chow can you be so tiresome? Surely you know I am thinking of his marrying one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that his purpose in moving here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPurpose? Nonsense! How can you say that? But he may very likely fall in love with one of them, so you must visit him as soon as he arrives.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI see no need for that. You and the girls may go\u2014or you may send them alone, which might be even better; since you are as handsome as any of them, Mr. Bingley might prefer you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear, you flatter me. I certainly have had my share of beauty, but I do not claim to be anything special now. When a woman has five grown daughters, she should stop thinking about her own beauty.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn such cases, a woman usually has little beauty left to think about.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut, my dear, you really must go and see Mr. Bingley when he comes to the neighbourhood.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is more than I can promise, I assure you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut think of your daughters. Just imagine what a fine marriage it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas are determined to go for that very reason; as you know, they usually do not visit newcomers. Indeed, you must go, because it will be impossible for us to visit him if you do not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are surely being too careful. I am sure Mr. Bingley will be pleased to see you; and I will send a short note with you, assuring him that I gladly consent to his marrying whichever girl he chooses\u2014though I must say a good word for my little Lizzy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI insist that you do no such thing. Lizzy is no better than the others: and I am sure she is not half as pretty as Jane, nor half as good-natured as Lydia.\u201d\"}"
