{"request_id":"21a06b340b0c582eb3bbcba8","rewritten_text":"We endured several miserable hours until eleven, when the trial was scheduled to begin. Since my father and the rest of the family were required to appear as witnesses, I went with them to the court. Throughout that wretched travesty of justice, I suffered a living torment. It was to be determined whether the product of my curiosity and unlawful experiments would bring about the deaths of two fellow human beings: one, a smiling infant filled with innocence and happiness; the other, murdered in a far more terrible manner, with every circumstance of disgrace that could make the crime unforgettable in its horror. Justine, too, was a worthy girl, endowed with qualities that promised a happy life; now everything was to be buried in a dishonored grave, and I was the cause! A thousand times I would rather have declared myself guilty of the crime charged against Justine, but I had been absent when it occurred, and such a confession would have been dismissed as the ravings of a madman, leaving her, who suffered because of me, unvindicated.

Justine looked composed. She wore mourning, and her face, always pleasing, was made exquisitely beautiful by the solemnity of her emotions. Yet she seemed assured of her innocence and did not tremble, though thousands stared at and cursed her, for any kindness her beauty might otherwise have inspired had been erased from the spectators’ minds by their imagined sense of the enormity she was believed to have committed. She was calm, but her calmness was plainly forced; and since her earlier confusion had been presented as evidence of guilt, she had steeled herself to appear courageous. On entering the court, she glanced around and soon saw where we sat. A tear appeared to cloud her eye when she recognized us, but she quickly mastered herself, and an expression of sorrowful affection seemed to proclaim her complete innocence.

The trial opened, and after the advocate for the prosecution had presented the charge, several witnesses were summoned. A number of unusual circumstances united against her, and they might have shaken anyone who lacked the certainty of her innocence that I possessed. She had been away all night on the night of the murder, and near morning a market-woman had seen her not far from the place where the murdered child’s body was later discovered. The woman asked what she was doing there, but Justine behaved strangely and gave only a confused, unintelligible reply. She returned home around eight o’clock, and when asked where she had spent the night, she answered that she had been searching for the child and urgently asked whether anything had been learned about him."}

