          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"14835041d53eaf0591efbfb5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma was not required, by anything she later discovered, to withdraw her poor opinion of Mrs. Elton. Her judgment had been largely correct. As Mrs. Elton appeared to her at this second meeting, so she appeared whenever they met again\u2014self-important, presumptuous, familiar, ignorant, and ill-bred. She possessed a little beauty and a little accomplishment, but so little judgment that she imagined herself arriving with superior knowledge of the world to enliven and improve a country neighbourhood; and she believed Miss Hawkins had occupied such a position in society that only Mrs. Elton\u2019s own importance could exceed it.\\n\\nThere was no reason to think Mr. Elton\u2019s opinion differed at all from his wife\u2019s. He seemed not only happy with her, but proud of her. He had the air of congratulating himself on having brought such a woman to Highbury, one whom even Miss Woodhouse could not equal; and most of her new acquaintances, inclined to praise, or unaccustomed to judging, following Miss Bates\u2019s goodwill, or simply assuming that the bride must be as clever and agreeable as she claimed, were perfectly satisfied. Thus Mrs. Elton\u2019s praises passed from one person to another as they naturally would, unchecked by Miss Woodhouse, who readily continued her first contribution and spoke graciously of her as \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed.\u201d\\n\\nIn one respect Mrs. Elton became even worse than she had seemed at first. Her feelings towards Emma changed. Probably offended by the little encouragement her offers of intimacy received, she withdrew in turn and gradually became much colder and more distant; and although the result was agreeable, the ill will that caused it necessarily increased Emma\u2019s dislike. Her manners, and Mr. Elton\u2019s as well, were unpleasant towards Harriet. They were mocking and neglectful. Emma hoped this would quickly cure Harriet; but the feelings that could inspire such conduct lowered them both considerably. There could be no doubt that poor Harriet\u2019s attachment had been offered up to marital openness, and that her own part in the story, presented in the least favourable light for her and the most comforting for him, had very likely been revealed as well. She was, naturally, the object of their shared dislike. When they had nothing else to discuss, it would always be easy to begin attacking Miss Woodhouse; and the hostility they dared not show by openly disrespecting her found a wider outlet in their contemptuous treatment of Harriet.\\n\\nMrs. Elton took a strong liking to Jane Fairfax, and did so from the beginning. It was not merely that, while she was at war with one young woman, the other might naturally seem more appealing; she had liked Jane from the very first. Nor was she content simply to express a natural and reasonable admiration. Without being asked, invited, or entitled to do so, she insisted on offering her assistance and friendship. Before Emma had lost her confidence, and around their third meeting, she heard all Mrs. Elton\u2019s chivalrous plans on the subject.\"}"
