{"request_id":"fc286c5b2fa1789b652ff91e","rewritten_text":"During his brief stay this time, Emma had scarcely seen him; but she had seen enough to feel that their first meeting was past, and to perceive that the mixture of pique and pretension now displayed in his manner had not improved him. Indeed, she was beginning to wonder greatly that she had ever found him agreeable at all; and the sight of him was so closely bound up with unpleasant feelings, that, except in a moral sense—as a penance, a lesson, and a useful humiliation to her own mind—she would have been grateful to know she need never see him again. She wished him well enough; but he caused her pain, and his welfare twenty miles away would have given her the greatest satisfaction.

The discomfort of his remaining in Highbury, however, would certainly be reduced by his marriage. Many anxious thoughts would be spared, and many awkward situations made easier by it. A _Mrs._ _Elton_ would provide an excuse for any alteration in their intercourse; their former intimacy might fade without comment. It would be almost like beginning their civil acquaintance anew.

Of the lady herself, Emma thought very little. She was doubtless good enough for Mr. Elton; accomplished enough for Highbury—and handsome enough, perhaps, to appear plain beside Harriet. As for connexion, Emma felt entirely at ease, convinced that, despite all his loudly asserted claims and contempt for Harriet, he had achieved nothing. On that point, the truth seemed discoverable. _What_ she was might remain uncertain; but _who_ she was could be learned; and, apart from the 10,000 l., she did not seem in any way superior to Harriet. She possessed neither name, blood, nor alliance. Miss Hawkins was the younger of two daughters of a Bristol—merchant, naturally, he must be called; but since the entire profits of his mercantile career appeared so modest, it was fair to suppose that the dignity of his trade had been equally modest. She had been accustomed to spend part of every winter in Bath; but Bristol was her home, the very centre of Bristol. Though her parents had died several years before, an uncle remained—in the law line—nothing more distinctly respectable was ventured about him than that he was in the law line; and the daughter had lived with him. Emma imagined him to be the drudge of some attorney, too dull to advance. And all the magnificence of the connexion appeared to rest upon the elder sister, who was _very_ _well_ _married_, to a gentleman in a _great_ _way_ near Bristol, who kept two carriages! That was the conclusion of the story; that was Miss Hawkins’s glory."}

