          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7e159621399d3fe00481c90b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After I had sent them in, I could not stay away from the places where they were, feeling as though they were more hopeful and less desperate when I was near. In this unreasonable agitation and distress of mind, I would wander the streets in the evening, passing those offices and houses where I had left the petitions. Even now, the tired western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night, with their lines of severe, closed mansions and their long rows of lamps, seem melancholy to me because of that association.\\n\\nThe daily visits I was allowed to make him had now been shortened, and he was watched more closely. Seeing, or imagining, that I was suspected of intending to carry poison to him, I asked to be searched before sitting beside his bed, and told the officer who was always present that I was ready to do anything that might convince him of the purity of my intentions. No one was unkind to him or to me. There was a duty to perform, and it was performed, but without harshness. The officer always assured me that he was worse, and some of the other sick prisoners in the room, together with prisoners who attended them as nurses\u2014criminals, but, thank God, not without kindness\u2014always gave the same account.\\n\\nAs the days passed, I observed increasingly that he would lie quietly gazing at the white ceiling, his face lacking all brightness until one of my words lit it for a moment, after which the light would fade again. At times he was nearly, or entirely, unable to speak; then he would reply by gently pressing my hand, and I gradually came to understand him very well.\\n\\nThe days had reached ten when I saw a greater change in him than before. His eyes were directed towards the door, and they brightened when I entered.\\n\\n\u201cDear boy,\u201d he said, as I sat beside his bed, \u201cI thought you was late. But I knowed you couldn\u2019t be that.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is exactly the time,\u201d I said. \u201cI waited for it at the gate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou always waits at the gate; don\u2019t you, dear boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. So as not to lose a moment of the time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank\u2019ee, dear boy, thank\u2019ee. God bless you! You\u2019ve never deserted me, dear boy.\u201d\\n\\nI pressed his hand without speaking, unable to forget that I had once intended to desert him.\\n\\n\u201cAnd the best thing of all,\u201d he said, \u201cyou\u2019ve been more comfortable alonger me, since I was under a dark cloud, than when the sun shone.\u201d\"}"
