          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4f107ad445c2ed16dc7810af\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This brief explanation with Mr. Knightley gave Emma great pleasure. It became one of the pleasant recollections of the ball, which she strolled about the lawn next morning to savour.\u2014She was exceedingly glad that they had reached so good an understanding concerning the Eltons, and that their opinions of husband and wife agreed so closely; while his praise of Harriet, and his admission in her favour, was especially gratifying. The Eltons\u2019 impertinence, which for several minutes had threatened to spoil the remainder of her evening, had proved the cause of some of its greatest satisfactions; and she anticipated another happy consequence\u2014the cure of Harriet\u2019s infatuation.\u2014From Harriet\u2019s way of describing the matter before they left the ballroom, she entertained strong hopes. It appeared that her eyes had suddenly been opened, and that she could now perceive Mr. Elton was not the superior being she had imagined. The fever had passed, and Emma had little fear that harmful politeness would make the pulse beat quickly again. She relied on the Eltons\u2019 ill feelings to provide all the pointed neglect that might still be needed.\u2014Harriet sensible, Frank Churchill not too deeply in love, and Mr. Knightley not disposed to quarrel with her\u2014how happy a summer lay before her! She was not to see Frank Churchill that morning. He had told her he could not give himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, since he was expected home by midday. She felt no disappointment. Having settled all these matters, examined them from every side, and set them right, she was turning toward the house, her spirits renewed for the claims of the two little boys and their grandfather, when the large iron sweep-gate opened, and two people entered whom she had never expected to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning upon his arm\u2014Harriet herself!\u2014One moment was enough to assure her that something extraordinary had occurred. Harriet looked pale and terrified, while he was attempting to comfort her.\u2014The iron gates and the front door stood less than twenty yards apart; all three were soon in the hall, and Harriet immediately sank into a chair and fainted. A young lady who faints must be restored; questions must be answered, and surprises explained. Such occurrences are highly interesting, but their suspense cannot continue long. In a few minutes Emma knew the whole story. Miss Smith and Miss Bickerton, another parlour-boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s who had also attended the ball, had gone out together and taken the Richmond road, which, though it seemed public enough to be safe, had caused them alarm.\u2014About half a mile beyond Highbury, after a sudden turn and with elms casting deep shade on either side, it became for a considerable distance extremely secluded; and when the young ladies had proceeded some way along it, they suddenly noticed, a short distance ahead, on a wider patch of grass beside the road, a group of gipsies.\"}"
