{"request_id":"16f13055a43b1e9bb2625c47","rewritten_text":"The young people were all eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of going there himself; it was only seventeen miles from Uppercross, and although it was November, the weather was by no means unpleasant. In short, Louisa, the most eager of them all, had decided to go. Besides the pleasure of doing as she pleased, she now had the added conviction that there was merit in standing by her own choice. She overcame all her father and mother’s wishes to postpone the visit until summer; and so to Lyme they were to go—Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth.

Their first careless plan had been to leave in the morning and return at night, but Mr. Musgrove would not agree to this for the sake of his horses. And when the matter was considered sensibly, a day in mid-November would leave little time to see a new place, once the seven hours required by the nature of the roads for the journey there and back had been deducted. They were therefore to spend the night there and not be expected home until dinner the following day. This was considered a considerable improvement. Though they all met at the Great House at a rather early breakfast hour and set off with great punctuality, it was well past noon before the two carriages—Mr. Musgrove’s coach, carrying the four ladies, and Charles’s curricle, in which he drove Captain Wentworth—were descending the long hill into Lyme and entering the still steeper street of the town itself. It was clear they would have little more than time to look around before the day’s light and warmth were gone.

After securing rooms and ordering dinner at one of the inns, the next thing to do was unquestionably to walk straight down to the sea. They had come too late in the year for any entertainment or variety that Lyme, as a public resort, might offer. The rooms were closed, nearly all the visitors had departed, and scarcely any families remained except the residents. Since there is nothing remarkable in the buildings themselves, it is the town’s striking situation that attracts attention: the principal street seeming almost to hurry into the water; the walk to the Cobb, curving around the pleasant little bay, which in season is lively with bathing machines and visitors; the Cobb itself, with its old features and new improvements; and the very beautiful line of cliffs extending eastward from the town. A stranger would have to be remarkably unobservant not to find charms in Lyme’s immediate surroundings that made him wish to know the place better."}

