inued to fall with dreadful rapidity and was now no more than half a mile from the surface. As a final measure, after removing my coat, hat, and boots, I cut the car itself loose from the balloon; it was of considerable weight. Clinging with both hands to the netting, I had barely time to notice that the entire country, as far as I could see, was thickly dotted with tiny dwellings before I plunged headfirst into the center of a fantastical-looking city, among a vast crowd of ugly little people. Not one spoke a word or made the slightest effort to help me. They simply stood there like a group of idiots, grinning absurdly and looking sideways at me and my balloon, with their hands planted on their hips.\u201d\"}"
