{"request_id":"4d2e1f0342a86cdccde2b6cc","rewritten_text":"We at last lightened the box by taking out two thirds of its contents, after which, with some difficulty, we managed to lift it from the hole. The removed articles were placed among the brambles, and the dog was left to watch them, Jupiter being strictly instructed neither, on any account, to leave the spot nor to open his mouth before we returned. We then hastened home with the chest, reaching the hut safely, though after exhausting labor, at one in the morning. As tired as we were, nothing more could immediately be done. We rested until two and ate supper; then set off for the hills, taking three strong sacks which, fortunately, were on the premises. A little before four we reached the pit, divided the remaining booty among us as evenly as possible, and, leaving the holes unfilled, started once more for the hut. There, for the second time, we laid down our golden burdens just as the first weak rays of dawn appeared above the treetops in the East.

We were now completely worn out; yet the intense excitement of the moment would not allow us to rest. After an uneasy sleep lasting three or four hours, we rose, apparently by mutual agreement, to inspect our treasure.

The chest had been filled to the top, and we devoted the entire day, along with most of the following night, to examining its contents. Nothing had been arranged; everything had been thrown in together without order. After carefully sorting it all, we discovered that our wealth was even greater than we had first imagined. The coins amounted to somewhat more than four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, their values estimated as accurately as possible from the tables of the period. There was not a single piece of silver. Everything was antique gold of many kinds—French, Spanish, and German currency, together with a few English guineas and some counters, examples of which we had never seen. Several coins were unusually large and heavy, but so worn that their inscriptions were unreadable. There was no American money. The jewels were harder to value. We found diamonds—some exceptionally large and fine—one hundred and ten altogether, none of them small; eighteen rubies of extraordinary brilliance; three hundred and ten emeralds, all very beautiful; and twenty-one sapphires, along with an opal. All these stones had been removed from their settings and cast loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we recovered among the other gold, seemed to have been battered with hammers, apparently to prevent their identification."}

