          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"17e619f89910a76e3a0c57e1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I don't spring at him suddenly, risking a clumsy half-measure; no, I move far off to one side and come at him by degrees, so he never dreams I mean to strike him; then, all at once, he is flat on his back, unable to understand how it happened. That was how I tackled brother Dowley. I began speaking in an easy, idle fashion, as though merely passing the time; and the oldest man alive could not have traced my starting point and guessed where I meant to end:\\n\\n\u201cBoys, there are many strange things about law, custom, usage, and all that sort of matter, when you examine them; yes, and about the course and advance of human opinion and action, too. There are written laws\u2014they perish; but there are unwritten laws\u2014_they_ endure forever. Consider the unwritten law of wages: it declares that they must rise, little by little, throughout the centuries. Observe how it operates. We know what wages are now, in this place and that and yonder; we take an average and call it the wages of to-day. We know what wages were a hundred years ago, and what they were two hundred years ago; that is as far back as we can go, but it is enough to show us the law of progress, the amount and rate of each recurring increase; and thus, without a document to guide us, we can come fairly near deciding what wages were three, four, and five hundred years ago. Good, so far. Do we stop there? No. We cease looking backward, turn about, and apply the law to the future. My friends, I can tell you what people's wages will be at any future date you care to name, for hundreds upon hundreds of years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. In seven hundred years wages will have risen to six times their present amount hereabouts, and farm hands will receive 3 cents a day, while mechanics will get 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI wish I might die now and live then!\u201d interrupted Smug, the wheelwright, a greedy light shining in his eye.\\n\\n\u201cAnd that is not all; they will receive their board besides\u2014such as it is: it will not fatten them. Two hundred and fifty years later\u2014mark this now\u2014a mechanic's wages will be\u2014remember, this is law, not guesswork; a mechanic's wages will then be _twenty_ cents a day!\u201d\\n\\nThere was a general gasp of astonishment and awe, while Dickon the mason murmured, with eyes and hands raised:\"}"
