{"request_id":"f9893daf2998817747629327","rewritten_text":"When he reached the top of the stairs, he pulled himself together and decided to give his famous demonic laugh. He had found it very useful on more than one occasion. People said it had turned Lord Raker’s wig gray in a single night, and it had certainly caused three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses to resign before completing their first month. So he laughed his most terrible laugh until the old vaulted ceiling echoed again and again. But scarcely had the frightening echo faded when a door opened, and Mrs. Otis appeared in a light blue dressing gown. “I’m afraid you’re feeling quite unwell,” she said, “so I’ve brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If it’s indigestion, you’ll find it an excellent remedy.” The ghost glared at her furiously and immediately began preparing to turn himself into a large black dog, a trick for which he was rightly famous. The family doctor had always blamed it for the permanent stupidity of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Honorable Thomas Horton. But the sound of footsteps approaching made him pause in his dreadful plan. Instead, he made himself faintly phosphorescent and disappeared with a deep graveyard groan, just as the twins reached him.

When he got back to his room, he completely collapsed and became overwhelmed by violent agitation. The twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s crude materialism were naturally very irritating, but what upset him most was that he had been unable to wear the suit of armor. He had hoped that even modern Americans would be thrilled by the sight of a Specter in Armor, if not for any sensible reason, then at least out of respect for their national poet Longfellow. The ghost himself had passed many weary hours enjoying Longfellow’s graceful, appealing poetry when the Cantervilles were staying in town. Besides, it was his own armor. He had worn it with great success at the Kenilworth tournament and had received high praise for it from none other than the Virgin Queen herself. Yet when he put it on, the weight of the enormous breastplate and steel helmet completely overwhelmed him. He fell heavily onto the stone floor, badly scraping both knees and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward, he was extremely ill and scarcely left his room, except to keep the bloodstain properly maintained. However, by taking excellent care of himself, he recovered and decided to make a third attempt to frighten the United States Minister and his family."}

