          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d90d27f38f23566d03f36134\",\"rewritten_text\":\"While these events were occurring in the country workhouse, Mr. Fagin sat in the old den\u2014the very room from which the girl had taken Oliver\u2014musing over a dull, smoky fire. A pair of bellows rested on his knee, with which he had seemingly been trying to stir it into livelier flame; but he had sunk into profound thought. His arms folded upon them, his chin supported by his thumbs, he gazed absently at the rusty bars.\\n\\nAt a table behind him sat the Artful Dodger, Master Charles Bates, and Mr. Chitling, all absorbed in a game of whist; the Artful playing dummy against Master Bates and Mr. Chitling. The first-named gentleman\u2019s face, always remarkably intelligent, gained still greater interest from his close attention to the game and his careful study of Mr. Chitling\u2019s hand. Whenever opportunity offered, he cast upon it a number of earnest looks, wisely directing his own play according to what he observed in his neighbour\u2019s cards. As the night was cold, the Dodger wore his hat, as, indeed, he often did indoors. He also kept a clay pipe between his teeth, removing it only briefly when he found it necessary to seek refreshment from a quart pot on the table, already filled with gin-and-water for the company.\\n\\nMaster Bates likewise watched the play; but, being more excitable than his accomplished friend, he applied himself more often to the gin-and-water and made many jokes and irrelevant observations, all most improper in a scientific rubber. The Artful, relying on their close friendship, took occasion more than once to reason solemnly with his companion about these offences. Master Bates accepted every remonstrance in excellent humour, merely asking his friend to be \u201cblowed,\u201d or to put his head in a sack, or answering with some other neatly fashioned jest of the same description, whose aptness greatly delighted Mr. Chitling. It was curious that the