{"request_id":"22a889cc4b73b16088eab031","rewritten_text":"I next bored a hole in the keg’s end above the pitcher, and inserted a soft-wood plug, shaped to taper like a cone. I pushed this plug farther in or drew it out, as required, until, after several trials, it reached precisely the degree of tightness at which the water seeping through the opening and dropping into the pitcher below would fill it to the brim in sixty minutes. This, naturally, was quickly and easily determined by observing what proportion of the pitcher filled during any given interval. Once this was arranged, the remainder of the plan was plain. I had fashioned my bed on the floor of the car so that, when lying down, my head came directly beneath the pitcher’s mouth. It was clear that, after an hour, the filled pitcher would overflow, and overflow at its mouth, which stood somewhat below the rim. It was equally clear that water falling from a height of more than four feet could do nothing but descend upon my face, with the certain result of waking me instantly, even from the deepest sleep in the world.

“It was fully eleven when I had finished these preparations, and I at once went to bed, entirely confident in my invention’s effectiveness. Nor was I disappointed. Exactly every sixty minutes I was awakened by my faithful chronometer; after emptying the pitcher into the keg’s bung-hole and carrying out the condenser’s duties, I returned to bed. These orderly interruptions disturbed my sleep even less than I had expected; and when I finally rose for the day, it was seven o’clock, while the sun had climbed many degrees above my horizon.

“April 3d. I found the balloon at a truly immense altitude, and the earth’s apparent convexity had increased considerably. Far below me in the ocean was a group of black specks, which were undoubtedly islands. In the distant north I saw a narrow, white, intensely brilliant line, or streak, along the horizon’s edge, and I had no doubt that it was the southern disk of the Polar Sea’s ice. My curiosity was strongly aroused, since I hoped to continue much farther north and might perhaps, at some point, find myself directly over the Pole itself. I now regretted that my great elevation would then prevent me from making as accurate a survey as I desired."}

