{"request_id":"0b46c639ec51508cdc2e0e6f","rewritten_text":"I cannot convey the rapturous joy with which I turned from political quarrels at home, and the physical ills of distant lands, to my own beloved home, that chosen dwelling of goodness and affection; to peace, and the exchange of every sacred sympathy. Had I never left Windsor, these feelings would not have been so powerful; but in Greece I had been exposed to fear and grievous change. There, after a season of anxiety and sorrow, I had watched two depart whose very names represented greatness and virtue. Yet such sorrows could never enter the domestic circle that remained to me, while, secluded in our cherished forest, we lived in tranquillity. Some slight alteration, indeed, came with the passing years; and time, as it always does, marked our pleasures and hopes with signs of mortality. Idris, the most loving wife, sister, and friend, was a gentle and devoted mother. With her, unlike many, motherhood was no pastime; it was a passion. We had three children; one, the second in age, died while I was in Greece. This had mingled grief and fear with the triumphant, rapturous feelings of maternity. Before that event, the little beings born of her, the young heirs of her brief life, had seemed assured of existence; now she feared that the merciless destroyer might seize her remaining darlings, as it had seized their brother. The slightest illness brought agonies of terror; she was wretched whenever she was separated from them. She had gathered her store of happiness into their fragile lives, and remained constantly watchful, lest the stealthy thief should once again carry off these precious gems. Fortunately, she had little reason for alarm. Alfred, now nine years old, was an upright, manly boy, with a bright brow, gentle eyes, and a mild yet independent nature. Our youngest was still an infant; but his soft cheek was touched with the roses of health, and his tireless liveliness filled our halls with innocent laughter.

Clara had passed the age which, through its silent ignorance, had been the source of Idris’s fears. Clara was dear to her, and to everyone. So much intelligence was joined with innocence, sensibility with restraint, and seriousness with perfect good humour; such extraordinary beauty was united to such winning simplicity, that she hung like a pearl within the shrine of our possessions, a treasure of wonder and excellence.

At the start of winter, our Alfred, now nine years old, went to school at Eton for the first time. To him this seemed the first step toward manhood, and he was pleased in proportion. Sharing study and recreation brought out the finest qualities of his character: his steadfast perseverance, generosity, and well-controlled firmness."}

