          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7c97f70b14832c6505c89907\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Saturday at noon I went to the well and watched for a while. Merlin was still burning smoke-powders, pawing the air, and muttering gibberish as vigorously as ever, but he looked rather dispirited, since naturally he had not yet started even a perspiration in that well. At last I said:\\n\\n\u201cHow does the matter promise by now, partner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBehold, I am even now engaged in testing the most powerful enchantment known to the princes of occult arts in the lands of the East; should it fail me, nothing can avail. Peace, until I have finished.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he raised a smoke that darkened the whole region, and must have made things uncomfortable for the hermits, since the wind blew their way and rolled down over their dens in a thick, billowing fog. He poured forth matching volumes of speech, twisted his body, and sawed the air with his hands in the strangest fashion. After twenty minutes he sank down panting and nearly spent. Then came the abbot with several hundred monks and nuns, followed by a multitude of pilgrims and a couple of acres of foundlings, all attracted by the prodigious smoke and all in a high state of excitement. The abbot anxiously asked for the result. Merlin said:\\n\\n\u201cIf any labor of mortal man could break the spell binding these waters, this which I have just attempted would have done so. It has failed; and thereby I now know that what I feared is an established truth: the sign of this failure is that the most potent spirit known to the magicians of the East, whose name none may utter and live, has laid his spell upon this well. No mortal breathes, nor ever shall, who can penetrate the secret of that spell, and without that secret none can break it. The water will flow no more forever, good Father. I have done what man could do. Suffer me to depart.\u201d\\n\\nOf course this threw the abbot into considerable consternation. He turned to me, the signs of it plain upon his face, and said:\\n\\n\u201cYe have heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPart of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all, then, not all! What part is true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has cast his spell upon the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s wounds, then are we ruined!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPossibly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not certainly? Ye mean, not certainly?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWherefore, ye also mean that when he says none can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, when he says that, he says what is not necessarily true. There are conditions under which an attempt to break it might have some chance\u2014that is, some small, some trifling chance\u2014of succeeding.\u201d\"}"
