{"request_id":"8ef58cf4e6feeaafc95f1a9b","rewritten_text":"Each compares it—not with the voice of a person from any nation whose language he understands—but with the opposite. The Frenchman takes it for the voice of a Spaniard, and “might have recognized some words had he known Spanish.” The Dutchman insists that it was a Frenchman’s voice; yet we are told that “not understanding French, this witness was examined through an interpreter.” The Englishman believes it was the voice of a German, and “does not understand German.” The Spaniard “is sure” it belonged to an Englishman, but “judges entirely by the intonation,” “as he has no knowledge of English.” The Italian thinks it was the voice of a Russian, but “has never conversed with a native of Russia.” A second Frenchman, moreover, disagrees with the first and is certain that the voice was Italian; but, not knowing that language, is, like the Spaniard, “convinced by the intonation.” How extraordinarily unusual, then, must that voice truly have been, about which testimony of this kind could be obtained!—a voice whose tones, even, were familiar to none of the inhabitants of Europe’s five great divisions! You may say that it could have been the voice of an Asiatic or an African. Asiatics and Africans are not common in Paris; but, without rejecting that conclusion, I will now draw your attention to three points. One witness calls the voice “harsh rather than shrill.” Two others describe it as “quick and unequal.” No witness mentioned hearing any words—or any sounds resembling words—that could be distinguished.

“I do not know,” Dupin continued, “what impression I have so far made upon your understanding; but I do not hesitate to say that legitimate deductions from even this part of the testimony—the part concerning the gruff and shrill voices—are sufficient in themselves to create a suspicion that should guide every further step in investigating the mystery. I said ‘legitimate deductions,’ but that does not fully express my meaning. I intended to suggest that these are the only proper deductions, and that the suspicion follows from them inevitably as their sole result. What that suspicion is, however, I will not yet say. I merely wish you to remember that, for me, it was powerful enough to give my inquiries in the chamber a definite direction and tendency.

“Let us now carry ourselves in imagination to this chamber. What shall we seek first? The means of escape used by the murderers. It is not too much to say that neither of us believes in supernatural events."}

