          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6ef9fc6d313cd5c187309d28\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We both agreed in loving Adrian, though Perdita, still not fully grown out of childhood, could not value his qualities as I did or share my sympathy with his interests and opinions. I was always with him. His gentle, sensitive nature gave our conversations a tender, almost unearthly quality. Yet he was also as cheerful as a lark singing from its high tower, as lofty in thought as an eagle, and as innocent as a mild-eyed dove. He could lighten Perdita\u2019s seriousness and ease the painful restlessness of my nature. I looked back on my troubled desires and painful struggles with other people as on a disturbed dream. I felt as changed as if I had taken on another body, with new senses and nerves that changed the universe\u2019s reflection in the mind\u2019s mirror. But I had not truly changed. I was still strong, still deeply hungry for sympathy, and still eager for active work. My manly virtues had not left me: the witch Urania had spared Samson\u2019s hair while he rested at her feet. Yet everything in me had become gentler and more human. Adrian taught me more than the cold facts of history and philosophy. Through them, he taught me to control my reckless, uncultivated spirit; and at the same time, he revealed the living page of his own heart, allowing me to feel and understand its wonderful nature.\\n\\nThe former queen of England had tried, from her son\u2019s earliest childhood, to plant bold and ambitious plans in his mind. She saw that he possessed genius and extraordinary talent, and she developed these qualities so that she could later use them to advance her own aims. She encouraged his hunger for knowledge and his fierce courage. She even accepted his untamed love of freedom, hoping that it would, as it often does, grow into a desire to command. She tried to raise him with resentment toward, and a wish for revenge against, those who had helped bring about his father\u2019s abdication. She failed in this. The reports he received, however distorted, of a great and wise nation claiming the right to govern itself filled him with admiration; from an early age, he became a republican on principle. Still, his mother did not give up. Along with his love of power and proud sense of birth, she encouraged ambition, patience, and self-control. She devoted herself to studying his character. Through praise, blame, and urging, she tried to find and strike the right chords. Though the music produced by her efforts sounded like discord to her, she based her hopes on his talents and remained certain that she would eventually win him over.\"}"
