{"request_id":"6076f67d39c77bbb083c8113","rewritten_text":"It was after two o’clock when, by a sudden turn, I found myself near the lake, beside a cove where a small skiff lay moored. It was not far from our house, and I saw my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I sprang into the boat and, well practiced in such feats, shoved it from the shore, applying all my strength to row swiftly across. As I approached, dressed in white, covered only by my tartan _rachan_, my hair streaming over my shoulders, and moving with a speed greater than anyone could have imagined I might give the boat, my father often told me that I looked more like a spirit than a human maiden. He held the boat as I reached the shore; I leapt lightly out and, in an instant, was in his arms.

And now I began to live. Everything around me had changed from dull uniformity into the brightest scene of joy and delight. The happiness I found in my father’s company surpassed my most hopeful expectations. We were constantly together, and the subjects of our conversation were inexhaustible. He had spent the sixteen years of his absence among nations almost unknown to Europe; he had travelled through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, and had entered among the dwellings of the natives with a freedom granted to few Europeans. His accounts of their customs, his anecdotes, and his descriptions of the scenery passed delicious hours when we were weary of discussing our own plans for the future.

The voice of affection was so unfamiliar to me that I listened with delight whenever he told me what he had felt for me during those long years of seeming forgetfulness. “At first,” he said, “I could not endure thinking of my poor little girl; but later, as grief faded and hope returned, I could turn only to her, and amid cities and deserts her little fairy form, as I imagined it, forever flitted before me. The northern breeze, when it refreshed me, seemed sweeter and more balmy because it appeared to carry some part of your spirit with it. I often thought I would return at once and take you with me to some fertile island where we might live peacefully forever. As I came back, my fervent hopes were clouded by so many fears; my impatience became painfully intense. I dared not imagine that the sun would shine and the moon rise, not upon your living form, but upon your grave. But no, it is not so; I have my Mathilda, my consolation and my hope.”"}

