          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"989cf8a80aed84934385a178\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond was exhilarated by the intense sensations of restored health; he felt that, as general of the Athenians, he occupied a position worthy of his ambition. In hoping to conquer Constantinople, he looked toward an event that would stand as a landmark in the wasteland of the ages, an achievement unmatched in human history: a city of immense historical associations, whose beautiful setting was the wonder of the world, and which for many centuries had been the stronghold of the Muslims, would be rescued from slavery and barbarism and restored to a people renowned for genius, civilization, and love of liberty. Perdita relied on his restored society, his love, his hopes, and his fame, as a Sybarite relies on a luxurious couch; every thought was rapture, and every emotion seemed immersed in a congenial, balmy element.\\n\\nWe reached Kishan on July 7. The weather throughout our journey had been serene. Each day, before dawn, we left our night\u2019s encampment and watched the shadows withdraw from hill and valley, along with the golden splendor announcing the sun\u2019s approach. The accompanying soldiers, with national vivacity, took enthusiastic pleasure in the sight of beautiful nature. The rising of the day-star was greeted by triumphant music, while birdsong, heard in snatches, filled the intervals between the melodies. At noon, we pitched our tents in a shaded valley or a wooded hollow among the mountains, while a stream murmuring over pebbles invited grateful sleep. Our evening march, calmer than the morning, was even more delightful. If the band played, they instinctively chose airs of restrained passion: the farewell of love or lament for absence, followed and concluded by some solemn hymn that harmonized with the tranquil beauty of evening and lifted the soul toward lofty, religious thought. Often every sound was suspended so that we could listen to the nightingale, while fireflies danced in bright patterns and the soft cooing of the aziolo promised fair weather to the travelers. Did we pass through a valley? Gentle shadows surrounded us, and rocks glowed with beautiful colors. If we crossed a mountain, Greece, like a living map, lay spread beneath us, its famous peaks piercing the sky and its rivers tracing silver lines through the fertile land. Almost afraid to breathe, we English travelers surveyed this splendid landscape with ecstasy, so unlike the sober colors and melancholy beauties of our native scenery. When we left Macedonia, the fertile but low plains of Thrace offered fewer delights; nevertheless, our journey remained interesting. An advance guard announced our approach, and the country people quickly began moving to honor Lord Raymond.\"}"
