          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4944c331321b0eee217666ab\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 19th. This morning, to my immense delight, at about nine o\u2019clock, the moon\u2019s surface being terribly near and my fears raised to their highest pitch, the pump of my condenser at last showed clear signs of a change in the atmosphere. By ten, I had reason to think its density had greatly increased. By eleven, scarcely any exertion was needed at the apparatus; and at noon, though somewhat doubtful, I ventured to unscrew the tourniquet. Finding no discomfort from this, I at length opened the gum-elastic chamber and detached it from around the car. As might have been anticipated, spasms and severe headache immediately followed an experiment so rash and dangerous. Yet these, along with the other impediments to breathing, were not so serious as to threaten my life; and I resolved to bear them as well as I could, since each moment of my ascent toward the denser strata near the moon would leave them behind. This ascent, however, remained exceedingly rapid; and it soon became distressingly clear that, though I had likely not been mistaken in expecting an atmosphere dense in proportion to the satellite\u2019s mass, I had erred in believing that this density, even at the surface, would sufficiently support the great weight enclosed in my balloon\u2019s car. It ought, nevertheless, to have done so, and to the same extent as at the earth\u2019s surface, the actual gravity of bodies on either planet being presumed to correspond with the atmospheric condensation. That it did not was proved abundantly by my headlong descent; the reason can only be accounted for by referring to those possible geological disturbances I have mentioned before. In any event, I was now near the planet and dropping with dreadful speed. I therefore lost no time in casting out first my ballast, then my water-casks, then my condensing apparatus and gum-elastic chamber, and finally every object in the car. All was useless. I continued to fall with frightful velocity and was now less than half a mile above the surface. As a final measure, after removing my coat, hat, and boots, I severed the car itself from the balloon, since it had considerable weight. Clinging with both hands to the netting, I had scarcely time to notice that the entire country, as far as I could see, was thickly dotted with tiny dwellings before I plunged headlong into the center of a strange-looking city, among a huge crowd of ugly little people. Not one spoke a word or made the slightest effort to help me; instead, they stood like a group of fools, grinning absurdly and casting sidelong looks at me and my balloon, their arms akimbo.\u201d\"}"
