          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"35edd6fb4172222960cde11d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He sprang to his feet and seized her arm roughly. \u201cShow him to me. Which one is he? Point him out. I must see him!\u201d he cried; but just then the Duke of Berwick\u2019s four-in-hand passed between them, and by the time it had cleared the way, the carriage had swept out of the park.\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019s gone,\u201d Sibyl murmured sadly. \u201cI wish you had seen him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo do I, for as surely as there is a God in heaven, if he ever does you any harm, I shall kill him.\u201d\\n\\nShe stared at him in horror. He repeated the threat. His words struck the air like a dagger. The people nearby began to gape, and a lady standing beside her tittered.\\n\\n\u201cCome away, Jim; come away,\u201d she whispered. He followed stubbornly as she made her way through the crowd. He was pleased with what he had said.\\n\\nWhen they reached the Achilles Statue, she turned back. Pity showed in her eyes, then changed to laughter on her lips. She shook her head at him. \u201cYou are foolish, Jim\u2014utterly foolish; only a bad-tempered boy, that is all. How can you say such dreadful things? You don\u2019t know what you are saying. You are merely jealous and unkind. Ah, I wish you would fall in love. Love makes people good, and what you said was wicked.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am sixteen,\u201d he replied, \u201cand I know what I am doing. Mother is no help to you. She doesn\u2019t know how to take care of you. I wish now I wasn\u2019t going to Australia at all. I am half-minded to abandon the whole business. I would, if my articles had not been signed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, don\u2019t be so solemn, Jim. You are like one of the heroes in those foolish melodramas Mother used to enjoy acting in. I am not going to quarrel with you. I have seen him, and oh! seeing him is perfect happiness. We won\u2019t quarrel. I know you would never hurt anyone I love, would you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot while you love him, I suppose,\u201d came the sullen reply.\\n\\n\u201cI shall love him forever!\u201d she cried.\\n\\n\u201cAnd he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cForever, too!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe had better.\u201d\\n\\nShe drew back from him. Then she laughed and laid her hand on his arm. He was only a boy.\\n\\nAt Marble Arch they hailed an omnibus, which set them down near their shabby home in the Euston Road. It was past five, and Sibyl had to lie down for a few hours before acting. Jim insisted that she do so. He said he would rather leave her while their mother was away. She was certain to make a scene, and he hated scenes of every sort.\"}"
