          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ddef6e51b6749ae354c9213b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Without giving herself time to think, and hardly knowing what she felt, Elizabeth finished this letter, immediately seized the other, and opened it with the greatest impatience. It had been written a day after the first letter ended, and read as follows:\\n\\n\u201cMy dearest sister, by now you will have received my hurried letter. I hope this one will be clearer, but although I am not pressed for time, my mind is so confused that I cannot promise to write coherently. Dearest Lizzy, I hardly know what to say, but I have bad news, and it cannot wait. However foolish a marriage between Mr. Wickham and our poor Lydia would be, we are now desperate to learn that it has happened, because there is too much reason to fear that they have not gone to Scotland. Colonel Forster arrived yesterday, having left Brighton the day before, only a few hours after the express. Although Lydia\u2019s brief letter to Mrs. F. led them to believe that she and Wickham were going to Gretna Green, Denny let something slip that showed he believed Wickham never meant to go there, or to marry Lydia at all. This was repeated to Colonel Forster, who immediately became alarmed and set out to follow their route. He traced them easily as far as Clapham, but no farther. On entering that place, they changed into a hackney coach and dismissed the chaise that had brought them from Epsom. All that is known after that is that they were seen continuing along the London road. I do not know what to think. After making every possible inquiry in that part of London, Colonel Forster came on to Hertfordshire, anxiously asking at every turnpike and at the inns in Barnet and Hatfield, but without success. No one had seen such people pass through. With the greatest concern, he came to Longbourn and told us his fears in a way that showed how deeply he felt them. I am truly sorry for him and Mrs. F., but no one can blame them. Our distress, my dear Lizzy, is very great. My father and mother believe the worst, but I cannot think so badly of him. Many things might make it better for them to marry privately in town rather than follow their original plan. And even if he could form such a plan against a young woman of Lydia\u2019s family\u2014which is unlikely\u2014can I believe her so completely without sense or virtue? Impossible! Yet I am troubled to find that Colonel Forster does not trust that they are married. When I expressed my hopes, he shook his head and said he feared Wickham was not a man who could be trusted.\u201d\"}"
