          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"25e5946d3cb9f987a79cf8de\",\"rewritten_text\":\"MR. Merton was greatly troubled by the marriage being postponed a second time, and Lady Julia, who had already ordered her wedding dress, did everything she could to persuade Sybil to end the engagement. Sybil loved her mother dearly, but she had placed her entire life in Lord Arthur\u2019s hands, and nothing Lady Julia said could shake her faith. Lord Arthur himself took several days to recover from his bitter disappointment, and for a while his nerves were thoroughly shattered. His excellent common sense soon returned, however, and his practical, well-balanced mind quickly decided what course to take. Since poison had failed completely, dynamite, or some other explosive, was plainly the next thing to attempt.\\n\\nHe therefore examined the list of his friends and relations once more and, after careful thought, resolved to blow up his uncle, the Dean of Chichester. The Dean, a man of considerable culture and learning, was passionately fond of clocks and possessed a remarkable collection of timepieces, dating from the fifteenth century to the present day. Lord Arthur saw that this interest of the good Dean\u2019s might provide an excellent means of executing his plan. Finding an explosive machine was, naturally, an entirely different problem. The London Directory offered him no help, and he thought there was little point in applying to Scotland Yard, since they never appeared to know anything about the dynamite party\u2019s activities until after an explosion\u2014and not much even then.\\n\\nThen he suddenly remembered his friend Rouvaloff, a young Russian with strongly revolutionary opinions, whom he had met at Lady Windermere\u2019s during the winter. Count Rouvaloff was said to be writing a life of Peter the Great and to have come to England to study documents concerning that Tsar\u2019s stay in the country as a ship\u2019s carpenter; yet it was widely suspected that he was a Ni