          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5524add799ce3dcf30552215\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He never tried a second time. The boys lay there, counting the slow moments until it seemed that time itself must end and eternity begin to fade. Then they were relieved to see that the sun was finally setting.\\n\\nOne snore stopped. Injun Joe sat up, looked around, and gave a grim smile at his companion, whose head had fallen onto his knees. He nudged him with his foot and said:\\n\\n\u201cHere! You\u2019re supposed to be keeping watch, aren\u2019t you? It\u2019s all right, though\u2014nothing\u2019s happened.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy! Was I asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, only partly. It\u2019s nearly time for us to leave, pard. What shall we do with the little swag we have left?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know\u2014leave it here, like we always do, I suppose. There\u2019s no point taking it away until we head south. Six hundred and fifty dollars in silver is a lot to carry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right\u2014it won\u2019t hurt to come here one more time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo\u2014but I\u2019d say we should come at night, as we used to. It\u2019s safer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, but listen; it may be a long time before I get the right chance for that job. Accidents could happen, and it isn\u2019t in a very good place. We\u2019ll bury it properly\u2014and bury it deep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood idea,\u201d said his companion. He crossed the room, knelt down, lifted one of the hearthstones at the back, and pulled out a bag that jingled pleasantly. He took twenty or thirty dollars for himself and the same amount for Injun Joe, then handed the bag to Joe, who was now kneeling in the corner and digging with his bowie knife.\\n\\nThe boys instantly forgot all their fears and misery. With shining eyes, they watched every movement. Luck! The treasure was more wonderful than they could imagine. Six hundred dollars was enough to make half a dozen boys rich! This was treasure hunting at its best\u2014