          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"85c20b5de1804c9adb18855c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Their coming had kindled a sort of fire in the heart of Saint Antoine, spreading swiftly as they advanced, and stirring into flickering flames of faces at nearly every door and window. Yet no one had followed them, and no man spoke when they entered the wine-shop, though every eye within was turned toward them.\\n\\n\u201cGood day, gentlemen!\u201d said Monsieur Defarge.\\n\\nPerhaps this served as a signal to loosen the general tongue. It drew back a chorus of \u201cGood day!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe weather is bad, gentlemen,\u201d said Defarge, shaking his head.\\n\\nAt this, each man glanced at his neighbour, then all lowered their eyes and sat in silence. Except for one man, who rose and went out.\\n\\n\u201cMy wife,\u201d said Defarge aloud, speaking to Madame Defarge, \u201cI have travelled several leagues with this good road-mender, called Jacques. I met him\u2014by chance\u2014a day and a half\u2019s journey from Paris. He is a good child, this road-mender called Jacques. Give him something to drink, my wife!\u201d\\n\\nA second man rose and went out. Madame Defarge placed wine before the road-mender called Jacques. He removed his blue cap to the company and drank. In the breast of his blouse he carried some coarse, dark bread; from time to time he ate it, sitting near Madame Defarge\u2019s counter, munching and drinking. A third man rose and went out.\\n\\nDefarge revived himself with a draught of wine\u2014but took less than he gave the stranger, since wine was no rarity to him\u2014and waited until the countryman had finished his breakfast. He looked at no one present, and no one now looked at him; not even Madame Defarge, who had taken up her knitting and was working.\\n\\n\u201cHave you finished your meal, friend?\u201d he asked, when the proper time came.\\n\\n\u201cYes, thank you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome, then! You shall see the room I told you that you might occupy. It will suit you wonderfully.\u201d\\n\\nFrom the wine-shop into the street, from the street into a courtyard, from the courtyard up a steep staircase, and from the staircase into a garret\u2014formerly the garret where a white-haired man had sat on a low bench, bent forward and busily making shoes.\\n\\nNo white-haired man was there now; but the three men who had separately left the wine-shop were there. Between them and the distant white-haired man was one small connection: they had once looked in at him through the cracks in the wall.\\n\\nDefarge carefully shut the door and spoke in a lowered voice:\\n\\n\u201cJacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness met by appointment, by me, Jacques Four.\u201d\"}"
