          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"134ddc3a49ef78785e444dfc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Col. Grangerford was a gentleman, you see\u2014one through and through, and so was his family. He was well born, as folks say, and that counts for as much in a man as in a horse, according to the Widow Douglas; and nobody ever disputed that she belonged to the highest aristocracy in our town. Pap said the same thing, too, though he warn\u2019t any more quality than a mudcat. Col. Grangerford was very tall and very thin, with a dark, pale complexion and not a trace of red anywhere. Every morning he shaved his whole narrow face clean, and he had the thinnest lips, the narrowest nostrils, a high nose, heavy eyebrows, and eyes black as could be, set so far back they seemed to be staring at you from caves, you might say. His forehead was high, and his straight black hair fell to his shoulders. His hands were long and slender, and every day he wore a fresh shirt and a complete linen suit, white enough to hurt your eyes; on Sundays he put on a blue tail-coat with brass buttons. He carried a mahogany cane topped with silver. There was no frivolity in him, not the least, and he was never noisy. He was as kind as a man could be\u2014you could sense it, and so you trusted him. Sometimes he smiled, and it was pleasant to see; but when he drew himself up like a liberty-pole and lightning began flashing beneath his eyebrows, you wanted to climb a tree before finding out what was wrong. He never needed to tell anyone to behave\u2014everybody had good manners in his presence. Everyone liked having him nearby, too; he was nearly always sunshine\u2014I mean, he made things feel like fair weather. When he became a cloudbank, it grew terribly dark for half a minute, and that was sufficient; nothing else would go wrong for a week.\\n\\nWhen he and the old lady came downstairs in the morning, the whole family rose from their chairs and wished them good-day, and nobody sat again until they had taken their seats. Then Tom and Bob went to the sideboard, where the decanter stood, mixed him a glass of bitters, and handed it over. He held it while they prepared their own, and then they bowed and said, \u201cOur duty to you, sir, and madam.\u201d They bowed the tiniest bit and replied thank you, and then all three drank. Bob and Tom poured a spoonful of water over the sugar and the little bit of whisky or apple brandy at the bottoms of their tumblers, gave it to me and Buck, and we drank to the old people as well.\"}"
