{"request_id":"9d8b8ae2045d2c787a75a527","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to hear that no disaster has accompanied the beginning of an enterprise you have viewed with such fearful forebodings. I arrived here yesterday, and my first task is to assure my dear sister of my safety and my growing confidence in the success of my undertaking.

I am already far north of London, and as I walk through the streets of Petersburg, I feel a cold northern breeze touch my cheeks, strengthening my nerves and filling me with delight. Can you understand this feeling? This breeze, which has travelled from the regions toward which I am heading, gives me a foretaste of those icy climates. Encouraged by this promising wind, my daydreams become more fervent and vivid. I try in vain to accept the belief that the pole is a place of frost and desolation; it always appears to my imagination as a region of beauty and delight. There, Margaret, the sun is forever visible, its broad disk barely skimming the horizon and spreading a perpetual splendour. There—for, with your permission, my sister, I will place some trust in earlier navigators—there snow and frost are banished; and, sailing across a calm sea, we may be carried to a land surpassing in wonders and beauty every region yet discovered on the inhabited globe. Its products and features may be unlike anything known, just as the phenomena of the heavens surely are in those unexplored solitudes. What might not be found in a country of eternal light? I may discover there the wondrous force that attracts the needle, and perhaps establish a thousand celestial observations whose apparent irregularities require only this voyage to make them forever consistent. I shall satisfy my eager curiosity by seeing a part of the world never visited before, and may set foot on land never before marked by human footsteps. These are my incentives, and they are enough to overcome every fear of danger or death and to make me begin this arduous voyage with the joy a child feels when he sets out in a small boat with his holiday companions on an expedition of discovery up his native river. But even if all these conjectures prove false, you cannot deny the priceless benefit I would bring to all humanity, even to the last generation, by discovering a passage near the pole to those countries that now require so many months to reach; or by uncovering the secret of the magnet, which, if it can be achieved at all, can only be accomplished through an undertaking such as mine.

These thoughts have driven away the agitation with which I began my letter, and I feel my heart glowing with an enthusiasm that lifts me toward heaven, for nothing calms the mind so much as a firm purpose—a point on which the soul can fix its intellectual gaze."}

