          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ac0809685183f1b94a7089a1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was something strange in all this that drew me in and enchanted me. He seemed to have awakened from a long, dreamlike sleep, and felt rather like one of the seven sleepers, or like Nourjahad in that lovely imitation of an Eastern tale. Diana was gone; his friends had changed or died, and now, upon waking, I was all he had left to love in the world.\\n\\nHow dear the waters, mountains, and woods of Loch Lomond became to me, now that I had such a beloved companion for my walks. With my father, I visited every beautiful place, whether on the islands or beside waterfalls sheltered by trees; every shaded path and every hollow tangled with undergrowth and fern. His conversation widened my thoughts. I felt renewed, as if I had gained all the freshness and energy of a new life. Since his arrival, I seemed to have been carried from a small corner of earth into a universe without limits to the imagination or understanding. Before, my life had been like a pleasant country stream, never meant to leave its native fields, but destined, when its work was done, to disappear quietly without a trace. Now it seemed like a varied river flowing through a fertile and beautiful landscape, always changing and always lovely. Alas, I did not know what desert it was nearing, what rocks would tear its waters, or what terrible scene would be reflected, even more horribly distorted, in its waves. Life was then brilliant; I was beginning to learn hope\u2014and what brings a more bitter despair to the heart than hope destroyed?\\n\\nIs it not strange that grief should so quickly follow such perfect happiness? I drank from an enchanted cup, but bitterness lay at the bottom of its long, sweet draught. My heart was filled with deep affection, yet it was calm because of its very depth and fullness. I had no idea that love could bring misery, and this lesson, which everyone must eventually learn, was taught to me in a way few are forced to endure. I mourn now, and must always mourn, those few short months of paradise. I had broken no command and eaten no apple, yet I was cruelly driven from it. Alas, my companion had, and I was thrown down in his fall. But I am straying from my story\u2014let sorrow come in its proper time; at this point I may still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this delightful companionship, when my aunt became ill. I spent an entire month nursing her in her room, but her illness was fatal, and she died, leaving me inconsolable for some time. Death is so terrible to the living; the bonds of habit are so strong, even when affection does not hold them together, that the heart must suffer when they are broken.\"}"
        }
