          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fe149de85d386354e4384762\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cDo you understand me? I have learned the language of despair; I know it all by heart, because I am Despair\u2014and a strange being I am: joyous, triumphant Despair. But those words are false, for the wave may be dark, but it is not bitter. We lie down and close our eyes with a gentle good night, and when we wake, we are free. Come, then, no more delay, you tardy one! Behold the pleasant potion! Look, I am a spirit of goodness, not a human maiden inviting you, and in winning tones\u2014oh, that they would win you!\u2014saying, Come and drink.\u201d\\n\\nAs I spoke, I kept my eyes fixed on his face. His exquisite beauty, the heavenly compassion shining from his eyes, and his gentle yet earnest expression of concern and wonder, even before he spoke, changed my overwrought feelings. They took from me all the sternness of despair and filled me only with the softest grief. I saw that his eyes were moist as well when he took both my hands in his. Sitting down beside me, he said:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a sad deed you would lead me to, dearest friend, and your sorrow must indeed be deep to fill you with such unhappy thoughts. You long for death, yet fear it, and wish me to be your companion. But I have less courage than you, and even with such company I dare not die. Listen to me, then reflect on whether you ought to win me to your plan. Even if, with the overbearing eloquence of despair, you could make black death so inviting that fair heaven itself appeared dark. Listen, I beg you, to one who has himself nourished desperate thoughts and longed impatiently for death, but who at last has trampled that phantom underfoot and crushed its sting. Come: since you have played Despair with me, I will play Una\u2019s part with you and bring you unharmed from his dark cavern. Listen to me, and let yourself be softened by words in which no selfish passion remains.\\n\\n\u201cWe do not know what this wide world means, with its strange mixture of good and evil. But we have been placed here and told to live and hope. I do not know what we are meant to hope for; yet there is some good beyond us that we must seek, and that is our earthly task. If misfortune comes against us, we must fight it; we must cast it aside and continue onward, discovering what it is our nature to desire. Whether this prospect of future good prepares us for another existence, I do not know; or whether we are merely workmen in God\u2019s vineyard, obliged to lend a hand in smoothing the way for those who come after us.\u201d\"}"
        }
