          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"628380680b417ccac23b87f8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At that moment, his eyes\u2014and mine as well\u2014fell on the scrap of parchment, which I had then taken for paper. It lay half buried in the sand, with one corner sticking out. Near the place where we found it, I noticed the remains of what seemed to be a ship\u2019s longboat. The wreck must have been there for a very long time, because it was barely possible to recognize the timbers as parts of a boat.\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jupiter picked up the parchment, wrapped the beetle in it, and handed it to me. Soon afterward we started home, and on the way we met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014. I showed him the insect, and he asked me to let him take it to the fort. When I agreed, he immediately put it into his waistcoat pocket, leaving behind the parchment in which it had been wrapped. I had kept that in my hand while he examined the beetle. Perhaps he feared I might change my mind and thought it safest to secure the prize at once\u2014you know how enthusiastic he is about everything connected with Natural History. At the same time, without realizing it, I must have put the parchment into my own pocket.\\n\\n\u201cYou remember that when I went to the table to draw a sketch of the beetle, I found no paper in its usual place. I looked in the drawer, but there was none there either. I searched my pockets, hoping to find an old letter, and my hand came upon the parchment. I explain exactly how it came into my possession because the circumstances made a strong impression on me.\\n\\n\u201cNo doubt you will think me fanciful\u2014but I had already formed a connection. I had joined two links in a great chain. There was a boat on a seacoast, and not far from it lay a parchment\u2014not paper\u2014with a skull drawn upon it. You will naturally ask, \u2018What is the connection?\u2019 I answer that the skull, or death\u2019s-head, is the familiar emblem of the pirate. The death\u2019s-head flag is raised in every engagement.\\n\\n\u201cI have said that the scrap was parchment, not paper. Parchment is durable\u2014almost impossible to destroy. Things of little importance are rarely written or drawn on parchment, since paper is much better suited to ordinary writing and drawing. This thought suggested that the death\u2019s-head must have some meaning or connection. I also noticed the shape of the parchment. Although one corner had been destroyed by some accident, it was clear that its original shape had been oblong.\"}"
