{"request_id":"c3514f2e64b8cc008a0dc727","rewritten_text":"It was past two o’clock when, after a sudden turn, I found myself near the lake, beside a cove where a little skiff was moored. It was not far from our house, and I saw my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I jumped into the boat and, well practiced in such feats, pushed it from shore and used all my strength to row quickly across. As I approached, dressed in white, covered only by my tartan _rachan_, with my hair streaming over my shoulders and moving faster than anyone could have thought I could make the boat go, my father often told me that I looked more like a spirit than a human girl. He held the boat as I reached the shore; I leapt lightly out and, in a moment, was in his arms.

And now I began to live. Everything around me changed from dull sameness into the brightest scene of joy and delight. The happiness I found in my father’s company far surpassed my hopeful expectations. We were always together, and we never ran out of things to discuss. He had spent the sixteen years of our separation among nations almost unknown in Europe. He had traveled through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, entering the homes of the native people with a freedom allowed to few Europeans. His accounts of their customs, along with his stories and descriptions of the landscape, filled many wonderful hours when we grew tired of discussing our plans for the future.

The voice of affection was so new to me that I listened with delight whenever he told me what he had felt for me during those long years of seeming forgetfulness. “At first,” he said, “I could not bear to think of my poor little girl. But later, as my grief faded and hope returned, I could think only of her. Among cities and deserts, the little fairy figure I imagined her to be was always passing before me. The northern breeze, as it refreshed me, seemed sweeter and more fragrant because it appeared to carry some part of your spirit with it. I often thought I would return at once and take you with me to some fertile island where we could live peacefully forever. As I came back, my eager hopes were overcome by so many fears that my impatience became terribly painful. I dared not imagine that the sun would shine and the moon rise not upon your living form, but upon your grave. But no, it is not so; I have my Mathilda, my comfort, and my hope.”"}

