{"request_id":"ac93537b55fbfa8c1807b835","rewritten_text":"Few could claim to possess so pure a heart, so much genuine humility of spirit joined with such firm trust in her own integrity and belief in the integrity of others. She had lived a secluded life from birth. Her mother had died when she was very young, but her father had devoted himself to her education. He held many unusual ideas, which shaped the system he followed in raising her. She knew the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those of England who had lived centuries earlier, yet she was almost ignorant of the events of the present day. She had read few authors who had written within at least the past fifty years, but apart from this limitation her reading was extensive. Thus, although she seemed less acquainted with the mysteries of life and society than he was, her knowledge was deeper and rested on firmer foundations; and even if her beauty and gentleness had not captivated him, her understanding would always have held him enthralled. He looked to her as his guide, and his adoration was such that he took pleasure in deepening within himself the sense of inferiority she sometimes inspired in him.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. He left college after her death and, for a time abandoning his old companions, withdrew to the neighborhood of his Diana, finding all his comfort in her sweet voice and still dearer caresses. This brief separation from his friends gave him the courage to assert his independence. He felt that, however much they might ridicule his intended marriage, they would not dare show it once the marriage had taken place. He therefore sought the consent of his guardian, which he obtained with some difficulty, and that of his beloved’s father, which was given more readily. Without telling anyone else of his plans, he had become Diana’s husband by the time he reached his twentieth birthday.

He loved her passionately, and her tenderness possessed a charm that left him unable to think of anything but her. He invited several college friends to visit him, but their frivolity disgusted him. Diana had torn away the veil that had kept him in childhood: he had become a man, and he was astonished that he had ever joined in the empty phrases and opinions of his fellow students, or that he had feared, even for a moment, the judgment of people like them. He abandoned his old friendships not from fickleness, but because they were truly beneath him."}

