          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7822fa1c5193157bcc0e55b4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For that reason, I determined to get down the moment we reached the town, and place myself beyond his hearing. I carried out this plan successfully. My small portmanteau lay in the boot beneath my feet; I had only to turn a hinge to release it. I flung it down before me, climbed down after it, and found myself left beside the first lamp, on the first stones of the town pavement. The convicts, meanwhile, continued on with the coach, and I knew where they would be taken away to the river. In imagination, I saw the boat, with its convict crew, waiting for them at the slime-streaked stairs\u2014heard once more the rough \u201cGive way, you!\u201d like an order addressed to dogs\u2014and saw again the wicked Noah\u2019s Ark lying upon the dark water.\\n\\nI could not have explained what frightened me, since my fear was wholly vague and undefined, but I was possessed by a powerful dread. As I went on toward the hotel, I felt that something far beyond the simple apprehension of an unpleasant or painful recognition caused me to tremble. I am certain it assumed no definite form, and that for a few minutes it was the return of the terror of childhood.\\n\\nThe coffee-room at the Blue Boar was empty, and I had not merely ordered my dinner there but had begun eating it before the waiter recognised me. After apologising for his failure of memory, he asked whether he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d I replied, \u201ccertainly not.\u201d\\n\\nThe waiter\u2014it was he who had brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials on the day I was bound\u2014seemed surprised. At the first opportunity, he placed a dirty, old copy of a local newspaper so squarely before me that I picked it up and read the following paragraph:\u2014\\n\\nOur readers will learn, not without some interest, concerning the recent romantic rise in fortune of a young artificer in iron from this neighbourhood (what a subject, by the way, for the magic pen of our still not universally acknowledged townsman TOOBY, the poet of our columns!) that the youth\u2019s earliest patron, companion, and friend was a highly respected person, not wholly unconnected with the corn and seed trade, whose eminently convenient and commodious business premises are situated within a hundred miles of the High Street. It is not without regard to our personal feelings that we record HIM as the Mentor of our young Telemachus, since it is gratifying to know that our town produced the founder of the latter\u2019s fortunes.\"}"
