{"request_id":"14cff2f1db7649479f6b2120","rewritten_text":"“Oh, that is nothing! It is a pleasure for me. I do not work for money; I live entirely for my art.”

Lord Arthur placed £4 2s. 6d. on the table, thanked the little German for his kindness, and, after successfully declining an invitation to meet some Anarchists at a meat tea the following Saturday, left the house and went to the Park.

For the next two days he was in a state of intense excitement, and on Friday at noon he drove to the Buckingham to await news. Throughout the afternoon, the stolid hall porter kept putting up telegrams from different parts of the country reporting the results of horse races, verdicts in divorce cases, the state of the weather, and similar matters, while the tape printed tedious details of an all-night sitting in the House of Commons and a minor panic on the Stock Exchange. At four o’clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur vanished into the library with the _Pall Mall_, the _St. James’s_, the _Globe_, and the _Echo_, to the immense indignation of Colonel Goodchild, who wished to read reports of a speech he had delivered that morning at the Mansion House about South African Missions and the advisability of having black Bishops in every province, and who, for some reason, had a strong dislike of the _Evening News_. None of the papers, however, contained even the slightest reference to Chichester, and Lord Arthur felt that the attempt must have failed. It was a terrible blow, and for a while he was completely unnerved. Herr Winckelkopf, whom he visited the next day, was full of elaborate apologies and offered to provide him with another clock free of charge, or a case of nitroglycerine bombs at cost price. But Lord Arthur had lost all faith in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that everything was so adulterated nowadays that even dynamite could scarcely be obtained in a pure state. The little German, however, while acknowledging that something must have gone wrong with the mechanism, still hoped that the clock might explode, and cited the case of a barometer he had once sent to the military Governor at Odessa. Though it had been set to explode after ten days, it had not done so for nearly three months. It was true that when it finally went off, it merely blew a housemaid to pieces, the Governor having left town six weeks earlier; but at least it proved that dynamite, as a destructive force, was, when controlled by machinery, powerful, though somewhat unpunctual."}

