          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1b12fc1842e4e98b74e3d56e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But no\u2014there was something even more dazzling than that. He would become a pirate! That was it! Now his future stretched clearly before him, blazing with unimaginable glory. How his name would spread through the world and make people tremble! How splendidly he would plow the dancing seas in his long, low, black-hulled racer, the Spirit of the Storm, with his grisly flag flying from the bow! And at the height of his fame, how he would suddenly appear in the old village and stride into church, brown and weather-beaten, dressed in his black velvet doublet and trunks, his huge jack-boots, crimson sash, belt bristling with horse-pistols, crime-rusted cutlass at his side, and slouch hat crowned with waving plumes, his black flag unfurled and displaying its skull and crossbones, while he listened in swelling ecstasy to the whispers: \u201cIt\u2019s Tom Sawyer the Pirate!\u2014the Black Avenger of the Spanish Main!\u201d\\n\\nYes, it was decided; his career was fixed. He would run away from home and begin it. He would leave the very next morning. So he must start preparing at once. He would gather his resources. Going to a rotten log nearby, he began digging beneath one end with his Barlow knife. Soon he struck wood that gave a hollow sound. He placed his hand there and solemnly pronounced this incantation:\\n\\n\u201cWhat hasn\u2019t come here, come! What\u2019s here, stay here!\u201d\\n\\nThen he scraped away the dirt and uncovered a pine shingle. Lifting it, he revealed a neatly made little treasure-house, its bottom and sides formed of shingles. A marble lay inside. Tom\u2019s astonishment was limitless! He scratched his head in perplexity and said:\\n\\n\u201cWell, that beats anything!\u201d\\n\\nThen he petulantly tossed the marble away and stood thinking. The fact was, one of his superstitions had failed him\u2014a superstition that he and all his comrades had always considered infallible. If a marble were buried with the proper incantations, left undisturbed for a fortnight, and then the spot opened with the incantation he had just spoken, all the marbles ever lost would have gathered there in the meantime, no matter how far apart they had been. But now the thing had plainly and undeniably failed. Tom\u2019s entire structure of belief was shaken to its foundations. He had often heard of the charm working, but never of its failing. It did not occur to him that he himself had tried it several times before, only to be unable afterward to locate the hiding-places. He puzzled over the matter for a while, and at last concluded that some witch had meddled and broken the charm.\"}"
