{"request_id":"4bbbc7bbb1dcb28530715715","rewritten_text":"He said that he was wasting his time uselessly where he was, and that letters from the friends he had made in London urged him to return and complete the negotiations they had begun for his Indian enterprise. He could postpone his departure no longer; but since his journey to London might be followed, sooner even than he now expected, by his longer voyage, he begged me to give him as much of my company as I could spare. He therefore entreated me to leave my lonely island and meet him at Perth, so that we might travel southward together. This letter partly restored me to life, and I resolved to leave my island after two days had passed.

Before departing, however, I had a duty to perform on which I shuddered to think: I must pack away my chemical instruments, and to do so I had to enter the room that had been the scene of my detestable work and handle the utensils whose sight revolted me. At daybreak the following morning, I gathered enough courage to unlock the door of my laboratory. The remains of the half-completed creature I had destroyed lay scattered across the floor, and I almost felt as though I had torn apart the living flesh of a human being. I stopped to steady myself, then entered the chamber. With a trembling hand I carried the instruments out, but reflected that I ought not to leave the remnants of my work to arouse the peasants’ horror and suspicion. Accordingly, I placed them in a basket with a large quantity of stones and, having set it aside, resolved to cast it into the sea that very night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach, occupied in cleaning and arranging my chemical apparatus.

Nothing could have been more thorough than the change in my feelings since the night the dæmon appeared. Before, I had viewed my promise with dark despair, as something that had to be fulfilled whatever the consequences; but now I felt as if a veil had been lifted from my eyes and that, for the first time, I saw plainly. The thought of resuming my labours did not enter my mind for a moment. The threat I had heard burdened my thoughts, but I did not consider that any voluntary action of mine could prevent it. I had decided within myself that creating another being like the fiend I had first made would be an act of the vilest and most monstrous selfishness, and I drove from my mind every thought that might lead me to another conclusion."}

