          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4ff58c6213f7cd47646f0b22\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her colour altered, and she said nothing more.\\n\\nMrs. Gardiner was standing slightly behind; and when she paused, he asked whether she would do him the honour of presenting him to her friends. This courtesy was wholly unexpected; and she could scarcely restrain a smile at his now seeking the acquaintance of some of those very people against whom his pride had rebelled when he offered himself to her. \u201cWhat a surprise he will have,\u201d she thought, \u201cwhen he discovers who they are! He takes them at present for people of fashion.\u201d\\n\\nThe introduction was made at once; and as she explained their relation to her, she cast a sly glance at him, to observe how he endured it, half expecting him to retreat as quickly as possible from such disreputable companions. That he was surprised by the connection was plain: nevertheless, he bore it with fortitude; and instead of withdrawing, turned back with them and began talking to Mr. Gardiner. Elizabeth could not help being pleased, nor could she help triumphing. It was comforting that he should learn she had relations of whom she need not be ashamed. She listened with the greatest attention to everything that passed between them, and delighted in every remark, every sentence from her uncle that displayed his intelligence, taste, or good breeding.\\n\\nTheir conversation soon came round to fishing; and she heard Mr. Darcy invite him, with the utmost civility, to fish there as often as he pleased while he remained in the neighbourhood, offering also to provide him with fishing tackle and indicating the parts of the stream where sport was generally best. Mrs. Gardiner, walking arm in arm with Elizabeth, gave her a look full of astonishment. Elizabeth said nothing, but was exceedingly gratified; the compliment must be intended entirely for her. Yet her wonder was intense, and she kept repeating to herself, \u201cWhy is he so changed? What can have caused it? It cannot be for me, it cannot be on my account that his manners have become so gentle. My reproofs at Hunsford could not have produced such an alteration. It is impossible that he should still love me.\u201d\\n\\nAfter they had walked for some time in this arrangement, the two ladies ahead and the two gentlemen behind, they returned to their places after going down to the river\u2019s edge to examine more closely a curious water-plant, and there happened to be a slight change. It was brought about by Mrs. Gardiner, who, tired from the morning\u2019s exercise, found Elizabeth\u2019s arm insufficient support and therefore chose her husband\u2019s instead.\"}"
