          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9c209f9fec092b190b834637\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is extraordinary! I must meet Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\nHallward rose and paced around the garden. After a while he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cDorian Gray is simply an inspiration for my art. You might see nothing in him. I see everything. He is never more present in my work than when I do not paint him. As I have said, he suggests a new style. I find him in the curves of certain lines and in the beauty and subtlety of certain colours. That is all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen why won\u2019t you display his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked.\\n\\n\u201cBecause, without meaning to, I have put into it some expression of this strange artistic worship, which I have naturally never mentioned to him. He knows nothing about it, and he must never know. But the world might guess, and I will not expose my soul to its shallow, prying eyes. My heart will never be placed beneath their microscope. There is too much of me in the painting, Harry\u2014too much of me!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPoets are not as careful as you. They know how useful passion can be when it is published. These days, a broken heart can sell many editions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hate them for that,\u201d Hallward cried. \u201cAn artist should create beautiful things, but put none of his own life into them. We live in an age when people treat art as if it were autobiography. We have lost the pure sense of beauty. One day I will show the world what beauty is; and for that reason, the world will never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you are wrong, Basil, but I will not argue. Only the intellectually lost ever argue. Tell me, is Dorian Gray very fond of you?\u201d\\n\\nThe painter thought for a few moments. \u201cHe likes me,\u201d he replied after a pause; \u201cI know he does. Of course, I flatter him terribly. I take a strange pleasure in saying things to him that I know I will regret. Usually he is charming to me, and we sit in the studio talking about a thousand things. Sometimes, however, he is terribly thoughtless and seems to take real pleasure in hurting me. Then I feel, Harry, that I have given my whole soul to someone who treats it as if it were a flower to wear in his coat\u2014a decoration to satisfy his vanity, an ornament for a summer\u2019s day.\u201d\"}"
