          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"41bc47684c90830d7e344683\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The studio was steeped in the rich fragrance of roses, and whenever the light summer breeze moved among the garden trees, the open door admitted the heavy scent of lilac, or the subtler perfume of the pink-blossomed thorn.\\n\\nFrom his corner of the Persian saddle-bag divan, where he lay smoking his customary endless cigarettes, Lord Henry Wotton could just see the honey-coloured, honey-sweet flowers of a laburnum. Its quivering branches appeared scarcely strong enough to support such a flame-like burden of beauty. From time to time, the fanciful shadows of birds flying past crossed the long curtains of tussore silk drawn before the great window, creating a brief Japanese effect and recalling to him the pale, jade-faced painters of Tokyo, who, through an art necessarily still, attempted to suggest speed and movement. The gloomy humming of bees forcing their way through the tall, uncut grass, or circling monotonously around the dusty golden horns of the wandering woodbine, seemed to deepen the oppressive quiet. London\u2019s muffled roar sounded like the sustained note of a far-off organ.\\n\\nAt the room\u2019s centre, fastened to an upright easel, stood the full-length portrait of a young man of remarkable beauty. A short distance before it sat the artist himself, Basil Hallward, whose sudden disappearance several years earlier had caused considerable public excitement and prompted so many strange speculations.\\n\\nAs the painter gazed at the graceful, handsome figure he had so skilfully reproduced in his art, a pleased smile crossed his face and seemed ready to remain there. Then he abruptly rose, shut his eyes, and pressed his fingers against the lids, as if trying to hold within his mind some curious dream from which he feared awakening.\\n\\n\u201cIt is your finest work, Basil\u2014the finest thing you have ever done,\u201d said Lord Henry lazily. \u201cYou must surely send it to the Grosvenor next year. The Academy is too vast and too vulgar. Whenever I have gone there, there have either been so many people that I could not see the pictures, which was dreadful, or so many pictures that I could not see the people, which was worse. The Grosvenor is truly the only place.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I shall send it anywhere,\u201d he replied, throwing back his head in that peculiar manner which had once made his Oxford friends laugh at him.\"}"
