{"request_id":"254e39bada060c4623f6f626","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to hear that no disaster has followed the beginning of an enterprise you feared so greatly. I arrived here yesterday, and my first duty is to assure my dear sister that I am well and growing more confident that my undertaking will succeed.

I am already far north of London, and as I walk through the streets of Petersburgh, I feel a cold northern breeze against my cheeks. It strengthens my nerves and fills me with delight. Can you understand this feeling? This wind, which has travelled from the regions I am approaching, gives me a first taste of those icy lands. Encouraged by this promising breeze, my daydreams become more passionate and vivid. I try in vain to believe that the pole is a place of frost and desolation; my imagination always shows it as a land of beauty and joy. There, Margaret, the sun is always visible, its broad disk barely touching the horizon and spreading endless light. There—for, with your permission, my sister, I will place some trust in earlier navigators—snow and frost are absent. Sailing across a calm sea, we may be carried to a land more wonderful and beautiful than any region yet discovered on the inhabited earth. Its natural features and products may be unlike anything known, just as the movements of the heavenly bodies surely are in those unexplored solitudes. What might not be found in a land of eternal light? I may discover there the mysterious force that draws the needle, and perhaps explain countless observations of the heavens whose apparent irregularities may be made consistent by this voyage. I will satisfy my eager curiosity by seeing a part of the world no one has visited before, and may set foot on land never touched by a human being. These hopes draw me onward. They are enough to overcome every fear of danger or death and to make me begin this difficult voyage with the happiness of a child sailing with his holiday companions in a small boat, exploring his native river. But even if all these guesses prove false, you cannot deny the priceless benefit I may bring to humanity, even to future generations, by finding a passage near the pole to countries that now take many months to reach, or by discovering the secret of the magnet, which, if it can be discovered at all, may be revealed only by an undertaking like mine.

These thoughts have calmed the agitation with which I began this letter. My heart now burns with an enthusiasm that seems to lift me toward heaven, for nothing quiets the mind more than a firm purpose—a point on which the soul can fix its inward gaze."}

