{"request_id":"77b7a211309a9cf47dbb40e5","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock I had actually reached an altitude of seventeen miles above the earth’s surface. It therefore seemed clear to me that my rate of ascent was not merely increasing, but that the upward movement would have been perceptible to some degree even if I had not released the ballast I did. The pains in my head and ears returned at intervals with great violence, and I still occasionally bled from the nose; yet, on the whole, I suffered far less than might have been expected. I was breathing, however, with increasing difficulty, and every inhalation brought on an uncomfortable spasm in my chest. I now unpacked the condensing apparatus and prepared it for immediate use.

“The view of the earth at this stage of my ascent was truly beautiful. To the west, north, and south, as far as I could see, stretched a boundless expanse of apparently motionless ocean, growing deeper and deeper blue with every moment and already beginning to show a slight curvature. Far to the east, though perfectly visible, lay the islands of Great Britain, the entire Atlantic coasts of France and Spain, and a small part of northern Africa. Not a trace of any individual building could be seen, and the proudest cities of mankind had completely vanished from the face of the earth. From the Rock of Gibraltar, now reduced to a dim speck, the dark Mediterranean, dotted with shining islands like the sky with stars, extended eastward as far as my sight reached, until its whole mass of water seemed finally to plunge over the abyss of the horizon. I found myself listening intently for the echoes of that mighty cataract. Above me, the sky was pitch-black, and the stars shone with brilliant clarity.

“The pigeons now appeared to be suffering greatly, and I decided to set them free. I first untied one, a beautiful gray-mottled bird, and placed him on the rim of the wickerwork. He seemed extremely distressed, looking anxiously around, fluttering his wings, and cooing loudly, but I could not persuade him to leave the car. At last I picked him up and threw him about half a dozen yards from the balloon. Instead of descending, as I had expected, he struggled desperately to return, uttering sharp, piercing cries. He finally regained his former place on the rim, but scarcely had he done so when his head sank onto his breast, and he fell dead inside the car.”"}

