          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f20cd3661ea479081d14706b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We were united in our love for Adrian, though she, still not wholly beyond childhood, could not value his merits as I did, nor share my sympathy with his pursuits and opinions. I was constantly beside him. There was such tenderness and sweetness in his nature that our conversations took on a gentle, almost unearthly character. Yet he was merry as a lark singing from its airy tower, lofty in thought as an eagle, and innocent as the mild-eyed dove. He could lighten Perdita\u2019s gravity and remove the sting from the tormenting energy of my own nature. I looked back upon my restless longings and painful conflicts with my fellow beings as upon a troubled dream, and seemed as greatly transformed as though I had passed into another form, whose new senses and nervous frame had changed the image of the visible universe in the mind\u2019s mirror. But this was not truly so; I remained the same in strength, in earnest hunger for sympathy, and in my desire for active exertion. My manly virtues had not abandoned me, for the witch Urania spared Sampson\u2019s locks while he rested at her feet; yet everything had become gentler and more humane. Nor did Adrian teach me merely the lifeless truths of history and philosophy. While he used them to help me master my own reckless and uncultivated spirit, he also revealed the living page of his heart, allowing me to feel and comprehend its marvelous character.\\n\\nThe former queen of England had, from her son\u2019s earliest infancy, tried to plant bold and ambitious schemes within his mind. She perceived that he possessed genius and extraordinary talent, and cultivated these qualities so that she might later employ them in advancing her own purposes. She fostered his thirst for knowledge and his impetuous courage; she even endured his untamable love of freedom, hoping that it would, as so often happens, develop into a passion for command. She sought to raise him with resentment toward, and a wish to avenge himself upon, those who had helped bring about his father\u2019s abdication. In this she failed. The reports given him, however distorted, of a great and wise nation claiming the right to govern itself, stirred his admiration; in his early years he became a republican on principle. His mother, nevertheless, did not give up hope. Along with love of authority and proud birth, she cultivated resolute ambition, patience, and self-command. She devoted herself to studying her son\u2019s character. Through praise, blame, and exhortation, she attempted to discover and touch the proper chords; and though the music produced by her efforts sounded discordant to her, she based her hopes on his talents and remained certain that she would eventually win him.\"}"
