          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"80ca192229b7cafdc3d0a09a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He made no second attempt. The boys lay there, counting the dragging moments until it seemed that time itself must be finished and eternity turning gray; then they were thankful to see that the sun was finally going down.\\n\\nOne snore stopped. Injun Joe sat upright, looked around, and grimly smiled at his companion, whose head had fallen forward onto his knees. He nudged him with his foot and said:\\n\\n\u201cHere! _You\u2019re_ a watchman, ain\u2019t you! All right, though\u2014nothing\u2019s happened.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy! Have I been asleep?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, partly, partly. It\u2019s nearly time for us to move, pard. What\u2019ll we do with the little swag we\u2019ve got left?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know\u2014leave it here as we always have, I reckon. No use carrying it away till we head south. Six hundred and fifty in silver is something to lug around.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014all right\u2014it won\u2019t hurt to come here once more.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo\u2014but I\u2019d say come at night, like we used to\u2014it\u2019s better.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but look here, it may be a long while before I get the proper chance at that job; accidents could happen; it ain\u2019t in such a good place. We\u2019ll bury it regular\u2014and bury it deep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood idea,\u201d said his companion. He crossed the room, knelt down, lifted one of the rear hearth-stones, and pulled out a bag that jingled pleasantly. He took twenty or thirty dollars for himself and the same amount for Injun Joe, then handed the bag to Joe, who was now on his knees in the corner, digging with his bowie-knife.\\n\\nThe boys instantly forgot all their fears and misery. With shining, greedy eyes they watched every movement. Luck!\u2014the magnificence of it surpassed anything they could imagine! Six hundred dollars was enough money to make half a dozen boys rich! Here was treasure-hunting under the best possible circumstances\u2014there would be no troublesome uncertainty about where to dig. Every moment they nudged one another\u2014eloquent nudges, easily understood, meaning simply: \u201cOh, but ain\u2019t you glad _now_ we\u2019re here!\u201d\\n\\nJoe\u2019s knife hit something.\\n\\n\u201cHello!\u201d he said.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d asked his companion.\\n\\n\u201cA half-rotten plank\u2014no, I believe it\u2019s a box. Here\u2014lend a hand and we\u2019ll find out what it\u2019s doing here. Never mind, I\u2019ve made a hole.\u201d\\n\\nHe thrust in his hand and pulled it out\u2014\\n\\n\u201cMan, it\u2019s money!\u201d\\n\\nThe two men inspected the handful of coins. They were gold. The boys above were just as thrilled and delighted as the men themselves.\\n\\nJoe\u2019s companion said:\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll finish this quickly. There\u2019s an old rusty pick among the weeds in the corner on the other side of the fireplace\u2014I noticed it a minute ago.\u201d\"}"
