          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7e27299c7e1201ae4ff807be\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She studied me sharply for a short while, then said in a low voice,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you want for them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly,\u201d I replied, \u201cthat you would not mistake them for the others. They may share the same blood, but, believe me, they are not of the same disposition.\u201d\\n\\nStill watching me closely, Miss Havisham repeated,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you want for them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou see, I am not clever enough,\u201d I said, feeling that I coloured slightly, \u201cto conceal from you, even if I wished to, that I do want something. Miss Havisham, if you would spend the money to render my friend Herbert a lasting service in life, though the circumstances require it to be done without his knowing, I could tell you how.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy must it be done without his knowledge?\u201d she asked, resting both hands on her stick so that she could observe me more closely.\\n\\n\u201cBecause,\u201d I said, \u201cI began doing him that service myself, over two years ago, without his knowledge, and I do not wish to be exposed. Why I am unable to complete it, I cannot say. That is part of a secret belonging to someone else, not to me.\u201d\\n\\nBy degrees she took her eyes from me and fixed them on the fire. She watched it for what, in the silence and the glow of the slowly burning-down candles, seemed a long while. Then the falling of some red coals roused her, and she looked back towards me\u2014first absently, then with attention that steadily gathered. Throughout this, Estella continued knitting. When Miss Havisham had settled her gaze upon me, she spoke as though our conversation had not been interrupted,\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhat else?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEstella,\u201d I said, turning to her and striving to steady my trembling voice, \u201cyou know that I love you. You know I have loved you for a long time and with all my heart.\u201d\\n\\nAt my words she lifted her eyes to my face, while her fingers continued their work, and regarded me without any change of expression. I noticed Miss Havisham look from me to her, and then from her back to me.\\n\\n\u201cI ought to have said this earlier, but for my long-standing error. It led me to hope that Miss Havisham intended us for each other. While I believed you were, in a manner, unable to help yourself, I held my tongue. But now I must say it.\u201d\\n\\nStill keeping her expression unchanged, and with her fingers moving on, Estella shook her head.\\n\\n\u201cI know,\u201d I said, replying to that gesture,\u2014\u201cI know. I do not hope ever to call you mine, Estella.\"}"
