{"request_id":"7bc7acbeaa21a113d9875353","rewritten_text":"I passed several funerals; only a few mourners attended them, and the spectators regarded them as omens of the gravest kind. Some watched the processions with wild eagerness—others fled timidly—and some wept aloud.

After providing immediate aid to the sick, Adrian’s chief effort had been to conceal the plague’s symptoms and progress from London’s inhabitants. He knew that fear and gloomy forebodings powerfully aided disease, and that despair and brooding anxiety made the human body especially vulnerable to infection. No disturbing sights were therefore visible: the shops were generally open, and the flow of pedestrians was maintained to some extent. Yet although the appearance of an infected city had been avoided, London seemed greatly changed to me, who had not seen it since the visitation began. There were no carriages, and grass had grown high in the streets; the houses looked desolate; most shutters were closed; and the people I encountered wore a ghastly, frightened expression, utterly unlike the Londoners’ usual businesslike manner. My solitary carriage drew attention as it rattled toward the Protectoral Palace—and the fashionable streets leading to it looked even more dreary and deserted. I found Adrian’s antechamber crowded; it was his hour for receiving petitioners. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited, watching them enter and leave. They were people from the middle and lower classes, whose livelihoods had vanished with the suspension of trade and of the energetic pursuit of money in all its branches that characterized our country. The newcomers showed anxiety, sometimes terror, sharply contrasting with the resigned, even satisfied expressions of those who had already been received. I could see my friend’s influence in their quicker movements and brighter faces. Two o’clock struck, and no one else was admitted; those who had been turned away departed sullenly or sorrowfully, while I entered the audience chamber.

I was struck by the improvement in Adrian’s health. He was no longer bent toward the ground like an over-nurtured spring flower that, growing beyond its strength, is weighed down even by its own crown of blossoms. His eyes were bright, his expression composed, and an air of concentrated energy filled his entire person, replacing its former languor. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who were sorting petitions or recording notes from that day’s audience. Two or three petitioners still waited. I admired his justice and patience. Those who had the means to live outside London, he immediately advised to leave it, providing them with the means to do so."}

