          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82d69027142f40bec27194ed\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t want to get mixed up in any business like that, I says to myself.\\n\\nWhen I came downstairs in the morning, the parlor was closed, and the watchers had gone. Nobody was about except the family, the widow Bartley, and our crowd. I studied their faces to see whether anything had happened, but I couldn\u2019t make out.\\n\\nToward midday the undertaker arrived with his man, and they placed the coffin in the center of the room on two chairs. Then they arranged all our chairs in rows, borrowing more from the neighbors until the hall, parlor, and dining-room were filled. I saw the coffin lid was as it had been before, but I didn\u2019t dare look beneath it with people all around.\\n\\nThen folks began pouring in, and the beats and girls took places in the front row at the coffin\u2019s head. For half an hour the people passed slowly in single file, looking down at the dead man\u2019s face for a minute. Some let fall a tear, and everything was quiet and solemn, except for the girls and beats holding handkerchiefs to their eyes, keeping their heads bowed, and sobbing a little. There was no other noise but feet scraping the floor and noses being blown\u2014because people always blow them more at a funeral than anywhere else except church.\\n\\nWhen the room was crammed full, the undertaker glided about in his black gloves with his gentle, soothing manner, putting on the final touches, arranging people and things shipshape and comfortable, and making no more noise than a cat. He never said a word; he shifted people, squeezed in latecomers, and opened passages with nods and hand signs. Then he stationed himself against the wall. He was the gentlest, smoothest, stealthiest man I ever saw; and he had no more smile than a ham.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeum\u2014a sick one; and when all was ready, a young woman sat down and worked it. It sounded pretty screechy and colicky, and everybody joined in singing, with Peter the only one who had a good voice, as I figured it. Then Reverend Hobson began speaking, slow and solemn; and right away the most outrageous racket anyone ever heard broke out in the cellar. It was only one dog, but he made an awful din and kept it going; the parson had to stand there over the coffin and wait\u2014you couldn\u2019t hear yourself think.\"}"
