          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"12726dcb70ce9648f65a85bf\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There comes a time in every properly raised boy\u2019s life when he desperately wants to go somewhere and dig for buried treasure. That desire suddenly seized Tom one day. He went looking for Joe Harper, but couldn\u2019t find him. Then he looked for Ben Rogers, who had gone fishing. At last he ran into Huck Finn the Red-Handed. Huck would do. Tom led him to a private spot and quietly explained his plan. Huck was willing. He was always ready to join any adventure that promised fun and cost nothing, since he had far too much free time. \u201cWhere\u2019ll we dig?\u201d Huck asked.\\n\\n\u201cOh, almost anywhere.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, is treasure buried everywhere?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, of course not. It\u2019s hidden in very particular places, Huck\u2014sometimes on islands, sometimes in rotten chests beneath the end of a branch on an old dead tree, exactly where the shadow falls at midnight; but usually under the floor of haunted houses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho hides it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRobbers, naturally\u2014who did you think? Sunday-school superintendents?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. If it were mine, I wouldn\u2019t hide it. I\u2019d spend it and have a good time.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo would I. But robbers don\u2019t do things that way. They always hide it and leave it there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t they ever come back for it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo. They mean to, but they usually forget the signs, or they die. Anyway, it stays there a long time and gets rusty. Then someone finds an old yellow paper explaining how to locate the signs\u2014a paper that takes about a week to decode because it\u2019s mostly symbols and hieroglyphics.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHieroglyphics?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPictures and things, you know, that don\u2019t seem to mean anything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave you got one of those papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how are you going to find the signs?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t need any signs. They always bury it under a haunted house, on an island, or beneath a dead tree with one branch sticking out. We\u2019ve searched Jackson\u2019s Island a little, and we can try again sometime. Then there\u2019s the old haunted house up the Still-House branch, and there are plenty of dead trees\u2014whole loads of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under all of them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat a thing to say! No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how will you know which one to search?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSearch them all!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTom, that\u2019ll take the whole summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so what? Suppose you find a brass pot with a hundred dollars in it, all rusty and gray, or a rotten chest full of diamonds. How would you like that?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes lit up.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s excellent. More than excellent for me. Just give me the hundred dollars, and I don\u2019t want any diamonds.\u201d\"}"
