{"request_id":"fa7619a08bf512aa6423d51f","rewritten_text":"The next day he did not leave the house. He spent most of his time in his own room, terrified of dying yet strangely indifferent to life. The feeling that he was being hunted, trapped, and followed had begun to control him. If the tapestry moved in the wind, he trembled. The dead leaves blown against the leaded windows seemed like his wasted resolutions and wild regrets. Whenever he closed his eyes, he saw the sailor’s face staring through the mist-covered glass, and horror seemed to grip his heart again.

But perhaps it was only his imagination that had summoned revenge from the night and placed those terrible images of punishment before him. Real life was chaotic, but imagination was frighteningly logical. It was imagination that made remorse follow sin. It was imagination that made every crime produce its own twisted consequences. In the ordinary world, the wicked were not punished and the good were not rewarded. The strong succeeded, while the weak were pushed into failure. That was all. Besides, if a stranger had been creeping around the house, the servants or keepers would have seen him. If footprints had appeared in the flower beds, the gardeners would have reported them. Yes, it had only been his imagination. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not returned to kill him. He had sailed away on his ship and sunk in some winter sea. At least he was safe from him. The man did not know who he was and could not know. The mask of youth had protected him.

And yet, if it had only been an illusion, how terrible it was to think that conscience could create such frightening ghosts, give them visible bodies, and make them move before him! What kind of life would he have if, day and night, the shadows of his crime watched him from silent corners, mocked him from hidden places, whispered in his ear while he sat at a feast, and woke him with icy fingers as he slept? As the thought entered his mind, he grew pale with fear, and the air suddenly seemed colder. Oh, in what mad moment had he killed his friend! How horrifying even the memory of the scene was! He saw everything again. Every terrible detail returned with greater horror. From the black cave of time, terrible and wrapped in scarlet, rose the image of his sin."}

