          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bf0027e818fc522ded4beadd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A few days later, Virginia and her curly-haired cavalier went riding on Brockley Meadows. While climbing through a hedge, she tore her riding habit badly. When she returned home, she decided to use the back staircase so that no one would see her. As she hurried past the Tapestry Chamber, whose door happened to be open, she thought she saw someone inside. Believing it was her mother\u2019s maid, who sometimes brought her work there, she looked in to ask her to mend the torn habit. To her great surprise, however, it was the Canterville Ghost himself. He sat by the window, watching the ruined gold of the yellowing trees blow through the air and the red leaves whirl wildly down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, and his whole manner showed deep sadness. He looked so lonely and so worn-out that little Virginia, who had first meant to run away and lock herself in her room, felt sorry for him and decided to comfort him. Her footsteps were so quiet, and his sadness so deep, that he did not notice her until she spoke.\\n\\n\u2018I am very sorry for you,\u2019 she said, \u2018but my brothers are going back to Eton tomorrow. Then, if you behave yourself, no one will bother you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018It is ridiculous to ask me to behave myself,\u2019 he replied, turning in surprise toward the pretty little girl who had dared to speak to him. \u2018Completely ridiculous. I must rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and walk about at night, if that is what you mean. It is the only reason I exist.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018That is no reason to exist at all, and you know you have behaved very wickedly. Mrs. Umney told us on the first day we arrived that you had killed your wife.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, I admit it,\u2019 said the Ghost irritably, \u2018but it was strictly a family matter and had nothing to do with anyone else.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018It is very wrong to kill anyone,\u2019 said Virginia, who sometimes had a gentle, serious Puritan manner inherited from an old New England ancestor.\\n\\n\u2018Oh, I hate the cheap harshness of abstract morality! My wife was very plain, never had my ruffs starched properly, and knew nothing about cooking. Why, I once shot a magnificent young buck in Hogley Woods, and do you know how she served it at the table? Still, it does not matter now, because it is all over. And I do not think it was very kind of her brothers to starve me to death, even though I did kill her.\u2019\"}"
