          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"92f01b747fbbb70a14d010ff\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThen listen! You!\u201d Monks replied. \u201cWhen his father fell ill in Rome, his wife\u2014my mother, from whom he had long been separated\u2014joined him there, traveling from Paris and taking me with her, supposedly to look after his property, though I cannot say why, since she had little affection for him, and he had none for her. He knew nothing of us, for his senses had failed him, and he slept on until the following day, when he died. Among the papers in his desk were two, dated on the night his illness began and addressed to you\u201d; he turned to Mr. Brownlow; \u201cwith a few short lines enclosed, and a note on the package saying it was not to be sent until after his death. One of the papers was a letter to this girl Agnes; the other was a will.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat did the letter say?\u201d Mr. Brownlow asked.\\n\\n\u201cThe letter? A sheet of paper covered with writing, containing a remorseful confession and prayers to God to help her. He had told the girl some story that a secret matter\u2014to be explained one day\u2014prevented him from marrying her at that time. So she had continued to trust him patiently, until she trusted too far and lost what no one could ever restore to her. She was then only a few months from giving birth. He told her everything he had intended to do, had he lived, to conceal her shame, and begged her, if he died, not to curse his memory or believe that the consequences of their sin would fall upon her or their child; all the guilt, he said, was his. He reminded her of the day he had given her the small locket and the ring engraved with her Christian name, leaving a blank for the name he hoped one day to give her. He begged her still to keep them and wear them next to her heart, as she had before. Then he continued wildly, repeating the same words again and again, as though he had lost his mind. I believe he had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, as Oliver\u2019s tears fell rapidly.\\n\\nMonks said nothing.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow continued for him, \u201cwas written in the same spirit as the letter. He spoke of the misery his wife had caused him; of your rebellious nature, vice, malice, and premature evil passions\u2014you, his only son, who had been taught to hate him\u2014and left you and your mother an annuity of eight hundred pounds each. He divided the greater part of his property into two equal shares: one for Agnes Fleming, and the other for their child, if it were born alive and ever reached adulthood.\u201d\"}"
