          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"49ac15bac84317c2f391fca0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke with warm praise of his sister\u2019s skill.\\n\\n\u201cI am very glad to hear such a good report of her,\u201d said Lady Catherine; \u201cand please tell her from me that she cannot expect to excel unless she practises a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he replied, \u201cthat she does not need such advice. She practises very regularly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo much the better. One cannot practise too much; and when I next write to her, I shall insist that she never neglect it for any reason. I often tell young ladies that no excellence in music can be achieved without constant practice. I have told Miss Bennet several times that she will never play really well unless she practises more; and although Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she is very welcome, as I have often told her, to come to Rosings every day and play the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. She would not be in anyone\u2019s way there, you know, in that part of the house.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy looked slightly embarrassed by his aunt\u2019s rudeness and made no reply.\\n\\nWhen coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth that she had promised to play for him, and she immediately sat down at the instrument. He pulled a chair close to her. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, then resumed speaking to her other nephew as before, until he moved away from her and, proceeding with his usual deliberation toward the pianoforte, positioned himself where he had a full view of the fair performer\u2019s face. Elizabeth noticed what he was doing, and at the first suitable pause turned to him with a mischievous smile.\\n\\n\u201cYou intend to frighten me, Mr. Darcy, by coming in such a formal manner to hear me. But I will not be alarmed, although your sister does play so well. I have a stubbornness that cannot bear being frightened at another person\u2019s command. My courage always rises with every attempt to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall not say that you are mistaken,\u201d he replied, \u201cbecause you could not truly believe that I meant to alarm you; and I have known you long enough to understand that you take great pleasure in occasionally expressing opinions which are not, in fact, your own.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at this portrait of herself and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin will give you a very amusing impression of me and teach you not to believe a word I say. I am especially unfortunate to have met someone so capable of exposing my true character in a part of the world where I had hoped to present myself with some credit.\u201d\"}"
