{"request_id":"2743ddbc57eb0be3b3f93b86","rewritten_text":"When I had stopped weeping, reason and memory returned, and I began to reflect more calmly on what had happened and how I ought to act. Only a few hours had passed, yet a great revolution had taken place in my life—the natural work of years had been completed since morning. My father was as dead to me, and for a moment I felt as if he, with his white hair, lay in his coffin, while I—my youth gone and old age approaching—mourned his timely death. But it was not so. I was still young—oh, far too young—and he was not dead to others; but I, most miserable, must never see or speak to him again. I must flee from him more urgently than from my greatest enemy; in solitude or in cities, I must never behold him again. That thought left me breathless with anguish, and, fixing itself in my imagination, made me unable for a time to follow any chain of ideas. I thought that from then on I would live in the dreariest seclusion. I would withdraw to the Continent and become a nun—not for religion’s sake, since I was not a Catholic, but so that I might be forever shut away from the world. There I would find solitude where I could weep, and the voices of life would never reach me.

But my father—my beloved and most wretched father? Would he die? Would he never overcome the fierce passion that now held merciless dominion over him? Might he not, many years from now, when age had quenched the burning feelings he now endured, become a father to me again? This thought smoothed my brow, and I could feel—and wept to feel it—a half-melancholy smile draw my lips away from their expression of suffering. I dared hope more favorably for my future life. Years must pass, but they would fly lightly, carried on by hope; or, if they passed heavily, they would still pass, and I would not have lost my father forever. Let him spend another sixteen years wandering desolately; let him once more utter his wild complaints to the vast forests and the tremendous cataracts of another land; let him again endure fearful dangers and hardships that subdued the soul; let the southern sun once more burn his passion-worn cheeks, and the cold night rains fall upon him and chill his blood.

To this life, miserable father, I devote you! Go! Let your days be spent among savages and your nights beneath the open sky! Let your limbs grow worn and your heart grow cold, and let all youth die within you!"}

