          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8594e470ab4431296cf2679c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He could not help himself. And it infuriated him to think, as he believed he saw, that Becky Thatcher had not once guessed he was even among the living. Yet she did see him, all the same; and she knew she was winning her battle, too, and was pleased to watch him suffer as she had suffered.\\n\\nAmy\u2019s cheerful chatter became unbearable. Tom hinted that he had matters to attend to; things that had to be done; and that time was passing quickly. But it was useless\u2014the girl kept chirping away. Tom thought, \u201cOh, hang her, am I never going to get rid of her?\u201d At last he really had to attend to those matters\u2014and she said innocently that she would be \u201caround\u201d when school was dismissed. He hurried off, hating her for saying it.\\n\\n\u201cAny other boy!\u201d Tom thought, grinding his teeth. \u201cAny boy in the whole town except that Saint Louis smarty who thinks he dresses so grand and belongs to the aristocracy! Oh, all right, I licked you the first day you ever set foot in this town, mister, and I\u2019ll lick you again! Just wait till I catch you outside! I\u2019ll just take and\u2014\u201d\\n\\nThen he acted out the beating of an imaginary boy\u2014pounding the air, kicking, and gouging. \u201cOh, you do, do you? You yell \u2019nough, do you? Now then, let that teach you!\u201d And with that the imaginary thrashing ended to his satisfaction.\\n\\nTom ran home at noon. His conscience could stand no more of Amy\u2019s thankful happiness, and his jealousy could endure no more of the other misery. Becky went on examining pictures with Alfred, but as the minutes passed and Tom did not appear to suffer, her triumph began to fade and her interest disappeared; seriousness and distraction came next, followed by melancholy. Two or three times she raised her head at the sound of footsteps, but each time it was a false hope; Tom did not come. At last she became thoroughly wretched and wished she had not carried matters so far. When poor Alfred, noticing that he was losing her without knowing why, kept crying, \u201cOh, here\u2019s a jolly one! Look at this!\u201d she finally lost patience and said, \u201cOh, don\u2019t bother me! I don\u2019t care about them!\u201d Then she burst into tears, rose, and walked away.\\n\\nAlfred followed close beside her and was about to try comforting her, but she said:\\n\\n\u201cGo away and leave me alone, can\u2019t you! I hate you!\u201d\\n\\nSo the boy stopped, wondering what he could possibly have done\u2014for she had said she would look at pictures throughout the noon recess\u2014and she continued on, crying. Then Alfred went thoughtfully into the empty schoolhouse. He was humiliated and angry. It was easy for him to see the truth: the girl had merely used him as a means of venting her spite against Tom Sawyer.\"}"
