{"request_id":"cacd6fc02048752d22456d18","rewritten_text":"Four days after these strange events, a funeral left Canterville Chase at about eleven o’clock at night. The hearse was pulled by eight black horses, each wearing a large tuft of nodding ostrich plumes on its head, and the leaden coffin was covered with a rich purple pall embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat of arms. Servants carrying lighted torches walked beside the hearse and coaches, and the entire procession was deeply impressive. Lord Canterville was the chief mourner, having come especially from Wales for the funeral, and sat in the first carriage with little Virginia. The United States Minister and his wife followed, then Washington and the three boys, while Mrs. Umney rode in the last carriage. Everyone felt that, since the ghost had frightened her for more than fifty years, she had earned the right to see the last of him. A deep grave had been dug in a corner of the churchyard, directly beneath the old yew tree, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier conducted the service with great solemnity. When the ceremony ended, the servants, following an old custom of the Canterville family, put out their torches. As the coffin was lowered into the grave, Virginia stepped forward and placed upon it a large cross made of white and pink almond blossoms. At that moment, the moon emerged from behind a cloud and bathed the little churchyard in silent silver, while a nightingale began singing in a distant copse. She remembered the ghost’s description of the Garden of Death; her eyes filled with tears, and she scarcely spoke during the drive home.

The next morning, before Lord Canterville left for town, Mr. Otis spoke with him about the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were extraordinarily magnificent, particularly a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, a superb example of sixteenth-century craftsmanship. Their value was so great that Mr. Otis had serious reservations about allowing his daughter to keep them.

‘My lord,’ he said, ‘I know that in this country mortmain is considered to apply to trinkets as well as land, and it seems perfectly clear to me that these jewels are, or ought to be, family heirlooms. I must therefore ask you to take them to London and regard them simply as part of your property, restored to you under certain strange circumstances. As for my daughter, she is only a child, and, fortunately, has little interest so far in such ornaments of empty luxury.’"}

