          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"011e22e677702190f3bcd459\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was something strange in all this that drew me in and enchanted me. He seemed to have awakened from a long, visionary sleep, and felt somewhat like one of the seven sleepers, or like Nourjahad in that beautiful imitation of an Eastern tale: Diana was gone; his friends had changed or died, and now, upon awakening, I was all he had left to love on earth.\\n\\nHow dear the waters, mountains, and woods of Loch Lomond became to me, now that I had so beloved a companion for my rambles. With my father, I visited every delightful place, whether on the islands or beside the waterfalls sheltered by trees; every shady path and every hollow tangled with undergrowth and fern. His conversation broadened my thoughts. I felt as though I had been recreated, carrying within me all the freshness and vitality of a new being. Since his arrival, I seemed to have been transported from a narrow patch of earth into a universe boundless to the imagination and understanding. Before, my life had been like a pleasant country stream, never meant to leave its native fields, but destined, once its quiet task was done, to be absorbed without leaving a trace. Now it seemed a varied river flowing through a fertile and lovely landscape, forever changing and forever beautiful. Alas! I did not know what desert it was approaching, what rocks would tear its waters, or what hideous scene would be reflected, more horribly distorted, in its waves. Life was then brilliant; I was beginning to learn hope\u2014and what brings more bitter despair to the heart than hope destroyed?\\n\\nIs it not strange that grief should so quickly follow happiness so divine? I drank from an enchanted cup, but gall lay at the bottom of its lingering sweetness. My heart was filled with deep affection, yet it was calm because of its very depth and fullness. I had no idea that misery could arise from love, and this lesson, which everyone must eventually learn, was taught to me in a way few are forced to endure. I mourn now, and must always mourn, those few short months of paradise; I had disobeyed no command, eaten no apple, and yet I was driven ruthlessly from it. Alas! My companion had, and I was cast down in his fall. But I am straying from my account\u2014let sorrow come at its appointed time; at this point in my story, I may still speak of happiness.\\n\\nThree months passed in this delightful companionship, when my aunt fell ill. I spent an entire month in her room nursing her, but her illness was fatal, and she died, leaving me inconsolable for some time. Death is so terrible to the living; the bonds of habit are so strong, even when affection does not bind them, that the heart must be tormented when they are broken.\"}"
