{"request_id":"4d915297e72fa9730cdf11b0","rewritten_text":"Four days after these strange events, a funeral set out from Canterville Chase at about eleven o’clock at night. The hearse was pulled by eight black horses, each wearing upon its head a large tuft of nodding ostrich plumes, while the leaden coffin was draped in a splendid purple pall embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat of arms. Servants carrying lighted torches walked beside the hearse and coaches, and the entire procession was deeply impressive. Lord Canterville, who had come specially from Wales for the funeral, was the chief mourner and sat in the first carriage with little Virginia. The United States Minister and his wife followed, then Washington and the three boys, while Mrs. Umney occupied the last carriage. Everyone felt that, having been frightened by the ghost for more than fifty years, she deserved to witness his final departure. A deep grave had been prepared in a corner of the churchyard, directly beneath the old yew-tree, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier conducted the service with great solemnity. Once the ceremony ended, the servants, following an ancient custom of the Canterville family, put out their torches. As the coffin was lowered into the grave, Virginia came forward and placed upon it a large cross of white and pink almond blossoms. At that moment the moon emerged from behind a cloud and bathed the small churchyard in quiet silver, while a nightingale began singing from a distant copse. She remembered the ghost’s account of the Garden of Death; her eyes filled with tears, and she scarcely spoke during the journey home.

The following morning, before Lord Canterville left for town, Mr. Otis spoke with him about the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were extraordinarily magnificent, particularly a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, which was a superb example of sixteenth-century workmanship. Their value was so considerable that Mr. Otis had serious reservations about permitting his daughter to keep them.

‘My lord,’ he said, ‘I understand that in this country mortmain is considered applicable to trinkets as well as land, and it seems perfectly evident to me that these jewels are, or ought to be, heirlooms belonging to your family. I must therefore ask you to take them with you to London and consider them merely a part of your property, restored to you under certain unusual circumstances. As for my daughter, she is only a child, and, fortunately, has so far little concern for such accessories of frivolous luxury."}

