{"request_id":"a8445d04d29a46be5be7f64e","rewritten_text":"We were raised together, and there was less than a year between our ages. I hardly need say that we never knew any division or quarrel. Harmony was the heart of our companionship, and the differences between our characters brought us even closer. Elizabeth was calmer and more thoughtful; but although I was passionate, I could focus more intensely and felt a stronger hunger for knowledge. She occupied herself with the poets’ airy creations. In the grand and wonderful scenes around our Swiss home—the majestic mountains, the changing seasons, storms and calm, winter’s silence, and the lively summers in the Alps—she found endless reasons for wonder and joy. While my companion looked at the magnificent sights of the world with a serious, contented mind, I loved studying their causes. To me, the world was a secret I longed to understand. Curiosity, serious efforts to discover nature’s hidden laws, and a joy almost like rapture when those laws were revealed to me are among the earliest feelings I can remember.

When a second son was born, seven years younger than I, my parents gave up their wandering life completely and settled in their native country. We owned a house in Geneva and a country home at Belrive, on the eastern shore of the lake, a little more than a league from the city. We lived mainly at the latter, and my parents led a fairly secluded life. I naturally avoided crowds and formed deep attachments to only a few people. I therefore cared little for my schoolmates in general, but I became the closest friend of one of them. Henry Clerval was the son of a Geneva merchant. He was a boy of unusual talent and imagination. He loved adventure, hardship, and even danger for their own sake. He had read deeply in books of chivalry and romance. He wrote heroic songs and began composing many tales of magic and knightly adventure. He tried to make us perform plays and take part in masquerades, with characters drawn from the heroes of Roncesvalles, King Arthur’s Round Table, and the chivalrous warriors who gave their lives to recover the holy sepulchre from the infidels.

No one could have had a happier childhood than I did. My parents were filled with kindness and generosity. We knew they were not tyrants who ruled our lives according to their own wishes, but the people who created and provided all the many joys we enjoyed."}

