{"request_id":"497fc6272ad680f562833c99","rewritten_text":"You will be glad to learn that no misfortune has attended the beginning of an enterprise you have viewed with such gloomy apprehension. I reached this place yesterday, and my first duty is to assure my dear sister of my safety and my growing confidence in the success of my undertaking.

I am already far north of London, and as I walk through the streets of Petersburgh, I feel a chilly northern wind upon my cheeks, strengthening my nerves and filling me with pleasure. Can you understand this sensation? This breeze, having travelled from the regions toward which I am journeying, offers me a foretaste of those frozen climates. Encouraged by this promising wind, my daydreams grow more ardent and vivid. I strive in vain to convince myself that the pole is a realm of frost and desolation; to my imagination it continually appears a place of beauty and delight. There, Margaret, the sun remains forever visible, its broad disk barely skimming the horizon and spreading an unending radiance. There—for, with your permission, my sister, I will place some faith in earlier navigators—snow and frost are banished; and, sailing across a tranquil sea, we may be carried to a land surpassing in marvels and beauty every region yet discovered upon the habitable globe. Its productions and features may be without parallel, just as the phenomena of the heavens surely are in those unexplored solitudes. What might not be found in a country of everlasting light? I may discover there the mysterious power that draws the needle, and perhaps settle a thousand celestial observations whose apparent irregularities need only this voyage to make them forever consistent. I shall satisfy my eager curiosity by seeing a portion of the world never before visited, and may set foot upon a land no human being has yet trodden. These are my inducements, and they suffice to overcome every fear of danger or death, leading me to begin this arduous voyage with the delight of a child who embarks in a small boat with his holiday companions on a voyage of discovery up his native river. But even if all these suppositions prove false, you cannot deny the immeasurable benefit I would bestow upon all mankind, even the generations yet unborn, by finding a passage near the pole to those countries, which now require so many months to reach; or by uncovering the secret of the magnet, which, if it can be achieved at all, may be accomplished only through an undertaking such as mine.

These thoughts have driven away the agitation with which I began my letter, and I feel my heart burning with an enthusiasm that lifts me toward heaven, for nothing so calms the mind as a firm purpose—a point upon which the soul may rest its intellectual gaze."}

