          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5fd1a87c40bc4ba2ba2a49b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I have said that he was a poet: at three and twenty he published his first poem, and the whole nation greeted it with enthusiasm and delight. His fortunate star seemed always to shine upon him; no reputation had ever been established so swiftly, and his fame was universal. The multitude praised the very poems that astonished the learned man in his study: there was not a single dissenting voice.\\n\\nIt was then, at the summit of his glory, that he became acquainted with Elinor. She was a young heiress of exquisite beauty, living under the guardianship of another: from the first moment they were seen together, they seemed made for each other. Elinor did not possess Woodville\u2019s genius, but she was generous and noble, and elevated by youth and by the love she inspired everywhere beyond concern for anything except virtue and excellence. She was lovely; her manners were open and unaffected; her deep blue eyes shone with a radiance that could only arise from sensibility united with wisdom.\\n\\nThey were made for one another, and soon fell in love. Woodville now felt, for the first time, love\u2019s delight; and Elinor was transported to possess the heart of one so beautiful and illustrious among his fellow men. Could anything but unmingled joy proceed from such a union?\\n\\nWoodville was a Poet\u2014every society sought him, and whenever he appeared all eyes were fixed upon him alone; yet he was the son of a poor clergyman, while Elinor was a wealthy heiress. Her guardian did not object to their mutual affection: Woodville\u2019s merit was too distinguished to invite objection on the ground of his lesser fortune; but her father\u2019s dying will forbade her to marry until she had reached her majority, and her inheritance depended upon her obeying that command. She had only just entered her twentieth year, so she and her lover were compelled to endure the delay. Still, they were constantly together, and their happiness seemed paradisal: they studied side by side, made plans for their future occupations, and, drinking love and joy from each other\u2019s eyes and words, scarcely lamented the postponement of their complete union. Woodville continually advanced in glory; and Elinor grew lovelier and wiser beneath the instruction of her accomplished lover.\\n\\nIn two months Elinor would be twenty-one, and everything was ready for their marriage. How can I recount the catastrophe that overtook so much joy? Yet the earth would not be the blighted and sorrowful earth it is if such an angelic pair had been permitted to exist for one another: search the world, and you will find no perfect happiness such as their marriage would have given them; the order of things established among us miserable dwellers on earth would have needed to be overturned before such consummate joy could be allowed.\"}"
