          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2ad328ae7efff38d6ddc6b96\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke warmly in praise of his sister\u2019s skill.\\n\\n\u201cI am very glad to hear such a good report of her,\u201d said Lady Catherine; \u201cand please tell her from me that she cannot expect to excel unless she practises a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he replied, \u201cthat she does not need such advice. She practises very regularly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo much the better. One cannot practise too much; and when I next write to her, I shall insist that she never neglect it. I often tell young ladies that no musical excellence can be gained without constant practice. I have told Miss Bennet several times that she will never play really well unless she practises more; and although Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she is very welcome, as I have often told her, to come to Rosings every day and play the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. She would not be in anyone\u2019s way there, you know.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy looked slightly embarrassed by his aunt\u2019s bad manners and said nothing.\\n\\nWhen coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth that she had promised to play for him, and she immediately sat down at the instrument. He pulled a chair close to her. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, then began talking, as before, to her other nephew. He eventually moved away from her and, with his usual calm deliberation, walked toward the pianoforte and placed himself where he had a full view of the performer\u2019s face. Elizabeth noticed what he was doing and, at the first suitable pause, turned to him with a playful smile.\\n\\n\u201cYou mean to frighten me, Mr. Darcy, by coming over in such a serious manner to hear me. But I will not be alarmed, even though your sister does play so well. I am stubborn and cannot bear to be frightened because someone else wishes it. My courage always grows whenever anyone tries to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall not say that you are mistaken,\u201d he replied, \u201cbecause you could not truly believe that I meant to alarm you. I have known you long enough to understand that you take great pleasure in sometimes claiming opinions that are not really your own.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at this description of her and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin will give you a very amusing idea of me and teach you not to believe anything I say. I am especially unfortunate to meet someone so capable of revealing my true character, in a place where I had hoped to make a respectable impression.\u201d\"}"
