          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"11120a7fe82798a1e4856bb4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter had taken an excellent house in Camden Place, an elevated, dignified setting suited to a man of consequence; and he and Elizabeth were both established there, greatly to their satisfaction.\\n\\nAnne entered it with a sinking heart, expecting to be imprisoned there for many months, and anxiously asking herself, \u201cOh! when shall I leave you again?\u201d Yet the unexpectedly warm welcome she received did her good. Her father and sister were pleased to see her, wanting to show her the house and furnishings, and greeted her kindly. When she sat down to dinner and made a fourth, they treated her presence as an advantage.\\n\\nMrs Clay was very agreeable and smiling, but her courtesies and smiles seemed more habitual. Anne had always believed that she would put on the proper appearance when Anne arrived, but the others\u2019 friendliness was unexpected. They were clearly in excellent spirits, and Anne soon heard why. They had no desire to hear about her. After allowing themselves a few compliments about how deeply she must have been missed in their former neighbourhood\u2014compliments Anne could not return\u2014they made only a few weak inquiries before the conversation became entirely their own. Uppercross aroused no interest, and Kellynch very little: everything was Bath.\\n\\nThey had the pleasure of assuring her that Bath had exceeded their expectations in every respect. Their house was unquestionably the finest in Camden Place; their drawing rooms possessed many clear advantages over all the others they had seen or heard about, and the superiority extended equally to the style of the decoration and the taste of the furniture. Their company was eagerly sought. Everyone wanted to visit them. They had declined many introductions, yet people whose names they did not know were constantly leaving cards.\\n\\nHere were abundant sources of pleasure. Could Anne wonder that her father and sister were happy? She might not wonder, but she had to sigh that her father felt no humiliation in his change of circumstances, saw nothing to regret in the responsibilities and dignity of a resident landowner, and found so much to be proud of in the small distinctions of a town. She had to sigh, smile, and wonder too, as Elizabeth flung open the folding doors and walked triumphantly from one drawing room to the other, boasting of their size; at the possibility that the woman who had once been mistress of Kellynch Hall could find space between two walls, perhaps thirty feet apart, something to take pride in.\\n\\nBut this was not all they had to make them happy. They had Mr Elliot as well.\"}"
