{"request_id":"868253734d9a4ede0b2e301a","rewritten_text":"The stars still shone brightly when I awoke, and Taurus high in the southern sky showed that it was midnight. I had been troubled by disturbing dreams. I thought I had been invited to Timon’s last feast; I arrived with a keen appetite, but when the covers were removed, only hot water sent up its unsatisfying steam. I fled from the host’s anger, though he had taken Raymond’s form. In my diseased imagination, the vessels he hurled after me were filled with foul vapour, while my friend’s shape, twisted in a thousand ways, grew into a gigantic phantom bearing the mark of pestilence on its brow. The growing shadow rose higher and higher, filling the hard vault that bent over, supported, and enclosed the world, and seeming at last to strain beyond it. The nightmare became torture. With a great effort I threw off sleep and brought reason back to its usual work. My first thought was Perdita. I had to return to her and support her, drawing from despair whatever strength might sustain her wounded heart, and recalling her from the wild extremes of grief through the strict laws of duty and the gentle tenderness of regret.

The stars’ position was my only guide. I turned away from the terrible ruins of the Golden City and, after great effort, managed to escape its enclosure. Outside the walls I met a company of soldiers. I borrowed a horse from one of them and hurried to my sister. The plain had changed during that short time. The camp had been broken up, and the remains of the scattered army appeared in small groups here and there. Every face was dark with sorrow, and every gesture showed astonishment and dismay.

With a heavy heart I entered the palace, afraid to move forward, speak, or look. Perdita sat in the middle of the hall on the marble floor, her head bowed to her breast, her hair disordered, and her fingers busily twisting together. She was as pale as marble, and agony tightened every feature. She noticed me and looked up questioningly. The faint hope in her glance was misery; the words died before I could speak, and I felt a dreadful smile crease my lips. She understood my gesture. Her head fell again, and her fingers began moving restlessly once more. At last I found my voice, but it frightened her. The unhappy girl had understood my look, and would not, for anything in the world, have the story of her terrible sorrow formed and confirmed by harsh, final words. Instead, she seemed to wish to turn my thoughts away from it. She rose from the floor. “Hush!” she whispered. “After much crying, Clara is asleep; we must not disturb her.”"}

