          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"68c6450a7894d013d93c3dfe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI now hasten to the most moving part of my story. I shall describe events that filled me with feelings which, changing what I had been, made me what I am.\\n\\n\u201cSpring advanced quickly; the weather grew fine, and the skies remained cloudless. I was astonished that the place which had once been barren and gloomy should now bloom with the most beautiful flowers and greenery. My senses were delighted and renewed by a thousand pleasant scents and a thousand beautiful sights.\\n\\n\u201cIt was on one of these days, when my cottagers had paused from their work as they often did\u2014the old man playing his guitar while the children listened\u2014that I noticed Felix\u2019s face was sorrowful beyond expression. He sighed repeatedly, and once his father stopped playing; from his manner, I guessed that he was asking his son the reason for his sadness. Felix answered cheerfully, and the old man was beginning his music again when someone knocked at the door.\\n\\n\u201cIt was a woman on horseback, accompanied by a countryman acting as her guide. She wore a dark dress and was covered by a thick black veil. Agatha asked her a question, but the stranger replied only by saying, in a sweet voice, the name Felix. Her voice was musical, though unlike that of either of my friends. At hearing the name, Felix hurried toward her. When she saw him, she lifted her veil, and I beheld a face of angelic beauty and expression. Her hair was shining raven-black and intricately braided; her eyes were dark but gentle, though lively; her features were perfectly proportioned, and her complexion wonderfully fair, with a lovely pink tint in each cheek.\\n\\n\u201cFelix seemed overwhelmed with delight at the sight of her. Every sign of sorrow disappeared from his face, which instantly expressed such ecstatic joy that I could scarcely have believed it capable of showing so much; his eyes sparkled and his cheeks flushed with pleasure. At that moment, I thought him as beautiful as the stranger. She seemed moved by different feelings; wiping a few tears from her lovely eyes, she extended her hand to Felix, who kissed it passionately and called her, as far as I could make out, his sweet Arabian. She did not seem to understand him, but smiled. He helped her dismount and, dismissing her guide, led her into the cottage. He and his father exchanged some words, and the young stranger knelt at the old man\u2019s feet and tried to kiss his hand, but he raised her and embraced her warmly.\\n\\n\u201cI soon realized that although the stranger made articulate sounds and seemed to possess a language of her own, neither she nor the cottagers understood the other.\u201d\"}"
