{"request_id":"3477e0c9e24926ea77819fe2","rewritten_text":"“It was only some time later that I recovered sufficiently to attend to the balloon’s ordinary needs. I then examined it carefully and, to my great relief, found it undamaged. All my instruments were safe, and fortunately I had lost neither ballast nor provisions. Indeed, I had secured them so firmly in place that such a loss was entirely impossible. When I looked at my watch, I found it was six o’clock. I was still rising rapidly, and my barometer showed that I had reached an altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Directly below me on the ocean lay a small black object, slightly oblong, apparently about the size of—and in every respect resembling—one of those children’s toys called a domino. Bringing my telescope to bear on it, I clearly recognized a British ninety-four-gun ship, close-hauled and pitching heavily in the sea, her bow pointed W.S.W. Apart from this ship, I saw nothing but ocean, sky, and the sun, which had risen long before.

“It is now high time that I explain to Your Excellencies the purpose of my perilous voyage. Your Excellencies will remember that difficult circumstances in Rotterdam had finally driven me to resolve upon suicide. It was not that I felt any real disgust for life itself, but that I was tormented beyond endurance by the incidental miseries of my situation. In this state of mind, wishing to live yet weary of living, the treatise at the bookseller’s stall opened a possibility to my imagination. I then made my final decision. I would depart, yet live—to leave the world, yet continue to exist—in short, to abandon riddles, I resolved, whatever might follow, to force my way, if possible, to the moon. Now, lest I be thought more insane than I truly am, I will describe as fully as I can the considerations that led me to believe that such an undertaking, though undoubtedly difficult and unquestionably dangerous, was not, for a daring spirit, entirely beyond the limits of possibility.

“The moon’s actual distance from the earth was the first matter to be considered. The mean, or average, distance between the centers of the two bodies is 59.9643 of the earth’s equatorial radii, or only about 237,000 miles. I say the mean or average distance, but it must be remembered that the moon’s orbit is an ellipse whose eccentricity amounts to no less than 0.05484 of the ellipse’s major semi-axis, while the earth’s center lies at its focus. Therefore, if I could somehow contrive to meet the moon, so to speak, at its perigee, the distance mentioned above would be considerably reduced.”"}

