          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"899b961bd229b16829c2051e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood-bye, Uncle George. If I stay any longer, I\u2019ll be late for lunch. Thank you for giving me the information I wanted. I always like to know everything about my new friends, and nothing about my old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you having lunch, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I invited myself and Mr. Gray. He is her newest prot\u00e9g\u00e9.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, to stop bothering me with her charity appeals. I\u2019m tired of them. Why, that good woman thinks I have nothing to do except write cheques for her foolish causes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, Uncle George, I\u2019ll tell her, but it won\u2019t make any difference. Philanthropic people lose all sense of humanity. That is their defining quality.\u201d\\n\\nThe old gentleman gave an approving growl and rang for his servant. Lord Henry walked through the low arcade into Burlington Street, then headed toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo that was the story of Dorian Gray\u2019s parentage. Though it had been told to him bluntly, it had still moved him with the suggestion of a strange, almost modern romance. A beautiful woman risking everything for a mad passion. A few wild weeks of happiness ended by a horrible, treacherous crime. Months of silent suffering, followed by a child born in pain. The mother taken by death, the boy left alone with an old, loveless man. Yes, it was an interesting background. It gave the boy a more complete shape, made him seem more perfect, in a way. Behind every beautiful thing that existed, there was something tragic. Worlds had to struggle and suffer before even the humblest flower could bloom.... And how charming he had been at dinner the night before, sitting across from him at the club with startled eyes and lips parted in frightened pleasure, while the red candle shades deepened the rose in his awakening face. Talking to him was like playing an exquisite violin. He responded to every touch and movement of the bow.... There was something intensely exciting about influencing someone. No other activity was like it. To project one\u2019s soul into some graceful form and let it remain there for a moment; to hear one\u2019s own ideas returned with all the added music of passion and youth; to pass one\u2019s temperament into another person as if it were a subtle liquid or a strange perfume: there was genuine joy in that\u2014perhaps the most satisfying joy left to us in an age as narrow and vulgar as our own, an age crude in its pleasures and ordinary in its ambitions.... He was a wonderful type, too, this boy whom chance had brought him to in Basil\u2019s studio\u2014or, at least, he could be shaped into a wonderful type.\"}"
