          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"642476220aff6036370f7b2e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was a delightful feeling of clearing things up and taking a quiet pause before beginning life anew in our village on Saturday nights, and it encouraged Joe to risk staying out half an hour later on Saturdays than at other times. When the half-hour and the rum and water ran out together, Joe rose to leave and took me by the hand.\\n\\n\u201cWait half a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the strange man. \u201cI think I have a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I do, the boy shall have it.\u201d\\n\\nHe found it among a handful of small change, wrapped it in some crumpled paper, and gave it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he said. \u201cMind! Your own.\u201d\\n\\nI thanked him, staring at him far beyond the limits of good manners and clinging tightly to Joe. He wished Joe good-night, and Mr. Wopsle good-night as well (for he left with us), but gave me only a look with his aiming eye\u2014no, not a look, since he closed it, though wonders can be done with an eye by concealing it.\\n\\nOn the way home, if I had felt like talking, the conversation would have been entirely mine, because Mr. Wopsle left us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, while Joe walked all the way home with his mouth wide open, rinsing the rum out with as much air as possible. But I was almost stunned by the reappearance of my old wrongdoing and old acquaintance, and could think of nothing else.\\n\\nMy sister was not in a particularly bad temper when we entered the kitchen, and Joe was encouraged by that unusual fact to tell her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll wager,\u201d said Mrs. Joe triumphantly, \u201cor he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nI took it from the paper, and it turned out to be genuine. \u201cBut what\u2019s this?\u201d said Mrs. Joe, dropping the shilling and snatching up the paper. \u201cTwo one-pound notes?\u201d\\n\\nThey were