          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7fe4e2a62ea215d3f551203e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No imagination can picture the sublimity that might have been displayed by a similar phenomenon occurring in the darkness of night. Hell itself might have seemed an appropriate image. Even as it was, my hair stood on end as I gazed far down into the yawning abysses, allowing my imagination, as it were, to descend and wander through the strange vaulted halls, ruddy gulfs, and red, ghastly chasms of that hideous, unfathomable fire. I had indeed made a narrow escape. Had the balloon remained only a little longer within the cloud\u2014that is, had the discomfort of getting wet not prompted me to release the ballast\u2014inevitable ruin would have followed. Such dangers, though rarely considered, are perhaps the greatest that balloons must face. By this time, however, I had reached too great an altitude to feel any further anxiety on that account.\\n\\n\u201cI was now rising rapidly, and by seven o\u2019clock the barometer showed an altitude of no less than nine and a half miles. I began to have great difficulty breathing. My head, too, was extremely painful; and after noticing moisture on my cheeks for some time, I finally discovered that it was blood, flowing quite rapidly from my eardrums. My eyes also caused me great distress. When I passed my hand over them, they seemed to have protruded from their sockets to a considerable degree; and every object in the car, even the balloon itself, appeared distorted. These symptoms exceeded anything I had expected and alarmed me. At that moment, very imprudently and without thinking, I threw three five-pound pieces of ballast from the car. The increased rate of ascent carried me too quickly, without sufficient gradual adjustment, into a highly rarefied layer of the atmosphere, and the result nearly proved fatal to both my expedition and myself. I was suddenly seized by a spasm lasting more than five minutes; and even after it had somewhat subsided, I could draw breath only at long intervals and in gasps\u2014bleeding heavily all the while from my nose and ears, and slightly from my eyes as well. The pigeons appeared extremely distressed and struggled to escape, while the cat mewed piteously and, with her tongue hanging from her mouth, staggered back and forth in the car as though poisoned. Only then did I realize how reckless I had been to release the ballast, and my agitation became extreme. I expected nothing less than death\u2014and death within a few minutes.\u201d\"}"
