          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9e0d12c72ac39a7f090ac013\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Shall I be carried to the skies, on beds of flowers and ease,\\nWhile others fight to win the prize, and sail through bloody seas?\\n\\nHe was considered a wonderful reader. At church \u201csociables,\u201d he was always asked to read poetry. When he finished, the ladies would raise their hands and let them drop helplessly into their laps, roll their eyes, and shake their heads, as if to say, \u201cWords cannot express it; it is too beautiful, TOO beautiful for this mortal earth.\u201d\\n\\nAfter the hymn, the Rev. Mr. Sprague became a bulletin board and read aloud \u201cnotices\u201d of meetings, societies, and other matters until it seemed the list would last until the end of time\u2014a strange custom still practiced in America, even in cities, in this age of plentiful newspapers. Often, the less reason there is to preserve a traditional custom, the harder it is to abandon it.\\n\\nThen the minister prayed. It was a good, generous prayer, full of details: he prayed for the church and its little children; for the village\u2019s other churches; for the village itself; for the county; for the State; for its officers; for the United States; for the nation\u2019s churches; for Congress; for the President; for government officials; for poor sailors tossed by stormy seas; for oppressed millions groaning under European monarchies and Eastern despotisms; for those who had the light and good news but lacked eyes to see or ears to hear; for the heathen on distant islands; and he ended by asking that the words he was about to speak might find grace and favor, like seeds planted in fertile soil that would eventually produce a thankful harvest of good. Amen.\\n\\nDresses rustled, and the standing congregation sat down. The boy whose story this book tells did not enjoy the prayer; he merely endured it\u2014if he even did that. He was restless throughout. Without meaning to, he kept count of the prayer\u2019s details, because he was not listening, yet knew the familiar ground and the minister\u2019s usual path across it. Whenever a small new point was inserted, his ear caught it and his whole nature objected. He thought additions were unfair and downright scoundrelly. During the prayer, a fly landed on the back of the pew before him and tormented him by calmly rubbing its hands together, wrapping its arms around its head, and polishing it so hard that it seemed almost to separate from the body, leaving its thin neck exposed; scraping its wings with its hind legs and smoothing them against its body as though they were coat-tails; carrying out its entire toilet as peacefully as if it knew it was completely safe.\"}"
