ght hear the nightingale, while fire-flies danced in luminous measure, and the gentle cooing of the aziolo promised fair weather to the travellers. Did we pass through a valley? Tender shadows surrounded us, and rocks glowed with beautiful colours. If we crossed a mountain, Greece, like a living map, lay spread below, her famous peaks piercing the ether and her rivers tracing silver lines through the fertile land. Almost afraid to breathe, we English travellers gazed in ecstasy upon this magnificent landscape, so unlike the sober colours and melancholy charms of our native scenery. After leaving Macedonia, the fertile yet low plains of Thrace offered fewer beauties; nevertheless, our journey remained absorbing. An advance guard announced our approach, and the country people soon began moving to honour Lord Raymond.\"}"
