{"request_id":"57c7dcea755ef489fdc09e70","rewritten_text":"My father had been, and his memory was, the very life of my life. I might feel gratitude toward another, but never again could I love or hope as I once had; everything was suffering, and even my pleasures were endured rather than enjoyed. I was like a lonely place among mountains, enclosed on every side by steep black precipices, where no warm ray could enter and from which there was no path to brighter fields. Thus, though the spirit of friendship comforted me for a time, it could not restore me. It came like some gentle visitation; it departed, and I scarcely felt its loss. The spirit of existence was dead within me; do not wonder, then, that when it came I did not welcome more gladly, or when it left I did not mourn more bitterly, heaven’s finest gift—a friend.

My friend’s name was Woodville. I shall briefly tell his history, that you may judge how cold my heart must have been, since neither his eloquent words nor tender sympathy could warm it; and since he too was deeply unhappy, we were well suited to console one another, had Misery’s Medusa head not hardened me into stone. Woodville’s misfortunes did not spring from the heart’s deepest source, as mine did; his was a natural grief, one meant not to destroy but to purify the heart, and from which, once its shadow had passed, he might emerge brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had received a classical education. He was among the very few whom Fortune favors from birth, upon whom she lavishes every gift of mind and person without limit, and whom, under her special protection, no slight imperfection or passing disappointment is permitted to touch. She seemed to have fashioned his mind from an excellence no dross could stain, and his understanding was such that no error could distort it. His genius was transcendent, and when it rose like a bright star in the east, every eye turned toward it in admiration. He was a Poet. That name has been so often debased that it cannot express all he was. He resembled a poet of old, crowned by the Muses in his cradle, with bees feeding upon his lips. As he moved among other men, he seemed surrounded by a heavenly halo that set him apart and raised him above them. It was his extraordinary beauty, the dazzling fire of his eyes, and his words, whose rich accents wrapped the listener in silent, ecstatic wonder, that made him surpass all others, so that beside him they seemed created only to serve his superior excellence."}

