          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8d86a6d37cc9dfad8dd1d410\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I wish to see you permanently well connected, and for that reason it will be prudent to have as few peculiar acquaintances as possible; therefore, I say that if you are still in this country when Mr. Martin marries, I hope your intimacy with the sisters will not lead you into an acquaintance with his wife, who will probably be nothing more than an uneducated farmer\u2019s daughter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo be sure. Yes. Not that I imagine Mr. Martin would ever marry anyone who had not received some education\u2014and been very properly brought up. Still, I do not mean to oppose my opinion to yours\u2014and I am certain I shall not seek his wife\u2019s acquaintance. I shall always feel great regard for the Miss Martins, particularly Elizabeth, and should be very sorry to lose them, since they are every bit as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, certainly I had better not visit her, if I can avoid it.\u201d\\n\\nEmma observed her throughout the changing tones of this speech, and detected no alarming signs of love. The young man had been her first admirer, but Emma trusted that he had no further hold upon her, and that Harriet, on her side, would offer no serious obstacle to any friendly arrangement Emma might make.\\n\\nThey encountered Mr. Martin the very next day while walking along the Donwell road. He was on foot, and after looking at Emma with great respect, turned with the most genuine pleasure toward her companion. Emma was not displeased to have such an opportunity for observation; and, walking a few yards ahead while they spoke together, she soon made her quick eye sufficiently familiar with Mr. Robert Martin. His appearance was extremely neat, and he seemed a sensible young man, but his person possessed no other advantage; and when compared with gentlemen, Emma thought he must lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s affections. Harriet was not without a feeling for manners; she had voluntarily remarked upon her father\u2019s gentleness with both admiration and surprise. Mr. Martin looked as though he had no idea what manners were.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, since Miss Woodhouse must not be left waiting; and Harriet then ran up to her with a smiling face and an excited flutter of spirits, which Miss Woodhouse hoped soon to calm.\\n\\n\u201cOnly imagine our happening to meet him!\u2014How very strange! He said it was quite by chance that he had not gone round by Randalls. He did not think we ever walked along this road. He thought we went toward Randalls on most days.\"}"
