{"request_id":"6d0407d42dc3278ebd564bf7","rewritten_text":"What kind of life could they have while Fate still held this terrible future in the balance? Their marriage had to be postponed, whatever the cost. He was completely determined about that. Though he loved the girl passionately, and the slightest touch of her fingers when they sat together sent a thrilling joy through every nerve, he understood just as clearly where his duty lay. He knew he had no right to marry until he had committed the murder. Once it was done, he could stand beside Sybil Merton at the altar and place his life in her hands without fear that he was doing wrong. Then he could take her in his arms, knowing she would never have to blush for him or lower her head in shame. But it had to be done first, and the sooner the better for them both.

Many men in his position would have chosen the easy path of pleasure instead of the difficult heights of duty; but Lord Arthur was too conscientious to put pleasure before principle. His love was more than passion, and Sybil represented everything good and noble to him. For a moment he naturally recoiled from what he had been asked to do, but that feeling soon disappeared. His heart told him it was not a sin but a sacrifice; his reason reminded him that there was no other choice. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others. Terrible as the task certainly was, he knew he must not let selfishness defeat love. Sooner or later, we are all forced to make the same decision; the same question is put to every one of us. Lord Arthur faced it early in life, before middle age had spoiled his nature with calculating cynicism or the shallow, fashionable selfishness of his time had worn away his heart, and he felt no doubt about doing his duty. Fortunately, he was not a dreamer or an idle dabbler. If he had been, he would have hesitated like Hamlet and allowed indecision to ruin his purpose. But he was practical by nature. To him, life meant action more than thought. He possessed the rarest quality of all: common sense.

By then, the wild and troubled feelings of the previous night had completely faded. He looked back almost with shame on his frantic wandering from street to street and his fierce emotional suffering. The very sincerity of that suffering now made it seem unreal. He wondered how he could have been foolish enough to rant and rave about what could not be avoided."}

