          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d47a2c349244cdffb0fa5074\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But enough of this. I will begin my tale: it is my final task, and I hope I have enough strength to complete it. I record no crimes; my faults may easily be forgiven, for they arose not from evil motives but from poor judgment. I believe few would claim that, through different conduct and greater wisdom, they could have escaped the misfortunes of which I am the victim. My fate has been ruled by necessity, a terrible necessity. It required hands stronger than mine\u2014stronger, I believe, than any human power\u2014to break the thick, adamantine chain that bound me, once breathing nothing but joy and filled with a warm love and delight in goodness, to misery that can end only, and is now about to end, in death. But I am forgetting myself; my tale remains untold. I will pause for a few moments, wipe my dim eyes, and try to lose the present obscure but heavy feeling of unhappiness in the sharper emotions of the past.\\n\\nI was born in England. My father was a man of rank. He had lost his own father early and was educated by a weak mother, with all the indulgence she believed due to a wealthy nobleman. He was sent to Eton and afterward to college, and from childhood was allowed the free use of large sums of money, thereby enjoying from his earliest youth the independence that a boy with such advantages always acquires at a public school.\\n\\nUnder the influence of these circumstances, his passions found deep soil in which they could take root and flourish, as flowers or weeds according to their nature. Having always been permitted to act for himself, his character became strongly marked at an early age and displayed a varied surface on which a keen observer might see the seeds of virtues and misfortunes. His careless extravagance, which led him to squander immense sums satisfying passing whims\u2014whims whose apparent intensity he dignified with the name of passions\u2014often revealed itself as boundless generosity. Yet while he earnestly concerned himself with the needs of others, his own desires were fulfilled to their fullest extent. He gave his money, but sacrificed none of his wishes to his gifts; he gave his time, which he did not value, and his affections, which he was glad to have awakened in any way.\\n\\nI do not say that, if his own desires had been set against those of others, he would have shown excessive selfishness; but that test was never made. He was raised in prosperity and surrounded by all its advantages; everyone loved him and wished to please him.\"}"
