          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9f7cd5ae451cc32ae5fa9c09\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I have no nerves for that sort of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you really feel comfortable spending the whole evening away from the poor boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; his father can, so why shouldn\u2019t I? Jemima is very careful, and she could send us word every hour about how he was. I really think Charles might as well have told his father that we would all come. I am no more worried about little Charles now than he is. I was terribly worried yesterday, but things are quite different today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you do not think it too late to let them know you are coming, suppose you went as well as your husband. Leave little Charles in my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot think it wrong while I stay with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d cried Mary, her eyes brightening. \u201cDear me! That is a very good idea, very good indeed. Of course, I may as well go as stay, since I am no use at home\u2014am I? It only makes me anxious. You, who do not have a mother\u2019s feelings, are much better suited to this. You can make little Charles do anything; he always obeys you at once. It will be much better than leaving him only with Jemima. Oh, I shall certainly go. I am sure I ought to, if I can, quite as much as Charles, because they are very eager for me to meet Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being left alone. It was an excellent idea of yours, Anne. I will go and tell Charles, and get ready at once. You can send for us immediately if anything is wrong; but I dare say there will be nothing to worry you. You may be sure I would not go if I did not feel completely at ease about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next moment she was knocking at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door. As Anne followed her upstairs, she arrived in time to hear the whole conversation, which began with Mary saying, in a tone of great excitement\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI mean to go with you, Charles, because I am no more useful at home than you are. If I shut myself away forever with the child, I could not persuade him to do anything he disliked. Anne will stay; she has offered to remain at home and take care of him. It was Anne\u2019s own idea, so I shall go with you. It will be much better, since I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband replied, \u201cand I should be very glad to have you come; but it seems rather unfair that she should be left at home alone to care for our sick child.\u201d\"}"
