          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"822a60cf999313a4528fc28d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe appeared deliberately to misunderstand the word she had repeated, and added hastily, \u201cYes, customers; in banking, we generally call those connected with us our customers. He was a French gentleman; a scientific gentleman; a man of considerable learning\u2014a Doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot from Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, yes, from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman came from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, he was well known in Paris. I had the honour of knowing him there. Our connection was in business, but confidential. At that time I was in our French House, and had been there\u2014oh! twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014I may ask, at what time, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI mean, miss, twenty years ago. He married\u2014an English lady\u2014and I was one of the trustees. His affairs, like those of many other French gentlemen and families, were wholly in Tellson\u2019s hands. In much the same manner, I am, or have been, trustee of one sort or another for scores of our customers. These are simply business connections, miss; there is no friendship in them, no special interest, nothing resembling sentiment. Throughout my business life I have moved from one to another, just as I move from one customer to another during my business day; in short, I have no feelings; I am merely a machine. To continue\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is my father\u2019s story, sir; and I am beginning to think\u201d\u2014the strangely furrowed forehead was fixed intently upon him\u2014\u201cthat when I was left an orphan, my mother having survived my father by only two years, it was you who brought me to England. I am nearly certain it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry took the small, hesitating hand that trustfully reached toward his, and with some ceremony raised it to his lips. He then led the young lady directly back to her chair and, keeping his left hand on its back while using his right by turns to rub his chin, tug at his wig near the ears, or emphasize his words, stood looking down at her face as she looked up at his.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you will see how accurately I described myself just now, when I said I had no feelings, and that all my relations with my fellow-creatures were merely business relations, when you consider that I have never seen you since. No; you have been Tellson\u2019s ward ever since, and I have been occupied with Tellson\u2019s other business ever since. Feelings! I have no time for them, no opportunity for them. I spend my entire life, miss, turning an enormous pecuniary Mangle.\u201d\"}"
