{"request_id":"103fda366f59c07184d3e08a","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sad and hopeless. She no longer enjoyed her usual activities; every pleasure seemed, to her, an insult to the dead. She believed that endless sorrow and tears were the only proper tribute she could offer to innocence so cruelly destroyed. She was no longer the happy girl who, in our younger days, had walked with me beside the lake and spoken excitedly of our future. The first of those sorrows meant to loosen our ties to the world had come to her, and its darkening influence had erased her sweetest smiles.

“When I think, my dear cousin,” she said, “of Justine Moritz’s miserable death, I no longer see the world and its works as I once did. Before this, the stories of vice and injustice that I read in books or heard from others seemed like tales of long-ago times or imaginary evils. At least, they were distant, and easier for reason to accept than for the imagination. But now misery has entered my own life, and people seem to me like monsters thirsting for one another’s blood. Yet I know I am being unfair. Everyone believed that poor girl was guilty; and if she had committed the crime for which she suffered, she would surely have been the most wicked of human beings. To murder, for a few jewels, the son of her benefactor and friend—a child she had cared for since birth and seemed to love as her own! I could never agree that any human being should be put to death, but I would certainly have thought such a person unfit to live among others. But she was innocent. I know it; I feel it. You believe the same, and that confirms me. Alas, Victor, when lies can look so much like truth, who can ever feel certain of happiness? I feel as though I am walking along the edge of a precipice, while thousands press around me and try to push me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, yet the killer has escaped. He walks freely through the world, perhaps even respected. But even if I were sentenced to die on the scaffold for those same crimes, I would not trade places with such a wretch.”

I listened to her words in the deepest agony. I was not the murderer in fact, but I was so in effect. Elizabeth saw my anguish in my face and gently took my hand. “My dearest friend,” she said, “you must calm yourself. These events have affected me, God knows how deeply, but I am not as miserable as you are."}

