          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"185d3bfabf9e4f40b016c49d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Watching him in such moods, I often reflected thoughtfully on the ancient philosophy of the Bi-Part Soul, and entertained the notion of a double Dupin\u2014the creative and the analytical.\\n\\nLet no one infer from what I have just said that I am revealing any mystery or composing a romance. What I have described in the Frenchman was simply the product of an excited, or perhaps diseased, intellect. Yet an example of the nature of his observations at such times will best explain my meaning.\\n\\nOne night we were walking along a long, filthy street near the Palais Royal. Apparently absorbed in thought, neither of us had uttered a word for at least fifteen minutes. Suddenly Dupin spoke:\\n\\n\u201cHe is a very small fellow, that is true, and would be better suited to the _Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere can be no doubt of that,\u201d I answered involuntarily, without at first noticing\u2014so deeply was I lost in thought\u2014the remarkable way in which his words corresponded with my own reflections. A moment later I recovered myself, and was profoundly astonished.\\n\\n\u201cDupin,\u201d I said solemnly, \u201cthis is beyond my understanding. I freely admit that I am amazed and can scarcely trust my senses. How could you know that I was thinking of \u2014\u2014?\u201d I stopped, wishing to determine beyond question whether he truly knew whom I had in mind.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014 of Chantilly,\u201d he said. \u201cWhy do you stop? You were observing to yourself that his small stature made him unfit for tragedy.\u201d\\n\\nThis was exactly the subject of my thoughts. Chantilly had once been a cobbler on the Rue St. Denis who, becoming infatuated with the stage, had attempted the _r\u00f4le_ of Xerxes in Cr\u00e9billon\u2019s tragedy of that name, and had been notoriously Pasquinaded for his trouble.\\n\\n\u201cFor Heaven\u2019s sake, tell me,\u201d I cried, \u201cthe method\u2014if there is a method\u2014by which you have managed to read my mind in this matter.\u201d Indeed, I was even more startled than I would have cared to admit.\\n\\n\u201cIt was the fruiterer,\u201d my friend replied, \u201cwho led you to conclude that the mender of soles was not tall enough for Xerxes _et id genus omne_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fruiterer! You amaze me\u2014I know no fruiterer at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe man who collided with you as we entered the street\u2014it may have been fifteen minutes ago.\u201d\\n\\nI now recalled that a fruiterer carrying a large basket of apples had indeed nearly knocked me down accidentally as we passed from the Rue C\u2014\u2014 into the thoroughfare where we stood; but how this could relate to Chantilly, I was utterly unable to understand.\"}"
