          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b43beed2d265bd4d51b0b1d8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Had she known that her sister wished to deprive her of such prospects and such realities as these, what would she have felt? Only her mother could have understood her feelings, having perhaps experienced nearly the same. Lydia\u2019s journey to Brighton was the sole comfort left to her amid the melancholy certainty that her husband never meant to go there himself.\\n\\nYet they knew nothing of what had occurred; and their delight continued, with scarcely any interruption, until the very day Lydia departed from home.\\n\\nElizabeth was now to see Mr. Wickham for the last time. Since her return, she had often been in his company, and her agitation had largely subsided; the agitation caused by her former partiality had vanished altogether. She had even learned to perceive, in the very mildness that had first charmed her, an affectation and monotony calculated to disgust and weary. His present conduct toward her gave her another cause for displeasure; for the desire he soon showed to renew the attentions that had distinguished the beginning of their acquaintance could only irritate her after all that had since passed. She ceased to feel any concern for him when she found herself chosen as the object of such empty and frivolous gallantry; and while firmly discouraging it, she could not help feeling the rebuke implied by his assumption that, however long his attentions had been withdrawn, and for whatever reason, her vanity would be pleased and her preference regained whenever he renewed them.\\n\\nOn the final day of the regiment\u2019s stay in Meryton, he dined at Longbourn with several other officers; and Elizabeth was so little inclined to part from him on friendly terms that, when he made some inquiry about how she had spent her time at Hunsford, she mentioned that Colonel Fitzwilliam and Mr. Darcy had both stayed three weeks at Rosings, and asked whether he knew the former.\\n\\nHe 