          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9ef66c946a2fa809f9dc008c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You must learn some of my philosophy. Think of the past only when remembering it gives you pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cannot credit you with any philosophy of that kind. _Your_ memories must be entirely free of blame, so the contentment they bring comes not from philosophy, but from something much better: ignorance. But with _me_, it is different. Painful memories intrude, and they cannot, and should not, be pushed away. I have been selfish all my life in practice, though not in principle. As a child, I was taught what was _right_, but not how to control my temper. I was given good principles, yet left to follow them with pride and conceit. Unfortunately, as an only son\u2014and for many years an only _child_\u2014I was spoiled by my parents. Though they were good themselves (my father especially, being entirely benevolent and kind), they allowed, encouraged, almost taught me to be selfish and overbearing; to care for no one beyond my own family; to think poorly of everyone else; and at least to _wish_ to think poorly of their judgment and worth compared with my own. That was what I was, from eight to twenty-eight; and I might still be so but for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth! How much I owe you! You taught me a lesson that was difficult at first, but immensely beneficial. You humbled me properly. I came to you without the slightest doubt that you would accept me. You showed me how inadequate all my claims were to please a woman worthy of being pleased.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHad you then convinced yourself that I would?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed I had. What will you think of my vanity? I believed you wished for, and expected, my attentions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy manner must have been at fault, though never intentionally, I assure you. I never meant to deceive you, but my spirits may often have led me astray. How you must have hated me after _that_ evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHated you! I was angry, perhaps, at first, but my anger soon found its proper direction.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. Did you blame me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, indeed. I felt nothing but surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise could not have been greater than _mine_ at being noticed by you. My conscience told me I deserved no special politeness, and I confess I did not expect to receive _more_ than was my due.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy purpose _then_,\u201d Darcy replied, \u201cwas to show you, through every courtesy within my power, that I was not so mean as to resent the past. I hoped to gain your forgiveness and lessen your poor opinion of me by showing you that I had taken your reproofs to heart.\u201d\"}"
