          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fa1dbd8b783015457caf1dd1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After some time, twelve o\u2019clock boomed from the tall tower at Westminster, and with every stroke of the resonant bell the night seemed to tremble. Then the railway lights went out, leaving one solitary lamp shining like a great ruby on a giant mast, and the city\u2019s roar grew fainter.\\n\\nAt two o\u2019clock he rose and wandered towards Blackfriars. How unreal everything appeared! How much it resembled a strange dream! The houses across the river seemed built from darkness. One might have thought that silver and shadow had remade the world. The vast dome of St. Paul\u2019s loomed like a bubble through the dim air.\\n\\nAs he neared Cleopatra\u2019s Needle, he saw a man leaning over the parapet. When he came closer, the man looked up, and the gaslight fell fully across his face.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the cheiromantist! No one could have mistaken that fat, flabby face, those gold-rimmed spectacles, that sickly, feeble smile, and that sensual mouth.\\n\\nLord Arthur stopped. A brilliant idea flashed through his mind, and he crept quietly up behind him. In an instant he had seized Mr. Podgers by the legs and thrown him into the Thames. There was a coarse oath, a heavy splash, and then silence. Lord Arthur peered anxiously over the parapet, but could see nothing of the cheiromantist except a tall hat spinning in an eddy of moonlit water. After a while it too sank, and no trace of Mr. Podgers remained. Once he thought he saw the bulky, misshapen figure swimming towards the staircase by the bridge, and a terrible sense of failure seized him, but it proved to be only a reflection. When the moon emerged from behind a cloud, the feeling vanished. At last he seemed to have fulfilled the decree of destiny. He drew a deep sigh of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name rose to his lips.\\n\\n\u2018Have you dropped anything, sir?\u2019 a voice suddenly said behind him.\\n\\nHe turned and saw a policeman carrying a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u2018Nothing of importance, sergeant,\u2019 he replied with a smile. Hailing a passing hansom, he climbed in and told the driver to take him to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nFor the next few days he swung between hope and fear. At times he almost expected Mr. Podgers to enter the room, yet at others he felt that Fate could not be so unjust to him. Twice he went to the cheiromantist\u2019s address in West Moon Street, but could not bring himself to ring the bell. He yearned for certainty and dreaded it.\\n\\nAt last it came. He was sitting in the club\u2019s smoking-room, drinking tea and listening rather wearily to Surbiton\u2019s account of the latest comic song at the Gaiety, when the waiter entered with the evening papers.\"}"
