          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fe2ab09ea0857dff93d40234\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I walked quickly, and we soon reached my college. Then I remembered\u2014with a shudder\u2014that the creature I had left in my apartment might still be there, alive and moving about. I dreaded seeing the monster, but I feared even more that Henry might see him. I therefore begged him to wait a few minutes at the foot of the stairs while I rushed up to my room. My hand was already on the lock before I came to my senses. I stopped, seized by a cold shiver. Then I flung the door open, as children do when they expect a spectre to be waiting on the other side; but nothing appeared. I stepped inside cautiously: the apartment was empty, and my bedroom had also been freed from its hideous guest. I could scarcely believe such good fortune had come to me, but once I was certain that my enemy had truly fled, I clapped my hands with joy and ran back down to Clerval.\\n\\nWe went up to my room, and the servant soon brought breakfast; but I could not control myself. I was not merely joyful; my flesh tingled with overwhelming sensitivity, and my pulse raced. I could not remain still for even a moment. I leaped over the chairs, clapped my hands, and laughed aloud. At first Clerval thought my unusual excitement came from happiness at his arrival. But when he watched me more closely, he saw a wildness in my eyes that he could not explain, and my loud, uncontrolled, joyless laughter frightened and astonished him.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\u201d he cried, \u201cwhat, for God\u2019s sake, is wrong? Do not laugh like that. How ill you are! What has caused all this?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not ask me,\u201d I cried, covering my eyes with my hands, for I thought I saw the dreaded spectre glide into the room. \u201c_He_ can tell you. Oh, save me! Save me!\u201d I imagined that the monster had seized me; I struggled violently and collapsed in a fit.\\n\\nPoor Clerval! What must he have felt? A meeting he had looked forward to with such joy had turned so strangely into bitterness. But I did not witness his grief, for I lay senseless and did not regain consciousness for a very long time.\\n\\nThis marked the beginning of a nervous fever that confined me for several months. Throughout that time, Henry was my only nurse. I later learned that, knowing my father was too old and unfit for such a long journey, and knowing how miserable my illness would make Elizabeth, he spared them this sorrow by concealing the severity of my condition.\"}"
