{"request_id":"6d0951199f6884595876e037","rewritten_text":"Each compares it—not with the voice of a person from any nation whose language he knows—but with the reverse. The Frenchman supposes it to be the voice of a Spaniard, and ‘might have distinguished some words _had he been acquainted with the Spanish._’ The Dutchman declares it was that of a Frenchman; yet we are told that ‘_not understanding French this witness was examined through an interpreter._’ The Englishman believes it to be the voice of a German, and ‘_does not understand German._’ The Spaniard ‘is sure’ it belonged to an Englishman, but ‘judges by the intonation’ alone, ‘_as he has no knowledge of the English._’ The Italian thinks it the voice of a Russian, though ‘_has never conversed with a native of Russia._’ A second Frenchman, too, disagrees with the first, and insists that the voice was Italian; but, _not being cognizant of that tongue_, is, like the Spaniard, ‘convinced by the intonation.’ Now, how extraordinarily strange must that voice actually have been, concerning which testimony of this kind _could_ be obtained!—in whose _tones_, even, inhabitants of the five great divisions of Europe could recognise nothing familiar! You may say that it could have been the voice of an Asiatic—or of an African. Asiatics and Africans are not numerous in Paris; but, without disputing the inference, I will now direct your notice only to three points. One witness calls the voice ‘harsh rather than shrill.’ Two others describe it as ‘quick and _unequal._’ No witness mentioned any words—or any sounds resembling words—as being distinguishable.

“I know not,” continued Dupin, “what impression I may thus far have produced upon your understanding; but I do not hesitate to assert that legitimate deductions even from this part of the testimony—the part concerning the gruff and shrill voices—are by themselves enough to awaken a suspicion that ought to guide all further progress in investigating the mystery. I said ‘legitimate deductions;’ but that does not completely convey my meaning. I intended to suggest that these deductions are the _sole_ proper ones, and that the suspicion follows _inevitably_ from them as their single result. What that suspicion is, however, I shall not yet reveal. I only wish you to remember that, to me, it was powerful enough to give a definite shape—a particular direction—to my inquiries in the chamber.

“Let us now carry ourselves, in imagination, into this chamber. What shall we first look for here? The means of escape used by the murderers. It is not too much to say that neither of us believes in præternatural events."}

