          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1bd6fc8a172d582d42f164d2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When he reached the top of the staircase, he pulled himself together and decided to give his famous peal of demonic laughter. He had found this extremely useful on more than one occasion. It was said to have turned Lord Raker\u2019s wig gray in a single night, and it had certainly made three of Lady Canterville\u2019s French governesses give notice before their month was over. So he laughed his most terrible laugh until the old vaulted ceiling rang and rang again. But scarcely had the frightening echo faded when a door opened, and Mrs. Otis appeared in a light-blue dressing gown. \u201cI\u2019m afraid you\u2019re far from well,\u201d she said, \u201cand I\u2019ve brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell\u2019s tincture. If it\u2019s indigestion, you\u2019ll find it an excellent remedy.\u201d The ghost glared at her furiously and immediately began preparing to transform himself into a large black dog, an ability for which he was rightly famous, and which the family doctor always blamed for the permanent idiocy of Lord Canterville\u2019s uncle, the Honorable Thomas Horton. The sound of approaching footsteps, however, made him pause in his dreadful plan, so he contented himself with becoming faintly phosphorescent and vanished with a deep churchyard groan, just as the twins reached him.\\n\\nWhen he reached his room, he completely broke down and became prey to the most violent agitation. The twins\u2019 vulgarity and Mrs. Otis\u2019s crude materialism were naturally extremely irritating, but what distressed him most was that he had been unable to wear the suit of armor. He had hoped that even modern Americans would be thrilled by the sight of a Specter in Armor, if for no more sensible reason than respect for their national poet Longfellow, whose graceful and appealing poetry he himself had used to pass many a weary hour when the Cantervilles were in town. Besides, it was his own suit. He had worn it with great success at the Kenilworth tournament and had received high praise for it from no less a person than the Virgin Queen herself. Yet when he put it on, he was completely overwhelmed by the weight of the enormous breastplate and steel helmet, and fell heavily onto the stone floor, badly scraping both knees and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.\\n\\nFor several days afterward he was extremely ill and scarcely left his room, except to keep the bloodstain properly maintained. However, by taking great care of himself, he recovered and resolved to make a third attempt to frighten the United States Minister and his family.\"}"
