{"request_id":"81af62c589e8a55741fb1d0c","rewritten_text":"Yet even then I had not drained the bitter draught to its dregs; I was not fully convinced of my loss; I did not yet feel in every beat, every nerve, every thought, that I alone remained of my kind—that I was the LAST MAN.

The sky grew overcast, and a fine rain began at sunset. Even the everlasting heavens weep, I thought; is it shameful, then, for mortal man to spend himself in tears? I recalled the old fables, where human beings are said to melt away through weeping into ever-flowing fountains. Ah, that it might be so; then my fate would in some measure resemble the watery death of Adrian and Clara. Oh, sorrow is fanciful; it spins a web upon which it traces the story of its misery through every shape and alteration around it; it mingles with all living nature; it draws nourishment from every object; like light, it fills everything, and, like light, lends its own colours to all.

In my search I had strayed some distance from the place where I had been cast ashore, and reached one of those watch-towers that stand at regular intervals along the Italian coast. I was pleased to find shelter, pleased to encounter a work of human hands after gazing so long upon nature’s desolate barrenness; therefore I entered and climbed the rough, winding stair into the guard-room. Fortune had been kind enough that no distressing trace of its former occupants remained; a few boards laid over two iron trestles and scattered with dried Indian-corn leaves formed the bed offered me. An open chest, holding some half-rotten biscuit, stirred an appetite that perhaps had been present before, though I had not noticed it until then. Thirst too—fierce and burning, caused by the seawater I had swallowed and by my bodily exhaustion—tormented me. Benevolent nature had made the satisfaction of these needs pleasurable, so that I—even I—was revived and soothed as I ate this wretched food and drank a little sour wine from a flask half-filled and left in the deserted dwelling. Then I lay down upon the bed, not unworthy of a shipwreck’s victim. The earthy scent of the dried leaves comforted my senses after the loathsome smell of seaweed. I forgot my lonely condition. I looked neither behind nor ahead; my senses sank into rest; I fell asleep and dreamed of all the beloved scenes inland—of haymakers, and the shepherd’s whistle to his dog when he called for help in driving the flock to its fold; of sights and sounds belonging to my boyhood’s mountain life, long since forgotten."}

