{"request_id":"590c917407c826d2d5fff984","rewritten_text":"I was born in a small place surrounded by the sea, a land often covered by clouds. When I think of the earth’s surface, with its endless oceans and unexplored continents, my birthplace seems only a tiny speck in the vast whole. Yet, measured by the power of the mind, it surpassed countries that were larger and more heavily populated. It is true that the human mind alone created everything that was good or great for humanity, while Nature herself was only its first servant. England, lying far to the north in the muddy sea, now appears in my dreams as a huge, well-crewed ship that mastered the winds and sailed proudly over the waves. In my childhood, she was the whole universe to me. When I stood on my native hills and saw plains and mountains extending to the farthest edge of my sight, dotted with my countrymen’s homes and made fertile by their work, I felt that the very centre of the earth was fixed in that place. The rest of the world seemed like a fable, and forgetting it would have required no effort from either my imagination or my understanding.

From the beginning, my life showed how powerfully change can shape a person’s fortunes. In my case, this quality was almost inherited. My father was one of those men to whom nature had given, in great abundance, the prized gifts of wit and imagination, but had then left his life’s ship to be driven by those winds, without giving him reason as a rudder or judgment as its pilot. His family background was obscure, but circumstances brought him into public notice at an early age, and he soon spent his small inheritance in the brilliant world of fashion and luxury in which he took part. During his brief, careless youth, the fashionable people of the time adored him, especially the young king, who escaped from political intrigues and the difficult duties of ruling to find constant amusement and renewed energy in my father’s company. My father’s impulses were never under his own control and repeatedly led him into troubles that only his cleverness could resolve. The growing burden of debts, both personal and commercial, would have crushed anyone else, but he carried it with a light heart and unbroken cheerfulness. Since his company was so important at the tables and gatherings of the wealthy, his failures were treated as minor faults, and he himself was welcomed with flattering praise.

But this kind of popularity, like all others, soon fades. Meanwhile, the many difficulties he faced grew at a frightening rate compared with his limited ability to escape them."}

