ould quickly cure Harriet; but the feelings capable of producing such conduct lowered them both considerably. It could hardly be doubted that poor Harriet\u2019s attachment had been offered up to conjugal openness, and that her own part in the affair, presented in the least favourable light for her and the most comforting for him, had probably been related too. She was naturally the object of their united dislike. Whenever they had nothing else to discuss, it would always be easy to begin abusing Miss Woodhouse; and the hostility they dared not display through open disrespect towards her found a wider outlet in their contemptuous treatment of Harriet.\\n\\nMrs. Elton took a strong liking to Jane Fairfax, and did so from the beginning. It was not merely that, during a state of conflict with one young woman, the other might be expected to seem more attractive; from their very first meeting she was interested in Jane. Nor was she content with expressing a natural and reasonable admiration: without being asked, without excuse, and without any particular right, she insisted on offering her assistance and friendship. Before Emma had lost her confidence, and around their third meeting, she heard all of Mrs. Elton\u2019s chivalrous plans on the subject.\"}"
