          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dec79a8cb7821c25c3fa8f4c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He crept silently from the wainscoting, wearing a wicked smile upon his cruel, wrinkled lips, while the moon concealed her face behind a cloud as he passed the great oriel window, where his arms and those of his murdered wife were displayed in azure and gold. He glided onward like some evil shadow, and even the darkness appeared to shrink from him. Once he fancied he heard a voice, and halted; but it was merely a dog baying at the Red Farm. So he continued, muttering odd sixteenth-century curses and from time to time flourishing the rusty dagger in the midnight air. At last he came to the passage corner leading to luckless Washington\u2019s chamber. There he paused briefly, the wind tossing his long grey locks around his head and twisting the nameless horror of the dead man\u2019s shroud into grotesque, fantastic folds. Then the clock struck the quarter, and he knew the moment had arrived. Chuckling softly, he turned the corner; but scarcely had he done so when, with a pitiful cry of terror, he recoiled and covered his blanched face with his long, bony hands. Directly before him stood a dreadful spectre, still as a carved figure and monstrous as a madman\u2019s nightmare! Its head was bald and shining; its face round, fat, and white; and some ghastly laughter seemed to have twisted its features into a permanent grin. Scarlet rays poured from its eyes, its mouth yawned like a broad well of flame, and a hideous garment, resembling his own, wrapped its silent snows around the Titan-like form. A placard hung upon its breast, covered with strange antique lettering\u2014apparently a scroll of disgrace, a record of savage sins, an appalling calendar of crime\u2014and in its right hand it held high a gleaming steel falchion.\\n\\nHaving never encountered a ghost before, he was naturally terrified. After one hurried second look at the dreadful phantom, he fled toward his room, stumbling over