          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f24739623924a28bdf3acd0d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how strange and mysterious they were! The soul contained something animal, while the body had its flashes of spirituality. The senses might become refined, and the intellect might sink into degradation. Who could tell where the bodily impulse ended and the psychic impulse began? How superficial were the arbitrary distinctions of common psychologists! Yet how hard it was to choose between the claims of the different schools! Was the soul a shadow dwelling in the house of sin? Or was the body truly contained within the soul, as Giordano Bruno had believed? The division of spirit from matter was a mystery, and so too was their union.\\n\\nHe began to ask himself whether psychology could ever become so exact a science that every small spring of life would be exposed to us. As matters stood, we continually misunderstood ourselves and seldom understood anyone else. Experience possessed no ethical value. It was simply the name people gave their errors. Moralists had generally treated it as a form of warning, claimed that it had some ethical power in shaping character, and praised it as something that taught us what to pursue and what to shun. But experience contained no driving force. It was no more an active cause than conscience itself. All it truly proved was that the future would resemble the past, and that the sin we had once committed with disgust, we would repeat many times, and with delight.\\n\\nIt seemed plain to him that experiment was the sole method by which one could reach a scientific analysis of the passions; and Dorian Gray was plainly a subject ready to hand, promising abundant and valuable results. His sudden, passionate love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological phenomenon of considerable interest. Curiosity had certainly played a large part in it, along with the longing for fresh experiences, but the passion was not simple; it was exceedingly complex. Whatever purely sensual instinct belonged to boyhood had been altered by the imagination, transformed into something that seemed to the young man himself far removed from the senses, and therefore all the more perilous. It was the passions whose beginnings we misunderstood that ruled us most tyrannically. Our feeblest motives were those whose character we recognized. Often, when we believed we were experimenting upon others, we were in fact experimenting upon ourselves.\\n\\nAs Lord Henry sat pondering these matters, there came a knock at the door. His valet entered and reminded him that it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
