          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"02037c0f6990083718c3c959\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOnly a tick.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere did you find him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOut in the woods.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat will you take for him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. I don\u2019t want to sell him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right. He\u2019s a mighty little tick, anyhow.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, anybody can chase down a tick that doesn\u2019t belong to them. I\u2019m satisfied with this one. He\u2019s good enough for me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSho, there are ticks aplenty. I could have a thousand if I wanted them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, why don\u2019t you? Because you know mighty well you can\u2019t. This is a pretty early tick, I reckon. It\u2019s the first I\u2019ve seen this year.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSay, Huck\u2014I\u2019ll give you my tooth for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nTom took out a scrap of paper and unrolled it with care. Huckleberry looked at it longingly. The temptation was powerful. At last he said:\\n\\n\u201cIs it genuine?\u201d\\n\\nTom raised his lip and displayed the gap.\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right,\u201d said Huckleberry, \u201cit\u2019s a trade.\u201d\\n\\nTom shut the tick inside the percussion-cap box that had recently served as the pinchbug\u2019s prison, and the boys went their separate ways, each feeling richer than before.\\n\\nWhen Tom arrived at the small, isolated frame schoolhouse, he marched in briskly, carrying himself like one who had come with all possible honesty and speed. He hung his hat on a peg and dropped into his seat with businesslike haste. The master, enthroned above them in his large splint-bottom armchair, had been dozing to the sleepy murmur of study. The interruption awakened him.\\n\\n\u201cThomas Sawyer!\u201d\\n\\nTom knew that hearing his full name meant trouble.\\n\\n\u201cSir!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome up here. Now, sir, why are you late again, as usual?\u201d\\n\\nTom was about to escape into a lie when he noticed two long tails of yellow hair falling down a back he recognized through love\u2019s electric sympathy; beside that figure stood _the only vacant place_ on the girls\u2019 side of the schoolhouse. He answered at once:\\n\\n\u201c_I stopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn!_\u201d\\n\\nThe master\u2019s pulse seemed to stop, and he stared helplessly. The hum of study died away. The pupils wondered whether this reckless boy had lost his senses. The master said:\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2014you did what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cStopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn.\u201d\\n\\nThere could be no misunderstanding the words.\\n\\n\u201cThomas Sawyer, this is the most astonishing confession I have ever heard. A mere ferule will not suffice for such an offence. Take off your jacket.\u201d\\n\\nThe master\u2019s arm continued until it was weary and the supply of switches had been greatly reduced. Then came the command:\\n\\n\u201cNow, sir, go sit with the girls! And let this be a warning to you.\u201d\\n\\nThe titter that passed around the room seemed to embarrass the boy, but in truth that effect came more from his reverent awe of his unknown idol and the fearful delight contained in his extraordinary good fortune.\"}"
