          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"01d42bb117315b96a448c572\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I wrap him in the paper and stuff part of it into his mouth\u2014that was the way.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you believe, then, that your master was actually bitten by the beetle, and that the bite caused his illness?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t believe nothing about it\u2014I know it. Why does he dream so much about gold, if it isn\u2019t because he was bitten by the gold-bug? I heard of those gold-bugs before this.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut how do you know that he dreams of gold?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do I know? Why, because he talks about it in his sleep\u2014that\u2019s how I know.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jup, perhaps you are right; but to what fortunate circumstance am I to credit the honor of a visit from you today?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter, massa?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid you bring any message from Mr. Legrand?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, massa, I bring this here letter;\u201d and Jupiter handed me a note, which read as follows:\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR \u2014\u2014Why have I not seen you for so long? I hope you have not been foolish enough to take offence at any slight _brusquerie_ of mine; but no, that seems unlikely. Since I saw you, I have had much cause for anxiety. I have something to tell you, though I scarcely know how to tell it, or whether I ought to tell it at all.\\n\\n\u201cI have not been entirely well for several days, and poor old Jup troubles me almost beyond endurance with his well-intentioned attentions. Would you believe it?\u2014the other day he had made ready a great stick with which to punish me for eluding him and spending the day, _solus_, among the hills on the mainland. I truly believe that my sickly appearance alone spared me a beating.\\n\\n\u201cI have added nothing to my collection since we met.\\n\\n\u201cIf you can possibly arrange it, come over with Jupiter. _Do_ come. I want to see you tonight, concerning business of importance. I assure you, it is of the _highest_ importance.\\n\\n\u201cEver yours, \u201cWILLIAM LEGRAND\u201d.\\n\\nSomething in the tone of this note filled me with uneasiness. Its entire manner differed greatly from Legrand\u2019s. What could he be dreaming about? What fresh whim had taken possession of his excitable mind? What \u201cbusiness of the highest importance\u201d could he have to conduct? Jupiter\u2019s report of him suggested nothing good. I feared that the unbroken weight of misfortune had finally disturbed my friend\u2019s reason. Without the slightest hesitation, therefore, I made ready to go with the negro.\\n\\nWhen we reached the wharf, I saw a scythe and three spades, all seemingly new, resting in the bottom of the boat in which we were to cross.\\n\\n\u201cWhat does all this mean, Jup?\u201d\"}"
