          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a571bc6848c0b657c237d821\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After joining in general sorrow over the dreadful outcome of the event, which Elizabeth considered almost certain, and Miss Bennet could not say was entirely impossible, the former continued, \u201cBut tell me everything about it that I have not already heard. Give me more details. What did Colonel Forster say? Had they suspected anything before the elopement? They must have seen the two of them together constantly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted that he had often suspected some affection, especially on Lydia\u2019s side, but nothing that caused him any alarm. I feel so sorry for him. His conduct was as attentive and kind as possible. He was coming to us to express his concern before he had any idea that they had not gone to Scotland. When that fear first spread, it hastened his journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny convinced that Wickham would not marry? Did he know they planned to run away? Had Colonel Forster spoken to Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when Colonel Forster questioned him, Denny denied knowing anything about their plan and would not state his true opinion. He did not repeat his belief that they would not marry, and because of that I hope he may have been misunderstood earlier.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster came in person, none of you doubted, I suppose, that they were truly married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such an idea have entered our minds? I felt somewhat uneasy\u2014slightly afraid for my sister\u2019s happiness with him as her husband, because I knew his behavior had not always been proper. My father and mother knew nothing of that; they only understood how imprudent the match must be. Kitty then admitted, with very natural pride at knowing more than the rest of us, that Lydia\u2019s last letter had prepared her for such a step. It seems she had known for many weeks that they were in love.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I believe not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think badly of Wickham himself? Does he know his true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must admit that he did not speak of Wickham as highly as he once had. He believed him imprudent and wasteful; and since this sad affair occurred, people say that he left Meryton deeply in debt. But I hope that is untrue.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secretive, if we had told people what we knew about him, this could not have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it would have been better,\u201d her sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cBut exposing someone\u2019s past faults without knowing what his present feelings were seemed unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
