{"request_id":"1a649e3e6642faf77ddbdf47","rewritten_text":"The young people were all eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of going there himself again; it was only seventeen miles from Uppercross, and though it was November, the weather was by no means unpleasant. In short, Louisa, the most eager of them all, having resolved to go, and now fortified by the notion of merit in insisting on her own way as well as the pleasure of doing so, overcame all her father and mother’s wishes to postpone the visit until summer; and to Lyme they were to go—Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth.

Their first careless plan had been to go in the morning and return at night; but Mr Musgrove would not agree to this, on account of his horses. And when the matter was sensibly considered, a day in mid-November would leave little time for seeing a new place, after allowing seven hours, as the nature of the country required, for the journey there and back. They were therefore to spend the night, and not be expected home until dinner the following day. This was considered a great improvement; and although they all assembled at the Great House at a fairly early breakfast hour and departed with great punctuality, it was well past noon before the two carriages—Mr Musgrove’s coach with the four ladies, and Charles’s curricle, in which he drove Captain Wentworth—were descending the long hill into Lyme and entering the still steeper street of the town. It was plain they would have little more than time to look around before the day’s light and warmth had disappeared.

After securing rooms and ordering dinner at one of the inns, the next thing to do was certainly to walk straight down to the sea. They had arrived too late in the year for any amusement or variety that Lyme, as a public resort, might provide. The rooms were closed, nearly all the lodgers had departed, and scarcely any families remained except the residents. Since the buildings themselves offer nothing remarkable, it is the town’s striking situation that attracts attention: the principal street almost rushing into the water; the walk to the Cobb, winding around the pleasant little bay, which in season is lively with bathing machines and visitors; the Cobb itself, with its old features and new improvements; and the very beautiful line of cliffs extending eastward from the town. These are what a stranger’s eye will seek, and he must be a very unusual stranger who sees no charm in Lyme’s immediate surroundings to make him wish to know the place better."}

