{"request_id":"9412f29369d66fe5a5ecc9fd","rewritten_text":"“I proceeded to reason thus—_a posteriori_. The murderers had escaped through one of these windows. If so, they could not have refastened the sashes from within, as they had been found fastened;—a consideration whose obviousness had brought the police investigation in this direction to an end. Yet the sashes _were_ fastened. They _must_, therefore, possess the means of fastening themselves. There was no avoiding this conclusion. I went to the unobstructed casement, drew out the nail with some difficulty, and tried to lift the sash. It resisted every effort, as I had expected. A concealed spring must, I now knew, be present; and this confirmation of my theory convinced me that my premises, at least, were sound, however mysterious the circumstances connected with the nails still remained. A careful examination soon revealed the hidden spring. I pressed it and, satisfied by the discovery, refrained from raising the sash.

“I replaced the nail and examined it closely. Someone passing through this window might have shut it again, allowing the spring to catch—but could not have put the nail back. The inference was obvious, and once more reduced the range of my inquiry. The assassins _must_ have escaped through the other window. Assuming, then, that the springs in both sashes were alike, as seemed likely, there _must_ be some difference in the nails, or at least in the way they were fixed. Climbing onto the bedstead’s sacking, I inspected the second casement minutely over the headboard. Reaching my hand behind the board, I soon found and pressed the spring, which proved, as I had supposed, identical with its neighbor. I then examined the nail. It was equally stout and appeared to be set in the same fashion—driven almost to the head.

“You may say that I was perplexed; but, if so, you have misunderstood the character of my deductions. To borrow a sporting expression, I had not once been ‘at fault.’ The scent had not been lost for a moment. No link in the chain was defective. I had followed the secret to its final result,—and that result was _the nail._ It had, I repeat, every appearance of resembling its counterpart in the other window; but that circumstance was utterly worthless (however conclusive it might appear) beside the fact that here, at this point, the clue ended. ‘There _must_ be something amiss,’ I said, ‘with the nail.’ I touched it; and the head, together with about a quarter-inch of the shank, came away in my fingers.”"}

