          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9ed3048b22bbfb874306a907\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We proclaimed ourselves freemen so loudly and earnestly that we drew the interested notice of that liberty-preaching orator and his patriotic company. They clustered around us and took on a very resolute air. The orator said:\\n\\n\u201cIf, indeed, ye are freemen, ye have nought to fear\u2014the God-given liberties of Britain surround ye as shield and shelter! (Applause.) Ye shall soon see. Bring forth your proofs.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat proofs?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cProof that ye are freemen.\u201d\\n\\nAh\u2014I remembered! I came to myself and held my tongue. But the king burst out:\\n\\n\u201cThou\u2019rt mad, man. It were more reasonable, and better, that this thief and scoundrel here prove we are _not_ freemen.\u201d\\n\\nYou see, he understood his own laws as people so often understand laws: by their words, not by their consequences. They acquire a _meaning_, and become remarkably vivid, when you have to apply them to yourself.\\n\\nEveryone shook his head and looked disappointed; some turned away, their interest gone. The orator said\u2014and now his voice had the practical tone of business rather than sentiment:\\n\\n\u201cAn ye know not your country\u2019s laws, it were time ye learned them. Ye are strangers here; that ye will not deny. Ye may be freemen, and we do not deny it; but ye may also be slaves. The law is plain: it doth not demand that the claimant prove ye are slaves; it requireth you to prove ye are not.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cGood sir, grant us only time to send to Astolat; or grant us only time to send to the Valley of Holiness\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPeace, good man, these are extraordinary requests, and you cannot expect them to be allowed. It would consume much time and cause your master needless inconvenience\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201c_Master_, fool!\u201d the king thundered. \u201cI have no master; I myself am the m\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSilence, for God\u2019s sake!\u201d\\n\\nI managed to get the words out soon enough to stop the king. We were already in trouble enough; it could do us no good to make these people think we were lunatics.\\n\\nThere is no point in dragging out the particulars. The earl housed us and put us up for sale at auction. That same damnable law had existed in our own South in my own day, more than thirteen hundred years afterward, and under it hundreds of freemen unable to prove they were freemen had been sold into lifelong slavery without the fact making any special impression on me; but once the law and auction block entered my own experience, what had previously seemed merely wrong became instantly hellish. Well, that is how we are made.\\n\\nYes, we were sold at auction, like swine. In a large town with a brisk market we ought to have fetched a good price; but this place was completely stagnant, and we were sold for a sum that shames me whenever I remember it.\"}"
