{"request_id":"133a515ed46804a405dccdf6","rewritten_text":"But this thought, which supported me at the beginning of my career, now serves only to drive me deeper into the dust. All my hopes and plans have come to nothing, and, like the archangel who reached for unlimited power, I am chained in an everlasting hell. My imagination was vivid, while my powers of analysis and practical work were strong; together, these qualities led me to imagine and create a man. Even now, I cannot remember without strong emotion the dreams I had while the work was unfinished. In my thoughts I walked through heaven, sometimes rejoicing in my abilities, sometimes burning with thoughts of what they might achieve. From childhood I had been filled with great hopes and high ambition; but how far I have fallen! Oh, my friend, if you had known me as I once was, you would not recognise me in this degraded state. Despair rarely touched my heart; I seemed carried toward a great destiny, until I fell—never, never to rise again.”

Must I then lose this remarkable being? I have longed for a friend, someone who would understand and love me. And now, on these empty seas, I have found such a person, but I fear I have gained him only to learn his worth and then lose him. I would persuade him to return to life, but he rejects the thought.

“I thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your kind concern for such a miserable wretch; but when you speak of new bonds and new affections, do you think they can replace those who are gone? Can any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman what Elizabeth was? Even when affection is not deeply stirred by exceptional goodness, the friends of our childhood always hold a certain power over us that few later friends can gain. They know the dispositions we had as children, which, however much they may later change, are never completely erased; and they can judge our actions more surely, knowing whether our motives are honest. A sister or brother can never, unless there were signs of it from childhood, suspect the other of deceit or dishonesty, while another friend, however devoted, may still be viewed with suspicion despite himself. But my friends were dear to me not only because of habit and shared memories, but because of their own virtues; and wherever I may be, the gentle voice of Elizabeth and Clerval’s conversation will always echo in my ears. They are dead, and only one feeling in such loneliness could persuade me to continue living. If I were involved in some great work or purpose that would greatly benefit my fellow human beings, then I could live to complete it."}

