          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e890f3e9efc5ef47a8f80ff1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"One day, while I was busy with my books and Mr. Pocket, I received a note in the mail. The sight of its outside threw me into great excitement; although I had never seen the handwriting before, I guessed whose it was. It had no formal opening\u2014not Dear Mr. Pip, Dear Pip, Dear Sir, or anything else\u2014but began like this:\\n\\n\u201cI am to come to London the day after tomorrow by the midday coach. I believe it was arranged that you should meet me? In any case, Miss Havisham thinks so, and I am writing as she instructed. She sends you her regards.\\n\\n\u201cYours, ESTELLA.\u201d\\n\\nIf there had been time, I would probably have ordered several new suits for the occasion. But there was not, so I had to be satisfied with the clothes I already owned. My appetite disappeared at once, and I had no peace or rest until the day came. Even then I found neither. I was worse than ever, and began haunting the coach office in Wood Street, Cheapside, before the coach had even left the Blue Boar in our town. Though I knew perfectly well that it would not arrive for hours, I still felt it unsafe to leave the office out of sight for more than five minutes. In this unreasonable state, I had completed the first half-hour of a four- or five-hour watch when Wemmick ran into me.\\n\\n\u201cHello, Mr. Pip,\u201d he said. \u201cHow are you? I would hardly have expected to find you in this part of town.\u201d\\n\\nI explained that I was waiting to meet someone arriving by coach, and asked about the Castle and the Aged.\\n\\n\u201cBoth doing very well, thank you,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cespecially the Aged. He is in wonderful spirits. He will be eighty-two on his next birthday. I am thinking of firing the cannon eighty-two times, if the neighbors do not object and the cannon can withstand the strain. But that is not London talk. Where do you think I am going?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo the office?\u201d I asked, since he was heading that way.\\n\\n\u201cAlmost,\u201d Wemmick replied. \u201cI am going to Newgate. We are handling a banker's-parcel case at present, and I have been down the road to look at the scene of the crime. Now I must have a word with our client.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDid your client commit the robbery?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cGood heavens, no,\u201d Wemmick answered very dryly. \u201cBut he is accused of it. You or I might be accused of it too. Either of us could be accused, you know.\u201d\"}"
