          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"68cd737c2965994c856ab92b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was short in stature but remarkably upright, and his voice was the sweetest I had ever heard. He began his lecture with a review of chemistry\u2019s history and the various advances made by learned men, speaking with fervor the names of its most distinguished discoverers. He then gave a brief account of the science\u2019s present state and explained many of its elementary terms. After conducting a few preliminary experiments, he concluded with a tribute to modern chemistry, whose words I shall never forget:\\n\\n\u201cThe ancient teachers of this science,\u201d he said, \u201cpromised impossibilities and accomplished nothing. The modern masters promise very little; they know that metals cannot be transmuted and that the elixir of life is a fantasy. Yet these philosophers, whose hands seem made only for dabbling in dirt and whose eyes are fixed upon the microscope or crucible, have truly performed miracles. They penetrate nature\u2019s hidden recesses and reveal how she works in secret. They rise into the heavens; they have discovered how blood circulates and the nature of the air we breathe. They have gained new and almost unlimited powers; they can command the thunder of heaven, imitate the earthquake, and even mock the invisible world with its own shadows.\u201d\\n\\nThose were the professor\u2019s words\u2014or rather, let me say, the words of fate, spoken to destroy me. As he continued, I felt as though my soul were wrestling with a tangible enemy. One by one, the keys that formed the mechanism of my being were struck; chord after chord resounded, and soon my mind was filled with a single thought, a single vision, a single purpose. So much has already been accomplished, cried the soul of Frankenstein\u2014far more will I achieve. Following the path already marked, I will open a new one, explore unknown powers, and reveal to the world the deepest mysteries of creation.\\n\\nI 