          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fdd2ec7377a212e27a0c775a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe seemed deliberately to misunderstand the word she had repeated when he added hurriedly, \u201cYes, customers; in banking, we usually call our clients our customers. He was a French gentleman; a man of science; a man of great learning\u2014a doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFrom Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, yes, from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman was from Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, he was well known in Paris. I had the honour of knowing him there. Our relationship was a business one, but confidential. At that time I was in our French branch, and had been there\u2014oh! twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014I may ask, what time was that, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI mean, miss, twenty years ago. He married an English lady, and I was one of the trustees. His affairs, like those of many other French gentlemen and families, were entirely in Tellson\u2019s hands. In the same way, I am, or have been, trustee of one kind or another for dozens of our customers. These are merely business relationships, miss; there is no friendship in them, no special interest, nothing resembling sentiment. Throughout my business life, I have passed from one to another just as I pass from one customer to another during my business day; in short, I have no feelings; I am merely a machine. To continue\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is my father\u2019s story, sir; and I am beginning to think\u201d\u2014the strangely furrowed forehead was fixed intently upon him\u2014\u201cthat when I was left an orphan because my mother survived my father by only two years, it was you who brought me to England. I am almost certain it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry took the hesitant little hand that trustingly reached toward his and, with some ceremony, raised it to his lips. He then led the young lady directly back to her chair and, holding its back with his left hand, using his right by turns to rub his chin, tug at his wig near the ears, or emphasize his words, stood looking down into her face while she looked up at his.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you will see how truthfully I spoke of myself just now, when I said I had no feelings, and that all my relationships with my fellow human beings were merely business relationships, when you consider that I have never seen you since. No; you have been a ward of Tellson\u2019s House ever since, and I have been occupied with Tellson\u2019s other business ever since. Feelings! I have no time for them, no opportunity for them. I spend my entire life, miss, turning an enormous financial Mangle.\u201d\"}"
