          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f6c713513c89dbaacd430cd6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She left everything behind her, except the memory that such things had once existed.\\n\\nAnne had not set foot in Kellynch since leaving Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. There had been no need, and on the few occasions when it might have been possible for her to visit the Hall, she had managed to avoid and escape them. Her first return was to take up her place again in the Lodge\u2019s modern, elegant rooms, and to delight its mistress\u2019s eyes.\\n\\nLady Russell\u2019s happiness at seeing her was mingled with some anxiety. She knew who had been visiting Uppercross. But fortunately, either Anne had gained in fullness and appearance, or Lady Russell imagined that she had; and while receiving her compliments, Anne enjoyed associating them with her cousin\u2019s silent admiration, and hoped she might be blessed with a second spring of youth and beauty.\\n\\nAs they began to talk, Anne soon perceived a change in her own mind. The subjects that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch, and that she had felt neglected and been obliged to suppress among the Musgroves, had now become matters of only secondary importance. Lately, she had even lost sight of her father, her sister, and Bath. Their affairs had been overwhelmed by those of Uppercross; and when Lady Russell returned to their former hopes and fears, expressing her satisfaction with the house in Camden Place that had been taken, and her regret that Mrs Clay remained with them, Anne would have been embarrassed to have it known how much more she was thinking of Lyme, Louisa Musgrove, and all her acquaintances there; how much more engaging to her were the home and friendship of the Harvilles and Captain Benwick than her father\u2019s house in Camden Place, or her sister\u2019s intimacy with Mrs Clay. She was truly obliged to make an effort to meet Lady Russell with anything resembling equal concern on subjects that naturally had the first claim upon her.\\n\\nAt first, there was some awkwardness when they spoke of another matter. They had to discuss the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had not been there five minutes the previous day before a complete account of it had reached her; yet it must still be discussed. She had to enquire into it, regret the imprudence, lament the consequences, and Captain Wentworth\u2019s name had to be spoken by them both. Anne knew she did not manage this as well as Lady Russell. She could not utter the name while looking directly into Lady Russell\u2019s eyes until she had adopted the expedient of briefly telling her what she thought of the attachment between him and Louisa.\"}"
