          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb1c0a2addce1ef698c29a41\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself; all the work of maintaining a slow and unsatisfactory correspondence with Elizabeth fell to Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had been over only a short while when Captain and Mrs Harville and Captain Benwick joined them. They had arranged to take their final walk around Lyme together. They needed to leave for Uppercross by one, and until then they meant to stay together outdoors for as long as possible.\\n\\nAnne found Captain Benwick coming near her as soon as they were properly out in the street. Their conversation the evening before had not made him reluctant to seek her company again. They walked together for some time, speaking as before about Mr Scott and Lord Byron, and still no more able than any other two readers to agree completely about the merits of either, until something caused an almost general change in the group. Then, instead of Captain Benwick, Captain Harville was walking beside her.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d he said, speaking rather quietly, \u201cyou have done a good thing by getting that poor fellow to talk so much. I wish he could have company like that more often. I know it is bad for him to be shut away as he is; but what can we do? We cannot leave him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI can easily believe that to be impossible; but perhaps, in time\u2014we know what time does in every kind of grief\u2014and you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend may still be considered a young mourner. It was only last summer, I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, quite true,\u201d he said with a deep sigh. \u201cOnly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd perhaps he did not know until later.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week of August, when he came home from the Cape, having just been made captain of the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, afraid to hear about him. He sent letters, but the Grappler had orders for Portsmouth. The news would have to reach him there, but who was to tell him? Not I. I would almost rather have been hanged from the yard-arm. No one could do it except that good fellow\u201d\u2014he pointed to Captain Wentworth\u2014\u201cThe Laconia had arrived at Plymouth the week before, and there was no danger of her being sent to sea again. He took his chance with the rest, applied for leave, but did not wait for the answer. He travelled night and day until he reached Portsmouth, rowed out to the Grappler at once, and never left the poor fellow for a week. That is what he did, and no one else could have saved poor James. You can judge, Miss Elliot, whether he is dear to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne considered the question with complete certainty and replied as fully as her own feelings allowed, and as much as he seemed able to bear. He was too deeply moved to continue the subject, and when he spoke again, it was about something entirely different.\"}"
