          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5070ce87b41daddaa3749cca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But my father was beside me to comfort me and drive away painful memories with hopeful thoughts. I felt that there was something sweet in grieving, because he was there to dry my tears.\\n\\nHe also drew my thoughts away from my sorrow by comparing it with his despair after losing my mother. Even then, I shuddered at the picture he painted of his feelings. He had a poet\u2019s imagination, and when he described the storm that had torn through his heart, his words were so vivid that I believed him even as I trembled. I wondered how he could ever return to ordinary life after his wild thoughts had seemed to bring him close to the supernatural. As he spoke, the ideas he expressed were so powerful that the human heart seemed too limited to contain them. His feelings seemed more suited to a spirit living among earthquakes and volcanoes than to a being confined to a mortal body and human form. But those were only memories; he had changed since then. Now he was all love and gentleness. When I looked at him in wonder as he spoke, the smile on his lips showed me that his heart was filled with the tenderest feelings.\\n\\nTwo months after my aunt\u2019s death, we moved to London, where my father guided me toward deeper studies than those I had pursued before. My progress delighted him. He stayed with me throughout my studies and helped with, or took part in, every lesson. We met a great deal of society, and each day my father tried to brighten with some new pleasure. His tender love for me, and the love and respect I returned, cast a charm over every moment. The hours passed slowly because we made use of every minute. We lived more in one week than many people do in several months, and the variety and freshness of our pleasures made each one more enjoyable.\\n\\nWe were constantly taking excursions together. Whether we went to see beautiful scenery, admire fine paintings, or simply seek whatever amusement happened to appear, I was always happy when I was near my father. I regretted whenever a third person joined us. Yet if I looked uneasily toward my father, his eyes would meet mine, shining with tenderness, and instantly restore joy to my heart. O, hours of perfect happiness! Though you were brief, memory has made you seem as long as a whole lifetime, when I look back through the fog of grief that rose immediately afterward, as if to hide you from my sight.\"}"
