{"request_id":"bcf4515a0106c8448aa0f118","rewritten_text":"He had also to consider Sybil’s father and mother, who were somewhat old-fashioned, and might perhaps oppose the marriage if there were anything resembling a scandal, though he was convinced that, if he explained the entire matter, they would be the first to understand the motives that had guided him. He therefore had every reason to choose poison. It was safe, certain, and quiet, and spared him the need for painful scenes, to which, like most Englishmen, he had a deep-rooted aversion.

Of poisons as a science, however, he knew absolutely nothing, and when the waiter appeared unable to discover anything in the library except _Ruff’s Guide_ and _Bailey’s Magazine_, he searched the shelves himself. At last he found a handsomely bound edition of the _Pharmacopoeia_, together with Erskine’s _Toxicology_, edited by Sir Mathew Reid, President of the Royal College of Physicians and one of the Buckingham’s oldest members, having been elected by mistake in place of someone else; a _contretemps_ that so infuriated the Committee that, when the proper candidate appeared, they black-balled him unanimously. Lord Arthur was considerably perplexed by the technical language in both volumes, and had begun to regret neglecting his classics at Oxford, when, in the second volume of Erskine, he came upon a full and highly interesting description of aconitine’s properties, written in reasonably plain English. It seemed precisely the poison he required. Its action was swift—almost instantaneous, in fact—entirely painless, and, when administered in a gelatine capsule, as Sir Mathew advised, by no means unpleasant. He therefore jotted on his shirt-cuff the quantity required for a fatal dose, replaced the books, and walked up St. James’s Street to Pestle and Humbey’s, the celebrated chemists. Mr. Pestle, who always waited upon members of the aristocracy himself, was rather startled by the request and murmured, with great deference, something about a medical certificate being required. But when Lord Arthur explained that the poison was intended for a large Norwegian mastiff he had to destroy, since it appeared to be developing rabies and had already bitten the coachman twice in the calf, he declared himself entirely satisfied, praised Lord Arthur’s remarkable knowledge of toxicology, and had the prescription prepared at once.

Lord Arthur placed the capsule in a charming little silver _bonbonnière_ he noticed in a Bond Street shop window, discarded Pestle and Humbey’s unattractive pill-box, and drove immediately to Lady Clementina’s."}

