          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"755d71160649c8e55c53c383\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHe was delivered to you. You see that I know the circumstances?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSuch is certainly the fact,\u201d said Defarge. His wife had conveyed to him, by an accidental touch of her elbow as she knitted and hummed, that he would do best to answer, but always briefly.\\n\\n\u201cIt was to you,\u201d said the spy, \u201cthat his daughter came; and from your care she took him, accompanied by a neat brown monsieur\u2014what is his name?\u2014in a little wig\u2014Lorry\u2014of Tellson and Company\u2019s bank\u2014across to England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSuch is the fact,\u201d Defarge repeated.\\n\\n\u201cVery interesting recollections!\u201d said the spy. \u201cI have known Doctor Manette and his daughter in England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes?\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cYou hear little of them now?\u201d asked the spy.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Defarge.\\n\\n\u201cIn fact,\u201d madame interposed, looking up from her work and her little song, \u201cwe never hear of them. We received word of their safe arrival, and perhaps one other letter, or perhaps two; but since then they have gradually gone their way in life\u2014we ours\u2014and we have had no correspondence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPrecisely so, madame,\u201d replied the spy. \u201cShe is going to be married.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGoing?\u201d echoed madame. \u201cShe was pretty enough to have married long ago. You English are cold, it seems to me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! You know I am English.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI can tell your tongue is,\u201d returned madame; \u201cand what the tongue is, I suppose the man is.\u201d\\n\\nHe did not regard this identification as a compliment; but he made the best of it and dismissed it with a laugh. After drinking his cognac to the last, he continued:\\n\\n\u201cYes, Miss Manette is to be married. But not to an Englishman; to one who, like herself, was born French. And speaking of Gaspard (ah, poor Gaspard! It was cruel, cruel!), it is a curious circumstance that she is to marry the nephew of Monsieur the Marquis, for whom Gaspard was raised to that height of so many feet; in other words, the present Marquis. But he lives unknown in England, where he is no Marquis; he is Mr. Charles Darnay. D\u2019Aulnais is his mother\u2019s family name.\u201d\\n\\nMadame Defarge continued knitting, but the news plainly affected her husband. However he tried, behind the little counter, to strike a light and light his pipe, he was disturbed, and his hand was unsteady. The spy would not have been a spy had he failed to notice this, or to store it in his mind.\\n\\nHaving made at least this one successful stroke, whatever it might ultimately be worth, and with no customers arriving to assist him in making another, Mr. Barsad paid for his drink and departed. Before leaving, he took the opportunity to say, politely, that he looked forward to the pleasure of seeing Monsieur and Madame Defarge again.\"}"
