          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b0406b2b6b723813356b157b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Having seen our friend duly settled in his new office, we turned our attention towards Windsor. Its proximity to London was enough to remove any sense of painful separation when we left Raymond and Perdita. We took our leave of them in the Protectoral Palace. It was amusing to see my sister enter, as it were, into the spirit of the drama, and strive to occupy her station with suitable dignity. Her inward pride and outward humility were now more than ever at variance. Her timidity was not assumed, but sprang from a fear of being insufficiently valued, that slight consciousness of the world\u2019s neglect which also marked Raymond. Perdita, however, thought more habitually of others than he did; and some of her bashfulness proceeded from a desire to spare those about her any sense of inferiority\u2014a feeling that had never entered her own mind. From the conditions of his birth and education, Idris would have been more suited to the forms of ceremony; but the ease with which such observances came to her through habit made them wearisome, while Perdita plainly took pleasure in her position, despite every disadvantage. She was too occupied by new ideas to suffer much at our departure; she embraced us affectionately and promised soon to visit, but she did not mourn the circumstances that occasioned our separation. Raymond\u2019s spirits were boundless; he scarcely knew how to employ his newly acquired power. His mind teemed with schemes, though he had settled upon none\u2014but he vowed to himself, his friends, and the world that the era of his Protectorship would be marked by some act of extraordinary glory. Thus we spoke of them and reflected as, with our numbers reduced, we returned to Windsor Castle. We were exceedingly glad to have escaped political turmoil and sought our solitude with renewed eagerness. We were not without occupation; but my ardent nature was now directed solely towards intellectual exertion, and I found severe study an excellent remedy for the fever of spirit which idleness would doubtless have brought upon me. Perdita had allowed us to bring Clara back with us to Windsor; and she, together with my two lovely infants, provided continual interest and amusement.\\n\\nThe sole circumstance that disturbed our tranquillity was Adrian\u2019s health. It was plainly failing, though no symptom appeared by which we could discover his illness, unless his brightened eyes, lively expression, and flushed cheeks made us fear consumption; yet he suffered neither pain nor apprehension. He devoted himself eagerly to books, and rested from his studies in the company he loved best\u2014that of his sister and myself.\"}"
