          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"32106fdab5d8541a381412f3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the centre of this fissure I noticed a white spot, though at first I could not tell what it might be. I altered the telescope\u2019s focus and looked again; now I recognized it as a human skull.\\n\\n\u201cOn making this discovery I was confident enough to believe the riddle solved; for the words \u2018main branch, seventh limb, east side,\u2019 could signify only the skull\u2019s position on the tree, while \u2018shoot from the left eye of the death\u2019s head\u2019 allowed but one meaning in connection with a search for buried treasure. I understood that a bullet was to be dropped from the skull\u2019s left eye, and that a bee-line\u2014or, in other words, a straight line\u2014drawn from the trunk\u2019s nearest point through \u2018the shot\u2019 (the place where the bullet landed), and continued fifty feet farther, would mark a precise spot; beneath that spot, I thought it at least possible, lay a valuable deposit.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this,\u201d I said, \u201cis remarkably plain, and, though clever, remains simple and definite. When you departed from the Bishop\u2019s Hotel, what happened next?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, after carefully taking the tree\u2019s bearings, I turned toward home. Yet the moment I left \u2018the devil\u2019s seat,\u2019 the circular fissure disappeared, and no matter how I turned, I could not see it again. What strikes me as the greatest ingenuity in the entire affair is the fact\u2014for repeated trials have convinced me it is one\u2014that this circular opening can be seen from no other accessible viewpoint than the narrow ledge on the rock\u2019s face.\\n\\n\u201cJupiter had accompanied me on this journey to the \u2018Bishop\u2019s Hotel.\u2019 He had doubtless noticed my absent-minded manner for several weeks, and took particular care not to let me remain alone. But the following day, rising very early, I managed to elude him and went into the hills to look for the tree. After considerable labor I discovered it. When I returned that night, my valet offered to flog me. As for the remainder of the adventure, I believe you know it as well as I do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI presume,\u201d said I, \u201cthat you failed to find the spot on your first digging attempt because of Jupiter\u2019s foolishness in allowing the bug to fall through the skull\u2019s right eye rather than the left.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cExactly. That error shifted the \u2018shot\u2019\u2014that is, the position of the peg nearest the tree\u2014by about two and a half inches. Had the treasure lain directly beneath \u2018the shot,\u2019 the mistake would have mattered little; but \u2018the shot\u2019 and the tree\u2019s nearest point served only as the two points from which a line of direction was established. Naturally, though insignificant at first, the error grew as we followed the line, and after fifty feet had carried us entirely away from the trail.\u201d\"}"
