{"request_id":"3aa43f2c869f841c19184e0e","rewritten_text":"Spirits of the storm, take me! Powers of destruction, open your arms wide and hold me forever! Unless some kinder power has ordained another end for me, so that after long suffering I may receive my reward and once more feel my heart beating beside the heart of another like myself.

The Tiber, the road laid out by nature’s own hand as it winds across her continent, lay at my feet, and many boats were moored along its banks. With a few books, provisions, and my dog, I would board one of them and drift down the river into the sea. Then, keeping close to shore, I would follow the beautiful coasts and sunlit headlands of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples and Calabria, and brave the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis. After that, with nothing to lose and therefore no fear, I would skim the ocean toward Malta and the distant Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople, whose familiar towers and inlets belonged to a different existence from my own. I would coast Asia Minor and Syria, pass the seven-mouthed Nile, and steer north again until, leaving forgotten Carthage and deserted Libya behind, I reached the Pillars of Hercules. And then—wherever it might be—the slimy caves and silent depths of the ocean could become my home before I completed this long voyage; or disease might strike my heart as I drifted alone across the tossing Mediterranean. Perhaps, at some place where I stopped, I might find what I sought—a companion. Or, if that could not be, then through endless years, bent and gray-haired, with youth already buried beside those I loved, the solitary wanderer would still raise his sail and grip the tiller. Still obeying the winds of heaven, he would pass one headland after another, anchor in one bay after another, and continue plowing the empty sea. Leaving behind the green lands of native Europe and following Africa’s tawny coast, after weathering the fierce seas around the Cape, I might finally moor my worn skiff in a creek shaded by the fragrant groves of the spice-scented islands in the distant Indian Ocean.

These are wild dreams. Yet ever since they came to me a week ago, as I stood on the heights of St. Peter’s, they have governed my imagination. I have chosen my boat and gathered my meager supplies. I have selected a few books; the chief among them are Homer and Shakespeare. But the libraries of the world are open to me, and in any port I can replenish my collection. I do not expect my circumstances to improve; but the monotonous present has become unbearable."}

