          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"166e29614c7f35a15cd25bf4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I ran to her, gathered her into my arms, showered her with kisses, and pleaded:\\n\\n\u201cSpeak, darling, speak! What is it?\u201d\\n\\nHer head went limp against my chest, and she gasped, almost inaudibly:\\n\\n\u201cHELLO-CENTRAL!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuick!\u201d I shouted to Clarence. \u201cTelephone the king\u2019s homeopath and tell him to come!\u201d\\n\\nWithin two minutes I was kneeling beside the child\u2019s crib, while Sandy sent servants hurrying in every direction throughout the palace. I understood the situation almost at once\u2014membranous croup! I bent over her and whispered:\\n\\n\u201cWake up, sweetheart! Hello-Central.\u201d\\n\\nShe opened her soft eyes languidly and managed to say:\\n\\n\u201cPapa.\u201d\\n\\nThat comforted me. She was not close to death yet. I sent for sulfur preparations and brought out the croup kettle myself; I do not sit and wait for doctors when Sandy or the child is ill. I knew how to care for both of them and had plenty of experience. This little one had spent much of its short life in my arms, and often I could soothe its troubles and make it laugh through the tears on its eyelashes when even its mother could not.\\n\\nSir Launcelot, dressed in his finest armor, came striding down the great hall on his way to the stock board. He was president of the stock board and occupied the Siege Perilous, which he had bought from Sir Galahad; for the stock board consisted of the Knights of the Round Table, and they now used the Round Table for business. Seats there were worth\u2014well, you would never believe the price, so there is no point in naming it. Sir Launcelot was a bear. He had cornered one of the new lines and was preparing to squeeze the shorts that day; but what did that matter? He was still the old Launcelot, and when he glanced through the doorway as he passed and learned that his pet was sick, that was enough. Bulls and bears could 