{"request_id":"5f493df47d1242584a11d922","rewritten_text":"It seemed to me that this quaint lie had been told in the simplest and most beautiful way; but I had heard it only once, and that made a difference. No doubt it had pleased the others when it was still fresh.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist was the first to wake, and he soon roused the rest with a practical joke of remarkably poor quality. He tied some metal mugs to a dog’s tail and let him loose. The animal raced around and around in a frenzy of terror, while all the other dogs bellowed after him, crashing and battering into everything in their path and creating a complete chaos of confusion, noise, and turmoil. Every man and woman in the crowd laughed until tears ran down their faces; some even fell from their chairs and rolled on the floor in ecstasy. They were exactly like children. Sir Dinadan was so proud of his achievement that he could not stop explaining, again and again until everyone was tired of hearing it, how the immortal idea had occurred to him. And, as humorists of his kind always do, he continued laughing at it long after everyone else had finished. He was so pleased with himself that he decided to make a speech—naturally, a humorous one. I do not think I had ever heard so many worn-out jokes strung together in my life. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than the circus clown. It seemed especially sad to sit there, thirteen hundred years before my birth, listening once more to the stale, flat, worm-eaten jokes that had given me indigestion when I was a boy, thirteen hundred years later. It nearly convinced me that no such thing as a new joke could exist. Everyone laughed at these ancient relics—but then, people always do; I had observed that centuries afterward. Of course, the scoffer did not laugh—I mean the boy. No, he scoffed; there was nothing he would not scoff at. He said most of Sir Dinadan’s jokes were rotten and the rest petrified. I said “petrified” was a good word; I myself believed that the only proper way to classify the majestic ages of some jokes was by geological periods. But that clever idea struck the boy in an empty place, since geology had not yet been invented. Still, I made a note of the remark and planned to educate the commonwealth up to it if I survived. There was no point in throwing away a good thing merely because the market was not yet ready.

Now Sir Kay rose and began to set his history machine going, using me as fuel."}

