          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"997cdaad0c4b67860fc2d9b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"With my heart pounding and filled with a fear I could not explain, I dismissed the servant, locked my door, and sat down to read my father\u2019s letter. These were the words it contained.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Child,\\n\\n\u201cI have betrayed your trust. I have tried to corrupt your mind and have made your innocent heart familiar with the looks and language of unlawful and monstrous passion. I must atone for these crimes and try, in some measure, to make my punishment fit my guilt. You are, I doubt not, prepared for what I am about to announce: we must separate and remain divided forever.\\n\\n\u201cI deprive you of your parent and only friend. You are cast shelterless upon the world; your hopes are destroyed; the peace and security of your pure mind have been ruined. Memory will bring before you terrible images of guilt and the anguish of innocent love betrayed. Yet I am the one who has brought all this misery upon you; I am the one who casts you out and has remorselessly placed the seal of distrust and agony upon the heart and brow of my own child; I, who with devilish recklessness have tried to steal away your loveliness and replace it with the foul deformity of sin. In the overwhelming anguish of my heart, I beg you to forgive me.\\n\\n\u201cI do not ask for your pity; you must, and do, abhor me. But pardon me, Mathilda, and do not let your thoughts pursue me into exile with unrelenting anger. I must never see you again, never hear your voice; yet the gentle whispers of your forgiveness will reach me and cool the burning of my disordered mind and heart. I am certain I would feel it even in my grave. I dare to press this request by telling you how miserably I was drawn into this net of fiery anguish, and of all my struggles to free myself. Indeed, if your soul were less pure and radiant, I would not try to excuse myself to you. I would fear that, if I led you to regard me with less abhorrence, you might hate vice less. But in addressing you, I feel as though I were appealing to an angelic judge. I cannot leave without your forgiveness; I must strive to obtain it, or despair. I therefore implore you to hear my words, and if, among the good, guilt may in any degree be lessened by fierce agony and remorse that tears the mind as madness perhaps does, you may think\u2014though I dare not\u2014that I have some claim to your compassion.\\n\\n\u201cI beg you to remember our first happy life on the shores of Loch Lomond. I had returned from sixteen years of weary wandering, during which, though I had endured many dangers and misfortunes, my affections had remained completely empty.\"}"
