content.text:
{"request_id":"12306b1c9ce327621947fdc9","rewritten_text":"We spent a few miserable hours until eleven o’clock, when the trial was to begin. Since my father and the rest of the family had to attend as witnesses, I went with them to the court. Throughout this wretched mockery of justice, I suffered unbearable torment. The question was whether the result of my curiosity and unlawful experiments would cause the deaths of two fellow human beings: one, a smiling baby filled with innocence and joy; the other, murdered in a far more terrible way, with every disgraceful detail that could make the crime unforgettable in its horror. Justine, too, was a good girl with qualities that promised her a happy life. Now everything would be buried in a shameful grave—and I was the cause. A thousand times I would rather have confessed to the crime blamed on Justine, but I had been away when it was committed. Such a confession would have been dismissed as the ravings of a madman and would not have saved the woman who suffered because of me.

Justine looked calm. She wore mourning clothes, and her face, always pleasant, was made extraordinarily beautiful by the seriousness of her feelings. She seemed confident in her innocence and did not tremble, though thousands stared at and cursed her. Any kindness her beauty might otherwise have inspired had been erased from the spectators’ minds by their belief in the terrible crime she was thought to have committed. She was peaceful, but her calm was clearly forced. Since her earlier confusion had been used as evidence of guilt, she steeled herself to appear brave. When she entered the courtroom, she looked around and quickly saw where we were sitting. A tear seemed to cloud her eye when she noticed us, but she soon recovered, and her sorrowful, affectionate expression seemed to prove that she was completely innocent.

The trial began. After the lawyer for the prosecution stated the charge, several witnesses were called. A number of strange facts were presented against her—facts that might have shaken anyone who did not possess the certainty of her innocence that I had. She had been away all night on the night of the murder, and near morning a market woman had seen her not far from the place where the murdered child’s body was later found. The woman asked what she was doing there, but Justine looked very strange and gave only a confused, unclear answer. She returned home around eight o’clock. When someone asked where she had spent the night, she said she had been searching for the child and urgently asked whether anyone had heard anything about him."}

