content.text:
{"request_id":"020fc2c2735ea2d2d8915b1b","rewritten_text":"We finally lightened the box by removing two-thirds of its contents. Then, with some difficulty, we managed to lift it from the hole. We placed the items we had removed among the brambles and left the dog to guard them. Jupiter strictly ordered him neither to leave the spot for any reason nor to make a sound until we returned. We hurried home with the chest, reaching the hut safely, though only after exhausting effort, at one in the morning. Completely worn out, we could do nothing more at once. We rested until two and ate supper. Immediately afterward, we set out for the hills, carrying three strong sacks that, fortunately, were on the premises. A little before four, we reached the pit, divided the remaining treasure among us as evenly as possible, and left the holes unfilled. Then we returned to the hut, where, for the second time, we placed our golden burdens just as the first faint rays of dawn appeared above the trees in the East.

We were now utterly exhausted, but the intense excitement of the moment kept us from resting. After an uneasy sleep lasting about three or four hours, we rose almost at the same time to examine our treasure.

The chest had been filled to the top, and we spent the entire day and most of the following night examining its contents. Nothing had been arranged; everything had been thrown in together. After carefully sorting it all, we discovered that we possessed even greater wealth than we had first imagined. The coins amounted to somewhat more than four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, based on the value of the pieces as accurately as we could estimate it from the tables of that period. There was not a single piece of silver. Everything was gold, old and varied in origin—French, Spanish, and German coins, along with a few English guineas and some counters, examples of which we had never seen before. Several coins were unusually large and heavy, but so worn that we could not read their inscriptions. There was no American money. We found the jewels harder to value. There were one hundred and ten diamonds, some extremely large and fine, and none of them small; eighteen rubies of remarkable brilliance; three hundred and ten emeralds, all very beautiful; and twenty-one sapphires, along with an opal. All the stones had been removed from their settings and thrown loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we found among the other gold, seemed to have been smashed with hammers, apparently to prevent anyone from identifying them."}

