          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6dc9b6e1b177ea0416758d2a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShucks, Tom,\u201d I say, \u201cI think you could tell a person. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, _I_ don\u2019t know. But he has to have one. All the nobility does.\u201d\\n\\nThat was just his way. If he didn\u2019t feel like explaining something to you, he wouldn\u2019t. You could question him for a week, and it wouldn\u2019t make any difference.\\n\\nHe had finished all that coat-of-arms business, so now he began the rest of that part of the work: planning a mournful inscription. Jim had to have one, he said, just like they all did. He made up several, wrote them on a piece of paper, and read them aloud:\\n\\n1. _Here a captive heart broke._\\n\\n2. _Here a poor prisoner, abandoned by the world and friends, worried away his sorrowful life._\\n\\n3. _Here a lonely heart broke, and a weary spirit went to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity._\\n\\n4. _Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, perished a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV._\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice trembled as he read them, and he nearly broke down. When he finished, he couldn\u2019t decide which one Jim should scratch onto the wall, because they were all so good. At last he said he would have Jim scratch them all on. Jim said it would take him a year to scratch so much writing onto the logs with a nail, and besides, he didn\u2019t know how to make letters. But Tom said he would draw the letters for him, and then Jim would only have to follow the lines. Then, after a while, he said:\\n\\n\u201cCome to think of it, the logs won\u2019t do. They don\u2019t have log walls in a dungeon; we have to carve the inscriptions into a rock. We\u2019ll get a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim said the rock would be worse than the logs. He said it would take him such a terribly long time to carve them into a rock that he would never get out. But Tom said he would let me help him. Then he looked over to see how Jim and I were doing with the pens. It was terribly tedious, difficult, and slow work. It gave my hands no chance to heal from the sores, and we hardly seemed to be making any progress. So Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cI know how to fix it. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the mournful inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with that same rock.\u201d\"}"
