{"request_id":"2deeadcaba2c43d1b5d214df","rewritten_text":"Once this was done, a few more loops were unfastened from the rim, another section of cloth was inserted, and the freed loops were then attached to their proper buttons. In this manner, the entire upper part of the bag could be placed between the netting and the hoop. It is clear that the hoop would now fall inside the car, while the whole weight of the car itself, together with everything in it, would be supported solely by the strength of the buttons. At first glance, this might seem an insufficient support; but it was not, since the buttons were not only very strong individually, but also so close together that each bore only a very small share of the total weight. Indeed, even if the car and its contents had been three times heavier, I would not have felt the least concern. I then raised the hoop again inside the gum-elastic covering and supported it at nearly its former height with three light poles prepared for the purpose. This was done, naturally, to keep the bag expanded at the top and maintain the lower part of the netting in its proper position. All that remained was to close the mouth of the enclosure, which was easily done by gathering the folds of material and twisting them very tightly inward with a kind of fixed tourniquet.

“In the sides of the covering, thus fitted around the car, three circular panes of thick but clear glass had been set, through which I could see without difficulty in every horizontal direction. In the section of cloth forming the bottom there was also a fourth window of the same kind, aligned with a small opening in the floor of the car itself. This allowed me to look directly downward. But since I had found it impossible to install a similar device overhead, because of the unusual way that opening had to be closed and the resulting wrinkles in the cloth, I could expect to see nothing directly above me. This, of course, mattered little; even if I had been able to place a window there, the balloon itself would have prevented me from using it.

“About a foot below one of the side windows was a circular opening eight inches across, fitted with a brass rim whose inner edge was threaded like a screw. The large tube of the condenser was screwed into this rim, while the body of the machine was, naturally, inside the gum-elastic chamber."}

