          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b1a02dc5afbc481dd801df4e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t want to get mixed up in any business like that, I said to myself.\\n\\nWhen I came downstairs in the morning, the parlor was closed up, and the watchers were gone. Nobody was around except the family, Widow Bartley, and our group. I watched their faces to see whether anything had happened, but I couldn\u2019t tell.\\n\\nAround midday the undertaker arrived with his assistant. They placed the coffin in the middle of the room on a pair of chairs, then arranged all our chairs in rows and borrowed more from the neighbors until the hall, parlor, and dining room were full. I saw that the coffin lid was as it had been before, but I didn\u2019t dare look beneath it with people around.\\n\\nThen people began to stream in. The women and girls took seats in the front row at the head of the coffin, and for half an hour the mourners filed past slowly, one by one, looking down at the dead man\u2019s face for a minute. Some shed a tear. Everything was very quiet and solemn, except for the women and girls holding handkerchiefs to their eyes, keeping their heads bowed, and sobbing softly. There was no other sound but feet scraping across the floor and noses being blown\u2014because people always blow them more at a funeral than anywhere else except church.\\n\\nWhen the place was packed, the undertaker moved about in his black gloves with his gentle, soothing manner, making the final arrangements and getting everyone and everything properly settled, without making more noise than a cat. He never spoke. He shifted people around, squeezed in those who arrived late, and opened passageways, all with nods and hand signals. Then he took his place against the wall. He was the gentlest, smoothest, stealthiest man I had ever seen, and he had no more smile on his face than a ham.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeon\u2014a sickly one. When everything was ready, a young woman sat down and played it. It was shrill and wheezy, and everyone joined in singing, though Peter was the only one who sounded good, in my opinion. Then Reverend Hobson began speaking, slowly and solemnly; and at once the most outrageous racket anyone had ever heard broke out in the cellar. It was only one dog, but he made an enormous noise and kept it up without stopping. The parson had to stand there over the coffin and wait\u2014you couldn\u2019t hear yourself think.\"}"
