          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"83dc60695c1dc31cd82027f2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He made her understand that Frank admired her greatly\u2014thought her very beautiful and charming; and since there was so much to be said in his favor, she felt she must not judge him too harshly. As Mrs. Weston observed, \u201call young people would have their little whims.\u201d\\n\\nAmong his new acquaintances in Surrey, one person was not so forgiving. In general, people throughout Donwell and Highbury judged him fairly; they made generous allowances for the small excesses of such a handsome young man\u2014one who smiled so often and bowed so well. But one person could not be softened in his criticism by bows or smiles: Mr. Knightley. The news was told to him at Hartfield. He was silent for the moment, but Emma soon heard him say to himself, while looking over a newspaper, \u201cHum! just the trifling, silly fellow I took him for.\u201d She was half inclined to resent this, but after watching him for a moment, she saw that he had said it only to ease his own feelings, not to provoke her; so she let it pass.\\n\\nAlthough Mr. and Mrs. Weston had brought bad news in one respect, their visit that morning was especially well timed in another. Something happened while they were at Hartfield that made Emma want their advice; and, even more fortunately, she wanted exactly the advice they gave.\\n\\nThis was what happened: The Coles had lived in Highbury for several years and were very good people\u2014friendly, generous, and unassuming. On the other hand, they came from a low social background, were in trade, and were only moderately genteel. When they first moved to the country, they lived quietly within their income, kept little company, and spent little on it. But during the last year or two, their means had greatly increased: the business in town had become more profitable, and fortune had generally favored them. As their wealth grew, so did their ambitions\u2014their need for a larger house and their desire for more company. They enlarged their house, increased their staff, and raised all their expenses; by now, in wealth and style of living, they were second only to the family at Hartfield. Their love of society and their new dining-room made everyone expect them to give dinner parties, and a few gatherings, mostly for single men, had already taken place. Emma could hardly imagine that they would dare invite the regular and most important families\u2014not Donwell, Hartfield, or Randalls. Nothing would persuade her to attend if they did, and she regretted that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal seem less significant than she wished.\"}"
