ins, all praise of me will be unnecessary. You must allow me to judge for myself, and do me the courtesy of believing what I say. I wish you to be very happy and very rich, and by refusing your hand, I do everything in my power to prevent your being otherwise. In making me this offer, you must have satisfied the delicacy of your feelings toward my family, and may take possession of the Longbourn estate whenever it falls to you without any self-reproach. This matter may therefore be regarded as finally settled.\u201d Rising as she spoke, she would have left the room, had not Mr. Collins addressed her again:\\n\\n\u201cWhen I have the honour of speaking to you next on this subject, I shall hope to receive a more favourable answer than the one you have now given me; though I am far from accusing you of cruelty at present, because I know it to be the established custom of your sex to reject a man upon his first application, and perhaps you have even now said as much to encourage my suit as would be consistent with the true delicacy of the female character.\u201d\"}"
