          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"96f1b5fd7204732456f09c46\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nothing Emma later discovered made her take back her poor opinion of Mrs. Elton. Her first judgment had been quite accurate. Mrs. Elton was exactly as she had seemed at their second meeting whenever they met again: self-important, presumptuous, overly familiar, ignorant, and badly mannered. She had a little beauty and some accomplishments, but so little good sense that she believed she had come with superior knowledge of the world to brighten and improve a country neighborhood. She imagined that Miss Hawkins had occupied such a position in society that only Mrs. Elton\u2019s own importance could exceed it.\\n\\nThere was no reason to think Mr. Elton viewed his wife any differently. He seemed not only happy with her but proud of her. He appeared to congratulate himself on bringing such a woman to Highbury, one whom even Miss Woodhouse could not equal. Most of her new acquaintances, eager to praise her or unused to judging others, followed Miss Bates\u2019s friendly example or simply assumed that the bride must be as clever and agreeable as she claimed. They were therefore quite satisfied. Mrs. Elton\u2019s praises passed from one person to another, as they naturally would, without being stopped by Miss Woodhouse, who willingly continued her first contribution and spoke graciously of Mrs. Elton as \u201cvery pleasant and very elegantly dressed.\u201d\\n\\nIn one way, Mrs. Elton became even worse than she had seemed at first. Her feelings toward Emma changed. Probably offended by the little encouragement Emma gave her attempts at friendship, she withdrew in return and gradually became much colder and more distant. Although this change was pleasant for Emma, the ill will behind it naturally increased her dislike. Mrs. Elton\u2019s behavior toward Harriet was unpleasant too, as was Mr. Elton\u2019s. They were mocking and careless. Emma hoped this would quickly cure Harriet of her attachment, but the feelings that could inspire such behavior lowered them both greatly. It was clear that poor Harriet\u2019s attachment had been revealed in the openness of married life, and that her part in the story had probably been told in the least favorable way for her and the most comforting for him. Harriet was, of course, the object of their shared dislike. Whenever they had nothing else to discuss, it would always be easy to start criticizing Miss Woodhouse; and the hostility they dared not show through open disrespect toward Emma found a wider outlet in their contemptuous treatment of Harriet.\\n\\nMrs. Elton took a strong liking to Jane Fairfax, and did so from the very beginning. It was not merely because, while she was at war with one young woman, another might seem especially appealing; she had liked Jane from their first meeting. Nor was she content simply to express a natural and reasonable admiration. Without being asked, invited, or given any special right, she insisted on offering Jane her help and friendship. Before Emma had lost her confidence, and around their third meeting, she heard all about Mrs. Elton\u2019s heroic plans on Jane\u2019s behalf.\"}"
