{"request_id":"034866775a9836bf57df8069","rewritten_text":"I am a native of a sea-girt corner, a cloud-shadowed land, which, when the globe’s surface—with its boundless ocean and pathless continents—rises before my mind, seems merely an insignificant speck within the vast whole; yet, weighed in the balance of intellectual power, it surpassed countries of greater size and far larger populations. How true it is that the human mind alone created whatever was good or noble for humanity, and that Nature herself served only as its first minister. England, set far to the north in the troubled sea, now appears in my dreams as a huge, well-manned ship that mastered the winds and rode proudly upon the waves. In my boyhood she was the universe to me. When I stood upon my native hills and watched plain and mountain extend to the farthest bounds of sight, dotted with my countrymen’s homes and made fertile by their labour, the very centre of the earth was fixed for me in that place, while the remainder of her globe seemed a fable, the forgetting of which would have demanded no effort from either imagination or understanding.

From the outset, my fortunes illustrated the power that change may exercise over the shifting course of human life. In my own case, this was almost an inheritance. My father was one of those men upon whom nature had lavishly bestowed the coveted gifts of wit and imagination, then left his vessel of life to be driven by those winds, without supplying reason as its rudder or judgment as its pilot. His birth was obscure; but circumstances soon brought him into public notice, and his modest paternal estate was quickly squandered amid the splendid world of fashion and luxury in which he played a part. During the brief years of heedless youth, he was idolized by the fashionable triflers of the time, not least by the young sovereign, who escaped party intrigues and the burdensome duties of kingship to find unfailing amusement and renewed spirits in his company. My father’s impulses, never subject to his own control, continually carried him into difficulties from which his ingenuity alone could release him; and the growing heap of debts of honour and commerce, which would have crushed another, he bore with lightness and unquenchable gaiety. Since his presence was so indispensable at the tables and gatherings of the wealthy, his failings were judged pardonable, and he himself was welcomed with intoxicating praise.

Such popularity, like all others, is fleeting; and the troubles of every sort confronting him increased at a terrifying rate in comparison with his limited means of escape."}

