          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"eb0f780a5141b7424006dad0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd think of this, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Oliver, as tears filled his eyes despite his efforts to hold them back. \u201cOh! Think how young and good she is, and how much pleasure and comfort she brings to everyone around her. I am sure\u2014certain\u2014quite certain\u2014that, for your sake, since you are so good yourself; for her own sake; and for the sake of everyone she makes happy, she will not die. Heaven would never let her die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing her hand on Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou think like a child, poor boy. But you remind me of my duty. I had forgotten it for a moment, Oliver, though I hope I may be forgiven, for I am old and have seen enough illness and death to know the pain of losing those we love. I have also seen enough to know that it is not always the youngest and best who are spared to those who love them. But this should comfort us in our sorrow, for Heaven is just. Such things teach us clearly that there is a brighter world than this one, and that the journey to it may be short. God\u2019s will be done! I love her, and He knows how deeply!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was surprised to see that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke, she stopped her grieving as if by a single effort. Straightening herself, she became calm and firm. He was even more astonished to find that this strength lasted, and that throughout all the care and watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie was always ready and composed. She carried out every duty that had fallen to her steadily and, to all appearances, even cheerfully. But Oliver was young and did not know what strong minds can endure in difficult times. How could he, when those who possess such strength so rarely understand it themselves?\\n\\nAn anxious night followed. By morning, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s fears had proved only too accurate. Rose was in the first stage of a severe and dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must act, Oliver, and not give in to useless grief,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing a finger to her lips as she looked steadily into his face. \u201cThis letter must be sent to Mr. Losberne as quickly as possible. It must be taken to the market town, which is no more than four miles away by the footpath across the fields, and then sent by a rider straight to Chertsey. The people at the inn will arrange this, and I know I can trust you to make sure it is done.\u201d\\n\\nOliver could not answer, but his anxious expression showed that he was ready to leave at once.\"}"
