{"request_id":"9d426b6b563ceadf38570d7d","rewritten_text":"It seemed to me that this quaint falsehood had been told in the simplest and loveliest way; but I had heard it only once, and that makes a difference; it had doubtless been agreeable to the others while it was still new.

Sir Dinadan the Humorist was the first to wake, and he quickly stirred the rest with a practical joke of remarkably poor quality. He fastened some metal mugs to a dog’s tail and let him loose. The animal raced wildly about in terror, while all the other dogs bayed after him, crashing and battering into everything in their path, creating a complete confusion and an ear-splitting uproar; whereupon every man and woman in the crowd laughed until tears ran down their faces, and some tumbled from their chairs and rolled about the floor in delight. They were exactly like children. Sir Dinadan was so pleased with his achievement that he could not stop repeating, again and again until everyone was tired of it, how his immortal idea had come to him; and, as humorists of his kind commonly do, he continued laughing at it long after everyone else had finished. So elated was he that he decided to give a speech—naturally, a comic one. I doubt that I have ever heard so many worn-out jokes strung together. He was worse than the minstrels, worse even than the circus clown. It seemed especially melancholy to sit there, thirteen hundred years before my birth, and hear once more those stale, flat, worm-eaten jokes that had given me the dry gripes when I was a boy, thirteen hundred years later. It nearly persuaded me that no new joke is possible. Everyone laughed at these ancient relics—but then, people always do; I had observed that centuries afterward. Of course the scoffer did not laugh—I mean the boy. No, he scoffed; there was nothing he would not scoff at. He said most of Sir Dinadan’s jokes were rotten, and the remainder petrified. I said “petrified” was a good word; for I myself believed that the only proper way to classify the majestic ages of certain jokes was by geological periods. But that clever notion struck the boy in an empty spot, since geology had not yet been invented. Still, I made a note of the remark and intended to educate the commonwealth up to it if I survived. There is no sense in discarding a good thing merely because the market is not yet ready for it.

Then Sir Kay rose and began to set his history-mill going, using me as fuel."}

