          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6060ff5b5bcdf8f8d641d98f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You must learn some of my philosophy. Think of the past only when remembering it gives you pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cannot credit you with that kind of philosophy. Your memories must be entirely free of blame, so the peace they bring comes not from philosophy but, much better, from ignorance. But that is not true for me. Painful memories return, and they cannot, and should not, be pushed away. I have been selfish all my life in practice, though not in principle. As a child, I was taught what was right, but not how to control my temper. I was given good principles, but left to follow them with pride and conceit. Sadly, as an only son\u2014and for many years an only child\u2014I was spoiled by my parents. Though they were good people, my father especially being kind and generous, they allowed, encouraged, and almost taught me to be selfish and overbearing; to care about no one outside my family; to think poorly of everyone else; and at least to wish to believe that their judgment and worth were less than my own. That was how I was from eight to twenty-eight, and I might still be that way if not for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth! How much I owe you! You taught me a lesson that was difficult at first, but greatly benefited me. You humbled me as I needed to be humbled. I came to you certain that you would welcome me. You showed me how inadequate all my claims were to please a woman worthy of being pleased.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHad you really convinced yourself that I would?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed I had. What will you think of my vanity? I believed you wanted and expected me to propose.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy manner must have been at fault, though I assure you it was not intentional. I never meant to deceive you, but my feelings may often have led me astray. How you must have hated me after that evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHated you! I was angry, perhaps, at first, but my anger soon found its proper direction.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. Did you blame me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, indeed. I felt nothing but surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise could not have been greater than mine at being noticed by you. My conscience told me I deserved no special politeness, and I admit I did not expect to receive more than I was owed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy purpose then,\u201d Darcy replied, \u201cwas to show you, through every courtesy I could offer, that I was not so petty as to resent the past. I hoped to gain your forgiveness and reduce your poor opinion of me by showing you that I had taken your criticisms to heart.\u201d\"}"
