          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"71b5b6216bf896bded940d0b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But he had oppressed no one, imprisoned no one; so far from harshly demanding payment of his dues, he had voluntarily surrendered them, cast himself upon a world offering him no favour, secured his own private position there, and earned his own living. Monsieur Gabelle had administered the impoverished, encumbered estate under written instructions: to spare the people, to provide whatever little could be provided\u2014fuel in winter, insofar as the heavy creditors allowed it, and produce in summer, insofar as it could be rescued from that same grasp\u2014and doubtless he had set out these facts in his plea and evidence, for his own protection, so that they must now come to light.\\n\\nThis strengthened the desperate determination Charles Darnay had begun to form: he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. Like the mariner of the old tale, the winds and currents had carried him within the Loadstone Rock\u2019s influence; it was drawing him toward itself, and he must go. Every thought that rose before him swept him onward, faster and faster, with increasing certainty, toward that terrible attraction. His hidden uneasiness had been that evil purposes were being carried out in his unhappy country by evil instruments, while he, who could not help knowing himself better than they, was absent instead of attempting to check the bloodshed and uphold the claims of mercy and humanity. With this uneasiness partly smothered and partly reproaching him, he had been led to compare himself pointedly with the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so powerful; and immediately after that comparison, so damaging to himself, came Monseigneur\u2019s sneers, which had wounded him deeply, followed by Stryver\u2019s, coarser and more galling than all, for reasons long remembered. Then came Gabelle\u2019s letter: an innocent prisoner\u2019s appeal, in peril of death, to his justice, honour, and good name.\\n\\nHis