he will,\u201d said Mr. Brownlow, while Oliver\u2019s tears streamed down.\\n\\nMonks said nothing.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow continued for him, \u201cwas written in the same spirit as the letter. He spoke of the misery his wife had caused him; of your rebellious nature, vice, spite, and early evil passions, you being his only son and raised to hate him; and left you and your mother an annuity of eight hundred pounds each. The greater part of his property he divided equally\u2014one share for Agnes Fleming, and the other for their child, if it were born alive and ever reached adulthood.\u201d\"}"
