          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b5449c233f87b8051f4aeab5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t want to get mixed up in any business like that, I told myself.\\n\\nWhen I came downstairs in the morning, the parlor was closed, and the watchers were gone. No one was there except the family, Widow Bartley, and our group. I studied their faces to see whether anything had happened, but I couldn\u2019t tell.\\n\\nAround midday, the undertaker arrived with his assistant. They placed the coffin in the middle of the room on two chairs, then arranged all our chairs in rows and borrowed more from the neighbors until the hall, parlor, and dining room were full. I saw that the coffin lid was as it had been before, but I didn\u2019t dare look beneath it with so many people around.\\n\\nThen people began to arrive. The women and girls took seats in the front row at the head of the coffin, and for half an hour the visitors filed past slowly, one at a time, looking down at the dead man\u2019s face for a minute. Some shed a tear. Everything was quiet and solemn, except for the women and girls holding handkerchiefs to their eyes, keeping their heads bowed, and sobbing softly. The only other sounds were feet scraping across the floor and people blowing their noses\u2014because people always blow their noses more at a funeral than anywhere else except church.\\n\\nWhen the room was packed, the undertaker moved about in his black gloves with gentle, soothing manners, making final adjustments and arranging people and things neatly and comfortably without making more noise than a cat. He never spoke. He shifted people around, squeezed in late arrivals, and opened paths through the crowd with nods and hand signals. Then he took his place against the wall. He was the gentlest, smoothest, quietest man I had ever seen, and he had no more smile on his face than a ham does.\\n\\nThey had borrowed a melodeon\u2014a sick-sounding one. When everything was ready, a young woman sat down and played it. It sounded shrill and wheezy, but everyone joined in singing, and Peter was the only one who sounded good, as far as I could tell. Then Reverend Hobson began speaking slowly and solemnly. At once, the most outrageous noise anyone had ever heard broke out in the cellar. It was only one dog, but he made an enormous racket and kept it up. The preacher had to stand there over the coffin and wait\u2014you couldn\u2019t hear yourself think.\"}"
