          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c5a6fbca1867536461636565\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You wouldn\u2019t know who I am unless you\u2019ve read a book called The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, but that doesn\u2019t matter. Mr. Mark Twain wrote that book, and he told the truth, mostly. There were things he exaggerated, but mostly he told the truth. That\u2019s nothing. I\u2019ve never known anybody who didn\u2019t lie now and then, except Aunt Polly, or the widow, or maybe Mary. Aunt Polly\u2014Tom\u2019s Aunt Polly, she is\u2014and Mary, and the Widow Douglas are all mentioned in that book, which is mostly true, with some exaggerations, as I said before.\\n\\nNow, this is how the book ends: Tom and I found the money the robbers had hidden in the cave, and it made us rich. We got six thousand dollars apiece\u2014all in gold. It was an enormous amount of money when it was piled up. Well, Judge Thatcher took it and invested it, and it earned us a dollar a day apiece all year round\u2014more than anyone could know what to do with. The Widow Douglas took me in as her son and said she would civilize me; but living in the house all the time was hard, considering how strictly regular and proper the widow was in everything she did. So when I couldn\u2019t stand it any longer, I ran away. I put on my old rags and went back to my sugar hogshead, and I was free and content. But Tom Sawyer tracked me down and said he was going to start a band of robbers, and I could join if I went back to the widow and behaved respectably. So I went back.\\n\\nThe widow cried over me and called me a poor lost lamb, along with a lot of other names, but she never meant any harm by it. She put me into those new clothes again, and all I could do was sweat and feel cramped. Then the old routine began again. The widow rang a bell for supper, and you had to come immediately. When you reached the table, you couldn\u2019t start eating at once; you had to wait for the widow to lower her head and grumble a little over the food, though there was really nothing wrong with it\u2014except that everything had been cooked separately. In a barrel of odds and ends, it\u2019s different: things get mixed together, the juices trade places, and everything tastes better.\\n\\nAfter supper she brought out her book and taught me about Moses and the Bulrushes, and I was eager to learn all about him. But after a while she let slip that Moses had been dead for a very long time. So then I didn\u2019t care about him anymore, because I don\u2019t have any interest in dead people.\"}"
