ng like that?\u201d interrupted Master Bates. \u201cHe doesn\u2019t know what you mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf you don\u2019t take pocket handkerchiefs and watches,\u201d said the Dodger, lowering his language to Oliver\u2019s level, \u201csomeone else will. Then the people who lose them will be worse off, and you\u2019ll be worse off too, while nobody will be even half a farthing better off except the fellows who get them\u2014and you\u2019ve just as much right to them as they do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course, of course!\u201d said the Jew, who had entered without Oliver noticing. \u201cIt all comes down to that, my dear; take the Dodger\u2019s word for it. Ha! ha! ha! He knows the principles of his trade.\u201d\\n\\nThe old man rubbed his hands together gleefully as he confirmed the Dodger\u2019s reasoning in these words, and chuckled with delight at his pupil\u2019s skill.\\n\\nThe conversation went no further at that time, because the Jew had returned home with Miss Betsy and a gentleman Oliver had never seen before. The Dodger addressed him as Tom Chitling; after lingering on the stairs to exchange a few flirtatious remarks with the lady, he now appeared.\"}"
