ced greater profits, and fortune in general had smiled upon them. As their wealth grew, so did their ambitions: they wanted a larger house and more company. They enlarged their house, increased their number of servants, and raised their expenses in every way; and by this time they were, in fortune and style of living, second only to the family at Hartfield. Their love of society and their new dining room prepared everyone for their giving dinner parties, and a few gatherings, chiefly among the single men, had already taken place. Emma could hardly imagine that they would presume to invite the established and best families\u2014not Donwell, Hartfield, or Randalls. Nothing would tempt her to go if they did, and she regretted that her father\u2019s well-known habits would make her refusal seem less meaningful than she wished.\"}"
