{"request_id":"8779685124064962aa222967","rewritten_text":"“Oh, that is nothing! It is a pleasure for me. I do not work for money; I live entirely for my art.”

Lord Arthur placed £4 2s. 6d. on the table, thanked the little German for his kindness, and, after managing to refuse an invitation to meet some Anarchists at a meat-tea the following Saturday, left the house and went to the Park.

For the next two days he was extremely excited, and on Friday at noon he drove to the Buckingham to wait for news. All afternoon, the expressionless hall-porter kept putting up telegrams from different parts of the country, reporting horse-race results, divorce verdicts, the weather, and similar matters. Meanwhile, the ticker printed dull details about an all-night sitting in the House of Commons and a minor panic on the Stock Exchange. At four o’clock the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur vanished into the library with the _Pall Mall_, the _St. James’s_, the _Globe_, and the _Echo_. This greatly annoyed Colonel Goodchild, who wanted to read reports of a speech he had given that morning at the Mansion House about South African Missions and the wisdom of having black Bishops in every province. For some reason, he strongly disliked the _Evening News_. None of the papers, however, contained the slightest mention of Chichester, and Lord Arthur realized that the attempt must have failed. It was a terrible disappointment, and for a while he was completely shaken. Herr Winckelkopf, whom he visited the next day, offered many elaborate apologies and proposed to give him another clock free of charge, or a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost price. But Lord Arthur had lost all faith in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf admitted that everything was so adulterated nowadays that even dynamite could scarcely be obtained in a pure form. The little German still believed the clock might go off, though he admitted that something must have gone wrong with its mechanism. He mentioned a barometer he had once sent to the military Governor at Odessa. It had been set to explode after ten days, but had failed to do so for nearly three months. It was true that, when it finally exploded, it only blew a housemaid to pieces; the Governor had left town six weeks earlier. Still, it proved that dynamite, as a destructive force, was powerful when controlled by machinery, even if somewhat unpunctual."}

