          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3e63acc848c16f82160f35d9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I want to see you permanently well connected, and for that reason it would be best for you to have as few unsuitable acquaintances as possible. So, if you are still in this country when Mr. Martin marries, I hope your friendship with the sisters will not lead you to become acquainted with his wife, who will probably be some uneducated farmer\u2019s daughter.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo be sure. Yes. Not that I think Mr. Martin would ever marry anyone who had not received some education and been very well brought up. Still, I do not mean to oppose my opinion to yours\u2014and I am sure I shall not seek his wife\u2019s acquaintance. I shall always feel great affection for the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and would be very sorry to give them up, since they are quite as well educated as I am. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, then certainly I had better not visit her, if I can avoid it.\u201d\\n\\nEmma watched her as she spoke, noting every change in her manner, and saw no troubling signs of love. The young man had been her first admirer, but Emma trusted that he had no other hold over her and that Harriet would offer no serious resistance to any friendly plan of her own.\\n\\nThey met Mr. Martin the very next day while walking along the Donwell road. He was on foot, and after looking at Emma with great respect, he turned to her companion with obvious pleasure. Emma was glad to have the chance to examine him. She walked a few yards ahead while they talked, and soon her sharp eyes had formed a clear opinion of Mr. Robert Martin. He was very neat and looked like a sensible young man, but he had no other advantage in appearance. When compared with gentlemen, Emma thought he would lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet\u2019s feelings. Harriet was not indifferent to good manners; she had noticed her father\u2019s gentleness herself, admiring it as much as she wondered at it. Mr. Martin looked as if he had no idea what good manners were.\\n\\nThey stayed together only a few minutes, since Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting. Harriet then ran back to her with a smiling face and such excitement that Miss Woodhouse hoped to calm her very soon.\\n\\n\u201cJust think of our happening to meet him! How very strange! He said it was pure chance that he had not gone around by Randalls. He did not think we ever walked along this road. He thought we went toward Randalls most days.\u201d\"}"
