          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"67fba371508931164dc0acbc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cSo, now, tell me all about it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, you see, it happened this way. The old mistress\u2014that\u2019s Miss Watson\u2014keeps picking on me and treats me pretty harshly, but she always said she wouldn\u2019t sell me down to New Orleans. Still, I\u2019d noticed a slave trader hanging around the place quite a bit lately, and I began to feel uneasy. One night I crept to the door pretty late. It wasn\u2019t fully closed, and I heard the old mistress telling the widow she was going to sell me down to New Orleans. She said she didn\u2019t want to, but she could get eight hundred dollars for me, and it was such a large sum she couldn\u2019t resist. The widow tried to make her promise she wouldn\u2019t do it, but I didn\u2019t wait to hear the rest. I got out of there mighty fast, I can tell you.\\n\\n\u201cI ran down the hill, expecting to steal a skiff somewhere along the shore above town, but people were still moving about, so I hid in the old, falling-down cooper\u2019s shop on the bank and waited for everyone to leave. Well, I stayed there all night. There was somebody around the whole time. Around six in the morning, skiffs began passing by, and by eight or nine, every skiff that went along was talking about how your father had come to town and said you\u2019d been killed. Those later skiffs were full of ladies and gentlemen going over to see the place. Sometimes they stopped at the shore and rested before crossing, so from their talk I learned everything about the killing. I was terribly sorry you\u2019d been killed, Huck, but I\u2019m not anymore.\\n\\n\u201cI lay there under the shavings all day. I was hungry, but I wasn\u2019t afraid, because I knew the old mistress and the widow were going to leave for the camp meeting right after breakfast and be gone all day. They knew I went off with the cattle around daylight, so they wouldn\u2019t expect to see me around the place and wouldn\u2019t miss me until evening, after dark. The other servants wouldn\u2019t miss me either, because they\u2019d slip away and take a holiday as soon as the old people were out of the way.\\n\\n\u201cWell, when it got dark, I set out along the river road and went about two miles or more, to where there weren\u2019t any houses. I\u2019d made up my mind what I was going to do. You see, if I kept trying to escape on foot, the dogs would track me. If I stole a skiff to cross over, they\u2019d notice that skiff was missing, and they\u2019d know about where I\u2019d landed on the other side and where to pick up my trail.\u201d\"}"
