          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"603eea467e9ca0af6bcdf6ad\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There comes a point in every properly made boy\u2019s life when he burns to go somewhere and dig for buried treasure. That longing seized Tom one day. He went out looking for Joe Harper, but could not find him. Then he tried Ben Rogers, who had gone fishing. Before long he came across Huck Finn the Red-Handed. Huck would do. Tom led him to a secluded spot and confided the plan to him. Huck was agreeable. Huck was always ready to join any venture that promised amusement and cost nothing, since he possessed an inconvenient excess of the kind of time that is not money. \u201cWhere\u2019ll we dig?\u201d Huck asked.\\n\\n\u201cOh, almost anywhere.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, is it buried everywhere?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, it ain\u2019t. It\u2019s hidden in very particular places, Huck\u2014sometimes on islands, sometimes in rotten chests beneath the end of a branch on an old dead tree, exactly where the shadow falls at midnight; but usually under the floor of haunted houses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho hides it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, robbers, naturally\u2014who did you suppose? Sunday-school superintendents?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. If it belonged to me, I wouldn\u2019t hide it; I\u2019d spend it and enjoy myself.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo would I. But robbers don\u2019t act that way. They always hide it and leave it there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t they ever come back for it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, they mean to, but usually forget the signs, or else they die. Anyway, it lies there a long while and grows rusty; then somebody discovers an old yellow paper explaining how to find the marks\u2014a paper that has to be worked out for about a week because it\u2019s mostly signs and hy\u2019roglyphics.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHyro-what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHy\u2019roglyphics\u2014pictures and such, you know, that don\u2019t appear to mean anything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHave you got one of those papers, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, then, how are you going to find the marks?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t need any marks. They always bury it under a haunted house or on an island, or beneath a dead tree with one branch sticking out. We\u2019ve searched Jackson\u2019s Island a little, and we can try again sometime; and there\u2019s the old haunted house up the Still-House branch, and plenty of dead-limb trees\u2014whole loads of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs it under every one?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat a question! No!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen how\u2019ll you know which one to try?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTry them all!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, Tom, that\u2019ll take the whole summer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so what? Suppose you find a brass pot containing a hundred dollars, all rusty and gray, or a rotten chest packed with diamonds. How would that be?\u201d\\n\\nHuck\u2019s eyes shone.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s bully. Bully enough for me. Just give me the hundred dollars, and I don\u2019t want any diamonds.\u201d\"}"
