          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"976a1803d8571863d9ce45c3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A FEW days later, Virginia and her curly-haired cavalier went riding across Brockley meadows. In forcing her way through a hedge, she tore her habit so badly that, when she got home, she resolved to use the back staircase so that no one would see her. As she hurried past the Tapestry Chamber, whose door stood open, she thought she caught sight of someone within. Supposing it to be her mother\u2019s maid, who sometimes brought her work there, she looked in to ask her to repair the habit. To her great astonishment, however, it was the Canterville Ghost himself! He sat beside the window, watching the ruined gold of the yellowing trees whirl through the air and the red leaves dance wildly down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, and his entire posture expressed profound dejection. Indeed, he looked so wretched and so thoroughly out of repair that little Virginia, whose first impulse had been to flee and lock herself in her room, was overcome with pity and resolved to comfort him. Her step was so quiet, and his melancholy so deep, that he did not notice her until she spoke.\\n\\n\u2018I am very sorry for you,\u2019 she said, \u2018but my brothers are returning to Eton to-morrow, and then, if you behave yourself, nobody will trouble you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018It is ridiculous to ask me to behave myself,\u2019 he replied, turning in astonishment toward the pretty little girl who had dared to address him, \u2018perfectly ridiculous. I must rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and wander about at night, if that is what you mean. It is the sole purpose of my existence.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018That is no purpose for existing at all, and you know you have behaved very wickedly. Mrs. Umney told us, on the first day we came here, that you had murdered your wife.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, I freely confess it,\u2019 said the Ghost peevishly, \u2018but it was strictly a family affair, and involved nobody else.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018It is very wrong to murder anyone,\u2019 said Virginia, who sometimes possessed a gentle Puritan solemnity inherited from an old New England ancestor.\\n\\n\u2018Oh, I detest the cheap harshness of abstract morality! My wife was extremely plain, never had my ruffs starched properly, and understood nothing about cooking. Why, there was a buck I had shot in Hogley Woods, a splendid pricket, and do you know how she had it served at table? Still, it does not matter now, since it is finished, and I do not think it was very kind of her brothers to starve me to death, even though I had killed her.\u2019\"}"
