{"request_id":"e2f6745d16cc4130ed80c7fb","rewritten_text":"“By eight o’clock I had in fact reached an altitude of seventeen miles above the earth’s surface. It therefore appeared clear to me that my rate of ascent was not merely increasing, but that some upward movement would have been perceptible even if I had not released the ballast. The pains in my head and ears returned violently at intervals, and I continued occasionally to bleed from the nose; yet, on the whole, I suffered far less than might have been anticipated. Breathing, however, grew more difficult with every moment, and each inhalation brought on a troublesome spasmodic movement of the chest. I now unpacked the condensing apparatus and prepared it for immediate employment.

“The prospect of the earth at this stage of my ascent was truly beautiful. Westward, northward, and southward, stretching as far as my sight reached, lay an apparently motionless and limitless expanse of ocean, which every instant deepened in blue and had already begun to show a slight convexity. Far away to the east, though perfectly distinguishable, were the islands of Great Britain, the whole Atlantic coast of France and Spain, and a small part of northern Africa. No individual buildings could be seen, and the loftiest cities of mankind had completely vanished from the earth’s surface. From the rock of Gibraltar, now reduced to a faint speck, the dark Mediterranean, sprinkled with gleaming islands like the sky with stars, extended eastward to the limit of my vision, until its whole body of water seemed finally to plunge over the horizon’s abyss, and I found myself listening eagerly for the reverberations of the enormous cataract. Above me, the sky was black as jet, and the stars shone with extraordinary brilliance.

“The pigeons at about this time appearing to suffer greatly, I resolved to set them free. I first untied one, a handsome gray-mottled bird, and placed him on the edge of the wickerwork. He seemed exceedingly distressed, gazing anxiously about, beating his wings, and cooing loudly, but could not be induced to leave the car. At last I picked him up and threw him some six yards from the balloon. Instead of descending, as I had expected, he struggled fiercely to return, uttering meanwhile very sharp and piercing cries. Eventually he regained his former place on the rim, but scarcely had he done so when his head sank onto his breast, and he fell dead inside the car.”"}

