{"request_id":"1509885fed354abc28dc2810","rewritten_text":"Indeed, I had no fear for her. She was what people call a remarkable woman, capable of managing worldly affairs without my help. To tell the truth, I believe she had always regarded me as an idle boy, a mere encumbrance, fit for nothing but building castles in the air, and was rather pleased to be rid of me. It was a dark night when I said goodbye to her. Taking with me, as aides-de-camp, the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble, I carried the balloon, with its car and equipment, by a circuitous route to the station where the other articles had been stored. We found everything there untouched, and I immediately set to work.

“It was the first of April. The night, as I have said, was dark; not a star could be seen, and a drizzling rain, falling at intervals, made us extremely uncomfortable. My chief concern, however, was the balloon, which, despite the varnish protecting it, was beginning to grow rather heavy with moisture; the powder, too, was liable to be damaged. I therefore kept my three duns working with great diligence, pounding ice around the central cask and stirring the acid in the others. They did not stop, however, pestering me with questions about what I intended to do with all this apparatus, and complained bitterly about the terrible labor I was forcing them to endure. They could not understand, they said, what good could possibly come from being soaked to the skin merely to take part in such dreadful incantations. I began to feel uneasy and worked with all my strength, for I sincerely believe the fools imagined I had made a pact with the devil, and that what I was now doing was, in short, nothing proper. I was therefore very afraid they would abandon me altogether. Still, I managed to calm them by promising to settle all their accounts in full as soon as I could bring the present undertaking to an end. They naturally put their own interpretation on these words, imagining, no doubt, that I would soon acquire vast sums of ready money; and so long as I paid everything I owed, with a little extra for their services, I daresay they cared very little what became of either my soul or my body.

“In about four and a half hours, I found the balloon sufficiently inflated. I therefore attached the car and placed all my equipment inside it—not forgetting the condensing apparatus, an ample supply of water, and a large quantity of provisions, including pemmican, which contains considerable nourishment in a comparatively small bulk."}

