          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f981c47f902745bc9f130959\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Countrymen, butchers, drovers, hawkers, boys, thieves, idlers, and every kind of low vagabond were mixed together in one mass. Drovers whistled, dogs barked, oxen bellowed and struggled, sheep bleated, and pigs grunted and squealed. Hawkers cried out, while shouts, curses, and quarrels rose from every side. Bells rang, and every public-house poured out a roar of voices. People crowded, pushed, drove, beat, whooped, and yelled. The ugly, clashing noise echoed through every corner of the market, while unwashed, unshaven, ragged, and dirty figures ran constantly back and forth, bursting in and out of the crowd. Altogether, it was a deafening and confusing scene that overwhelmed the senses.\\n\\nMr. Sikes, pulling Oliver behind him, forced his way through the thickest part of the crowd and paid little attention to the many sights and sounds that amazed the boy. He nodded two or three times to friends who passed, and refused several invitations to have a morning drink. He kept moving until they had escaped the noise and made their way through Hosier Lane into Holborn.\\n\\n\u201cNow, young \u2019un!\u201d said Sikes, looking up at the clock of St. Andrew\u2019s Church. \u201cNearly seven! You must walk faster. Come on\u2014don\u2019t start lagging behind already, Lazy-legs!\u201d\\n\\nAs he spoke, Mr. Sikes jerked his little companion by the wrist. Oliver quickened his pace to a trot, somewhere between walking and running, and did his best to keep up with the housebreaker\u2019s long, quick strides.\\n\\nThey continued at that speed until they had passed Hyde Park Corner and were heading toward Kensington. Then Sikes slowed down until an empty cart, some distance behind them, caught up. Seeing \u201cHounslow\u201d written on it, he asked the driver, as politely as he could, whether he would carry them as far as Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cJump up,\u201d said the man. \u201cIs that your boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, he\u2019s my boy,\u201d replied Sikes, staring hard at Oliver and absently putting his hand into the pocket where he kept the pistol.\\n\\n\u201cYour father walks a little too fast for you, doesn\u2019t he, my lad?\u201d the driver asked, noticing that Oliver was out of breath.\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Sikes cut in. \u201cHe\u2019s used to it. Here, take my hand, Ned. Get in!\u201d\\n\\nSpeaking to Oliver in this way, he helped him into the cart. The driver pointed to a pile of sacks and told him to lie there and rest.\\n\\nAs they passed one milestone after another, Oliver wondered more and more where his companion intended to take him. They passed Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, and Brentford, yet continued steadily on as if their journey had only just begun.\"}"
