          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"594e5331fdc8be644c7196ca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The following day was fixed for the production (so that our honour might not grow cold through delay), and on that day Drummle appeared with a small, polite acknowledgment in Estella\u2019s handwriting, stating that she had had the honour of dancing with him several times. This left me no alternative but to regret that I had been \u201cbetrayed into a warmth which,\u201d and, generally speaking, to deny as untenable the notion that I was to be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat snorting at each other for an hour, while the Grove contradicted everybody indiscriminately, and at last the advancement of good feeling was pronounced to have proceeded at a remarkable speed.\\n\\nI describe this casually, but it was anything but casual to me. I cannot properly convey the pain I felt at the thought of Estella showing favour to such a contemptible, awkward, sullen fool, so far beneath the common level. Even now, I believe it must have arisen from some pure flame of generosity and selflessness in my love for her, that I could not bear the idea of her stooping to that hound. Certainly I should have been wretched whatever person she had favoured; but a more deserving object would have produced distress of another sort and intensity.\\n\\nIt was not difficult for me to discover, and I soon did discover, that Drummle had begun following her closely, and that she permitted him to do so. Before long he was constantly after her, and he and I encountered each other every day. He persisted in a dull, stubborn fashion, while Estella kept him in pursuit; sometimes encouraging him, sometimes discouraging him, sometimes nearly flattering him, sometimes plainly scorning him, sometimes seeming to know him perfectly, and sometimes hardly recalling who he was.\\n\\nThe Spider, as Mr. Jaggers had named him, was accustomed to waiting in ambush, and possessed the patience of his kind. Besides this, he had a thick-headed faith in his wealth and his family\u2019s importance, which occasionally served him well\u2014almost supplying the place of focus and resolute intention. Thus the Spider, watching Estella with dogged persistence, outlasted many more brilliant insects, and would often uncoil himself and drop down at precisely the right moment.\\n\\nAt a certain Assembly Ball in Richmond (there were Assembly Balls in most places in those days), where Estella had surpassed all the other beauties, this clumsy Drummle lingered so persistently around her, and with such tolerance from her, that I determined to speak with her about him. I seized the next opportunity, when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home and sat apart among some flowers, prepared to leave.\"}"
