{"request_id":"e682e6e24773bc2e569c6ee2","rewritten_text":"Many American ladies, on leaving their native country, adopt an appearance of chronic ill health, believing it to be a mark of European refinement, but Mrs. Otis had never made that mistake. She possessed a magnificent constitution and an extraordinary supply of energy. Indeed, in many ways she was quite English, and an excellent example of the fact that nowadays we have almost everything in common with America except, of course, the language. Her eldest son, christened Washington by his parents in a moment of patriotism—a choice he never stopped regretting—was a fair-haired, rather handsome young man who had prepared himself for American diplomacy by leading the German at the Newport Casino for three consecutive seasons. Even in London he was known as an excellent dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his only weaknesses; otherwise, he was extremely sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis was a fifteen-year-old girl, lithe and lovely as a fawn, with a bold freedom in her large blue eyes. She was a remarkable horsewoman and had once raced old Lord Bilton on her pony twice around the park, winning by a length and a half just before the statue of Achilles, to the immense delight of the young Duke of Cheshire. He proposed to her on the spot, and his guardians sent him back to Eton that very night, weeping bitterly. After Virginia came the twins, generally known as ‘The Stars and Stripes,’ because they were always being switched. They were charming boys and, apart from the worthy Minister, the only genuine republicans in the family.

Since Canterville Chase lies seven miles from Ascot, the nearest railway station, Mr. Otis had telegraphed for a waggonette to meet them, and they set off on their drive in high spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air was delicately scented by the pine woods. From time to time they heard a wood pigeon absorbed in its own sweet call, or glimpsed, deep among the rustling ferns, the pheasant’s burnished breast. Little squirrels watched them from the beech trees as they passed, while rabbits darted through the undergrowth and over the mossy knolls, their white tails raised in the air. As they entered the avenue leading to Canterville Chase, however, the sky suddenly darkened with clouds, a strange stillness seemed to settle over the air, and a great flock of rooks flew silently above them. Before they reached the house, several large drops of rain had fallen.

An old woman stood on the steps to welcome them, neatly dressed in black silk, with a white cap and apron."}

