          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ed6721e8209a6f0227f554f6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After some time, twelve o\u2019clock boomed from the tall tower at Westminster. With every stroke of the deep bell, the night seemed to shake. Then the railway lights went out, leaving one lonely lamp shining like a large ruby on a giant mast, and the city\u2019s roar grew quieter.\\n\\nAt two o\u2019clock he rose and walked toward Blackfriars. Everything looked unreal, like a strange dream. The houses across the river seemed made of darkness. It was as though silver and shadow had remade the world. The great dome of St. Paul\u2019s appeared through the dim air like a bubble.\\n\\nAs he neared Cleopatra\u2019s Needle, he saw a man leaning over the parapet. When he came closer, the man looked up, and the gaslight fell fully across his face.\\n\\nIt was Mr. Podgers, the cheiromantist! No one could mistake that fat, flabby face, the gold-rimmed spectacles, the weak, sickly smile, or the sensual mouth.\\n\\nLord Arthur stopped. A brilliant idea came to him, and he quietly crept up behind. In an instant he seized Mr. Podgers by the legs and threw him into the Thames. There was a rough oath, a heavy splash, and then silence. Lord Arthur looked anxiously over the parapet, but could see nothing of the cheiromantist except a tall hat spinning in a whirl of moonlit water. After a while it sank too, and no sign of Mr. Podgers remained. Once he thought he saw the large, misshapen figure swimming toward the bridge staircase, and a terrible sense of failure seized him. But it was only a reflection, and when the moon came out from behind a cloud, the feeling disappeared. At last he seemed to have carried out the decree of destiny. He breathed a deep sigh of relief, and Sybil\u2019s name rose to his lips.\\n\\n\u2018Have you dropped anything, sir?\u2019 a voice suddenly asked behind him.\\n\\nHe turned and saw a policeman holding a bull\u2019s-eye lantern.\\n\\n\u2018Nothing important, sergeant,\u2019 he replied with a smile. Hailing a passing hansom, he climbed in and told the driver to take him to Belgrave Square.\\n\\nFor the next few days he moved between hope and fear. At times he almost expected Mr. Podgers to walk into the room, yet at others he felt that Fate could not treat him so unfairly. Twice he went to the cheiromantist\u2019s address in West Moon Street, but he could not make himself ring the bell. He wanted certainty, but was afraid of it.\\n\\nAt last it came. He was sitting in the club\u2019s smoking-room, drinking tea and listening rather wearily to Surbiton\u2019s account of the latest comic song at the Gaiety, when the waiter entered with the evening papers.\"}"
