          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"20d96d5a925a59de93417536\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIf, however, you want one for home use, I can provide an excellent device and guarantee that you will be satisfied with the result. May I ask who it is for? If it is for the police, or anyone connected with Scotland Yard, I am afraid I can do nothing for you. English detectives are really our best friends. I have always found that, by relying on their stupidity, we can do exactly as we please. I could not spare one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d said Lord Arthur, \u201cthat it has nothing to do with the police. In fact, the clock is meant for the Dean of Chichester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear me! I had no idea you felt so strongly about religion, Lord Arthur. Few young men do these days.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid you overestimate me, Herr Winckelkopf,\u201d said Lord Arthur, blushing. \u201cThe truth is, I know nothing about theology.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen it is purely a private matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPurely private.\u201d\\n\\nHerr Winckelkopf shrugged and left the room. A few minutes later he returned with a round piece of dynamite about the size of a penny and a pretty little French clock, topped by an ormolu figure of Liberty trampling the hydra of Despotism.\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s face brightened when he saw it. \u201cThat is exactly what I want,\u201d he cried. \u201cNow tell me how it goes off.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! That is my secret,\u201d replied Herr Winckelkopf, looking at his invention with justifiable pride. \u201cTell me when you want it to explode, and I will set the mechanism for that exact moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, today is Tuesday. If you could send it at once\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is impossible. I have a great deal of important work to do for some friends of mine in Moscow. Still, I might send it tomorrow.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, that will be quite soon enough,\u201d said Lord Arthur politely, \u201cif it is delivered tomorrow night or Thursday morning. As for the explosion, let us say Friday at exactly noon. The Dean is always at home then.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFriday, at noon,\u201d repeated Herr Winckelkopf. He wrote it down in a large ledger lying on a desk near the fireplace.\\n\\n\u201cAnd now,\u201d said Lord Arthur, rising, \u201cplease tell me how much I owe you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is such a small matter, Lord Arthur, that I do not wish to charge you. The dynamite costs seven shillings and sixpence, the clock three pounds ten, and the carriage about five shillings. I am only too happy to oblige a friend of Count Rouvaloff\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut your trouble, Herr Winckelkopf?\u201d\"}"
