          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"63a9859283c5189c3f3a3052\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sydney Carton paused in the street, still unsure where to go. \u201cAt Tellson\u2019s bank at nine,\u201d he said thoughtfully. \u201cWould it be wise to show myself in the meantime? I think so. These people should know that a man like me is here; it is a sensible precaution, and may be necessary preparation. But care, care, care! Let me think it through!\u201d\\n\\nHe stopped himself from following the direction his steps had begun to take, turned once or twice through the darkening street, and considered what might follow from the idea. His first impression was confirmed. \u201cIt is best,\u201d he said at last, having made up his mind, \u201cthat these people should know that a man like me is here.\u201d Then he turned towards Saint Antoine.\\n\\nDefarge had described himself that day as the keeper of a wine-shop in the Saint Antoine suburb. Anyone who knew the city well could find his house without asking questions. After locating it, Carton left the narrower streets and ate dinner at a place serving refreshments, then fell into a deep sleep. For the first time in many years, he had no strong drink. Since the previous night, he had taken nothing but a little weak, light wine; and the night before, he had slowly poured the brandy onto Mr. Lorry\u2019s hearth, like a man who had finished with it.\\n\\nIt was seven o\u2019clock when he awoke refreshed and went out into the streets again. On his way to Saint Antoine, he stopped at a shop window with a mirror and slightly fixed his loose cravat, coat collar, and untidy hair. Then he went straight to Defarge\u2019s and entered.\\n\\nThere was no customer in the shop except Jacques Three, with his restless fingers and croaking voice. Carton had seen this man on the Jury. He stood drinking at the small counter and talking with the Defarges, husband and wife. The Vengeance joined in, as though she were a regular member of the establishment.\\n\\nCarton entered, sat down, and asked, in very poor French, for a small measure of wine. Madame Defarge gave him a careless glance, then a sharper one, and then another sharper one. At last she came over and asked what he had ordered.\\n\\nHe repeated what he had already said.\\n\\n\u201cEnglish?\u201d Madame Defarge asked, raising her dark eyebrows with interest.\\n\\nHe looked at her as though even one French word took him time to understand, then answered in the same strong foreign accent as before.\"}"
