          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b5a01f906bcb8ba95ee7daa6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Now that he had no work to occupy him, he placed the knuckles of his right hand in the hollow of his left, then the knuckles of his left hand in the hollow of his right, and then passed a hand over his bearded chin, repeating these movements in regular alternation without a moment\u2019s pause. Calling him back from the wandering state into which he always fell after speaking was like rousing a very weak person from a faint, or trying, in the hope of some revelation, to hold back the spirit of a dying man.\\n\\n\u201cDid you ask me for my name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly I did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that all?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\nWith a weary sound that was neither a sigh nor a groan, he bent over his work again, until the silence was broken once more.\\n\\n\u201cYou are not a shoemaker by trade?\u201d said Mr. Lorry, looking steadily at him.\\n\\nHis haggard eyes turned toward Defarge, as though he wished to pass the question on to him; but when no help came from that direction, they dropped to the ground and then returned to the questioner.\\n\\n\u201cI am not a shoemaker by trade? No, I was not a shoemaker by trade. I\u2014I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe drifted away again, for several minutes, continuing those measured movements of his hands all the while. At last his eyes slowly returned to the face from which they had wandered. When they settled on it, he started and continued like a sleeper suddenly awakened, returning to a subject from the previous night.\\n\\n\u201cI asked permission to teach myself, and after a long time, with great difficulty, I received it, and I have made shoes ever since.\u201d\\n\\nAs he held out his hand for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry said, still looking steadily into his face:\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette, do you remember nothing of me?\u201d\\n\\nThe shoe fell to the ground, and he sat staring fixedly at the questioner.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette\u201d\u2014Mr. Lorry placed his hand on Defarge\u2019s arm\u2014\u201cdo you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Is there no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time rising in your mind, Monsieur Manette?\u201d\\n\\nAs the captive of so many years sat staring in turn at Mr. Lorry and Defarge, some long-erased traces of an intensely active intelligence in the center of his forehead gradually pushed through the black mist that had fallen over him. They were clouded again, grew fainter, and disappeared; but they had been there.\"}"
