{"request_id":"1a7231d367b6b7ba7c4445b9","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me; I was never truly mad. I was always aware of my condition when my wild thoughts seemed to drive me toward insanity, and I revealed them to no one but silence and solitude. The people around me saw none of this. They saw only a poor girl broken in spirit, who spoke in a low, gentle voice, and from beneath whose lowered lids tears would sometimes slip, though she tried to hide them. One who loved to be alone and shrank from notice; who never smiled; oh, no! I never smiled—and that was all.

Well, I escaped. I left my guardian’s house and was never heard from again; from the letters I left and other circumstances, it was believed that I had planned to destroy myself. I was therefore sought with less care than otherwise would have been the case, and soon every trace and memory of me was lost. I left London in a small vessel bound for a port in the north of England. Now that I had succeeded in my attempt and was entirely alone, peace returned to me. The sea was calm, and the vessel moved gently onward. I sat upon the deck beneath the open sky, and thought myself transformed. I was no longer the wild, raving, and most miserable Mathilda, but a young hermitess devoted to seclusion, striving to keep her heart free from all tumult and unholy despair. The fanciful, nunlike dress I had adopted; the knowledge that my very existence was a secret known only to myself; and the solitude to which I was forever destined nourished gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze playing in my hair revived me, and I watched with quiet eyes the sunbeams glittering on the waves, and the birds chasing one another over the water, just brushing it with their feathers. I slept, too, undisturbed by dreams, and awoke refreshed to enjoy my tranquil freedom once more.

In four days we reached the harbor for which we were bound. I would not remain on the coast, but proceeded inland at once. I had already chosen the place where I would live. It would be a solitary house on a broad plain, far from every other dwelling: a place where I could see the entire horizon and wander widely without being troubled by the sight of my fellow creatures. I was not misanthropic, but I felt that the gentle current of my feelings depended upon my being alone. I settled in a vast solitude, on a dreary heath strewn with stones, among which short grass grew, with here and there a few rushes beside a little pool."}

