          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"40acc37a8dc6c8c241922664\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I have said that he was a poet: at twenty-three he published his first poem, and the entire nation greeted it with enthusiasm and delight. His good fortune never deserted him; no reputation had ever been made so quickly, and his fame was universal. The multitude praised the same poems that astonished the scholar in his study: there was not a single dissenting voice.\\n\\nIt was then, at the height of his glory, that he met Elinor. She was a young heiress of exquisite beauty, living under the care of her guardian; from the moment they were seen together, they seemed made for each other. Elinor did not possess Woodville\u2019s genius, but she was generous and noble, and elevated by her youth and by the love she inspired everywhere beyond any knowledge except that of virtue and excellence. She was lovely; her manner was frank and simple; her deep blue eyes shone with a radiance that could come only from sensibility joined with wisdom.\\n\\nThey were made for each other, and soon fell in love. Woodville felt the joy of love for the first time, while Elinor was enraptured to possess the heart of one so beautiful and celebrated among his fellow men. Could anything but unalloyed happiness come from such a union?\\n\\nWoodville was a poet\u2014every society sought him, and all eyes turned toward him alone when he appeared; but he was the son of a poor clergyman, while Elinor was a wealthy heiress. Her guardian did not object to their mutual affection: Woodville\u2019s merit was too distinguished to invite criticism on account of his lesser fortune. But her father\u2019s dying will forbade her to marry before she came of age, and her inheritance depended on her obeying this command. She had just entered her twentieth year, and she and her lover were forced to submit to the delay. Yet they were always together, and their happiness seemed almost paradisal: they studied together and made plans for their future lives; absorbing love and joy from each other\u2019s eyes and words, they scarcely complained about postponing their complete union. Woodville\u2019s fame continued to rise, and Elinor became more lovely and wise beneath the instruction of her accomplished lover.\\n\\nIn two months Elinor would be twenty-one; everything was ready for their union. How can I describe the catastrophe that followed such happiness? Yet the earth would not be the blighted, sorrowful place it is if a pair of such angelic beings had been allowed to live for each other. Search the whole world, and you will not find the perfect happiness their marriage would have brought them; the order of things established among us miserable inhabitants of earth would have had to change before such complete joy could be permitted.\"}"
