{"request_id":"bbb573c9787c8867be2e8975","rewritten_text":"Elizabeth was sorrowful and dispirited; she no longer found pleasure in her usual occupations. Every enjoyment seemed to her a sacrilege against the dead; she believed that endless grief and tears were the only fitting tribute she could offer to innocence so cruelly destroyed. She was no longer the happy girl who, in our earlier youth, had walked with me along the lake and spoken with delight of our hopes for the future. The first of those sorrows sent to loosen our ties to the earth had come upon her, and its shadowing influence had extinguished her dearest smiles.

“When I think, my dear cousin,” she said, “of the wretched death of Justine Moritz, I no longer see the world and all it contains as I once did. Before this, I regarded the accounts of vice and injustice that I read in books or heard from others as stories of ancient times or imaginary evils; at least, they seemed distant, and more familiar to reason than to the imagination. But now misery has entered my own life, and men seem to me like monsters thirsting for one another’s blood. Yet I am surely unjust. Everyone believed that poor girl guilty; and if she could have committed the crime for which she suffered, she would certainly have been the most depraved of human beings. To murder, for the sake of a few jewels, the son of her benefactor and friend—a child she had nursed from birth and seemed to love as though he were her own! I could not consent to the death of any human being, but I would certainly have thought such a creature unfit to remain among men. But she was innocent. I know it; I feel it: she was innocent. You believe the same, and that confirms me. Alas, Victor, when falsehood can resemble truth so closely, who can ever feel secure in happiness? I feel as though I were walking along the edge of a precipice, toward which thousands are pressing and trying to drive me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, and the murderer has escaped; he walks freely through the world, perhaps even respected. Yet if I were condemned to die on the scaffold for those same crimes, I would not exchange places with such a wretch.”

I listened to her words in the deepest agony. I was not the murderer in fact, but in effect I was the true one. Elizabeth saw my anguish in my face and, gently taking my hand, said, “My dearest friend, you must compose yourself. These events have affected me—God knows how deeply—but I am not as miserable as you are.”"}

