          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b43d52b16ac77e4eda96f7b1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNow,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, gently placing his hand on the Doctor\u2019s arm again, after a brief silence on both sides, \u201cwhat would you attribute this attack to?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe,\u201d replied Doctor Manette, \u201cthat there had been a powerful and extraordinary revival of the chain of thought and memory that first caused the illness. Some intense associations of an extremely distressing nature were vividly brought back, I think. It is likely that a fear had long been hidden in his mind: that those associations might return\u2014under certain circumstances, perhaps, or on a particular occasion. He tried unsuccessfully to prepare himself; perhaps the effort to prepare made him less able to endure it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould he remember what happened during the relapse?\u201d Mr. Lorry asked, with understandable hesitation.\\n\\nThe Doctor looked miserably around the room, shook his head, and answered in a low voice, \u201cNot at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd as for the future?\u201d Mr. Lorry prompted.\\n\\n\u201cAs for the future,\u201d said the Doctor, regaining his firmness, \u201cI should have great hope. Since Heaven, in its mercy, restored him so quickly, I should have great hope. He yielded beneath the pressure of something complicated, long feared, dimly anticipated, and resisted for a long time; and, recovering after the cloud had broken and passed, I should hope that the worst was over.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well! That is good news. I am thankful!\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI am thankful!\u201d repeated the Doctor, bowing his head reverently.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two other matters,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cabout which I am anxious to be informed. May I continue?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou cannot do your friend a greater service.\u201d The Doctor offered him his hand.\\n\\n\u201cThen, the first. He is studious by nature and unusually energetic; he devotes himself with great enthusiasm to gaining professional knowledge, conducting experiments, and many other pursuits. Now, does he do too much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think not. It may be the nature of his mind to require constant occupation in an unusual degree. That may be partly natural to him and partly the result of suffering. The less he occupied himself with healthy matters, the more danger there would be of his mind turning in an unhealthy direction. He may have observed this in himself and discovered it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are certain that he is not under too much strain?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe I am quite certain.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Manette, if he were overworked now\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lorry, I doubt whether that would be easy. There has been violent pressure in one direction, and it requires a counterweight.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cForgive me, as a persistent man of business. Suppose for a moment that he was overworked; would it reveal itself through some recurrence of this disorder?\u201d\"}"
