          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bdcd1b02147a49ed77a3cee1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself; all the work of maintaining a slow and unsatisfactory correspondence with Elizabeth fell to Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had been over only a short while when Captain and Mrs. Harville and Captain Benwick joined them. They had arranged to take their final walk around Lyme together. They ought to leave for Uppercross by one, and until then they meant to stay together and outdoors for as long as possible.\\n\\nAnne found Captain Benwick drawing near her as soon as they were properly out in the street. Their conversation the previous evening had not discouraged him from seeking her company again; and they walked together for some time, speaking as before of Mr. Scott and Lord Byron, still as unable as before\u2014and as unable as any two readers\u2014to agree exactly about the merits of either, until something caused an almost general rearrangement among the party, and Captain Harville was beside her instead of Captain Benwick.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d he said, speaking rather quietly, \u201cyou have done a good deed by making that poor fellow talk so much. I wish he could have such company more often. I know it is bad for him to be shut up as he is; but what can we do? We cannot separate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI can easily believe that to be impossible; but perhaps, in time\u2014we know what time does in every case of grief, and you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend may still be called a young mourner\u2014only last summer, as I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, quite true,\u201d he said with a deep sigh, \u201conly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd perhaps he did not know until rather later.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week of August, when he came home from the Cape, having just been appointed to the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, dreading to hear of him; he sent in letters, but the Grappler was under orders for Portsmouth. The news would have to follow him there, but who was to tell him? Not I. I would sooner have been hanged from the yard-arm. No one could do it but that good fellow\u201d\u2014he pointed to Captain Wentworth. \u201cThe Laconia had arrived at Plymouth the week before, with no danger of being sent to sea again. He took his chance with the rest; applied for leave of absence, but without waiting for the reply, travelled night and day until he reached Portsmouth, rowed out to the Grappler at once, and never left the poor fellow for a week. That is what he did, and no one else could have saved poor James. You may judge, Miss Elliot, whether he is dear to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne considered the question with complete certainty, and said as much in reply as her own feelings could express, or as his seemed able to endure, for he was too deeply moved to continue the subject. When he spoke again, it was about something entirely different.\"}"
