          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"271fec49e3e45d191682c4a0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIf I can\u2019t live on that, I won\u2019t ask anyone to give me a lift. Is that satisfactory?\u201d\\n\\nThere was an enormous roar of applause, and from its midst the king\u2019s voice rose:\\n\\n\u201cRemove his bonds and set him free! Let all, high and low, rich and poor, pay him homage, for he has become the king\u2019s right hand, clothed with power and authority, and his seat is on the highest step of the throne! Now sweep away this creeping night and bring back the light and cheer, so that all the world may bless you.\u201d\\n\\nBut I said:\\n\\n\u201cThat an ordinary man should be shamed before the world is nothing; but it would dishonor the _king_ if anyone who saw his minister naked did not also see him freed from his shame. If I might ask that my clothes be brought back\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey are not suitable,\u201d the king interrupted. \u201cFetch garments of another kind; dress him like a prince!\u201d\\n\\nMy plan worked. I wanted to keep matters as they were until the eclipse became total; otherwise they would try again to make me dismiss the darkness, and of course I could not do that. Sending for the clothes bought some time, but not enough. So I needed another excuse. I said it would be only natural for the king to change his mind and regret, at least somewhat, what he had done in the excitement; therefore I would let the darkness deepen for a while, and if, after a reasonable time, the king still held to his decision, the darkness would be dismissed. Neither the king nor anyone else was satisfied with this arrangement, but I had to stand firm.\\n\\nIt grew darker and darker, blacker and blacker, while I struggled with those awkward sixth-century clothes. At last it became completely dark, and the multitude groaned in horror as the cold, uncanny night breezes moved through the place and the stars appeared and twinkled overhead. At last the eclipse was total, and I was very glad, though everyone else was miserable\u2014which was only natural. I said:\\n\\n\u201cThe king, by remaining silent, still stands by the terms.\u201d Then I raised my hands and held them there for a moment. Then I said, with the most terrible solemnity: \u201cLet the enchantment dissolve and pass away harmlessly!\u201d\\n\\nFor a moment there was no response in that profound darkness and graveyard silence. But when the sun\u2019s silver rim emerged a moment or two later, the assembly erupted in a tremendous shout and came pouring down like a flood to smother me with blessings and gratitude; and Clarence was certainly not the last of the wave.\"}"
