{"request_id":"6e36423d146e5f33a2f18f99","rewritten_text":"The stars still shone brightly when I awoke, and Taurus high in the southern sky showed that it was midnight. I had awakened from troubled dreams. I thought I had been invited to Timon’s final feast; I arrived with a keen appetite, the covers were removed, and the hot water sent up its unsatisfying steam, while I fled from the host’s anger, for he had taken Raymond’s form. To my diseased imagination, the vessels he hurled after me were filled with foul vapour, and my friend’s shape, twisted by a thousand distortions, grew into a gigantic phantom bearing the sign of pestilence upon its brow. The rising shadow grew and grew, filling the adamantine vault that arched above, sustained, and enclosed the world, and seeming at last to strain beyond it. The nightmare became torture; with a powerful effort I threw off sleep and restored reason to its accustomed functions. My first thought was Perdita. I must return to her; I must support her, drawing from despair whatever nourishment might best sustain her wounded heart, and recalling her from the wild excesses of grief through the austere laws of duty and the gentle tenderness of regret.

The stars’ position was my only guide. I turned away from the terrible ruin of the Golden City and, after great exertion, managed to free myself from its enclosure. Outside the walls I met a company of soldiers; I borrowed a horse from one of them and hurried to my sister. The plain had changed during that brief interval. The encampment had been broken up; remnants of the disbanded army gathered in small groups here and there. Every face was clouded, and every gesture expressed astonishment and dismay.

With a heavy heart I entered the palace, afraid to move forward, speak, or look. In the middle of the hall was Perdita. She sat on the marble floor, her head bowed to her breast, her hair disordered, her fingers busily entwined. She was pale as marble, and agony tightened every feature. She saw me and looked up questioningly; the faint hope in her glance was misery. The words died before I could utter them, and I felt a ghastly smile crease my lips. She understood my gesture; once more her head fell, and once more her fingers moved restlessly. At last I found my voice, but it frightened her. The unhappy girl had understood my expression, and she would not, for anything in the world, have the story of her terrible misery formed and confirmed by harsh, irrevocable words. Instead, she seemed to wish to turn my thoughts from the subject. She rose from the floor. “Hush!” she whispered. “After so much weeping, Clara is asleep; we must not disturb her.”"}

