          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"37f2a65eb65d62feda5177a0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was the beginning of February, and Anne, who had been in Bath for a month, was becoming very eager for news from Uppercross and Lyme. She wanted to hear far more than Mary had told her. It had been three weeks since she had received any news at all. She knew only that Henrietta had returned home, and that Louisa, though thought to be recovering quickly, was still in Lyme. One evening, while thinking deeply about them all, she received a letter from Mary, thicker than usual, and, to make the pleasure and surprise greater, it came with the compliments of Admiral and Mrs Croft.\\n\\nThe Crofts must be in Bath! That was interesting news. They were people toward whom her heart naturally turned.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is this?\u201d cried Sir Walter. \u201cThe Crofts have arrived in Bath? The Crofts who rent Kellynch? What have they brought you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Such letters are useful passports. They secure an introduction. Still, I should have visited Admiral Croft in any case. I know what is owed to my tenant.\u201d\\n\\nAnne could listen no longer; she could not even have said how the poor Admiral\u2019s complexion escaped notice. Her letter held all her attention. It had been started several days earlier.\\n\\n\u201cFebruary 1st.\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR ANNE,\\n\\nI will not apologize for my silence, because I know how little people think about letters in a place like Bath. You must be far too happy to care about Uppercross, which, as you know, offers very little to write about. We had a very dull Christmas; Mr and Mrs Musgrove did not give a single dinner party during the holidays. I do not count the Hayters as anyone. The holidays are finally over; I believe no children ever had such long ones. I am sure I never did. The house was cleared yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be surprised to hear that they have not gone home. Mrs Harville must be an unusual mother to keep them away so long. I cannot understand it. They are not at all well-behaved children, in my opinion; but Mrs Musgrove seems to like them just as much, if not more, than her grandchildren. What terrible weather we have had! You may not notice it in Bath, with your fine pavements, but in the country it matters greatly. No one has visited me since the second week of January, except Charles Hayter, who had been calling much more often than I wanted. Between ourselves, I think it is a great pity Henrietta did not stay in Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her a little farther from him.\u201d\"}"
