          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"63768fc8e409fb3f4484cd68\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It seemed to consist of back lanes, ditches, and small gardens, giving the impression of a somewhat dreary seclusion. Wemmick\u2019s house was a tiny wooden cottage surrounded by garden plots, with its upper part shaped and painted like a battery fitted with guns.\\n\\n\u201cMy own work,\u201d said Wemmick. \u201cLooks well; doesn\u2019t it?\u201d\\n\\nI praised it highly. I believe it was the smallest house I had ever seen, with the strangest Gothic windows (most of them sham, by a long way), and a Gothic door almost too narrow to enter.\\n\\n\u201cThat\u2019s a genuine flagstaff, you see,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cand on Sundays I hoist a real flag. Now look here. Once I\u2019ve crossed this bridge, I raise it up\u2014so\u2014and cut off communication.\u201d\\n\\nThe bridge was merely a plank, spanning a chasm about four feet across and two feet deep. Still, it was delightful to observe the pride with which he lifted it and secured it, smiling with genuine enjoyment rather than doing it mechanically.\\n\\n\u201cAt nine every night, Greenwich time,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cthe gun goes off. There he is, you see! And when you hear him fire, I think you\u2019ll call him a Stinger.\u201d\\n\\nThe cannon in question stood in a separate fortress made of lattice-work. An ingenious little tarpaulin arrangement, rather like an umbrella, sheltered it from the weather.\\n\\n\u201cThen, at the rear,\u201d said Wemmick, \u201cout of sight, so it doesn\u2019t interfere with the fortification idea\u2014for it\u2019s a rule of mine that if you have an idea, you should carry it through and maintain it\u2014I don\u2019t know whether you agree\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI said that I certainly did.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014At the rear, there\u2019s a pig, along with fowls and rabbits; then I put together my own little frame, you see, and grow cucumbers. At supper you\u2019ll see what kind of salad I can produce. So, sir,\u201d said Wemmick, smiling once more, though quite seriously as he shook his head, \u201cif you imagine the little place under siege, it could last a devil of a long time for provisions.\u201d\\n\\nHe then led me to a bower about a dozen yards away, though it was reached by such cleverly winding paths that it took a considerable time to arrive. In this retreat our glasses had already been laid out. Our punch was cooling in an ornamental lake beside which the bower stood. The water, with an island in its centre that might have supplied the supper salad, was circular, and he had built a fountain in it. When a small mill was set in motion and a cork removed from a pipe, the fountain played with such force that it thoroughly wetted the back of your hand.\"}"
