{"request_id":"7b2b6dcac42c330d1ed1539b","rewritten_text":"Among the other public buildings in a certain town, which for many reasons it is wiser not to name, and to which I will give no invented name, there stands one found in most towns, large or small: namely, a workhouse. In this workhouse was born, on a day and date I need not repeat, since they cannot matter to the reader at this point, the child whose name appears at the head of this chapter.

For a long time after the parish surgeon brought him into this world of sorrow and trouble, it was very doubtful whether the child would live long enough to receive a name. If he had not survived, these memoirs would probably never have appeared; or, if they had, they would have filled only a couple of pages and earned the remarkable distinction of being the shortest and most accurate biography in the literature of any age or country.

Although I would not claim that being born in a workhouse is the luckiest or most desirable event that can happen to a human being, I do say that, in Oliver Twist’s case, it was the best thing that could possibly have happened to him. The truth is, Oliver had great difficulty beginning the business of breathing—a troublesome habit, though custom has made it necessary for comfortable living. For some time he lay gasping on a small flock mattress, wavering between this world and the next, with the balance clearly favouring the latter. If, during that short period, he had been surrounded by careful grandmothers, worried aunts, experienced nurses, and deeply learned doctors, he would certainly have been killed at once. Fortunately, the only people present were a poor old woman, made rather confused by an unusual amount of beer, and a parish surgeon who handled such cases by contract. Oliver and Nature fought the matter out between them. In the end, after a few struggles, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and announced to the workhouse inmates that the parish had acquired a new burden by raising as loud a cry as could reasonably be expected from a male infant who had not possessed that useful organ, a voice, for more than three minutes and a quarter."}

