          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"78c63ce90dd29340bd0f474c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now come to my own story. There is little to tell about the early part of my life, and I will be brief; but I must be permitted to dwell somewhat on my childhood, so that it may be understood how, when one hope failed, all life would become a blank; and how, when the only affection I was allowed to cherish was destroyed, my existence was extinguished with it.\\n\\nI have said that my aunt was very unlike my father. I believe that, without the slightest trace of an evil nature, she possessed the coldest heart that ever beat in a human breast: it was entirely incapable of affection. She took me under her protection because she believed it her duty; but she had lived alone for so long, undisturbed by the noise and chatter of children, that she could not bear to have her quiet disturbed. She had never married; and for the last five years had lived in complete solitude on an estate inherited through her mother, on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland. In his letters, my father had expressed the wish that she would live with me at his family home, situated in beautiful countryside near Richmond in Yorkshire. She refused this proposal, but as soon as she had settled the affairs that her brother\u2019s departure had placed in her care, she left England and took me with her to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nThe care of me as a baby, and afterward until I had reached my eighth year, fell to a servant of my mother\u2019s who had accompanied us into retirement for that purpose. I was kept in a distant part of the house and saw my aunt only at fixed times. This happened twice each day: once around noon, when she came to my nursery, and once after dinner, when I was brought to her. She never caressed me, and throughout the time I remained in the room she seemed afraid that some childish impulse might annoy her. My good nurse always instructed me with the greatest care before allowing me to enter the parlor\u2014and the fear inspired by my aunt\u2019s cold looks and few, restrained words was so great that I rarely failed in my lessons or broke the exemplary stillness I had been taught to maintain during these brief visits.\\n\\nUnder my good nurse\u2019s care, I ran freely through our park and the neighboring fields. As the child of the deepest love, I showed from my earliest years the greatest sensitivity of feeling. I cannot express how passionately I loved everything, even the inanimate objects that surrounded me.\"}"
