im was like playing an exquisite violin. He responded to every touch and tremor of the bow.... There was something profoundly fascinating in exercising influence. No other occupation resembled it. To cast one\u2019s soul into some gracious form and allow it to remain there briefly; to hear one\u2019s own intellectual opinions returned with all the added music of youth and passion; to pass one\u2019s temperament into another as if it were a delicate fluid or unfamiliar perfume: there was genuine pleasure in that\u2014perhaps the most satisfying pleasure left to us in an age as narrow and vulgar as our own, an age grossly sensual in its pleasures and grossly ordinary in its ambitions.... This boy was a wonderful type, too, whom by such a curious chance he had encountered in Basil\u2019s studio\u2014or, at least, could be shaped into a wonderful type.\"}"
