          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5a46f6325bec91ea0232d7b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Deprived of ordinary resources, the analyst casts himself into the spirit of his adversary, identifies himself with him, and not infrequently perceives at once the only means\u2014sometimes absurdly simple ones\u2014by which he may lure him into error or hasten him into miscalculation.\\n\\nWhist has long been remarked for its influence upon what is called the calculating faculty; and men of the highest intellectual order have been known to find in it an apparently inexplicable pleasure, while rejecting chess as frivolous. There is certainly no similar pursuit that so severely exercises the power of analysis. The finest chess-player in Christendom _may_ be little more than the finest player of chess; but skill at whist implies an ability to succeed in all those more serious enterprises in which one mind contends with another. By skill, I mean that perfection of the game which embraces an understanding of _all_ the sources from which legitimate advantage can be obtained. These are not merely numerous but varied, and often lie in regions of thought wholly inaccessible to ordinary comprehension. To watch closely is to remember accurately; and in this respect the concentrated chess-player will perform well at whist, while Hoyle\u2019s rules (being founded upon the game\u2019s simple mechanism) are generally easy enough to understand. Thus a retentive memory, together with adherence to \u201cthe book,\u201d is commonly considered the whole of good play. Yet the analyst\u2019s ability appears in matters beyond the boundaries of mere rule. He silently makes countless observations and deductions. His companions may do the same; but the difference in the amount of information acquired lies less in the soundness of the inference than in the quality of the observation. The essential knowledge is knowing _what_ to observe. Our player limits himself in no way; and because the game is his object, he does not dismiss deductions drawn from matters outside it. He studies his partner\u2019s face, comparing it closely with the countenance of each opponent. He examines how the cards are arranged in every hand; often counting trump by trump and honor by honor, through the looks their holders cast upon one another. He marks every change of expression as the play advances, collecting a store of thought from differences showing certainty, surprise, triumph, or disappointment. From the way a trick is gathered, he decides whether the person who took it can win another in that suit. He detects a card played as a feint by the manner in which it is cast upon the table. A careless or unintended word; the accidental dropping or turning of a card, together with the concern or indifference shown about hiding it; the counting of tricks and the order in which they are placed; confusion, hesitation, eagerness, or alarm\u2014all furnish his seemingly intuitive perception with signs of the actual state of affairs.\"}"
