          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c8c0a1ee160b345e1ddf34a3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I must give you warning that I shall tell Sybil everything. She is lunching with me to-morrow to discuss bonnets, and if Mr. Podgers discovers that you have a violent temper, a tendency toward gout, or a wife residing in Bayswater, I shall certainly inform her of it all.\u2019\\n\\nLord Arthur smiled and shook his head. \u2018I am not afraid,\u2019 he replied. \u2018Sybil knows me as thoroughly as I know her.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Ah! I am rather sorry to hear you say that. The soundest foundation for marriage is a mutual misunderstanding. No, I am not cynical at all; I have simply acquired experience, which amounts to much the same thing. Mr. Podgers, Lord Arthur Savile is longing to have his hand read. Do not tell him that he is engaged to one of the loveliest girls in London, for that was printed in the _Morning Post_ a month ago.\\n\\n\u2018My dear Lady Windermere,\u2019 exclaimed the Marchioness of Jedburgh, \u2018please let Mr. Podgers remain a little longer. He has just said that I ought to go on the stage, and I am deeply interested.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018If he has told you that, Lady Jedburgh, I shall certainly carry him off. Come here at once, Mr. Podgers, and read Lord Arthur\u2019s hand.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well,\u2019 said Lady Jedburgh, making a small _moue_ as she rose from the sofa, \u2018if I am not permitted to go on the stage, I must at least be permitted to join the audience.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Naturally; we are all going to be in the audience,\u2019 said Lady Windermere. \u2018And now, Mr. Podgers, be certain to tell us something pleasant. Lord Arthur is one of my particular favourites.\u2019\\n\\nBut when Mr. Podgers looked at Lord Arthur\u2019s hand, he became strangely pale and remained silent. A shiver appeared to run through him, while his large bushy eyebrows jerked convulsively, in the peculiar, irritating fashion they assumed when he was perplexed. Then several great drops of sweat appeared upon his yellow brow, like poisonous dew, and his fat fingers became cold and clammy.\\n\\nLord Arthur could not help observing these unusual signs of agitation, and for the first time in his life he felt afraid. His first impulse was to flee the room, but he mastered it. Whatever the truth might be, it was better to know the worst than to remain trapped in this dreadful uncertainty.\\n\\n\u2018I am waiting, Mr. Podgers,\u2019 he said.\\n\\n\u2018We are all waiting,\u2019 cried Lady Windermere in her brisk, impatient fashion, but the cheiromantist gave no answer.\\n\\n\u2018I believe Arthur is going on the stage,\u2019 said Lady Jedburgh, \u2018and that, after your scolding, Mr. Podgers is afraid to tell him so.\u2019\"}"
