{"request_id":"66b7445dc57c07d50acf23d2","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a gentle breeze quickly fanned it into flame. I noticed this as well and made a fan from branches, which revived the embers when they were nearly extinguished. When night came again, I was pleased to find that the fire gave light as well as heat, and that discovering this element was useful for preparing food. Some of the scraps the travelers had left had been roasted and tasted far more savory than the berries I gathered from the trees. I therefore tried to prepare my food in the same way, placing it on the glowing embers. I found that the berries were spoiled by this treatment, while the nuts and roots were greatly improved.

“Food, however, became scarce, and I often spent the entire day searching in vain for a few acorns to ease the pangs of hunger. When I realized this, I resolved to leave the place I had inhabited until then and seek one where my few needs could be more easily met. During this migration, I deeply mourned the loss of the fire I had gained by accident and did not know how to reproduce. I spent several hours seriously considering this problem, but at last had to abandon every attempt to replace it. Wrapping myself in my cloak, I crossed the forest toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days and finally discovered open country. A heavy snowfall had occurred the night before, and the fields were covered in one unbroken white expanse. The sight was bleak, and my feet were chilled by the cold, damp substance covering the ground.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I longed for food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on a rise, which had doubtless been built for the use of some shepherd. This was a new sight to me, and I examined the structure with great curiosity. Finding the door open, I entered. An old man sat inside near a fire, over which he was preparing his breakfast. He turned at the sound, and when he saw me, he shrieked loudly and, leaving the hut, ran across the fields with a speed his weakened body scarcely seemed capable of. His appearance, unlike anything I had ever seen before, and his flight surprised me somewhat. But I was enchanted by the hut; snow and rain could not enter it, the ground was dry, and it seemed to me then as exquisite and divine a refuge as Pandæmonium seemed to the demons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire."}

