          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"77b2f5a200bf0b8b86c3dc4e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot, of Kellynch Hall in Somersetshire, never picked up any book for his own amusement except the Baronetage. There he found occupation for an idle hour and comfort in a troubled one; there his faculties were stirred to admiration and respect as he contemplated the small surviving number of the earliest patents; there any unpleasant feelings caused by domestic affairs naturally changed into pity and contempt as he turned through the nearly endless creations of the last century; and there, if every other page failed him, he could read his own history with an interest that never diminished. This was the page at which his favourite volume always opened:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married, July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, in the county of Gloucester, by whom (who died in 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a stillborn son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nThat was exactly how the paragraph had originally come from the printer; but Sir Walter had improved it by adding, for his own and his family\u2019s information, these words after the date of Mary\u2019s birth: \u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq. of Uppercross, in the county of Somerset,\u201d and by inserting with perfect accuracy the day of the month on which he had lost his wife.\\n\\nThen came the history and rise of the ancient and respectable family, in the usual language: how it had first settled in Cheshire; how it was mentioned by Dugdale; how its members had served as high sheriff, represented a borough in three successive parliaments, shown loyalty, and received the dignity of a baronet in the first year of Charles II, along with all the Marys and Elizabeths they had married. Altogether, it filled two handsome duodecimo pages and ended with the arms and motto: \u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset,\u201d followed once more by Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting:\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity was the beginning and end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character: vanity of appearance and of position. He had been remarkably handsome in his youth and, at fifty-four, was still a very fine man. Few women could care more about their appearance than he did, nor could the valet of any newly made lord be more pleased with the place he occupied in society. He considered the blessing of beauty inferior only to the blessing of a baronetcy; and Sir Walter Elliot, who possessed both gifts, was the constant object of his deepest respect and devotion.\\n\\nHis good looks and rank had one legitimate claim on his attachment, since to them he must have owed a wife of far superior character to anything his own deserved.\"}"
