          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5df56e97abb647b3d83c1fe7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter had secured a very fine house in Camden Place, an elevated and dignified position, suited to a man of consequence; and he and Elizabeth were both established there, greatly to their satisfaction.\\n\\nAnne entered it with a sinking heart, expecting to be confined there for many months, and saying anxiously to herself, \u201cOh! when shall I leave you again?\u201d Yet the welcome she received was unexpectedly cordial, and did her good. Her father and sister were pleased to see her, partly so that they might show her the house and its furnishings, and they greeted her kindly. When she made a fourth at dinner, her presence was remarked upon as an advantage.\\n\\nMrs Clay was very agreeable, and smiled a great deal, though her politeness and smiles seemed more habitual. Anne had always supposed that she would make the proper pretence upon her arrival, but the others\u2019 attentions were unforeseen. They were plainly in excellent spirits, and she soon heard the reasons. They had no desire to hear from her. After allowing for some compliments about being greatly missed in their former neighbourhood, which Anne could not offer, they made only a few weak enquiries before the conversation became entirely their own. Uppercross inspired no interest, and Kellynch very little: everything was Bath.\\n\\nThey were delighted to assure her that Bath had surpassed their expectations in every particular. Their house was certainly the finest in Camden Place; their drawing-rooms possessed many clear advantages over all the others they had seen or heard about, and the superiority extended equally to the manner of decoration and the taste of the furniture. Their acquaintance was eagerly sought. Everyone wished to visit them. They had declined many introductions, yet people whose names they did not know were constantly leaving cards.\\n\\nHere were abundant sources of pleasure. Could Anne be surprised that her father and sister were happy? She might not be surprised, but she had to sigh that her father felt no humiliation in the change, saw nothing to regret in the responsibilities and dignity of a resident landowner, and found so much to boast of in the small distinctions of a town; and she had to sigh, smile, and wonder as Elizabeth flung open the folding doors and walked proudly from one drawing-room into the other, boasting of their size; at the thought that the woman who had once been mistress of Kellynch Hall could find room for pride between two walls perhaps thirty feet apart.\\n\\nBut this was not all they had to make them happy. They had Mr Elliot as well.\"}"
