prudently; and I know it was his opinion\u2014whether rightly or not, I cannot of course judge\u2014that your father and sister, in their civilities and invitations, intended a match between the heir and the young lady, and that such a marriage could never satisfy his notions of wealth and independence. That was why he withdrew, I assure you. He told me the entire story. He kept nothing from me. It was curious that, having just left you in Bath, my first and chief acquaintance after marrying should be your cousin; and that, through him, I should continually hear about your father and sister.\u201d\"}"
