          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3723ed09f216183f99987f34\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This brief explanation with Mr. Knightley gave Emma considerable pleasure. It was one of the pleasant memories of the ball, which she walked around the lawn the next morning to enjoy. She was extremely glad that they had reached such a good understanding about the Eltons, and that their opinions of both husband and wife were so similar; and his praise of Harriet, his concession in her favour, was especially gratifying. The Eltons\u2019 impertinence, which for a few minutes had threatened to ruin the rest of her evening, had become the cause of some of its greatest satisfactions; and she looked forward to another happy result\u2014the cure of Harriet\u2019s infatuation. From Harriet\u2019s way of speaking about the incident before they left the ballroom, she had strong hopes. It seemed as if Harriet\u2019s eyes had suddenly been opened, enabling her to see that Mr. Elton was not the superior creature she had believed him to be. The fever was over, and Emma could have little fear that harmful politeness would quicken the pulse again. She relied on the Eltons\u2019 ill feeling to provide all the pointed neglect that might still be necessary. Harriet rational, Frank Churchill not too deeply in love, and Mr. Knightley unwilling to quarrel with her\u2014how happy a summer must lie before her!\\n\\nShe was not to see Frank Churchill that morning. He had told her that he could not allow himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, since he was expected home by midday. She did not regret it.\\n\\nHaving arranged all these matters, examined them from every angle, and set them right, she was just turning toward the house, her spirits renewed for the demands of the two little boys as well as their grandfather, when the great iron sweep-gate opened, and two people entered whom she had never expected to see together\u2014Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning on his arm\u2014Harriet herself! One moment was enough to convince her that something extraordinary had occurred. Harriet looked pale and frightened, while he was trying to comfort her. The iron gates and the front door were not twenty yards apart; all three were soon in the hall, and Harriet immediately sank into a chair and fainted.\\n\\nA young lady who faints must be revived; questions must be answered, and surprises explained. Such events are deeply interesting, but their suspense cannot last long. Within a few minutes Emma knew the whole story.\\n\\nMiss Smith and Miss Bickerton, another parlour boarder at Mrs. Goddard\u2019s who had also attended the ball, had gone out walking together and taken the Richmond road, which, though it appeared public enough to be safe, had soon alarmed them. About half a mile beyond Highbury, after a sudden turn, and heavily shaded by elms on both sides, it became secluded for a considerable distance; and when the young ladies had gone some way along it, they suddenly saw, a short distance ahead, on a wider patch of grass beside the road, a group of gypsies.\"}"
