          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ad84e1821018697a0afd394c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His understanding and temperament, though different from her own, would have fulfilled all her hopes. Their marriage would have benefited them both: her ease and liveliness might have softened his mind and improved his manners; while his judgment, knowledge, and experience of the world would have given her benefits of even greater value.\\n\\nBut no such happy marriage could now show the admiring crowd what true married happiness was. A very different marriage, making the other impossible, was soon to take place in their family.\\n\\nShe could not imagine how Wickham and Lydia were to be supported in even tolerable independence. But she could easily guess how little lasting happiness could belong to a couple brought together only because their passions were stronger than their virtue.\\n\\nMr. Gardiner soon wrote to his brother again. He briefly answered Mr. Bennet\u2019s thanks, assuring him of his eagerness to promote the welfare of any member of his family, and ended by begging that the subject never be mentioned to him again. The main purpose of his letter was to tell them that Mr. Wickham had decided to leave the militia.\\n\\n\u201cIt was very much my wish that he should do so,\u201d he added, \u201cas soon as his marriage was settled. And I think you will agree that leaving that regiment is highly advisable, both for his sake and my niece\u2019s. Mr. Wickham intends to join the Regulars; and among his former friends there are still some able and willing to help him in the army. He has been promised an ensigncy in General----\u2019s regiment, now stationed in the north. It is an advantage that it should be so far from this part of the kingdom. He gives fair promises; and I hope that, among different people, where each will have a reputation to protect, they will both behave more prudently. I have written to Colonel Forster, informing him of our present arrangements and asking 