          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f8de2c575c5b28a76331d11e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter?\u201d demanded Oliver.\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d replied the Dodger. \u201cDo you see that old fellow at the bookstall?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe old gentleman across the street?\u201d said Oliver. \u201cYes, I see him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019ll do,\u201d said the Dodger.\\n\\n\u201cA first-rate mark,\u201d observed Master Charley Bates.\\n\\nOliver looked from one to the other in the greatest surprise, but he was given no chance to ask questions; the two boys had already crossed the road stealthily and slipped in behind the old gentleman toward whom his attention had been directed. Oliver followed a few paces behind them and, uncertain whether to go forward or turn back, stood watching in silent amazement.\\n\\nThe old gentleman looked thoroughly respectable, with a powdered head and gold spectacles. He wore a bottle-green coat with a black velvet collar and white trousers, and carried a smart bamboo cane beneath his arm. He had picked up a book from the stall and stood there reading as intently as though he were seated in his own study, in his favorite chair. It was quite possible that he imagined himself there; for his absorption made it clear that he noticed neither the bookstall, nor the street, nor the boys, nor anything at all except the book itself. He read straight through it, turning the page when he reached the bottom, beginning at the top line of the next, and continuing steadily with the greatest interest and eagerness.\\n\\nWhat were Oliver\u2019s horror and alarm, as he stood a few paces away with his eyes opened as wide as they could go, when he saw the Dodger thrust his hand into the old gentleman\u2019s pocket and pull out a handkerchief? He watched him pass it to Charley Bates, and then saw them both run around the corner at full speed!\\n\\nIn an instant, the whole mystery of the handkerchiefs, watches, jewels, and the Jew rushed into the boy\u2019s mind.\\n\\nFor a moment he stood there, his blood tingling through every vein with terror until he felt as though he were standing in a fire. Then, confused and frightened, he ran; without knowing what he was doing, he fled as fast as his feet could carry him.\\n\\nIt all happened within a minute. At the very moment Oliver began to run, the old gentleman put his hand into his pocket, discovered that his handkerchief was gone, and turned sharply around. Seeing the boy racing away at such speed, he naturally concluded that Oliver was the thief. Shouting \u201cStop, thief!\u201d with all his strength, he ran after him, still holding the book.\"}"
