          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"63be236c4d2d6776058e5a38\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhy, you don\u2019t mean to say\u2014\u201d my sister began.\\n\\n\u201cYes, I do, Mum,\u201d said Pumblechook; \u201cbut wait a little. Go on, Joseph. Well done! Go on!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat would the company present say,\u201d Joe continued, \u201cto twenty pounds?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHandsome would be the word,\u201d my sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cWell, then,\u201d said Joe, \u201cit\u2019s more than twenty pounds.\u201d\\n\\nThat abject hypocrite, Pumblechook, nodded once more and said, with a patronizing laugh, \u201cIt\u2019s more than that, Mum. Good again! Follow her up, Joseph!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen, to finish the matter,\u201d said Joe, handing the bag delightedly to my sister, \u201cit\u2019s five-and-twenty pounds.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s five-and-twenty pounds, Mum,\u201d echoed that basest of swindlers, Pumblechook, rising to shake her hand; \u201cand it\u2019s no more than your merits (as I said when my opinion was requested), and I wish you joy of the money!\u201d\\n\\nHad the villain stopped there, his case would have been bad enough, but he deepened his guilt by proceeding to take me into custody, with a patronage that made all his former criminality seem insignificant.\\n\\n\u201cNow you see, Joseph and wife,\u201d said Pumblechook, taking me by the arm above the elbow, \u201cI\u2019m one of those who always carry through whatever they begin. This boy must be bound, immediately. That\u2019s _my_ way. Bound immediately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGoodness knows, Uncle Pumblechook,\u201d said my sister, clutching the money, \u201cwe\u2019re deeply indebted to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever mind me, Mum,\u201d returned that diabolical cornchandler. \u201cA pleasure is a pleasure everywhere. But this boy, you know; we must have him bound. I said I would see to it\u2014to tell you the truth.\u201d\\n\\nThe Justices were sitting in the nearby Town Hall, and we went over at once to have me bound apprentice to Joe in the presence of the Magistrates. I say we went over, but Pumblechook pushed me along exactly as though I had just picked a pocket or set fire to a hayrick; indeed, the general belief in Court was that I had been caught red-handed. As Pumblechook shoved me ahead of him through the crowd, I heard some people say, \u201cWhat\u2019s he done?\u201d and others, \u201cHe\u2019s a young \u2019un, too, but looks bad, don\u2019t he?\u201d One person with a mild and benevolent face even gave me a tract decorated with a woodcut of a malicious young man equipped with a complete sausage-shop of fetters, and entitled TO BE READ IN MY CELL.\\n\\nThe Hall seemed a strange place to me, with pews higher than those in a church\u2014and people leaning over them to watch\u2014and mighty Justices, one with a powdered head, reclining in chairs with folded arms, or taking snuff, or sleeping, or writing, or reading the newspapers\u2014and several shining black portraits on the walls, which my unartistic eye saw as a mixture of hardbake and sticking-plaster.\"}"
