{"request_id":"9f576bde091779a9b6a21c79","rewritten_text":"The next morning, when the Otis family met for breakfast, they discussed the ghost at length. The United States Minister was naturally a little annoyed that his gift had not been accepted. “I have no wish,” he said, “to hurt the ghost personally, and I must say that, considering how long he has lived in the house, it is not at all polite of him to throw pillows at me”—a very fair remark, at which, I am sorry to say, the twins burst out laughing. “On the other hand,” he continued, “if he really refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we shall have to take away his chains. It would be impossible to sleep with such a noise outside the bedrooms.”

For the rest of the week, however, they were not disturbed. The only thing that attracted attention was the bloodstain on the library floor, which kept appearing again. This was certainly strange, since Mr. Otis always locked the door at night and kept the windows firmly barred. The stain’s chameleon-like colors also caused much comment. Some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian red; then it became vermilion, then a deep purple. Once, when they came downstairs for family prayers, following the simple rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they found it bright emerald green. These changing colors amused the family greatly, and they freely placed bets about them each evening. The only person who did not enjoy the joke was little Virginia. For some unexplained reason, she was always very upset by the bloodstain, and she nearly cried on the morning it turned emerald green.

The ghost appeared for the second time on Sunday night. Soon after the family had gone to bed, they were suddenly frightened by a terrible crash in the hall. When they ran downstairs, they found that a large suit of old armor had come loose from its stand and fallen onto the stone floor. The Canterville ghost was sitting in a high-backed chair, rubbing his knees with a look of intense pain. The twins had brought their pea-shooters with them, and immediately fired two pellets at him, with the accurate aim that can only come from long practice against a writing master. Meanwhile, the United States Minister pointed his revolver at the ghost and, following Californian etiquette, ordered him to hold up his hands. The ghost sprang up with a furious scream and swept through them like mist, blowing out Washington Otis’s candle as he passed and leaving them all in complete darkness."}

