          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"68f04781ca23263404bbadfd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But my father was at my side to comfort me and banish painful memories with brighter hopes: I thought it sweet to grieve, since he might dry my tears.\\n\\nAgain, he turned my thoughts from sorrow by comparing it with his own despair when he lost my mother. Even then I shuddered at the picture he painted of his passions: he possessed a poet\u2019s imagination, and when he described the whirlwind that had torn through his feelings, he gave his words such a vivid stamp of life that I believed him even as I trembled. I wondered how he could ever have returned to the duties of life after his wild thoughts had seemed to bring him into kinship with the unearthly; as he spoke, the ideas he conveyed were so tremendous that it seemed the human heart was far too limited to contain them. His feelings appeared more suited to a spirit dwelling amid earthquakes and volcanoes than to one enclosed in a mortal body and human form. But these were only memories; he had changed since then. Now he was all affection and gentleness; and whenever I looked up at him in wonder as he spoke, the smile upon his lips assured me that his heart was filled with the mildest passions.\\n\\nTwo months after my aunt\u2019s death, we moved to London, where my father guided me toward deeper studies than those that had previously occupied me. My progress delighted him; he remained with me throughout my studies and helped with, or took part in, every lesson. We saw much of society, and no day passed without my father trying to adorn it with some fresh pleasure. The tender attachment he felt for me, and the love and reverence with which I returned it, cast a charm over every moment. The hours passed slowly, for every minute was put to use; we lived more in a week than many people do in several months, and the variety and novelty of our pleasures gave each one its relish.\\n\\nWe were constantly making excursions together. Whether to visit lovely scenery, admire fine paintings, or sometimes merely to seek whatever amusement chance might offer, I was always happy when near my father. I regretted whenever a third person joined us; yet if I glanced uneasily toward my father, his eyes would rest upon me, shining with tenderness, and instantly restore joy to my heart. O, hours of profound delight! Brief as you were, memory makes you last as long as an entire life when viewed through the veil of grief that rose immediately afterward, as though to hide you from my sight.\"}"
