{"request_id":"b624fde6144022b6d7d7ad90","rewritten_text":"We finally lightened the box by removing two-thirds of its contents, after which, with some difficulty, we managed to lift it from the hole. We placed the articles we had taken out among the brambles, and left the dog to guard them, with Jupiter’s strict orders neither to leave the spot for any reason nor to open his mouth until we returned. We then hurried home with the chest, reaching the hut safely, though after exhausting labor, at one o’clock in the morning. As worn out as we were, we could do nothing more immediately. We rested until two and ate supper; then we set out for the hills, armed with three sturdy sacks that, fortunately, were on the premises. A little before four we reached the pit, divided the remaining booty among us as equally as possible, and, leaving the holes unfilled, started back to the hut. There, for the second time, we deposited our golden burdens just as the first faint streaks of dawn appeared above the treetops in the East.

We were now completely exhausted, but the intense excitement of the moment would not let us rest. After an uneasy sleep lasting three or four hours, we rose, apparently by mutual agreement, to examine our treasure.

The chest had been filled to the brim, and we spent the entire day and most of the following night inspecting its contents. Nothing had been arranged or organized; everything had been piled in at random. After sorting it all carefully, we discovered that we possessed even greater wealth than we had first imagined. In coins there was somewhat more than four hundred and fifty thousand dollars, the value of the pieces being estimated as accurately as possible from the tables of the period. There was not a single piece of silver. Everything was gold, ancient and varied—French, Spanish, and German money, along with a few English guineas and some counters, specimens of which we had never seen before. Several coins were unusually large and heavy, but so worn that we could make nothing of their inscriptions. There was no American money. We found the jewels more difficult to value. There were one hundred and ten diamonds, some exceedingly large and fine, and none of them small; eighteen rubies of remarkable brilliance; three hundred and ten emeralds, all extremely beautiful; and twenty-one sapphires, together with an opal. All these stones had been broken from their settings and thrown loose into the chest. The settings themselves, which we found among the other gold, seemed to have been battered with hammers, as though to prevent their identification."}

