          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3178bb8943b9aebd06d3aa2f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNothing but a tick.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere did you get him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOut in the woods.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat will you take for him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. I don\u2019t want to sell him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right. It\u2019s a mighty small tick, anyway.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, anyone can catch a tick that doesn\u2019t belong to them. I\u2019m satisfied with it. It\u2019s good enough for me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSure, there are ticks everywhere. I could have a thousand if I wanted to.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, why don\u2019t you? Because you know perfectly well you can\u2019t. This is an early tick, I reckon. It\u2019s the first one I\u2019ve seen this year.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSay, Huck\u2014I\u2019ll give you my tooth for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nTom took out a scrap of paper and carefully unrolled it. Huckleberry looked at it wistfully. The temptation was very strong. At last he said:\\n\\n\u201cIs it genuine?\u201d\\n\\nTom lifted his lip and showed the gap.\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right,\u201d said Huckleberry, \u201cit\u2019s a trade.\u201d\\n\\nTom shut the tick inside the percussion-cap box that had recently served as the pinchbug\u2019s prison, and the boys went their separate ways, each feeling richer than before.\\n\\nWhen Tom reached the small, isolated frame schoolhouse, he walked in briskly, with the manner of someone who had come as quickly and honestly as possible. He hung his hat on a peg and dropped into his seat with businesslike speed. The master, enthroned in his large splint-bottom armchair, had been dozing, lulled by the sleepy hum of study. The interruption woke him.\\n\\n\u201cThomas Sawyer!\u201d\\n\\nTom knew that when his full name was spoken, trouble was coming.\\n\\n\u201cSir!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome up here. Now, sir, why are you late again, as usual?\u201d\\n\\nTom was about to take refuge in a lie when he saw two long tails of yellow hair hanging down a back he recognized through love\u2019s electric sympathy; beside that figure was the only vacant place on the girls\u2019 side of the schoolhouse. He said at once:\\n\\n\u201c_I stopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn!_\u201d\\n\\nThe master\u2019s pulse seemed to stop, and he stared helplessly. The hum of study died away. The pupils wondered whether this reckless boy had lost his mind. The master said:\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2014you did what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cStopped to talk with Huckleberry Finn.\u201d\\n\\nThere was no mistaking the words.\\n\\n\u201cThomas Sawyer, this is the most astonishing confession I have ever heard. A mere whipping will not answer for this offense. Take off your jacket.\u201d\\n\\nThe master\u2019s arm continued until it was tired and the supply of switches had been noticeably reduced. Then came the order:\\n\\n\u201cNow, sir, go and sit with the girls! And let this be a warning to you.\u201d\\n\\nThe titter that rippled around the room seemed to embarrass the boy, but in truth that effect came more from his reverent awe of his unknown idol and the fearful delight contained in his extraordinary good fortune.\u201d\"}"
