          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7128bb25a3b6a8ebb85af254\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, your father, of course, may spare you, if your mother can. Daughters are never so important to a father. And if you stay another _month_ in full, I shall be able to take one of you as far as London, since I am going there in early June for a week; and as Dawson has no objection to the barouche-box, there will be ample room for one of you\u2014and, indeed, if the weather happens to be cool, I should not object to taking you both, since neither of you is large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind, madam; but I believe we must follow our original plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine appeared resigned. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I always speak my mind, and I cannot endure the thought of two young women travelling post by themselves. It is highly improper. You must find some way to send someone. I have the greatest dislike in the world to that sort of thing. Young women should always be properly guarded and attended, according to their position in life. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I insisted that two male servants accompany her. Miss Darcy, the daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley, and Lady Anne could not have appeared properly in any other manner. I am extremely attentive to such matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am glad I thought to mention it; for it would truly be discreditable to _you_ to let them go alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle is sending a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle! He keeps a male servant, does he? I am very glad you have someone who considers such matters. Where will you change horses? Oh, Bromley, of course. If you mention my name at the Bell, they will attend to you.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had many other questions about their journey; and since she did not answer them all herself, Elizabeth had to pay attention\u2014which she considered fortunate; for, with her mind so occupied, she might otherwise have forgotten where she was. Reflection had to be saved for solitary hours: whenever she was alone, she surrendered to it as her greatest relief; and not a day passed without a solitary walk, during which she could indulge in all the pleasure of painful memories.\\n\\nShe was well on her way to knowing Mr. Darcy\u2019s letter by heart. She studied every sentence; and her feelings toward its writer were sometimes entirely different. When she recalled the manner in which he had addressed her, she was still filled with indignation; but when she considered how unjustly she had condemned and reproached him, her anger turned upon herself, and his disappointed feelings became an object of compassion.\"}"
