          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a1aa94277872d86870b80a53\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat conclusion, then, follows? What impression has this made upon your imagination?\u201d\\n\\nI felt my flesh creep as Dupin asked me this. \u201cA madman,\u201d I said, \u201chas committed the deed\u2014some raving lunatic escaped from a nearby _Maison de Sant\u00e9._\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn some respects,\u201d he replied, \u201cyour idea is not without merit. But the voices of madmen, even during their wildest fits, never resemble that peculiar voice heard upon the stairs. Madmen belong to some nation, and their language, however incoherent in meaning, always retains the coherence of syllables. Besides, a madman\u2019s hair is not like what I now hold in my hand. I pulled this small tuft from Madame L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s tightly clenched fingers. Tell me what you make of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I cried, completely shaken; \u201cthis hair is extraordinary\u2014this is not _human_ hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not claimed that it is,\u201d he said; \u201cbut before we settle that question, I want you to examine the little sketch I have drawn on this paper. It is a _fac-simile_ of what one part of the testimony described as \u2018dark bruises, and deep indentations of fingernails,\u2019 upon the throat of Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye, and another part (by Messrs. Dumas and Etienne) called a \u2018series of livid spots, evidently the impression of fingers.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cYou will see,\u201d my friend continued, spreading the paper on the table before us, \u201cthat this drawing suggests a firm, unyielding grip. There is no sign of _slipping_. Each finger has held\u2014perhaps until the victim died\u2014the terrible grasp by which it first sank into the flesh. Now try to place all your fingers simultaneously into the corresponding impressions as you see them.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, but failed.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps we are not testing this fairly,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper lies on a flat surface, while the human throat is cylindrical. Here is a piece of wood whose circumference is approximately that of the throat. Wrap the drawing around it and try again.\u201d\\n\\nI did so, but the difficulty was even clearer than before. \u201cThis,\u201d I said, \u201cis the mark of no human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow read,\u201d Dupin replied, \u201cthis passage from Cuvier.\u201d\\n\\nIt was a detailed anatomical and general description of the large fulvous Ourang-Outang of the East Indian Islands. The gigantic size, immense strength and agility, savage ferocity, and imitative tendencies of these mammals are well known to everyone. At once, I understood the full horror of the murder.\\n\\n\u201cThe description of the digits,\u201d I said when I finished reading, \u201ccorresponds exactly with this drawing.\u201d\"}"
