          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b3ff5128e01528f3c5e32e38\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Many years ago, I became close friends with a Mr. William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and had once been wealthy, but a series of misfortunes had left him poor. To escape the shame caused by his troubles, he left New Orleans, the city of his ancestors, and settled on Sullivan\u2019s Island, near Charleston, South Carolina.\\n\\nThe island is a very unusual place. It is made almost entirely of sea sand and is about three miles long. Nowhere is it more than a quarter of a mile wide. A barely visible creek separates it from the mainland, winding through a wilderness of reeds and mud that was a favorite haunt of the marsh hen. As one might expect, the vegetation is sparse, or at least stunted. There are no large trees. Near the western end, where Fort Moultrie stands and a few miserable wooden buildings shelter people escaping Charleston\u2019s dust and fever during the summer, the prickly palmetto can be found. But except for this western point and a strip of hard white beach along the sea, the island is covered by thick growths of sweet myrtle, highly valued by English gardeners. Here the shrubs often grow fifteen or twenty feet high, forming an almost impassable thicket and filling the air with their scent.\\n\\nDeep inside this thicket, not far from the island\u2019s eastern, more distant end, Legrand had built a small hut. I first met him there by accident. Our acquaintance soon became a friendship, for the recluse had much to inspire interest and respect. He was well educated and unusually intelligent, but he was also misanthropic and prone to strange changes between excitement and sadness. He owned many books but seldom used them. His main pleasures were shooting and fishing, or wandering along the beach and through the myrtles in search of shells and insects\u2014his insect collection might have been envied by Swammerdamm. An old Black man named Jupiter usually joined him on these trips. Jupiter had been freed before the family\u2019s misfortunes, but neither threats nor promises could persuade him to give up what he believed was his right to follow his young \u201cMassa Will.\u201d It is possible that Legrand\u2019s relatives, thinking his mind somewhat unstable, had encouraged Jupiter\u2019s stubbornness so he could watch over and protect the wanderer.\"}"
