          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6dd3c6ef20e35c64dca60269\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then I repeated to myself the beautiful passage describing Dante\u2019s entrance into the earthly Paradise. I thought how sweet it would be, as I wandered along those lovely banks, to see the chariot of light descend with my long-lost parent, restored to me at last. While I waited for that moment, I imagined gathering the flowers that grew there into a wreath and crowning myself with joy. I would sing _sul margine d\u2019un rio_, my father\u2019s favorite song, and my voice, drifting through the still air, would tell him, wherever he sat waiting for our reunion, that his daughter had come. Then the mark of misery would have vanished from my brow, and I could raise my eyes without fear to meet his, which always shone with the gentle light of innocent love. When I thought of the magical expression in those deep eyes, I wept, but softly, afraid that my sobs might disturb the enchanted scene.\\n\\nI was so completely lost in this dream that I walked on without noticing where I was going. At last I bent down to pick a flower for my wreath on that barren plain, where no flower grew. Then I awoke from my daydream and realized that I did not know where I was.\\n\\nThe sun had set, and the rosy color it had given the clouds was almost gone. Wind swept across the plain. I looked around but saw nothing that showed me where I was. I had lost my way and tried in vain to find the path. I continued wandering, while the growing darkness erased every mark that might have guided me. At last everything was hidden in the deepest night. I grew tired, and since my servant was to spend the night in the nearby village, my absence would alarm no one. I was safe in that lonely place from every intruder, so I decided to remain there until morning. In truth, I was too tired to walk farther. The air was cold, but I did not care about my physical discomfort. During my two years of solitude, I had become used to every kind of weather, since no change of season had stopped my constant wandering.\\n\\nI lay on the grass, surrounded by darkness that not even the faintest ray of light could enter. There was no sound, for the deep night had put the insects to sleep\u2014the only creatures living in that lonely place, where no tree or bush could shelter anything else. A wonderful silence filled the air. It calmed my senses while stirring my soul; my mind rushed from one image to another and seemed to grasp eternity.\"}"
