          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5b6917414b7c736fd2c7203a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But that is of little consequence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI may as well ask,\u201d she replied, \u201cwhy, with such an obvious intention of offending and insulting me, you thought fit to tell me that you liked me against your will, your reason, and even your character? Was that not some excuse for rudeness, if I _was_ rude? But I have other causes for resentment. You know that I have. If my own feelings had not already decided against you, if they had been indifferent, or even favourable, do you imagine that anything could persuade me to accept the man who has been the instrument of destroying, perhaps forever, the happiness of a dearly loved sister?\u201d\\n\\nAs she spoke these words, Mr. Darcy changed colour; but the emotion soon passed, and he listened without trying to interrupt her as she went on,--\\n\\n\u201cI have every reason to think badly of you. No motive can excuse the unjust and ungenerous conduct you displayed _there_. You cannot deny that you were the chief, if not the sole, means of separating them, of exposing one to the world\u2019s censure for fickleness and instability, the other to its ridicule for disappointed hopes, and of involving them both in the deepest misery.\u201d\\n\\nShe stopped, and observed with considerable indignation that he listened in a manner showing himself entirely untouched by remorse. He even regarded her with a smile of affected disbelief.\\n\\n\u201cCan you deny that you did so?\u201d she repeated.\\n\\nHe then answered with assumed calmness, \u201cI have no intention of denying that I did all within my power to part my friend from your sister, or that I am pleased with my success. I have treated _him_ more kindly than I have treated myself.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth scorned the appearance of noticing this courteous remark, though its meaning was not lost on her, nor was it likely to win her over.\\n\\n\u201cBut this is not the only matter,\u201d she continued, \u201con which my dislike rests. My opinion of you had been formed long before it occurred. Your character was revealed in the account I received many months ago from Mr. Wickham. What have you to say on that subject? By what fancied act of friendship can you defend yourself here? or beneath what false representation can you deceive others?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou take a lively interest in that gentleman\u2019s affairs,\u201d said Darcy, in a less composed tone and with a deeper flush.\\n\\n\u201cWho, knowing what his misfortunes have been, could help taking an interest in him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHis misfortunes!\u201d Darcy repeated contemptuously,--\u201cyes, his misfortunes have indeed been considerable.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd caused by you,\u201d cried Elizabeth energetically; \u201cYou have brought him to his present condition of poverty--comparative poverty.\u201d\"}"
