          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d86c09c18e0a722fdedb65b7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat conclusion, then, has followed? What impression have I produced upon your imagination?\u201d\\n\\nA shudder passed over me as Dupin put the question. \u201cA madman,\u201d I answered, \u201chas committed the deed\u2014some raving lunatic escaped from a neighboring _Maison de Sant\u00e9._\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn certain respects,\u201d he replied, \u201cyour notion is not without relevance. Yet the voices of madmen, even during their wildest paroxysms, are never found to correspond with that singular voice heard upon the stairs. Madmen belong to some nation, and their speech, however incoherent in its words, always possesses the coherence of syllabification. Moreover, a madman\u2019s hair is not like that which I now hold. I drew this small tuft from the tightly clenched fingers of Madame L\u2019Espanaye. Tell me what you can infer from it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I cried, thoroughly unnerved; \u201cthis hair is extraordinary\u2014this is not _human_ hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not claimed that it is,\u201d said he; \u201cbut before settling that question, I want you to examine the little sketch I have made on this paper. It is a _fac-simile_ of what one part of the testimony calls \u2018dark bruises, and deep indentations of finger nails,\u2019 upon Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s throat, and what another portion (given by Messrs. Dumas and Etienne) describes as a \u2018series of livid spots, evidently the impression of fingers.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cYou will observe,\u201d my friend continued, laying the paper upon the table between us, \u201cthat the drawing suggests a firm, unchanging grip. There is no sign of _slipping_. Each finger has remained\u2014perhaps until the victim\u2019s death\u2014in the dreadful grasp by which it first sank into the flesh. Now try placing all your fingers simultaneously into the corresponding impressions as they appear here.\u201d\\n\\