          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"53d9dcdac9eaeef4fb0f3894\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His understanding and disposition, though different from her own, would have fulfilled all her hopes. Their union must have benefited them both: her ease and animation might have softened his mind and improved his manners; while from his judgment, knowledge, and experience of the world, she would have gained advantages of greater consequence.\\n\\nBut no such fortunate marriage could now show the admiring multitude what true conjugal happiness was. A union of an entirely different character, and one that made the other impossible, was soon to take place in their family.\\n\\nHow Wickham and Lydia were to be maintained in tolerable independence, she could not conceive. Yet how little lasting happiness could belong to a couple brought together only because their passions had proved stronger than their virtue, she could readily imagine.\\n\\nMr. Gardiner soon wrote once more to his brother. In answer to Mr. Bennet\u2019s acknowledgments, he replied briefly, assuring him of his eagerness to advance the welfare of any member of his family; and ended by begging that the matter might never be mentioned to him again. The chief purpose of his letter was to tell them that Mr. Wickham had determined to leave the militia.\\n\\n\u201cIt was my earnest wish that he should do so,\u201d he added, \u201cas soon as his marriage was settled. And I believe you will agree with me in thinking a removal from that corps highly advisable, both for his sake and my niece\u2019s. Mr. Wickham means to enter the Regulars; and among his former friends are still some who can and will assist him in the army. He has been promised an ensigncy in General----\u2019s regiment, now stationed in the north. It is an advantage that it should be so far from this part of the kingdom. He makes a fair promise; and I hope that, among new people, where each will have a character to maintain, they will both act with greater prudence. I have written to Colonel Forster, informing him of our present arrangements, and asking him to satisfy Mr. Wickham\u2019s several creditors in and around Brighton with assurances of speedy payment, for which I have made myself responsible. Will you also take the trouble of carrying similar assurances to his creditors in Meryton, whose names I shall enclose according to his account? He has declared all his debts; at least, I hope he has not deceived us. Haggerston has our instructions, and everything will be settled within a week. They will then join his regiment, unless they are first invited to Longbourn; and Mrs. Gardiner tells me that my niece is very eager to see you all before she leaves the south.\"}"
