          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ae6de7961f5e24b107fd601a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But he had oppressed no one and imprisoned no one. Far from harshly demanding payment of what he was owed, he had willingly given it up, faced a world that offered him no favor, made a place for himself there, and earned his own living. Monsieur Gabelle had managed the poor, debt-ridden estate according to written instructions: to spare the people, giving them what little could be given\u2014fuel in winter, if the powerful creditors allowed it, and whatever produce could be saved from their grip in summer. No doubt he had included these facts in his defense and evidence, for his own protection, so that they could not fail to appear now.\\n\\nThis strengthened the desperate decision Charles Darnay had begun to form: he would go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. Like the sailor in the old story, the winds and currents had carried him into the reach of the Loadstone Rock. It was pulling him toward itself, and he had to go. Everything that came to his mind pushed him onward, faster and more steadily, toward that terrible attraction. His hidden uneasiness had been that evil men were carrying out evil purposes in his unhappy country, while he, knowing himself better than they were, was not there trying to stop the bloodshed and defend mercy and humanity. With this uneasiness partly suppressed and partly accusing him, he had been led to compare himself with the brave old gentleman whose sense of duty was so strong. That comparison, which made him look bad, was immediately followed by Monseigneur\u2019s sneers, which had wounded him deeply, and then by Stryver\u2019s, which were especially crude and painful because of old reasons. After these came Gabelle\u2019s letter: an innocent prisoner\u2019s appeal to his justice, honor, and good name, with death threatening him.\\n\\nHis decision was made. He had to go to Paris.\\n\\nYes. The Loadstone Rock was drawing him, and he had to sail on until he struck it