          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"521f60934e6f87368dc41a38\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 7th. I rose early and, to my great joy, finally saw what I could only assume was the North Pole itself. It was there beyond doubt, directly beneath my feet. But, alas, I had risen to such an enormous height that nothing could be seen clearly. Judging from the sequence of figures showing my altitude at different times between six A.M. on April 2nd and twenty minutes before nine A.M. that same day, when the barometer stopped working, it was reasonable to conclude that by four o\u2019clock on the morning of April 7th the balloon had reached a height of at least 7,254 miles above sea level. This height may seem enormous, but the estimate used to calculate it was probably far below the truth. In any case, I could certainly see the earth\u2019s entire major diameter. The whole northern hemisphere lay below me like a map drawn in projection, and the equator itself formed the edge of my horizon. Your Excellencies can easily imagine that the unexplored regions within the Arctic Circle, though directly beneath me and therefore seen without foreshortening, were still too small in themselves and too far from my viewpoint to be examined with much accuracy. Even so, what I could see was strange and exciting. North of that great rim mentioned earlier, which may\u2014with some qualification\u2014be called the limit of human discovery in these regions, one continuous, or nearly continuous, sheet of ice stretched onward. During the first few degrees of its course, its surface became noticeably flatter; farther on, it sank into a level plane; and at last, growing somewhat concave, it ended at the Pole in a sharply defined circular center. Seen from the balloon, this center appeared to measure about sixty-five seconds of arc. Its dark color varied in intensity, but it was always darker than any other point on the visible hemisphere, sometimes deepening into complete, impenetrable blackness. Beyond this, little could be determined. By noon, the circular center had greatly decreased in size, and by seven P.M. I had lost sight of it altogether. The balloon had passed over the western edge of the ice and was moving rapidly away toward the equator.\\n\\n\u201cApril 8th. I noticed that the earth\u2019s apparent diameter had decreased, and that its general color and appearance had also changed considerably.\"}"
