          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a087100be6c7976ceb3cd2ed\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Could not determine whether the voice belonged to a man or a woman. Could not understand what was spoken, but thought the language was Spanish. This witness described the condition of the room and the bodies as we described it yesterday.\\n\\n\u201c_Henri Duval_, a neighbor and silversmith by occupation, states that he was among the group who first entered the house. In general, he confirms Mus\u00e8t\u2019s testimony. Immediately after forcing their way in, they shut the door again to keep out the crowd, which gathered very quickly despite the late hour. This witness believed the shrill voice to be that of an Italian. He was certain it was not French. He could not tell whether it was a man\u2019s voice; it might have been a woman\u2019s. He did not know Italian. Though unable to distinguish the words, he was persuaded by the intonation that the speaker was Italian. He knew Madame L. and her daughter, and had often spoken with both. He was certain the shrill voice belonged to neither of the deceased.\\n\\n\u201c\u2014\u2014_Odenheimer, restaurateur._ This witness offered his evidence voluntarily. As he did not speak French, his examination was conducted through an interpreter. He was born in Amsterdam. He was passing the house when the shrieks began. They continued for several minutes\u2014perhaps ten. They were prolonged and loud\u2014terrible and distressing. He was among those who entered the building. In every respect except one, he confirmed the preceding testimony. He was certain the shrill voice was a man\u2019s\u2014that of a Frenchman. He could not make out the words. They were loud, rapid, and uneven, apparently uttered in fear as well as anger. The voice was harsh\u2014not truly shrill, but harsh. He could not describe it as a shrill voice. The gruff voice repeatedly said \u2018_sacr\u00e9_,\u2019 \u2018_diable_,\u2019 and once \u2018_mon Dieu._\u2019\\n\\n\u201c_Jules Mignaud_, banker, of Mignaud et Fils, Rue Deloraine. He is the elder Mignaud. Madame L\u2019Espanaye possessed some property. In the spring of the year\u2014eight years earlier\u2014she opened an account with his banking house. She made frequent deposits of small amounts. She drew nothing until the third day before her death, when she personally withdrew 4000 francs. The money was paid in gold, and a clerk carried it to her home.\\n\\n\u201c_Adolphe Le Bon_, clerk at Mignaud et Fils, states that on the day in question, around noon, he accompanied Madame L\u2019Espanaye to her residence with the 4000 francs, packed in two bags. When the door was opened, Mademoiselle L. appeared and took one bag from him, while the old lady took the other.\u201d\"}"
