          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"88f41c16285a996e5d45a375\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Saturday at noon I went to the well and watched for a while. Merlin was still burning smoke powders, pawing the air, and muttering gibberish as vigorously as ever, but he looked rather discouraged, since naturally he had not yet raised even a sweat in that well. At last I said:\\n\\n\u201cHow does the matter look by now, partner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBehold, I am even now engaged in testing the most powerful enchantment known to the princes of occult arts in the lands of the East; should it fail me, nothing can help. Silence, until I am finished.\u201d\\n\\nThis time he raised a smoke that darkened the whole region and must have made things unpleasant for the hermits, since the wind was blowing toward them and rolled over their dens in a thick, billowing fog. He poured out an equal volume of words, twisted his body, and sawed the air with his hands in the most extraordinary fashion. After twenty minutes he collapsed, panting and nearly exhausted. Then the abbot arrived with several hundred monks and nuns, followed by a multitude of pilgrims and a couple of acres of foundlings, all drawn by the enormous smoke and all in a state of great excitement. The abbot anxiously asked for the result. Merlin said:\\n\\n\u201cIf any labor a mortal could perform might break the spell binding these waters, the one I have just attempted would have done so. It has failed; therefore I now know that what I feared is an established truth: the sign of this failure is that the most powerful spirit known to the magicians of the East, whose name no one may speak and live, has cast his spell upon this well. No mortal breathes, nor ever will, who can discover the secret of that spell, and without that secret no one can break it. The water will never flow again, good Father. I have done what a man could do. Let me go.\u201d\\n\\nNaturally, this threw the abbot into considerable consternation. He turned to me, the signs of it plain on his face, and said:\\n\\n\u201cYou have heard him. Is it true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSome of it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot all, then\u2014not all! Which part is true?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat the spirit with the Russian name has cast his spell upon the well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s wounds, then we are ruined!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPossibly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not certainly? You mean, not certainly?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen you also mean that when he says no one can break the spell\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. When he says that, he says something that is not necessarily true. There are conditions under which an attempt to break it might have some chance\u2014that is, some small, some trifling chance\u2014of succeeding.\u201d\"}"
