          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5078c586eff0b453a1e8db8e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then they passed lonely brickfields. The fog was thinner here, and he could see the strange, bottle-shaped kilns, their orange flames spreading like fans. A dog barked as they went by, and somewhere far off in the darkness a wandering seagull cried. The horse stumbled in a rut, swerved, and then broke into a gallop.\\n\\nAfter a while they left the clay road and rattled once more over streets paved with rough stones. Most of the windows were dark, but now and then strange shadows appeared against a blind lit from behind. He watched them with curiosity. They moved like huge puppets and gestured like living creatures. He hated them. A dull anger filled his heart. As they turned a corner, a woman shouted something at them from an open doorway, and two men chased the hansom for about a hundred yards. The driver struck at them with his whip.\\n\\nIt is said that passion makes a person think in circles. Certainly, with terrible repetition, Dorian Gray\u2019s bitten lips formed and re-formed those subtle words about soul and sense, until he found in them a complete expression of his mood. By approving them intellectually, he justified passions that would still have controlled him without that excuse. From one part of his mind to another crept the same thought; and the wild desire to live, the most terrible of all human appetites, stirred every trembling nerve and fibre into action. Ugliness, which had once disgusted him because it made things real, now became precious to him for that very reason. Ugliness was the only reality. The rough fight, the filthy den, the crude violence of a life without order, even the degradation of thieves and outcasts, seemed more vivid in their powerful reality than all the beautiful forms of art and the dreamy shadows of song. They were what he needed to forget. In three days he would be free.\\n\\nSuddenly the driver stopped with a jerk at the top of a dark lane. Above the low roofs and uneven chimney stacks rose the black masts of ships. Coils of white mist clung to the yards like ghostly sails.\\n\\n\u201cSomewhere around here, sir, isn\u2019t it?\u201d he asked hoarsely through the trap.\\n\\nDorian started and looked around. \u201cThis will do,\u201d he replied. He got out quickly, paid the driver the extra fare he had promised, and walked swiftly toward the quay. Here and there, a lantern shone at the stern of a huge merchant ship. Its light trembled and broke apart in the puddles. A red glow came from a steamer heading out to sea as it took on coal. The slimy pavement looked like a wet raincoat.\"}"
