          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"73fcfb95ebd905cafa4f8ffd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat\u2019s the matter?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean? Ten o\u2019clock at night?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir. You told me to wake you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I remember. Very well, very well.\u201d\\n\\nAfter making several dull attempts to fall asleep again, which the man skillfully prevented by stirring the fire for five minutes, he got up, put on his hat, and went out. He turned into the Temple and, after reviving himself by walking twice along King\u2019s Bench Walk and Paper Buildings, entered Stryver\u2019s chambers.\\n\\nStryver\u2019s clerk, who never took part in these meetings, had gone home, so Stryver himself opened the door. He wore slippers and a loose dressing gown, with his throat bare for comfort. Around his eyes was the wild, strained, worn look seen in heavy drinkers of his kind, from the portrait of Jeffries onward, and visible in different forms in the portraits of every age of drinking.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re a little late, Memory,\u201d said Stryver.\\n\\n\u201cAbout the usual time; perhaps a quarter of an hour later.\u201d\\n\\nThey entered a gloomy room lined with books and covered with papers, where a bright fire burned. A kettle steamed on the hob, and amid the scattered papers stood a shining table covered with wine, brandy, rum, sugar, and lemons.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019ve had your bottle, I see, Sydney.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTwo tonight, I think. I was dining with the day\u2019s client\u2014or watching him dine; it\u2019s the same thing!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was an excellent point, Sydney, the one you used to establish the identification. How did you think of it? When did it occur to you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought he was a rather handsome fellow, and that I might have been much the same kind of man if I\u2019d had any luck.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Stryver laughed until his prematurely large belly shook.\\n\\n\u201cYou and your luck, Sydney! Get to work, get to work.\u201d\\n\\nThe jackal sullenly loosened his clothes, went into the next room, and returned with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and two towels. He soaked the towels, partly wrung them out, folded them around his head in a horrible fashion, sat at the table, and said, \u201cNow I\u2019m ready!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot much reducing to do tonight, Memory,\u201d said Mr. Stryver cheerfully, as he searched through his papers.\\n\\n\u201cHow much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly two sets.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere they are, Sydney. Begin!\u201d\\n\\nThe lion then settled on his back on a sofa beside the drinking table, while the jackal sat at his own paper-covered desk across from him, with bottles and glasses within easy reach.\"}"
