          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb2772b1bf1316b63d999ee7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI\u2019m going, Master Will\u2014I truly am,\u201d the Black man replied promptly. \u201cI\u2019m almost at the end now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201c_At the end!_\u201d Legrand cried, nearly screaming. \u201cDo you mean you\u2019re at the end of that limb?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAlmost at de end, massa\u2014o-o-o-o-oh! Lord have mercy! What is this here on the tree?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell?\u201d Legrand cried, greatly pleased. \u201cWhat is it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, it\u2019s nothing but a skull\u2014somebody left his head up in the tree, and the crows have eaten every bit of flesh off it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA skull, you say? Very well\u2014how is it fastened to the limb? What holds it there?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSure enough, massa; I must look. Why, this is a very curious thing, upon my word\u2014there\u2019s a great big nail in the skull, fastening it to the tree.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow, Jupiter, do exactly as I tell you\u2014do you hear?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, massa.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPay attention, then\u2014find the skull\u2019s left eye.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHum! Hoo! That\u2019s a good one! Why, there isn\u2019t any left eye at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCurse your stupidity! Do you know your right hand from your left?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, I know that\u2014I know all about it. It\u2019s my left hand that I chop wood with.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course! You\u2019re left-handed, and your left eye is on the same side as your left hand. Now, I suppose you can find the skull\u2019s left eye, or the place where its left eye was. Have you found it?\u201d\\n\\nThere was a long pause. At last the Black man asked,\\n\\n\u201cIs de skull\u2019s lef eye on de same side as de skull\u2019s lef hand, too?\u2014\u2019cause de skull ain\u2019t got no hand at all\u2014never mind! I got de lef eye now\u2014here\u2019s de lef eye! What must I do with it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet the beetle drop through it, as far as the string will reach\u2014but be careful not to release your hold on the string.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll done, Master Will; mighty easy thing to put de bug through de hole\u2014look out for him down below!\u201d\\n\\nDuring this exchange, no part of Jupiter could be seen; but the beetle he had allowed to descend was now visible at the end of the string, shining like a globe of polished gold in the last rays of the setting sun, some of which still faintly lit the height where we stood. The scarab hung completely clear of the branches and, if released, would have fallen at our feet. Legrand immediately took the scythe and cut a circular space three or four yards across directly beneath the insect. When he had finished, he ordered Jupiter to release the string and climb down from the tree.\"}"
