{"request_id":"119b3a74fce5ed871a719658","rewritten_text":"When Tom woke in the morning, he wondered where he was. He sat up, rubbed his eyes, and looked around. Then he remembered. It was the cool gray dawn, and a delicious sense of rest and peace lay in the deep, all-encompassing calm and silence of the woods. Not a leaf moved; not a sound intruded upon great Nature’s meditation. Dewdrops glittered on the leaves and grasses. A white layer of ash covered the fire, and a thin blue thread of smoke rose straight into the air. Joe and Huck were still asleep.

Far away in the woods, a bird called; another answered; soon the hammering of a woodpecker could be heard. Gradually the cool, dim gray of morning brightened, and sounds multiplied as life revealed itself. The wonder of Nature waking from sleep and beginning its work unfolded before the thoughtful boy. A little green worm crawled across a dewy leaf, lifting two-thirds of its body into the air from time to time and “sniffing around,” then moving on again—for, Tom said, it was measuring. When the worm came near him, he sat as still as stone, his hopes rising and falling as the creature continued toward him or seemed ready to go elsewhere. At last, after a painful pause with its curved body raised, it came decisively down upon Tom’s leg and began traveling over him. His whole heart leaped with joy—for that meant, beyond any doubt, that he was going to have a new suit of clothes, a splendid pirate uniform.

Then a procession of ants appeared from nowhere in particular and went about their work. One struggled bravely along with a dead spider five times its own size in its arms, dragging it straight up a tree trunk. A brown-spotted ladybug climbed the dizzy height of a blade of grass. Tom bent close to it and said, “Ladybug, ladybug, fly away home, your house is on fire, your children are alone,” and it took wing and flew off to investigate—which did not surprise the boy, since he knew from experience that this insect was gullible about fires, and he had taken advantage of its simplicity more than once. Next came a tumblebug, sturdily pushing its ball. Tom touched it and watched it draw its legs against its body and pretend to be dead. By then the birds were almost rioting. A catbird, the Northern mockingbird, landed in a tree above Tom’s head and poured out imitations of its neighbors in delighted ecstasy. Then a shrill jay swept down like a flash of blue flame and stopped on a twig almost within the boy’s reach. It cocked its head and studied the strangers with intense curiosity. A gray squirrel and a large “fox” squirrel came scurrying by, pausing at intervals to sit up, inspect the boys, and chatter at them. The wild creatures had probably never seen a human being before and scarcely knew whether they should be afraid."}

