{"request_id":"d34d506050e765777d16567c","rewritten_text":"We were raised together, being separated in age by less than a year. It is needless to say that we were strangers to every form of disagreement or quarrel. Harmony was the spirit of our companionship, and the differences and contrasts in our characters brought us closer. Elizabeth possessed a calmer, more inward nature; yet, despite all my ardour, I could devote myself more intensely and was more powerfully possessed by the desire for knowledge. She occupied herself with pursuing the airy visions of the poets; and in the grand and marvellous scenes around our Swiss home—the majestic mountains, the changing seasons, storm and tranquillity, winter’s silence, and the vitality and tumult of our Alpine summers—she found abundant material for wonder and pleasure. While my companion regarded the splendid appearances of nature with a thoughtful and contented mind, I took delight in examining their causes. The world seemed to me a mystery I longed to understand. Curiosity, serious inquiry into nature’s concealed laws, and a joy almost amounting to rapture when they were revealed to me, are among the earliest feelings I can recall.

When a second son was born, seven years younger than I, my parents abandoned their wandering life altogether and settled in their native land. We had a house in Geneva and a _campagne_ at Belrive, on the lake’s eastern shore, a little more than a league from the city. We lived chiefly at the latter, and my parents’ lives were spent in considerable seclusion. I was inclined to shun crowds and to devote myself warmly to a few. I was therefore unconcerned with my schoolmates generally; but I formed the closest friendship with one of them. Henry Clerval was the son of a Geneva merchant. He was a boy of remarkable talent and imagination. He loved enterprise, hardship, and even danger for their own sake. He had read deeply in books of chivalry and romance. He composed heroic songs and began writing many tales of enchantment and knightly adventure. He attempted to make us perform plays and take part in masquerades, whose characters came from the heroes of Roncesvalles, King Arthur’s Round Table, and the chivalrous company who shed their blood to recover the holy sepulchre from the infidels.

No human being could have enjoyed a happier childhood than I did. My parents were filled with the very spirit of kindness and indulgence. We understood that they were not tyrants governing our lives according to their whims, but the agents and makers of all the many pleasures we possessed."}

