          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"637e478a568843a3f43dcd42\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But how could I, a poor, confused village boy, escape the strange contradiction that even the best and wisest people fall into every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may all be true,\u201d I said to Biddy, \u201cbut I admire her terribly.\u201d\\n\\nIn short, when I reached that point, I turned onto my face, grabbed the hair on both sides of my head, and pulled hard. All the while, I knew my heart\u2019s madness was so extreme and misplaced that I felt my face deserved punishment for belonging to such a fool. I almost thought I should lift it by my hair and bang it against the pebbles.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and she tried no longer to reason with me. Her hand was comforting, though rough from work. She placed it over my hands one after another and gently drew them from my hair. Then she softly patted my shoulder to soothe me. With my face against my sleeve, I cried a little\u2014just as I had in the brewery yard\u2014and vaguely felt that someone, or perhaps everyone, had treated me very badly. I could not say which.\\n\\n\u201cI am glad of one thing,\u201d said Biddy. \u201cYou have felt able to trust me, Pip. And I am glad of another: you know, of course, that you can rely on me to keep your confidence and always deserve it. If your first teacher\u2014dear me, such a poor teacher, and one who needed teaching herself!\u2014were teaching you now, she thinks she knows what lesson she would give you. But it would be a difficult one to learn, and you have already gone beyond her, so it is no use now.\u201d With a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank and said, in a fresh, pleasant tone, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, rising, putting my arm around her neck, and kissing her, \u201cI shall always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you become a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never shall, so that means always. Not that I have anything to tell you, because you know everything I know\u2014as I told you at home the other night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Biddy softly, looking away toward the ships. Then, in the same pleasant tone as before, she repeated, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did. The summer afternoon slowly faded into evening, and it was very beautiful.\"}"
        }
