{"request_id":"c20f12334ee1826e5fe278bf","rewritten_text":"Do not misunderstand me; I was never truly mad. I remained aware of my condition when my fevered thoughts appeared to drive me toward insanity, and revealed them to nothing but silence and solitude. Those about me perceived none of this. They saw only a poor girl crushed in spirit, speaking in a soft and gentle voice, with tears sometimes slipping from beneath her lowered lids, which she tried to conceal. One who preferred solitude and recoiled from notice; who never smiled; oh, no! I never smiled—and that was all.

Well, I escaped. I departed from my guardian’s house and was never heard of again; from the letters I left and other circumstances, it was supposed that I had intended to destroy myself. I was therefore pursued less diligently than might otherwise have been the case, and before long every trace and remembrance of me disappeared. I left London aboard a small vessel sailing for a port in the north of England. Now that my attempt had succeeded, and I was entirely alone, peace returned to me. The sea lay calm and the vessel moved softly onward. I sat upon the deck beneath the open sky, and thought myself transformed. I was no longer the wild, raving, most miserable Mathilda, but a young Hermitess devoted to seclusion, striving to keep her heart free from all agitation and unholy despair. The fanciful, nunlike dress I had taken up; the knowledge that my existence was a secret known only to myself; and the solitude to which I was forever destined nourished gentle thoughts within my wounded heart. The breeze stirring my hair revived me, while I watched quietly the sunbeams glittering upon the waves and the birds chasing one another across the water, their feathers just touching its surface. I slept, too, without troubled dreams, and awoke refreshed to enjoy my peaceful freedom once more.

After four days we reached the harbour for which we were bound. I would not stay upon the coast, but went inland at once. I had already chosen the place where I would live. It was to be a solitary house upon a broad plain, far from every other dwelling, where I could see the entire horizon and wander widely without being disturbed by the sight of my fellow creatures. I was not misanthropic, but I felt that the gentle flow of my feelings depended upon solitude. I settled myself in a vast loneliness: upon a bleak heath scattered with stones, among which short grass grew, with here and there a few rushes beside a small pool."}

