{"request_id":"5f2276fd8abda27aa9e3420a","rewritten_text":"The next morning, when the Otis family gathered for breakfast, they discussed the ghost at length. The United States Minister was naturally somewhat annoyed that his gift had not been accepted. “I have no desire,” he said, “to cause the ghost any personal harm, and I must say that, considering how long he has lived in this house, it is hardly polite to throw pillows at him”—a perfectly reasonable observation, at which, I regret to say, the twins burst into loud laughter. “On the other hand,” he continued, “if he truly refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we shall have to take away his chains. It would be impossible to sleep with that noise going on outside the bedrooms.”

For the rest of the week, however, they were not disturbed. The only thing that attracted attention was the bloodstain on the library floor, which kept reappearing. This was certainly strange, since Mr. Otis always locked the door at night and kept the windows firmly barred. The stain’s chameleon-like colors also provoked much comment. Some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian red; then it became vermilion, then a deep purple; and once, when they came downstairs for family prayers according to the simple rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they found it a vivid emerald green. These kaleidoscopic changes naturally amused everyone, and bets were freely placed each evening. The only person who did not join in the joke was little Virginia, who, for some unexplained reason, was always deeply upset by the bloodstain and nearly cried on the morning it turned emerald green.

The ghost appeared for the second time on Sunday night. Shortly after the family had gone to bed, they were suddenly startled by a terrible crash in the hall. Running downstairs, they found that a large suit of old armor had come loose from its stand and fallen onto the stone floor. Seated in a high-backed chair was the Canterville ghost, rubbing his knees with an expression of intense agony. The twins, having brought their pea-shooters, immediately fired two pellets at him with the accuracy that can be achieved only through long, careful practice on a writing master. Meanwhile, the United States Minister aimed his revolver at him and called, in accordance with Californian etiquette, for him to hold up his hands. The ghost sprang up with a wild cry of rage and swept through them like mist, extinguishing Washington Otis’s candle as he passed and leaving them all in complete darkness."}

