          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7c81b05cf11111f11b63115d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd I call upon you, spirits of the dead, and upon you, wandering servants of vengeance, to help and guide me in my work. Let the cursed, hellish monster drink deeply of agony; let him feel the despair that now torments me.\u201d\\n\\nI had begun my invocation solemnly, with a sense of awe that almost convinced me the spirits of my murdered friends heard and approved my devotion. But as I finished, the furies took hold of me, and rage choked my voice.\\n\\nThe stillness of the night was broken by a loud, demonic laugh. It rang in my ears for a long time; the mountains echoed it, and I felt as though all hell surrounded me with mockery and laughter. Surely, in that moment, I would have gone mad and ended my miserable life, if my vow had not been heard and I had not been kept alive for vengeance. The laughter faded, and a familiar, hated voice, apparently close beside my ear, spoke in a clear whisper: \u201cI am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have decided to live, and I am satisfied.\u201d\\n\\nI rushed toward the place from which the sound had come, but the devil escaped my grasp. Suddenly, the moon\u2019s broad disk rose and shone directly upon his ghastly, twisted form as he fled with more than human speed.\\n\\nI pursued him, and for many months this has been my task. Following a faint clue, I traced the winding Rhone, but without success. Then the blue Mediterranean came into view, and by a strange chance I saw the fiend enter it at night and hide aboard a ship bound for the Black Sea. I took passage on the same vessel, but somehow he escaped me.\\n\\nThrough the wild regions of Tartary and Russia, though he continued to evade me, I always followed his trail. Sometimes peasants, frightened by this horrible apparition, told me where he had gone. Sometimes he himself, fearing that if I lost every trace of him I would despair and die, left a sign to guide me. Snow fell upon 