          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bbf8dc2fb0a14c6d42a4b44e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But there was no laughter in the little town that peaceful Saturday afternoon. The Harpers and Aunt Polly\u2019s family were going into mourning, with deep grief and many tears. An unusual silence filled the village, though it was quiet enough at the best of times. The villagers went about their business distractedly and spoke little, but they sighed often. The Saturday holiday felt like a burden to the children. They had no interest in their games and gradually abandoned them.\\n\\nThat afternoon Becky Thatcher found herself wandering sadly around the empty schoolhouse yard. But there was nothing there to comfort her. She said to herself:\\n\\n\u201cOh, if only I had a brass andiron knob again! But now I have nothing to remember him by.\u201d And she held back a small sob.\\n\\nAfter a moment she stopped and thought:\\n\\n\u201cIt was right here. Oh, if I could do it over, I wouldn\u2019t say that\u2014I wouldn\u2019t say it for anything in the world. But he\u2019s gone now; I\u2019ll never, never, never see him again.\u201d\\n\\nThe thought overwhelmed her, and she wandered away with tears running down her cheeks. Then a group of boys and girls\u2014Tom\u2019s and Joe\u2019s playmates\u2014came by and stood looking over the picket fence. In solemn voices they talked about how Tom had done this or that the last time they saw him, and how Joe had said some small thing that now seemed full of terrible meaning. Each speaker pointed to the exact place where the lost boys had stood, then added something like, \u201cAnd I was standing just so\u2014just as I am now, and if you were him\u2014I was this close\u2014and he smiled, just like this\u2014and then something seemed to go all over me, something awful, you know\u2014and I never guessed what it meant, of course, but I can see now!\u201d\\n\\nThen they argued over who had seen the dead boys last while they were alive. Many claimed that sad honor and offered evidence, though the witnesses had altered it to some degree. When they finally decided who really had seen the boys last and spoken the final words to them, those lucky ones assumed a kind of sacred importance. Everyone else stared at them and envied them. One poor boy, with no other glory to claim, said with obvious pride:\\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer licked me once.\u201d\\n\\nBut that attempt at fame failed. Most of the boys could say the same, which made the distinction seem too common. The group slowly drifted away, still remembering the lost heroes in hushed, respectful voices.\"}"
