{"request_id":"a55ffbf0a552f7fbfe34fced","rewritten_text":"Far into the distance stretched a line of flickering gas lamps, and outside a small walled house stood a lone hansom cab, its driver asleep inside. He walked quickly toward Portland Place, glancing back from time to time as if afraid someone was following him. At the corner of Rich Street, two men stood reading a small notice posted on a wall. Curious, he crossed over. As he approached, the word “Murder,” printed in black letters, caught his eye. He stopped, and a deep flush spread across his face. The notice offered a reward for information leading to the arrest of a man of medium height, between thirty and forty, wearing a bowler hat, a black coat, and checked trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. He read it again and again, wondering whether the miserable man would be caught and how he had received his scar. Perhaps one day his own name would be posted on the walls of London. Perhaps a price would be placed on his head as well.

The thought filled him with horror. He turned away and hurried into the night.

He scarcely knew where he went. He vaguely remembered wandering among filthy houses, lost in a vast web of dark streets, and it was bright dawn when he finally found himself in Piccadilly Circus. As he walked home toward Belgrave Square, he met the large wagons heading for Covent Garden. The carters, dressed in white smocks, with friendly sunburned faces and rough, curly hair, strode along confidently, cracking their whips and calling to one another now and then. On the back of a huge gray horse, leader of a noisy team, sat a chubby boy with a bunch of primroses in his battered hat. He held tightly to the mane with his small hands and laughed. The great heaps of vegetables looked like jade against the morning sky, like green jade against the pink petals of some marvelous rose. Lord Arthur felt strangely moved, though he could not say why. Something in the dawn’s delicate beauty seemed deeply sad to him, and he thought of all the days that begin beautifully and end in storms. These countrymen, too, with their rough, cheerful voices and easy manner—what a strange London they must see! A London free from the night’s sin and the day’s smoke, a pale, ghostlike city, a deserted town of tombs. He wondered what they thought of it, and whether they knew anything of its splendor and shame, its fierce, fiery joys and terrible hunger, of all it creates and destroys from morning to evening."}

