          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"afb67465f546b7d4ee7b201e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must go, not knowing what will happen to me; but I will return here, or join your party, as soon as I can. One word or look will decide whether I enter your father\u2019s house this evening or never again.\u201d\\n\\nA letter like this could not be recovered from quickly. Half an hour alone with her thoughts might have calmed her; but the ten minutes that passed before she was interrupted, with all the limits imposed by her situation, could do nothing to restore her peace. Each moment only brought new agitation. Her happiness was overwhelming. And before she had moved beyond the first rush of feeling, Charles, Mary, and Henrietta all entered.\\n\\nThe need to appear like herself immediately caused a struggle; but after a while she could do no more. She began to understand nothing they said and had to claim that she felt unwell and excuse herself. They could then see that she looked very ill, and were shocked and worried. They would not leave her for anything. This was terrible. If they had only gone away and left her quietly in that room, it would have cured her; but having them all standing or waiting around her was unbearable, and in desperation she said she would go home.\\n\\n\u201cBy all means, my dear,\u201d cried Mrs. Musgrove. \u201cGo home at once and take care of yourself, so that you may be well enough for the evening. I wish Sarah were here to treat you, but I am no doctor. Charles, ring for a chair. She must not walk.\u201d\\n\\nBut the chair would never do. It was worse than anything! To lose the chance of speaking two words to Captain Wentworth during a quiet, solitary walk up the town\u2014and she felt almost certain of meeting him\u2014was impossible to bear. She strongly objected to the chair. Mrs. Musgrove, who thought only of one kind of illness, asked anxiously whether Anne had fallen; whether she had recently slipped and struck her head; and, after becoming fully convinced that there had been no fall, was able to let her leave cheerfully, expecting to find her better that night.\\n\\nAnxious to leave no possible precaution unmentioned, Anne struggled to say\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid, ma\u2019am, that this has not been fully understood. Please be so kind as to tell the other gentlemen that we hope to see your entire party this evening. I am afraid there has been some mistake; and I especially wish you to assure Captain Harville and Captain Wentworth that we hope to see them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! my dear, it is perfectly understood, I assure you.\u201d\"}"
