          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e1c6c10d8c636d64e73bb4ea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But in that bitter tirade against Chantilly, which appeared in yesterday\u2019s Mus\u00e9e, the satirist made some disgraceful references to the cobbler\u2019s change of name when he took up acting, and quoted a Latin line we had often discussed. I mean the line\\n\\nPerdidit antiquum litera prima sonum.\\n\\n\u201cI had told you that this referred to Orion, once written Urion; and because of certain sharp points in that explanation, I knew you could not have forgotten it. So it was clear that you would connect the ideas of Orion and Chantilly. I saw that you had done so from the kind of smile that crossed your lips. You were thinking of the poor cobbler\u2019s sacrifice. Until then, you had been walking bent over; but now I saw you straighten to your full height. I was sure that you were thinking of Chantilly\u2019s small figure. At that moment I interrupted your thoughts by observing that, since he was in fact a very little man\u2014that Chantilly\u2014he would be better suited to the Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s.\u201d\\n\\nSoon afterward, we were reading an evening edition of the Gazette des Tribunaux when the following paragraphs caught our attention.\\n\\n\u201cExtraordinary Murders.\u2014This morning, at about three o\u2019clock, the residents of the Quartier St. Roch were awakened by a series of terrible screams, apparently coming from the fourth floor of a house in the Rue Morgue. The house was known to be occupied only by Madame L\u2019Espanaye and her daughter, Mademoiselle Camille L\u2019Espanaye. After an unsuccessful attempt to enter in the usual way, the gate was broken open with a crowbar, and eight or ten neighbors went inside with two gendarmes. By then the screams had stopped; but as the group hurried up the first flight of stairs, two or more harsh voices arguing angrily were heard, apparently coming from the upper part of the house. When they reached the second landing, those sounds had also stopped, and everything was completely quiet. The group spread out and rushed from room to room. When they reached a large back room on the fourth floor, they found the door locked, with the key inside, and forced it open. What they saw filled everyone present with both horror and astonishment.\\n\\n\u201cThe room was in complete disorder, with furniture broken and scattered everywhere. There was only one bedstead; the bed had been taken off it and thrown into the middle of the floor. A razor covered with blood lay on a chair.\"}"
