          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"70bf53e1978e0ed425ed6006\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I ran to her, gathered her into my arms, covered her with kisses, and pleaded:\\n\\n\u201cSpeak, darling, speak! What is it?\u201d\\n\\nHer head went limp against my chest, and she gasped so softly I could hardly hear her:\\n\\n\u201cHELLO-CENTRAL!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cQuick!\u201d I shouted to Clarence. \u201cTelephone the king\u2019s homeopath and tell him to come!\u201d\\n\\nWithin two minutes I was kneeling beside the child\u2019s crib, while Sandy sent servants rushing in every direction throughout the palace. I understood the situation almost at once\u2014membranous croup! I bent over her and whispered:\\n\\n\u201cWake up, sweetheart! Hello-Central.\u201d\\n\\nShe opened her gentle eyes slowly and managed to say:\\n\\n\u201cPapa.\u201d\\n\\nThat comforted me. She was not close to death yet. I sent for sulfur preparations and brought out the croup kettle myself; I do not sit and wait for doctors when Sandy or the child is ill. I knew how to care for both of them and had plenty of experience. This little one had spent much of its short life in my arms, and often I could ease its troubles and make it laugh through the tears on its eyelashes when even its mother could not.\\n\\nSir Launcelot, wearing his finest armor, came striding down the great hall on his way to the stock board. He was its president and occupied the Siege Perilous, which he had bought from Sir Galahad. The stock board consisted of the Knights of the Round Table, who now used the Round Table for business. Seats there were worth\u2014well, you would never believe the price, so there is no point in naming it. Sir Launcelot was a bear, and he had cornered one of the new lines. He was preparing to squeeze the shorts that day; but what did that matter? He was still the old Launcelot, and when he glanced through the doorway, learned that his pet was sick, and saw her condition, nothing else mattered. Bulls and bears could fight among themselves; he would come in and stand by little Hello-Central with all his strength. And that was exactly what he did. He tossed his helmet into a corner, and within half a minute had put a new wick in the alcohol lamp and was heating the croup kettle. By then Sandy had built a blanket canopy over the crib, and everything was ready.\\n\\nSir Launcelot brought the kettle to a boil. Together, he and I filled it with unslaked lime and carbolic acid, adding a little lactic acid, then poured in water and placed the steam spout beneath the canopy. Everything was in order now, and we sat on opposite sides of the crib to keep watch.\"}"
