          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"070e09c3a18747a0f0e1df26\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 19th. This morning, to my great joy, at about nine o\u2019clock, with the moon\u2019s surface frighteningly near and my fears at their highest, the pump of my condenser finally showed clear signs of a change in the atmosphere. By ten, I had reason to believe its density had increased greatly. By eleven, the apparatus required very little effort; and at noon, after some hesitation, I dared to unscrew the tourniquet. When I felt no discomfort from doing so, I opened the rubber chamber completely and removed it from around the car. As might be expected, spasms and severe headaches immediately followed an experiment so sudden and dangerous. But these troubles, along with the other difficulties of breathing, were not severe enough to threaten my life. I therefore resolved to endure them as best I could, since I was rapidly leaving them behind as I approached the denser layers near the moon. My descent, however, remained extremely rapid; and it soon became alarmingly clear that, although I had probably been right to expect an atmosphere whose density matched the satellite\u2019s mass, I had been wrong to suppose that even at the surface it would be dense enough to support the great weight of my balloon\u2019s car. Yet it should have done so, to the same degree as at Earth, if the actual gravity of bodies on either planet corresponded to the condensation of its atmosphere. My steep fall proved that this was not so; the reason can only be explained by those possible geological disturbances I mentioned earlier. In any case, I was now close to the planet and descending with terrifying speed. I immediately threw overboard my ballast, then my water kegs, then the condenser and rubber chamber, and finally every object in the car. But none of this helped. I continued to fall horribly fast and was now less than half a mile from the surface. As a final measure, after removing my coat, hat, and boots, I cut the car itself loose from the balloon. It was quite heavy, and, clinging with both hands to the netting, I had barely time to notice that the entire country, as far as I could see, was thickly dotted with tiny houses before I plunged headfirst into the center of a strange-looking city and into the middle of a huge crowd of ugly little people. Not one spoke a word or made the slightest effort to help me. They simply stood there like a group of idiots, grinning foolishly and looking sideways at me and my balloon, with their arms akimbo.\u201d\"}"
