          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e129b704e2f6b48a6c2eae0b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBasil, this is extraordinary! I must see Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\nHallward rose from his seat and paced up and down the garden. After a while he returned. \u201cHarry,\u201d he said, \u201cDorian Gray is simply a motive in art to me. You might see nothing in him. I see everything in him. He is never more present in my work than when no image of him appears. As I have said, he suggests a new manner. I find him in the curves of certain lines, in the beauty and subtlety of certain colours. That is all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen why won\u2019t you exhibit his portrait?\u201d Lord Henry asked.\\n\\n\u201cBecause, without meaning to, I have put into it some expression of all this strange artistic idolatry, which, of course, I have never wanted to mention to him. He knows nothing about it. He must never know anything about it. But the world might guess, and I will not expose my soul to their shallow, prying eyes. My heart will never be placed beneath their microscope. There is too much of myself in the thing, Harry\u2014too much of myself!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPoets are not as scrupulous as you. They know how useful passion can be for publication. These days, a broken heart will go through many editions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hate them for it,\u201d Hallward cried. \u201cAn artist should create beautiful things, but put none of his own life into them. We live in an age when men treat art as though it were meant to be autobiography. We have lost the abstract sense of beauty. One day I will show the world what it is; and for that reason the world will never see my portrait of Dorian Gray.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you are wrong, Basil, but I will not argue with you. Only the intellectually lost ever argue. Tell me, is Dorian Gray very fond of you?\u201d\\n\\nThe painter thought for a few moments. \u201cHe likes me,\u201d he replied after a pause; \u201cI know he likes me. Of course, I flatter him terribly. I take a strange pleasure in saying things to him that I know I will regret having said. Usually, he is charming to me, and we sit in the studio talking about a thousand things. Sometimes, however, he is horribly thoughtless and seems to take genuine delight in hurting me. Then I feel, Harry, that I have given my entire soul to someone who treats it as though it were a flower to place in his coat, a piece of decoration to flatter his vanity, an ornament for a summer\u2019s day.\u201d\"}"
