          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e1b5d833555e4b024fee4a99\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months passed, and winter was well underway. I had spent most of that time at school and could spell, read, and write a little. I could also recite the multiplication table as far as six times seven is thirty-five, and I don\u2019t believe I could ever get beyond that, even if I lived forever. I don\u2019t care much for mathematics anyway.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I learned to tolerate it. Whenever I got especially tired, I played hooky, and the beating I received the next day actually did me good and cheered me up. So the longer I attended, the easier it became. I was also getting used to the widow\u2019s ways, and they didn\u2019t bother me quite so much. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed weighed on me most of the time, but before the cold weather came, I sometimes slipped away and slept in the woods, which gave me a break. I still preferred the old ways, but I was beginning to like the new ones a little, too. The widow said I was improving slowly but surely and doing very well. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning at breakfast, I accidentally knocked over the saltcellar. I reached for some salt as quickly as I could, meaning to throw it over my left shoulder and prevent bad luck, but Miss Watson got there first and stopped me. She said, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; you\u2019re always making such a mess!\u201d The widow tried to say something kind for me, but I knew that wouldn\u2019t keep the bad luck away. After breakfast I went outside feeling nervous and uneasy, wondering where the bad luck would strike me and what form it would take. There are ways to prevent some kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of them. So I didn\u2019t try to do anything; I just walked along sadly, keeping watch.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile in the tall board fence. An inch of fresh snow covered the ground, and I saw someone\u2019s tracks. They had come up from the quarry, stood near the stile for a while, and then continued around the garden fence. It was strange that they hadn\u2019t come in after lingering there so long. I couldn\u2019t understand it. Somehow, it seemed very curious. I was about to follow the tracks around, but first I bent down to examine them.\"}"
