          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b87b609e1cbc0ef4f9047b51\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These deputies had been sent by them to Adrian, chosen as arbiter; and they had travelled from Paris to Calais through empty towns and desolate country, all the while indulging a fierce hatred of one another; and now they argued their respective causes with unabated party spirit.\\n\\nBy examining the deputies separately, and after much inquiry, we discovered the real condition of affairs at Paris. Since parliament had appointed him Ryland\u2019s deputy, all the surviving English had yielded to Adrian. He was our captain, to conduct us from our native land to unknown regions, our lawgiver, and our protector. When our plan of emigration was first arranged, no lasting separation of our members had been intended, and command of the entire body, rising by successive degrees of authority, culminated in the Earl of Windsor. But unforeseen events altered our plans and caused most of our number to remain divided from the supreme chief for nearly two months. They had crossed over in two separate groups; and upon reaching Paris, disagreement sprang up between them.\\n\\nThey had discovered Paris to be deserted. When the plague first appeared, returning travellers and merchants, together with letters, regularly informed us of the destruction wrought by disease upon the continent. But as mortality increased, this intercourse diminished and finally stopped. Even in England, communication between one part of the island and another grew slow and infrequent. No vessel crossed the current separating Calais from Dover; or, if some sorrowful voyager, eager to learn whether his relatives lived or died, departed from the French coast to rejoin us, the hungry sea often swallowed his small craft, or within a day or two the disorder seized him, and he died before he could relate the story of France\u2019s desolation. We were therefore largely ignorant of the continent\u2019s condition, and still entertained some faint hope of finding many companions scattered across its broad extent. Yet the same causes that had so terribly reduced the English population had possessed even greater opportunity for destruction in the neighboring country. France was empty; along the whole road from Calais to Paris, not a single human being appeared. In Paris a few remained, perhaps a hundred, who, resigned to their approaching fate, wandered through the capital\u2019s streets and gathered to speak of former times, with that liveliness and even cheerfulness which rarely abandons the people of this nation.\\n\\nThe English entered Paris without opposition. Its tall buildings and narrow streets were without life. A few pallid figures could be seen at their customary meeting place in the Tuileries; they wondered why the islanders had come to their doomed city\u2014for in the extremity of misery, sufferers always believe their own portion of calamity the most severe, just as, when enduring intense pain, we would exchange the particular torment under which we writhe for any other inflicted upon another part of the body.\"}"
