          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ef19a0f446bbcc69ca1c1e3a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The doctor was an old man; a very pleasant, kindly-looking old man when I woke him. I told him my brother and me had been over on Spanish Island hunting yesterday afternoon, and had camped on part of a raft we found. About midnight, I said, he must have kicked his gun in his sleep, because it went off and shot him in the leg. We wanted the doctor to go over there and mend it, without saying anything or letting anybody know, because we meant to come home this evening and surprise the family.\\n\\n\u201cWho are your folks?\u201d he asks.\\n\\n\u201cThe Phelpses, down yonder.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says. Then, after a moment:\\n\\n\u201cHow did you say he got shot?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe had a dream,\u201d I says, \u201cand it shot him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cRemarkable dream,\u201d he says.\\n\\nThen he lit his lantern, fetched his saddle-bags, and we set off. But when he saw the canoe, he didn\u2019t like her appearance\u2014said she was large enough for one, but didn\u2019t seem safe enough for two. I says:\\n\\n\u201cOh, you needn\u2019t be scared, sir; she carried the three of us easy enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat three?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, me and Sid, and\u2014and\u2014and _the guns;_ that\u2019s what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he says.\\n\\nStill, he put his foot on the gunwale and rocked her, then shook his head and said he reckoned he\u2019d look for a larger one. But all the others were locked and chained; so he took my canoe and told me to wait until he returned, or search farther around, or perhaps go home and get things ready for the surprise if I wanted to. But I said I didn\u2019t; so I explained exactly how to find the raft, and he started off.\\n\\nI got an idea soon afterward. I says to myself, suppose he can\u2019t mend that leg in three shakes of a sheep\u2019s tail, as the saying goes? Suppose it takes him three or four days? What are we going to do\u2014hang around there until he gives the whole thing away? No, sir; I know what _I\u2019ll_ do. I\u2019ll wait, and when he comes back, if he says he has to go again, I\u2019ll get down there too, even if I have to swim; then we\u2019ll tie him up, keep him, and push off down the river. When Tom is finished with him, we\u2019ll pay him whatever it\u2019s worth, or all we have, and then let him get ashore.\\n\\nSo I crawled into a lumber pile to sleep; and the next time I woke, the sun was high above my head! I rushed out and went to the doctor\u2019s house, but they told me he had left sometime during the night and hadn\u2019t returned yet.\"}"
