          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0a91c433d8efea75266bbfa9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Great God! If for a single moment I had imagined the hellish purpose of my fiendish enemy, I would sooner have exiled myself forever from my native land and wandered friendless across the earth than agreed to this wretched marriage. But, as though endowed with magical powers, the monster had blinded me to his true intentions; and while I believed I had prepared only my own death, I hastened that of a far more beloved victim.\\n\\nAs the date set for our marriage approached, whether through cowardice or some prophetic instinct, I felt my heart sink within me. Yet I concealed my feelings beneath an appearance of cheerfulness that brought smiles and happiness to my father\u2019s face, though it scarcely deceived Elizabeth\u2019s ever-watchful, discerning eyes. She anticipated our union with calm contentment, mixed with a little fear born of the misfortunes of the past: the fear that what now seemed certain, tangible happiness might soon dissolve into an airy dream, leaving behind only profound and lasting regret.\\n\\nPreparations were made, congratulatory visits were received, and everything wore a cheerful aspect. As best I could, I locked within my own heart the anxiety that consumed me and entered with apparent sincerity into my father\u2019s plans, though they might serve only as decorations for my tragedy. Through my father\u2019s efforts, the Austrian government had restored part of Elizabeth\u2019s inheritance to her. She possessed a small estate on the shores of Lake Como. It was agreed that immediately after our marriage we would go to Villa Lavenza and spend the first days of our happiness beside the beautiful lake on which it stood.\\n\\nMeanwhile, I took every precaution to protect myself should the fiend attack me openly. I constantly carried pistols and a dagger, remained alert against every deception, and by these means gained some measure of peace. Indeed, as the date 