          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"20316fcf2c84ebe5d5e7d84f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On that subject, therefore, I shall remain completely silent; and you may be sure that, once we are married, no ungenerous reproach will ever pass my lips.\u201d\\n\\nIt was now absolutely necessary to interrupt him.\\n\\n\u201cYou are too hasty, sir,\u201d she cried. \u201cYou forget that I have given no answer. Let me give it without any further delay. Accept my thanks for the compliment you are paying me. I am very aware of the honour of your proposal, but I cannot do anything except refuse it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am not now learning,\u201d replied Mr. Collins, with a formal wave of his hand, \u201cthat young ladies commonly reject the advances of the man they secretly intend to accept when he first asks for their favour; and that sometimes the refusal is repeated a second or even a third time. I am therefore not at all discouraged by what you have just said, and hope to lead you to the altar before long.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUpon my word, sir,\u201d cried Elizabeth, \u201cthat is a very strange hope after what I have declared. I assure you that I am not one of those young ladies\u2014if such young ladies exist\u2014who are bold enough to risk their happiness on the chance of being asked a second time. I am completely serious in refusing you. You could not make me happy, and I am convinced that I am the last woman in the world who could make you happy. Indeed, if your friend Lady Catherine knew me, I am sure she would find me entirely unfit for the position.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf it were certain that Lady Catherine would think so,\u201d said Mr. Collins very seriously, \u201cbut I cannot imagine that her Ladyship would disapprove of you at all. And you may be sure that, when I have the honour of seeing her again, I shall speak in the highest terms of your modesty, thrift, and other pleasing qualities.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, Mr. Collins, all praise of me will be unnecessary. You must allow me to judge for myself, and do me the courtesy of believing what I say. I wish you to be very happy and very rich, and by refusing your hand, I am doing everything I can to prevent you from being otherwise. In making me this offer, you must have satisfied your sense of duty toward my family, and you may take possession of the Longbourn estate when it comes to you without any guilt. This matter may therefore be considered finally settled.\u201d As she spoke, she rose and would have left the room, had not Mr. Collins addressed her again:\\n\\n\u201cWhen I have the honour of speaking to you next about this matter, I hope to receive a more favourable answer than the one you have now given me. Still, I am far from accusing you of cruelty at present, because I know it is the established custom of your sex to reject a man the first time he proposes; and perhaps you have already said as much as would encourage my suit while remaining consistent with the true delicacy of a woman\u2019s character.\u201d\"}"
