          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3aab911a4ae02526aae28b38\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma Woodhouse, handsome, intelligent, and wealthy, with a comfortable home and cheerful nature, appeared to possess many of life\u2019s greatest blessings; and had spent almost twenty-one years in the world with scarcely anything to trouble or upset her.\\n\\nShe was the younger of two daughters belonging to a most loving and indulgent father; and, after her sister\u2019s marriage, had become mistress of his household at a very early age. Her mother had been dead too long for Emma to retain more than a vague memory of her tenderness; and her place had been filled by an excellent governess, who had come scarcely short of a mother in affection.\\n\\nMiss Taylor had been part of Mr. Woodhouse\u2019s family for sixteen years, serving less as a governess than as a friend, deeply fond of both daughters, but especially of Emma. Between _them_, the closeness was rather that of sisters. Even before Miss Taylor had formally given up her position as governess, the gentleness of her disposition had scarcely permitted her to enforce restraint; and with the appearance of authority now long gone, they had lived together as two mutually devoted friends, Emma doing exactly as she pleased; she valued Miss Taylor\u2019s judgment highly, but was guided chiefly by her own.\\n\\nThe true drawbacks of Emma\u2019s situation were, in fact, the ability to have nearly everything her own way, and a tendency to think somewhat too highly of herself; these were the faults that threatened to diminish her many pleasures. Yet the danger was currently so unnoticed that she did not regard them as misfortunes in the least.\\n\\nSorrow arrived\u2014a mild sorrow\u2014but not in the form of any unpleasant self-awareness.\u2014Miss Taylor married. It was the loss of Miss Taylor that first brought Emma grief. On the wedding-day of this beloved friend, Emma first sat in sustained melancholy. Once the wedding was over and the newly married couple had departed, she and her father were left to dine together, with no expectation of a third person to brighten a long evening. Her father settled himself to sleep after dinner, as he usually did, leaving her only to sit and reflect on what she had lost.\\n\\nThe marriage promised complete happiness for her friend. Mr. Weston was a man of irreproachable character, comfortable means, appropriate age, and agreeable manners; and Emma found some comfort in remembering the selfless, generous friendship with which she had always desired and encouraged the match. Still, it had been a dark morning\u2019s business for her. Miss Taylor\u2019s absence would be felt every hour of every day. She remembered her former kindness\u2014the kindness and affection of sixteen years\u2014how Miss Taylor had taught her and played with her from the age of five\u2014how she had devoted all her abilities to keeping Emma attached and entertained when she was well\u2014and how she had nursed her through the many illnesses of childhood.\"}"
