          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d576230b1301826f02a04524\",\"rewritten_text\":\"These deputies had been sent by their parties to Adrian, who had been chosen as arbiter. They had traveled from Paris to Calais through empty towns and deserted countryside, all the while nursing a fierce hatred for one another. Now they argued their separate cases with complete partisan passion.\\n\\nBy questioning the deputies separately and investigating at length, we learned the true situation in Paris. Since Parliament had appointed him Ryland\u2019s deputy, all the surviving English had submitted to Adrian. He was our captain, meant to lead us from our homeland to unknown lands; our lawgiver and protector. When we first planned our emigration, we had not expected our members to remain separated. The command of the whole group, rising through its ranks of authority, ended with the Earl of Windsor. But unforeseen events changed our plans and caused most of our people to remain apart from the supreme chief for nearly two months. They had crossed over in two separate groups, and when they reached Paris, disagreements broke out between them.\\n\\nThey had found Paris deserted. When the plague first appeared, returning travelers and merchants, along with letters, regularly told us how disease was spreading across the continent. But as deaths increased, this contact weakened and finally stopped. Even in England, communication between different parts of the island became slow and rare. No ship crossed the waters between Calais and Dover. If some sorrowful traveler, wanting to learn whether his relatives were alive, left the French shore to return to us, the hungry sea often swallowed his small boat. Or, after a day or two, the disease infected him, and he died before he could report the desolation of France. We therefore knew very little about conditions on the continent, and still held some vague hope of finding many companions there. But the same forces that had so terribly reduced the English population had caused even greater destruction in the neighboring country. France was empty. Along the entire road from Calais to Paris, not one person was seen. In Paris, perhaps a hundred people remained. Accepting their approaching fate, they wandered through the capital\u2019s streets and gathered to talk about earlier times, with the liveliness and even cheerfulness that rarely leaves the people of this nation.\\n\\nThe English took possession of Paris without opposition. Its tall buildings and narrow streets were lifeless. A few pale figures could be seen at the usual meeting place in the Tuileries. They wondered why the islanders had come to their doomed city\u2014for in extreme misery, sufferers always believe that their own share of disaster is the worst. Just as, when we endure severe pain, we would trade the particular torment affecting us for any other pain placed elsewhere in the body.\"}"
