{"request_id":"c555f8956d848c9c9e79e257","rewritten_text":"But this thought, which sustained me at the beginning of my career, now serves only to cast me deeper into the dust. All my hopes and speculations are worthless, and, like the archangel who sought omnipotence, I am bound in an everlasting hell. My imagination was lively, while my powers of analysis and application were formidable; through their union I conceived the notion and accomplished the creation of a man. Even now I cannot recall without fervour the dreams I entertained while the work remained unfinished. In thought I walked through heaven, at one moment rejoicing in my abilities, at another consumed by visions of their consequences. From childhood I had been filled with lofty hopes and ambitious aims; yet how far have I fallen! Oh, my friend, had you known me as I once was, you would scarcely recognise me in this degraded condition. Despair seldom entered my heart; a glorious destiny seemed to carry me forward, until I fell—never, never to rise again.”

Must I, then, lose this admirable being? I have yearned for a friend; I have searched for one who would understand and love me. Behold, upon these desolate seas I have found such a person, yet I fear I have gained him only to learn his worth and then lose him. I would reconcile him to life, but he rejects the thought.

“I thank you, Walton,” he said, “for your benevolent intentions toward so wretched a creature; but when you speak of new bonds and renewed affections, do you imagine that any can replace those who are gone? Can any man be to me what Clerval was, or any woman become another Elizabeth? Even when affection is not powerfully stirred by some superior excellence, the companions of our childhood always hold a particular influence over our minds, one that scarcely any later friend can acquire. They know our childish dispositions, which, however altered afterward, are never wholly destroyed; and they can judge our conduct with greater certainty concerning the honesty of our motives. A sister or brother can never, unless such signs appeared in early life, suspect the other of deceit or treachery, whereas another friend, however devoted, may, despite himself, be regarded with distrust. But I possessed friends dear not merely through habit and association, but because of their own virtues; and wherever I may be, the gentle voice of my Elizabeth and Clerval’s conversation will forever sound in my ear. They are dead, and only one feeling in such solitude can persuade me to continue living. If I were occupied in some noble enterprise or purpose, filled with broad usefulness to my fellow creatures, then I could live to complete it."}

