content.text:
{"request_id":"58de768d377a3dbc1882bcbe","rewritten_text":"Yet even now I had not drunk the bitter potion to the last drop; I was not fully convinced of my loss. I did not yet feel in every heartbeat, every nerve, and every thought that I alone remained of my kind—that I was the LAST MAN.

The sky grew dark, and a light rain began at sunset. Even the eternal heavens weep, I thought; is it shameful, then, for mortal man to spend himself in tears? I remembered the old stories in which human beings dissolve through weeping into ever-flowing fountains. Ah, if only that were so; then my fate would in some way resemble the watery deaths of Adrian and Clara. Grief is strange; it weaves a web from every shape and change around it, tracing the story of its sorrow. It joins itself to all living nature and finds nourishment in every object; like light, it fills everything, and, like light, it gives everything its own colours.

In my search I had wandered some distance from the place where I had been cast ashore, and reached one of the watch-towers that stood at regular intervals along the Italian coast. I was glad of shelter, and glad to find something made by human hands after staring so long at nature’s bleak emptiness. I entered and climbed the rough, winding stairs to the guard-room. Fortune had been kind enough to leave no terrible trace of its former occupants. A few planks, laid across two iron trestles and covered with dried corn leaves, made the bed offered to me. An open chest containing some half-rotten biscuits awakened an appetite that may have been there already, though I had not noticed it until then. I was also tormented by a fierce, burning thirst, caused by the seawater I had drunk and by the exhaustion of my body. Kind nature had made the satisfaction of these needs pleasurable, so that—even I!—felt refreshed and calm as I ate the poor food and drank a little sour wine from a flask left half full in the abandoned dwelling. Then I stretched myself on the bed, not unworthy of a shipwreck victim. The earthy smell of the dried leaves soothed me after the hateful scent of seaweed. I forgot my loneliness. I looked neither backward nor forward; my senses settled into rest. I fell asleep and dreamed of beloved inland scenes: haymakers, and a shepherd’s whistle to his dog when he called for help driving the flock into the fold; sights and sounds from the mountain life of my childhood, which I had long forgotten."}

