{"request_id":"9966a8dc900fd0074a181320","rewritten_text":"The next day he did not leave the house and spent most of his time in his own room, sick with a wild fear of death, yet indifferent to life itself. The feeling of being hunted, trapped, and tracked down had begun to control him. If the tapestry merely stirred in the wind, he trembled. The dead leaves blown against the leaded windows seemed to him like his own wasted resolutions and reckless regrets. Whenever he closed his eyes, he saw the sailor’s face peering through the mist-streaked glass, and horror seemed once again to place its hand upon his heart.

But perhaps it was only his imagination that had summoned vengeance from the night and placed those hideous forms of punishment before him. Real life was chaotic, but there was something terribly logical about the imagination. It was the imagination that made remorse follow sin. It was the imagination that made every crime produce its deformed offspring. In the ordinary world of fact, the wicked were not punished, nor the good rewarded. Success went to the strong, and failure was forced upon the weak. That was all. Besides, if a stranger had been prowling around the house, the servants or keepers would have seen him. If footprints had been found in the flower beds, the gardeners would have reported them. Yes, it had been nothing but fancy. Sibyl Vane’s brother had not returned to kill him. He had sailed away in his ship and sunk in some winter sea. From him, at least, he was safe. Why, the man did not know who he was and could not know. The mask of youth had protected him.

And yet, if it had been only an illusion, how terrible it was to think that conscience could summon such dreadful phantoms, give them visible form, and make them move before one! What kind of life would his be if, day and night, the shadows of his crime peered at him from silent corners, mocked him from hidden places, whispered in his ear as he sat at the feast, and woke him with icy fingers while he slept? As the thought passed through his mind, he grew pale with terror, and the air seemed suddenly to turn colder. Oh, in what wild hour of madness he had killed his friend! How ghastly the mere memory of the scene was! He saw it all again. Every hideous detail returned with greater horror. From the black cavern of time, terrible and wrapped in scarlet, rose the image of his sin."}

