          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cab5b7e94e01d10c01497e9f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He mounted the fiery horse that had carried him to victory in Greece. He wore himself out with exhausting exercise, dulling the pain of his troubled mind through physical sensation.\\n\\nSlowly, he recovered his senses. At last, like someone emerging from the effects of poison, he raised his head above the vapours of fever and passion into the calm air of reflection. He considered what should be done. He was first struck by how much time had passed since madness, rather than reason, had guided his actions. A month had gone by, and in all that time he had not seen Evadne. Her hold over him, tied to only a few of the lasting feelings in his heart, had greatly weakened. He was no longer her slave, nor her lover. He would never see her again, and by fully returning to Perdita, he would earn her trust.\\n\\nYet, as he made this decision, his imagination brought before him the miserable home of the Greek girl\u2014a home she had refused to exchange for greater luxury because of her noble principles. He remembered the splendour of her appearance and circumstances when he had first known her. He remembered her life in Constantinople, surrounded by every sign of Eastern magnificence; and he thought of her present poverty, her daily labour, her lonely condition, and her faded, hunger-stricken face. Compassion filled his heart. He would see her once more. He would find some way to restore her to society and to the enjoyment of her rank. After that, their separation would happen naturally.\\n\\nThen he thought again of how, throughout this long month, he had avoided Perdita, fleeing from her as though from the stings of his own conscience. But he was awake now. All this would be corrected, and future devotion would erase the memory of this single stain on the peace of their life. The thought made him cheerful, and he calmly and firmly planned the conduct he would follow. He remembered that he had promised Perdita to attend that very evening\u201419 October, the anniversary of his election as Protector\u2014a festival held in his honour. May the festival prove a good sign of the happiness of the years ahead. First, he would visit Evadne briefly. He would not remain, but he owed her an explanation and some compensation for his long, unexplained absence. Then he would go to Perdita, to the world he had forgotten, to the duties of society, the splendour of rank, and the enjoyment of power.\\n\\nAfter the scene described in the preceding pages, Perdita had expected a complete change in Raymond\u2019s manner and conduct. She looked forward to open communication and a return to the affectionate habits that had once made her life so happy.\"}"
