          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8fa60f2bcc146121c5b48510\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 12th. A remarkable change occurred in the balloon\u2019s direction, and though I had fully expected it, the event gave me the most unmistakable pleasure. After reaching, on its previous course, approximately the twentieth parallel of southern latitude, it suddenly veered eastward at a sharp angle, and continued in that direction throughout the day, keeping almost, if not entirely, within the exact plane of the lunar ellipse. One notable result of this alteration in course was a very distinct swaying of the car\u2014a motion that continued, to a greater or lesser extent, for many hours.\\n\\n\u201cApril 13th. I was again greatly frightened by a recurrence of the loud, crackling noise that had terrified me on the tenth. I reflected long on the matter, but could reach no satisfactory explanation. The earth\u2019s apparent diameter had diminished considerably, now subtending from the balloon an angle of scarcely more than twenty-five degrees. The moon was wholly invisible, being nearly at my zenith. I remained in the plane of the ellipse, but advanced only slightly eastward.\\n\\n\u201cApril 14th. The earth\u2019s diameter decreased with extraordinary rapidity. To-day I became firmly convinced that the balloon was actually ascending the line of apsides toward the point of perigee\u2014in other words, following the direct path that would carry it straight to the moon at the portion of its orbit nearest the earth. The moon itself was immediately overhead and therefore concealed from my sight. Much prolonged labor was required to condense the atmosphere.\\n\\n\u201cApril 15th. The continents and seas upon the earth could no longer be distinguished, even in outline, with anything like clarity. About noon I became conscious, for the third time, of that dreadful sound which had previously so amazed me. This time, however, it lasted several moments and grew louder as it continued. At last, while I stood stupefied and terrified, expecting some unknown and horrible destruction, the car shook with tremendous violence, and a vast flaming mass of material, whose nature I could not discern, rushed past the balloon with the roar and thunder of a thousand voices. When my fear and astonishment had somewhat abated, I found little difficulty in believing it to be some enormous volcanic fragment cast out from the world toward which I was rapidly drawing, and probably belonging to that peculiar class of substances sometimes found upon the earth and called meteoric stones for lack of a better name.\\n\\n\u201cApril 16th. To-day, looking upward as best I could through each side window in turn, I saw, to my great delight, a very small part of the moon\u2019s disk extending, as it were, on every side beyond the balloon\u2019s enormous circumference.\"}"
