          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7d38c01153256ead69bc4ce1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The day went by much as the preceding one had. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley had passed part of the morning with the invalid, who continued, though slowly, to recover; and, in the evening, Elizabeth joined them in the drawing-room. The loo table, however, was not brought out. Mr. Darcy was writing, while Miss Bingley, seated beside him, watched his letter taking shape and repeatedly diverted his attention with messages for her sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley were playing piquet, and Mrs. Hurst was watching their game.\\n\\nElizabeth took up her needlework and found sufficient amusement in observing what passed between Darcy and his companion. The lady\u2019s continual praises, whether of his handwriting, the regularity of his lines, or the length of his letter, together with the complete indifference with which he received them, made a curious dialogue, and perfectly agreed with Elizabeth\u2019s opinion of both.\\n\\n\u201cHow pleased Miss Darcy will be to receive such a letter!\u201d\\n\\nHe made no reply.\\n\\n\u201cYou write remarkably quickly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are mistaken. I write rather slowly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow many letters you must have to write over the course of a year! Business letters as well! How detestable I should find them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is fortunate, then, that they fall to me rather than to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPray tell your sister that I am eager to see her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have already told her so once, at your request.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI fear you do not like your pen. Let me mend it for you. I am remarkably good at mending pens.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you\u2014but I always mend my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do you manage to write so evenly?\u201d\\n\\nHe remained silent.\\n\\n\u201cTell your sister I am delighted to hear of her improvement on the harp, and pray inform her that I am quite enchanted by her lovely little design for a table, which I