          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5b299e5ef8f38b9114a622a2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No letter of condolence had been sent to Ireland. The omission had been punished upon the sinner; for when poor Lady Elliot herself died, no letter of condolence arrived at Kellynch, and there was therefore too much reason to fear that the Dalrymples regarded the connection as ended. How this troublesome matter might be repaired, and they be received again as cousins, was the question; and, in a more sensible way, neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot considered it insignificant. \u201cFamily connections were always worth preserving, and good society always worth pursuing; Lady Dalrymple had taken a house in Laura Place for three months, and would be living handsomely. She had been at Bath the previous year, and Lady Russell had heard her described as a charming woman. It was greatly to be wished that the connection might be restored, if it could be done without any sacrifice of propriety on the Elliots\u2019 part.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter, however, would employ his own methods, and eventually wrote his right honourable cousin a very elegant letter, full of explanation, regret, and entreaty. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot admired it; but it achieved its purpose, producing three lines of scrawl from the Dowager Viscountess: \u201cShe was highly honoured, and would be happy in their acquaintance.\u201d The labour of the affair was finished, and its pleasures began. They paid visits in Laura Place, obtained the cards of Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and the Honourable Miss Carteret, to be displayed wherever they would be most conspicuous; and \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place\u201d\u2014\u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret\u201d\u2014were mentioned to everyone.\\n\\nAnne felt ashamed. Even had Lady Dalrymple and her daughter been particularly agreeable, she would have been embarrassed by the excitement they caused; but they were nothing. They possessed no superiority of manner, accomplishment, or understanding. Lady Dalrymple had earned the reputation of \u201ca charming woman\u201d because she smiled and gave every person a civil reply. Miss Carteret, with even less to say, was so plain and awkward that she could never have been admitted into Camden Place except for her birth.\\n\\nLady Russell admitted that she had expected more; yet \u201cit was an acquaintance worth having.\u201d And when Anne ventured to express her opinion of them to Mr Elliot, he agreed that they were nothing in themselves, but still insisted that, as a family connection, as good society, and as people capable of gathering good society about them, they had their importance. Anne smiled and said,\\n\\n\u201cMy notion of good company, Mr Elliot, is the company of intelligent, well-informed people, who have much to say; that is what I call good company.\u201d\"}"
