          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"24f9a36dd448cfaf43400021\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Meanwhile, the force of gravity would steadily diminish in proportion to the squares of the distances, and so, with a velocity increasing enormously, I would eventually reach those remote regions where the earth\u2019s attraction was overtaken by the moon\u2019s. For that reason, I did not think it necessary to burden myself with provisions for more than forty days.\\n\\n\u201cThere remained, however, another difficulty that caused me some concern. It has been observed that, during balloon ascents to any considerable height, people suffer not only pain in breathing but also great discomfort in the head and body, often accompanied by nosebleeds and other alarming symptoms, all becoming more troublesome as the altitude increases. This was a somewhat disturbing thought. Was it not likely that these symptoms would continue to worsen, perhaps until death itself brought them to an end? In the end, I decided that they would not. Their cause was to be found in the gradual removal of the usual atmospheric pressure from the body\u2019s surface, and the resulting swelling of the superficial blood vessels\u2014not in any actual breakdown of the animal system, as occurs in difficulty breathing, when the atmosphere is too thin to renew the blood properly in one of the heart\u2019s ventricles. Except for the failure of that renewal, I could see no reason why life could not continue even in a vacuum; for the expansion and contraction of the chest, commonly called breathing, is purely muscular action, and the cause rather than the effect of respiration. In short, I believed that, as the body grew accustomed to the absence of atmospheric pressure, the painful sensations would gradually lessen\u2014and to endure them while they lasted, I trusted confidently in the iron strength of my constitution.\\n\\n\u201cThus, Your Excellencies, I have described some, though by no means all, of the considerations 