          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7cf892cd572d43d455a9cf84\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental qualities called analytical are, by themselves, difficult to analyze. We recognize them only by their effects. Among other things, we know that, when possessed to an unusual degree, they give their owner intense pleasure. Just as a strong man takes pride in his physical power and enjoys exercises that use his muscles, so the analyst delights in the mental activity that untangles problems. He enjoys even the simplest tasks that bring his talent into play. He likes riddles, puzzles, and hieroglyphics, and in solving each he shows a sharpness of insight that seems almost supernatural to ordinary minds. His conclusions, reached through the very essence of method, seem in fact to have all the appearance of intuition.\\n\\nThe ability to solve problems may be greatly strengthened by mathematical study, especially by its highest branch, which has been unfairly called analysis merely because it works backward. Yet calculation is not, by itself, analysis. A chess player, for example, calculates without analyzing. It follows that the effect of chess on the mind is widely misunderstood. I am not writing a treatise, but merely introducing a somewhat unusual narrative with observations made almost at random; I will therefore say that the higher and more useful powers of the reflective mind are exercised more clearly by the quiet game of draughts than by all the elaborate frivolity of chess. In chess, where the pieces move in different and unusual ways and have changing values, people mistake complexity for depth, as they often do. Attention is intensely engaged. If it weakens for even a moment, an oversight occurs and leads to harm or defeat. Since the possible moves are not only numerous but complicated, such oversights become more likely; and in nine games out of ten, the more focused player defeats the more naturally clever one. In draughts, by contrast, where the moves are distinctive and vary little, mistakes caused by carelessness are less likely. Since simple attention is left relatively unused, any advantage either player gains comes from superior insight. To make this less abstract, imagine a game of draughts reduced to four kings, where, naturally, no oversight need be expected. It is clear that, if the players are otherwise equal, victory can be decided only by some unusual move resulting from a powerful effort of intellect.\"}"
