          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"82b24a6d3ea40efc992177fd\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Having seen our friend properly settled in his new office, we turned our attention toward Windsor. Its nearness to London was enough to remove any sense of painful separation when we left Raymond and Perdita. We said goodbye to them in the Protectoral Palace. It was charming to see my sister enter, as it were, into the spirit of the drama and try to fill her position with appropriate dignity. Her inward pride and modesty were now more than ever at war. Her shyness was not affected, but arose from a fear of not being properly valued, that slight sense of the world\u2019s neglect which also marked Raymond. Yet Perdita thought more constantly of others than he did; and part of her bashfulness came from a desire to spare those around her any feeling of inferiority\u2014a feeling that never entered her own mind. Given his birth and education, Idris would have been better suited to the forms of ceremony; but the ease with which habit enabled him to perform such actions made them tedious, whereas Perdita, despite every disadvantage, plainly enjoyed her situation. She was too full of new ideas to feel much sorrow at our departure. She took an affectionate leave of us and promised to visit soon, but she did not regret the circumstances that had brought about our separation. Raymond\u2019s spirits were boundless; he did not know what to do with his newly acquired power. His head was crowded with plans, though he had settled on none; but he promised himself, his friends, and the world that the era of his Protectorship would be marked by some act of extraordinary glory. Thus we spoke of them and reflected as, with our numbers reduced, we returned to Windsor Castle. We were intensely relieved to have escaped political turmoil and sought our solitude with renewed eagerness. We had no lack of occupation; but my restless energy was now directed solely toward intellectual effort, and I found hard study an excellent remedy for the fever of spirit that, in idleness, would surely have overtaken me. Perdita had allowed us to bring Clara back with us to Windsor, and she, together with my two lovely infants, was a constant source of interest and amusement.\\n\\nThe only thing that disturbed our peace was Adrian\u2019s health. It was clearly failing, though he showed no symptom that could reveal his illness\u2014unless his brighter eyes, animated expression, and flushed cheeks made us fear consumption. Yet he suffered neither pain nor apprehension. He devoted himself eagerly to books and rested from his studies in the company he loved best: his sister and me.\"}"
