          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"abb3ddb98a370c594384dda9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was plenty of cold water with which to finish the feast. After a while Becky suggested that they move on again. Tom was silent for a moment. Then he said:\\n\\n\u201cBecky, can you bear it if I tell you something?\u201d\\n\\nBecky\u2019s face went pale, but she thought she could.\\n\\n\u201cWell, then, Becky, we must stay here, where there\u2019s water to drink. That little piece is our last candle!\u201d\\n\\nBecky burst into tears and sobs. Tom did what he could to comfort her, but with little effect. At last Becky said:\\n\\n\u201cTom!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Becky?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ll miss us and search for us!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, they will! Certainly they will!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMaybe they\u2019re searching for us now, Tom.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, I suppose they may be. I hope they are.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen would they miss us, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen they get back to the boat, I suppose.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTom, it might be dark then\u2014would they notice that we hadn\u2019t come?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. But in any case, your mother would miss you as soon as they got home.\u201d\\n\\nThe frightened look on Becky\u2019s face brought Tom to his senses, and he realized he had made a mistake. Becky was not supposed to have gone home that night! The children fell silent and thoughtful. A moment later, a fresh outburst of grief from Becky showed Tom that the thought in his mind had struck hers as well\u2014that Sunday morning might be half over before Mrs. Thatcher discovered that Becky was not at Mrs. Harper\u2019s.\\n\\nThe children fixed their eyes on their piece of candle and watched it melt slowly and mercilessly away; saw the half-inch wick stand alone at last; saw the weak flame rise and fall, climb the thin column of smoke, linger at its top for a moment, and then\u2014the horror of complete darkness descended!\\n\\nHow long afterward it was that Becky slowly became aware she was crying in Tom\u2019s arms, neither of them could tell. All they knew was that, after what seemed an immense stretch of time, both awoke from a dead stupor of sleep and returned to their misery. Tom said it might be Sunday now\u2014perhaps Monday. He tried to get Becky to talk, but her sorrow was too overwhelming; all her hope was gone. Tom said they must have been missed long ago, and surely the search was continuing. He would shout, and perhaps someone would come. He tried it, but in the darkness the distant echoes sounded so terrible that he did not try again.\\n\\nThe hours dragged away, and hunger came to torment the captives once more. A portion of Tom\u2019s half of the cake remained; they divided it and ate. But they seemed hungrier than before.\"}"
