          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5732f9e48abed5099d140bc0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNext, we constructed a wire fence.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA wire fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. You yourself suggested it two or three years ago.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, I remember\u2014the occasion when the Church first tested her strength against us, and soon decided it wiser to wait for a more hopeful season. Well, how have you laid out the fence?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI run twelve enormously strong wires\u2014bare, not insulated\u2014from a large dynamo in the cave\u2014a dynamo with no brushes but one positive and one negative\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, exactly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe wires leave the cave and enclose a circle of level ground one hundred yards across; they form twelve separate fences, ten feet apart\u2014that is, twelve circles inside one another\u2014and their ends return to the cave.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood; continue.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe fences are attached to stout oak posts spaced only three feet apart, and the posts are buried five feet deep.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is solid enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. Outside the cave, the wires have no ground connection. They leave from the dynamo\u2019s positive brush; there is a ground connection through the negative brush; the opposite ends return to the cave, each grounded separately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, no, that will never do!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy not?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt costs too much\u2014it wastes power. You need no ground connection except the one through the negative brush. Bring the other end of every wire back into the cave and secure each one separately, but _without_ grounding it. Now consider the saving. A cavalry charge rushes at the fence; you use no power and spend no money, because there is only one ground connection until the horses strike the wire. The instant they touch it, they make a connection with the negative brush _through the ground_ and fall dead. Don\u2019t you understand? You expend no energy until it is required; the lightning is waiting, ready, like a charge in a gun, but costs nothing until you discharge it. Yes, the single ground connection\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course! I can\u2019t imagine how I missed that. It is not merely cheaper, but more effective than the other arrangement, since broken or tangled wires would do no damage.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, particularly if we keep a telltale in the cave and cut off the broken wire. Well, proceed. The gatlings?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes\u2014that has been arranged. In the middle of the innermost circle, on a broad platform six feet high, I have placed a battery of thirteen Gatling guns and stocked it with ample ammunition.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is right. They cover every approach, and when the Church\u2019s knights come, there will be music. The edge of the precipice above the cave\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have put a wire fence there, along with a Gatling.\u201d\"}"
