          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e6e131a810513afff7f894b7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For years, Dorian Gray was unable to escape this book\u2019s influence. Or perhaps it would be truer to say that he never wished to escape it. From Paris he obtained no fewer than nine large-paper copies of the first edition, having each bound in a different colour to match his shifting moods and the changing fancies of a nature over which he sometimes seemed almost wholly to have lost command. The hero, that extraordinary young Parisian in whom romantic and scientific temperaments were so oddly mingled, became for him a sort of prophetic image of himself. Indeed, the entire book appeared to tell the story of his own life, written before he had lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect he was luckier than the novel\u2019s fantastic hero. He never knew\u2014nor, in truth, had any reason to know\u2014that rather grotesque fear of mirrors, polished metal, and still water which overtook the young Parisian so early, caused by the sudden decay of a beauty that had once apparently been so remarkable. With an almost cruel delight\u2014and perhaps cruelty has a place in nearly every joy, as surely as it does in every pleasure\u2014he would read the book\u2019s final portion, with its genuinely tragic, though somewhat exaggerated, account of the grief and despair of a man who had himself lost what he had valued most dearly in others and in the world.\\n\\nFor the remarkable beauty that had so captivated Basil Hallward, and many others besides, seemed never to desert him. Even those who had heard the worst things about him\u2014and from time to time strange rumours of his way of life spread through London and became the talk of the clubs\u2014could not believe anything dishonourable of him when they saw him. He always appeared like one who had kept himself unstained by the world. Men who spoke coarsely fell silent when Dorian Gray entered the room. There was something in the purity of his face that reproached them. His mere presence seemed to bring back the memory of the innocence they had corrupted. They wondered how someone so charming and graceful could have escaped the taint of an age both sordid and sensual.\\n\\nOften, after returning from one of those mysterious, lengthy absences that prompted such strange speculation among those who were his friends, or believed themselves to be, he would steal upstairs to the locked room, unlock the door with the key that now never left him, and stand with a mirror before the portrait Basil Hallward had painted of him, looking first at the wicked, aging face on the canvas, and then at the beautiful young face smiling back from the polished glass.\"}"
