{"request_id":"fc54b0849a46c5e6aa1c344c","rewritten_text":"My father was gone, and his memory had been the life of my life. I might feel gratitude toward another, but I could never again love or hope as I once had. Everything was suffering; even my pleasures were endured rather than enjoyed. I was like a lonely place among mountains, enclosed on every side by steep black cliffs, where no warmth could enter and from which there was no path to sunnier fields. So, although friendship’s spirit comforted me for a time, it could not restore me. It came like a gentle visitor; it left, and I scarcely felt the loss. The spirit of existence was dead within me. Do not be surprised, then, that when it came I did not welcome it more gladly, or that when it departed I did not mourn more bitterly the best gift of heaven—a friend.

My friend’s name was Woodville. I will briefly tell his story, so that you may understand how cold my heart must have been not to be warmed by his eloquent words and tender sympathy. Since he too was deeply unhappy, we were well suited to comfort each other, if Misery had not turned my heart to stone with the gaze of Medusa. Woodville’s troubles did not come from the deepest part of his heart, as mine had. His was a natural grief, one that would purify rather than destroy the heart, and from which he might emerge, once its shadow had passed, brighter and happier than before.

Woodville was the son of a poor clergyman and had received a classical education. He was one of the very few whom Fortune favors from birth, giving them every gift of mind and body with limitless generosity. Under her special protection, no slight flaw or passing disappointment was allowed to touch them. She seemed to have formed his mind with an excellence that no impurity could stain, and his understanding was such that no error could mislead it. His genius was extraordinary, and when it rose like a bright star in the east, every eye turned toward it in admiration. He was a poet. That name has been degraded so often that it cannot express all that he was. He was like a poet of ancient times, crowned by the Muses in his cradle, with bees feeding upon his lips. As he moved among other men, he seemed surrounded by a heavenly glow that set him apart and raised him above them. His remarkable beauty, the dazzling fire of his eyes, and the rich music of his words, which filled listeners with silent, ecstatic wonder, made him seem superior to everyone else; beside him, they appeared created only to serve his greater excellence."}

