{"request_id":"dc5b918dfb77bc4e893be1ea","rewritten_text":"Many American ladies, when they leave their homeland, take on an appearance of lasting ill health, believing it to be a mark of European refinement. Mrs. Otis, however, had never made that mistake. She had a magnificent constitution and an extraordinary amount of energy. In fact, in many ways she was quite English, and a perfect example of the truth that nowadays we have almost everything in common with America—except, of course, the language. Her eldest son had been named Washington by his parents in a moment of patriotism, a decision he never stopped regretting. He was a fair-haired, fairly handsome young man who had prepared himself for American diplomacy by leading the German at the Newport Casino for three seasons in a row. Even in London he was known as an excellent dancer. Gardenias and the peerage were his only weaknesses; otherwise, he was extremely sensible. Miss Virginia E. Otis was a fifteen-year-old girl, graceful and beautiful as a fawn, with a bold, lively look in her large blue eyes. She was a remarkable horsewoman and had once raced old Lord Bilton twice around the park on her pony. She won by a length and a half, just before the statue of Achilles, to the great delight of the young Duke of Cheshire. He proposed to her on the spot, and his guardians sent him back to Eton that very night, weeping bitterly. After Virginia came the twins, who were usually called ‘The Stars and Stripes’ because they were always being spanked. They were charming boys and, apart from the worthy Minister, the only true republicans in the family.

Since Canterville Chase was seven miles from Ascot, the nearest railway station, Mr. Otis had telegraphed for a waggonette to meet them. They set off on their drive in high spirits. It was a beautiful July evening, and the air was filled with the delicate scent of the pine woods. From time to time they heard a wood pigeon cooing over its own sweet voice, or saw the pheasant’s shining breast deep among the rustling ferns. Little squirrels watched them from the beech trees as they passed, while rabbits darted through the brushwood and over the mossy mounds, their white tails raised in the air. But as they entered the avenue leading to Canterville Chase, the sky suddenly darkened with clouds. A strange stillness seemed to settle over the air, a large flock of rooks flew silently above them, and before they reached the house, several heavy drops of rain had fallen.

An old woman stood on the steps to welcome them. She was neatly dressed in black silk, with a white cap and apron."}

