          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6dcda22cfb6fc454ce0236df\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Well, three or four months slipped by, and winter was well along by then. I had been attending school nearly all the time and could spell, read, and write a little, and could recite the multiplication table as far as six times seven is thirty-five. I don\u2019t believe I could ever have gone beyond that, even if I lived forever. I never had much faith in mathematics, anyhow.\\n\\nAt first I hated school, but after a while I got so I could endure it. Whenever I grew especially tired, I played hookey, and the whipping I received the next day did me good and lifted my spirits. Thus, the longer I attended, the easier it became. I was also getting somewhat accustomed to the widow\u2019s ways, and they didn\u2019t grate on me quite so much. Living in a house and sleeping in a bed weighed on me pretty heavily most of the time, but before the cold weather came I would sometimes slip away and sleep in the woods, which gave me a rest. I still preferred the old ways, though I was beginning to like the new ones a little, too. The widow said I was improving slowly but surely and doing very satisfactorily. She said she wasn\u2019t ashamed of me.\\n\\nOne morning at breakfast I happened to knock over the salt-cellar. I reached for some salt as quickly as I could, intending to toss it over my left shoulder and ward off the bad luck, but Miss Watson got there first and stopped me. She said, \u201cTake your hands away, Huckleberry; what a mess you are always making!\u201d The widow spoke up kindly for me, but I knew well enough that wouldn\u2019t prevent the bad luck. After breakfast I went out feeling anxious and unsteady, wondering where it would strike me and what form it would take. There are ways to avoid certain kinds of bad luck, but this wasn\u2019t one of those kinds; so I didn\u2019t try to do anything, but merely trudged along downhearted and watchful.\\n\\nI went down to the front garden and climbed over the stile in the high board fence. An inch of fresh snow covered the ground, and I noticed someone\u2019s tracks. They had come up from the quarry, lingered around the stile for a while, and then continued along the garden fence. It was strange that they hadn\u2019t come in after waiting there so long. I couldn\u2019t understand it. Somehow, it was very curious. I meant to follow the tracks around, but first I bent down to examine them.\"}"
