          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"78bd89f4557d9f24bf67955d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Mr. Hiram B. Otis, the American Minister, bought Canterville Chase, everyone told him he was making a very foolish decision, since there was no doubt the place was haunted. Indeed, Lord Canterville himself, a man of scrupulous honour, had felt obliged to mention the fact to Mr. Otis when they discussed the terms.\\n\\n\u201cWe have not wished to live there ourselves,\u201d said Lord Canterville, \u201csince my grandaunt, the Dowager Duchess of Bolton, was frightened into a fit from which she never truly recovered. Two skeleton hands had been placed on her shoulders while she was dressing for dinner. And I feel bound to tell you, Mr. Otis, that the ghost has been seen by several living members of my family, as well as by the parish rector, the Reverend Augustus Dampier, who is a Fellow of King\u2019s College, Cambridge. After the unfortunate incident involving the Duchess, none of our younger servants would remain with us, and Lady Canterville often slept very little at night because of the mysterious noises coming from the corridor and the library.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy Lord,\u201d replied the Minister, \u201cI will take the furniture and the ghost at an assessed value. I come from a modern country, where we have everything money can buy; and with all our lively young men painting the Old World red and carrying off your finest actresses and prima donnas, I suppose that if there were such a thing as a ghost in Europe, we would have it at home very soon, either in one of our public museums or traveling the roads as a show.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI fear the ghost does exist,\u201d said Lord Canterville with a smile, \u201cthough it may have resisted the offers of your enterprising impresarios. It has been well known for three centuries\u2014since 1584, in fact\u2014and it always appears before the death of a member of our family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, so does the family doctor, for that matter, Lord Canterville. But there is no such thing as a ghost, sir, and I suppose the laws of Nature are not going to be suspended for the sake of the British aristocracy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are certainly very natural in America,\u201d answered Lord Canterville, who did not entirely understand Mr. Otis\u2019s last remark. \u201cIf you do not mind having a ghost in the house, then everything is settled. Only remember that I warned you.\u201d\\n\\nA few weeks later, the purchase was completed, and at the end of the season the Minister and his family went down to Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis, who, as Miss Lucretia R. Tappan of West 53rd Street, had been a celebrated New York belle, was now a very handsome middle-aged woman, with fine eyes and a superb profile.\"}"
