rote Lord Canterville to say so, and Lord Canterville replied with great pleasure, sending his warmest congratulations to the Minister\u2019s worthy wife.\\n\\nThe Otises, however, were mistaken, for the ghost was still in the house. Though now almost an invalid, he was far from willing to let matters rest, especially after hearing that the guests included the young Duke of Cheshire. The Duke\u2019s great-uncle, Lord Francis Stilton, had once wagered a hundred guineas with Colonel Carbury that he would play dice with the Canterville ghost. The next morning, Lord Francis was found lying on the card-room floor in such a helpless paralytic condition that, although he lived to a great age, he was never again able to say anything except \u2018Double Sixes.\u2019\"}"
