          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f92bf7350ce93262a40a89f8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I have not the nerves for such a thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you really feel at ease spending the entire evening away from the poor boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; you see, his papa can, so why should not I? Jemima is so attentive, and she could send us word every hour about him. I truly think Charles might as well have told his father that we would all come. I am no more frightened about little Charles now than he is. Yesterday I was terribly alarmed, but today the situation is quite different.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you do not think it too late to give notice for yourself, suppose you went as well as your husband. Leave little Charles in my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot object while I stay with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d cried Mary, her eyes lighting up. \u201cDear me! That is an excellent idea, truly. Of course, I may as well go as stay, since I am of no use at home\u2014is that not so? It only worries me. You, who have no mother\u2019s feelings, are much the more suitable person. You can make little Charles do anything; he always obeys you at once. It will be far better than leaving him with Jemima alone. Oh, I shall certainly go; I am sure I ought to, if I can, every bit as much as Charles, because they are extremely eager for me to become acquainted with Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being left by yourself. It was an excellent thought of yours, Anne. I will go tell Charles and get ready immediately. You can send for us at a moment\u2019s notice, you know, if anything is wrong; but I dare say there will be nothing to frighten you. You may be certain I would not go unless I felt completely easy about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next moment she was knocking at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door, and as Anne followed her upstairs, she arrived in time to hear the entire conversation, which began with Mary saying, in a voice of great triumph\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI intend to go with you, Charles, since I am no more useful at home than you are. If I shut myself away forever with the child, I should not be able to persuade him to do anything he disliked. Anne will stay; Anne has offered to remain at home and care for him. It was Anne\u2019s own suggestion, and so I shall go with you, which will be much better, for I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband replied, \u201cand I should be very pleased to have you come; but it seems rather unfair that she should be left at home alone to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
