          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f122fe4bcc02276faa42201b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But there was no laughter in the little town that peaceful Saturday afternoon. The Harpers and Aunt Polly\u2019s family were being plunged into mourning, with profound grief and many tears. An unusual silence settled over the village, though it was quiet enough under ordinary circumstances. The villagers went about their affairs distractedly and spoke little; but they sighed often. The Saturday holiday seemed a burden to the children. They had no enthusiasm for their games and gradually abandoned them.\\n\\nThat afternoon Becky Thatcher found herself wandering sadly around the deserted schoolhouse yard, feeling deeply melancholy. But she found nothing there to comfort her. She spoke to herself:\\n\\n\u201cOh, if only I had a brass andiron knob again! But now I have nothing to remember him by.\u201d And she stifled a small sob.\\n\\nAfter a while she stopped and said to herself:\\n\\n\u201cIt was right here. Oh, if I could do it over again, I wouldn\u2019t say that\u2014I wouldn\u2019t say it for anything in the world. But he\u2019s gone now; I\u2019ll never, never, never see him again.\u201d\\n\\nThe thought overwhelmed her, and she wandered away with tears running down her cheeks. Then a fairly large group of boys and girls\u2014Tom\u2019s and Joe\u2019s playmates\u2014came by and stood looking over the picket fence, speaking reverently about how Tom had done this or that the last time they saw him, and how Joe had said one small thing or another (filled with terrible prophecy, as they could easily recognize now). Each speaker pointed out the exact place where the lost boys had stood, then added something like, \u201cAnd I was standing just so\u2014just as I am now, and if you were him\u2014I was this close\u2014and he smiled, just like this\u2014and then something seemed to pass all over me, like\u2014awful, you know\u2014and I never guessed what it meant, of course, but I can see now!\u201d\\n\\nThen they argued over who had seen the dead boys last while they were alive. Many claimed that mournful distinction and offered evidence, more or less altered by the witness. When it was finally decided who really had seen the departed boys last and exchanged the final words with them, the fortunate ones assumed a kind of sacred importance and were stared at and envied by everyone else. One poor fellow, who had no other glory to offer, said with obvious pride in the memory:\\n\\n\u201cWell, Tom Sawyer licked me once.\u201d\\n\\nBut that attempt at glory failed. Most of the boys could say the same, which made the distinction far too common. The group drifted away, still recalling the lost heroes in hushed, awed voices.\"}"
