{"request_id":"6e1ae747f2ac8fac89f9c6cc","rewritten_text":"‘Oh, that is nothing! It is a pleasure to me. I do not work for money; I devote my whole life to my art.’

Lord Arthur placed £4, 2s. 6d. upon the table, thanked the little German for his kindness, and, after managing to refuse an invitation to meet some Anarchists at a meat-tea the next Saturday, left the house and went to the Park.

For the following two days he remained in a state of intense excitement, and on Friday at twelve o’clock drove down to the Buckingham to await news. Throughout the afternoon the stolid hall-porter continued putting up telegrams from different parts of the country, reporting horse-race results, divorce-court verdicts, the weather, and similar matters, while the tape rattled out tedious particulars of an all-night sitting in the House of Commons and a slight panic on the Stock Exchange. At four the evening papers arrived, and Lord Arthur withdrew to the library with the _Pall Mall_, the _St. James’s_, the _Globe_, and the _Echo_, greatly angering Colonel Goodchild, who wished to read accounts of a speech he had made that morning at the Mansion House about South African Missions and the desirability of having black Bishops in every province, and who, for some reason, had a strong dislike of the _Evening News_. None of the papers, however, contained the faintest reference to Chichester, and Lord Arthur concluded that the attempt had failed. It was a dreadful disappointment, and for some time he was completely unnerved. Herr Winckelkopf, whom he visited the next day, was lavish with apologies and offered either to provide another clock without charge or to sell him a case of nitro-glycerine bombs at cost. But Lord Arthur had lost all confidence in explosives, and Herr Winckelkopf himself admitted that everything is so adulterated nowadays that even dynamite can scarcely be obtained pure. The little German, though acknowledging that something must have gone wrong with the mechanism, still hoped that the clock might explode eventually, and cited the example of a barometer he had once sent to the military Governor at Odessa. Though set to explode after ten days, it had failed to do so for nearly three months. It was true that, when it finally went off, it only blew a housemaid to pieces, the Governor having left town six weeks earlier; but it proved at least that dynamite, as a destructive power, was, when controlled by machinery, a potent though somewhat unpunctual agent."}

