{"request_id":"f53b0498fabbb168252a1336","rewritten_text":"I could remember what things were like during our great civil war in the nineteenth century. In the North, a carpenter earned three dollars a day, valued in gold; in the South, he earned fifty dollars, paid in Confederate paper money worth a dollar a bushel. In the North, a pair of overalls cost three dollars—one day’s wages; in the South, they cost seventy-five dollars—two days’ wages. Other prices followed the same pattern. So wages were twice as high in the North as in the South, because Northern wages had twice the purchasing power.

Yes, I made several acquaintances in the hamlet, and I was greatly pleased to find our new coins in circulation—many milrays, many mills, many cents, quite a few nickels, and some silver. These were being used by the artisans and ordinary people generally. There was even some gold, though that was kept at the bank, or rather, the goldsmith’s. I went in while Marco, Marco’s son, was arguing with a shopkeeper over a quarter pound of salt, and asked them to change a twenty-dollar gold piece. They did so—but only after biting the coin, ringing it on the counter, testing it with acid, and asking where I had gotten it, who I was, where I came from, where I was going, when I expected to arrive, and perhaps two hundred other questions. When they ran out of questions, I volunteered plenty of information myself. I told them I owned a dog named Watch, that my first wife was a Free Will Baptist, that her grandfather was a Prohibitionist, and that I once knew a man with two thumbs on each hand and a wart inside his upper lip who died hoping for a glorious resurrection, and so on and so on, until even that hungry village questioner looked satisfied—and slightly annoyed. Still, he had to respect a man with my financial strength, so he did not argue with me. I noticed, however, that he took out his irritation on his assistants, which was perfectly natural.

Yes, they changed my twenty, but I thought it strained the bank a little. That was understandable, since it was like walking into a poor village store in the nineteenth century and suddenly asking the owner to change a two-thousand-dollar bill. He might be able to do it, but he would still wonder why a small farmer was carrying so much money in his pocket. That was probably what this goldsmith thought, too, because he followed me to the door and stood there watching me leave with reverent admiration."}

