          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f0a021c6277b9077c91cd38e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The nephew glanced at him, but in vain, as he proceeded toward the door.\\n\\n\u201cGood night!\u201d said the uncle. \u201cI expect the pleasure of seeing you again in the morning. Sleep well! Light Monsieur my nephew to his chamber there!--And burn Monsieur my nephew in his bed, if you please,\u201d he added to himself, before ringing his little bell once more and calling his valet to his own bedroom.\\n\\nAfter the valet had come and gone, Monsieur the Marquis paced back and forth in his loose chamber-robe, gently preparing himself for sleep on that hot, still night. Rustling around the room, his softly slippered feet making no sound upon the floor, he moved like a cultivated tiger: he looked like some enchanted marquis of the irredeemably wicked kind in a tale, whose recurring transformation into tiger-shape was either ending or beginning.\\n\\nHe crossed his luxurious bedroom from end to end, again reviewing the fragments of the day\u2019s journey that rose unbidden in his thoughts: the slow climb up the hill at sunset, the sinking sun, the descent, the mill, the prison on the crag, the little village in the hollow, the peasants at the fountain, and the road-mender in his blue cap pointing beneath the carriage to the chain. That fountain brought to mind the Paris fountain, the small bundle on the step, the women stooping over it, and the tall man with raised arms crying, \u201cDead!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am cool now,\u201d said Monsieur the Marquis, \u201cand can go to bed.\u201d\\n\\nAccordingly, leaving only one light burning upon the broad hearth, he allowed his thin gauze curtains to fall around him, and heard the night break its silence with a long sigh as he settled himself to sleep.\\n\\nThe stone faces on the outer walls stared blindly into the black night for three long hours; for three long hours, the horses in the stables rattled at their racks, the dogs barked, and the owl made a sound bearing very little likeness to the sound men-poets conventionally assign to owls. Yet it is the stubborn habit of such creatures almost never to say what has been written down for them.\\n\\nFor three long hours, the stone faces of the chateau, lion and human, stared blindly into the night. Dead darkness covered the whole landscape, and dead darkness added its hush to the dust settling quietly along every road. The burial-place had reached such a state that its small mounds of poor grass could no longer be distinguished from one another; the figure upon the Cross might have descended, for anything visible of it. In the village, taxers and taxed slept soundly. Dreaming, perhaps, of feasts, as the starving commonly do, and of comfort and rest, as the driven slave and yoked ox may, its gaunt inhabitants slept deeply, and were fed and freed.\"}"
