{"request_id":"cbc3f7403e665cf1e0d49c92","rewritten_text":"What kind of life would theirs be while Fate still held this terrible fortune in the balance? The marriage had to be postponed, whatever the cost. He was completely resolved on that. Though he loved the girl passionately, and the slightest touch of her fingers when they sat together sent every nerve in his body trembling with exquisite joy, he understood just as clearly where his duty lay. He knew he had no right to marry until he had committed the murder. Once that was done, he could stand before the altar with Sybil Merton and place his life in her hands without fear that he was doing wrong. Then he could take her in his arms, knowing she would never have to blush for him or lower her head in shame. But it had to be done first, and the sooner the better for them both.

Many men in his position would have chosen the easy path of pleasure over the difficult heights of duty; but Lord Arthur was too conscientious to place pleasure above principle. His love was more than mere passion, and Sybil represented to him everything good and noble. For a moment he felt a natural revulsion at what he had been asked to do, but it soon faded. His heart told him it was not a sin but a sacrifice; his reason reminded him that no other course was open. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others. However terrible the task before him, he knew he must not let selfishness triumph over love. Sooner or later, we are all called upon to make the same decision; the same question is put to every one of us. Lord Arthur faced it early in life, before his nature had been spoiled by the calculating cynicism of middle age or his heart eaten away by the shallow, fashionable selfishness of the present day, and he felt no hesitation about doing his duty. Fortunately, he was no mere dreamer or idle dilettante. Had he been, he would have hesitated like Hamlet and allowed indecision to ruin his purpose. But he was practical by nature. To him, life meant action rather than thought. He possessed the rarest quality of all: common sense.

By now, the wild and turbulent feelings of the previous night had completely disappeared, and he looked back almost with shame on his mad wandering from street to street and his fierce emotional torment. The very sincerity of his suffering made it seem unreal now. He wondered how he could have been foolish enough to rant and rave about the inevitable."}

