          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a4e57901b1e6239d861e913c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cUnless you call Miss Havisham a he. And I doubt even you would go that far.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Havisham, uptown?\u201d said Joe.\\n\\n\u201cIs there any Miss Havisham downtown?\u201d my sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cShe wants this boy to go and play there. And of course he\u2019s going. He\u2019d better play there, too,\u201d said my sister, shaking her head at me to encourage me to be extremely light and playful, \u201cor I\u2019ll work him.\u201d\\n\\nI had heard of Miss Havisham uptown\u2014everyone for miles around had heard of Miss Havisham uptown\u2014as an immensely rich and grim woman who lived in a large, gloomy house barricaded against robbers and led a secluded life.\\n\\n\u201cWell, to be sure!\u201d said Joe, astonished. \u201cI wonder how she came to know Pip!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNoodle!\u201d cried my sister. \u201cWho said she knew him?\u201d\\n\\n\u201c\u2014Which some person,\u201d Joe politely hinted again, \u201cmentioned when he said she wanted him to go and play there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd couldn\u2019t she ask Uncle Pumblechook whether he knew of a boy to go and play there? Isn\u2019t it just barely possible that Uncle Pumblechook might be one of her tenants, and that he might sometimes\u2014we won\u2019t say quarterly or half-yearly, since that would demand too much of you\u2014but sometimes\u2014go there to pay his rent? And couldn\u2019t she then ask Uncle Pumblechook whether he knew of a boy to go and play there? And couldn\u2019t Uncle Pumblechook, always considerate and thoughtful toward us\u2014though you may not think so, Joseph,\u201d she added in a tone of deepest reproach, as if he were the most heartless of nephews, \u201cthen mention this boy, standing prancing here\u201d\u2014which I solemnly declare I was not doing\u2014\u201cto whom I have forever been a willing slave?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood again!\u201d cried Uncle Pumblechook. \u201cWell put! Nicely pointed! Very good! Now, Joseph, you know the case.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, Joseph,\u201d said my sister, still reproachful, while Joe apologetically rubbed the back of his hand repeatedly across his nose, \u201cyou do not yet\u2014though you may not think so\u2014know the case. You may believe that you do, but you do not, Joseph. For you do not know that Uncle Pumblechook, realizing that, for all we know, this boy\u2019s fortune may be made by his going to Miss Havisham\u2019s, has offered to take him into town tonight in his own chaise-cart, keep him there tonight, and take him himself to Miss Havisham\u2019s tomorrow morning. And mercy me!\u201d cried my sister, suddenly throwing off her bonnet in desperation, \u201chere I stand talking to mere mooncalves, while Uncle Pumblechook waits, the mare is catching cold at the door, and the boy is covered with soot and dirt from the crown of his head to the sole of his foot!\u201d\"}"
