          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8282d5f1a8c89e907e1158f8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For this reason, I decided to get out as soon as we reached the town and place myself beyond his hearing. I carried out this plan successfully. My small portmanteau was in the boot beneath my feet; I only had to turn a hinge to remove it. I threw it down before me, climbed down after it, and was left beside the first lamp, on the first stones of the town pavement. As for the convicts, they continued on with the coach, and I knew where they would be taken away to the river. In my imagination, I saw the boat with its convict crew waiting for them at the slime-covered steps\u2014heard again the rough \u201cGive way, you!\u201d like an order given to dogs\u2014and saw again the wicked Noah\u2019s Ark lying on the black water.\\n\\nI could not have explained what I feared, because my fear was completely vague and undefined, but I was filled with terror. As I walked toward the hotel, I felt that a dread far greater than the simple fear of an unpleasant or painful meeting made me tremble. I am sure it had no clear shape, and that for a few minutes it was the return of my childhood terror.\\n\\nThe coffee-room at the Blue Boar was empty. I had ordered my dinner there and sat down to eat it before the waiter recognized me. After apologizing for failing to remember me, he asked whether he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook.\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d I said, \u201ccertainly not.\u201d\\n\\nThe waiter\u2014it was he who had brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials on the day I was bound\u2014seemed surprised. At the first opportunity, he placed a dirty, old copy of a local newspaper directly in my path. I picked it up and read this paragraph:\u2014\\n\\nOur readers will learn, with some interest, concerning the recent romantic rise in fortune of a young ironworker from this neighbourhood (what a subject, by the way, for the magic pen of our still not universally acknowledged townsman TOOBY, the poet of our columns!), that the youth\u2019s first patron, companion, and friend was a highly respected man connected in some way with the corn and seed trade. His extremely convenient and spacious business premises are located within a hundred miles of the High Street. It is not without personal feeling that we record HIM as the Mentor of our young Telemachus, for it is pleasing to know that our town produced the founder of the young man\u2019s fortune.\"}"
