          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a49d7acf9c38c9807363b688\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Wickham was so completely satisfied with this conversation that he never again troubled himself, or annoyed his dear sister Elizabeth, by bringing up the subject; and she was glad to find that she had said enough to keep him silent.\\n\\nThe day of his and Lydia\u2019s departure soon arrived, and Mrs. Bennet was forced to endure a separation which, since her husband had no intention of joining her plan for them all to go to Newcastle, was likely to last at least a year.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear Lydia,\u201d she cried, \u201cwhen shall we meet again?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Lord! I don\u2019t know. Perhaps not for two or three years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWrite to me very often, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAs often as I can. But you know married women never have much time for writing. My sisters may write to me. They will have nothing else to do.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Wickham\u2019s farewells were much more affectionate than his wife\u2019s. He smiled, looked handsome, and said many charming things.\\n\\n\u201cHe is as fine a fellow,\u201d said Mr. Bennet, as soon as they had left the house, \u201cas I have ever seen. He simpers, smirks, and makes love to us all. I am immensely proud of him. I defy even Sir William Lucas himself to produce a more valuable son-in-law.\u201d\\n\\nThe loss of her daughter made Mrs. Bennet very low for several days.\\n\\n\u201cI often think,\u201d she said, \u201cthat there is nothing so dreadful as parting from one\u2019s friends. One feels so forlorn without them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is the consequence, you see, madam, of marrying a daughter,\u201d said Elizabeth. \u201cIt must make you more content that your other four are unmarried.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is no such thing. Lydia does not leave me because she is married, but only because her husband\u2019s regiment happens to be so far away. If it had been nearer, she would not have gone so soon.\u201d\\n\\nBut the dispirited state into which this event had thrown her was soon relieved, and her mind opened again to the excitement of hope through a piece of news that had begun circulating. The housekeeper at Netherfield had received orders to prepare for her master\u2019s arrival. He was coming in a day or two to shoot there for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet was extremely agitated. By turns, she looked at Jane, smiled, and shook her head.\\n\\n\u201cWell, well, so Mr. Bingley is coming down, sister,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet, for Mrs. Philips had first brought her the news. \u201cWell, so much the better. Not that I care about it, though. He is nothing to us, you know, and I am sure I never want to see him again. But still, he is very welcome to come to Netherfield if he wishes.\u201d\"}"
