          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f019120692a0609bc7397658\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But how could I, a poor, bewildered country boy, escape that remarkable inconsistency into which the finest and wisest men fall every day?\\n\\n\u201cIt may all be perfectly true,\u201d I said to Biddy, \u201cbut I admire her terribly.\u201d\\n\\nIn short, when I reached that point, I rolled over onto my face, seized the hair on either side of my head, and gave it a good wrench. All the while I knew that the madness of my heart was so utterly mad and misplaced that I was fully aware my face would have deserved it if I had hauled it up by my hair and dashed it against the pebbles as punishment for belonging to such a fool.\\n\\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and she attempted no further reasoning with me. She laid her hand\u2014which was a comforting hand, though roughened by work\u2014on my hands in turn, and gently drew them from my hair. Then she patted my shoulder softly and soothingly, while I cried a little with my face against my sleeve, just as I had done in the brewery yard, and felt dimly certain that somebody, or perhaps everybody, had treated me very badly; I could not say which.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019m glad of one thing,\u201d said Biddy, \u201cand that is that you\u2019ve felt able to trust me, Pip. And I\u2019m glad of another: you know, of course, that you can rely on me to keep your confidence and always be worthy of it. If your first teacher\u2014dear! such a poor teacher, and herself so much in need of teaching\u2014were teaching you now, she thinks she knows what lesson she would give you. But it would be a difficult one to learn, and you have gone beyond her, so it is useless now.\u201d With a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank and said, in a newly cheerful tone, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I cried, rising, putting my arm around her neck, and kissing her, \u201cI shall always tell you everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUntil you\u2019re a gentleman,\u201d said Biddy.\\n\\n\u201cYou know I never shall be, so that means always. Not that I have anything to tell you, since you know everything I know\u2014as I told you at home the other night.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Biddy very softly, looking away toward the ships. Then, returning to her earlier pleasant tone, she repeated, \u201cShall we walk a little farther, or go home?\u201d\\n\\nI told Biddy that we would walk a little farther, and we did. The summer afternoon gradually softened into summer evening, and it was very beautiful.\"}"
