          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e66aa7faec5d84b5a9fbc3ee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I am further informed by Mrs. Otis, who, I may observe, is no insignificant authority on Art\u2014having enjoyed the privilege of spending several winters in Boston during her girlhood\u2014that these jewels are of considerable monetary value, and, if put up for sale, would command a high price. In these circumstances, Lord Canterville, I am certain you will understand how impossible it would be for me to permit them to remain in the possession of anyone in my family; indeed, all such empty ornaments and toys, however appropriate or indispensable to the dignity of the British aristocracy, would be wholly out of place among people raised upon the stern, and, I believe, immortal principles of republican simplicity. I should perhaps add that Virginia is most eager for you to let her keep the box as a remembrance of your unfortunate but misguided ancestor. Since it is exceedingly old, and therefore in rather poor repair, you may perhaps decide to grant her request. For my own part, I admit that I am greatly astonished to find a child of mine showing sympathy with medi\u00e6valism in any shape, and can explain it only by remembering that Virginia was born in one of your London suburbs soon after Mrs. Otis had returned from a journey to Athens.\u2019\\n\\nLord Canterville listened with great seriousness to the worthy Minister\u2019s speech, occasionally tugging at his grey moustache to conceal an involuntary smile. When Mr. Otis had finished, he shook him warmly by the hand and said, \u2018My dear sir, your charming little daughter performed a very important service for my unfortunate ancestor, Sir Simon, and I and my family are deeply indebted to her for her extraordinary courage and pluck. The jewels plainly belong to her, and, egad, I believe that if I were heartless enough to take them away, the wicked old fellow would be out of his grave within a fortnight, making my life a perfect misery. As for their being heirlooms, nothing is an heirloom unless it is so specified in a will or legal document, and the existence of these jewels has been entirely unknown. I assure you that I have no greater claim to them than your butler, and when Miss Virginia grows up I daresay she will be glad to possess pretty things to wear. Besides, Mr. Otis, you forget that you purchased the furniture and the ghost at a valuation, and whatever belonged to the ghost passed immediately into your possession; for, whatever activity Sir Simon may have displayed in the corridor at night, in the eyes of the law he was truly dead, and you obtained his property by purchase.\u2019\"}"
