          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e4831bbef4cda0d8a6cb6876\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nearly a year had passed since my father\u2019s return, and the seasons had almost completed their course. It was now the end of May; the woods were dressed in their freshest green, and the sweet scent of newly mown grass filled the fields. I thought that the mild air and Nature\u2019s lovely aspect might help awaken gentle sensations in him, and inspire feelings of peace and affection, preparing him for the confidence I resolved to gain from him.\\n\\nI therefore selected the evening of one of these days for my attempt. I asked him to walk with me, and conducted him to a nearby beech wood, whose light shade protected us from the slanting, dazzling rays of the setting sun. After walking for some time in silence, I sat down with him upon a mossy hillock. It is strange, but even now I seem to see the place: many of the slender, smooth trunks were entwined with ivy, whose shining leaves of deepest green stood out against the white bark and the pale leaves of the young beech shoots growing from their parent trunks. The short grass was mixed with moss and partly covered by the dead leaves of the previous autumn, which the winds had gathered here and there into little mounds. A few stumps, thick with moss, stood around us. The leaves stirred softly in the breeze, and through their green canopy the bright blue sky could be seen. As evening advanced, the distant trunks were reddened by the sun, and the wind fell completely still, while a few birds flew past us toward their evening rest.\\n\\nIt was here, then, that we sat together; and when you hear all that passed\u2014all that terrible suffering which tore our souls even in this peaceful place, which might, but for strange passions, have been a paradise to us\u2014you will not wonder that I remember it as I looked upon it, hoping its calm would bring calm to me and inspire me not only with courage, but with persuasive words. I saw all these things and observed them absently in my mind while trying to arrange my thoughts in proper order for the attempt. My heart beat rapidly as I gathered strength to speak to him, for I was resolved not to be repulsed, yet trembled to imagine what effect my words might produce. At last, with much hesitation, I began:\\n\\n\u201cYour kindness toward me, my dearest father, and the affection\u2014the excessive affection\u2014you showed me when you first returned will, I hope, excuse me in your eyes for daring to speak to you, though with the tender love of a daughter, yet also with the freedom of a friend and an equal.\u201d\"}"
