{"request_id":"a82c2ec1b456670cf6a6b056","rewritten_text":"I do not understand at all why she wished to become intimate with me; but if the same circumstances occurred again, I am sure I would be deceived again. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday, and in the meantime I received neither a note nor a line. When she came, it was very clear that she took no pleasure in the visit. She offered a slight, formal apology for not calling sooner, said nothing about wishing to see me again, and was in every respect so changed that, when she left, I was fully resolved never to continue the acquaintance. I pity her, though I cannot help blaming her. She was very wrong to single me out as she did; I can safely say that every step toward intimacy began with her. But I pity her because she must feel that she has behaved wrongly, and because I am quite sure that concern for her brother is the cause. I need not explain myself further; and though _we_ know this concern to be entirely needless, if she feels it, it readily accounts for her conduct toward me. Since he is so deservedly dear to his sister, whatever anxiety she may feel for him is natural and affectionate. Still, I cannot help wondering that she should have such fears now, because if he had cared for me at all, we must have met long, long ago. He knows that I am in town, I am certain, from something she herself said; yet from the way she speaks, one would think she wished to persuade herself that he is truly attached to Miss Darcy. I cannot understand it. If I were not afraid of judging her harshly, I should almost be tempted to say that all this strongly suggests deceit. I will try to banish every painful thought and think only of what will make me happy: your affection and the unfailing kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Write to me very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned something about his never returning to Netherfield, and about giving up the house, but she spoke without certainty. We had better not mention it. I am extremely glad that you have such pleasant news from our friends at Hunsford. Please go to see them, with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

This letter caused Elizabeth some pain; but her spirits revived when she reflected that Jane would at least no longer be deceived by the sister. All hope concerning the brother was now completely gone."}

