          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"02dd82d50fdce65b0f51619c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Winters at the latitude of Sullivan\u2019s Island are rarely severe, and in autumn it is an unusual event for a fire to be considered necessary. Yet around the middle of October, 18\u2014, there came a day of extraordinary cold. Just before sunset, I made my way through the evergreens to my friend\u2019s hut, which I had not visited for several weeks. At that time I lived in Charleston, nine miles from the island, and travel there and back was far less convenient than it is today. When I reached the hut, I knocked as usual. Receiving no answer, I found the key where I knew it was hidden, unlocked the door, and entered. A bright fire was burning on the hearth. It was a novelty, and a welcome one. I removed my overcoat, settled into an armchair beside the crackling logs, and patiently awaited the arrival of my hosts.\\n\\nSoon after dark they came in and greeted me warmly. Jupiter, grinning from ear to ear, hurried about preparing marsh-hens for supper. Legrand was in one of his fits\u2014what else can I call them?\u2014of excitement. He had discovered an unknown bivalve, belonging to a new genus; and, beyond that, with Jupiter\u2019s help, he had tracked down and captured a _scarab\u00e6us_ he believed to be entirely new. He wanted my opinion of it the next day.\\n\\n\u201cAnd why not tonight?\u201d I asked, rubbing my hands over the fire and wishing the entire tribe of _scarab\u00e6i_ to the devil.\\n\\n\u201cAh, if only I had known you were here!\u201d Legrand said. \u201cBut it has been so long since I saw you; how could I have expected you to visit me on this particular night? On my way home I met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014 from the fort, and, foolishly, lent him the beetle. So you cannot see it until morning. Stay here tonight, and I will send Jup down for it at sunrise. It is the most beautiful thing in creation!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat? Sunrise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNonsense! No\u2014the beetle. It is a brilliant gold color, about the size of a large hickory nut, with two jet-black spots near one end of its back and another, somewhat longer, at the other. The antennae are\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDey ain\u2019t no tin in him, Massa Will, I keep tellin\u2019 you,\u201d Jupiter interrupted. \u201cDe bug is a goole bug, solid, every bit of him, inside and all, \u2019cept his wing\u2014never feel half so hebby a bug in my life.\u201d\"}"
