{"request_id":"8962971c7f22a1c208d9bdba","rewritten_text":"“It was some time before I recovered enough to attend to the balloon’s ordinary needs. When I did, I examined it carefully and, to my great relief, found it undamaged. All my equipment was safe, and fortunately I had lost neither ballast nor provisions. I had secured everything so firmly that such a loss was impossible. Looking at my watch, I saw that it was six o’clock. I was still rising quickly, and my barometer showed that I had reached an altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Directly below me on the ocean was a small black object, slightly oblong, apparently about the size and shape of one of the children’s toys called a domino. I aimed my telescope at it and clearly saw that it was a British ship carrying ninety-four guns, sailing close-hauled and pitching heavily in the sea, with her bow pointed W.S.W. Apart from this ship, I saw nothing but the ocean, the sky, and the sun, which had risen long before.

“It is now time that I explain to Your Excellencies the purpose of my dangerous journey. You will remember that difficult circumstances in Rotterdam had finally driven me to consider suicide. It was not that I had any real hatred of life itself, but that the troubles connected with my situation had worn me down beyond endurance. In this state of mind, wishing to live but tired of living, the treatise I found at the bookseller’s stall opened a possibility to my imagination. At last I made my decision. I would leave, yet continue to live; I would depart from the world, yet still exist—in short, to put the matter plainly, I resolved, whatever might happen, to try to reach the moon. Now, so that I may not seem more insane than I truly am, I will explain as clearly as I can the thoughts that led me to believe that such an achievement, though certainly difficult and extremely dangerous, was not entirely beyond the reach of a bold spirit.

“The moon’s true distance from the earth was the first matter to consider. The average distance between the centers of the two bodies is 59.9643 times the earth’s equatorial radius, or only about 237,000 miles. I say average distance, because the moon’s orbit is an ellipse, with an eccentricity equal to no less than 0.05484 of the ellipse’s major semi-axis, while the earth’s center lies at its focus. Therefore, if I could somehow arrange to meet the moon at its nearest point to the earth, the distance given above would be considerably reduced.”"}

