{"request_id":"305f30f6198102ee63b3aa18","rewritten_text":"Far into the distance ran the curve of flickering gas lamps, and outside a small walled house stood a solitary hansom, its driver asleep inside. He walked quickly toward Portland Place, glancing back from time to time as though afraid he was being followed. At the corner of Rich Street, two men stood reading a small bill pasted on a hoarding. Curiosity stirred in him, and he crossed over. As he approached, the word “Murder,” printed in black letters, caught his eye. He started, and a deep flush spread across his cheek. It was an advertisement offering a reward for information leading to the arrest of a man of medium height, between thirty and forty, wearing a billycock hat, a black coat, and checked trousers, with a scar on his right cheek. He read it again and again, wondering whether the wretched man would be caught, and how he had received his scar. Perhaps one day his own name might be posted on the walls of London. Perhaps one day a price would be placed on his head as well.

The thought filled him with sickening horror. He turned sharply and hurried on into the night.

He scarcely knew where he went. He dimly remembered wandering through a maze of sordid houses, lost in a vast web of gloomy streets, and it was bright dawn when he finally found himself in Piccadilly Circus. As he walked home toward Belgrave Square, he met the great wagons heading for Covent Garden. The white-smocked carters, with their pleasant sunburned faces and coarse, curly hair, strode firmly onward, cracking their whips and calling to one another now and then. On the back of a huge gray horse, leader of a clattering team, sat a chubby boy with a bunch of primroses in his battered hat, gripping the mane tightly in his small hands and laughing. The great heaps of vegetables looked like masses of jade against the morning sky, like green jade against the pink petals of some marvelous rose. Lord Arthur felt strangely moved, though he could not say why. Something in the delicate beauty of dawn seemed to him unbearably pathetic, and he thought of all the days that begin in beauty and end in storm. These countrymen, too, with their rough, good-natured voices and careless manner—what a strange London they saw! A London untouched by the sin of night and the smoke of day, a pale, ghostlike city, a desolate town of tombs. He wondered what they thought of it, and whether they knew anything of its splendor and shame, its fierce, fiery joys and terrible hunger, of all it created and destroyed from morning to evening."}

