          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"68cbd3ff147825495c5b7a41\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Biddy cried; the darkening garden, the lane, and the stars appearing overhead were blurred in my own sight.\\n\\n\u201cNothing was ever discovered, Biddy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNothing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo you know what became of Orlick?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cJudging by the color of his clothes, I should think he is working in the quarries.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen of course you have seen him? Why are you looking at that dark tree in the lane?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI saw him there on the night she died.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was not the last time either, Biddy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo; I have seen him there since we began walking here. It is useless,\u201d said Biddy, placing her hand on my arm as I was about to run out, \u201cyou know I would not deceive you; he was not there a minute, and he is gone.\u201d\\n\\nMy indignation was renewed by the discovery that this fellow still pursued her, and I felt an enduring hatred for him. I told her so, and said I would spend any amount of money or take any trouble to drive him from that country. Gradually she led me into calmer conversation, and told me how Joe loved me, and how Joe never complained of anything\u2014she did not say, of me; she had no need; I understood what she meant\u2014but always did his duty in his station, with a strong hand, a quiet tongue, and a gentle heart.\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, it would be difficult to say too much in his praise,\u201d I said; \u201cand Biddy, we must often speak of these things, because of course I shall be coming down here often now. I am not going to leave poor Joe alone.\u201d\\n\\nBiddy said not a word.\\n\\n\u201cBiddy, don\u2019t you hear me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, Mr. Pip.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLeaving aside your calling me Mr. Pip\u2014which seems to me in poor taste, Biddy\u2014what do you mean?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do I mean?\u201d Biddy asked timidly.\\n\\n\u201cBiddy,\u201d I said, in a self-righteously assertive 