{"request_id":"fc4cfe68ab2a52634ba745b9","rewritten_text":"I passed several funerals, attended by only a few mourners, and the spectators saw them as terrible omens. Some watched the processions with wild eagerness; others fled in fear, while some wept aloud.

After caring for the sick, Adrian’s main effort had been to hide the plague’s symptoms and spread from the people of London. He knew that fear and gloomy expectations helped disease, and that despair and constant worry made the human body especially open to infection. So no shocking signs were allowed to appear: most shops remained open, and some traffic still moved through the streets. Yet to me, who had not seen London since the plague began, the city looked greatly changed. There were no carriages, and grass had grown tall in the streets. The houses looked deserted; most shutters were closed; and the people I met wore a pale, frightened expression, very different from the usual practical manner of Londoners. My lone carriage drew attention as it rattled toward the Protectoral Palace, and the fashionable streets leading there looked even more bleak and empty. I found Adrian’s antechamber crowded, for it was his hour to receive visitors. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited and watched the petitioners enter and leave. They were mostly people of the middle and lower classes, whose livelihoods had disappeared with the end of trade and of the country’s usual busy pursuit of money in every form. The newcomers looked anxious, sometimes terrified, unlike those who had already been heard, whose faces showed resignation and even satisfaction. I could see my friend’s influence in their quicker steps and brighter expressions. At two o’clock, no more people were admitted. Those who had been turned away left angrily or sadly, while I entered the audience chamber.

I was surprised by how much Adrian’s health had improved. He was no longer bent toward the ground like an overfed spring flower that, growing beyond its strength, is weighed down by its own crown of blossoms. His eyes were bright, his face calm, and a sense of focused energy filled his whole body, replacing its former weakness. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who were sorting petitions and recording notes from that day’s meetings. Two or three petitioners were still waiting. I admired his fairness and patience. Anyone who could live outside London was immediately advised to leave, and Adrian gave them the means to do so."}

