          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"679ff5f904bf7a0c8662e098\",\"rewritten_text\":\"A few days later, Virginia and her curly-haired cavalier went riding across Brockley Meadows. She tore her riding habit badly while forcing her way through a hedge, and when she returned home, decided to go upstairs by the back staircase so no one would see her. As she hurried past the Tapestry Chamber, whose door happened to be open, she thought she saw someone inside. Assuming it was her mother\u2019s maid, who sometimes brought her work there, she looked in to ask her to mend the habit. To her immense surprise, however, it was the Canterville Ghost himself. He sat by the window, watching the ruined gold of the yellowing trees blow through the air and the red leaves dance wildly down the long avenue. His head rested on his hand, and his whole posture expressed profound depression. Indeed, he looked so forlorn and so thoroughly worn out that little Virginia, whose first impulse had been to run away and lock herself in her room, felt sorry for him and resolved to comfort him. Her step was so light, and his melancholy so deep, that he did not notice her until she spoke.\\n\\n\u2018I\u2019m so sorry for you,\u2019 she said, \u2018but my brothers are returning to Eton tomorrow, and then, if you behave yourself, no one will bother you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018It is absurd to ask me to behave myself,\u2019 he replied, turning in astonishment toward the pretty little girl who had dared to address him. \u2018Completely absurd. I must rattle my chains, groan through keyholes, and walk about at night, if that is what you mean. It is my only reason for existing.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018That is no reason to exist at all, and you know you have behaved very wickedly. Mrs. Umney told us, on the first day we arrived here, that you had killed your wife.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, I freely admit it,\u2019 said the Ghost petulantly, \u2018but it was strictly a family matter and concerned no one else.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018It is very wrong to kill anyone,\u2019 said Virginia, who sometimes possessed a gentle Puritan seriousness inherited from an old New England ancestor.\\n\\n\u2018Oh, I detest the cheap severity of abstract morality! My wife was extremely plain, never had my ruffs properly starched, and knew nothing about cooking. Why, there was a buck I had shot in Hogley Woods\u2014a magnificent pricket\u2014and do you know how she had it served at table? Still, it does not matter now, because it is all over. And I do not think it was very kind of her brothers to starve me to death, even though I did kill her.\u2019\"}"
