till travelling northward, we spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could now almost imagine myself among the Swiss mountains. The small patches of snow remaining on the northern slopes, the lakes, and the rushing of the rocky streams were all familiar and beloved sights. Here, too, we formed several acquaintances, who nearly succeeded in deceiving me into happiness. Clerval\u2019s pleasure was proportionately greater than mine; among men of talent his mind expanded, and he discovered in his own nature capacities and resources greater than he could have imagined possessing while he had associated with his inferiors.\"}"
