          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1fa77b69b5359ba074c2444c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When Mr. Hiram B. Otis, the American Minister, purchased Canterville Chase, everyone told him he was acting most foolishly, since there was no question that the house was haunted. Indeed, Lord Canterville himself, a gentleman of the strictest honour, had considered it his duty to inform Mr. Otis of the fact while they were negotiating the terms.\\n\\n\u2018We have not chosen to live there ourselves,\u2019 said Lord Canterville, \u2018ever since my grandaunt, the Dowager Duchess of Bolton, was so terrified that she suffered a fit from which she never truly recovered. Two skeleton hands had been laid upon her shoulders while she was dressing for dinner. And I feel obliged to tell you, Mr. Otis, that the ghost has been seen by several living members of my family, as well as by the parish rector, the Rev. Augustus Dampier, who is a Fellow of King\u2019s College, Cambridge. After the Duchess\u2019s unfortunate accident, none of our younger servants would remain with us, and Lady Canterville frequently slept very little at night because of the strange noises coming from the corridor and the library.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My Lord,\u2019 replied the Minister, \u2018I will take the furniture and the ghost at their assessed value. I come from a modern country, where money can purchase everything; and with all our lively young men painting the Old World red, while carrying away your finest actresses and prima-donnas, I suppose that if there were such a thing as a ghost in Europe, we should soon have it at home, either in one of our public museums or travelling about as a spectacle.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I am afraid the ghost does exist,\u2019 said Lord Canterville with a smile, \u2018although it may have declined the offers of your enterprising showmen. It has been known for three centuries\u2014since 1584, in fact\u2014and it always appears before the death of a member of our family.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, so does the family doctor, for that matter, Lord Canterville. But there is no such thing as a ghost, sir, and I reckon the laws of Nature are not about to be suspended for the sake of the British aristocracy.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You are certainly very natural in America,\u2019 answered Lord Canterville, who had not entirely understood Mr. Otis\u2019s last remark, \u2018and if you have no objection to a ghost in the house, then all is well. You must only remember that I warned you.\u2019\\n\\nA few weeks later the purchase was completed, and at the end of the season the Minister and his family went down to Canterville Chase. Mrs. Otis, who as Miss Lucretia R. Tappan of West 53rd Street had been a celebrated New York belle, was now a very handsome middle-aged woman, with beautiful eyes and a magnificent profile.\"}"
