          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"da40b12b2fb9d83761c54fea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In truth, he had not suspected his own influence at all. He had followed her into the shrubbery without any thought of trying to use it. Anxious to see how she would bear the news of Frank Churchill\u2019s engagement, he had come with no selfish purpose, indeed with no plan beyond trying, if she gave him the chance, to comfort or advise her.\u2014Everything else had been the work of the moment, the immediate result of what he heard and felt. The wonderful certainty that she was completely indifferent to Frank Churchill, that her heart was entirely free of him, had awakened the hope that, in time, he might win her affection himself;\u2014but it was not a present hope. In that brief moment, when eagerness overcame judgment, he had only dared to hope she would say that she did not forbid him to try to win her.\u2014The greater hopes that gradually appeared were all the more delightful.\u2014The affection he had asked permission to create, if he could, was already his!\u2014In less than half an hour, he had gone from deep distress to something so close to perfect happiness that it could be called nothing else.\\n\\n_Her_ change was just as great.\u2014That half-hour had given them both the same precious certainty that they were loved, and had removed from each the same measure of ignorance, jealousy, and doubt.\u2014On his side, the jealousy had lasted a long time, beginning with Frank Churchill\u2019s arrival, or even with the expectation of it.\u2014He had been in love with Emma and jealous of Frank Churchill from nearly the same time; one feeling had probably revealed the other to him. It was his jealousy of Frank Churchill that had driven him away from the country.\u2014The party at Box Hill had decided him to leave. He would spare himself the sight of such openly allowed and encouraged attentions again.\u2014He had gone away to learn indifference.\u2014But he had chosen the wrong place. There was too much family happiness in his brother\u2019s home; women appeared too kindly there; and Isabella was too much like Emma\u2014differing only in those obvious weaknesses that made Emma\u2019s brilliance stand out before him\u2014for much progress to be possible, even if he had stayed longer.\u2014Still, he had remained, firmly, day after day, until the morning\u2019s post brought the story of Jane Fairfax.\u2014Then, with a happiness he had every right, and did not hesitate, to feel, since he had never thought Frank Churchill worthy of Emma, he became so deeply concerned and anxious for her that he could stay no longer. He had ridden home through the rain and, immediately after dinner, walked up to see how this sweetest and best of all creatures, faultless despite all her faults, had borne the discovery.\"}"
