{"request_id":"3d7bd82511169350cab19956","rewritten_text":"It is only four o’clock; yet it is winter, and the sun has already gone down: no clouds drift across the clear, frosty sky to catch its slanting rays, though the air itself is faintly tinged with rose, a colour reflected once more upon the snow-covered ground. I live in a lonely cottage on a solitary, spacious heath: no sound of life reaches me. I look upon the desolate plain, white except for a few dark patches where the midday sun has melted the snow from the tops of those sharp, pointed hillocks, on which it lay less thickly than across the level ground: a few birds peck at the hard ice covering the pools—for the frost has lasted long.

I am in a strange frame of mind. I am alone—entirely alone—in the world—the blight of misfortune has passed over me and withered me; I know that I am soon to die, and I feel happy—joyous. I feel my pulse; it beats quickly: I lay my thin hand against my cheek; it burns: a slight, rapid spirit remains within me, now giving out its final sparks. I shall never see the snows of another winter—I truly believe I shall never again feel the life-giving warmth of another summer sun; and with this conviction I begin to write my tragic history. Perhaps a story like mine ought to perish with me, but an impulse I cannot explain draws me onward, and I am too weak in body and mind to resist the smallest prompting. While life was still strong in me, I thought there was a sacred horror in my tale that made it unfit to be spoken, and now, on the verge of death, I profane its mysterious terrors. It is like the wood of the Eumenides, which none but the dying may enter; and Oedipus is about to die.

What am I writing?—I must gather my thoughts. I do not know whether anyone will read these pages except you, my friend, who will receive them after my death. I do not address them to you alone, since it will please me to dwell upon our friendship in a manner unnecessary if you were their only reader. I shall therefore tell my story as though I were writing for strangers. You have often asked the reason for my solitary life; my tears; and, above all, my impenetrable and unkind silence. In life I dared not reveal it; in death I uncover the mystery. Others will turn these pages over carelessly: to you, Woodville, kind and affectionate friend, they will be dear—the precious memorials of a broken-hearted girl who, while dying, is still warmed by gratitude toward you: your tears will fall upon the words recording my misfortunes; I know they will—and while I live, I thank you for your sympathy."}

