. Brownlow, as Oliver\u2019s tears fell rapidly.\\n\\nMonks said nothing.\\n\\n\u201cThe will,\u201d Mr. Brownlow continued for him, \u201cwas written in the same spirit as the letter. He spoke of the misery his wife had caused him, and of the rebellious nature, wickedness, spite, and early evil passions of you, his only son, whom she had raised to hate him. He left you and your mother an annuity of eight hundred pounds each. He divided the greater part of his property into two equal shares: one for Agnes Fleming, and the other for their child, if it was born alive and ever reached adulthood.\u201d\"}"
