          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"34f596576a105a495a44e5d0\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I don\u2019t rush at him suddenly and risk making a clumsy half-measure of it; no, I move far off to one side and work my way toward him gradually, so he never suspects I mean to strike him at all. Then, in an instant, he is flat on his back and cannot imagine how it happened. That was how I dealt with brother Dowley. I began speaking in a lazy, easy manner, as though I were only talking to pass the time; and the oldest man alive could not have traced my starting point and guessed where I intended to end up:\\n\\n\u201cBoys, there are many curious things about law, custom, usage, and all that sort of thing, when you examine them closely; yes, and about the direction and progress of human opinion and action, too. There are written laws\u2014they perish; but there are also unwritten laws\u2014they are eternal. Take the unwritten law of wages: it says they must rise, little by little, steadily through the centuries. And notice how it works. We know what wages are now, here and there and elsewhere; we take an average and call that the wages of today. We know what wages were a hundred years ago, and what they were two hundred years ago; that is as far back as we can go, but it is enough to show us the law of progress, the measure and rate of each increase. Thus, without a document to guide us, we can come fairly close to determining what wages were three, four, and five hundred years ago. Good, so far. Do we stop there? No. We stop looking backward, turn around, and apply the law to the future. My friends, I can tell you what people\u2019s wages will be at any future date you care to name, for hundreds and hundreds of years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. In seven hundred years, wages will have risen to six times what they are now, here in your region, and farmhands will be paid 3 cents a day, and mechanics 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI wish I could die now and l