{"request_id":"aef8629c8cb7be57397ba328","rewritten_text":"Many years ago, I became acquainted with a Mr. William Legrand. He came from an old Huguenot family and had once been wealthy, but a series of misfortunes had reduced him to poverty. To escape the humiliation caused by his losses, he left New Orleans, the city of his ancestors, and settled on Sullivan’s Island, near Charleston, South Carolina.

The island is a very unusual place. It consists of little more than sea sand and is about three miles long. Nowhere is it more than a quarter of a mile wide. A barely visible creek separates it from the mainland, winding through a wilderness of reeds and mud, a favorite refuge of the marsh hen. As one might expect, the vegetation is sparse, or at least stunted. No trees of any size can be seen. Near the western end, where Fort Moultrie stands and where a few miserable wooden buildings house, during summer, those fleeing Charleston’s dust and fever, the bristly palmetto does grow. But apart from this western point and a strip of firm, white beach along the seacoast, the entire island is covered by dense growths of sweet myrtle, so highly valued by English gardeners. Here the shrubs often reach fifteen or twenty feet in height, forming an almost impenetrable thicket and filling the air with their fragrance.

Deep within this thicket, not far from the island’s eastern, more remote end, Legrand had built himself a small hut. He lived there when I first met him by mere chance. Our acquaintance soon became a friendship, for the recluse possessed much to inspire interest and respect. I found him well educated and unusually intelligent, though marked by misanthropy and prone to strange shifts between enthusiasm and melancholy. He owned many books but seldom used them. His principal pastimes were shooting and fishing, or wandering along the beach and through the myrtles in search of shells and insects—his collection of the latter might have been envied by Swammerdamm. On these excursions he was generally accompanied by an old Black man named Jupiter, who had been freed before the family’s reverses but could be persuaded neither by threats nor promises to give up what he regarded as his duty: following his young “Massa Will.” It is possible that Legrand’s relatives, believing his mind somewhat unstable, had deliberately encouraged Jupiter’s stubbornness so that he might watch over and protect the wanderer."}

