          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"66388c77f03f2642a9703661\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cMen marry because they are tired; women because they are curious. Both are disappointed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think I\u2019m likely to marry, Harry. I\u2019m too much in love. That\u2019s one of your sayings. I\u2019m putting it into practice, as I do everything you say.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho are you in love with?\u201d Lord Henry asked after a pause.\\n\\n\u201cAn actress,\u201d said Dorian Gray, blushing.\\n\\nLord Henry shrugged. \u201cThat\u2019s a rather ordinary beginning.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou wouldn\u2019t say that if you saw her, Harry.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWho is she?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHer name is Sibyl Vane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever heard of her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo one has. But they will someday. She\u2019s a genius.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear boy, no woman is a genius. Women are a decorative sex. They never have anything to say, but they say it charmingly. Women represent matter\u2019s triumph over mind, just as men represent mind\u2019s triumph over morals.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHarry, how can you say that?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Dorian, it\u2019s quite true. I\u2019m studying women at present, so I ought to know. The subject isn\u2019t as difficult as I thought. In the end, I find there are only two kinds of women: the plain and the painted. Plain women are very useful. If you want a reputation for respectability, you only have to take them down to supper. The other women are very charming. They make one mistake, however. They paint their faces to look young. Our grandmothers painted theirs so they could speak brilliantly. Rouge and wit used to go together. That\u2019s over now. As long as a woman can look ten years younger than her own daughter, she\u2019s perfectly satisfied. As for conversation, there are only five women in London worth talking to, and two of them can\u2019t be admitted to decent society. But tell me about your genius. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh, Harry, your opinions frighten me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNever mind that. How long have you known her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAbout three weeks.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where did you meet her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ll tell you, Harry, but you mustn\u2019t be unsympathetic. After all, it never would have happened if I hadn\u2019t met you. You filled me with a wild desire to know everything about life. For days after meeting you, something seemed to pulse through my veins. As I lounged in the park or walked down Piccadilly, I looked at everyone who passed and wondered, with a mad curiosity, what kind of lives they led. Some fascinated me. Others filled me with terror. There was a delicious poison in the air. I had a passion for sensations....\u201d\"}"
