          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2d5d174b9570b4f0edc19766\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He was short but remarkably upright, and his voice was the sweetest I had ever heard. He began his lecture by reviewing the history of chemistry and the improvements made by different learned men, speaking with passion as he pronounced the names of the most distinguished discoverers. He then gave a brief account of the science as it stood and explained many of its basic terms. After performing a few preliminary experiments, he ended with a praise of modern chemistry, whose words I shall never forget:\\n\\n\u201cThe ancient teachers of this science,\u201d he said, \u201cpromised impossibilities and accomplished nothing. The modern masters promise very little; they know that metals cannot be changed into one another and that the elixir of life is a fantasy. Yet these philosophers, whose hands seem made only for dabbling in dirt and whose eyes are fixed on the microscope or crucible, have truly performed miracles. They enter nature\u2019s hidden places and reveal how she works there. They rise into the heavens; they have discovered how blood circulates and the nature of the air we breathe. They have gained new and almost unlimited powers; they can command the thunder, imitate an earthquake, and even mock the invisible world with its own shadows.\u201d\\n\\nThose were the professor\u2019s words\u2014or rather, the words of fate, spoken to destroy me. As he continued, I felt as though my soul were struggling with a real enemy. One by one, the keys that formed the mechanism of my being were pressed; chord after chord sounded, and soon my mind held one thought, one idea, one purpose. So much has been done, cried the soul of Frankenstein\u2014far more will I achieve. Following the path already marked, I will open a new one, explore unknown powers, and reveal to the world the deepest mysteries of creation.\\n\\nI did not close my eyes that night. My inner being was in rebellion and turmoil; I felt that order would come from it, but I had no power to create that order. Gradually, after dawn, sleep came. I awoke, and the thoughts of the previous night seemed like a dream. Only one decision remained: to return to my former studies and devote myself to a science for which I believed I had a natural talent. That same day I visited M. Waldman. In private, his manner was even gentler and more appealing than in public. During his lecture, there had been a certain dignity in his bearing; in his own home, this was replaced by the greatest friendliness and kindness.\"}"
