          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b24506a2daafbe8d6b6806eb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Nearly a year had passed since my father\u2019s return, and the seasons had almost completed their cycle. It was now the end of May; the woods were covered in their freshest green, and the sweet scent of newly mown grass filled the fields. I thought the gentle air and beautiful face of Nature might help awaken mild feelings in him, giving him a peaceful and loving mood before I tried to gain the confidence I had resolved to seek.\\n\\nI therefore chose the evening of one of these days for my attempt. I invited him to walk with me and led him to a nearby beech wood, whose light shade protected us from the slanting, dazzling rays of the setting sun. After walking silently for some time, I sat with him on a mossy hillock. It is strange, but even now I seem to see the place. Many of the slender, smooth trunks were wrapped in ivy, whose shining, darkest-green leaves stood out against the white bark and the pale leaves of the young beech shoots growing from their parent trunks. The short grass was mixed with moss and partly covered by the dead leaves of the previous autumn, which the wind had gathered here and there into little mounds. A few moss-covered stumps stood nearby. The leaves moved gently in the breeze, and through their green canopy we could see the bright blue sky. As evening approached, the distant trunks turned red in the sunlight, and the wind died completely, while a few birds flew past us toward their evening rest.\\n\\nHere we sat together. When you hear all that happened\u2014all that terrible suffering that tore our souls even in this peaceful place, which might have been a paradise to us without those strange passions\u2014you will not wonder that I remember it as I looked upon it. Its calm helped calm me and gave me not only courage but persuasive words. I saw all these things and noted them absently while trying to arrange my thoughts in the right order for my attempt. My heart beat quickly as I prepared to speak, for I was determined not to be rejected, yet I trembled to imagine what effect my words might have on him. At last, after much hesitation, I began:\\n\\n\u201cYour kindness to me, my dearest father, and the affection\u2014the excessive affection\u2014you showed me when you first returned will, I hope, excuse me for daring to speak to you, though with the tender affection of a daughter, yet also with the freedom of a friend and an equal.\u201d\"}"
