          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"805c390427ccb6ddedbc9883\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, your father, certainly, may spare you, if your mother is able. Daughters are never of such importance to a father. And if you remain another _month_ in full, I shall be able to take one of you as far as London, since I am going there at the beginning of June, for a week; and as Dawson has no objection to the barouche-box, there will be ample room for one of you\u2014and, indeed, should the weather prove cool, I should not mind taking you both, as neither of you is large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are all kindness, madam; but I believe we must adhere to our original plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine appeared resigned. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I always speak my mind, and I cannot endure the thought of two young women travelling post alone. It is highly improper. You must find some way to send someone. I have the greatest dislike in the world to that kind of thing. Young women ought always to be properly protected and attended, in keeping with their station in life. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I insisted that two men-servants accompany her. Miss Darcy, the daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley, and Lady Anne, could not properly have appeared otherwise. I am extremely particular about such matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am glad it occurred to me to say so; for it would truly be discreditable to _you_ to allow them to go alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle is to send a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle! He keeps a man-servant, does he? I am very pleased that you have someone who attends to such matters. Where will you change horses? Oh, Bromley, naturally. If you give my name at the Bell, you will be properly attended.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had many more questions concerning their journey; and since she did not answer every one herself, attention was required\u2014which Elizabeth considered fortunate; for, with her mind so engaged, she might otherwise have forgotten where she was. Reflection had to be saved for solitary hours: whenever she was alone, she surrendered to it as the greatest relief; and not a day passed without a solitary walk, during which she could indulge in all the pleasure of disagreeable recollections.\\n\\nShe was well on her way to knowing Mr. Darcy\u2019s letter by heart. She examined every sentence; and her feelings toward its writer were sometimes entirely different. When she recalled the manner of his address, she remained filled with indignation: but when she reflected on how unjustly she had condemned and reproached him, her anger turned upon herself; and his disappointed feelings became objects of compassion.\"}"
