{"request_id":"0972a2d7e6b153af652cb93f","rewritten_text":"The following morning, when the Otis family assembled at breakfast, they spoke at considerable length about the ghost. The United States Minister was naturally somewhat irritated to discover that his gift had not been accepted. ‘I have no desire,’ he said, ‘to cause the ghost any personal harm, and I must admit that, seeing how long he has occupied the house, I do not think it especially courteous to throw pillows at him’—a perfectly reasonable observation, at which, I regret to say, the twins broke into loud laughter. ‘On the other hand,’ he went on, ‘if he truly refuses to use the Rising Sun Lubricator, we shall have to remove his chains. It would be utterly impossible to sleep with such a racket outside the bedrooms.’

For the remainder of the week, however, they were left in peace. The sole matter that attracted notice was the bloodstain’s constant reappearance on the library floor. This was certainly peculiar, since Mr. Otis always locked the door at night, and the windows were kept firmly barred. The stain’s chameleon-like colouring, too, provoked much discussion. On some mornings it was a dull, almost Indian red; then it became vermilion, then a deep purple; and once, when they came down for family prayers, conducted according to the plain rites of the Free American Reformed Episcopalian Church, they discovered it to be a vivid emerald green. These kaleidoscopic transformations naturally entertained the whole party, and wagers were made freely each evening. The only one who failed to enjoy the joke was little Virginia, who, for some unexplained reason, was always greatly troubled by the bloodstain, and nearly wept on the morning it appeared emerald green.

The ghost’s second appearance occurred on Sunday night. Not long after they had retired, they were suddenly startled by a terrible crash in the hall. Hurrying downstairs, they saw that a large suit of antique armour had come loose from its stand and fallen onto the stone floor, while the Canterville ghost sat in a high-backed chair, rubbing his knees with an expression of intense pain. The twins had brought their pea-shooters and immediately fired two pellets at him, with the precision of aim that can be acquired only through long, careful practice upon a writing-master. Meanwhile, the United States Minister covered him with his revolver and, following Californian etiquette, ordered him to hold up his hands! With a furious shriek, the ghost sprang to his feet and passed through them like mist, snuffing out Washington Otis’s candle as he went and leaving them all in complete darkness."}

