          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8eed299b871e26596b7fff6c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mary never wrote to Bath herself; all the labor of maintaining a slow and unsatisfactory correspondence with Elizabeth fell to Anne.\\n\\nBreakfast had scarcely been over when Captain and Mrs. Harville and Captain Benwick joined them; they had arranged to take their final walk around Lyme together. They should leave for Uppercross by one, and until then they meant to remain together and outside for as long as possible.\\n\\nAnne discovered Captain Benwick drawing near her as soon as they were properly in the street. Their conversation of the previous evening had not made him reluctant to seek her company again; and for some time they walked together, speaking as before about Mr. Scott and Lord Byron, still no more able than before\u2014or than any other two readers\u2014to agree precisely about the merits of either, until something caused an almost general rearrangement of the party, and Captain Harville took Captain Benwick\u2019s place beside her.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Elliot,\u201d he said, in a rather quiet voice, \u201cyou have done a good thing by getting that poor fellow to talk so much. I wish he could have such companionship more often. I know it is bad for him to be confined as he is; but what can we do? We cannot separate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI can readily believe that impossible; but perhaps, with time\u2014we know what time accomplishes in every kind of sorrow, and you must remember, Captain Harville, that your friend may still be considered a young mourner\u2014only last summer, as I understand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAy, quite true,\u201d (with a heavy sigh) \u201conly June.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd perhaps he did not know until rather later.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot until the first week of August, when he returned from the Cape, having just been made captain of the Grappler. I was at Plymouth, fearing to hear of him; he sent in letters, but the Grappler had orders for Portsmouth. The news would have to reach him there, but who was to deliver it? not I. I would almost as soon have been hanged from the yard-arm. No one could do it except that good fellow\u201d (pointing to Captain Wentworth). \u201cThe Laconia had entered Plymouth the week before, and there was no likelihood of her being sent to sea again. He took his chance with the rest; applied for leave of absence, but without waiting for the answer travelled night and day until he reached Portsmouth, rowed out to the Grappler at once, and did not leave the poor fellow for a week. That is what he did, and no one else could have saved poor James. You may judge, Miss Elliot, whether he is dear to us!\u201d\\n\\nAnne considered the question with complete certainty, and answered as fully as her own feelings allowed, or as much as his seemed able to endure, for he was too deeply moved to continue the subject; and when he spoke again, it was about something entirely different.\"}"
