          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6bd488ef44da8180dd593eea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Even reading about the dead man's luxury was wonderful.\\n\\nThen he turned to embroidery and to the tapestries that served as frescoes in the cold rooms of northern Europe. As he studied the subject\u2014and he had an extraordinary ability to become completely absorbed in whatever he was doing\u2014he was almost saddened by the thought of how time destroyed beautiful and marvelous things. He, at least, had escaped that fate. Summer came after summer, and the yellow jonquils bloomed and died many times, while nights of horror repeated the story of their shame, but he remained unchanged. No winter damaged his face or dulled his flowerlike beauty. How different it was with material things! Where had they gone? Where was the great crocus-colored robe, made by brown girls for Athena's pleasure, on which the gods fought the giants? Where was the enormous canopy Nero stretched across Rome's Colosseum, that purple Titan's sail showing the starry sky and Apollo driving a chariot drawn by white horses with golden reins? He longed to see the strange tablecloths made for the Priest of the Sun, displaying every delicacy and dish needed for a feast; King Chilperic's burial cloth, with its three hundred golden bees; the fantastic robes that angered the Bishop of Pontus, decorated with \u201clions, panthers, bears, dogs, forests, rocks, hunters\u2014all, in fact, that a painter can copy from nature\u201d; and the coat once worn by Charles of Orleans, whose sleeves carried the verses of a song beginning \u201c_Madame, je suis tout joyeux_.\u201d The music was embroidered in gold thread, and each note, square-shaped in those days, was made with four pearls. He read about the room prepared at the palace in Rheims for Queen Joan of Burgundy, decorated with \u201cthirteen hundred and twenty-one parrots, made in embroidery, and bearing the king\u2019s arms, and five hundred and sixty-one butterflies, whose wings were similarly marked with the queen\u2019s arms, the whole worked in gold.\u201d Catherine de Medicis had a mourning bed made of black velvet covered with crescents and suns. Its damask curtains showed leafy wreaths and garlands on a gold-and-silver background, with pearl embroidery along the edges. It stood in a room hung with rows of the queen\u2019s emblems, cut from black velvet and set against silver cloth. Louis XIV had gold-embroidered caryatids fifteen feet high in his apartment.\"}"
