{"request_id":"854e40ed32ff698bc28a2f58","rewritten_text":"When I had stopped weeping, reason and memory returned, and I began to consider more calmly what had occurred and how it became me to act. Only a few hours had gone by, yet a mighty revolution had taken place in my regard—the natural work of years had been accomplished since morning. My father was as dead to me, and for a moment I felt as though he, with his white hairs, lay in his coffin, while I—my youth vanished in approaching age—wept over his timely dissolution. But it was not so: I was still young, oh, far too young, and he was not dead to others; yet I, most miserable, must never see or speak with him again. I must flee from him more earnestly than from my greatest enemy; in solitude or in cities, I must never behold him again. That thought left me breathless with anguish, and, fixing itself upon my imagination, made me unable for a while to pursue any chain of ideas. Henceforth, I thought, I would live in the dreariest seclusion. I would withdraw to the Continent and become a nun—not for religion’s sake, since I was not a Catholic, but so that I might be forever shut away from the world. There I should find solitude in which to weep, and the voices of life could never reach me.

But my father—my beloved and most wretched father? Would he die? Would he never master the fierce passion that now held pitiless dominion over him? Might he not, many years hence, when age had extinguished the burning feelings he now endured, become once more a father to me? This thought smoothed my brow, and I could feel—and wept to feel it—a half-melancholy smile draw from my lips their look of suffering. I dared hope more favorably for my future life. Years must pass, but hope would wing them swiftly away; or, if they passed heavily, still they would pass, and I had not lost my father forever. Let him spend another sixteen years in desolate wandering; let him once again utter his wild complaints to the vast woods and tremendous cataracts of another land; let him again endure fearful danger and soul-subduing hardships; let the southern sun once more scorch his passion-worn cheeks, and the cold rains of night fall upon him and chill his blood.

To this life, miserable father, I devote you! Go! Let your days be spent among savages and your nights beneath the canopy of heaven! Let your limbs grow worn and your heart grow cold, and let all youth die within you!"}

