          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"37014b6adf18852d95aa2651\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI am not eating at home, so I shall not need you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, sir,\u201d said the man as he left the room.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Alan, there is no time to lose. How heavy this chest is! I\u2019ll carry it for you. You bring the other things.\u201d He spoke quickly and with authority. Campbell felt himself being controlled by him. They left the room together.\\n\\nWhen they reached the top landing, Dorian took out the key and turned it in the lock. Then he stopped, and a troubled expression came over his face. He shuddered. \u201cI don\u2019t think I can go in, Alan,\u201d he whispered.\\n\\n\u201cIt means nothing to me. I don\u2019t need you,\u201d Campbell said coldly.\\n\\nDorian opened the door halfway. At once he saw the face of his portrait leering in the sunlight. The torn curtain lay on the floor before it. He remembered that the night before, for the first time in his life, he had forgotten to cover the fatal canvas. He was about to rush forward, but drew back with a shudder.\\n\\nWhat was that disgusting red moisture shining wetly on one of the hands, as if the canvas had sweated blood? How horrible it was! For a moment, it seemed even more horrible than the silent thing he knew was stretched across the table\u2014the thing whose grotesque, misshapen shadow on the stained carpet showed that it had not moved, but was still there, just as he had left it.\\n\\nHe drew a deep breath, opened the door a little farther, and, with his eyes half closed and his head turned away, walked quickly inside, determined not to look even once at the dead man. Then he bent down, picked up the gold-and-purple hanging, and threw it over the picture.\\n\\nHe stopped there, afraid to turn around, and fixed his eyes on the complicated pattern before him. He heard Campbell bringing in the heavy chest, the irons, and the other things he had requested for his terrible work. He began to wonder whether he and Basil Hallward had ever met, and, if they had, what they had thought of each other.\\n\\n\u201cLeave me now,\u201d said a stern voice behind him.\\n\\nHe turned and hurried out, only noticing that the dead man had been pushed back into the chair and that Campbell was staring into a shining yellow face. As he went downstairs, he heard the key turning in the lock.\\n\\nIt was well after seven when Campbell returned to the library. He was pale, but completely calm. \u201cI have done what you asked me to do,\u201d he muttered.\"}"
