{"request_id":"67a82955c1e16671aa62601d","rewritten_text":"Perdita’s name was never spoken; yet I could not doubt that his love for her occasioned the wavering purpose he displayed. And who was more deserving of love than my noble-hearted sister? Who better merited the hand of this self-exalted king than she, whose look was worthy of a queen among nations? She loved him as he loved her, though disappointment had subdued her passion, while ambition struggled fiercely against his.

We went together to the House that evening. Raymond, though he knew that his plans and prospects were to be debated and determined in the expected discussion, was cheerful and unconcerned. As we entered the coffee-room, a murmur like that of ten thousand swarming bee-hives overwhelmed us. Groups of politicians stood gathered, their brows anxious and their voices loud or solemn. The aristocratic party, the wealthiest and most powerful men in England, seemed less disturbed than the rest, since the question was to be considered without their intervention. By the fire stood Ryland with his adherents. Ryland was of obscure birth, but possessed enormous wealth, inherited from his father, a manufacturer. As a young man, he had witnessed the king’s abdication and the union of the two Houses of Lords and Commons; he had sympathized with these popular encroachments, and making them firmer and more extensive had been the work of his life. Since that time, the influence of the landed proprietors had increased; and at first Ryland had not regretted Lord Raymond’s schemes, which diverted many of his rival’s supporters. But matters had now gone too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the restoration of sovereignty as something that would return to them their lost power and rights. The nearly extinguished spirit of royalty stirred again in men’s minds; and they, willing slaves and self-appointed subjects, stood ready to bow their necks beneath the yoke. A few upright and manly spirits still remained, the pillars of the state; but the word republic had become stale to common ears, and many—the event would show whether they formed a majority—longed for royalty’s glitter and display. Ryland was awakened to opposition; he declared that his own forbearance had allowed this party to grow, but the season of indulgence was over, and with a single sweep of his arm he would clear away the cobwebs obscuring his countrymen’s sight.

When Raymond entered the coffee-room, his friends greeted him almost with a cheer. They clustered around him, reckoned their numbers, and explained why they were now certain to gain the support of various members who had not yet announced themselves."}

