{"request_id":"765a6f637d94ef8298f340e7","rewritten_text":"I drilled a hole in the end of the keg above the pitcher and fitted it with a soft-wood plug, cut into a tapering, conical shape. I pushed the plug in or drew it out as needed until, after several experiments, it reached exactly the right tightness: the water seeping through the hole and falling into the pitcher below would fill it to the brim in sixty minutes. This was easily determined by observing how much of the pitcher filled during any given interval. Once this was arranged, the rest of the plan was obvious. I had made my bed on the floor of the car so that, when I lay down, my head was directly beneath the pitcher’s mouth. It was clear that, after an hour, the full pitcher would overflow, and that it would spill from the mouth, which was slightly lower than the rim. It was equally clear that water falling from a height of more than four feet could only land on my face, with the certain result of waking me instantly, even from the deepest sleep.

“It was nearly eleven when I finished these arrangements, and I immediately went to bed, fully confident in my invention’s effectiveness. Nor was I disappointed. Every sixty minutes, I was awakened by my reliable timepiece; after emptying the pitcher into the keg’s bung-hole and carrying out the condenser’s duties, I returned to bed. These regular interruptions disturbed my sleep even less than I had expected. When I finally rose for the day, it was seven o’clock, and the sun stood many degrees above the line of my horizon.

“April 3. I found the balloon at an immense altitude, and the earth’s apparent curvature had increased considerably. Far below me in the ocean lay a cluster of black specks, which were undoubtedly islands. Far to the north, I saw a thin, white, intensely brilliant line or streak along the horizon’s edge, and I had no doubt that it was the southern edge of the Polar Sea’s ice. My curiosity was greatly aroused, for I hoped to travel much farther north and might perhaps, at some point, find myself directly above the Pole. I now regretted that my great altitude would prevent me from making as accurate a survey as I wished."}

