          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9ed9dbf0e1cf1efbbdb7b791\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I wonder how long you would have continued, if you had been left to yourself. I wonder when you would have spoken if I had not asked you! My decision to thank you for your kindness to Lydia certainly had a powerful effect. Too powerful, I fear; for what becomes of the moral if our happiness comes from a broken promise, since I ought not to have mentioned the subject? This will never do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou need not trouble yourself. The moral will be entirely sound. Lady Catherine\u2019s unjustifiable efforts to separate us were what removed all my doubts. I do not owe my present happiness to your eager wish to express your gratitude. I was not in the mood to wait for you to begin. My aunt\u2019s information had given me hope, and I was determined to learn everything at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLady Catherine has been immensely useful, which ought to please her, since she loves being useful. But tell me, why did you come to Netherfield? Was it only to ride to Longbourn and feel embarrassed? Or had you intended something more serious?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy true purpose was to see you and judge, if I could, whether I might ever hope to make you love me. My declared purpose\u2014or the one I admitted to myself\u2014was to see whether your sister still cared for Bingley and, if she did, to make the confession to him that I have since made.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you ever have the courage to tell Lady Catherine what is going to happen to her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am more likely to need time than courage, Elizabeth. But it must be done; and if you will give me a sheet of paper, I shall do it immediately.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if I did not have a letter of my own to write, I might sit beside you and admire the steadiness of your handwriting, as another young lady once did. But I have an aunt as well, who must not be neglected any longer.\u201d\\n\\nBecause she was unwilling to admit how greatly she had exaggerated her intimacy with Mr. Darcy, Elizabeth had not yet replied to Mrs. Gardiner\u2019s long letter. But now she had something to tell her that she knew would be most welcome, and she was almost ashamed to realize that her uncle and aunt had already lost three days of happiness. She immediately wrote as follows:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI should have thanked you sooner, my dear aunt, as I ought, for your long, kind, and satisfying account of everything; but, to tell the truth, I was too cross to write. You imagined more than truly existed. But now imagine as much as you please; let your fancy run free and indulge your imagination in every direction the subject allows, and unless you believe me actually married, you cannot be very far wrong.\"}"
