          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a1c08ee1c025d80ba42d179b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She left everything behind her, except the memory that such things had existed.\\n\\nAnne had not entered Kellynch since leaving Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. There had been no need, and on the few occasions when it might have been possible for her to go to the Hall, she had managed to avoid and escape it. Her first return was to take up her place in the Lodge\u2019s modern, elegant rooms and delight its mistress.\\n\\nSome anxiety mingled with Lady Russell\u2019s joy at seeing her. She knew who had been visiting Uppercross. But fortunately, either Anne had improved in fullness and appearance, or Lady Russell imagined that she had; and as Anne accepted her compliments, she enjoyed connecting them with her cousin\u2019s silent admiration and hoped she might be blessed with a second spring of youth and beauty.\\n\\nWhen they began to talk, Anne soon became aware of a change in her mind. The subjects that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch, and that she had felt neglected and been forced to suppress among the Musgroves, had now become matters of secondary importance. She had lately even lost sight of her father, her sister, and Bath. Their concerns had been overwhelmed by those of Uppercross; and when Lady Russell returned to their former hopes and fears, expressing her satisfaction with the house in Camden Place that had been taken and her regret that Mrs Clay should still remain with them, Anne would have been ashamed to admit how much more she was thinking of Lyme, Louisa Musgrove, and all her acquaintances there; how much more interesting to her were the home and friendship of the Harvilles and Captain Benwick than her father\u2019s house in Camden Place or her sister\u2019s intimacy with Mrs Clay. She was actually obliged to make an effort to meet Lady Russell with anything resembling equal concern on subjects that naturally had the first claim upon her.\\n\\nAt