          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"239a9bfae05f1e088e66406b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Four days after these strange events, a funeral left Canterville Chase at about eleven o\u2019clock at night. The hearse was pulled by eight black horses, each wearing a large, nodding plume of ostrich feathers on its head. The lead coffin was covered with a rich purple cloth, embroidered in gold with the Canterville coat of arms. Servants carrying lit torches walked beside the hearse and carriages, making the whole procession deeply impressive. Lord Canterville was the chief mourner. He had come especially from Wales for the funeral and sat in the first carriage with little Virginia. The United States Minister and his wife followed, then Washington and the three boys, while Mrs. Umney rode in the last carriage. Everyone felt that, since the ghost had frightened her for more than fifty years, she deserved to see the end of him. A deep grave had been dug in a corner of the churchyard, beneath the old yew tree, and the Rev. Augustus Dampier conducted the service with great solemnity. When it was over, the servants followed an old Canterville family custom and put out their torches. As the coffin was lowered into the grave, Virginia stepped forward and placed on it a large cross made of white and pink almond blossoms. At that moment, the moon emerged from behind a cloud and filled the small churchyard with quiet silver light, while a nightingale began singing in a distant grove. Virginia remembered the ghost\u2019s description of the Garden of Death. Her eyes filled with tears, and she scarcely spoke during the journey home.\\n\\nThe next morning, before Lord Canterville left for London, Mr. Otis spoke with him about the jewels the ghost had given Virginia. They were extremely magnificent, especially a ruby necklace in an old Venetian setting, a superb example of sixteenth-century craftsmanship. Their value was so great that Mr. Otis had serious doubts about letting his daughter keep them.\\n\\n\u2018My lord,\u2019 he said, \u2018I know that in this country the law of mortmain applies to jewels as well as land, and it seems clear to me that these jewels are, or ought to be, family heirlooms belonging to your family. I must therefore ask you to take them to London and consider them simply part of your property, restored to you under these unusual circumstances. As for my daughter, she is only a child and, fortunately, has little interest in such objects of empty luxury.\u2019\"}"
