          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c6f77d595f1c2f119b66c481\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut, in fact, once we have risen to any given altitude, the amount of air overcome by rising still farther is by no means proportional to the additional height climbed (as can plainly be seen from what was stated earlier), but follows a continually diminishing ratio. It is therefore clear that, however high we may ascend, we cannot, strictly speaking, reach a boundary beyond which no atmosphere exists. It must continue, I argued, though perhaps in a state of infinite rarefaction.\\n\\n\u201cOn the other hand, I knew that arguments had been advanced to prove the existence of a real and definite limit to the atmosphere, beyond which there is absolutely no air. Yet one circumstance, overlooked by those who maintained such a limit, seemed to me\u2014though not a positive refutation of their belief\u2014nevertheless worthy of the most serious investigation. When the intervals between Encke\u2019s comet\u2019s successive arrivals at perihelion are compared, after allowing with the greatest accuracy for all disturbances caused by the planets, the periods appear gradually to diminish; that is, the major axis of the comet\u2019s ellipse is slowly but steadily growing shorter. Now this is exactly what should occur if the comet encountered resistance from an extremely rare ethereal medium filling the regions of its orbit. Such a medium, by slowing the comet\u2019s velocity, must increase its centripetal force by weakening its centrifugal force. In other words, the sun\u2019s attraction would continually gain strength, and the comet would be drawn closer at every revolution. Indeed, there is no other way to explain the variation in question. But again: the actual diameter of this comet\u2019s nebulosity is observed to contract rapidly as it approaches the sun, and to expand just as rapidly as it travels away toward its aphelion. Was I not justified, with M. Valz, in supposing that this ap