          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5a4c6e5fc54d1572551c4f4a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Elizabeth was sorrowful and dispirited; she had lost all pleasure in her usual occupations, and every enjoyment seemed to her a sacrilege against the dead. She believed that endless grief and tears were the fitting tribute owed to innocence so cruelly ruined and destroyed. She was no longer the happy girl who, in our earlier youth, had walked with me beside the lake and spoken with delight of the hopes before us. The first of those sorrows sent to loosen our attachment to the earth had come upon her, and its shadowing influence had extinguished her sweetest smiles.\\n\\n\u201cWhen I think, my dear cousin,\u201d she said, \u201cof the wretched death of Justine Moritz, I no longer behold the world and its works as they once appeared. Formerly, I regarded the accounts of vice and injustice that I read in books or heard from others as stories of distant times or imagined evils; at least, they seemed remote, and more familiar to reason than to the imagination. But now misery has entered my own home, and men seem to me like monsters thirsting for one another\u2019s blood. Yet I am surely unjust. Everyone believed that poor girl guilty; and if she could have committed the crime for which she suffered, she must indeed have been the most depraved of human beings. To murder, for the sake of a few jewels, the son of her benefactor and friend\u2014a child she had nursed from birth and seemed to love as her own! I could never consent to the death of any human being, but certainly I should have judged such a creature unfit to live among men. Yet she was innocent. I know it, I feel it; you believe the same, and that confirms me. Alas, Victor, when falsehood can resemble truth so closely, who can feel secure in any happiness? I seem to be walking along the brink of a precipice, toward which thousands press, trying to cast me into the abyss. William and Justine were murdered, while the killer remains at large; he moves freely through the world, perhaps even respected. But though I were condemned to die upon the scaffold for those same crimes, I would not exchange places with such a wretch.\u201d\\n\\nI heard these words in the deepest anguish. Though I had not committed the murders directly, I was in truth their real cause. Elizabeth saw my suffering in my face and, gently taking my hand, said, \u201cMy dearest friend, you must compose yourself. These events have wounded me, God knows how deeply; but I am not as miserable as you are.\"}"
