          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3a1091782ed6c70f9dee5c46\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her situation was truly one of extraordinary trial and difficulty. Though she felt the strongest, most urgent desire to uncover the mystery surrounding Oliver\u2019s history, she could not betray the confidence placed in her by the wretched woman with whom she had just spoken, who had trusted her as a young and innocent girl. The woman\u2019s words and manner had touched Rose Maylie\u2019s heart; and, joined to her love for her young charge, and scarcely less sincere or fervent, was her earnest wish to bring the outcast back to repentance and hope.\\n\\nThey intended to remain in London for only three days before leaving for several weeks in a distant part of the coast. It was now midnight on the first day. What action could she decide upon that might be carried out within forty-eight hours? Or how could she delay the journey without arousing suspicion?\\n\\nMr. Losberne was with them and would remain for the next two days; but Rose knew the excellent gentleman\u2019s impulsive nature too well, and could too clearly foresee the anger with which, in the first burst of his indignation, he would view the person responsible for Oliver\u2019s recapture, to entrust him with the secret while no experienced person was present to support her arguments on the girl\u2019s behalf. These considerations made the greatest caution and discretion necessary in telling Mrs. Maylie, whose first instinct would certainly be to consult the worthy doctor about the matter. Seeking legal advice, even had she known how to do so, was hardly possible for the same reason. For a moment she thought of asking Harry for help; but this brought back the memory of their last parting, and it seemed unworthy of her to summon him when\u2014tears rising to her eyes as she followed the thought\u2014he might by now have learned to forget her and be happier away.\\n\\nTroubled by these conflicting thoughts, leaning first toward one course and then another, and shrinking from each as new considerations arose, Rose spent a sleepless, anxious night. After further reflection the next day, she reached the desperate decision to consult Harry.\\n\\n\u201cIf it pains him to return here,\u201d she thought, \u201chow much more it will pain me! But perhaps he will not come; he may write, or he may come in person and carefully avoid seeing me\u2014he did so when he left. I scarcely expected it; but it was better for both of us.\u201d At this point Rose let the pen fall and turned away, as though the very paper that would carry her message must not witness her tears.\"}"
