          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4f05e10462f3aa34c68ae686\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We proclaimed ourselves freemen so loudly and earnestly that we drew the interested attention of the liberty-preaching orator and his patriotic crowd. They gathered around us and took up a very determined stance. The orator said:\\n\\n\u201cIf you are truly freemen, you have nothing to fear\u2014the God-given liberties of Britain surround you as shield and shelter!\u201d (Applause.) \u201cYou shall soon see. Produce your proof.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat proof?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cProof that you are freemen.\u201d\\n\\nAh\u2014I remembered! I came to my senses and said nothing. But the king burst out:\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re insane, man. It would be more reasonable for this thief and scoundrel here to prove that we are _not_ freemen.\u201d\\n\\nYou see, he knew his own laws as other people so often know laws: by their words, not their effects. They take on a _meaning_ and become very vivid when you have to apply them to yourself.\\n\\nEveryone shook his head and looked disappointed; some turned away, no longer interested. The orator said\u2014and this time in the tones of business, not sentiment:\\n\\n\u201cIf you do not know your country\u2019s laws, it is time you learned them. You are strangers to us; you will not deny that. You may be freemen\u2014we do not deny it\u2014but you may also be slaves. The law is clear: it does not require the claimant to prove that you are slaves; it requires you to prove that you are not.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cDear sir, give us only time to send to Astolat; or give us only time to send to the Valley of Holiness\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPeace, good man. These are extraordinary requests, and you cannot expect them to be granted. It would take much time and cause your master unreasonable inconvenience\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201c_Master_, idiot!\u201d the king stormed. \u201cI have no master; I myself am the m\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSilence, for God\u2019s sake!\u201d\\n\\nI got the words out in time to stop the king. We were already in enough trouble; it could not help us to give these people the idea that we were lunatics.\\n\\nThere is no point in dragging out the details. The earl housed us and sold us at auction. That same infernal law had existed in our own South in my own time, more than thirteen hundred years later, and under it hundreds of freemen who could not prove they were freemen had been sold into lifelong slavery without making any particular impression on me. But the moment the law and auction block entered my personal experience, something that had merely seemed improper became suddenly hellish. Well, that is how we are made.\\n\\nYes, we were sold at auction, like swine. In a large town with an active market, we should have brought a good price; but this place was utterly stagnant, and we were sold for a figure that shames me whenever I think of it.\"}"
