          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"36ed5eb85c518785f20df116\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On that subject, therefore, I shall remain entirely silent; and you may be assured that, once we are married, no ungenerous reproach shall ever escape me.\u201d\\n\\nIt was now absolutely necessary to stop him.\\n\\n\u201cYou are too hasty, sir,\u201d she exclaimed. \u201cYou forget that I have given no answer. Let me do so without further delay. Accept my thanks for the compliment you offer me. I am very sensible of the honour of your proposals, but it is impossible for me to do anything but refuse them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am not now to be informed,\u201d replied Mr. Collins, with a formal gesture of the hand, \u201cthat it is customary for young ladies to reject the addresses of the man they secretly intend to accept when he first seeks their favour; and that the refusal is sometimes repeated a second, or even a third, time. I am therefore not at all discouraged by what you have just said, and shall hope to conduct you to the altar before long.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cUpon my word, sir,\u201d cried Elizabeth, \u201cyour hope is a most extraordinary one after my declaration. I assure you that I am not one of those young ladies (if any such exist) who are bold enough to risk their happiness on the possibility of being asked a second time. I am entirely serious in my refusal. You could not make _me_ happy, and I am convinced that I am the last woman in the world who could make _you_ so. Indeed, if your friend Lady Catherine knew me, I am persuaded she would consider me wholly unfit for the position.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWere it certain that Lady Catherine would think so,\u201d said Mr. Collins, very gravely\u2014\u201cbut I cannot suppose that her Ladyship would disapprove of you in the least. And you may be sure that, when I have the honour of seeing her again, I shall speak in the strongest terms of your modesty, economy, and other agreeable qualities.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, Mr. Collins, all commendation of me will be needless. You must allow me to judge for myself, and do me the compliment of believing what I say. I wish you every happiness and great wealth, and by refusing your hand, do everything in my power to keep you from being otherwise. In making me this offer, you must have satisfied the delicacy of your feelings toward my family, and may take possession of the Longbourn estate whenever it falls to you without any self-reproach. This matter may therefore be regarded as finally settled.\u201d As she spoke, she rose and would have left the room, had not Mr. Collins addressed her in this manner\u2014\\n\\n\u201cWhen I have the honour of speaking to you again on this subject, I shall hope to receive a more favourable answer than the one you have now given me; though I am far from accusing you of cruelty at present, since I know it to be the established custom of your sex to reject a man upon his first application, and perhaps you have even now said as much as would encourage my suit while remaining consistent with the true delicacy of the female character.\u201d\"}"
