{"request_id":"a25b2bff0987dbf516e104c4","rewritten_text":"I cannot describe the rapturous delight with which I turned from political quarrels at home and the physical hardships of distant countries to my own dear home, that chosen refuge of goodness and love; to peace, and the exchange of every sacred sympathy. Had I never left Windsor, these emotions would not have been so intense; but in Greece I had been prey to fear and terrible upheaval. There, after a period of anxiety and sorrow, I had watched two depart whose very names symbolized greatness and virtue. Yet such miseries could never enter the domestic circle that remained to me. Secluded in our beloved forest, we lived in tranquillity. The passing years did bring some small changes, and time, as it always does, marked our pleasures and hopes with signs of mortality. Idris, the most affectionate wife, sister, and friend, was a tender and loving mother. For her, as for many, motherhood was not a pastime; it was a passion. We had had three children; one, the second in age, died while I was in Greece. This had mingled grief and fear with the triumphant, rapturous feelings of motherhood. Before that event, the little beings born from her, the young heirs of her brief life, had seemed assured of existence; now she feared that the pitiless destroyer might seize her remaining darlings, as it had seized their brother. The slightest illness brought waves of terror; she was miserable whenever she was separated from them. She had gathered her happiness into their fragile lives and remained constantly watchful, lest the stealthy thief should once again steal away these precious gems. Fortunately, she had little cause for fear. Alfred, now nine years old, was an upright, manly little fellow, with a bright brow, gentle eyes, and a mild yet independent nature. Our youngest was still an infant; but his downy cheek glowed with the roses of health, and his tireless liveliness filled our halls with innocent laughter.

Clara had passed the age which, through its silent ignorance, had been the source of Idris’s fears. Clara was dear to her, and to everyone. So much intelligence was joined with innocence, sensibility with self-control, and seriousness with perfect good humour; such extraordinary beauty was united to such winning simplicity, that she hung like a pearl in the shrine of our possessions, a treasure of wonder and excellence.

At the beginning of winter, our Alfred, now nine years old, went to school at Eton for the first time. To him, this seemed the first step toward manhood, and he was accordingly delighted. Sharing study and recreation with others brought out the finest parts of his character: his steady perseverance, generosity, and well-controlled firmness."}

