          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"019eaa7e587a4a3a64a8685d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Then I repeated to myself that beautiful passage describing Dante\u2019s entrance into the earthly Paradise, and thought how sweet it would be, as I wandered along those lovely banks, to see the chariot of light descend with my long-lost parent, restored at last to me. As I waited in expectation of that moment, I imagined that, from the beautiful flowers growing there, I would weave a wreath and crown myself for joy. I would sing _sul margine d\u2019un rio_, my father\u2019s favourite song, and my voice, drifting through the still air, would tell him, wherever he sat awaiting our reunion, that his daughter had come. Then the mark of misery would have vanished from my brow, and I would lift my eyes without fear to meet his, which always shone with the gentle radiance of innocent love. When I thought of the magical expression in those deep eyes, I wept, but softly, lest my sobs disturb the enchanted scene.\\n\\nI was so completely absorbed in this reverie that I continued walking without noticing where my feet carried me. I actually bent down to pick a flower for my wreath on that barren plain, where no flower grew, before awakening from my daydream and discovering that I had no idea where I was.\\n\\nThe sun had set, and the rosy colour it had cast upon the clouds during its descent had almost faded. A wind swept across the plain. I looked around and saw nothing that could tell me where I was; I had lost my way and tried in vain to find the path. I wandered on, while the gathering darkness blurred every trace that might have guided me. At last everything was hidden beneath the profound darkness of night. I grew tired, and, knowing that my servant was to sleep that night in the neighbouring village, so that my absence would alarm no one, and that in this wild place I was safe from every intruder, I resolved to spend the night where I was. Indeed, I was too weary to walk farther. The air was cold, but I paid no attention to the discomfort, thinking myself well accustomed to the weather after two years of solitude, during which no change of season had interrupted my continual wanderings.\\n\\nI lay on the grass, surrounded by darkness that no faint ray of light penetrated. There was no sound, for the deep night had lulled the insects to sleep\u2014the only creatures living in that lonely place, where no tree or shrub could shelter anything else. A wondrous silence filled the air, calming my senses while stirring my soul. My mind rushed from one image to another and seemed to grasp eternity.\"}"
