          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5e5e1fc6f2f24f3495d5a9e5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Good heavens, she was a masterful talker. It would not have been proper for me to suggest that it was very late, that I was tired, and that I was desperately sleepy. I wished I had gone to bed when I had the chance. Now I had to endure it; there was no other choice. So she went on and on, her voice tinkling through the otherwise profound, ghostly silence of the sleeping castle, until at last there came, as though from far beneath us, a distant sound like a muffled shriek\u2014with such agony in it that my flesh crawled. The queen stopped, and pleasure lit her eyes; she tilted her graceful head as a bird does when listening. Again the sound forced its way upward through the stillness.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cIt is a truly stubborn soul, and has endured long. It has been many hours now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEndured what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe rack. Come\u2014you shall see a cheerful sight. If he does not yield his secret now, you shall see him torn apart.\u201d\\n\\nWhat a smooth, silky hellion she was; and how calm and serene she remained, while the cords running down my legs ached in sympathy with that man\u2019s suffering. Escorted by armored guards carrying blazing torches, we marched along echoing corridors and down stone stairways, damp and dripping, smelling of mold and centuries of imprisoned darkness\u2014a cold, uncanny journey, and a long one, made neither shorter nor more pleasant by the sorceress\u2019s conversation, which concerned the prisoner and his crime. He had been accused by an anonymous informer of killing a stag in the royal preserves. I said:\\n\\n\u201cAnonymous testimony is not really sufficient, Your Highness. It would be fairer to confront the accused with his accuser.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI had not considered that, since it is of little importance. But even if I wished to, I could not, because the accuser came masked by night, told the forester, and immediately departed again, so the forester does not know him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen this Unknown is the only person who saw the stag killed?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, no man saw the killing, but this Unknown saw the bold wretch near the place where the stag lay, and came with loyal zeal to betray him to the forester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo the Unknown was near the dead stag as well? Is it not possible that he killed it himself? His loyal zeal\u2014in a mask\u2014seems just a little suspicious. But what does Your Highness hope to gain by racking the prisoner? Where is the benefit?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe will not confess otherwise, and then his soul would be lost. By law, his life is forfeited for his crime\u2014and I will certainly see that he pays for it!\u2014but it would endanger my own soul to let him die without confession or absolution.\u201d\"}"
