          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"46bf51a01c7e04e78b51f4a8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To look at a star in brief, sideways glances\u2014to turn the outer parts of the retina toward it, which are more sensitive to faint light than the inner parts\u2014is to see the star clearly and appreciate its brightness best. That brightness fades as soon as we look directly at it. More rays reach the eye in the second case, but the first gives us a finer ability to understand what we see. Too much depth can confuse and weaken thought; even Venus can disappear from the sky if we examine it too steadily, intensely, or directly.\\n\\n\u201cAs for these murders, let us investigate them ourselves before forming an opinion. An inquiry will amuse us,\u201d [I thought this an odd word to use, but said nothing] \u201cand, besides, Le Bon once did me a service for which I am grateful. We will see the place with our own eyes. I know G\u2014\u2014, the Prefect of Police, and can easily obtain the necessary permission.\u201d\\n\\nThe permission was obtained, and we went at once to the Rue Morgue. It is one of those miserable streets between the Rue Richelieu and the Rue St. Roch. We reached it late in the afternoon, since this area was far from where we lived. The house was easy to find; many people were still staring at its closed shutters with pointless curiosity from across the street. It was an ordinary Parisian house, with a gateway and, beside it, a glass watch-box with a sliding window panel, marking the concierge\u2019s lodge. Before entering, we walked up the street, turned into an alley, and then turned again to pass behind the building. All the while, Dupin examined the entire neighborhood and the house with such careful attention that I could see no purpose in it.\\n\\nWe retraced our steps, returned to the front of the house, rang, and showed our credentials. The officers in charge admitted us. We went upstairs to the room where Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s body had been found, and where both bodies still lay. As usual, the room had been left in disorder. I saw nothing beyond what had been reported in the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux.\u201d Dupin examined everything, including the victims\u2019 bodies. We then went through the other rooms and into the yard, with a gendarme accompanying us the entire time.\"}"
