          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bc1a51519827897d42e0d5d6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Deprived of ordinary resources, the analyst enters the mind of his opponent, identifies with him, and often sees at once the only ways\u2014sometimes absurdly simple ones\u2014in which he can lure him into error or rush him into miscalculation.\\n\\nWhist has long been recognized for its effect on what is called the calculating faculty; and men of the highest intellect have been known to take an apparently inexplicable delight in it while dismissing chess as frivolous. Certainly, nothing of a similar kind places so great a demand upon the power of analysis. The finest chess player in Christendom may be little more than the finest player of chess; but skill at whist implies an ability to succeed in all those more important undertakings in which one mind contends with another. By skill, I mean perfection in the game that includes an understanding of every source from which legitimate advantage may be gained. These sources are not only numerous but varied, and often lie in regions of thought entirely inaccessible to ordinary understanding. To observe attentively is to remember clearly; and in this respect the focused chess player will do very well at whist, while Hoyle\u2019s rules\u2014which are themselves based on the game\u2019s simple mechanics\u2014are generally easy enough to understand. Thus a retentive memory and playing \u201cby the book\u201d are commonly considered the whole of good play. But the analyst\u2019s skill appears in matters beyond the limits of mere rules. He silently makes countless observations and deductions. His companions may do the same; the difference in the amount of information gained lies less in the soundness of the deduction than in the quality of the observation. The essential knowledge is knowing what to observe. Our player limits himself in no way; and because the game is his object, he does not dismiss deductions drawn from things outside it. He studies his partner\u2019s face, comparing it carefully with the faces of each opponent. He considers how the cards are arranged in every hand, often counting trump by trump and honor by honor through the glances their holders cast at one another. He notices every change of expression as play proceeds, drawing a wealth of information from differences in the signs of certainty, surprise, triumph, or disappointment. From the way a trick is gathered, he judges whether the player who takes it can win another in that suit. He recognizes a feint by the manner in which the card is laid upon the table. A casual or careless word; the accidental dropping or turning of a card, together with the anxiety or indifference shown about hiding it; the counting of tricks and the order in which they are arranged; embarrassment, hesitation, eagerness, or fear\u2014all provide his seemingly intuitive perception with clues to the true state of affairs.\"}"
