          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4c78ac55f8a1b5c5e32e515f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat conclusion, then, have you reached? What impression has this made on your imagination?\u201d\\n\\nA chill ran over me as Dupin asked the question. \u201cA madman,\u201d I said, \u201ccommitted the deed\u2014some raving lunatic who escaped from a nearby _Maison de Sant\u00e9._\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn some ways,\u201d he replied, \u201cyour idea is not unreasonable. But the voices of madmen, even during their wildest fits, never sound like that strange voice heard on the stairs. Madmen belong to some nation, and however confused their words may be, their syllables still follow the patterns of a language. Besides, a madman\u2019s hair would not be like what I now hold in my hand. I pulled this small tuft from Madame L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s tightly clenched fingers. Tell me what you make of it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDupin!\u201d I cried, completely shaken. \u201cThis hair is extremely strange\u2014this is not _human_ hair.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not claimed that it is,\u201d he said. \u201cBut before we settle that question, look at the small drawing I have made on this paper. It is a _fac-simile_ of what one part of the testimony described as \u2018dark bruises, and deep indentations of finger nails\u2019 on Mademoiselle L\u2019Espanaye\u2019s throat, and what another account, by Messrs. Dumas and Etienne, called a \u2018series of livid spots, evidently the impression of fingers.\u2019\\n\\n\u201cYou will see,\u201d my friend continued, spreading the paper on the table before us, \u201cthat the drawing suggests a firm, unchanging grip. There is no sign of _slipping._ Each finger kept its position\u2014perhaps until the victim died\u2014and left the terrible marks where it first pressed into the flesh. Now try placing all your fingers at once into the corresponding impressions.\u201d\\n\\nI tried, but could not do it.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps we are not testing this fairly,\u201d he said. \u201cThe paper lies on a flat surface, while a human throat is cylindrical. Here is a piece of wood whose circumference is about the same as the throat. Wrap the drawing around it and try again.\u201d\\n\\nI did so, but the difficulty was even clearer. \u201cThis,\u201d I said, \u201cis the mark of no human hand.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow read this passage from Cuvier,\u201d Dupin replied.\\n\\nIt was a detailed anatomical and general description of the large, tawny Ourang-Outang of the East Indian Islands. Everyone knows enough of these animals\u2019 enormous size, tremendous strength and agility, savage nature, and tendency to imitate. At once, I understood the full horror of the murders.\\n\\n\u201cAs I finish reading,\u201d I said, \u201cthe description of the fingers exactly matches this drawing.\u201d\"}"
