          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e2eb89b06c366a19ffce1713\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI now examined the death\u2019s-head closely. Its outer edges\u2014the edges of the drawing nearest the vellum\u2019s border\u2014were much clearer than the rest. It was evident that the caloric had acted imperfectly or unevenly. I at once lit a fire and exposed every part of the parchment to a glowing heat. At first, the sole result was that the skull\u2019s faint lines grew stronger; but, after I continued the experiment, there appeared at the corner of the slip, diagonally opposite the place where the death\u2019s-head had been drawn, the figure of what I initially took to be a goat. A more careful inspection, however, convinced me that it was meant for a kid.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHa! ha!\u201d said I, \u201ccertainly I have no right to laugh at you\u2014a million and a half in money is far too serious a matter for jesting\u2014but you are not going to form a third link in your chain\u2014you will not discover any particular connection between your pirates and a goat\u2014pirates, you know, have nothing to do with goats; they belong to the farming interest.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut I have just said that the figure was not a goat.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, then, a kid\u2014much the same thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMuch the same, but not entirely,\u201d said Legrand. \u201cYou may have heard of a certain Captain Kidd. I immediately regarded the animal\u2019s figure as a sort of punning or hieroglyphic signature. I call it a signature because its position on the vellum suggested that interpretation. The death\u2019s-head at the diagonally opposite corner likewise seemed to have the character of a stamp or seal. Yet I was greatly perplexed by the absence of everything else\u2014of the body belonging to my imagined instrument, and of the text required by my context.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI suppose you expected to find a letter between the stamp and the signature.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSomething of that sort. In truth, I felt irresistibly possessed by a foreboding of some immense good fortune about to arrive. I can hardly explain why. Perhaps it was, after all, more a wish than a genuine belief; but do you know that Jupiter\u2019s foolish remark about the bug being made of solid gold had a remarkable influence on my imagination? And then there was the succession of accidents and coincidences\u2014they were extraordinarily striking. Do you see how accidental it was that these events should have taken place on the one day in the entire year when it has been, or might be, cool enough for a fire, and that without the fire, or without the dog\u2019s intervention at the exact moment of his appearance, I should never have noticed the death\u2019s-head, and thus never become the possessor of the treasure?\u201d\"}"
