{"request_id":"3079a1cc21e9476bebfa8899","rewritten_text":"The young people were all eager to see Lyme. Captain Wentworth spoke of going there again himself. It was only seventeen miles from Uppercross, and although it was November, the weather was not at all bad. In short, Louisa, the most eager of them all, decided they must go. Besides enjoying the chance to do as she pleased, she now felt she deserved credit for standing by her own choice. She overcame all her parents’ wishes to postpone the visit until summer, and so Charles, Mary, Anne, Henrietta, Louisa, and Captain Wentworth were to go to Lyme.

Their first careless plan was to go in the morning and return at night. Mr. Musgrove would not agree, however, because of his horses. Once they considered the matter sensibly, they saw that a day in the middle of November would leave little time to explore a new place, since the journey there and back required seven hours. They would therefore spend the night and not be expected home until dinner the next day. Everyone thought this a great improvement. Although they met at the Great House for a fairly early breakfast and set off exactly on time, it was well past noon before the two carriages—the four ladies in Mr. Musgrove’s coach and Captain Wentworth with Charles in his curricle—were going down the long hill into Lyme and entering the town’s even steeper street. It was clear they would have barely enough time to look around before the day’s light and warmth disappeared.

After arranging rooms and ordering dinner at one of the inns, the next thing to do was plainly to walk straight down to the sea. They had come too late in the year to enjoy the entertainments and variety Lyme offered as a seaside resort. The rooms were closed, nearly all the visitors had left, and hardly any families remained except the residents. Since the buildings themselves had little to admire, a visitor’s attention would turn to the town’s remarkable setting: the main street almost rushing into the water; the walk to the Cobb, curving around the pleasant little bay, which in season was lively with bathing machines and visitors; the Cobb itself, with its old features and new improvements; and the beautiful line of cliffs stretching east of the town. A very unusual visitor indeed would fail to find enough charm in Lyme’s immediate surroundings to wish to know the place better."}

