          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"13f0cd530e6e5cbd1bdd37b6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Wickham was so entirely content with this conversation that he never again troubled himself, or annoyed his dear sister Elizabeth, by bringing up the subject; and she was glad to discover that she had said enough to keep him silent.\\n\\nThe day of his and Lydia\u2019s departure soon arrived; and Mrs. Bennet was obliged to endure a separation which, since her husband had no intention of joining her plan for them all to go to Newcastle, was likely to last at least a year.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my dear Lydia,\u201d she exclaimed, \u201cwhen shall we see you again?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Lord! I cannot say. Perhaps not for two or three years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWrite to me very frequently, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAs often as I can. But you know married women have little time for writing. My sisters can write to me. They will have nothing else to occupy them.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Wickham\u2019s farewells were far more affectionate than his wife\u2019s. He smiled, appeared handsome, and uttered many agreeable things.\\n\\n\u201cHe is as fine a fellow,\u201d said Mr. Bennet, as soon as they had left the house, \u201cas ever I saw. He simpers, smirks, and makes love to us all. I am excessively proud of him. I defy even Sir William Lucas to produce a more valuable son-in-law.\u201d\\n\\nThe loss of her daughter made Mrs. Bennet very melancholy for several days.\\n\\n\u201cI often think,\u201d she said, \u201cthat there is nothing so dreadful as parting from one\u2019s friends. One feels so desolate without them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is the result, you see, madam, of marrying a daughter,\u201d said Elizabeth. \u201cIt should make you more content that your other four remain single.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is no such thing. Lydia does not leave me because she is married, but merely because her husband\u2019s regiment happens to be so distant. Had it been nearer, she would not have gone so soon.\u201d\\n\\nYet the dispirited state into which this event had cast her was soon eased, and her mind once more opened to the excitement of hope through a piece of news then beginning to circulate. The housekeeper at Netherfield had received orders to prepare for her master\u2019s arrival, as he was coming down in a day or two to shoot there for several weeks. Mrs. Bennet was all agitation. By turns, she looked at Jane, smiled, and shook her head.\\n\\n\u201cWell, well, and so Mr. Bingley is coming down, sister,\u201d (for Mrs. Philips had first brought her the news). \u201cWell, so much the better. Not that I care about it, though. He is nothing to us, you know, and I am sure I never wish to see him again. But still, he is very welcome to come to Netherfield, if he chooses.\u201d\"}"
