          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"913f7f4b2799140f623842bb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was invitation enough.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, my dear, you must know that Mrs. Long says Netherfield has been taken by a young man of considerable fortune from the north of England; that he arrived on Monday in a chaise and four to inspect the place, and was so pleased with it that he immediately agreed with Mr. Morris; that he is to move in before Michaelmas, and that some of his servants are to be in the house by the end of next week.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat is his name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBingley.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs he married or single?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, single, my dear, certainly! A single man of great fortune; four or five thousand a year. What a splendid thing for our girls!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow so? How can it concern them?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d his wife replied, \u201chow can you be so tiresome? Surely you know that I am thinking of his marrying one of them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that his purpose in settling here?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPurpose? Nonsense, how can you say such a thing! But it is very possible that he may fall in love with one of them, and so you must visit him as soon as he arrives.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI see no reason for that. You and the girls may go\u2014or you may send them alone, which might be even better; for since you are as handsome as any of them, Mr. Bingley may prefer you to the whole party.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear, you flatter me. I certainly have had my share of beauty, but I do not claim to be anything remarkable now. When a woman has five grown daughters, she ought to stop dwelling on her own beauty.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIn such circumstances, a woman does not often have much beauty to dwell upon.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut, my dear, you really must go and see Mr. Bingley when he comes into the neighbourhood.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is more than I can promise, I assure you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut think of your daughters. Just imagine what a fine establishment it would be for one of them. Sir William and Lady Lucas are resolved to go, solely for that reason; for ordinarily, you know, they visit no newcomers. Indeed, you must go, because it will be impossible for us to visit him if you do not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are surely too scrupulous. I dare say Mr. Bingley will be delighted to see you; and I will send a few lines with you to assure him of my hearty consent to his marrying whichever of the girls he chooses\u2014though I must say a good word for my little Lizzy.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI insist that you do no such thing. Lizzy is not in the least better than the others: and I am sure she is not half as handsome as Jane, nor half as good-humoured as Lydia.\u201d\"}"
