          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0f1d5e315267a18e888c0d5d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Otis was greatly troubled by Lord Canterville\u2019s refusal and urged him to reconsider, but the good-natured peer remained resolute. At last, he persuaded the Minister to let his daughter keep the gift the ghost had given her. Thus, when the young Duchess of Cheshire was presented at the Queen\u2019s first drawing-room in the spring of 1890, on the occasion of her marriage, everyone admired her jewels. Virginia had received the coronet, the reward of every well-behaved American girl, and married her boy-lover as soon as he came of age. They were both so charming, and so deeply in love, that everyone rejoiced in the match\u2014except the old Marchioness of Dumbleton, who had hoped to secure the Duke for one of her seven unmarried daughters and had given no fewer than three costly dinner-parties to that end, and, oddly enough, Mr. Otis himself. Mr. Otis was very fond of the young Duke personally, but in principle he disliked titles and, as he put it, \u2018was not without apprehension lest, amid the enervating influences of a pleasure-loving aristocracy, the true principles of republican simplicity should be forgotten.\u2019 His objections were entirely overruled, however, and I believe that when he walked up the aisle of St. George\u2019s, Hanover Square, with his daughter on his arm, there was no prouder man anywhere in England.\\n\\nAfter their honeymoon, the Duke and Duchess went to Canterville Chase, and the afternoon after their arrival they walked to the lonely churchyard beside the pine-woods. At first there had been much dispute over the inscription on Sir Simon\u2019s tombstone, but in the end it was decided to engrave only the old gentleman\u2019s initials and the verse from the library window. The Duchess had brought some beautiful roses, which she scattered over the grave, and after standing there for a while they wandered into the ruined chancel of the old abbey. The Duchess sat on a fallen pillar, while her husband reclined at her feet, smoking a cigarette and gazing up at her lovely eyes. Suddenly he tossed away his cigarette, took her hand, and said, \u2018Virginia, a wife should keep no secrets from her husband.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Dear Cecil! I have no secrets from you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Yes, you have,\u2019 he replied with a smile. \u2018You have never told me what happened when you were shut up with the ghost.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I have never told anyone, Cecil,\u2019 Virginia said gravely.\"}"
