          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"328643b59fa05264427312ab\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Other chances to make her observations could not fail to arise. Anne had soon been in the company of all four together often enough to form an opinion, though she was too wise to admit as much at home, where she knew it would satisfy neither husband nor wife. For while she considered Louisa to be rather the favourite, she could not help thinking, as far as she dared judge from memory and experience, that Captain Wentworth was in love with neither of them. They were more in love with him; yet even in their case it was not love. It was a slight fever of admiration, but it might, and probably must, end in love with one of them. Charles Hayter seemed conscious of being slighted, while Henrietta sometimes appeared divided between the two. Anne longed for the power to explain to them all what they were doing, and to point out some of the dangers to which they were exposing themselves. She attributed guile to none of them. It was deeply satisfying to believe that Captain Wentworth was not in the least aware of the pain he was causing. There was no triumph\u2014no pitiful triumph\u2014in his manner. He had probably never heard, and never considered, any claims Charles Hayter might have had. He was only wrong in accepting the attentions\u2014for accepting was the proper word\u2014of two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a brief struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to leave the field. Three days had passed without his coming once to Uppercross: a most decisive change. He had even refused a regular invitation to dinner; and when Mr Musgrove found him on that occasion with some large books before him, Mr and Mrs Musgrove were certain that all could not be right, and spoke gravely of his studying himself to death. Mary hoped and believed that Henrietta had given him a definite dismissal, while her husband lived in constant expectation of seeing him the next day. Anne could only think that Charles Hayter was wise.\\n\\nOne morning around this time, while Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth had gone shooting together, the sisters in the Cottage were sitting quietly at work when the sisters from the Mansion-house appeared at the window.\\n\\nIt was a very fine November day, and the Miss Musgroves crossed the small grounds and stopped for no purpose except to say that they were going to take a _long_ walk, and therefore assumed that Mary would not wish to go with them. When Mary immediately replied, somewhat jealous at not being considered a good walker, \u201cOh, yes, I should very much like to join you; I am very fond of a long walk,\u201d Anne felt certain, from the looks of the two girls, that this was precisely what they did not want. She again admired the kind of necessity that family habits seemed to create: everything had to be communicated, and everything had to be done together, however unwanted or inconvenient.\"}"
