          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2a272adab01fc675c14e7056\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, burglar\u2019s tools. That seems to reassure you quite a lot. But what made you react that way? What did you think we had found?\u201d\\n\\nHuck was trapped\u2014the man was watching him closely. He would have given anything for a believable answer, but nothing came to him. The questioning eyes seemed to bore deeper and deeper. A foolish reply occurred to him, and there was no time to consider it, so he blurted it out weakly:\\n\\n\u201cSunday-school books, maybe.\u201d\\n\\nPoor Huck was too upset to smile, but the old man laughed loudly and happily, shaking all over from head to foot. At last he said that a laugh like that was worth money because it reduced a man\u2019s doctor bills enormously. Then he added:\\n\\n\u201cPoor fellow, you\u2019re pale and worn out\u2014you\u2019re not well at all. No wonder you\u2019re nervous and confused. But you\u2019ll recover. Rest and sleep should set you right, I hope.\u201d\\n\\nHuck was annoyed with himself for acting like such a fool and showing such suspicious excitement. He had abandoned the idea that the parcel brought from the tavern was the treasure as soon as he heard the conversation at the widow\u2019s stile. He had only decided that it probably was not the treasure; he had not known for certain. So the suggestion that a bundle had been captured was too much for him to handle calmly. Still, he was glad the incident had happened, because now he knew beyond any doubt that this bundle was not the bundle. His mind was peaceful and completely at ease. In fact, everything now seemed to be going exactly as it should. The treasure had to remain in No. 2, the men would be caught and jailed that day, and he and Tom could take the gold that night without trouble or fear of interruption.\\n\\nJust as breakfast ended, someone knocked at the door. Huck rushed to find a hiding place, since he did not want even the slightest connection with what had happened during the night. The Welshman admitted several ladies and gentlemen, including the Widow Douglas, and noticed groups of townspeople climbing the hill to look at the stile. So the news had spread. The Welshman had to tell the visitors the story of the night. The widow openly expressed her gratitude for being saved.\\n\\n\u201cPlease don\u2019t mention it, ma\u2019am. There is someone to whom you may owe more than you owe me and my boys, but he won\u2019t let me reveal his name. We would not have been there without him.\u201d\"}"
