{"request_id":"0c515064fce665f285310f66","rewritten_text":"The only question that seemed to trouble him was whom to do away with; for he was not blind to the fact that murder, like the religions of the pagan world, requires a victim as well as a priest. Not being a genius, he had no enemies, and indeed he felt that this was not the time to indulge any personal resentment or dislike, since the mission he had undertaken was one of great and solemn importance. He therefore wrote a list of his friends and relatives on a sheet of notepaper and, after careful thought, chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old lady who lived in Curzon Street and was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been very fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her, and since he was wealthy himself, having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property when he came of age, there was no possibility of gaining any vulgar financial advantage from her death. Indeed, the more he considered the matter, the more she seemed exactly the right person, and, feeling that any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he resolved to make his arrangements at once.

The first thing to do was, of course, to settle with the cheiromantist. He sat down at a small Sheraton writing table near the window, drew a cheque for £105 payable to the order of Mr. Septimus Podgers, enclosed it in an envelope, and told his valet to take it to West Moon Street. He then telephoned the stables for his hansom and dressed to go out. As he left the room, he looked back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never let her know what he was doing for her sake, but would keep the secret of his self-sacrifice hidden forever in his heart.

On his way to the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist’s and sent Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus, with lovely white petals and staring pheasants’ eyes. On arriving at the club, he went straight to the library, rang the bell, and ordered the waiter to bring him a lemon-and-soda and a book on toxicology. He had decided that poison was the best method for this troublesome business. Anything resembling personal violence was extremely distasteful to him; besides, he was anxious not to murder Lady Clementina in any way that might attract public attention, since he hated the thought of being lionised at Lady Windermere’s or seeing his name appear in the columns of vulgar society newspapers."}

