{"request_id":"9afada6954588d2c5078451d","rewritten_text":"Summer went on, and beneath the sun’s fierce rays, the plague sent its deadly arrows across the earth. The nations under its power bowed their heads and died. The corn that had grown in abundance lay rotting in the fields by autumn, while the miserable man who had gone out to gather food for his children lay stiff and plague-stricken in the furrow. The green woods waved their branches proudly, while the dying lay beneath their shade, answering the solemn rustling with discordant cries. Bright birds flitted through the trees; the careless deer rested unharmed on the ferns. Oxen and horses wandered from their unguarded stables and grazed among the wheat, for death struck human beings alone.

As summer and death continued, our fears grew. My poor love and I looked at each other, then at our children. “We will save them, Idris,” I said. “I will save them. Years from now, we will tell them of the fears that have passed away with the danger. Even if they are the only people left on earth, they will live; their cheeks will not grow pale, and their sweet voices will not weaken.” Our eldest understood something of what was happening, and sometimes, with a serious expression, asked me why such terrible destruction had come. But he was only ten, and the joy of youth soon drove needless worry from his face. Evelyn, a laughing cherub and playful child, knew nothing of pain or sorrow. Shaking his light curls from his eyes, he made the halls ring with his laughter and, in countless innocent ways, drew our attention to his games. Clara, our lovely, gentle Clara, was our support, comfort, and joy. She made it her duty to care for the sick, comfort the grieving, help the old, and join in the games and revive the spirits of the young. She moved through the rooms like a good spirit sent from heaven to brighten our dark hour with a light not of this world. Gratitude and praise followed wherever she went. Yet when she stood simply before us, playing with our children or eagerly doing small kindnesses for Idris, one wondered in what beautiful feature of her pure face, or in what soft note of her moving voice, so much courage, wisdom, and active goodness could live.

Summer passed slowly, because we trusted that winter would at least slow the disease. To hope that it would disappear entirely was too precious and too deeply felt to be spoken. Whenever someone carelessly expressed such a thought, the listeners answered with sudden tears and passionate sobs, showing how deep their fears were and how little hope remained."}

