          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5ab2843fdd1f12923d48c99f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I call upon you, spirits of the dead, and upon you, wandering ministers of vengeance, to aid me and guide me in my work. Let the cursed, hellish monster drink deeply of agony; let him feel the despair that now torments me.\u201d\\n\\nI had begun my invocation solemnly, with an awe that almost convinced me the shades of my murdered friends heard and approved my devotion. But as I finished, the furies took hold of me, and rage choked my voice.\\n\\nThrough the stillness of night, I was answered by a loud, fiendish laugh. It rang long and heavily in my ears; the mountains echoed it, and I felt as though all hell surrounded me with mockery and laughter. Surely, in that moment, frenzy would have seized me and I would have ended my miserable existence, had not my vow been heard and my life been reserved for vengeance. The laughter faded, and then a familiar, hated voice, apparently close beside my ear, addressed me in an audible whisper: \u201cI am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have resolved to live, and I am satisfied.\u201d\\n\\nI rushed toward the place from which the sound had come, but the devil escaped my grasp. Suddenly, the moon\u2019s broad disk rose and shone directly upon his ghastly, distorted form as he fled with more than human speed.\\n\\nI pursued him, and for many months this has been my task. Following a faint clue, I traced the winding course of the Rh\u00f4ne, but in vain. The blue Mediterranean came into view, and by a strange chance I saw the fiend enter it by night and conceal himself aboard a vessel bound for the Black Sea. I took passage on the same ship, but somehow he escaped me.\\n\\nAcross the wilds of Tartary and Russia, though he continued to elude me, I have always followed his trail. Sometimes peasants, terrified by the dreadful apparition, told me where he had gone; sometimes he himself, fearing that if I lost every trace of him I would despair and die, left some sign to guide me. Snow fell upon my head, and I saw the imprint of his enormous foot across the white plain. You, who are only beginning life, to whom care is unfamiliar and agony unknown, how can you understand what I have suffered and still suffer? Cold, hunger, and fatigue were the least of the pains I was destined to endure. I was cursed by some devil and carried my eternal hell with me; yet a benevolent spirit continued to follow and direct my steps, and whenever I complained most bitterly, it would suddenly free me from difficulties that seemed impossible to overcome. Sometimes, when hunger overcame nature and exhaustion brought me down, a meal had been prepared for me in the desert, restoring and strengthening me.\"}"
