{"request_id":"f63f2f20983d10810c9d89fe","rewritten_text":"“It was only after some time had passed that I recovered sufficiently to attend to the balloon’s ordinary concerns. I then inspected it carefully and, to my immense relief, found it undamaged. All my instruments were secure, and, fortunately, I had lost neither ballast nor provisions. Indeed, I had fastened them so firmly in their places that such a mishap was wholly impossible. On consulting my watch, I saw that it was six o’clock. I was still rising swiftly, and my barometer indicated a current altitude of three and three-quarter miles. Directly below me on the ocean was a small black object, somewhat oblong, apparently about the size of, and closely resembling in every respect, one of those children’s toys known as a domino. Applying my telescope to it, I clearly made out a British ninety-four-gun ship, close-hauled and pitching heavily upon the sea, her head pointed W.S.W. Apart from this vessel, I saw nothing except the ocean, the sky, and the sun, which had risen long before.

“It is now more than time that I should explain to your Excellencies the purpose of my perilous journey. Your Excellencies will remember that the distressing circumstances in Rotterdam had finally driven me to contemplate suicide. It was not, however, that I felt any actual hatred of life itself, but that I was worn beyond endurance by the incidental miseries connected with my condition. In this frame of mind, desiring to live yet exhausted by living, the treatise at the bookseller’s stall suggested a possibility to my imagination. I then reached my final decision. I resolved to leave, yet remain alive—to quit the world, yet continue to exist—in short, to abandon enigmas, I determined, whatever might follow, to force a passage, if possible, to the moon. Now, lest I be thought more insane than I truly am, I shall set forth, as clearly as I can, the reasons that led me to believe such an undertaking, though certainly difficult and undeniably dangerous, was not entirely, for a daring spirit, outside the limits of possibility.

“The moon’s true distance from the earth was the first matter requiring consideration. The mean, or average, distance between the centres of the two planets is 59.9643 of the earth’s equatorial radii, or only about 237,000 miles. I say the mean or average distance, but it must be remembered that the moon’s orbit is an ellipse whose eccentricity amounts to no less than 0.05484 of the ellipse’s major semi-axis, while the earth’s centre lies at its focus. Thus, if I could somehow contrive to encounter the moon, so to speak, at its perigee, the distance mentioned above would be considerably reduced.”"}

