          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"871eac3c316656ef91737ad7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We proclaimed ourselves freemen so loudly and earnestly that the liberty-preaching orator and his patriotic crowd took an interested notice of us. They gathered around and adopted a very determined attitude. The orator said:\\n\\n\u201cIf you truly are freemen, you have nothing to fear\u2014the God-given liberties of Britain surround you as shield and shelter! (Applause.) You shall soon see. Bring out your proof.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat proof?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cProof that you are freemen.\u201d\\n\\nAh\u2014I remembered! I came to my senses and said nothing. But the king angrily burst out:\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019re insane, man. It would be more reasonable for this thief and scoundrel here to prove that we are not freemen.\u201d\\n\\nYou see, he understood his own laws the way people so often understand laws: by their words, not by their effects. They take on a meaning, and become very vivid, when you have to apply them to yourself.\\n\\nEveryone shook his head and looked disappointed; some turned away, no longer interested. The orator spoke again, this time in the practical tone of business rather than feeling:\\n\\n\u201cIf you do not know your country\u2019s laws, it is time you learned them. You are strangers here; you will not deny that. You may be freemen\u2014we do not deny it\u2014but you may also be slaves. The law is clear: it does not require the claimant to prove that you are slaves; it requires you to prove that you are not.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cDear sir, give us only time to send to Astolat; or give us only time to send to the Valley of Holiness\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPeace, good man. These are extraordinary requests, and you cannot expect them to be granted. It would take a great deal of time and unfairly inconvenience your master\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMaster, idiot!\u201d the king shouted. \u201cI have no master. I myself am the m\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSilence, for God\u2019s sake!\u201d\\n\\nI got the words out in time to stop the king. We were already in enough trouble; it could not help us to make these people think we were lunatics.\\n\\nThere is no point in giving all the details. The earl housed us and then put us up for auction. That same terrible law had existed in our own South in my own time, more than thirteen hundred years later. Under it, hundreds of freemen who could not prove they were freemen had been sold into lifelong slavery, without making any particular impression on me. But the moment the law and auction block became part of my own experience, something that had merely seemed wrong before suddenly became hellish. Well, that is how we are made.\\n\\nYes, we were sold at auction, like pigs. In a large town with a busy market, we would have brought a good price. But this place was completely stagnant, and we were sold for an amount that still makes me ashamed whenever I think of it.\"}"
