          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"69835daa1ba8a77147d3dcfe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I don\u2019t have the nerve for that sort of thing.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut could you really be comfortable spending the whole evening away from the poor boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; you see, his papa can, so why shouldn\u2019t I? Jemima is so careful, and she could send us word every hour about how he was. I really think Charles might as well have told his father that we would all come. I am no more alarmed about little Charles now than he is. I was dreadfully alarmed yesterday, but the situation is very different today.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, if you don\u2019t think it too late to give notice for yourself, suppose you went as well as your husband. Leave little Charles in my care. Mr. and Mrs. Musgrove cannot think it wrong while I remain with him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you serious?\u201d cried Mary, her eyes brightening. \u201cDear me! That is a very good idea, very good indeed. Of course, I may as well go as not, since I am no use at home\u2014isn\u2019t that so? And staying here only harasses me. You, who have no mother\u2019s feelings, are much the more suitable person. You can make little Charles do anything; he always obeys you at once. It will be much better than leaving him only with Jemima. Oh, I shall certainly go; I am sure I ought to, if I can, quite as much as Charles, because they are extremely eager for me to become acquainted with Captain Wentworth, and I know you do not mind being left alone. It was an excellent idea of yours, Anne, truly. I will go and tell Charles, and get ready at once. You can send for us, you know, at a moment\u2019s notice, if anything is wrong; but I dare say there will be nothing to alarm you. You may be sure I would not go if I did not feel completely at ease about my dear child.\u201d\\n\\nThe next moment she was knocking at her husband\u2019s dressing-room door, and as Anne followed her upstairs, she arrived in time to hear the entire conversation, which began with Mary saying, in a tone of great triumph\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI mean to go with you, Charles, because I am no more useful at home than you are. If I shut myself away forever with the child, I should not be able to persuade him to do anything he disliked. Anne will stay; Anne has offered to remain at home and take care of him. It was Anne\u2019s own suggestion, and so I shall go with you, which will be much better, since I have not dined at the other house since Tuesday.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThis is very kind of Anne,\u201d her husband replied, \u201cand I should be very glad to have you go; but it seems rather hard that she should be left at home alone to nurse our sick child.\u201d\"}"
        }
