          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e06c2f233d778303313dd9ea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was a delightful feeling of clearing things up and taking a quiet pause before beginning life anew in our village on Saturday nights, which encouraged Joe to risk staying out half an hour later on Saturdays than at other times. When the half-hour and the rum and water had run out together, Joe rose to leave and took me by the hand.\\n\\n\u201cWait half a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the strange man. \u201cI believe I have a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I have, the boy shall have it.\u201d\\n\\nHe picked it from a handful of small change, wrapped it in some crumpled paper, and handed it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he said. \u201cMind! Your own.\u201d\\n\\nI thanked him, staring at him much longer than good manners allowed, and clinging tightly to Joe. He wished Joe good-night, and Mr. Wopsle good-night (who left with us), while he gave me only a look from his aiming eye\u2014no, not a look, since he closed it, though wonders can be performed with an eye by concealing it.\\n\\nOn the way home, had I felt inclined to talk, the conversation would have been entirely mine, for Mr. Wopsle left us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe walked all the way home with his mouth wide open, rinsing the rum away with as much air as possible. But I was somehow stunned by the return of my old wrongdoing and old acquaintance, and could think of nothing else.\\n\\nMy sister was not in a particularly bad temper when we appeared in the kitchen, and Joe, encouraged by this uncommon circumstance, told her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll wager,\u201d said Mrs. Joe triumphantly, \u201cor he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nI took it from the paper, and it turned out to be genuine. \u201cBut what\u2019s this?\u201d said Mrs. Joe, dropping the shilling and snatching up the paper. \u201cTwo one-pound notes?\u201d\\n\\nThey were nothing less than two thick, overheated one-pound notes that seemed to have enjoyed the closest acquaintance with every cattle-market in the county. Joe seized his hat again and hurried with them to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was away, I sat on my usual stool and stared blankly at my sister, fairly certain that the man would not be there.\\n\\nSoon Joe returned, saying the man had gone, but that he, Joe, had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
