          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"00110d422957ac1bda2a0656\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But in that bitter _tirade_ against Chantilly, which appeared in yesterday\u2019s \u2018_Mus\u00e9e_,\u2019 the satirist, making some shameful allusions to the cobbler\u2019s change of name when he assumed the buskin, quoted a Latin line about which we had often spoken. I mean the line\\n\\nPerdidit antiquum litera prima sonum.\\n\\n\u201cI had told you that this referred to Orion, once written Urion; and, from certain pungent associations attached to the explanation, I knew you could not have forgotten it. It was evident, then, that you would inevitably unite the two notions of Orion and Chantilly. That you had united them I perceived from the nature of the smile which crossed your lips. You were thinking of the poor cobbler\u2019s sacrifice. Until then, you had been stooping as you walked; but now I saw you straighten yourself to your full height. I was consequently certain that your thoughts had turned to Chantilly\u2019s diminutive stature. At that moment I broke into your reflections by observing that, since he was really a very small fellow\u2014that Chantilly\u2014he would be better suited to the _Th\u00e9\u00e2tre des Vari\u00e9t\u00e9s_.\u201d\\n\\nSoon afterward, we were examining an evening edition of the \u201cGazette des Tribunaux,\u201d when the following paragraphs caught our notice.\\n\\n\u201c_Extraordinary Murders_.\u2014This morning, at about three o\u2019clock, the residents of the Quartier St. Roch were awakened by a series of dreadful shrieks, apparently coming from the fourth floor of a house in the Rue Morgue, known to be occupied solely by Madame L\u2019Espanaye and her daughter, Mademoiselle Camille L\u2019Espanaye. After some delay, caused by an unsuccessful effort to gain entrance in the ordinary way, the gateway was forced with a crowbar, and eight or ten neighbors entered, accompanied by two _gendarmes_. By then the cries had stopped; but as the group hurried up the first staircase, two or more harsh voices, angrily disputing, were heard and seemed to come from the upper portion of the house. When the second landing was reached, these sounds too had ceased, and all was completely silent. The party dispersed and rushed from room to room. On reaching a large rear chamber on the fourth floor, (the door having been found locked, with the key within, and therefore forced open,) a scene met their eyes that filled everyone present with horror as well as astonishment.\\n\\n\u201cThe room was in the greatest confusion\u2014the furniture broken and scattered everywhere. There was but one bedstead; the bed had been taken from it and cast into the center of the floor. Upon a chair lay a razor, stained with blood.\"}"
