          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5861a48ee0c3f60b87bc5c9b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI must point out, Sir Walter,\u201d said Mr Shepherd one morning at Kellynch Hall, as he put down the newspaper, \u201cthat the present situation is very much in our favour. This peace will be putting all our wealthy naval officers ashore. They will all be looking for a home. There could not be a better time, Sir Walter, to have a choice of tenants\u2014very responsible tenants. Many a great fortune was made during the war. If a rich admiral should come our way, Sir Walter\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe would be a very fortunate man, Shepherd,\u201d replied Sir Walter; \u201cthat is all I have to say. Kellynch Hall would indeed be a prize to him; perhaps the greatest prize of all, however many he may have taken before; eh, Shepherd?\u201d\\n\\nMr Shepherd laughed, as he knew he was expected to, and then continued\u2014\\n\\n\u201cI venture to observe, Sir Walter, that, in business, gentlemen of the navy are good people to deal with. I have had some experience of their methods; and I freely admit that they hold very liberal views, and are as likely to make desirable tenants as any class of people one could meet. Therefore, Sir Walter, what I would suggest is this: if, as a result of rumours spreading about your intentions\u2014which must be considered possible, since we know how difficult it is to keep the actions and plans of one part of the world from the notice and curiosity of another; prominence has its price\u2014I, John Shepherd, might conceal any family matters I wished, because no one would think it worth observing me; but Sir Walter Elliot has eyes upon him that may be very difficult to evade. Therefore, I venture to say that it will not greatly surprise me if, despite all our caution, some rumour of the truth should spread. In that event, as I was about to observe, applications will certainly follow, and I should consider those from wealthy naval commanders especially worth attending to; and I beg leave to add that I can come over at any time within two hours, to spare you the trouble of replying.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter merely nodded. But soon afterward, rising and walking about the room, he remarked sarcastically\u2014\\n\\n\u201cThere are few among the gentlemen of the navy, I imagine, who would not be surprised to find themselves in a house of this kind.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey would look around them, no doubt, and bless their good fortune,\u201d said Mrs Clay, who was present; her father had driven her over, since nothing benefited Mrs Clay\u2019s health so much as a drive to Kellynch. \u201cBut I entirely agree with my father that a sailor might be a very desirable tenant.\u201d\"}"
