          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1fa2d7df9096e6d3ef6fd242\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It should be said in the young lady\u2019s favor that she did not expressly declare she would not, but only voiced an emphatic and earnest wish to be \u201cblessed\u201d if she did; a courteous and delicate way of evading the request, showing that she possessed the natural good manners which cannot endure causing a fellow-creature the pain of a blunt and direct refusal.\\n\\nThe Jew\u2019s face fell. He turned from this young lady, who was gaily, almost splendidly dressed in a red gown, green boots, and yellow curl-papers, to the other female.\\n\\n\u201cNancy, my dear,\u201d said the Jew soothingly, \u201cwhat do _you_ say?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat it won\u2019t do; so there\u2019s no use trying it on, Fagin,\u201d Nancy replied.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by that?\u201d asked Mr. Sikes, looking up surlily.\\n\\n\u201cWhat I say, Bill,\u201d the lady answered calmly.\\n\\n\u201cWhy, you\u2019re exactly the person for it,\u201d argued Mr. Sikes; \u201cnobody round here knows anything about you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd since I don\u2019t want \u2019em to, either,\u201d Nancy replied in the same composed tone, \u201cit\u2019s rather more no than yes with me, Bill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe\u2019ll go, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\n\u201cNo, she won\u2019t, Fagin,\u201d said Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cYes, she will, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes.\\n\\nAnd Mr. Sikes was right. Through a mixture of threats, promises, and bribes, the lady was at last persuaded to undertake the commission. She was not restrained by the same concerns as her agreeable companion; for, having lately moved into the neighborhood of Field Lane from the distant but respectable suburb of Ratcliffe, she had no equal fear of being recognized by her many acquaintances.\\n\\nAccordingly, with a clean white apron fastened over her gown and her curl-papers tucked beneath a straw bonnet\u2014both garments supplied from the Jew\u2019s inexhaustible stores\u2014Miss Nancy prepared to set out upon her errand.\\n\\n\u201cWait a moment, my dear,\u201d said the Jew, producing a small covered basket. \u201cCarry that in one hand. It looks more respectable, my dear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGive her a door-key for the other one, Fagin,\u201d said Sikes; \u201cit makes the thing look real and genivine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, my dear, so it does,\u201d said the Jew, hanging a large street-door key upon the forefinger of the young lady\u2019s right hand. \u201cThere; very good! Very good indeed, my dear!\u201d said the Jew, rubbing his hands.\\n\\n\u201cOh, my brother! My poor, dear, sweet, innocent little brother!\u201d cried Nancy, bursting into tears and wringing the little basket and street-door key in an agony of distress. \u201cWhat has become of him? Where have they taken him? Oh, have pity, and tell me what has been done with the dear boy, gentlemen; do, gentlemen, if you please, gentlemen!\u201d\"}"
