          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"590dd219c889d00b60691ecc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret lowering herself enough to visit the Coles. The visit gave her many pleasant memories the next day; and whatever she might be thought to have sacrificed in dignified seclusion would be more than repaid by the glory of popularity. She must have delighted the Coles\u2014worthy people who deserved to be made happy!\u2014and left behind her a name that would not soon fade.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even in memory, is uncommon; and there were two matters about which she was not entirely at ease. She wondered whether, as one woman to another, she had not violated her duty by revealing her suspicions about Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings to Frank Churchill. It was hardly right; but the idea had been so powerful that it had escaped her, and his acceptance of everything she told him was such a compliment to her insight that she could not be completely sure she ought to have kept silent.\\n\\nThe other regret also concerned Jane Fairfax, and in that case she had no doubt. She sincerely and unmistakably regretted the inferiority of her own playing and singing. She deeply mourned the idleness of her childhood\u2014and sat down to practise energetically for an hour and a half.\\n\\nHarriet\u2019s arrival interrupted her; and if Harriet\u2019s praise could have satisfied her, she might soon have been consoled.\\n\\n\u201cOh! If only I could play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t put us together, Harriet. My playing is no more like hers than a lamp is like sunlight.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear\u2014I think you play the better of the two. I think you play quite as well as she does. I am sure I would much rather hear you. Everyone said last night how well you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnyone who knew anything about it must have noticed the difference. The truth is, Harriet, that my playing is merely good enough to be praised, while Jane Fairfax\u2019s is far beyond that.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I shall always think you play quite as well as she does, or that, if there is any difference, no one could ever detect it. Mr. Cole said how much taste you had; and Mr. Frank Churchill spoke at length about your taste, saying that he valued taste much more than execution.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! But Jane Fairfax has both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you sure? I saw that she had execution, but I did not know she had any taste. No one mentioned it. And I hate Italian singing\u2014there is no understanding a word of it. Besides, if she plays so very well, you know, it is no more than she is required to do, since she will have to teach.\u201d\"}"
