          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"89d024e0c4fd07d79160d4f8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Were our proud dreams meant to fade like this? Our name was written \u201ca little lower than the angels\u201d; yet we were no better than mayflies. We had called ourselves the \u201cparagon of animals,\u201d and now we were a \u201cquintessence of dust.\u201d We had complained that the pyramids had outlasted the embalmed body of their builder. Alas, even the simple shepherd\u2019s straw hut we passed on the road held within its structure the promise of a longer life than the entire human race. How could we reconcile this sad change with our former hopes and our apparent powers?\\n\\nSuddenly, an inner voice, clear and distinct, seemed to say: Thus it was decreed from eternity. The horses that carry Time forward had this hour and its fulfillment chained to them from the moment the void gave birth to its burden. Would you read backward the unchangeable laws of Necessity?\\n\\nMother of the world! Servant of the Almighty! Eternal, unchanging Necessity, who with busy fingers are always weaving the unbreakable chain of events\u2014I will not complain of your actions. If my human mind cannot accept that all things are right, then, since whatever happens must happen, I will sit among the ruins and smile. Truly, we were not born to enjoy, but to submit and to hope.\\n\\nWill the reader grow tired if I describe in detail our long journey from Paris to Geneva? If I recorded, day by day, the countless miseries of our condition in journal form, could my hand write, or language provide words to express, the variety of our suffering\u2014the crowding of one terrible event upon another? Have patience, reader, whoever you are and wherever you live. Whether you are a spiritual being or descended from some surviving pair, your nature will be human and your home will be the earth. Here you will read of the deeds of the vanished race, and wonder whether those who endured what is recorded here were made of fragile flesh and delicate bodies like your own. Indeed they were\u2014so weep; for surely, solitary being, you will be gentle in spirit. Shed tears of compassion, but meanwhile attend to this tale and learn the deeds and sufferings of your predecessors.\\n\\nYet the final events that marked our passage through France were so filled with strange horror and dark misery that I dare not linger over them. If I examined every incident, each tiny fraction of a second would contain a dreadful story, whose smallest word would chill the blood in your young veins. It is right that I should raise this monument of the former race for your instruction, but not that I should lead you through hospital wards or the hidden rooms of a charnel house.\"}"
