          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"49a50d80a92e12b65487eaf7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I am also informed by Mrs. Otis, who, I may say, is no minor authority on art\u2014having had the privilege of spending several winters in Boston as a girl\u2014that these gems are worth a great deal of money and would command a high price if sold. Under these circumstances, Lord Canterville, I am sure you will understand how impossible it would be for me to let them remain in the possession of any member of my family; indeed, all such empty ornaments and toys, however appropriate or necessary to the dignity of the British aristocracy, would be entirely out of place among people raised according to the strict, and, I believe, immortal principles of republican simplicity. I should perhaps add that Virginia is very eager for you to let her keep the box as a memento of your unfortunate but misguided ancestor. Since it is extremely old and therefore rather badly damaged, you may perhaps decide to grant her request. For my part, I admit that I am quite surprised to find a child of mine showing sympathy with medievalism in any form, and can explain it only by the fact that Virginia was born in one of your London suburbs shortly after Mrs. Otis returned from a trip to Athens.\u2019\\n\\nLord Canterville listened with great seriousness to the worthy Minister\u2019s speech, occasionally tugging at his grey moustache to conceal an involuntary smile. When Mr. Otis had finished, he shook his hand warmly and said, \u2018My dear sir, your charming little daughter rendered my unfortunate ancestor, Sir Simon, a very important service, and my family and I are deeply indebted to her for her extraordinary courage and spirit. The jewels plainly belong to her, and, by heaven, I believe that if I were heartless enough to take them away, the wicked old fellow would be out of his grave within a fortnight, making my life a misery. As for their being heirlooms, nothing is an heirloom unless it is identified as such in a will or other legal document, and the existence of these jewels was entirely unknown. I assure you that I have no more claim to them than your butler does, and when Miss Virginia grows up, I daresay she will be glad to have pretty things to wear. Besides, you forget, Mr. Otis, that you bought the furniture and the ghost at an assessed value, and anything belonging to the ghost passed immediately into your possession. Whatever activity Sir Simon may have displayed in the corridor at night, legally speaking he was dead, and you acquired his property by purchase.\u2019\"}"
