          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e873084bccea92c004d64355\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, however, the lieutenant asked why he had come so far across the ice in such a strange vehicle.\\n\\nHis face immediately took on an expression of the deepest gloom, and he replied, \u201cTo find someone who fled from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did the man you pursued travel in the same way?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I think we have seen him, for the day before we picked you up, we saw some dogs pulling a sledge, with a man in it, across the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThis caught the stranger\u2019s attention, and he asked many questions about the route the d\u00e6mon, as he called him, had taken. Soon afterward, when he was alone with me, he said, \u201cI have surely aroused your curiosity, as well as that of these good people; but you are too considerate to ask questions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly; it would be very rude and inhuman of me to trouble you with my curiosity.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet you rescued me from a strange and dangerous situation; you have kindly brought me back to life.\u201d\\n\\nSoon afterward he asked whether I thought the breaking ice had destroyed the other sledge. I replied that I could not say with certainty, since the ice had not broken until nearly midnight, and the traveller might have reached safety before then; but I had no way of knowing.\\n\\nFrom that time, a new energy seemed to animate the stranger\u2019s failing body. He was extremely eager to go on deck and watch for the sledge that had appeared before; but I persuaded him to remain in the cabin, because he was far too weak to endure the cold air. I promised that someone would watch for him and tell him at once if anything new came into view.\\n\\nThis is my journal of everything connected with this strange event up to the present day. The stranger\u2019s health has gradually improved, but he is very quiet and seems uneasy whenever anyone except me enters his cabin. Still, his manner is so gentle and pleasant that all the sailors are interested in him, although they have spoken with him very little. As for me, I am beginning to love him like a brother, and his constant, profound sorrow fills me with sympathy and compassion. He must have been a noble person in better days, for even now, despite his ruin, he is so attractive and kind.\\n\\nI said in one of my letters, my dear Margaret, that I would find no friend on the open ocean; yet I have found a man whom, before misery had broken his spirit, I would have been glad to have as the brother of my heart.\"}"
