          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b357eba22c59d8452a088c69\",\"rewritten_text\":\"We would wake after a while and look around to see what had caused it, and perhaps spot a steamboat coughing upstream, so far off toward the opposite bank that you couldn\u2019t tell anything about her except whether she was a stern-wheeler or a side-wheeler. Then for about an hour there would be nothing to hear or see\u2014only complete loneliness. Next you might see a raft drifting by, far away, and perhaps a fellow on it chopping, because they are almost always doing that on a raft; you would see the axe flash and fall\u2014you heard nothing; you saw the axe rise again, and by the time it was above the man\u2019s head, then you heard the _k\u2019chunk!_\u2014it had taken all that time to cross the water. So we spent the day idling around, listening to the silence. Once there was a heavy fog, and the rafts and other things passing by were beating tin pans so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t run them down. A scow or raft passed so close that we could hear them talking, cursing, and laughing\u2014hear them clearly; but we couldn\u2019t see any sign of them. It made you feel uneasy; it was like spirits carrying on in the air. Jim said he believed they were spirits, but I said:\\n\\n\u201cNo; spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Damn this damn fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAs soon as night fell we pushed off; when we had reached about the middle, we left her alone and let her drift wherever the current carried her. Then we lit our pipes, dangled our legs in the water, and talked about all sorts of things\u2014we were always naked, day and night, whenever the mosquitoes allowed it. The new clothes Buck\u2019s family had made for me were too fine to be comfortable, and besides, I never cared much for clothes anyway.\\n\\nSometimes we had the whole river to ourselves for a very long time. There were the banks and islands over there, across the water; perhaps a spark\u2014which was a candle in a cabin window; and sometimes on the water you could see a spark or two\u2014on a raft or scow, you know. Maybe you could hear a fiddle or a song drifting over from one of those boats. It is wonderful to live on a raft. We had the sky above us, scattered with stars, and we would lie on our backs and look up at them, discussing whether they had been made or had simply happened. Jim believed they had been made, but I thought they had happened; I figured it would have taken too long to _make_ so many.\"}"
