          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e53c236427c4c6ea418ad587\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was the beginning of February, and Anne, who had been in Bath for a month, was becoming very eager for news from Uppercross and Lyme. She wanted to hear far more than Mary had told her. It had been three weeks since she had heard anything at all. She knew only that Henrietta was home again, and that Louisa, though thought to be recovering quickly, was still in Lyme. She was thinking of them all very intently one evening when a letter from Mary, thicker than usual, was delivered to her; and, to increase the pleasure and surprise, it came with the compliments of Admiral and Mrs Croft.\\n\\nThe Crofts must be in Bath! That was something to interest her. They were people toward whom her heart naturally turned.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is this?\u201d cried Sir Walter. \u201cThe Crofts have arrived in Bath? The Crofts who rent Kellynch? What have they brought you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Such letters are useful passports. They secure an introduction. I should have called on Admiral Croft in any case. I know what is owed to my tenant.\u201d\\n\\nAnne could listen no longer; she could not even have said how the poor Admiral\u2019s complexion escaped notice. Her letter absorbed her attention. It had been begun several days earlier.\\n\\n\u201cFebruary 1st.\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR ANNE,\\n\\nI make no apology for my silence, because I know how little people think about letters in a place like Bath. You must be far too happy to care about Uppercross, which, as you know very well, offers little to write about. We have had a very dull Christmas; Mr and Mrs Musgrove have not held a single dinner party throughout the holidays. I do not count the Hayters as anyone. The holidays, however, are finally over: I believe no children ever had such long ones. I am sure I never did. The house was cleared yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be surprised to hear that they have never gone home. Mrs Harville must be an unusual mother to part with them for so long. I cannot understand it. They are not at all well-behaved children, in my opinion; but Mrs Musgrove seems to like them quite as much, if not more, than her grandchildren. What dreadful weather we have had! It may not be noticed in Bath, with your fine pavements; but in the country it is a serious matter. No one has called on me since the second week of January, except Charles Hayter, who had been calling much more often than was welcome. Between ourselves, I think it a great pity Henrietta did not remain in Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her a little farther from him.\u201d\"}"
