          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c236bf68317cac74f07dade\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dawn had come when I set the Manuscript aside. The rain had nearly stopped; the world was gray and mournful, and the spent storm sighed and sobbed itself to sleep. I went to the stranger\u2019s room and listened at his door, which stood slightly open. I could hear his voice, so I knocked. There was no answer, though the voice continued. I peered inside. The man lay on his back in bed, speaking brokenly but with spirit, punctuating his words with his arms, which he flung about restlessly, as the sick do in delirium. I slipped in quietly and bent over him. His mutterings and exclamations continued. I spoke\u2014only a word, to draw his attention. At once, his glassy eyes and ashen face lit with pleasure, gratitude, joy, and welcome:\\n\\n\u201cOh, Sandy, you have come at last\u2014how I have longed for you! Sit beside me\u2014do not leave me\u2014never leave me again, Sandy, never again. Where is your hand? Give it to me, dear; let me hold it\u2014there\u2014now all is well, all is peace, and I am happy again\u2014we are happy again, are we not, Sandy? You are so dim, so indistinct; you are only a mist, a cloud, but you are here, and that is blessing enough. And I have your hand; do not take it away\u2014it is only for a little while. I shall not need it long.... Was that the child?... Hello, Central!... She does not answer. Asleep, perhaps? Bring her when she wakes, and let me touch her hands, her face, her hair, and say good-bye.... Sandy! Yes, you are there. I lost myself for a moment and thought you had gone.... Have I been ill long? It must be so; it seems months to me. And such dreams! Such strange and terrible dreams, Sandy! Dreams as real as reality\u2014delirium, of course, but so real! Why, I thought the king was dead; I thought you were in Gaul and could not get home; I thought there was a revolution. In the fantastic frenzy of those dreams, I thought Clarence and I and a handful of my cadets fought and destroyed the entire chivalry of England! But even that was not the strangest. I seemed to be a creature from a distant, unborn age, centuries in the future, and even that was as real as the rest! Yes, I seemed to have flown back from that age into our own, then forward to it again, and been set down, a stranger and abandoned, in that strange England, with an abyss of thirteen centuries yawning between you and me\u2014between me and my home and my friends\u2014between me and everything dear to me, everything that could make life worth living!\u201d\"}"
