          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"fe195c1d2d33dec625a94a89\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Good heavens, she was a masterful talker. It would not have been proper for me to say that it was very late, that I was tired, and that I could hardly keep my eyes open. I wished I had gone to bed when I had the chance. Now I had to endure it; there was no other choice. So she went on and on, her voice tinkling through the otherwise deep, ghostly silence of the sleeping castle. At last there came, as if from far beneath us, a distant sound like a muffled shriek\u2014with such agony in it that my flesh crawled. The queen stopped, and her eyes shone with pleasure. She tilted her graceful head like a bird listening for a sound. Again the cry forced its way up through the silence.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cIt is a stubborn soul, and has endured long. It has been many hours now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEndured what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe rack. Come\u2014you shall see a cheerful sight. If he does not reveal his secret now, you shall see him torn apart.\u201d\\n\\nWhat a smooth, heartless devil she was, remaining so calm and serene while the cords in my legs ached in sympathy with that man's suffering. Armored guards carrying blazing torches led us through echoing corridors and down damp, dripping stone stairs that smelled of mold and centuries of trapped darkness. It was a long, cold, frightening journey, made neither shorter nor more pleasant by the sorceress's talk about the prisoner and his crime. An anonymous informer had accused him of killing a stag in the royal hunting grounds. I said:\\n\\n\u201cAnonymous testimony is not really fair, Your Highness. It would be better to confront the accused with his accuser.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI had not considered that, since it seemed of little importance. But even if I wished to, I could not, because the accuser came masked at night, told the forester, and immediately departed again. Thus the forester does not know him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen was this Unknown the only person who saw the stag being killed?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed, no man saw the killing. But this Unknown saw the bold fellow near the place where the stag lay, and, out of loyal zeal, reported him to the forester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo the Unknown was near the dead stag as well? Is it not possible that he killed it himself? His loyal zeal\u2014while masked\u2014seems somewhat suspicious. But why do you intend to torture the prisoner? What good will it do?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe will not confess otherwise, and then his soul would be lost. By law, his crime has cost him his life\u2014and I will certainly see that he pays it!\u2014but I would endanger my own soul if I allowed him to die without confessing and receiving absolution.\u201d\"}"
