          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c1953fdfeadb3295db0532d4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, I misunderstood you. I thought you said \u2018anybody,\u2019 and assumed that \u2018anybody\u2019 included\u2014well, anybody; that is, everybody.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt does\u2014anybody of noble birth; and preferably royal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat, it seems to me, may well be,\u201d said the abbot, who saw a chance to smooth matters and avert disaster, \u201cfor surely so marvelous a gift would not be granted to reveal the affairs of beings lesser than those born near the heights of greatness. Our King Arthur\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould you know about him?\u201d the enchanter interrupted.\\n\\n\u201cMost gladly, and with gratitude.\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was immediately filled with awe and interest again, the incorrigible idiots. They watched the incantations intently, then looked at me with a \u201cThere, now, what can you say to that?\u201d expression when the announcement came:\\n\\n\u201cThe king is weary from the hunt and has lain in his palace these two hours, sleeping dreamlessly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGod\u2019s blessing upon him!\u201d said the abbot, crossing himself. \u201cMay that sleep refresh both body and soul.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd so it might, if he were asleep,\u201d I said, \u201cbut the king is not sleeping; the king is riding.\u201d\\n\\nThere was trouble again\u2014a conflict of authority. No one knew which of us to believe; I still had some reputation left. The magician\u2019s scorn was aroused, and he said:\\n\\n\u201cBehold, I have seen many wonderful soothsayers, prophets, and magicians in my lifetime, but never one who could sit idle and see into the heart of things without a single incantation to help him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou have lived in the woods and lost much by it. I use incantations myself, as this good brotherhood knows\u2014but only on important occasions.\u201d\\n\\nWhen it comes to sarcasm, I reckon I know how to hold my own. That jab made the fellow squirm. The abbot asked about the queen and the court, and received this answer:\\n\\n\u201cThey are all asleep, overcome by fatigue, just as the king is.\u201d\\n\\nI said:\\n\\n\u201cThat is merely another lie. Half of them are occupied with their amusements; the queen and the other half are not sleeping\u2014they are riding. Now perhaps you can exert yourself a little and tell us where the king and queen, and all those riding with them, are going at this moment.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey sleep now, as I said; but tomorrow they will ride, for they are setting out on a journey toward the sea.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd where will they be the day after tomorrow at vespers?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFar north of Camelot, with half their journey completed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is another lie\u2014by a distance of a hundred and fifty miles.\u201d\"}"
