          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c78ae69a4919492cf9d56ef4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Her complexion changed, and she said no more.\\n\\nMrs. Gardiner was standing a little behind, and when she paused, he asked whether she would do him the honour of introducing him to her friends. This display of civility caught her completely unprepared; she could scarcely suppress a smile at his now seeking the acquaintance of some of the very people against whom his pride had rebelled when he proposed to her. \u201cHow surprised he will be,\u201d she thought, \u201cwhen he discovers who they are! He takes them for people of fashion.\u201d\\n\\nThe introduction was made at once; and as she explained their relationship to her, she stole a sly glance at him to see how he endured it, half expecting him to retreat as quickly as possible from such disgraceful companions. That he was surprised by the connection was obvious; nevertheless, he bore it with fortitude. Far from leaving, he turned back with them and began speaking with Mr. Gardiner. Elizabeth could not help being pleased\u2014could not help feeling triumphant. It was comforting that he should know she had relations of whom there was no reason to be ashamed. She listened with the greatest attention to everything that passed between them and took pride in every expression, every sentence from her uncle that revealed his intelligence, taste, or good manners.\\n\\nThe conversation soon turned to fishing, and she heard Mr. Darcy invite him, with the utmost civility, to fish there as often as he wished while he remained in the neighbourhood. He offered at the same time to provide him with fishing tackle and pointed out the parts of the stream where the best sport was usually found. Mrs. Gardiner, walking arm in arm with Elizabeth, gave her a look that expressed her astonishment. Elizabeth said nothing, but was deeply gratified; the compliment must be intended entirely for her. Yet her amazement was extreme, and she kept repeating to herself, \u201cWhy is he so changed? What could have caused it? It cannot be for me; it cannot be on my account that his manner has softened so much. My reproofs at Hunsford could not have produced such a change. It is impossible that he should still love me.\u201d\\n\\nAfter they had walked this way for some time, with the two ladies ahead and the two gentlemen behind, a small change occurred when they resumed their places after going down to the riverbank to examine more closely a curious water plant. It began with Mrs. Gardiner, who, tired from the morning\u2019s exercise, found Elizabeth\u2019s arm insufficient support and therefore chose her husband\u2019s instead.\"}"
