          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"35f06d4021a5dc0a5262a329\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYes, burglar\u2019s tools. That seems to ease your mind considerably. But what caused that change in you? What did _you_ think we had found?\u201d\\n\\nHuck was in a tight spot\u2014the questioning eye was fixed on him\u2014and he would have given anything for some material from which to shape a believable answer. Nothing came to him. The eye seemed to bore deeper and deeper. A foolish reply suggested itself, and there was no time to consider it, so he blurted it out at random, weakly:\\n\\n\u201cSunday-school books, perhaps.\u201d\\n\\nPoor Huck was too troubled to laugh, but the old man roared with delight, shaking every part of himself from head to foot. At last he declared that a laugh like that was money in a man\u2019s pocket, since it reduced the doctor\u2019s bill enormously. Then he continued:\\n\\n\u201cPoor fellow, you\u2019re pale and worn\u2014you\u2019re not well at all. No wonder you\u2019re somewhat flighty and unsteady. But you\u2019ll recover. Rest and sleep will set you right, I hope.\u201d\\n\\nHuck was annoyed to realize what a fool he had made of himself and how suspiciously excited he had appeared. He had abandoned the notion that the parcel brought from the tavern was the treasure as soon as he heard the conversation at the widow\u2019s stile. Yet he had only concluded that it was not the treasure; he had not known for certain that it wasn\u2019t. Thus the mention of a captured bundle had overwhelmed his self-control. Still, on the whole, he was glad the incident had occurred, because he now knew beyond any doubt that this bundle was not _the_ bundle, and his mind was consequently peaceful and thoroughly at ease. Indeed, everything now seemed to be moving in exactly the right direction. The treasure must still be in No. 2, the men would be caught and imprisoned that day, and he and Tom could take the gold that night without difficulty or fear of interruption.\\n\\nBreakfast had scarcely ended when someone knocked at the door. Huck sprang toward a hiding-place, unwilling to be connected in even the slightest way with the recent affair. The Welshman admitted several ladies and gentlemen, including the Widow Douglas, and observed groups of townspeople making their way up the hill to gaze at the stile. The news had evidently spread. The Welshman was obliged to recount the night\u2019s events to his visitors. The widow openly expressed her gratitude for being saved.\\n\\n\u201cPlease don\u2019t mention it, madam. There is someone to whom you may owe more than you do to me and my boys, perhaps, but he won\u2019t permit me to reveal his name. We would not have been there without him.\u201d\"}"
