          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1078770e2d7a81e5b6b8d01a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"This was Mrs. Umney, the housekeeper, whom Mrs. Otis, at Lady Canterville\u2019s earnest request, had agreed to retain in her former position. She gave each of them a low curtsey as they got out and said in a quaint, old-fashioned manner, \u201cI welcome you to Canterville Chase.\u201d Following her, they went through the fine Tudor hall into the library, a long, low room panelled in black oak, with a large stained-glass window at the far end. Tea had been prepared for them there, and, after removing their wraps, they sat down and began looking around while Mrs. Umney waited on them.\\n\\nSuddenly Mrs. Otis noticed a dull red stain on the floor beside the fireplace and, unaware of what it really meant, said to Mrs. Umney, \u201cI\u2019m afraid something has been spilled there.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, madam,\u201d replied the old housekeeper in a low voice, \u201cblood has been spilled on that spot.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow dreadful,\u201d cried Mrs. Otis. \u201cI don\u2019t like bloodstains in a sitting room at all. It must be removed immediately.\u201d\\n\\nThe old woman smiled and answered in the same low, mysterious voice, \u201cIt is the blood of Lady Eleanore de Canterville, who was murdered on that very spot by her husband, Sir Simon de Canterville, in 1575. Sir Simon survived her by nine years, then disappeared suddenly under very mysterious circumstances. His body has never been found, but his guilty spirit still haunts the Chase. Tourists and others have greatly admired the bloodstain, and it cannot be removed.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat is nonsense,\u201d cried Washington Otis. \u201cPinkerton\u2019s Champion Stain Remover and Paragon Detergent will clean it up in no time.\u201d Before the terrified housekeeper could stop him, he had dropped to his knees and was vigorously scrubbing the floor with a small stick of what appeared to be black cosmetic. Within moments, no trace of the bloodstain remained.\\n\\n\u201cI knew Pinkerton would do it,\u201d he exclaimed triumphantly, looking around at his admiring family. But no sooner had he spoken than a terrible flash of lightning illuminated the gloomy room, a frightening peal of thunder made them all leap to their feet, and Mrs. Umney fainted.\\n\\n\u201cWhat an outrageous climate!\u201d said the American Minister calmly as he lit a long cheroot. \u201cI suppose the old country is so overpopulated that there isn\u2019t enough decent weather for everyone. I have always believed that emigration is the only solution for England.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Hiram,\u201d cried Mrs. Otis, \u201cwhat are we to do with a woman who faints?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCharge it to her as breakage,\u201d answered the Minister. \u201cShe won\u2019t faint again after that.\u201d And within a few moments Mrs. Umney certainly recovered.\"}"
