          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9539e6516572dc38e56453b9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I know she would!\u201d\\n\\nThe Widow Douglas\u2019s splendid hospitality was a tempting lure, and it, together with Tom\u2019s urging, soon carried the decision. They resolved to tell no one about the night\u2019s plans. Presently Tom thought that perhaps Huck might come that very night and give the signal. The idea took much of the eagerness from his expectations. Yet he could not endure giving up the entertainment at Widow Douglas\u2019s. And why should he, he argued\u2014the signal had not come the night before, so why was it any more likely tonight? The certain pleasure of the evening outweighed the doubtful treasure; and, like a boy, he decided to follow the stronger impulse and not think about the box of money again that day.\\n\\nThree miles below town, the ferryboat halted at the entrance to a wooded hollow and tied up. The crowd poured ashore, and soon the distant forest and jagged heights rang on every side with shouts and laughter. Every possible way of becoming hot and tired was tried, and presently the rovers straggled back to camp, armed with hearty appetites; then the destruction of the good things commenced. After the meal came a pleasant period of resting and talking beneath the spreading oaks. Presently someone called out:\\n\\n\u201cWho\u2019s ready for the cave?\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was. Bundles of candles were obtained, and at once there was a general scramble up the hill. The cave\u2019s mouth opened partway up the hillside, shaped like the letter A. Its huge oaken door stood open. Inside was a little chamber, cold as an icehouse, its walls formed by Nature from solid limestone damp with a chilly sweat. It was romantic and mysterious to stand there in the deep darkness and gaze out over the green valley gleaming in the sunlight. But the solemnity of the scene soon faded, and the rough play began again. As soon as a candle was lit, everyone rushed at its owner; a struggle and brave defense followed, but the candle was quickly knocked down or blown out, bringing a delighted uproar of laughter and another chase. Yet everything ends. By-and-by the procession filed down the steep slope of the main passage, its flickering line of lights faintly showing the towering rock walls nearly to where they met sixty feet above. This main passage was only eight or ten feet across. Every few steps, other high, narrower cracks branched away on either side\u2014for McDougal\u2019s cave was merely a huge maze of twisting aisles that joined one another, emerged again, and led nowhere.\"}"
