          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0bcd3c16b41010ca92eb7244\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Not everyone would have behaved with such kindness as she did, and refused to accept Jane\u2019s answer; but she firmly declared yesterday that she would _not_ write the refusal Jane had urged her to send. She would wait\u2014and, sure enough, by yesterday evening everything was settled: Jane was to go. Quite a surprise to me! I had not the slightest notion! Jane took Mrs. Elton aside and immediately told her that, after considering the advantages of Mrs. Smallridge\u2019s situation, she had resolved to accept it. I knew nothing of it until the whole matter was settled.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou spent the evening with Mrs. Elton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, all of us; Mrs. Elton insisted that we should come. It was arranged on the hill, while we were walking about with Mr. Knightley. \u2018You _must_ _all_ spend your evening with us,\u2019 she said\u2014\u2018I absolutely must have you _all_ come.\u2019\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Knightley was there too, was he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, not Mr. Knightley; he refused from the beginning; and although I thought he would come, since Mrs. Elton declared she would not excuse him, he did not\u2014but my mother, Jane, and I were all there, and we had a very agreeable evening. Such kind friends, you know, Miss Woodhouse, must always seem agreeable, though everyone appeared rather tired after the morning\u2019s party. Even pleasure, you know, can be exhausting\u2014and I cannot say that any of them seemed to have enjoyed it very much. Still, _I_ shall always consider it a pleasant party, and feel deeply obliged to the kind friends who included me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Fairfax, I suppose, though you did not know it, had been deciding the whole day?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say she had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhenever the time comes, it must be unwelcome to her and all her friends\u2014but I hope her engagement will have every possible advantage\u2014I mean, in regard to the family\u2019s character and manners.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear Miss Woodhouse. Yes, indeed, it contains everything in the world that could make her happy. Except the Sucklings and Bragges, there is no other nursery establishment in all Mrs. Elton\u2019s acquaintance so liberal and elegant. Mrs. Smallridge is a most delightful woman!\u2014A style of living almost equal to Maple Grove\u2014and as for the children, except the little Sucklings and little Bragges, there are nowhere such elegant, sweet children. Jane will be treated with such consideration and kindness!\u2014It will be nothing but pleasure, a life of pleasure. And her salary!\u2014I really cannot presume to tell you what her salary is, Miss Woodhouse.\u201d\"}"
