{"request_id":"8a0dd14a09cd0e8e9cf10c00","rewritten_text":"When he reached the top of the stairs, he regained his composure and resolved to produce his famous peal of demonic laughter. He had found this remarkably effective on more than one occasion. It was reputed to have turned Lord Raker’s wig grey in one night, and had certainly caused three of Lady Canterville’s French governesses to give notice before completing their month. He therefore laughed his most dreadful laugh until the ancient vaulted ceiling echoed repeatedly. Yet scarcely had the terrible reverberation faded when a door opened and Mrs. Otis appeared in a pale blue dressing-gown. “I fear you are feeling very unwell,” she said, “and I have brought you a bottle of Dr. Dobell’s tincture. If the trouble is indigestion, you will find it an excellent remedy.” The ghost glowered at her furiously and immediately began preparing to transform himself into a great black dog, a feat for which he was deservedly famous, and which the family doctor always credited with causing the lasting idiocy of Lord Canterville’s uncle, the Hon. Thomas Horton. The noise of footsteps drawing near, however, made him pause in his sinister undertaking, so he settled for becoming dimly phosphorescent and disappeared with a deep graveyard groan, just as the twins reached him.

Once back in his room, he completely collapsed and fell into the most violent agitation. The twins’ vulgarity and Mrs. Otis’s crude materialism were naturally highly irritating, but what troubled him most was his inability to wear the suit of armour. He had hoped that even modern Americans would be excited by the sight of a Spectre in Armour, if for no better reason than respect for their national poet Longfellow, whose elegant and appealing verse had helped him pass many a tedious hour when the Cantervilles were in town. Moreover, it was his own armour. He had worn it with great success at the Kenilworth tournament and had received lavish praise for it from none other than the Virgin Queen herself. But when he put it on, the enormous breastplate and steel helmet had overwhelmed him with their weight; he fell hard upon the stone floor, badly scraping both knees and bruising the knuckles of his right hand.

For several days afterward he was exceedingly ill and scarcely left his room, except to keep the bloodstain properly maintained. In time, however, by taking excellent care of himself, he recovered and determined to make a third attempt to frighten the United States Minister and his family."}

