          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4d607690ec6e417781ab923f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She left everything behind her, except the memory that such things had once existed.\\n\\nAnne had not returned to Kellynch since leaving Lady Russell\u2019s house in September. There had been no need, and whenever it had been possible for her to go to the Hall, she had found a way to avoid it. Her first return was to take her place again in the Lodge\u2019s modern, elegant rooms and delight its mistress.\\n\\nLady Russell\u2019s joy at seeing her was mixed with some anxiety. She knew who had been spending time at Uppercross. Fortunately, either Anne had grown healthier and better-looking, or Lady Russell imagined that she had; and as Anne accepted her compliments, she amused herself by connecting them with her cousin\u2019s silent admiration and hoping that she might enjoy a second spring of youth and beauty.\\n\\nAs they talked, Anne soon noticed a change in her own mind. The subjects that had filled her heart when she left Kellynch, and that she had felt neglected and been forced to suppress among the Musgroves, had now become matters of only secondary importance. She had lately lost sight even of her father, her sister, and Bath. The concerns of Uppercross had pushed everything else aside. When Lady Russell returned to their former hopes and fears, expressed her satisfaction that a house in Camden Place had been taken, and regretted that Mrs Clay was still living with them, Anne would have been ashamed to admit how much more she was thinking about Lyme, Louisa Musgrove, and all her acquaintances there. The home and friendship of the Harvilles and Captain Benwick interested her far more than her father\u2019s house in Camden Place or her sister\u2019s closeness to Mrs Clay. She actually had to make an effort to show Lady Russell anything like equal concern about subjects that naturally deserved her first attention.\\n\\nAt first, there was some awkwardness when they spoke of another matter. They had to discuss the accident at Lyme. Lady Russell had been at the Lodge less than five minutes the previous day when the full story had reached her; but it still had to be discussed. She had to ask questions, regret the imprudence, mourn the result, and both of them had to mention Captain Wentworth\u2019s name. Anne knew she did not manage this as well as Lady Russell. She could not say his name while looking directly at Lady Russell until she had found a way to explain briefly what she thought of his attachment to Louisa.\"}"
