          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ef48347e3a6817f70b2a620d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cThis must have been around that very period of Mr Elliot\u2019s life,\u201d said Anne, \u201cwhich has always aroused my particular curiosity. It must have been about the same time that he became known to my father and sister. I never knew him myself; I only heard about him; but there was something in his conduct then, toward my father and sister, and later in the circumstances of his marriage, which I could never quite reconcile with his present character. It seemed to reveal a very different kind of man.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI know it all, I know it all,\u201d cried Mrs Smith. \u201cHe had been introduced to Sir Walter and your sister before I knew him, but I heard him speak of them constantly. I know he was invited and encouraged, and I know he chose not to go. I may be able to satisfy you on points you would hardly expect; and as for his marriage, I knew everything about it at the time. I was aware of all the arguments for and against it; I was the friend to whom he confided his hopes and plans; and although I had not known his wife before\u2014her inferior position in society, in fact, made that impossible\u2014I knew her throughout the rest of her life, or at least until the last two years, and can answer any question you may wish to ask.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo,\u201d said Anne, \u201cI have no particular inquiry to make about her. I have always understood that they were not a happy couple. But I should like to know why, at that stage of his life, he slighted my father\u2019s acquaintance as he did. My father was certainly inclined to treat him with great kindness and proper attention. Why did Mr Elliot withdraw?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr Elliot,\u201d replied Mrs Smith, \u201cat that period of his life, had one aim: to make his fortune, and by a somewhat faster method than the law. He was determined to do it through marriage. At least, he was determined not to damage it through an imprudent marriage; and I know it was his belief\u2014whether rightly or not, I cannot of course decide\u2014that your father and sister, in their courtesies and invitations, were planning a match between the heir and the young lady, and it was impossible that such a match could satisfy his ideas of wealth and independence. That was why he withdrew, I assure you. He told me the whole story. He kept nothing from me. It was curious that, having just left you behind in Bath, my first and closest acquaintance after marrying should be your cousin; and that, through him, I should continually hear about your father and sister.\u201d\"}"
