          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"05047ff19c53311f2a85f76b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cYou\u2019ve fostered an artificial soul and spirit in him, ma\u2019am, quite unbecoming to a person of his station: as the board, Mrs. Sowerberry, who are practical philosophers, will tell you. What business have paupers with soul or spirit? It\u2019s sufficient that we allow \u2019em living bodies. If you\u2019d kept the boy on gruel, ma\u2019am, this would never have occurred.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDear, dear!\u201d ejaculated Mrs. Sowerberry, devoutly lifting her eyes toward the kitchen ceiling: \u201cthis is the result of being liberal!\u201d\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry\u2019s liberality toward Oliver had consisted in lavishly giving him all the dirty scraps and odds and ends nobody else would eat; so there was considerable meekness and self-sacrifice in her willingly enduring Mr. Bumble\u2019s severe accusation, of which, to do her justice, she was entirely innocent in thought, word, and deed.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d said Mr. Bumble, when the lady lowered her eyes to earth once more; \u201cthe only course now, as I understand it, is to leave him in the cellar for a day or two, until he\u2019s somewhat starved down; then bring him out, and keep him on gruel throughout the apprenticeship. He comes from a bad family. Excitable dispositions, Mrs. Sowerberry! Both nurse and doctor said that his mother forced her way here, through difficulties and pain that would have killed any respectable woman, weeks beforehand.\u201d\\n\\nAt this stage of Mr. Bumble\u2019s speech, Oliver, hearing enough to understand that his mother was being mentioned, began kicking again, with such force that every other sound was drowned. Sowerberry returned at that moment. Oliver\u2019s offence having been described to him, with whatever exaggerations the ladies considered most likely to inflame his anger, he unlocked the cellar door in an instant and hauled his rebellious apprentice out by the collar.\\n\\nOliver\u2019s clothes had been ripped during the beating he had suffered; his face was bruised and scratched, and his hair lay scattered across his forehead. The angry flush had not faded, however; and when dragged from his prison, he glared boldly at Noah and appeared entirely undaunted.\\n\\n\u201cNow, you\u2019re a fine young fellow, aren\u2019t you?\u201d said Sowerberry, shaking Oliver and boxing his ear.\\n\\n\u201cHe called my mother names,\u201d Oliver answered.\\n\\n\u201cWell, and what if he did, you little ungrateful wretch?\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry. \u201cShe deserved what he said, and worse.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe didn\u2019t,\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\n\u201cShe did,\u201d said Mrs. Sowerberry.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s a lie!\u201d said Oliver.\\n\\nMrs. Sowerberry broke into a torrent of tears.\\n\\nThat torrent of tears left Mr. Sowerberry no choice. Had he paused for even a moment before punishing Oliver with the utmost severity, it must be obvious to every experienced reader that, according to all established precedents in matrimonial disputes, he would have been a brute, an unnatural husband, an insulting creature, a poor counterfeit of a man, and several other agreeable characters too numerous to list within this chapter\u2019s limits.\"}"
