          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6a42f47ba113c16fd9d15dec\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret her condescension in visiting the Coles. The call supplied her with many agreeable memories on the following day; and whatever she might be thought to have sacrificed in dignified retirement would be fully compensated by the splendour of popularity. She must have given the Coles\u2014worthy people, who deserved to be made happy!\u2014great pleasure, and left behind her a name not soon to fade.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even in recollection, is uncommon; and there were two matters about which she was not entirely at ease. She questioned whether, in revealing her suspicions of Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings to Frank Churchill, she had not violated the duty of woman toward woman. It was scarcely proper; yet the thought had been so powerful that it escaped her, and his readiness to accept everything she said was such a compliment to her discernment that she could not quite decide whether silence would have been better.\\n\\nThe other regret concerned Jane Fairfax as well; and in that case she had no uncertainty. She sincerely and completely lamented the inferiority of her own playing and singing. She deeply mourned the idleness of her childhood\u2014and sat down to practise energetically for an hour and a half.\\n\\nHarriet\u2019s arrival then interrupted her; and had Harriet\u2019s praise been enough to satisfy her, she might soon have recovered her comfort.\\n\\n\u201cOh! if only I could play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not put us together, Harriet. My playing is no more like hers than a lamp is like sunshine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! dear\u2014I think you play the better of the two. I think you play quite as well as she does. I am certain I would much rather listen to you. Everyone last night said how well you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnyone who understood the matter must have perceived the difference. The truth is, Harriet, that my playing is merely good enough to be praised, while Jane Fairfax\u2019s is far beyond it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I shall always think you play quite as well as she does, or that, if there is a difference, no one could ever discover it. Mr. Cole said how much taste you possessed; and Mr. Frank Churchill spoke at length of your taste, saying he valued taste much more than execution.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! but Jane Fairfax possesses both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you certain? I saw that she had execution, but I did not know she had taste. No one mentioned it. And I detest Italian singing.\u2014One cannot understand a word. Besides, if she plays so very well, you know, it is only what she is required to do, since she will have to teach.\u201d\"}"
