{"request_id":"2ee6357631fd31f2ee7a2d3e","rewritten_text":"Among the other public buildings in a certain town—which, for many reasons, it would be prudent not to name, and to which I will give no invented name—there stands one anciently common to most towns, large or small: namely, a workhouse. In this workhouse was born, on a day and date I need not trouble myself to repeat, since it can have no possible importance to the reader at this stage of the matter, the mortal being whose name appears at the head of this chapter.

For a long time after it was brought into this world of sorrow and trouble by the parish surgeon, it remained very doubtful whether the child would survive to bear any name at all. If not, it is more than likely that these memoirs would never have appeared; or, if they had, consisting of no more than a couple of pages, they would have possessed the priceless distinction of being the most concise and faithful biography in the literature of any age or country.

Although I am not prepared to claim that being born in a workhouse is, in itself, the most fortunate or enviable event that can befall a human being, I do say that, in this particular case, it was the best thing that could possibly have happened to Oliver Twist. The truth is, Oliver had great difficulty taking upon himself the task of breathing—a troublesome practice, but one custom has made necessary for our comfortable existence. For some time he lay gasping on a small flock mattress, precariously balanced between this world and the next, with the balance decidedly favouring the latter. Now, if during that brief period Oliver had been surrounded by careful grandmothers, anxious aunts, experienced nurses, and doctors of profound wisdom, he would most certainly have been killed in no time. As it happened, there was no one present but a pauper old woman, made rather hazy by an unusual allowance of beer, and a parish surgeon who handled such matters by contract. Oliver and Nature fought the matter out between them. The result was that, after a few struggles, Oliver breathed, sneezed, and announced to the workhouse inmates that a new burden had been placed upon the parish by raising as loud a cry as could reasonably have been expected from a male infant who had not possessed that very useful appendage, a voice, for much longer than three minutes and a quarter."}

