          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4acf743273c4a628ec523f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u2018Good heavens!\u2019 the Duchess said to herself. \u2018So he really is some kind of chiropodist after all. How dreadful. I hope he is a foreigner, at least. That would make it rather less unpleasant.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I must certainly present him to you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Present him!\u2019 exclaimed the Duchess. \u2018You don\u2019t mean that he is here?\u2019 She began searching for a little tortoiseshell fan and a very ragged lace shawl, ready to leave at a moment\u2019s notice.\\n\\n\u2018Of course he is here; I should never think of giving a party without him. He says that I have a perfectly psychic hand, and that if my thumb had been even slightly shorter, I would have become a confirmed pessimist and entered a convent.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Oh, I understand!\u2019 said the Duchess, greatly relieved. \u2018He tells fortunes, I suppose?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Misfortunes as well,\u2019 replied Lady Windermere, \u2018and in enormous quantities. Next year, for example, I am in serious danger on both land and sea, so I intend to live in a balloon and have my dinner hauled up to me in a basket every evening. It is all written on my little finger\u2014or perhaps in the palm of my hand; I forget which.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018But surely that is tempting Providence, Gladys.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My dear Duchess, surely Providence is capable of resisting temptation by now. I think everyone ought to have their hands read once a month, simply to learn what they should avoid doing. Naturally, one does it anyway, but being warned is so agreeable. Now, if someone does not fetch Mr. Podgers immediately, I shall have to go myself.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Allow me to go, Lady Windermere,\u2019 said a tall, handsome young man who stood nearby, listening to the conversation with an amused smile.\\n\\n\u2018Thank you very much, Lord Arthur; but I am afraid you would not know him.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018If he is as remarkable as you claim, Lady Windermere, I could hardly fail to find him. Describe him to me, and I will bring him here at once.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, he does not resemble a chiromantist in the least. I mean, he is neither mysterious, nor esoteric, nor romantic in appearance. He is a short, stout man with an amusing bald head and enormous gold-rimmed spectacles\u2014something between a family doctor and a country solicitor. I am truly sorry, but it is not my fault. People are so irritating. All my pianists look precisely like poets, and all my poets look precisely like pianists. I remember last season inviting a most terrible conspirator to dinner, a man who had blown up countless people, always wore a coat of mail, and carried a dagger up his sleeve; and do you know, when he arrived, he looked exactly like a pleasant old clergyman and spent the whole evening telling jokes?\u2019\"}"
