          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ca4b03de6a168043658cbaf7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"To look at him, one might have thought Nemesis had taken Pallas\u2019s shield and revealed the Gorgon\u2019s head to him. He appeared petrified, his face marble-like in its melancholy. He had enjoyed the refined and luxurious existence of a young man born to wealth and position, a life made exquisite by its freedom from vulgar anxieties and its graceful, youthful carelessness; but now, for the first time, he understood the dreadful mystery of Destiny, the terrible significance of Doom.\\n\\nHow insane and monstrous it all appeared! Could it be that upon his hand, in letters he himself could not decipher but another man could read, there lay some dreadful secret of sin, some blood-red mark of guilt? Was escape impossible? Were we merely chessmen, shifted by an invisible force, vessels shaped by the potter\u2019s whim for honour or disgrace? His reason rebelled, yet he felt that tragedy was descending upon him, and that he had suddenly been required to carry an unbearable burden. Actors were fortunate indeed. They could decide whether to enter tragedy or comedy, whether to suffer or rejoice, laugh or weep. Real life was otherwise. Most men and women were compelled to play parts for which they were wholly unsuited. Our Guildensterns perform Hamlet for us, while our Hamlets must jest like Prince Hal. The world is a stage, but the casting is wretched.\\n\\nSuddenly Mr. Podgers came into the room. On seeing Lord Arthur, he started, and his coarse, heavy face turned a kind of greenish yellow. Their eyes met, and for a moment neither spoke.\\n\\n\u2018The Duchess has left one of her gloves here, Lord Arthur, and has sent me to return it to her,\u2019 Mr. Podgers said at last. \u2018Ah, there it is on the sofa! Good evening.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Mr. Podgers, I insist that you give me a direct answer to a question I am about to ask you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Some other time, Lord Arthur, but the Duchess is waiting. I fear I must leave.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018You will not leave. The Duchess can wait.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Ladies ought not to be kept waiting, Lord Arthur,\u2019 said Mr. Podgers, with his sickly smile. \u2018The fair sex is inclined to impatience.\u2019\\n\\nLord Arthur\u2019s finely carved lips twisted with irritated disdain. At that moment the unfortunate Duchess seemed scarcely important to him. He crossed the room to where Mr. Podgers stood and extended his hand.\\n\\n\u2018Tell me what you saw there,\u2019 he said. \u2018Tell me the truth. I must know. I am not a child.\u2019\"}"
