          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"92050f2b66ac2d3022447f13\",\"rewritten_text\":\"How long I remained in this condition, it is impossible to say. It must, however, have been a considerable time, for when I partly recovered my awareness, I found dawn breaking, the balloon at a tremendous height above a wilderness of ocean, and no sign of land anywhere within the immense horizon. Yet my sensations on recovering were by no means as agonizing as might have been expected. Indeed, there was something almost like the beginning of madness in the calm inspection I began to make of my situation. I raised each of my hands to my eyes in turn and wondered what event could have caused the swelling of the veins and the terrible blackness of my fingernails. I then carefully examined my head, shaking it repeatedly and feeling it with close attention, until I managed to convince myself that it was not, as I had half suspected, larger than my balloon. Next, with an air of understanding, I felt in both my trouser pockets and, finding a set of tablets and a toothpick case missing, tried to account for their disappearance. Unable to do so, I felt deeply frustrated. It now occurred to me that I was suffering severe discomfort in the joint of my left ankle, and a faint awareness of my situation began to emerge in my mind. But, strangely enough, I was neither astonished nor horrified. If I felt any emotion at all, it was a sort of amused satisfaction at the cleverness I was about to show in freeing myself from this predicament; and I never for a moment regarded my eventual safety as doubtful. For several minutes I remained absorbed in the deepest thought. I distinctly remember repeatedly pressing my lips together, placing my forefinger beside my nose, and making other gestures and grimaces familiar to men who, comfortably settled in their armchairs, ponder matters of difficulty or importance. Having, as I believed, gathered my thoughts sufficiently, I now, with great caution and deliberation, put my hands behind my back and unfastened the large iron buckle belonging to the waistband of my trousers. The buckle had three prongs, which, being somewhat rusty, turned only with great difficulty on their axes. After some effort, however, I brought them to right angles with the body of the buckle, and was pleased to find that they stayed firmly in that position. Holding the instrument I had made between my teeth, I then began to untie my cravat. I had to pause several times before I could complete this maneuver, but at last I succeeded.\"}"
        }
