          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9690d3b203283c19207bed1f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAnd consider, ma\u2019am,\u201d said Oliver, as tears forced their way into his eyes despite his efforts to hold them back. \u201cOh! Consider how young and good she is, and how much pleasure and comfort she brings to everyone around her. I am sure\u2014certain, completely certain\u2014that, for your sake, since you are so good yourself; for her own sake; and for the sake of all she makes happy, she will not die. Heaven will never let her die so young.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHush!\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing her hand on Oliver\u2019s head. \u201cYou think like a child, poor boy. But you remind me of my duty, nevertheless. I had forgotten it for a moment, Oliver, though I hope I may be forgiven, for I am old and have witnessed enough illness and death to know the agony of being separated from those we love. I have seen enough, too, to know that it is not always the youngest and best who are spared to those who love them; but this should comfort us in our sorrow, for Heaven is just. Such things teach us, with terrible clarity, that there is a brighter world than this, and that the journey to it is swift. God\u2019s will be done! I love her; and He knows how deeply!\u201d\\n\\nOliver was surprised to see that, as Mrs. Maylie spoke these words, she checked her grief as if by a single effort; and, straightening herself as she spoke, became calm and resolute. He was even more astonished to find that this strength endured, and that throughout all the care and watching that followed, Mrs. Maylie was always ready and composed, carrying out every duty that had fallen to her steadily and, to all outward appearances, even cheerfully. But he was young and did not know what strong minds can endure under trying circumstances. How could he, when those who possess them so rarely understand their own strength?\\n\\nAn anxious night followed. When morning came, Mrs. Maylie\u2019s fears had been only too fully confirmed. Rose was in the first stage of a severe and dangerous fever.\\n\\n\u201cWe must act, Oliver, and not surrender to useless grief,\u201d said Mrs. Maylie, placing a finger to her lips as she looked steadily into his face. \u201cThis letter must be sent to Mr. Losberne as quickly as possible. It must be taken to the market town, which is no more than four miles away by the footpath across the fields, and from there sent by a mounted messenger directly to Chertsey. The people at the inn will arrange this, and I know I can trust you to see it done.\u201d\\n\\nOliver could not reply, but his anxious expression showed that he wanted to leave at once.\"}"
