{"request_id":"4a7004c6b7c86ec34dd3a038","rewritten_text":"Each witness compares it—not with the voice of someone from a country whose language he knows, but with the voice of someone whose language he does not know. The Frenchman thinks it was the voice of a Spaniard, and “might have distinguished some words had he been acquainted with the Spanish.” The Dutchman says it was a Frenchman’s voice; but we are told that “not understanding French this witness was examined through an interpreter.” The Englishman believes it was the voice of a German, and “does not understand German.” The Spaniard “is sure” it was an Englishman’s voice, but “judges by the intonation” alone, “as he has no knowledge of the English.” The Italian thinks it was the voice of a Russian, but “has never conversed with a native of Russia.” A second Frenchman also disagrees with the first, insisting that the voice was Italian; yet, not knowing that language, he is, like the Spaniard, “convinced by the intonation.” How strangely unusual, then, must that voice really have been, if testimony like this could be gathered about it—if even in its tones people from the five great regions of Europe could recognize nothing familiar! You may say it could have been the voice of an Asian or an African. Asians and Africans are not common in Paris; but, without rejecting that possibility, I will point out three things. One witness calls the voice “harsh rather than shrill.” Two others describe it as “quick and unequal.” No witness identified any words, or even any sounds resembling words.

“I do not know,” continued Dupin, “what impression I have made on your understanding so far; but I will say without hesitation that the proper conclusions from even this part of the testimony—the part concerning the rough and shrill voices—are enough by themselves to create a suspicion that should guide all further investigation of the mystery. I said ‘proper conclusions,’ but that does not fully express my meaning. I mean that these are the only correct conclusions, and that the suspicion follows from them inevitably as their one result. I will not yet say what that suspicion is. I only want you to remember that, for me, it was strong enough to give my inquiries in the chamber a definite direction.

“Let us now imagine ourselves in that chamber. What should we look for first? The means by which the murderers escaped. It is safe to say that neither of us believes in supernatural events."}

