          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"acc6d016f015a2621f828c3c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Mr. Darcy spoke warmly in praise of his sister\u2019s skill.\\n\\n\u201cI am very pleased to hear so favorable an account of her,\u201d said Lady Catherine; \u201cand pray tell her from me that she cannot hope to excel unless she practises a great deal.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you, madam,\u201d he answered, \u201cthat she has no need of such advice. She practises very regularly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo much the better. One cannot practise too much; and when I next write to her, I shall insist that she neglect it on no account. I often tell young ladies that no musical excellence can be gained without constant practice. I have told Miss Bennet several times that she will never play truly well unless she practises more; and though Mrs. Collins has no instrument, she is quite welcome, as I have often said, to come to Rosings every day and play the pianoforte in Mrs. Jenkinson\u2019s room. She would be in no one\u2019s way there, you know, in that part of the house.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Darcy appeared somewhat embarrassed by his aunt\u2019s lack of manners and made no reply.\\n\\nWhen coffee was finished, Colonel Fitzwilliam reminded Elizabeth that she had promised to play for him, and she immediately sat down at the instrument. He brought a chair close beside her. Lady Catherine listened to half a song, then resumed her conversation with her other nephew, until he moved away from her and, proceeding with his customary deliberation toward the pianoforte, placed himself where he had a full view of the fair performer\u2019s face. Elizabeth noticed his purpose, and at the first suitable pause turned to him with a mischievous smile.\\n\\n\u201cYou intend to frighten me, Mr. Darcy, by coming in such solemn state to hear me. But I shall not be alarmed, though your sister does play so well. I have a stubbornness that cannot endure being frightened at another person\u2019s pleasure. My courage always increases with every effort to intimidate me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI shall not say that you are mistaken,\u201d he replied, \u201cbecause you could not truly suppose that I meant to alarm you; and I have known you long enough to understand that you take great pleasure in sometimes declaring opinions which are not really your own.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth laughed heartily at this portrait of herself, and said to Colonel Fitzwilliam, \u201cYour cousin will give you a very amusing idea of me, and teach you never to believe a word I say. I am especially unfortunate to meet someone so capable of revealing my true character, in a part of the world where I had hoped to present myself with some credit.\u201d\"}"
