          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"64ec38bd8120d525083f637f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At least, that was what he thought. But who could say? ... No. There had been nothing else. He had spared her out of vanity. He had put on the mask of goodness through hypocrisy. For curiosity, he had attempted self-denial. He understood that now.\\n\\nBut this murder\u2014would it pursue him all his life? Was he forever to be weighed down by his past? Was he truly going to confess? Never. There was only one piece of evidence remaining against him. The picture itself\u2014that was evidence. He would destroy it. Why had he kept it for so long? Once, he had taken pleasure in watching it change and grow old. Lately, he had found no pleasure in it. It had kept him awake at night. When he was away, terror had filled him at the thought of other eyes seeing it. It had cast melancholy over his passions. The mere recollection of it had spoiled many joyful moments. It had been like a conscience to him. Yes, it had been his conscience. He would destroy it.\\n\\nHe glanced around and saw the knife with which he had stabbed Basil Hallward. He had cleaned it repeatedly, until not a trace remained upon it. It was bright and gleaming. Just as it had killed the painter, so it would kill the painter\u2019s work, and everything that work signified. It would destroy the past, and once the past was dead, he would be free. It would end this monstrous life of the soul, and without its hideous warnings, he would be at peace. He grasped the knife and drove it into the picture.\\n\\nA cry rang out, followed by a crash. The cry was so terrible in its agony that the frightened servants awoke and crept from their rooms. Two gentlemen passing through the square below stopped and looked up at the great house. They continued on until they encountered a policeman, whom they brought back with them. The man rang the bell several times, but no one answered. Apart from a light in one of the upper windows, the house was completely dark. After a while, he went away and stood beneath a neighboring portico, watching.\\n\\n\u201cWhose house is that, Constable?\u201d asked the older of the two gentlemen.\\n\\n\u201cMr. Dorian Gray\u2019s, sir,\u201d the policeman replied.\\n\\nAs they walked away, they looked at one another and sneered. One of them was Sir Henry Ashton\u2019s uncle.\\n\\nInside, in the servants\u2019 quarters, the half-dressed domestics spoke to one another in hushed whispers. Old Mrs. Leaf wept and wrung her hands. Francis was pale as death.\\n\\nAfter roughly a quarter of an hour, he took the coachman and one of the footmen and crept upstairs.\"}"
