          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"32748ce178f9bc2925fdf084\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But now she wished it had never happened. She supposed he had drunk too freely of Mr. Weston\u2019s excellent wine, and was certain he would be inclined to talk absurdly.\\n\\nTo restrain him as far as her own conduct could, she at once prepared to speak with perfect calm and solemnity about the weather and the night. Yet scarcely had she started, scarcely had they passed the sweep-gate and joined the other carriage, when she found her topic interrupted\u2014her hand taken, her attention claimed, and Mr. Elton actually making passionate love to her: seizing the valuable opportunity, declaring feelings that must already be known, hoping\u2014fearing\u2014adoring\u2014prepared to die if she rejected him; while flattering himself that his fervent attachment, incomparable love, and unprecedented passion could not fail to produce some effect, and, in short, being firmly determined to be accepted seriously as soon as possible. It was truly so. Without hesitation\u2014without apology\u2014and with little visible embarrassment, Mr. Elton, Harriet\u2019s lover, was declaring himself _her_ lover. She attempted to stop him, but without success; he continued, saying everything. Angry though she was, the thought of the situation made her resolve to control herself when she answered. She felt that half this foolishness must be caused by drink, and could therefore hope it might belong only to the present hour. Accordingly, combining seriousness with playfulness, as she hoped would best suit his divided condition, she replied,\\n\\n\u201cI am exceedingly astonished, Mr. Elton. This to _me_! You have forgotten yourself\u2014you mistake me for my friend\u2014any message for Miss Smith I shall gladly deliver; but no more of this to _me_, if you please.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Smith!\u2014a message for Miss Smith!\u2014What could she possibly mean?\u201d\u2014And he repeated her words with such confident