          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f0617b38ce740fdc756cbd70\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dear me, she was a wonderful talker. It would not have been fitting for me to hint that it was very late, that I was tired, and that sleep weighed heavily upon me. I wished I had gone to bed when I had the opportunity. Now I had to endure it; there was no alternative. So she tinkled on and on through the otherwise deep, ghostly silence of the sleeping castle, until presently there arose, seemingly from far beneath us, a distant sound like a muffled shriek\u2014with such agony in it that my flesh crept. The queen paused, and pleasure shone in her eyes; she inclined her graceful head as a bird does when listening. Again the sound forced its way upward through the stillness.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is it?\u201d I asked.\\n\\n\u201cIt is truly a stubborn soul, and endureth long. It is many hours now.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEndureth what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe rack. Come\u2014ye shall see a merry sight. If he yield not his secret now, ye shall see him torn apart.\u201d\\n\\nWhat a smooth-tongued fiend she was; and how calm and tranquil, while the cords running down my legs ached in sympathy with that man's suffering. Led by armored guards carrying blazing torches, we marched through echoing passages and down damp, dripping stone stairs that smelled of mold and centuries of captive darkness\u2014a cold, eerie journey, and a lengthy one, made neither shorter nor more pleasant by the sorceress's account of the prisoner and his offense. An anonymous informer had accused him of killing a stag in the royal preserves. I said:\\n\\n\u201cAnonymous testimony is not quite the proper thing, your Highness. It would be fairer to confront the accused with his accuser.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI had not considered it, since it seemed a matter of little importance. But if I wished to do so, I could not, because the accuser came masked by night, told the forester, and immediately departed again, so that the forester knoweth him not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen was this Unknown the only person who saw the stag killed?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMarry, _no_ man _saw_ the killing, but this Unknown saw this bold wretch near the place where the stag lay, and came with most loyal zeal and betrayed him to the forester.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSo the Unknown was near the dead stag as well? Is it not possible that he killed it himself? His loyal zeal\u2014in a mask\u2014seems somewhat suspicious. But what does your Highness hope to gain by racking the prisoner? Where is the benefit?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe will not confess otherwise; and then his soul would be lost. By law his life is forfeited for the crime\u2014and surely will I see that he pay it!\u2014but it would endanger my own soul to let him die without confession and absolution.\"}"
