          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e2ccd8e25c6d7b4558df61ad\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Dawn had arrived when I put the Manuscript away. The rain was nearly over; the world looked gray and mournful, while the spent storm sighed and sobbed itself into rest. I went to the stranger\u2019s room and listened at his door, which stood slightly open. I could hear his voice, and knocked. No answer came, though the voice continued. I peered inside. The man lay on his back in bed, speaking in broken phrases but with animation, emphasizing them with his arms, which he flung about restlessly, as the sick do in delirium. I entered quietly and leaned over him. His mutterings and exclamations continued. I spoke\u2014only a word, to attract his notice. At once his glassy eyes and ashen face brightened with pleasure, gratitude, joy, and welcome:\\n\\n\u201cOh, Sandy, you have come at last\u2014how I have yearned for you! Sit beside me\u2014do not go\u2014never leave me again, Sandy, never again. Where is your hand?\u2014give it to me, dear, let me hold it\u2014there\u2014now everything is well, everything is peaceful, and I am happy again\u2014we are happy again, are we not, Sandy? You are so indistinct, so shadowy, merely a mist, a cloud, but you are here, and that is blessedness enough; and I have your hand; do not withdraw it\u2014it will be only for a little while, and I shall not need it long.... Was that the child?... Hello\u2014Central!... she does not answer. Perhaps she is asleep? Bring her when she wakes, and let me touch her hands, her face, her hair, and say good-bye.... Sandy! Yes, you are there. I lost myself for a moment and thought you had gone.... Have I been ill long? It must be so; it seems months to me. And such dreams! such strange and terrible dreams, Sandy! Dreams as real as reality\u2014delirium, of course, but so real! Why, I thought the king was dead, that you were in Gaul and unable to return, that there had been a revolution; in the wild frenzy of those dreams, I thought Clarence and I and a small band of my cadets fought and destroyed all the chivalry of England! Yet even that was not the strangest. I seemed to be a being from some distant unborn age, centuries ahead, and even that felt as real as everything else! Yes, I seemed to have flown backward from that age into our own, then forward to it once more, and to have been left, a stranger and forsaken, in that strange England, with an abyss thirteen centuries wide gaping between you and me! between me and my home and my friends! between me and everything dear to me, everything that could make life worth living!\"}"
