          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e55bee03d4940b18cf3e0a9a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"My father\u2019s family name was Pirrip, and my Christian name was Philip. But my baby tongue could not say either name properly, so I called myself Pip, and everyone else came to call me Pip.\\n\\nI know Pirrip was my father\u2019s family name because it was written on his tombstone, and because my sister, Mrs. Joe Gargery, told me so. She was married to the blacksmith. I never saw my father or mother, and never saw a picture of either of them, since they lived before photographs existed. So my first ideas about their appearance came foolishly from their tombstones. The shape of the letters on my father\u2019s stone made me imagine him as a short, broad, dark man with curly black hair. From the wording and style of the inscription, \u201c_Also Georgiana Wife of the Above_,\u201d I decided, as a child, that my mother had freckles and poor health. Beside their grave stood five small stone markers, each about a foot and a half long, arranged in a neat row. They honored my five little brothers, who had given up trying to survive in this world very early. Because of them, I firmly believed that they had all been born lying on their backs with their hands in their trouser pockets, and had never removed them during their lives.\\n\\nWe lived in the marsh country, beside the river and, following its winding course, twenty miles from the sea. My first clear and powerful understanding of the world came on a cold, raw afternoon near evening. Then I learned for certain that this bleak place, covered with nettles, was the churchyard; that Philip Pirrip, formerly of this parish, and Georgiana, his wife, were dead and buried; that Alexander, Bartholomew, Abraham, Tobias, and Roger, their infant children, were also dead and buried; and that the dark, flat wilderness beyond the churchyard, crossed by ditches, raised banks, and gates, with cattle scattered across it, was the marshes. I learned that the low, dull line in the distance was the river; that the wild, distant place from which the wind came rushing was the sea; and that the small, shivering child who was growing frightened and beginning to cry was Pip.\\n\\n\u201cBe quiet!\u201d shouted a terrible voice, as a man rose among the graves beside the church porch. \u201cStay still, you little devil, or I\u2019ll cut your throat!\u201d\\n\\nHe was a frightening man dressed all in rough gray clothes, with a large iron shackle on his leg.\"}"
