          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"af336f07c9b8b95f279315b5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was plenty of cold water with which to end the feast. After a while Becky proposed that they go on again. Tom remained quiet for a moment, then said:\\n\\n\u201cBecky, can you stand it if I tell you something?\u201d\\n\\nBecky\u2019s face grew pale, but she believed she could.\\n\\n\u201cWell, then, Becky, we have to stay here, where there\u2019s water to drink. That small piece is our last candle!\u201d\\n\\nBecky burst into tears and lamentations. Tom tried to comfort her, but with little success. At last Becky said:\\n\\n\u201cTom!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, Becky?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ll miss us and search for us!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, they will! Of course they will!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps they\u2019re looking for us now, Tom.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, I suppose they may be. I hope they are.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen will they miss us, Tom?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhen they return to the boat, I suppose.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTom, it may be dark by then\u2014would they notice that we hadn\u2019t come?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t know. But your mother would miss you as soon as they reached home.\u201d\\n\\nThe frightened expression on Becky\u2019s face brought Tom back to his senses, and he realized he had blundered. Becky was not supposed to have gone home that night! The children fell silent and thoughtful. Soon another outburst of grief from Becky showed Tom that the thought in his own mind had entered hers as well\u2014that Sunday morning might be half over before Mrs. Thatcher learned that Becky was not at Mrs. Harper\u2019s.\\n\\nThe children fixed their eyes on their little piece of candle and watched it slowly and mercilessly melt away; saw the last half-inch of wick stand by itself; saw the weak flame rise and sink, climb the slender pillar of smoke, pause at its top for a moment, and then\u2014the terror of complete darkness descended!\\n\\nHow much later it was when Becky slowly became aware that she was weeping in Tom\u2019s arms, neither of them could say. They knew only that, after what seemed an immense span of time, both awoke from a deathlike stupor of sleep and returned once more to their misery. Tom said it might be Sunday now\u2014perhaps Monday. He tried to make Becky talk, but her sorrow weighed too heavily; all her hope had vanished. Tom said they must have been missed long before, and surely the search was continuing. He would shout, and perhaps someone would come. He tried, but in the darkness the far-off echoes sounded so dreadful that he did not try again.\\n\\nThe hours slipped away, and hunger came to torment the captives once more. A piece of Tom\u2019s half of the cake remained; they divided it and ate. Yet they seemed hungrier than before.\"}"
