          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3642342bb4b9ce779cec0ed6\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Meanwhile, the force of gravity would steadily decrease according to the square of the distance. As my speed increased enormously, I would eventually reach those distant regions where the moon\u2019s attraction replaced the earth\u2019s. For that reason, I did not think it necessary to carry more provisions than would last forty days.\\n\\n\u201cThere was, however, another difficulty that caused me some concern. It has been observed that during balloon ascents to great heights, people suffer not only pain when breathing but also severe discomfort in the head and body, often with nosebleeds and other alarming symptoms. These troubles become worse as the altitude increases. This was a disturbing thought. Was it not likely that the symptoms would continue to grow, perhaps until death ended them? In the end, I decided that this was not so. They seemed to result from the gradual removal of the usual atmospheric pressure from the body\u2019s surface, which caused the small blood vessels near the skin to expand. They did not arise from any actual breakdown of the body, as breathing difficulties do when the air is too thin to renew the blood properly in one of the heart\u2019s chambers. If the blood could still be renewed, I saw no reason why life could not continue even in a vacuum. The expansion and contraction of the chest, commonly called breathing, is purely muscular action, and is the cause rather than the result of respiration. In short, I believed that as the body grew used to the lack of atmospheric pressure, the painful sensations would gradually lessen. Until then, I trusted in the iron strength of my constitution to endure them.\\n\\n\u201cThus, Your Excellencies, I have described some, though certainly not all, of the thoughts that led me to plan a journey to the moon. I shall now tell you what happened when I attempted a voyage so daring in its design and, in any case, entirely without precedent in human history.\\n\\n\u201cAfter reaching the altitude I mentioned earlier\u2014three miles and three-quarters\u2014I threw a quantity of feathers out of the car and found that I was still rising fast enough. There was therefore no need to release any ballast. I was pleased, because I wanted to keep as much weight with me as possible, for reasons that will be explained later.\"}"
