          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9c02a74a44a997ff6c8359e5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I now turn to my own history. There is little to tell of the earlier part of my life, and I shall pass over it quickly; yet I must linger somewhat upon my childhood, so that it may be understood how, when one hope failed, all existence would have become a blank; and how, when the sole affection I was allowed to cherish was destroyed, my life was extinguished along with it.\\n\\nI have already said that my aunt differed greatly from my father. I believe that, without the least trace of an evil heart, she possessed the coldest that ever occupied a human breast: it was wholly incapable of affection. She took charge of me because she believed it her duty; but having lived too long alone, undisturbed by the noise and prattle of children, she could not permit me to interrupt her tranquillity. She had never married; and for the preceding five years had lived in complete solitude upon an estate inherited through her mother, on the shores of Loch Lomond in Scotland. In his letters my father had expressed the wish that she would live with me at his family residence, situated in a beautiful district near Richmond in Yorkshire. She refused this proposal; but once she had settled the affairs which her brother\u2019s departure had placed in her hands, she left England and carried me with her to her Scottish estate.\\n\\nThe care of me as an infant, and afterward until I had reached my eighth year, fell to a servant of my mother\u2019s who had accompanied us into retirement for that purpose. I was lodged in a distant part of the house and saw my aunt only at appointed times. This happened twice each day: once, around noon, she came to my nursery; and once, after dinner, I was brought to her. She never embraced me, and during the whole time I remained in the room seemed afraid that some childish prank might trouble her. My excellent nurse always instructed me with the utmost care before allowing me to enter the parlour\u2014and the dread inspired by my aunt\u2019s cold looks and few, constrained words was so strong that I rarely failed in her lessons or departed from the exemplary quiet I had been taught to maintain during these brief visits.\\n\\nUnder my good nurse\u2019s care I roamed freely through our park and the neighbouring fields. As the child of the deepest love, I showed from my earliest years the greatest tenderness of feeling. I cannot express the passion with which I loved everything, even the lifeless objects that surrounded me.\"}"
