{"request_id":"4891025a5a46f03eb29ca83f","rewritten_text":"Mrs Musgrove took the first chance to be alone with Anne and said, “Oh, Miss Anne, I cannot help wishing Mrs Charles had some of your way with those children. They are completely different with you! But, of course, they are generally so spoiled. It is a pity you cannot teach your sister how to manage them. They are as fine, healthy children as anyone could see, poor little dears—without any bias on my part; but Mrs Charles knows no more how to treat them—! Good heavens, how troublesome they sometimes are. I assure you, Miss Anne, it keeps me from wanting them at our house as often as I otherwise would. I believe Mrs Charles is not pleased that I do not invite them more often; but you know it is very unpleasant to have children who must be corrected every moment—‘don’t do this,’ and ‘don’t do that’—or who can only be kept reasonably orderly with more cake than is good for them.”

Mary, meanwhile, had told Anne this: “Mrs Musgrove believes all her servants are so reliable that questioning it would be almost treason; but I am sure, without exaggerating, that her upper housemaid and laundrymaid spend the whole day wandering around the village instead of doing their work. I meet them wherever I go; and I declare, I never enter my nursery twice without seeing them. If Jemima were not the most trustworthy, steady creature in the world, it would be enough to corrupt her; for she tells me they are always trying to persuade her to go walking with them.” From Mrs Musgrove, on the other hand, Anne heard: “I make it a rule never to interfere in any of my daughter-in-law’s affairs, because I know it would not do; but I shall tell you, Miss Anne, since you may be able to put matters right, that I have a poor opinion of Mrs Charles’s nurserymaid. I hear strange things about her; she is always out and about; and from my own knowledge I can say she dresses so finely that she is likely to spoil any servants she comes near. Mrs Charles certainly swears by her, I know; but I give you this warning so that you may keep watch. If you notice anything wrong, you need not hesitate to mention it.”

Again, Mary complained that Mrs Musgrove often failed to give her the precedence she deserved when they dined at the Great House with other families. She could not see why she should be treated as so much at home that she lost her proper place."}

