{"request_id":"5f6d8aa315dc2a08e3a39334","rewritten_text":"I encountered several funerals; they were thinly attended by mourners, and the spectators considered them omens of the gravest kind. Some watched the processions with frantic eagerness—others timidly ran away—while some wept aloud.

After providing immediate aid to the sick, Adrian’s principal effort had been to conceal the plague’s symptoms and advance from London’s inhabitants. He knew that fear and gloomy forebodings greatly aided disease, and that despondency and brooding anxiety made the human constitution especially open to infection. Thus no indecorous sights were visible: the shops were mostly open, and the flow of passengers was maintained to some extent. Yet, though the aspect of an infected city had been avoided, London seemed to me, who had not seen it since the visitation began, profoundly altered. No carriages appeared, and grass had grown tall in the streets; the houses looked desolate, most shutters were closed, and the people I encountered wore a ghastly, frightened expression, far removed from the Londoners’ customary businesslike manner. My solitary carriage drew attention as it rattled toward the Protectoral Palace—and the fashionable streets leading there looked still more dreary and deserted. I found Adrian’s antechamber crowded, for it was his hour of audience. Unwilling to interrupt his work, I waited, observing the petitioners enter and depart. They were drawn from the middle and lower classes, whose livelihoods had vanished with the suspension of trade and of the energetic money-making spirit that marked our country in every branch. The newcomers bore an anxious, sometimes terrified air, sharply contrasting with the resigned, even contented appearance of those who had been heard. I could discern my friend’s influence in their brisker movements and brightened faces. Two o’clock sounded, and thereafter no one was admitted; the disappointed departed sullenly or sorrowfully, while I entered the audience chamber.

I was impressed by the improvement in Adrian’s health. He was no longer bowed to the earth, like an over-tended spring flower that, rising beyond its strength, is burdened even by its own crown of blossoms. His eyes were bright, his expression calm, and an atmosphere of concentrated energy pervaded his whole person, replacing its former languor. He sat at a table with several secretaries, who sorted petitions or recorded notes taken during that day’s audience. Two or three petitioners still waited. I admired his justice and patience. Those able to live outside London he immediately advised to leave it, providing them with the means to do so."}

