          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6459c79c4192a2253bb45015\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The party was divided between two adjoining benches. Anne sat on the front one, and Mr Elliot had managed, with help from his friend Colonel Wallis, to secure a seat beside her. Miss Elliot, surrounded by her cousins and the main focus of Colonel Wallis\u2019s attention, was perfectly satisfied.\\n\\nAnne was in an ideal mood for the evening\u2019s entertainment. It gave her just enough to occupy her: she had feeling for the tender music, energy for the lively, attention for the scientific, and patience for the dull. She had never enjoyed a concert more, at least during the first act. Near its end, during the interval after an Italian song, she explained the song\u2019s words to Mr Elliot. A concert programme lay between them.\\n\\n\u201cThis,\u201d she said, \u201cis almost the meaning\u2014or rather, the general idea of the words. One must not really speak of the meaning of an Italian love song. Still, this is as close as I can come, since I do not claim to understand the language. I am a very poor Italian scholar.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, yes, I can see that. I can see you know nothing about it. You know only enough of the language to translate, at sight, these inverted, rearranged, shortened Italian lines into clear, understandable, elegant English. You need say no more about your ignorance. Here is complete proof.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI will not argue with such generous politeness; but I should be sorry to be tested by a true expert.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have not had the pleasure of visiting Camden Place for so long,\u201d he replied, \u201cwithout learning something about Miss Anne Elliot. I consider her a woman too modest for the world to know even half her accomplishments, and too accomplished for such modesty to be natural in any other woman.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFor shame! For shame! That is far too much flattery. I have forgotten what comes next,\u201d she said, turning to the programme.\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps,\u201d Mr Elliot said quietly, \u201cI have known your character longer than you realize.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed? How could that be? You can have known it only since I came to Bath, unless you heard me spoken of earlier by my own family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI knew of you by reputation long before you came to Bath. I had heard you described by people who knew you well. I have known your character for many years. Your appearance, your nature, your accomplishments, your manner\u2014they were all familiar to me.\u201d\\n\\nMr Elliot had achieved the interest he hoped to awaken. No one can resist the attraction of such a mystery.\"}"
