          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"3d38837f751276944bb338b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The doctor was an old man\u2014a very pleasant, kind-looking old man when I woke him. I told him that my brother and I had been hunting on Spanish Island yesterday afternoon, had camped on part of a raft we found, and that around midnight my brother must have kicked his gun in his sleep, because it went off and shot him in the leg. We wanted the doctor to go there, fix it, and keep quiet about it, because we planned to come home that evening and surprise the family.\\n\\n\u201cWho is your family?\u201d he asked.\\n\\n\u201cThe Phelpses, down yonder.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he said. After a moment, he asked, \u201cHow did you say he got shot?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe had a dream,\u201d I said, \u201cand it shot him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cStrange dream,\u201d he said.\\n\\nThen he lit his lantern, got his saddlebags, and we started. But when he saw the canoe, he did not like the look of it. He said it was big enough for one person, but did not seem safe for two. I said:\\n\\n\u201cOh, you needn\u2019t be afraid, sir. It carried the three of us easily enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat three?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy, me and Sid, and\u2014and\u2014and the guns; that\u2019s what I mean.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d he said.\\n\\nBut he put his foot on the gunwale, rocked the canoe, shook his head, and said he thought he would look for a bigger one. They were all locked and chained, though, so he took my canoe and told me to wait until he returned. He said I could search farther, or perhaps go home and get everyone ready for the surprise, if I wanted to. But I said I did not. I told him exactly how to find the raft, and then he left.\\n\\nI had an idea soon afterward. I said to myself, suppose he cannot fix that leg in three shakes of a sheep\u2019s tail, as the saying goes? Suppose it takes him three or four days? What will we do\u2014stay there until he gives everything away? No, sir; I knew what I would do. I would wait, and when he came back, if he said he had to go anywhere else, I would get down there too, even if I had to swim. We would tie him up, keep him there, and push off down the river. When Tom was finished with him, we would pay him whatever it was worth\u2014or all the money we had\u2014and then let him go ashore.\\n\\nSo I crawled into a lumber pile to sleep. The next time I woke, the sun was high overhead. I rushed out and went to the doctor\u2019s house, but they told me he had left sometime during the night and had not returned yet.\"}"
