          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e12647f09dcc4815df9291e7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At last, \u201cCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay,\u201d was brought before the court.\\n\\nHis judges sat on the Bench wearing feathered hats, but the rough red cap and tricolour cockade were the headwear seen almost everywhere else. Looking at the Jury and the unruly crowd, he might have thought the usual order of things had been turned upside down, and that criminals were judging honest men. The lowest, cruelest, and worst people in the city\u2014never lacking in such people\u2014controlled the scene. They shouted comments, applauded, objected, guessed the outcome, and pushed it forward without restraint. Most of the men carried weapons of different kinds. Some of the women wore knives or daggers; some ate and drank as they watched, and many knitted. One of these knitters had an extra piece of work tucked under her arm. She sat in the front row beside a man Darnay had not seen since arriving at the Barrier, but whom he immediately recognized as Defarge. He noticed her whispering to him once or twice, and she seemed to be his wife. Yet what struck him most was that, although they were as close to him as possible, neither ever looked at him. They appeared to be waiting for something with stubborn determination. They watched the Jury, and nothing else. Beneath the President sat Doctor Manette, wearing his usual quiet clothes. As far as the prisoner could see, he and Mr. Lorry were the only men there who were not connected with the Tribunal and who still wore their ordinary clothing instead of the rough dress of the Carmagnole.\\n\\nCharles Evr\u00e9monde, called Darnay, was accused by the public prosecutor of being an emigrant, whose life was forfeited to the Republic under the decree that banished all emigrants on pain of death. It did not matter that the decree had been dated after his return to France. He was there, and the decree was there; he had been captured in France, and his head was demanded.\\n\\n\u201cTake off his head!\u201d cried the crowd. \u201cAn enemy of the Republic!\u201d\\n\\nThe President rang his bell to silence them, then asked the prisoner whether it was not true that he had lived for many years in England.\\n\\nIt was certainly true.\\n\\nThen was he not an emigrant? What did he call himself?\\n\\nNot an emigrant, he hoped, according to the meaning and spirit of the law.\\n\\nWhy not? the President asked.\\n\\nBecause he had willingly given up a title he disliked and a position he disliked, and had left his country\u2014he submitted, before the Tribunal began using the word emigrant in its present meaning\u2014to live by his own work in England rather than by the labor of France\u2019s overburdened people.\"}"
