          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e294252bf12464d492116528\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u2018Good heavens!\u2019 the Duchess said to herself. \u2018So he really is some kind of chiropodist after all. How dreadful. I hope he is at least a foreigner. That would make it a little less bad.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I must introduce him to you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Introduce him!\u2019 cried the Duchess. \u2018You don\u2019t mean to say he is here?\u2019 She began searching for a small tortoiseshell fan and a very worn lace shawl, so she could leave at a moment\u2019s notice.\\n\\n\u2018Of course he is here. I would never give a party without him. He says I have a purely psychic hand, and that if my thumb had been even slightly shorter, I would have become a complete pessimist and entered a convent.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Oh, I see!\u2019 said the Duchess, greatly relieved. \u2018He tells fortunes, I suppose?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018And misfortunes too,\u2019 Lady Windermere replied, \u2018and plenty of them. Next year, for example, I am in great danger both on land and at sea, so I am going to live in a balloon and have my dinner lifted up to me in a basket every evening. It is all written on my little finger\u2014or on the palm of my hand; I forget which.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018But surely that is tempting Providence, Gladys.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My dear Duchess, surely Providence can resist temptation by now. I think everyone should have their hands read once a month, so they know what not to do. Of course, one does it anyway, but it is so pleasant to be warned. Now, if someone does not fetch Mr. Podgers at once, I shall have to go myself.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Let me go, Lady Windermere,\u2019 said a tall, handsome young man who stood nearby, listening with an amused smile.\\n\\n\u2018Thank you so much, Lord Arthur; but I am afraid you would not recognise him.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018If he is as wonderful as you say, Lady Windermere, I could hardly miss him. Tell me what he looks like, and I will bring him to you at once.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, he does not look like a chiromantist at all. I mean, he is not mysterious, secretive, or romantic-looking. He is a short, stout man with a funny bald head and large gold-rimmed spectacles\u2014something between a family doctor and a country lawyer. I am really sorry, but it is not my fault. People are so annoying. All my pianists look exactly like poets, and all my poets look exactly like pianists. I remember last season inviting a terrible conspirator to dinner, a man who had blown up countless people, always wore a coat of mail, and carried a dagger up his sleeve. And do you know, when he arrived, he looked just like a pleasant old clergyman and told jokes all evening?\u2019\"}"
