{"request_id":"817640a2307633dff2ce1280","rewritten_text":"This being done, a few more loops were released from the rim, another section of the cloth was brought forward, and the freed loops fastened to their appropriate buttons. By this means, the entire upper portion of the bag could be placed between the netting and the hoop. It was clear that the hoop would now sink inside the car, while the whole weight of the car, together with everything in it, would be supported solely by the strength of the buttons. At first this might appear an insufficient reliance; yet it was not so, since the buttons were themselves exceedingly strong, and were set so near one another that each bore only a very small share of the total weight. In fact, even if the car and its contents had weighed three times as much, I should have felt no concern. I then lifted the hoop once more inside the gum-elastic covering, and held it at nearly its former elevation with three light poles prepared for the purpose. This was done, naturally, to keep the bag expanded at the top and maintain the lower portion of the netting in its proper position. Nothing now remained except to close the mouth of the enclosure; this was easily done by collecting the folds of the material and twisting them very tightly inward with a sort of fixed tourniquet.

“In the sides of the covering, thus arranged around the car, three circular panes of thick yet transparent glass had been set, through which I could readily see in every horizontal direction. In the section of cloth forming the bottom there was also a fourth window of the same kind, aligned with a small opening in the floor of the car itself. This allowed me to look directly downward; but, having found it impossible to install a similar device above, because of the peculiar method used to close that opening and the resulting folds in the cloth, I could expect to see nothing lying directly overhead. This was of little importance, of course, since even had I been able to put a window there, the balloon itself would have made it useless.

“About a foot beneath one of the side windows was a circular opening eight inches across, furnished with a brass rim whose inner edge was shaped to receive the turns of a screw. The broad tube of the condenser was screwed into this rim, while the body of the apparatus lay, naturally, within the gum-elastic chamber.”"}

