          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2a70b89797b0a67e12e0a936\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I walked quickly, and we soon reached my college. Then I remembered, with a shudder, that the creature I had left in my apartment might still be there, alive and moving about. I dreaded seeing the monster, but I feared even more that Henry might see him. I begged him to wait a few minutes at the bottom of the stairs, then rushed toward my room. My hand was already on the lock before I stopped myself. A cold shiver passed over me. I threw the door open, as children do when they expect a ghost to be waiting on the other side, but nothing appeared. I stepped inside cautiously. The apartment was empty, and my bedroom was also free of its horrible guest. I could hardly believe such good fortune, but once I was certain that my enemy had fled, I clapped my hands with joy and ran back down to Clerval.\\n\\nWe went up to my room, and the servant soon brought breakfast. But I could not control myself. I felt more than joy; my whole body tingled with nervous excitement, and my pulse raced. I could not stay still for even a moment. I jumped over the chairs, clapped my hands, and laughed loudly. At first Clerval thought my unusual excitement came from happiness at seeing him. But when he looked at me more closely, he saw a wildness in my eyes that he could not explain. My loud, uncontrolled, empty laughter frightened and amazed him.\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\u201d he cried, \u201cwhat on earth is wrong? Do not laugh like that. You are very ill! What has caused this?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDo not ask me,\u201d I cried, covering my eyes with my hands, because I thought I saw the dreaded spectre enter the room. \u201c_He_ can tell you. Oh, save me! Save me!\u201d I imagined that the monster had seized me. I struggled violently and fell unconscious.\\n\\nPoor Clerval! What must he have felt? A meeting he had expected to be so joyful had turned so strangely bitter. But I did not witness his grief, for I lay senseless and did not regain consciousness for a very long time.\\n\\nThis was the beginning of a nervous fever that confined me for several months. Throughout that time, Henry was my only nurse. I later learned that, knowing my father was too old and unfit for such a long journey, and knowing how terribly my illness would distress Elizabeth, he spared them that suffering by hiding how serious my condition was.\"}"
