{"request_id":"8d1245fb936891a0b2fd0eed","rewritten_text":"“But, in fact, once we have risen to any given height, the amount of air passed through during any further ascent is not at all proportional to the extra height climbed, as what I have already said clearly shows. Instead, the proportion continually decreases. It is therefore clear that, no matter how high we rise, we cannot literally reach a boundary beyond which there is no atmosphere. I argued that it must continue to exist, even if only in a state of infinite thinness.

“On the other hand, I knew that many arguments had been offered to prove that the atmosphere has a real, definite boundary, beyond which there is absolutely no air. Yet those who defended such a boundary seemed to me to have overlooked a fact which, though it did not positively disprove their belief, deserved very serious investigation. When the intervals between Encke’s comet’s successive returns to perihelion are compared, after allowing as accurately as possible for all disturbances caused by the planets, the periods appear to grow shorter. In other words, the major axis of the comet’s orbit is slowly but steadily decreasing. This is exactly what we should expect if the comet encountered resistance from an extremely thin ether filling the regions of its orbit. Such a medium, by slowing the comet, would increase its centripetal force by weakening its centrifugal force. In other words, the sun’s attraction would continually become stronger, and the comet would be drawn closer to it with every revolution. Indeed, there is no other way to explain this change.

“But again: the actual diameter of the same comet’s nebulous cloud is observed to shrink rapidly as it approaches the sun, and to expand just as rapidly as it travels away toward its aphelion. Was I not justified, with M. Valz, in supposing that this apparent change in volume was caused by the compression of the same ether I mentioned earlier, which becomes denser as it nears the sun? The lens-shaped phenomenon also called the zodiacal light deserved attention. This glow, so visible in the tropics and impossible to mistake for meteoric light, rises obliquely from the horizon and generally follows the direction of the sun’s equator. It seemed clearly to me to be a thin atmosphere extending outward from the sun, at least beyond Venus’s orbit, and perhaps much farther. Indeed, I could not suppose that this medium was limited to the comet’s orbital path or to the sun’s immediate neighborhood.”"}

