          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"a1d4d5474fb9c511cc440788\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wish we had a donkey. We could all come on donkeys\u2014Jane, Miss Bates, and me\u2014with my caro sposo walking beside us. I really must speak to him about buying one. In country life, I consider it almost necessary; for however many resources a woman may have, she cannot always be confined at home. And there are such long walks, you know\u2014in summer, dust, and in winter, mud.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will find neither between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and now it is perfectly dry. Come on a donkey, if you prefer it. You can borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I would like everything to suit you as much as possible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat I am sure you would. Indeed, I do you justice, my good friend. Beneath that particular dry, blunt manner, I know you have the warmest heart. As I tell Mr. E., you are a complete humorist. Yes, believe me, Knightley, I am fully aware of your consideration for me throughout this whole plan. You have found exactly the thing to please me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding a table in the shade. He wanted to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the party; and he knew that having any of them sit outdoors to eat would inevitably make him ill. Mr. Woodhouse must not be lured away to his misery under the plausible excuse of a morning drive and an hour or two at Donwell.\\n\\nHe was invited sincerely. No hidden dangers waited to reproach him for his trusting acceptance. He did agree. He had not been to Donwell for two years. \u201cSome very fine morning, he, Emma, and Harriet could go perfectly well; and he could sit quietly with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls walked around the gardens. He did not suppose they could be damp now, in the middle of the day. He would be extremely pleased to see the old house again, and very happy to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton and any other neighbors. He could see no objection at all to his, Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there some very fine morning. He thought it very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014very kind and sensible\u2014much cleverer than dining out. He was not fond of dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was fortunate in receiving everyone\u2019s immediate agreement. The invitation was welcomed so warmly that it seemed, like Mrs. Elton, they all regarded the plan as a special compliment to themselves. Emma and Harriet expressed the highest expectations of pleasure from it; and Mr. Weston, without being asked, promised to bring Frank to join them if possible\u2014a display of approval and gratitude that could have been spared. Mr. Knightley was therefore obliged to say that he would be glad to see him; and Mr. Weston promised to write at once and use every argument to persuade him to come.\"}"
