          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"38f7c6b1929b89365da18842\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, however, the lieutenant asked why he had ventured so far across the ice in such an unusual vehicle.\\n\\nHis face immediately took on an expression of profound gloom, and he answered, \u201cTo find one who escaped from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did the man you were following travel in the same manner?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I believe we have seen him, for the day before we rescued you, we observed some dogs pulling a sledge, with a man seated in it, over the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThis caught the stranger\u2019s attention, and he asked many questions about the course which the d\u00e6mon, as he called him, had taken. Shortly afterward, when he was alone with me, he said, \u201cI have surely aroused your curiosity, as I have that of these good people; but you are too thoughtful to question me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly; it would be exceedingly impertinent and inhuman of me to trouble you with my curiosity.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet you saved me from a strange and dangerous situation; you have kindly brought me back to life.\u201d\\n\\nSoon afterward he asked whether I believed the breaking ice had destroyed the other sledge. I replied that I could not say with certainty, since the ice had not broken until nearly midnight, and the traveller might have reached safety before then; but I had no means of knowing.\\n\\nFrom that moment, a renewed spirit of life seemed to animate the stranger\u2019s failing body. He showed the greatest eagerness to go upon deck and watch for the sledge that had appeared before; but I persuaded him to stay in the cabin, as he is far too weak to endure the raw air. I promised that someone would keep watch for him and immediately inform him if any new object came into view.\\n\\nThis is my journal, my dear Margaret, of everything connected with this strange event up to the present time. The stranger has gradually regained his health, but remains very quiet and seems uneasy whenever anyone besides myself enters his cabin. Still, his manner is so gentle and conciliating that all the sailors are interested in him, though they have spoken with him very little. For my part, I am beginning to love him like a brother, and his continual, profound sorrow fills me with sympathy and compassion. He must have been a noble being in happier days, for even now, amid his ruin, he is so attractive and amiable.\\n\\nI said in one of my letters, my dear Margaret, that I would find no friend upon the broad ocean; yet I have found a man whom, before misery had broken his spirit, I would gladly have embraced as the brother of my heart.\"}"
