          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9d93e662f12dffc1700c6137\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Weeks passed, and I became more and more absorbed in you. Then something changed. I had painted you as Paris in delicate armor, and as Adonis wearing a hunter\u2019s cloak and carrying a polished boar spear. Crowned with heavy lotus blossoms, you had sat at the front of Adrian\u2019s barge, looking across the green, muddy Nile. You had leaned over the still pool of a Greek woodland and seen, in its quiet silver surface, the wonder of your own face. And it had all been what art should be\u2014unconscious, ideal, and distant. One day, a day I sometimes think was fatal, I decided to paint a wonderful portrait of you as you truly were, not in the clothing of ancient times, but in your own clothes and in your own age. I cannot say whether it was the realism of the method, or simply the power of your personality, shown to me directly and without mist or veil. But as I worked, every flake and layer of paint seemed to reveal my secret. I became afraid that others would discover my worship of you. I felt, Dorian, that I had said too much, that I had put too much of myself into the painting. So I decided never to let it be exhibited. You were a little annoyed, but you did not understand what it meant to me. Harry, whom I told about it, laughed at me. I did not mind. When the picture was finished and I sat alone with it, I felt that I had been right.... Well, after a few days it left my studio. Once I had escaped the unbearable fascination of its presence, I began to think I had been foolish to imagine I had seen anything in it beyond the fact that you were extremely handsome and that I could paint. Even now, I cannot help thinking it is a mistake to believe that the passion felt during creation is truly shown in the work created. Art is always more abstract than we imagine. Form and color tell us about form and color\u2014that is all. It often seems to me that art hides the artist far more completely than it reveals him. So when I received this offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the main work in my exhibition. It never occurred to me that you would refuse. I see now that you were right. The picture cannot be shown. You must not be angry with me, Dorian, for telling you all this.\"}"
