          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"80ab970d339574bded3ac28b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The powerful voice\u2014almost mechanical, we might call it, since we could not connect it with any pipe or keyboard\u2014fell silent. The girl turned to help her aged companion and finally noticed us.\\n\\nHe was her father, and since childhood she had guided his blind steps. They were Germans from Saxony who had emigrated only a few years earlier and formed new friendships with the nearby villagers. Around the time the plague began, a young German student had joined them. Their simple story was easy to understand. He, a nobleman, loved the poor musician\u2019s beautiful daughter and followed them while fleeing persecution from his family. But soon the great equalizer came, its sharp scythe cutting down the tall flowers of the field along with the grass. The young man was one of its first victims. She kept herself alive for her father\u2019s sake. Because he was blind, she could continue a deception that had begun by accident. Now, as the only survivors in the country, he did not know what had happened. He was unaware that when he listened to his daughter\u2019s music, the silent mountains, lifeless lake, and unfeeling trees were, apart from himself, her only listeners.\\n\\nOn the very day we arrived, she had been struck by the illness. She was horrified by the thought of leaving her old, blind father alone on the empty earth, but she could not bring herself to tell him the truth. The depth of her despair gave her extraordinary strength. At the usual evening hour, she led him to the chapel. Though she trembled and wept for him, she played without losing the rhythm or missing a note the hymn celebrating the creation of the beautiful earth, which would soon become her grave.\\n\\nWe came to her like visitors from heaven. Her carefully built courage and barely maintained strength vanished when she saw us. With a scream, she ran to Adrian, clasped his knees, and cried only, \u201cO save my father!\u201d Then, sobbing and shrieking hysterically, she released the grief she had held back for so long.\\n\\nPoor girl! She and her father now lie side by side beneath the tall walnut tree where her lover rests, the place she had shown us in her final moments. Her father eventually understood that she was in danger. Unable to see the changes in her beloved face, he stubbornly held her hand until death made it cold and stiff. Even then he neither moved nor spoke, until kind death took him, twelve hours later, into his unbroken rest. They lie beneath the earth, with the tree as their monument. I still remember that sacred place, framed by the rugged Jura and the distant, endless Alps. The church spire they once visited still rises above the surrounding trees. And though her hand is cold, I still imagine the divine music they loved drifting around them and comforting their gentle ghosts.\"}"
