          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"79b9d7d1ae2c28406c23cb41\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cOh, your father, of course, may spare you, if your mother can. Daughters never matter quite so much to a father. And if you stay one more complete _month_, I may be able to take one of you as far as London, since I am going there for a week early in June. Dawson does not object to the barouche-box, so there will be plenty of room for one of you\u2014and, if the weather happens to be cool, I would not mind taking you both, since neither of you is large.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are very kind, madam; but I believe we must follow our original plan.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine appeared to accept this. \u201cMrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I always speak my mind, and I cannot bear the thought of two young women travelling post by themselves. It is highly improper. You must find a way to send someone. I have the greatest dislike of such things. Young women should always be properly guarded and attended, according to their position in life. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I insisted that two male servants travel with her. Miss Darcy, the daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley, and Lady Anne, could not properly have appeared in any other way. I pay close attention to all such matters. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am glad I thought to mention it; it would truly be disgraceful to _you_ to let them go alone.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy uncle is sending a servant for us.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Your uncle! He keeps a male servant, does he? I am very glad you have someone who considers such matters. Where will you change horses? Oh, Bromley, of course. If you give my name at the Bell, they will take good care of you.\u201d\\n\\nLady Catherine had many more questions about their journey; and since she did not answer every one herself, Elizabeth had to pay attention\u2014which she considered fortunate. Otherwise, with her mind so occupied, she might have forgotten where she was. She would have to save her thoughts for private hours. Whenever she was alone, she surrendered to them as her greatest relief; and not a day passed without a solitary walk, during which she could enjoy all the painful pleasure of unpleasant memories.\\n\\nShe was soon likely to know Mr. Darcy\u2019s letter by heart. She studied every sentence, and her feelings toward its writer often changed greatly. When she remembered the manner in which he had addressed her, she was still filled with anger. But when she considered how unfairly she had judged and reproached him, her anger turned against herself, and she began to pity his disappointed feelings.\"}"
