          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"be1711d58e31572a63c685d9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There would soon be other chances for Anne to observe them. She had spent enough time with all four together to form an opinion, though she was too sensible to admit it at home, knowing that neither husband nor wife would have been pleased. She thought Louisa was rather the favourite, but, judging as carefully as she could from memory and experience, she could not believe Captain Wentworth was in love with either of them. They were more in love with him; yet even that was not truly love. It was a brief fever of admiration, though it might, and probably would, end in love with someone. Charles Hayter seemed aware that he was being neglected, while Henrietta sometimes appeared torn between the two men. Anne longed to tell them all what they were doing and to warn them of the trouble they were bringing upon themselves. She suspected no one of deceit. It gave her the greatest satisfaction to believe that Captain Wentworth was completely unaware of the pain he was causing. There was no triumph, not even a mean kind of triumph, in his manner. He had probably never heard of, or even considered, any claim Charles Hayter might have. His only mistake was accepting the attentions\u2014for accepting was the right word\u2014of two young women at once.\\n\\nAfter a brief struggle, however, Charles Hayter seemed to leave the field. Three days passed without his coming to Uppercross even once, a very clear change. He had also refused a regular invitation to dinner. On that occasion Mr Musgrove found him with several large books in front of him, and Mr and Mrs Musgrove became sure that something was wrong. With serious faces, they spoke of his studying himself to death. Mary hoped and believed that Henrietta had plainly rejected him, while her husband lived in constant expectation of seeing him the next day. Anne could only think that Charles Hayter was wise.\\n\\nOne morning around this time, Charles Musgrove and Captain Wentworth having gone shooting together, the sisters in the Cottage were sitting quietly at work when the sisters from the Mansion-house came to the window to visit them.\\n\\nIt was a very fine November day. The Miss Musgroves crossed the small grounds and stopped only to say that they were going for a _long_ walk, and therefore assumed Mary would not want to join them. Mary immediately replied, somewhat jealous that they did not consider her a good walker, \u201cOh, yes, I should very much like to come with you; I am very fond of a long walk.\u201d From the looks of the two girls, Anne was convinced that this was exactly what they had not wanted. Once again, she was struck by the pressure created by the family\u2019s habits: everything had to be shared, and everything had to be done together, however unwanted or inconvenient.\"}"
