          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bafd1773deac3eacc055326f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Emma did not regret lowering herself enough to visit the Coles. The visit gave her many pleasant memories the next day; and whatever she might have lost in dignified privacy would surely be more than repaid by the glory of being popular. She must have delighted the Coles\u2014good people who deserved to be made happy!\u2014and left behind a name that would not soon be forgotten.\\n\\nPerfect happiness, even in memory, is uncommon; and there were two things that troubled her. She wondered whether she had broken the duty women owed one another by revealing her suspicions about Jane Fairfax\u2019s feelings to Frank Churchill. It had hardly been right; but the thought had been so strong that it had escaped her. And his acceptance of everything she said was such a compliment to her insight that she could not be completely sure she should have kept silent.\\n\\nHer other regret also concerned Jane Fairfax, and about that she had no doubt. She sincerely and completely regretted that her own playing and singing were inferior. She deeply mourned the idleness of her childhood, then sat down and practised hard for an hour and a half.\\n\\nHarriet\u2019s arrival interrupted her; and if Harriet\u2019s praise could have satisfied her, she might soon have felt better.\\n\\n\u201cOh! If only I could play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t put us together, Harriet. My playing is no more like hers than a lamp is like sunshine.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear\u2014I think you play better than she does. I think you play quite as well as she does. I am sure I would much rather hear you. Everyone said last night how well you played.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnyone who knew anything about music must have noticed the difference. The truth is, Harriet, that my playing is just good enough to be praised, but Jane Fairfax\u2019s is far better.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I shall always think you play quite as well as she does, or that, if there is a difference, no one would ever notice it. Mr. Cole said you had so much taste; and Mr. Frank Churchill spoke a great deal about your taste, saying he valued taste much more than technical skill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh! But Jane Fairfax has both, Harriet.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAre you sure? I saw that she had technical skill, but I did not know she had any taste. No one mentioned it. And I hate Italian singing\u2014there is no understanding a word of it. Besides, if she plays so very well, you know, it is only what she must do, because she will have to teach.\u201d\"}"
