o do with William\u2019s concerns, but it really is extremely important that _I_ should see Knightley today; and it therefore becomes a matter of very serious inconvenience that I should have taken that hot walk for nothing.\u201d\\n\\nEmma felt she could do nothing better than go straight home. In all likelihood, she was being waited for there at that very moment; and Mr. Knightley might be spared sinking further into hostility toward Mr. Elton, if not toward William Larkins.\\n\\nAs she took her leave, she was pleased to find Miss Fairfax determined to accompany her from the room, and even downstairs; this gave her an opportunity, which she immediately used, to say,\"}"
