          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2122af6cf691f37b28f99bfc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was at this point that his eyes, and mine as well, lighted upon the scrap of parchment, which I had then taken for paper. It lay partly buried in the sand, with one corner protruding. Close to where we discovered it, I noticed the remains of what seemed to be the hull of a ship\u2019s longboat. The wreck must have lain there for an immense length of time, since scarcely any likeness to boat timbers remained.\\n\\n\u201cWell, Jupiter picked up the parchment, wrapped the beetle in it, and handed it to me. Shortly afterward we started homeward, and on the way encountered Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014. I showed him the insect, and he asked me to allow him to carry it to the fort. When I agreed, he immediately thrust it into his waistcoat pocket, leaving behind the parchment in which it had been wrapped, which I had kept in my hand while he examined it. Perhaps he feared I might change my mind and thought it prudent to secure the prize without delay\u2014you know how ardent he is about everything relating to Natural History. At the same time, without realizing it, I must have put the parchment into my own pocket.\\n\\n\u201cYou recollect that, when I went to the table to make a sketch of the beetle, I found no paper in its usual place. I searched the drawer, but found none there either. I then felt through my pockets, hoping to discover an old letter, and my hand came upon the parchment. I describe exactly how it came into my possession because the circumstances affected me with unusual force.\\n\\n\u201cNo doubt you will call me fanciful\u2014but I had already formed a sort of connexion. I had joined two links in a great chain. There was a boat upon a sea-coast, and near the boat lay a parchment\u2014not paper\u2014with a skull drawn upon it. You will naturally ask, \u2018What is the connexion?\u2019 I answer that the skull, or death\u2019s-head, is the familiar emblem of the pirate. The death\u2019s-head flag is raised in every engagement.\\n\\n\u201cI have said that the scrap was parchment, not paper. Parchment is lasting\u2014almost imperishable. Things of little importance are seldom committed to parchment; for ordinary drawing or writing, it is far less suitable than paper. This thought suggested that the death\u2019s-head must have some meaning\u2014some relevance. I also noticed the shape of the parchment. Though one corner had been accidentally torn away, it was still evident that its original form had been oblong.\"}"
