          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c4a0d71791f7e65f5a48497\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Wake up after a while and look around to see what had caused the sound, and maybe spot a steamboat coughing its way upstream. It would be so far off, toward the opposite bank, that you couldn\u2019t tell anything about it except whether it was a stern-wheel or side-wheel boat. Then, for about an hour, there would be nothing to hear or see\u2014only complete loneliness. Next you might see a raft drifting by, far away, with perhaps some fellow chopping on it, since they were almost always doing that on a raft. You would see the axe flash and fall, but hear nothing. You would see it rise again, and by the time it was above the man\u2019s head, you would hear the _k\u2019chunk!_ It had taken all that time for the sound to cross the water. So we spent the day idling around and listening to the silence. Once there was a heavy fog, and the rafts and other things passing by beat on tin pans so the steamboats would not run them down. A scow or raft passed so close that we could hear the people talking, cursing, and laughing\u2014hear them clearly\u2014but we could not see any sign of them. It made you feel uneasy; it was like spirits carrying on in the air. Jim said he believed they were spirits, but I said:\\n\\n\u201cNo; spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Dern the dern fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAs soon as night came, we pushed off. When we reached about the middle of the river, we left the raft alone and let the current carry it wherever it wanted. Then we lit our pipes, dangled our legs in the water, and talked about all sorts of things. We were always naked, day and night, whenever the mosquitoes allowed it. The new clothes Buck\u2019s family had made for me were too fine to be comfortable, and besides, I never cared much for clothes anyway.\\n\\nSometimes we had the whole river to ourselves for a very long time. There were the banks and islands across the water, and perhaps a spark\u2014which was a candle shining from a cabin window. Sometimes you could see one or two sparks on the water, from a raft or scow, you know. Maybe you could hear a fiddle or a song drifting over from one of those boats. It is lovely to live on a raft. We had the sky above us, covered with stars, and we would lie on our backs looking up at them and arguing about whether they had been made or had simply appeared. Jim believed they were made, but I believed they had just happened; I figured it would have taken too long to _make_ so many.\"}"
