          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0c1b35008e7838f706290d7f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mental qualities described as analytical are, in themselves, scarcely open to analysis. We recognize them only through their effects. Among other things, we know that, when possessed to an excessive degree, they are a source of the keenest pleasure to their possessor. As a strong man takes pride in his physical power, delighting in exercises that call his muscles into action, so the analyst rejoices in that mental activity which disentangles. He finds pleasure even in the most trivial occupations that bring his talent into play. He enjoys enigmas, conundrums, and hieroglyphics, displaying in solving each a degree of acumen that seems supernatural to ordinary understanding. His conclusions, reached through the very essence of method, have, in fact, the entire appearance of intuition.\\n\\nThe faculty of solving problems may be greatly strengthened by mathematical study, especially by its highest branch, which has been unjustly called analysis, merely because of its backward operations, as though it were analysis par excellence. Yet calculation is not, in itself, analysis. A chess player, for example, does one without attempting the other. It follows that chess is widely misunderstood in its effects on the mind. I am not writing a treatise now, but merely introducing a somewhat unusual narrative with observations made almost at random; I will therefore take this opportunity to assert that the higher and more useful powers of the reflective intellect are exercised more decisively by the quiet game of draughts than by all the elaborate frivolity of chess. In chess, where the pieces move in different and bizarre ways and possess varied and shifting values, what is merely complicated is mistaken\u2014a common error\u2014for what is profound. Attention is called into forceful action. If it falters for even a moment, an oversight occurs, causing injury or defeat. Since the possible moves are not only numerous but intricate, the chances of such oversights increase; and in nine games out of ten, it is the more concentrated rather than the more perceptive player who wins. In draughts, by contrast, where the moves are unique and vary little, the likelihood of carelessness is reduced, and with mere attention left comparatively unoccupied, whatever advantages either side gains come from superior acumen. To be less abstract, suppose a game of draughts in which the pieces have been reduced to four kings, so that, naturally, no oversight need be expected. It is clear that the victory can then be decided\u2014the players being otherwise equal\u2014only by some recherch\u00e9 move resulting from a powerful exertion of intellect.\"}"
