          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"90064d9f7611cba086d88ed2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Do not misunderstand me; I was never truly mad. I was always aware of my condition when my wild thoughts seemed to drive me toward insanity, but I revealed them to no one except silence and solitude. The people around me saw none of this. They saw only a poor girl broken in spirit, who spoke in a soft, gentle voice, and from whose lowered eyes tears sometimes slipped, though she tried to hide them. They saw someone who loved to be alone and shrank from notice; someone who never smiled\u2014oh, no! I never smiled\u2014and that was all.\\n\\nWell, I escaped. I left my guardian\u2019s house and was never heard from again. From the letters I left and other circumstances, people believed that I had planned to destroy myself. They therefore searched for me less carefully than they otherwise might have done, and soon every trace and memory of me was lost. I left London in a small vessel bound for a port in the north of England. Once I had succeeded in my attempt and was completely alone, peace returned to me. The sea was calm, and the vessel moved gently onward. I sat on deck beneath the open sky and thought that I had become a different creature. I was no longer the wild, raving, and most miserable Mathilda, but a young hermitess devoted to seclusion, who must strive to keep her heart free from all turmoil and hopeless despair. The fanciful, nunlike dress I had adopted; the knowledge that my existence was a secret known only to myself; and the solitude to which I was forever destined all nourished gentle thoughts in my wounded heart. The breeze playing in my hair revived me. I watched quietly as sunlight glittered on the waves and birds chased one another over the water, their feathers just brushing its surface. I slept without troubling dreams and awoke refreshed, ready to enjoy my peaceful freedom again.\\n\\nAfter four days, we reached the harbor for which we were bound. I would not stay on the coast, but went inland at once. I had already chosen the place where I would live. It would be a solitary house on a broad plain, far from any other dwelling, where I could see the entire horizon and wander freely without being disturbed by the sight of other people. I was not misanthropic, but I felt that the gentle flow of my feelings depended on my being alone. I settled in a vast solitude: a bleak heath scattered with stones, among which short grass grew, with a few rushes here and there beside a small pool.\"}"
