bearing little resemblance to the noise conventionally assigned to owls by men who write poetry. But such creatures stubbornly refuse, almost always, to say what men have prescribed for them.\\n\\nFor three heavy hours, the stone faces of the chateau, lion and human, stared blindly into the night. Dead darkness covered the entire landscape, and dead darkness added its own hush to the dust settling silently on every road. The burial ground had reached such a state that its small mounds of poor grass could no longer be distinguished from one another; for all that could be seen of it, the figure on the Cross might have come down. In the village, tax collectors and taxpayers slept soundly. Dreaming, perhaps, of banquets, as the starving commonly do, and of comfort and rest, as the driven slave and yoked ox may, its lean inhabitants slept deeply, fed and free.\"}"
