          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"bad4ea985d4a18c774412f5f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He said nothing about it, however, until their visitor had left for the night. As soon as the man was gone, he turned to his daughter and said,\\n\\n\u201cJane, I congratulate you. You will be a very happy woman.\u201d\\n\\nJane immediately went to him, kissed him, and thanked him for his kindness.\\n\\n\u201cYou are a good girl,\u201d he replied, \u201cand I am very pleased to think you will be so happily settled. I have no doubt that you will do very well together. Your tempers are not at all unlike. You are both so willing to give way that nothing will ever be decided; so trusting that every servant will cheat you; and so generous that you will always spend more than you earn.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI hope not. Carelessness or thoughtlessness about money would be unforgivable in _me_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cSpend more than they earn! My dear Mr. Bennet,\u201d cried his wife, \u201cwhat can you mean? He has four or five thousand a year, and probably more.\u201d Then, turning to her daughter, she said, \u201cOh, my dear, dear Jane, I am so happy! I am sure I shall not sleep a moment tonight. I knew this would happen. I always said it must, in the end. I was certain you could not be so beautiful for nothing! I remember that as soon as I saw him, when he first came to Hertfordshire last year, I thought it very likely that you would come together. Oh, he is the handsomest young man anyone has ever seen!\u201d\\n\\nWickham and Lydia were completely forgotten. Jane was now, beyond question, her favourite child. At that moment she cared about no one else. Her younger sisters soon began asking her for the happy things she might one day be able to provide.\\n\\nMary asked to use the library at Netherfield, while Kitty begged earnestly for a few balls there every winter.\\n\\nFrom then on, Bingley naturally became a daily visitor at Longbourn. He often arrived before breakfast and always stayed until after supper, except when some hateful neighbour\u2014someone he could not possibly dislike enough\u2014had invited him to dinner, and he felt obliged to accept.\\n\\nElizabeth now had little time to talk with her sister. Whenever Bingley was there, Jane had no attention to give anyone else. But Elizabeth found herself very useful to both of them during the periods of separation that sometimes had to occur. When Jane was absent, Bingley always kept close to Elizabeth so that he could talk about her; and when Bingley had gone, Jane constantly sought the same comfort.\\n\\n\u201cHe has made me so happy,\u201d she said one evening, \u201cby telling me that he had no idea I was in town last spring!\"}"
