          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5e433ad4d445303c25ce7c57\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No imagination can picture the sublimity that might have been displayed by a similar phenomenon occurring in the darkness of night. Hell itself might have served as an appropriate image. Even as it was, my hair rose on end as I gazed far down into the yawning abysses, allowing fancy, as it were, to descend and wander through the strange vaulted halls, ruddy gulfs, and red, ghastly chasms of that hideous, unfathomable fire. I had indeed escaped narrowly. Had the balloon remained only a little longer within the cloud\u2014that is, had the discomfort of becoming wet not induced me to release the ballast\u2014inevitable destruction would have followed. Such dangers, though seldom considered, are perhaps the greatest that balloons must face. By this time, however, I had reached too great an elevation to feel further anxiety on that account.\\n\\n\u201cI was now ascending rapidly, and by seven o\u2019clock the barometer showed an altitude of no less than nine and a half miles. I began to experience great difficulty in breathing. My head was also exceedingly painful; and, after noticing moisture on my cheeks for some time, I finally discovered that it was blood, flowing rather quickly from my eardrums. My eyes, too, caused me considerable distress. When I passed my hand over them, they seemed to have protruded from their sockets to a remarkable degree; and every object in the car, even the balloon itself, appeared distorted. These symptoms exceeded my expectations and alarmed me. At this point, with great imprudence and no reflection, I cast from the car three five-pound portions of ballast. The increased speed of ascent thereby produced carried me too swiftly, and without adequate gradation, into a highly rarefied layer of atmosphere; the consequence nearly proved fatal both to my expedition and to me. I was suddenly overcome by a spasm lasting more than five minutes, and even after it had somewhat subsided, I could draw breath only at long intervals and with gasps\u2014bleeding profusely all the while from my nose and ears, and slightly even from my eyes. The pigeons seemed extremely distressed and struggled to get free; while the cat mewed miserably and, her tongue hanging from her mouth, staggered back and forth in the car as though poisoned. Only now did I realize, too late, how reckless I had been in throwing out the ballast, and my agitation became intense. I expected nothing short of death, and death within a few minutes.\u201d\"}"
