          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d2a18e27e7458f1a742dff9e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Their arrival had lit a kind of fire in the heart of Saint Antoine, spreading quickly as they came. It stirred and flickered in the faces seen at most doors and windows. Yet no one followed them, and no man spoke when they entered the wine-shop, though every eye turned toward them.\\n\\n\u201cGood day, gentlemen!\u201d said Monsieur Defarge.\\n\\nPerhaps this was a signal for everyone to speak. It brought back a chorus of \u201cGood day!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is bad weather, gentlemen,\u201d said Defarge, shaking his head.\\n\\nAt this, every man looked at his neighbour, then lowered his eyes and sat in silence. Except for one man, who rose and went out.\\n\\n\u201cMy wife,\u201d said Defarge aloud, speaking to Madame Defarge, \u201cI travelled several leagues with this good road-mender, called Jacques. I met him by accident, a day and a half\u2019s journey from Paris. He is a good fellow, this road-mender called Jacques. Give him something to drink, my wife!\u201d\\n\\nA second man rose and went out. Madame Defarge placed wine before the road-mender called Jacques. He removed his blue cap to the company and drank. He carried some coarse, dark bread inside his blouse; from time to time he ate it while sitting near Madame Defarge\u2019s counter, munching and drinking. A third man rose and went out.\\n\\nDefarge refreshed himself with a drink of wine, but took less than he had given the stranger, since wine was no rarity to him. He waited until the countryman had finished his breakfast. He looked at no one present, and no one now looked at him\u2014not even Madame Defarge, who had taken up her knitting and was working.\\n\\n\u201cHave you finished your meal, friend?\u201d he asked after a suitable time.\\n\\n\u201cYes, thank you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCome, then! You shall see the room I told you that you could occupy. It will suit you perfectly.\u201d\\n\\nFrom the wine-shop into the street, from the street into a courtyard, from the courtyard up a steep staircase, and from the staircase into a garret\u2014formerly the garret where a white-haired man had sat on a low bench, bent forward and deeply occupied in making shoes.\\n\\nThe white-haired man was gone now. But the three men who had left the wine-shop one by one were there. Between them and the white-haired man, far away, there was only one small connection: they had once looked in at him through the cracks in the wall.\\n\\nDefarge carefully closed the door and spoke in a quiet voice:\\n\\n\u201cJacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness I met by arrangement, I, Jacques Four.\u201d\"}"
