          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8ff6eb42da0f647ed5aab894\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His manner was stubborn in the extreme, and \u201cthat cursed bug\u201d were the only words that escaped his lips during the journey. For my part, I carried a couple of dark lanterns, while Legrand contented himself with the scarab, which he had tied to the end of a piece of whipcord and twirled back and forth with the air of a conjurer as he walked. When I saw this final, unmistakable proof of my friend\u2019s mental derangement, I could scarcely keep from tears. Still, I thought it best to indulge his fancy, at least for the moment, or until I could take some more forceful action with a reasonable chance of success. Meanwhile, I tried, entirely without success, to learn the purpose of the expedition. Having persuaded me to accompany him, he seemed unwilling to discuss any subject of lesser importance, and answered all my questions only with, \u201cWe shall see!\u201d\\n\\nWe crossed the creek at the island\u2019s upper end in a skiff, then climbed the high ground along the mainland shore and proceeded northwest through a country so wild and desolate that there was no sign of a human footprint anywhere. Legrand led decisively, stopping only for an instant now and then to consult what seemed to be landmarks he had arranged on an earlier occasion.\\n\\nWe traveled in this way for about two hours, and the sun was just setting when we entered a region far drearier than anything we had yet seen. It was a kind of plateau near the summit of an almost inaccessible hill, thickly wooded from base to peak and scattered with enormous crags that seemed to rest loosely on the ground. In many places, they were kept from plunging into the valleys below only by the trees against which they leaned. Deep ravines running in various directions gave the scene an even sterner solemnity.\\n\\nThe natural platform we had climbed was densely covered with brambles. We soon found that it would have been impossible to force our way through them without a scythe, so Jupiter, at his master\u2019s direction, cleared a path for us to the foot of an enormously tall tulip tree. It stood on the level ground with eight or ten oaks, towered above them all, and surpassed every other tree I had ever seen in the beauty of its foliage and shape, the broad spread of its branches, and the majestic character of its appearance.\"}"
