gree? He had never spoken of their marriage except as something far in the future.\\n\\nWhen they first raised the subject, he was so miserable that they nearly lost hope. A second mention caused less pain. He began to think that it would happen and that he could not stop it\u2014a very encouraging step toward accepting it. Still, he was not happy. In fact, he seemed so distressed that his daughter lost courage. She could not bear to see him suffer or to know that he imagined himself neglected. Although she almost accepted the assurances of both Mr. Knightleys that, once the marriage was over, his distress would soon end as well, she hesitated. She could not go on.\"}"
