{"request_id":"8551e04b90e14093ced205cc","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a soft breeze soon fanned it into flame. I noticed this too and fashioned a fan from branches, which revived the embers whenever they were almost dying out. When night returned, I was delighted to discover that the fire supplied light as well as warmth, and that finding this element benefited me in preparing food. Some of the offal left by the travellers had been roasted, and it tasted far more savoury than the berries I collected from the trees. I therefore attempted to prepare my food in the same way, laying it upon the glowing embers. I found that the berries were spoiled by the process, while the nuts and roots were greatly improved.

“Food, however, grew scarce, and I frequently spent an entire day searching fruitlessly for a few acorns to ease the pangs of hunger. When I discovered this, I determined to leave the place I had until then inhabited and seek another where my few necessities might be more readily supplied. During this migration I deeply regretted losing the fire, which I had gained by accident and did not know how to make again. I devoted several hours to seriously considering the problem, but was forced to abandon every effort to replace it. Wrapping myself in my cloak, I crossed the wood toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days, and at last found the open country. A heavy fall of snow had occurred the previous night, and the fields were one unbroken expanse of white; the sight was desolate, and the cold, wet substance covering the ground chilled my feet.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I yearned for food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on a rise of ground, which had doubtless been built for the use of some shepherd. This was a new object to me, and I inspected the building with intense curiosity. Finding the door open, I went inside. An old man was seated there beside a fire, over which he was preparing his breakfast. He turned at the sound, and on seeing me, gave a loud shriek and, leaving the hut, ran across the fields with a speed his weakened body scarcely seemed capable of. His appearance, unlike that of anyone I had previously seen, and his flight surprised me somewhat. Yet I was captivated by the hut; snow and rain could not enter it, the floor was dry, and it seemed to me then as exquisite and divine a refuge as Pandæmonium seemed to the dæmons of hell after their sufferings in the lake of fire."}

