          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c957a9877f02133281ec81d5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cAre you speaking of\u2014Mr. Knightley?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOf course I am. I could never have meant anyone else\u2014and so I thought you understood. When we spoke of him, it seemed perfectly clear.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot entirely,\u201d Emma replied, forcing herself to remain calm, \u201cbecause everything you said then seemed to me to concern someone else. I could almost swear that you had _named_ Mr. Frank Churchill. I am certain that you spoke of the service Mr. Frank Churchill had done you by protecting you from the gypsies.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Miss Woodhouse, how you forget!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Harriet, I remember perfectly the substance of what I said at the time. I told you that I did not wonder at your attachment; that, considering the service he had done you, it was extremely natural. You agreed, speaking very warmly of your gratitude for that service, and even describing what you had felt when you saw him come forward to rescue you. The impression remains very strong in my memory.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, dear,\u201d cried Harriet, \u201cnow I remember what you mean; but I was thinking of something quite different then. It was not the gypsies\u2014it was not Mr. Frank Churchill I meant. No!\u201d she added with some emphasis. \u201cI was thinking of something far more precious\u2014of Mr. Knightley\u2019s coming to ask me to dance when Mr. Elton would not dance with me, and when there was no other partner in the room. That was the kind action; that was the noble benevolence and generosity; that was the service that first made me feel how superior he was to every other person on earth.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cGood God!\u201d cried Emma. \u201cThis has been a most unfortunate\u2014most dreadful mistake! What is to be done?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou would not have encouraged me, then, if you had understood me? At least, I cannot be worse off than I would have been if the other had been the person; and now\u2014it _is_ possible\u2014\u201d\\n\\nShe paused for several moments. Emma could not speak.\\n\\n\u201cI do not wonder, Miss Woodhouse,\u201d she continued, \u201cthat you should see a great difference between the two, whether in relation to me or to anyone else. You must think one five hundred million times above me compared with the other. But I hope, Miss Woodhouse, that supposing\u2014that if\u2014strange as it may seem\u2014. You know they were your own words, that even more wonderful things had happened, and matches of greater inequality had taken place than one between Mr. Frank Churchill and me. Therefore, it seems possible that even such a thing as this may have happened before\u2014and if I should be fortunate beyond expression\u2014if Mr. Knightley should truly\u2014if _he_ does not mind the difference, I hope, dear Miss Woodhouse, that you will not oppose it or try to place obstacles in the way.\u201d\"}"
