          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ae8fb3463bf1a26f0f0fec4f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The mighty voice\u2014one might call it inorganic, since we could in no way connect it with the mechanism of pipe or key\u2014ceased its sonorous utterance, and the girl, turning to help her aged companion, at last became aware of us.\\n\\nIt was her father; and from childhood she had guided his sightless steps. They were Germans from Saxony who, having emigrated there only a few years earlier, had formed new bonds with the neighboring villagers. Around the time the pestilence began, a young German student had joined them. Their simple story was easy to understand. He, a nobleman, loved the poor musician\u2019s beautiful daughter and followed them in flight from the persecutions of his friends; but soon the great leveller came with his unblunted scythe, mowing down, along with the grass, the field\u2019s tall flowers. The young man was among the first victims. She preserved herself for her father\u2019s sake. His blindness allowed her to maintain a deception that had begun by accident\u2014and now, as solitary beings and the only survivors in the land, he remained unaware of the change, not knowing that when he listened to his child\u2019s music, the silent mountains, senseless lake, and unconscious trees were, except for himself, her only listeners.\\n\\nOn the very day we arrived, she had been seized by the symptomatic illness. She was paralyzed with terror at the thought of leaving her aged, blind father alone upon the empty earth; yet she lacked the courage to reveal the truth, and the extremity of her despair drove her to extraordinary exertions. At the usual vesper hour, she led him to the chapel; and, though trembling and weeping for his sake, played without a fault in time or a wrong note the hymn composed to celebrate the creation of the adorned earth, soon to become her tomb.\\n\\nWe came upon her like visitors from heaven itself; her exalted courage and barely maintained firmness vanished at the sight of relief. With a shriek she rushed toward us, clasped Adrian\u2019s knees, and, saying only, \u201cO save my father!\u201d with sobs and hysterical cries, opened the long-sealed floodgates of her grief.\\n\\nPoor girl! She and her father now lie side by side beneath the tall walnut tree where her lover rests, and which she had indicated to us in her final moments. Her father, at last conscious of his daughter\u2019s danger but unable to see the changes in her beloved face, stubbornly held her hand until death chilled and stiffened it. Even then he neither moved nor spoke, until, twelve hours later, kindly death carried him to his unbroken rest. They lie beneath the sod, the tree their monument; the sacred place remains clear in my memory, shadowed by the craggy Jura and the distant, immeasurable Alps. The church spire they once attended still rises among the enclosing trees; and though her hand is cold, I seem still to hear the strains of divine music they loved drifting around them, comforting their gentle ghosts.\"}"
