          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0ddbf2a8a0b4e504c83b0be9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNow,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, gently placing his hand on the Doctor\u2019s arm once more, after a brief silence on both sides, \u201cto what would you attribute this attack?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe,\u201d replied Doctor Manette, \u201cthat there had been a powerful and unusual revival of the chain of thought and memory that originally caused the malady. Some intense associations of an exceedingly painful nature were vividly brought back, I think. It is likely that a fear had long been hidden in his mind, that those associations might return\u2014say, under certain circumstances\u2014say, on a particular occasion. He tried vainly to prepare himself; perhaps the attempt to prepare himself left him less able to endure it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould he remember what happened during the relapse?\u201d asked Mr. Lorry, with understandable hesitation.\\n\\nThe Doctor gazed mournfully around the room, shook his head, and answered in a quiet voice, \u201cNot at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow, regarding the future,\u201d suggested Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cAs to the future,\u201d said the Doctor, regaining his firmness, \u201cI should have great hope. Since Heaven, in its mercy, restored him so quickly, I should have great hope. He yielded beneath the pressure of a complicated something, long feared, long dimly anticipated, and long resisted; and recovering after the cloud had broken and passed, I should hope that the worst was behind him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well! That is good comfort. I am thankful!\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI am thankful!\u201d repeated the Doctor, bowing his head reverently.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two other matters,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cabout which I am anxious to be informed. May I continue?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou cannot render your friend a better service.\u201d The Doctor extended his hand to him.\\n\\n\u201cTo the first, then. He is studious by habit and unusually energetic; he applies himself with great enthusiasm to gaining professional knowledge, carrying out experiments, and many other things. Now, does he do too much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think not. It may be the nature of his mind to require occupation constantly and in an unusual degree. That may be partly natural to him, and partly the consequence of suffering. The less it was occupied with healthy matters, the more it would risk turning toward unhealthy ones. He may have watched himself and discovered this.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are certain that he is not under too much strain?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe I am quite certain.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Manette, if he were overworked now\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lorry, I doubt whether that could readily happen. There has been a violent strain in one direction, and it requires a counterweight.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cForgive me, as a persistent man of business. Supposing for a moment that he were overworked; would it reveal itself through some recurrence of this disorder?\u201d\"}"
