{"request_id":"1dbe3d15cd9378fd0fd4fa2d","rewritten_text":"What kind of life could they lead while Fate still kept this dreadful fortune suspended in the balance? The marriage must be delayed, whatever the cost. On that point he was entirely determined. Though he loved the girl passionately, and the lightest touch of her fingers as they sat together sent every nerve in his body trembling with exquisite delight, he understood just as plainly where duty called him, and knew perfectly that he had no right to marry until he had carried out the murder. Once that was done, he could stand beside Sybil Merton at the altar and place his life in her keeping without fear that he was doing wrong. Then he could draw her into his arms, certain that she would never need to blush for him or lower her head in shame. But the deed had to come first; and the sooner it was accomplished, the better for them both.

Many men in his circumstances would have chosen the primrose path of pleasure rather than the steep ascent of duty; but Lord Arthur was too conscientious to put enjoyment before principle. His love contained more than passion alone; to him Sybil represented everything good and noble. For a brief time he felt a natural horror of the task demanded of him, but it soon disappeared. His heart assured him that this was not sin but sacrifice, while his reason showed him that no other path remained. He had to choose between living for himself and living for others, and however terrible the burden placed upon him, he knew he must not let selfishness prevail over love. Sooner or later, every one of us is required to settle the same question—the same issue is placed before us all. Lord Arthur faced it early, before his character had been damaged by the calculating cynicism of middle age or his heart eaten away by the shallow, fashionable selfishness of the present day, and he did not hesitate to perform his duty. Happily, too, he was no dreamer or useless dilettante. If he had been, he would have faltered like Hamlet and allowed indecision to ruin his purpose. But he was practical by nature. To him, life meant action more than reflection. He possessed that rarest of qualities, common sense.

By now the wild, confused emotions of the preceding night had entirely faded, and he looked back almost shamefacedly on his frantic wandering from street to street and his fierce anguish of feeling. The very genuineness of his suffering now made it appear unreal. He wondered how he could have been foolish enough to rave and storm over what could not be avoided."}

