hed safely a town about twenty leagues from De Lacey\u2019s cottage, where her attendant fell dangerously ill. Safie cared for her with the greatest devotion, but the poor girl died, leaving the Arabian alone, unfamiliar with the country\u2019s language and entirely ignorant of the ways of the world. Yet she came into kind hands. The Italian had named the place to which they were traveling, and after her death, the woman of the house where they had stayed took care that Safie should reach her lover\u2019s cottage safely.\u201d\"}"
