          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"5e5b24bfcebcdf176e656066\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I don\u2019t rush at him suddenly and risk making a clumsy mess of it. No, I move far off to one side and work my way toward him little by little, so he never suspects I mean to strike him; then, all at once, he is flat on his back and cannot imagine how it happened. That was how I dealt with Brother Dowley. I began speaking in an easy, comfortable way, as if I were only talking to pass the time. The oldest man alive could not have guessed from where I started where I intended to end up:\\n\\n\u201cBoys, there are many strange things about law, custom, usage, and all the rest, when you stop to examine them; yes, and about the direction and progress of human opinion and action, too. There are written laws\u2014they die; but there are also unwritten laws\u2014they are eternal. Consider the unwritten law of wages: it says wages must rise, little by little, throughout the centuries. Notice how it works. We know what wages are now, in one place and another; we take an average and call that today\u2019s wage. We know what wages were a hundred years ago and two hundred years ago. That is as far back as we can go, but it is enough to show us the law of progress\u2014the size and speed of each increase. So, without any document, we can come fairly close to deciding what wages were three, four, or five hundred years ago. Good, so far. Do we stop there? No. We stop looking backward, turn around, and apply the law to the future. My friends, I can tell you what people\u2019s wages will be at any future date you care to name, for hundreds and hundreds of years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat, goodman, what?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes. In seven hundred years, wages will have risen to six times what they are now in this region, and farmhands will receive 3 cents a day, while mechanics will receive 6.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI wish I might die now and live then!\u201d interrupted Smug, the wheelwright, with a greedy light in his eyes.\\n\\n\u201cAnd that is not all; they will receive their food as well\u2014such as it is. It will not fill them up. Two hundred and fifty years later\u2014pay attention now\u2014a mechanic\u2019s wage will be\u2014remember, this is law, not guesswork\u2014his wage will then be _twenty_ cents a day!\u201d\\n\\nEveryone gasped in astonishment. Dickon the mason murmured, raising his eyes and hands:\"}"
