          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"70ed0ff9447925190d17eafe\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Rising early, and once more managing to elude the watchfulness of my creditors, I hastened to the bookseller\u2019s stall and spent the little ready money I had on several volumes concerning Mechanics and Practical Astronomy. Having brought them safely home, I devoted every moment I could spare to reading them, and soon acquired, as I believed, enough proficiency in such studies to carry out my design. During this interval, I also made every effort to appease the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble. At length I succeeded\u2014partly by selling sufficient household furniture to meet half their demand, and partly by promising to pay the remainder when a small project I claimed to have in hand was completed, for assistance with which I asked their services. By these means\u2014for they were ignorant men\u2014I had little trouble in winning them to my purpose.\\n\\n\u201cThings being thus settled, I managed, with my wife\u2019s help and with the utmost secrecy and caution, to dispose of the property still remaining to me, and to borrow, in small amounts and under various pretences, without considering how I should ever repay them, a considerable sum in ready money. With these funds I gradually obtained fine cambric muslin, in pieces of twelve yards each; twine; a quantity of caoutchouc varnish; a large, deep wicker basket made to order; and various other articles required for constructing and fitting out a balloon of extraordinary size. I instructed my wife to assemble this as quickly as possible, giving her all the necessary directions concerning the particular method. Meanwhile, I fashioned the twine into a net of adequate dimensions, fitted it with a hoop and the requisite cords, and purchased a quadrant, a compass, a spy-glass, an ordinary barometer with several important alterations, and two astronomical instruments less commonly known. I then found opportunities to carry by night, to a secluded place east of Rotterdam, five iron-bound casks, each holding about fifty gallons, together with one larger cask; six tinned-ware tubes, three inches in diameter, properly shaped and ten feet long; a quantity of a certain metallic substance, or semi-metal, which I shall not identify; and twelve demijohns filled with a very common acid. The gas produced from these last materials is one that no person besides myself has ever generated\u2014or, at least, ever employed for a similar purpose. I should have no objection to revealing the secret, except that it properly belongs to a citizen of Nantz, in France, who communicated it to me conditionally.\u201d\"}"
