          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f1c5b569eb8b6dff133aab6b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"And I summon you, spirits of the dead, and you wandering agents of vengeance, to assist and guide me in my task. Let the accursed, hellish monster drink deeply of suffering; let him know the despair that now torments me.\u201d\\n\\nI had begun my adjuration solemnly, with an awe that nearly convinced me the shades of my murdered friends heard and approved my devotion; but as I finished, the furies took possession of me, and rage strangled my voice.\\n\\nThrough the silence of night, a loud and fiendish laugh answered me. It sounded long and heavily in my ears; the mountains echoed it back, and I felt as though all hell encircled me with mockery and laughter. Surely, at that instant, frenzy would have seized me and I would have ended my wretched life, had not my vow been heard and I been preserved for vengeance. The laughter faded, and then a familiar, hated voice, seemingly close beside my ear, spoke to me in a clear whisper: \u201cI am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have resolved to live, and I am satisfied.\u201d\\n\\nI sprang toward the place from which the sound came, but the devil escaped my grasp. Suddenly the moon\u2019s broad disk rose and cast its full light upon his ghastly, distorted form as he fled with more than human speed.\\n\\nI pursued him, and for many months this has been my occupation. Following a faint clue, I traced the windings of the Rhone, but without success. The blue Mediterranean came into view, and by a strange chance I saw the fiend enter it by night and conceal himself aboard a vessel bound for the Black Sea. I took passage on the same ship, but somehow he escaped me.\\n\\nThrough the wild regions of Tartary and Russia, though he continued to elude me, I have always followed his trail. At times the peasants, terrified by this dreadful apparition, told me where he had gone; at times he himself, fearing that if I lost every trace of him I would despair and die, left some sign to direct me. Snow fell upon my head, and I saw the mark of his enormous foot across the white plain. To you, just entering life, for whom care is unfamiliar and agony unknown, how can you comprehend what I have suffered and still suffer? Cold, hunger, and weariness were the smallest of the pains appointed for me; some devil had cursed me, and I carried an everlasting hell within me. Yet a benevolent spirit still followed and guided my steps, and whenever I complained most bitterly, it would suddenly free me from difficulties that seemed impossible to overcome. Sometimes, when hunger overcame nature and exhaustion brought me low, a meal had been prepared for me in the wilderness, restoring and strengthening me.\"}"
