          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ec1ffd7c84fc95b3bf2f92ea\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Why, we might build a very exciting superstition around this clue. I suppose you would call the bug _scarab\u00e6us caput hominis_, or something similar\u2014there are many such names in natural histories. But where are the _antenn\u00e6_ you mentioned?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe _antenn\u00e6!_\u201d said Legrand, who seemed to be growing strangely heated about the matter. \u201cSurely you can see the _antenn\u00e6_. I made them as clear as they are on the real insect, and I assume that is enough.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well,\u201d I said, \u201cperhaps you did\u2014but I still cannot see them.\u201d I handed him the paper without saying more, since I did not want to upset him. Yet I was greatly surprised by what had happened. His bad temper confused me\u2014and, as for the beetle drawing, there were plainly _no antenn\u00e6_ visible, while the whole figure looked very much like the usual pictures of a death\u2019s-head.\\n\\nHe took the paper irritably and was about to crumple it, apparently to throw it into the fire, when a passing glance at the drawing suddenly caught his attention. At once his face became intensely red, and then, just as quickly, extremely pale. For several minutes he sat examining the drawing closely. At last he stood, took a candle from the table, and went to sit on a sea-chest in the farthest corner of the room. There he studied the paper anxiously again, turning it every way. He said nothing, but his behavior astonished me. Still, I thought it wiser not to worsen his growing bad mood with any remarks. Soon he took a wallet from his coat pocket, placed the paper carefully inside it, and put both into a writing desk, which he locked. He now seemed calmer, but his earlier enthusiasm had completely vanished. He appeared less angry than lost in thought. As the evening passed, he became more deeply absorbed in his thoughts, and none of my attempts at conversation could draw him out. I had planned to spend the night at the hut, as I often had before, but seeing him in this state, I decided to leave. He did not ask me to stay; however, as I departed, he shook my hand with even greater warmth than usual.\\n\\nAbout a month later\u2014and during that time I had seen nothing of Legrand\u2014I received a visit in Charleston from his servant, Jupiter. I had never seen the good old Black man look so discouraged, and I feared that some serious misfortune had happened to my friend.\"}"
