          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"75f566d843515e95a238fb13\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When we got back to the raft and he came to count everything up, he found he had collected eighty-seven dollars and seventy-five cents. He had also carried off a three-gallon jug of whisky, which he found beneath a wagon when he was starting home through the woods. The king said that, all things considered, it surpassed any day he had ever spent in the missionary business. There was no use arguing, he said; heathens were worthless compared with pirates when it came to working a camp meeting.\\n\\nThe duke had thought he was doing pretty well until the king came along to show him up, but afterward he did not think so highly of himself. He had set up and printed two small jobs for farmers in that printing office\u2014horse bills\u2014and collected four dollars. He had also secured ten dollars\u2019 worth of advertisements for the paper, which he said he would print for four dollars if they paid in advance, and they did. The paper cost two dollars a year, but he took three subscriptions at fifty cents each, provided they paid in advance. They had intended to pay in cordwood and onions as usual, but he said he had just bought the business, had reduced the price as far as he could afford, and would operate it for cash. He set up a short poem he had written himself, straight from his own head\u2014three verses, rather sweet and sad\u2014called \u201cYes, Crush, Cold World, This Breaking Heart,\u201d and left it set and ready to print in the paper without charging anything. Altogether, he had taken in nine dollars and fifty cents, and said he had done a reasonably good day\u2019s work.\\n\\nThen he showed us another small job he had printed without charging us, since it was for our benefit. It showed a runaway Black man with a bundle tied to a stick over his shoulder, and \u201c$200 Reward\u201d beneath it. The text was all about Jim and described him exactly. It said he had run away from 