{"request_id":"339717fd6a651e00b3e5df39","rewritten_text":"It then showed that I was at an altitude of 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles, and I was therefore able to see an area of the earth equal to no less than one three-hundred-and-twentieth of its entire surface. At nine o’clock I had again lost sight of land to the east, but not before I noticed that the balloon was drifting rapidly to the north-northwest. The ocean’s curve beneath me was very clear, although my view was often blocked by masses of cloud floating back and forth. I now observed that even the lightest vapors never rose more than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I tried throwing a handful of feathers through the valve. They did not float as I had expected, but dropped straight down like a bullet, all together and with tremendous speed, disappearing from sight within a few seconds. At first I could not understand this strange event; I could not believe that my rate of ascent had suddenly increased so enormously. But I soon realized that the atmosphere was now too thin to support even feathers. They really were falling, as they seemed to be, with great speed, and I had been confused by the combined speeds of their descent and my own rise.

“By ten o’clock I found that I had very little to occupy my immediate attention. Everything was going well, and I believed the balloon was rising faster every moment, although I no longer had any way to measure how quickly the increase was occurring. I felt no pain or discomfort and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I occupied myself by examining my various instruments and by renewing the air inside the chamber. I decided to do this every forty minutes, more to protect my health than because such frequent renewal was truly necessary. Meanwhile, I could not help imagining what lay ahead. My fancy wandered through the moon’s wild, dreamlike regions. My imagination, free at last from its restraints, moved wherever it pleased among the constantly changing wonders of a shadowy, unstable land. At one moment there were ancient forests, jagged cliffs, and waterfalls crashing loudly into bottomless chasms. Then I suddenly entered quiet noonday solitudes, where no earthly wind ever came, and where vast fields of poppies and slender, lily-like flowers stretched into the distance, forever silent and still."}

