          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"707b2a5afa03a198e52509ab\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cNow,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, gently placing his hand on the Doctor\u2019s arm again, after a brief silence from them both, \u201cwhat do you think caused this attack?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe,\u201d replied Doctor Manette, \u201cthat the thoughts and memories that first caused the illness returned with unusual force. I think some powerful and deeply painful associations were brought vividly back. He may have long feared that they would return\u2014under certain circumstances, perhaps on a particular occasion. He tried to prepare himself, but in vain; perhaps that attempt left him less able to endure them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWould he remember what happened during the relapse?\u201d Mr. Lorry asked hesitantly.\\n\\nThe Doctor looked sadly around the room, shook his head, and answered in a low voice, \u201cNot at all.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd what of the future?\u201d Mr. Lorry suggested.\\n\\n\u201cAs for the future,\u201d said the Doctor, regaining his confidence, \u201cI have great hope. Since Heaven, in its mercy, restored him so quickly, I have great hope. He gave way under the pressure of something complicated, long feared, dimly anticipated, and resisted for a long time. Now that the cloud has broken and passed, I hope the worst is over.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, well! That is comforting. I am thankful!\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI am thankful!\u201d repeated the Doctor, bowing his head reverently.\\n\\n\u201cThere are two other matters,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cabout which I would like your advice. May I continue?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou could do your friend no better service.\u201d The Doctor offered him his hand.\\n\\n\u201cThen, the first. He is very studious and unusually energetic. He works with great enthusiasm to gain professional knowledge, conduct experiments, and pursue many other interests. Now, does he work too hard?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think not. His mind may naturally need constant occupation. That may be partly its nature and partly the result of his suffering. The less it was occupied with healthy matters, the more likely it was to turn in an unhealthy direction. He may have watched himself and discovered this.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are certain he is not under too much strain?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI believe I am quite certain.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Manette, if he were overworked now\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Lorry, I doubt that would be easy. He has suffered violent pressure in one direction, and he needs an opposing weight.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cForgive me, as a persistent man of business. Suppose, for the moment, that he was overworked; would that appear as a return of this disorder?\u201d\"}"
