          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"854deb3de1c01a4ca16110ee\",\"rewritten_text\":\"He chose Friday, the 17th of August, for his appearance, and spent most of the day examining his wardrobe, finally settling on a large slouch hat with a red feather, a winding-sheet frilled at wrists and neck, and a rusty dagger. Toward evening a fierce rainstorm broke, and the wind blew so strongly that every window and door in the old house shook and rattled. Indeed, it was precisely the sort of weather he adored. His plan was as follows. He would creep quietly to Washington Otis\u2019s room, gibber at him from the foot of the bed, and stab himself three times in the throat to the accompaniment of slow music. He held Washington in particular dislike, knowing perfectly well that he was the one who regularly removed the celebrated Canterville bloodstain with Pinkerton\u2019s Paragon Detergent. After reducing the rash and foolhardy youth to a state of utter terror, he would go on to the room occupied by the United States Minister and his wife, place a clammy hand upon Mrs. Otis\u2019s forehead, and hiss the dreadful secrets of the charnel-house into her trembling husband\u2019s ear. As for little Virginia, he had not entirely decided. She had never insulted him, and she was pretty and gentle. A few hollow groans from the wardrobe, he thought, ought to suffice; or, if that did not wake her, he could claw at the counterpane with fingers twitching as if palsied. Regarding the twins, however, he was firmly resolved to teach them a lesson. First, naturally, he would sit upon their chests, creating the suffocating sensation of nightmare. Then, since their beds stood very near one another, he would position himself between them as a green, ice-cold corpse until terror paralysed them; finally, he would cast off the winding-sheet and crawl around the room with bleached white bones and one rolling eyeball, in the guise of \u2018Dumb Daniel, or the Suicide\u2019s Skeleton,\u2019 a role in which he had more than once made a powerful impression, and which he considered fully equal to his celebrated part of \u2018Martin the Maniac, or the Masked Mystery.\u2019\\n\\nAt half-past ten he heard the family retiring. For a while he was distracted by the twins\u2019 wild peals of laughter; with the carefree merriment of schoolboys, they were plainly entertaining themselves before going to bed. But at a quarter past eleven everything was quiet, and when midnight sounded, he ventured forth. The owl struck against the windowpanes, the raven croaked from the ancient yew, and the wind roamed mournfully around the house like a wandering soul. The Otis family, however, slept unaware of their fate, while high above the rain and tempest he could hear the United States Minister\u2019s steady snoring.\"}"
