{"request_id":"73d6f25c2d5ebf02da25be4b","rewritten_text":"It then indicated that I had reached an altitude of 132,000 feet, or twenty-five miles, and I therefore surveyed at that moment an area of the earth amounting to no less than one three-hundred-and-twentieth of its entire surface. At nine o’clock I had once again lost sight of land to the east, but not before I noticed that the balloon was drifting rapidly toward the N. N. W. The ocean’s curvature beneath me was unmistakable, though my view was often interrupted by masses of cloud floating back and forth. I now observed that even the lightest vapors rose no higher than ten miles above sea level.

“At half past nine I tried throwing a handful of feathers through the valve. They did not float as I had expected, but dropped straight down, like a bullet, all together and with tremendous speed—vanishing from sight within a few seconds. At first I could not understand this extraordinary phenomenon, since I could not believe that my rate of ascent had suddenly accelerated so enormously. But it soon occurred to me that the atmosphere was now far too thin to support even feathers; that they had truly fallen, as they seemed to, with great rapidity; and that I had been deceived by the combined speeds of their descent and my own ascent.

“By ten o’clock I found that I had very little to occupy my immediate attention. Everything was proceeding smoothly, and I believed the balloon was rising with a speed that increased every moment, though I no longer had any means of measuring that increase. I felt no pain or discomfort of any kind and was in better spirits than at any time since leaving Rotterdam. I occupied myself by examining my various instruments and by renewing the air inside the chamber. I decided to do the latter at regular intervals of forty minutes, more for the sake of preserving my health than because such frequent renewal was absolutely necessary. Meanwhile, I could not help forming anticipations. Fancy wandered through the moon’s wild and dreamlike regions. Imagination, feeling herself free at last, roamed wherever she pleased among the ever-changing wonders of a shadowy, unstable land. Now I saw ancient, hoary forests, jagged precipices, and waterfalls plunging noisily into bottomless abysses. Then I suddenly entered silent noonday solitudes, where no heavenly wind ever intruded, and where vast fields of poppies and slender, lily-like flowers stretched into the distance, forever silent and still."}

