          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"02d6f1b6a7f9cc0c9bd1be89\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cIs that all? It isn\u2019t much! Many are under the Republic\u2019s displeasure and must look out through the little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which is he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am he. Necessarily, since I am the last.\u201d\\n\\nIt is Jarvis Lorry who has answered all the earlier questions. It is Jarvis Lorry who has climbed down and now stands with his hand on the coach door, replying to a group of officials. They stroll around the carriage and slowly climb onto the box to inspect the little luggage it carries on the roof; the country people lingering nearby press closer to the coach doors and stare greedily inside. A small child, carried by its mother, stretches out its short arm so that it may touch the wife of an aristocrat who has gone to the Guillotine.\\n\\n\u201cHere are your papers, Jarvis Lorry, countersigned.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne may leave, citizen?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne may leave. Forward, my postilions! A good journey!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI salute you, citizens\u2014and the first danger is past!\u201d\\n\\nThese are again Jarvis Lorry\u2019s words as he clasps his hands and looks upward. There is terror in the carriage, weeping, and the heavy breathing of the unconscious traveler.\\n\\n\u201cAre we not going too slowly? Can\u2019t they be persuaded to go faster?\u201d Lucie asks, clinging to the old man.\\n\\n\u201cIt would look like flight, my darling. I must not urge them too strongly; it would arouse suspicion.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLook back, look back, and see whether we are being pursued!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe road is clear, my dearest. So far, no one is pursuing us.\u201d\\n\\nHouses in twos and threes pass us, along with solitary farms, ruined buildings, dye-works, tanneries, and the like; then open country and avenues of leafless trees. The hard, uneven pavement lies beneath us, while soft, deep mud borders it on either side. At times we turn into the mud along the road to avoid stones that clatter against us and shake us; at others, we become stuck in ruts and bogs. Our impatience then becomes so agonizing that, in our wild alarm and haste, we want to get out and run\u2014hide\u2014do anything rather than stop.\\n\\nFrom the open country, back again among ruined buildings, solitary farms, dye-works, tanneries, and the like, cottages in twos and threes, and avenues of leafless trees. Have these men deceived us and taken us back by another road? Is this not the same place twice? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look back, look back, and see whether we are being pursued! Hush! The posting-house.\\n\\nSlowly, our four horses are taken out; slowly, the coach stands in the little street, stripped of horses and with no apparent chance of ever moving again; slowly, the new horses come into view, one by one; slowly, the new postilions follow, sucking and plaiting their whip lashes; slowly, the old postilions count their money, make incorrect additions, and reach dissatisfied conclusions.\"}"
