          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7bbf54c8a3fd1fee598da015\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Now that he had no work to occupy him, he placed the knuckles of his right hand in the hollow of his left, then the knuckles of his left hand in the hollow of his right. Then he passed a hand over his bearded chin, repeating these movements in a steady pattern without pause. Bringing him back from the wandering state he always fell into after speaking was like rousing a very weak person from a faint, or trying to hold back the spirit of a dying man in the hope of hearing some final revelation.\\n\\n\u201cDid you ask me for my name?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly I did.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIs that all?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne Hundred and Five, North Tower.\u201d\\n\\nWith a tired sound that was neither a sigh nor a groan, he bent over his work again, until the silence was broken once more.\\n\\n\u201cYou are not a shoemaker by trade?\u201d said Mr. Lorry, watching him closely.\\n\\nHis worn eyes turned toward Defarge, as though he wished to pass the question to him. But when no help came from that direction, he looked back at the questioner after first lowering his gaze to the ground.\\n\\n\u201cI am not a shoemaker by trade? No, I was not a shoemaker by trade. I\u2014I learned it here. I taught myself. I asked permission to\u2014\u201d\\n\\nHe drifted away again, even for several minutes, all the while making those measured movements with his hands. At last his eyes slowly returned to the face from which they had wandered. When they settled on it, he started and continued like a sleeper suddenly awakened, returning to a subject from the night before.\\n\\n\u201cI asked permission to teach myself, and after a long time, with great difficulty, I received it. I have made shoes ever since.\u201d\\n\\nAs he held out his hand for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry continued to look steadily into his face.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette, do you remember nothing of me?\u201d\\n\\nThe shoe fell to the ground, and he sat staring at the questioner.\\n\\n\u201cMonsieur Manette\u201d\u2014Mr. Lorry placed his hand on Defarge\u2019s arm\u2014\u201cdo you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Is there no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no memory of former times rising in your mind, Monsieur Manette?\u201d\\n\\nAs the prisoner of so many years sat staring by turns at Mr. Lorry and Defarge, faint traces of the alert intelligence once visible in the center of his forehead slowly pushed through the black mist that had covered him. They clouded over again, grew weaker, and disappeared; but they had been there.\"}"
