          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b4285d23e42bd1a86711b3d8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Sir Walter Elliot, of Kellynch Hall in Somersetshire, was a man who, for his own entertainment, never opened any book except the Baronetage; in it he found employment for an idle hour, and comfort in a troubled one. There his faculties were stirred to admiration and reverence as he considered the small surviving number of the earliest patents; there unpleasant feelings caused by domestic matters turned naturally into pity and disdain while he examined the nearly countless creations of the last century; and there, when every other page failed to interest him, he could read his own history with unfailing attention. This was the page to which the cherished volume always opened:\\n\\n\u201cELLIOT OF KELLYNCH HALL.\\n\\n\u201cWalter Elliot, born March 1, 1760, married, July 15, 1784, Elizabeth, daughter of James Stevenson, Esq. of South Park, in the county of Gloucester, by which lady (who died 1800) he has issue Elizabeth, born June 1, 1785; Anne, born August 9, 1787; a still-born son, November 5, 1789; Mary, born November 20, 1791.\u201d\\n\\nThe paragraph had originally appeared from the printer\u2019s hands exactly in that form; but Sir Walter had improved it by adding, for his own and his family\u2019s information, these words after the date of Mary\u2019s birth\u2014\u201cMarried, December 16, 1810, Charles, son and heir of Charles Musgrove, Esq. of Uppercross, in the county of Somerset,\u201d and by inserting with the greatest precision the day of the month on which he had lost his wife.\\n\\nThere followed, in the customary language, the history and advancement of the ancient and respectable family: how it had first settled in Cheshire; how it was mentioned by Dugdale; how its members had served as high sheriff, represented a borough in three successive parliaments, displayed loyalty, and received the dignity of baronet in the first year of Charles II, together with all the Marys and Elizabeths whom they had married. Altogether, it filled two handsome duodecimo pages, ending with the arms and motto\u2014\u201cPrincipal seat, Kellynch Hall, in the county of Somerset,\u201d\u2014and Sir Walter\u2019s handwriting once more in this conclusion:\\n\\n\u201cHeir presumptive, William Walter Elliot, Esq., great grandson of the second Sir Walter.\u201d\\n\\nVanity formed both the beginning and the end of Sir Walter Elliot\u2019s character: vanity of person and of position. He had been remarkably handsome when young, and at fifty-four remained a very fine man. Few women could think more highly of their appearance than he did, nor could the valet of any newly made lord be more pleased with the station he occupied in society. He regarded the blessing of beauty as second only to that of a baronetcy; and Sir Walter Elliot, who possessed both advantages, was the continual object of his deepest respect and devotion.\\n\\nHis good looks and rank had one reasonable claim upon his attachment, since to them he must have owed a wife of far superior character to anything his own qualities deserved.\"}"
