          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"882141724c3aea8093ac975a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Countrymen, butchers, drovers, hawkers, boys, thieves, idlers, and vagabonds of every inferior sort were crowded together in one mass; the drovers\u2019 whistling, the dogs\u2019 barking, the oxen\u2019s bellowing and plunging, the sheep\u2019s bleating, the pigs\u2019 grunting and squealing, the hawkers\u2019 cries, and the shouts, oaths, and quarrels on every side; the ringing of bells and tumult of voices pouring from each public-house; the pressing, jostling, driving, beating, whooping, and yelling; the dreadful, discordant din echoing from every part of the market; and the unwashed, unshaven, ragged, and filthy figures continually darting to and fro, and bursting in and out of the crowd; made it a deafening and bewildering spectacle, which utterly confused the senses.\\n\\nMr. Sikes, pulling Oliver behind him, forced his way through the densest part of the throng, paying scarcely any heed to the many sights and sounds that so amazed the boy. He nodded two or three times to a friend who passed; and, declining just as many offers to take a morning dram, continued firmly onward until they had escaped the uproar and passed through Hosier Lane into Holborn.\\n\\n\u201cNow, young \u2019un!\u201d said Sikes, glancing up at the clock of St. Andrew\u2019s Church, \u201cclose upon seven! You must quicken your pace. Come, don\u2019t begin lagging already, Lazy-legs!\u201d\\n\\nWith these words, Mr. Sikes gave his little companion\u2019s wrist a jerk; Oliver increased his pace to a sort of trot between a brisk walk and a run, and kept alongside the house-breaker\u2019s rapid strides as best he could.\\n\\nThey continued at this speed until they had gone beyond Hyde Park Corner and were proceeding toward Kensington, when Sikes slackened his pace until an empty cart some distance behind them caught up. Noticing \u201cHounslow\u201d painted upon it, he asked the driver, with all the civility he could muster, whether he would carry them as far as Isleworth.\\n\\n\u201cJump up,\u201d said the man. \u201cIs that your boy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; he\u2019s my boy,\u201d answered Sikes, staring hard at Oliver and absently placing his hand in the pocket containing the pistol.\\n\\n\u201cYour father walks a little too fast for you, doesn\u2019t he, my lad?\u201d asked the driver, observing that Oliver was breathless.\\n\\n\u201cNot at all,\u201d Sikes cut in. \u201cHe\u2019s accustomed to it. Here, take my hand, Ned. In with you!\u201d\\n\\nSpeaking thus to Oliver, he helped him into the cart; and the driver, indicating a pile of sacks, told him to lie there and rest.\\n\\nAs they passed the successive milestones, Oliver grew increasingly curious about where his companion intended to take him. Kensington, Hammersmith, Chiswick, Kew Bridge, and Brentford were all left behind; yet they continued onward as steadily as though their journey had only just commenced.\"}"
