{"request_id":"1828e99ff4814fcd2818b4f8","rewritten_text":"O spirits of the storm, take me! Powers of destruction, open your arms and hold me forever! Unless some kinder power has chosen another end for me, so that after long suffering I may receive my reward and once more feel my heart beating beside the heart of someone like myself.

The Tiber, the road laid out by nature’s own hand as it winds across the continent, lay at my feet, and many boats were tied along its banks. With a few books, some provisions, and my dog, I would board one of them and drift down the river into the sea. Then, keeping close to land, I would follow the beautiful shores and sunny headlands of the blue Mediterranean, pass Naples and travel along Calabria, and risk the twin dangers of Scylla and Charybdis. With a fearless purpose—for what had I left to lose?—I would skim across the sea toward Malta and the distant Cyclades. I would avoid Constantinople, whose familiar towers and inlets belonged to a different life from mine. I would follow the coasts of Asia Minor and Syria, pass the seven-mouthed Nile, and turn north again. Leaving forgotten Carthage and deserted Libya behind, I would reach the Pillars of Hercules. And then—anywhere would do—the slimy caves and silent depths of the ocean might become my home before I finished this long voyage, or disease might strike my heart as I drifted alone across the restless Mediterranean. Or, at some place where I stopped, I might find what I sought: a companion. If not, then through endless years, bent and gray-haired, with youth already buried beside those I loved, the lonely wanderer would still raise his sail and hold the tiller. Still obeying the winds of heaven, I would pass one headland after another, anchor in one bay after another, and continue crossing the empty sea. Leaving behind Europe’s green native shores, I would travel down Africa’s tawny coast, survive the fierce seas around the Cape, and at last moor my worn boat in a creek shaded by the fragrant groves of the spice-filled islands of the far Indian Ocean.

These are wild dreams. Yet ever since they came to me a week ago, as I stood on the heights of St. Peter’s, they have ruled my imagination. I have chosen my boat and gathered my small supplies. I have selected a few books, chiefly Homer and Shakespeare. But the libraries of the world are open to me, and I can replace my books at any port. I do not expect my life to improve, but the dull present has become unbearable."}

