          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"28da8864fb56c4b65007002f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cBut,\u201d said I, handing the slip back to him, \u201cI am no less bewildered than before. Had all the jewels of Golconda been promised me for solving this enigma, I am certain I could never have won them.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet,\u201d said Legrand, \u201cthe solution is not nearly so difficult as a first hurried examination of the characters might lead you to believe. These characters, as anyone might readily surmise, constitute a cipher\u2014that is, they carry a meaning; but, knowing what we do of Kidd, I could not imagine him capable of devising any of the more intricate cryptographs. I immediately concluded that this was a simple kind\u2014one which, nevertheless, would seem to the sailor\u2019s rough understanding utterly impossible to decipher without the key.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd you actually solved it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cEasily; I have solved others ten thousand times more obscure. Circumstances, together with a particular bent of mind, have made such puzzles interesting to me, and it may reasonably be questioned whether human ingenuity can create an enigma that human ingenuity, properly applied, cannot unravel. Indeed, once I had established that the characters were connected and legible, I scarcely considered the mere difficulty of discovering their meaning.\\n\\n\u201cIn this instance\u2014as in every case of secret writing\u2014the first matter to determine is the cipher\u2019s language; for the methods of solution, particularly with the simpler ciphers, depend upon and vary according to the character of the particular idiom. Usually, there is no choice but to test, guided by probability, every language known to the person attempting the solution, until the correct one is found. But with the cipher before us, the signature removed all uncertainty. The play upon the word \u2018Kidd\u2019 can be understood in no language except English. Without that clue, I should have begun with Spanish and French, the languages in which a secret of this sort would most naturally have been written by a pirate of the Spanish Main. As it was, I assumed the cryptograph was English.\\n\\n\u201cYou will notice that there are no spaces between the words. Had spaces been present, the task would have been comparatively simple. I should then have begun by comparing and analyzing the shorter words; and if a one-letter word had appeared, as would most likely be the case\u2014\u2018a\u2019 or \u2018I,\u2019 for instance\u2014I should have regarded the solution as certain. But since there are no divisions, my first step was to determine the letters occurring most often, as well as those appearing least frequently.\u201d\"}"
