          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2e31b60c2527bebe393a07b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGood-bye, Uncle George. I\u2019ll be late for lunch if I stay any longer. Thank you for giving me the information I wanted. I always like to know everything about my new friends, and nothing about my old ones.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhere are you having lunch, Harry?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt Aunt Agatha\u2019s. I\u2019ve invited myself and Mr. Gray. He\u2019s her latest _prot\u00e9g\u00e9_.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHumph! Tell your Aunt Agatha, Harry, not to trouble me anymore with her charity appeals. I\u2019m sick of them. Why, the good woman thinks I have nothing to do but write cheques for her silly causes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll right, Uncle George, I\u2019ll tell her, but it won\u2019t make any difference. Philanthropic people lose all sense of humanity. That is their defining characteristic.\u201d\\n\\nThe old gentleman growled approvingly and rang for his servant. Lord Henry walked through the low arcade into Burlington Street and headed toward Berkeley Square.\\n\\nSo that was the story of Dorian Gray\u2019s parentage. Crude as the account had been, it had still moved him with the suggestion of a strange, almost modern romance. A beautiful woman risking everything for a mad passion. A few wild weeks of happiness cut short by a hideous, treacherous crime. Months of silent agony, followed by a child born in pain. The mother taken by death, the boy left to solitude and the tyranny of an old, loveless man. Yes; it was an interesting background. It gave the boy distinction, made him more perfect, so to speak. Behind every exquisite thing that existed, there was something tragic. Worlds had to be in labor so that the meanest flower might bloom.... And how charming he had been at dinner the night before, sitting opposite him at the club with startled eyes and lips parted in frightened pleasure, the red candle shades deepening the rose in his awakening face. Talking to him was like playing an exquisite violin. He responded to every touch and tremor of the bow.... There was something terribly enthralling about exercising influence. No other activity was like it. To project one\u2019s soul into some gracious form and let it remain there for a moment; to hear one\u2019s own intellectual views echoed back with all the added music of passion and youth; to convey one\u2019s temperament into another as though it were a subtle fluid or a strange perfume: there was genuine joy in that\u2014perhaps the most satisfying joy left to us in an age as limited and vulgar as our own, an age grossly carnal in its pleasures and grossly common in its aims.... He was a marvelous type, too, this boy whom, by such a curious chance, he had met in Basil\u2019s studio\u2014or could be shaped into a marvelous type, at any rate.\"}"
