          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ce6a6438791d2302e2643fa9\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cApril 12th. A remarkable change occurred in the balloon\u2019s direction, and although I had fully anticipated it, it gave me the most unmistakable delight. After following its former course to about the twentieth parallel of southern latitude, it suddenly veered eastward at an acute angle and continued in that direction throughout the day, keeping nearly, if not entirely, in the exact plane of the lunar ellipse. Remarkably, this change of route caused a very noticeable swaying of the car\u2014a motion that continued, to a greater or lesser degree, for many hours.\\n\\n\u201cApril 13th. I was again greatly alarmed by a recurrence of the loud, crackling noise that had terrified me on the tenth. I reflected on it for a long time but could reach no satisfactory conclusion. The earth\u2019s apparent diameter had greatly diminished and now subtended, from the balloon, an angle of scarcely more than twenty-five degrees. The moon was completely invisible, being almost directly overhead. I remained in the plane of the ellipse but made little eastward progress.\\n\\n\u201cApril 14th. The earth\u2019s diameter was shrinking with extreme rapidity. Today I became firmly convinced that the balloon was actually ascending along the line of apsides toward the point of perigee\u2014in other words, following the direct course that would carry it straight to the moon at the point in its orbit nearest the earth. The moon itself was directly overhead and therefore hidden from my sight. Great and prolonged effort was required to condense the atmosphere.\\n\\n\u201cApril 15th. The outlines of continents and seas could no longer be distinguished on the earth with anything approaching clarity. Around noon, I became aware for the third time of that terrifying sound which had previously astonished me. This time, however, it continued for several moments, growing louder as it went on. At last, while I stood stupefied and terrified, expecting some dreadful destruction but not knowing what, the car shook with extraordinary violence, and a gigantic, flaming mass of material, which I could not identify, came roaring and booming past the balloon with the sound of a thousand thunders. When my fear and astonishment had somewhat subsided, I had little difficulty in supposing that it was some enormous volcanic fragment expelled from the world toward which I was rapidly approaching\u2014most likely one of that strange class of substances sometimes found on earth and called meteoric stones for lack of a better name.\\n\\n\u201cApril 16th. Today, looking upward as best I could through each side window in turn, I saw, to my great delight, a very small portion of the moon\u2019s disk protruding on every side beyond the balloon\u2019s enormous circumference.\u201d\"}"
