          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"52d888a4d73cf4f73061a7b7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The next day was chosen for the production, lest our honour should grow cold through delay, and Drummle appeared with a polite little statement in Estella\u2019s handwriting, saying that she had had the honour of dancing with him several times. This left me no choice but to regret that I had been \u201cbetrayed into a warmth which,\u201d and, in general, to deny as impossible the idea that I was to be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat snorting at each other for an hour, while the Grove contradicted everything indiscriminately. At last, we declared that the cause of good feeling had advanced at an astonishing rate.\\n\\nI speak of this lightly, but it was no light matter to me. I cannot fully express the pain it caused me to think that Estella should show favour to such a contemptible, clumsy, sulky fool, so far below even the average. To this day, I believe it was because of the pure generosity and selflessness of my love for her that I could not bear the thought of her lowering herself to that hound. No doubt I should have been miserable whoever had won her favour; but a more worthy object would have caused a different kind and degree of suffering.\\n\\nIt was easy to discover, and I soon did discover, that Drummle had begun following her closely, and that she allowed him to do so. Before long, he was always pursuing her, and he and I crossed paths every day. He continued in a dull, stubborn way, while Estella kept him going: sometimes encouraging him, sometimes discouraging him, sometimes almost flattering him, sometimes openly despising him, sometimes seeming to know him very well, and sometimes barely remembering who he was.\\n\\nThe Spider, as Mr. Jaggers had called him, was accustomed to waiting in ambush and had the patience of his kind. Besides this, he had a foolish confidence in his money and his family\u2019s importance, which sometimes served him well, almost taking the place of focus and firm purpose. Thus the Spider, stubbornly watching Estella, outlasted many brighter insects, and would often uncoil himself and drop down at exactly the right moment.\\n\\nAt a certain Assembly Ball in Richmond\u2014there were Assembly Balls in most places then\u2014Estella had outshone all the other beauties. Drummle blundered around her so persistently, and with such tolerance from her, that I decided to speak to her about him. I took the next opportunity, when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home and was sitting apart among some flowers, ready to leave.\"}"
