d, \u201clet it stand. Now, looking at you, sir, I shouldn\u2019t be surprised if you were planning and arranging to have an agreeable home of your own someday, when you\u2019ve grown weary of all this work.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Jaggers nodded thoughtfully two or three times and actually sighed. \u201cPip,\u201d he said, \u201cwe won\u2019t speak of \u2018poor dreams\u2019; you know more of such matters than I do, having much fresher experience in that direction. But now to this other business. I\u2019ll put a case before you. Mind! I admit nothing.\u201d\\n\\nHe waited until I had declared that I fully understood his explicit statement that he admitted nothing.\\n\\n\u201cNow, Pip,\u201d said Mr. Jaggers, \u201csuppose this case. Suppose a woman, in circumstances such as you have described, kept her child hidden, and was compelled to tell the fact to her legal adviser, because he represented to her that, with regard to the scope of his defence, he had to know how matters stood concerning that child.\u201d\"}"
