{"request_id":"19e401faba9a79a8d7f53468","rewritten_text":"Indeed, I felt no concern for her. She was what is commonly called a remarkable woman, quite capable of managing worldly affairs without my help. To speak honestly, I think she had always regarded me as an idle lad, a mere encumbrance, fit for nothing except constructing castles in the air, and was somewhat pleased to be rid of me. It was a dark night when I said farewell to her, and, taking as my aides-de-camp the three creditors who had caused me so much trouble, carried the balloon, with its car and equipment, by a circuitous route to the station where the remaining articles had been stored. We found everything there untouched, and I at once set about my work.

“It was the first of April. The night, as I have already said, was dark; not a star could be seen; and a drizzling rain, falling from time to time, made us extremely uncomfortable. My principal worry, however, was the balloon, which, despite the varnish protecting it, was beginning to become rather heavy from the damp; the powder, too, was in danger of being spoiled. I consequently kept my three duns busily employed, breaking ice around the central cask and stirring the acid in the others. They did not, nevertheless, stop pestering me with questions about what I meant to do with all this apparatus, and complained bitterly of the dreadful labor to which I subjected them. They could not understand, they said, what benefit could come from being soaked to the skin merely to take part in such frightful incantations. I grew anxious and worked with all my strength, for I sincerely believe the fools imagined I had made a bargain with the devil, and that, in brief, my present undertaking was anything but proper. I was therefore very much afraid they would abandon me altogether. Still, I managed to quiet them by promising to settle every account in full as soon as I had brought the current business to an end. They naturally put their own construction upon these assurances, supposing, no doubt, that I would soon possess enormous sums of ready money; and so long as I paid everything I owed them, with a little extra for their services, I daresay they cared very little what happened to either my soul or my body.

“In about four and a half hours I judged the balloon sufficiently inflated. I therefore fastened on the car and placed all my instruments inside it—not forgetting the condensing apparatus, a plentiful supply of water, and a large store of provisions, including pemmican, which contains much nourishment in a comparatively small space."}

