          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"37f1a3d7b6b63d150179b782\",\"rewritten_text\":\"But I wonder how long you _would_ have continued, had you been left to yourself. I wonder when you _would_ have spoken if I had not questioned you! My determination to thank you for your kindness to Lydia certainly produced a powerful effect. _Too powerful_, I fear; for what becomes of the moral, if our happiness arises from breaking a promise, since I ought not to have brought up the subject? This will never do.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou need not trouble yourself. The moral will remain entirely just. Lady Catherine\u2019s unjustifiable attempts to part us were what dispelled all my doubts. I do not owe my present happiness to your eager wish to express your gratitude. I was in no mood to wait for you to begin. My aunt\u2019s information had inspired me with hope, and I was resolved immediately to learn everything.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLady Catherine has been of immeasurable service, which ought to please her, since she delights in being useful. But tell me, why did you come to Netherfield? Was it only to ride to Longbourn and feel embarrassed? or had you planned consequences of a more serious nature?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy true object was to see _you_, and to determine, if possible, whether I could ever hope to make you love me. My declared object, or the one I admitted to myself, was to discover whether your sister still cared for Bingley, and, if she did, to make to him the confession I have since made.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you ever find the courage to tell Lady Catherine what is going to happen to her?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am more likely to require time than courage, Elizabeth. But it must be done; and if you will give me a sheet of paper, it shall be done at once.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd if I had not a letter of my own to write, I might sit beside you and admire the steadiness of your handwriting, as another young lady once did. But I have an aunt as well, who must not be neglected any longer.\u201d\\n\\nBecause she was unwilling to admit how greatly she had exaggerated her intimacy with Mr. Darcy, Elizabeth had not yet replied to Mrs. Gardiner\u2019s lengthy letter; but now, having _that_ to communicate which she knew would be most welcome, she was nearly ashamed to discover that her uncle and aunt had already been deprived of three days\u2019 happiness, and at once wrote as follows:--\\n\\n\u201cI should have thanked you sooner, my dear aunt, as I ought, for your long, kind, and satisfying account of everything; but, to tell the truth, I was too cross to write. You imagined more than truly existed. But _now_ imagine as much as you please; release your fancy, indulge your imagination in every flight the subject can possibly inspire, and unless you suppose me actually married, you cannot be very far wrong.\"}"
