          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"93fcd1f735069f6736145feb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond was uplifted by the powerful sensations of restored health. As general of the Athenians, he felt he held a position worthy of his ambition. In hoping to conquer Constantinople, he looked toward an event that would stand out across the ages: an achievement unmatched in human history. A city rich in historic associations, whose beautiful setting was the wonder of the world, and which had been the Moslems\u2019 stronghold for centuries, would be freed from slavery and barbarism and returned to a people famous for genius, civilization, and love of liberty. Perdita depended on his restored society, his love, hopes, and fame, as a Sybarite depends on a luxurious couch; every thought brought delight, and every feeling seemed immersed in a warm, welcoming atmosphere.\\n\\nWe reached Kishan on July 7. The weather throughout our journey had been calm. Each day, before dawn, we left our night\u2019s camp and watched the shadows withdraw from hill and valley before the sun\u2019s golden arrival. The soldiers traveling with us, with their usual liveliness, took enthusiastic pleasure in the beauty of nature. They greeted the rising sun with triumphant music, while birds, heard now and then, filled the pauses. At noon, we set up our tents in a shady valley or a wooded hollow among the mountains, while a stream murmuring over pebbles invited grateful sleep. Our evening march was calmer and even more delightful than the morning\u2019s eager activity. When the band played, it instinctively chose music of restrained feeling: a lover\u2019s farewell or a lament for absence, followed and ended by a solemn hymn that suited the peaceful beauty of evening and lifted the soul toward noble, religious thoughts. Often every sound stopped so that we could hear the nightingale, while fireflies danced brightly and the soft cooing of the aziolo promised fair weather to the travelers. When we passed through a valley, gentle shadows surrounded us and rocks glowed with beautiful colors. When we crossed a mountain, Greece lay below like a living map, its famous peaks rising into the sky and its rivers tracing silver lines through the fertile land. Almost afraid to breathe, we English travelers gazed in wonder at this magnificent landscape, so unlike the sober colors and melancholy beauty of our native scenery. After we left Macedonia, the fertile but low plains of Thrace offered fewer sights, though the journey remained interesting. An advance guard announced our approach, and the country people quickly began preparing to honor Lord Raymond.\"}"
