{"request_id":"cfd11dd0ec60b75b80112617","rewritten_text":"Madame and Mademoiselle L’Espanaye were not killed by spirits. Their murderer was a physical being and escaped by physical means. But how? Fortunately, there is only one way to reason this out, and it must lead to a definite answer. Let us examine every possible way out. The killers were clearly in the room where Mademoiselle L’Espanaye was found, or at least in the adjoining room, when the group came up the stairs. So we need look for an exit only from those two rooms. The police had searched the floors, ceilings, and walls in every direction. No secret passage could have escaped them. Still, I did not rely on their eyes; I examined everything myself. There were no hidden exits. Both doors from the rooms into the hallway were securely locked, with the keys inside. Let us consider the chimneys. Though they were of ordinary width for eight or ten feet above the fireplaces, they were too narrow throughout the rest of their length to admit even a large cat. Since escape by these means was absolutely impossible, we are left with the windows. No one could have escaped through the windows of the front room without being seen by the crowd in the street. The murderers must, therefore, have gone through the windows of the back room. Once reasoning has brought us to this conclusion so clearly, we must not reject it because it seems impossible. We need only show that these apparent “impossibilities” are not truly impossible.

“There are two windows in the room. One is completely visible and has no furniture blocking it. The lower part of the other is hidden by the head of the large bed, which stands pressed against it. The first window was found firmly fastened from inside. It resisted all the force of those who tried to raise it. A large hole had been drilled in its frame on the left, and a very strong nail had been driven into it almost to the head. When the other window was examined, a similar nail was found in the same position, and a forceful attempt to raise that sash also failed. The police were now completely convinced that no one had escaped through the windows. Therefore, they thought it unnecessary to remove the nails and open them.

“My own examination was more careful, for the reason I have just explained: I knew that here, above all, every apparent impossibility had to be shown not to be impossible in reality."}

