          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"36c325d05741b30d88abc132\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Reading over his letter again while answering it had such a softening effect that it was especially necessary to strengthen her resolve with a few firm expressions; and she was so troubled by the thought of making him unhappy, thought so much about what his mother and sisters would think and say, and was so anxious that they should not consider her ungrateful, that Emma believed, if the young man had crossed her path at that moment, he would have been accepted after all.\\n\\nThe letter, however, was written, sealed, and sent. The matter was settled, and Harriet was safe. She was rather downcast all evening, but Emma could make allowances for her kind-hearted regrets, sometimes easing them by speaking of her own affection, and sometimes by bringing Mr. Elton to mind.\\n\\n\u201cI shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d she said in a rather sorrowful tone.\\n\\n\u201cNor, if you were, could I ever bear to part with you, my Harriet. You are far too necessary at Hartfield to be spared to Abbey-Mill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I am sure I should never want to go there; for I am never happy anywhere but at Hartfield.\u201d\\n\\nSome time later, she said, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be very surprised if she knew what had happened. I am sure Miss Nash would be\u2014for Miss Nash thinks her own sister very well married, and she is only married to a linen-draper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne ought to regret seeing greater pride or refinement in a schoolteacher, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would envy you such an opportunity to marry. Even this conquest would seem valuable in her eyes. As for anything better suited to you, I suppose she is completely unaware of it. The attentions of a certain person can hardly yet be part of Highbury gossip. So far, I believe you and I are the only people whose understanding has been enlightened by his looks and manners.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet blushed and smiled, and said something about being surprised that people should like her so much. The thought of Mr. Elton was certainly encouraging; but after a while she again felt tenderly toward the rejected Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow he has received my letter,\u201d she said softly. \u201cI wonder what they are all doing\u2014whether his sisters know\u2014if he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope he will not take it too badly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet us think of those absent friends who are more cheerfully occupied,\u201d cried Emma. \u201cAt this moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is showing your picture to his mother and sisters, telling them how much more beautiful the original is, and, after being asked for it five or six times, allowing them to hear your name\u2014your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
