          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e4b86bf5c002061bf450efc8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"For years, Dorian Gray could not escape the influence of this book. Or perhaps it would be more accurate to say that he never tried to escape it. From Paris he obtained no fewer than nine large-paper copies of the first edition and had them bound in different colours, so they would match his various moods and the shifting fancies of a nature over which he sometimes seemed to have almost completely lost control. The hero, that remarkable young Parisian in whom romantic and scientific temperaments were so strangely combined, became for him a kind of prophetic image of himself. Indeed, the entire book seemed to contain the story of his own life, written before he had lived it.\\n\\nIn one respect he was luckier than the novel\u2019s fantastic hero. He never knew\u2014never had any reason to know\u2014that almost grotesque fear of mirrors, polished metal, and still water which came over the young Parisian so early in life, caused by the sudden decay of a beauty that had once apparently been so extraordinary. With almost cruel delight\u2014and perhaps cruelty has a place in nearly every joy, as surely as it does in every pleasure\u2014he would read the latter part of the book, with its genuinely tragic, though somewhat exaggerated, account of the grief and despair of a man who had himself lost what he had valued most dearly in others and in the world.\\n\\nFor the extraordinary beauty that had so captivated Basil Hallward, and many others besides him, seemed never to leave Dorian. Even those who had heard the worst things about him\u2014and from time to time strange rumours about his way of life spread through London and became the talk of the clubs\u2014could not believe anything dishonourable of him when they saw him. He always looked like someone who had kept himself untouched by the world. Men who spoke crudely fell silent when Dorian Gray entered the room. There was something in the