{"request_id":"71d9b622afc6e0824357930a","rewritten_text":"Few people could claim to have such a pure heart and such genuine humility, joined with firm trust in her own honesty and faith in the honesty of others. She had lived a secluded life from birth. Her mother had died when she was very young, but her father had devoted himself to educating her. He held many unusual ideas, which shaped the system he used to raise her. She knew the heroes of Greece and Rome, and those of England who had lived centuries earlier, but she knew almost nothing of current events. She had read few writers from the previous fifty years, though apart from that limitation her reading was very broad. Thus, although she seemed less familiar with the secrets of life and society than he was, her knowledge was deeper and rested on firmer foundations. Even if her beauty and gentleness had not captivated him, her intelligence would always have held him under its power. He looked to her as his guide, and he adored her so deeply that he enjoyed making himself feel even more inferior whenever she caused him to feel that way.

When he was nineteen, his mother died. He left college after this and, for a time, separated himself from his old friends, retiring to the neighborhood of his Diana and finding all his comfort in her sweet voice and still dearer caresses. This brief separation from his companions gave him the courage to assert his independence. He felt that, whatever ridicule they might express about his planned marriage, they would not dare show it after the marriage had taken place. So he sought the consent of his guardian, which he obtained with some difficulty, and the consent of his beloved’s father, which came more easily. Without telling anyone else of his plans, he became Diana’s husband before he reached his twentieth birthday.

He loved her passionately, and her tenderness charmed him so completely that he could think of nothing but her. He invited some of his college friends to visit him, but their foolishness disgusted him. Diana had torn away the veil that had kept him in childhood. He had become a man, and he was amazed that he had ever joined in the empty words and opinions of his fellow students, or that he had ever feared the judgment of people like them. He abandoned his old friendships not because he was fickle, but because they truly no longer suited him."}

