          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"18fa17850ff4b6879b354bdc\",\"rewritten_text\":\"No letter of condolence had been sent to Ireland. The neglect had come back upon the guilty party; when poor Lady Elliot herself died, no letter of condolence arrived at Kellynch. There was therefore too much reason to fear that the Dalrymples considered the family connection ended. How to put this troubling matter right and be accepted as cousins again was the question. In a more sensible way, neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot thought it unimportant. \u201cFamily connections were always worth preserving, and good company always worth seeking. Lady Dalrymple had taken a house in Laura Place for three months and would be living in style. She had been in Bath the year before, and Lady Russell had heard her described as a charming woman. It was very desirable to renew the connection, if it could be done without any loss of propriety on the Elliots\u2019 part.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter, however, chose his own method. At last he wrote his right honourable cousin a very fine letter, full of explanation, regret, and entreaty. Neither Lady Russell nor Mr Elliot admired it, but it achieved what was needed: three lines of writing from the Dowager Viscountess. \u201cShe was very much honoured and would be happy to make their acquaintance.\u201d The difficult part was over, and the rewards began. They paid a visit to Laura Place, received the cards of Dowager Viscountess Dalrymple and the Honourable Miss Carteret, and placed them wherever they would be most noticed. \u201cOur cousins in Laura Place\u201d\u2014\u201cOur cousin, Lady Dalrymple and Miss Carteret\u201d\u2014were mentioned to everyone.\\n\\nAnne was ashamed. Even if Lady Dalrymple and her daughter had been very pleasant, she would still have been ashamed of the excitement they caused; but they were nothing special. They had no superiority in manner, accomplishments, or understanding. Lady Dalrymple had earned the name \u201ca charming 