          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6f4caf4cd891455698438fef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Wake up after a while and look around to see what had caused it, and maybe spot a steamboat coughing upstream, so far off toward the opposite bank that you couldn\u2019t make out anything about her except whether she was stern-wheel or side-wheel; then for nearly an hour there\u2019d be nothing to hear and nothing to see\u2014just complete loneliness. Next you might see a raft drifting by, far off yonder, and perhaps some fellow on it chopping, because they\u2019re nearly always doing that on a raft; you\u2019d see the axe flash and fall\u2014you wouldn\u2019t hear a thing; you\u2019d watch it rise again, and when it was above the man\u2019s head, then you\u2019d hear the _k\u2019chunk!_\u2014it had taken all that time to cross the water. So we\u2019d spend the day loafing around, listening to the quiet. Once there was a heavy fog, and the rafts and other things passing by were banging tin pans so the steamboats wouldn\u2019t run them down. A scow or raft passed so near that we could hear them talking, cussing, and laughing\u2014hear them clearly; but we couldn\u2019t see the least sign of them; it made you feel creepy; it was like spirits carrying on in the air. Jim said he reckoned they were spirits; but I says:\\n\\n\u201cNo; spirits wouldn\u2019t say, \u2018Dern the dern fog.\u2019\u201d\\n\\nAs soon as it was fully dark we pushed off; when we had reached about midstream we left her alone and let her drift wherever the current chose to take her; then we lit our pipes, dangled our legs in the water, and talked about all sorts of things\u2014we were always naked, day and night, whenever the mosquitoes allowed it\u2014the new clothes Buck\u2019s family had made for me were too fine to be comfortable, and besides, I never cared much for clothes, anyway.\\n\\nSometimes we\u2019d have the whole river to ourselves for the longest while. There were the banks and islands over across the water; and perhaps a spark\u2014which was a candle shining from a cabin window; and sometimes on the water you could see a spark or two\u2014on a raft or scow, you know; and maybe hear a fiddle or a song drifting over from one of those boats. It\u2019s wonderful living on a raft. We had the sky above us, thick with stars, and we\u2019d lie on our backs looking up at them, arguing over whether they had been made or had simply happened. Jim said they were made, but I said they happened; I figured it would have taken too long to _make_ so many.\"}"
