          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c6af8b9d0e04572924cfe285\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was at Warwick Castle that I encountered the curious stranger I am about to describe. Three things drew me to him: his frank simplicity, his astonishing knowledge of ancient armor, and the ease of his company\u2014for he did all the talking. We joined, as quiet people do, the rear of the group being led through the castle, and he immediately began saying things that interested me. As he continued, speaking softly, pleasantly, and smoothly, he seemed to drift imperceptibly out of the present world and age into some distant era and forgotten land. Gradually he cast such a spell over me that I seemed to move among the ghosts, shadows, dust, and mold of a gray antiquity, conversing with one of its relics. Just as I might speak of my closest friends or enemies, or my most familiar neighbors, he spoke of Sir Bedivere, Sir Bors de Ganis, Sir Launcelot of the Lake, Sir Galahad, and all the other great names of the Round Table\u2014and as he went on, he came to look incredibly, impossibly old: faded, dry, musty, and ancient. Presently he turned to me and said, as casually as one might discuss the weather or any other ordinary subject\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou know about the transmigration of souls; do you know about the transposition of epochs\u2014and bodies?\u201d\\n\\nI said I had never heard of it. He seemed so little interested\u2014just as when people talk about the weather\u2014that he did not notice whether I answered him or not. There was half a moment of silence, broken at once by the droning voice of the paid guide:\\n\\n\u201cAncient hauberk, dating from the sixth century, the time of King Arthur and the Round Table; said to have belonged to the knight Sir Sagramor le Desirous; observe the round hole through the chain mail in the left breast; unaccounted for; believed to have been made by a bullet after firearms were invented\u2014perhaps maliciously by Cromwell\u2019s soldiers.\u201d\\n\\nMy acquaintance smiled\u2014not with a modern smile, but with one that must have fallen out of common use many centuries ago\u2014and murmured, apparently to himself:\\n\\n\u201cKnow this well: _I saw it done._\u201d Then, after a pause, he added: \u201cI did it myself.\u201d\\n\\nBy the time I had recovered from the electric shock of this remark, he was gone.\\n\\nThat evening I sat beside my fire at the Warwick Arms, immersed in a dream of the olden days, while rain struck the windows and wind roared around the eaves and corners. From time to time I dipped into old Sir Thomas Malory\u2019s enchanting book, feasted on its rich abundance of marvels and adventures, breathed the fragrance of its obsolete names, and dreamed again.\"}"
