          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"19b57f5d765c265ae6250b6c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Jane admitted that she was somewhat surprised by the match; but she spoke less of her astonishment than of her sincere wishes for their happiness, nor could Elizabeth persuade her to think it unlikely. Kitty and Lydia were very far from envying Miss Lucas, since Mr. Collins was merely a clergyman; and the event affected them in no other respect than as news to circulate through Meryton.\\n\\nLady Lucas could not fail to feel triumphant at being able to return to Mrs. Bennet the consolation of having a daughter well married; and she visited Longbourn rather more frequently than before to declare how happy she was, though Mrs. Bennet\u2019s sour countenance and unkind observations might have been sufficient to banish happiness altogether.\\n\\nBetween Elizabeth and Charlotte there was a reserve that kept them both silent on the matter; and Elizabeth was convinced that genuine confidence could never exist between them again. Her disappointment in Charlotte led her to regard her sister with increased affection, certain that her opinion of Jane\u2019s uprightness and delicacy could never be shaken, and becoming each day more concerned for her happiness, since Bingley had now been away a week and there was still no news of his return.\\n\\nJane had replied to Caroline\u2019s letter without delay, and was counting the days until she might reasonably expect another. Mr. Collins\u2019s promised letter of thanks arrived on Tuesday, addressed to their father, and composed with all the solemn gravity of gratitude that a year\u2019s residence with the family might have inspired. Having relieved his conscience on that subject, he went on to tell them, in many delighted expressions, of his happiness at having secured the affection of their amiable neighbour, Miss Lucas. He then explained that his eagerness to accept their kind invitation to return to Longbourn had arisen solely from his wish to enjoy her company, and that he hoped to come back on the Monday fortnight. Lady Catherine, he added, approved his marriage so warmly that she wished it to occur as soon as possible; and he trusted this would prove an irresistible argument with his amiable Charlotte for fixing an early day on which to make him the happiest of men.\\n\\nMr. Collins\u2019s return to Hertfordshire was no longer welcome to Mrs. Bennet. On the contrary, she was as ready to complain of it as her husband. It was very odd that he should come to Longbourn rather than Lucas Lodge; it was inconvenient as well as extremely troublesome. She disliked having visitors in the house while her health was so poor, and lovers were the most disagreeable people of all.\"}"
