          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e726e79c2cf24b10e0a2181d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"At the stairs where we had taken him aboard, and ever since, I had watched carefully for any sign that we were suspected. I had seen none. We certainly had not been, and at that time we were just as certainly neither accompanied nor followed by any boat. If any boat had been waiting on us, I would have run ashore and forced her either to continue on or to make her purpose clear. But we held our course without any sign of interference.\\n\\nHe wore his boat-cloak and looked, as I have said, like a natural part of the scene. It was remarkable\u2014but perhaps the miserable life he had led explained it\u2014that he was the least anxious of us all. He was not indifferent, for he told me that he hoped to live to see his gentleman become one of the finest gentlemen in a foreign country; nor, as I understood him, was he willing simply to submit or resign himself. But he had no intention of meeting danger halfway. When it came to him, he faced it; until then, he would not trouble himself.\\n\\n\u201cIf you knew, dear boy,\u201d he said to me, \u201cwhat it is to sit here alongside my dear boy and have my smoke, after being shut up day after day between four walls, you\u2019d envy me. But you don\u2019t know what it is.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think I know the pleasures of freedom,\u201d I answered.\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d he said, shaking his head gravely. \u201cBut you don\u2019t know it as I do. You must have been under lock and key, dear boy, to know it as I do\u2014but I\u2019m not going to be downhearted.\u201d\\n\\nIt seemed inconsistent to me that, for any ruling idea, he should have endangered his freedom and even his life. But I reflected that perhaps freedom without danger was so far removed from the whole habit of his existence that it could not mean to him what it would mean to another man. I was not far wrong, for after smoking a little he said:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cYou see, dear boy, when I was over there, on the other side of the world, I was always looking toward this side; and it became dull to be there, even though I was growing rich. Everybody knew Magwitch, and Magwitch could come and go, and nobody\u2019s head would be troubled by him. They aren\u2019t so easy about me here, dear boy\u2014wouldn\u2019t be, at least, if they knew where I was.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIf all goes well,\u201d I said, \u201cyou will be perfectly free and safe again within a few hours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell,\u201d he replied, drawing a long breath, \u201cI hope so.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd think so?\u201d\"}"
