          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"756209e0d8885b72ec3ce22d\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cJoseph!! Joseph!!!\u201d At that, he shook his head and tapped it, showing how inadequate he considered Joseph.\\n\\n\u201cBut my dear young friend,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, \u201cyou must be hungry, you must be exhausted. Sit down. Here is a chicken from the Boar, here is a tongue from the Boar, and here are one or two little things from the Boar, which I hope you will not despise. But do I,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, rising again the instant after he had sat down, \u201csee before me the boy I once played with in his happy infancy? And may I\u2014_may_ I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nBy this May I, he meant to ask whether he might shake hands. I agreed, and he did so fervently, then sat down again.\\n\\n\u201cHere is wine,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook. \u201cLet us drink to Fortune, and may she always choose her favourites with equal judgment! And yet I cannot,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, rising again, \u201csee before me One\u2014and also drink to One\u2014without once more expressing\u2014May I\u2014_may_ I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nI said he might, and he shook hands with me again, emptied his glass, and turned it upside down. I did the same; and if I had turned myself upside down before drinking, the wine could not have gone more directly to my head.\\n\\nMr. Pumblechook helped me to the liver wing and the best slice of tongue (none of those remote Pork No Thoroughfares now), while taking, comparatively speaking, no care of himself. \u201cAh! Poultry, poultry! You little knew,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, addressing the fowl in the dish, \u201cwhen you were a young fledgling, what awaited you. You little knew you were destined to refresh beneath this humble roof one who\u2014Call it weakness, if you will,\u201d said Mr. Pumblechook, rising again, \u201cbut may I? _may_ I\u2014?\u201d\\n\\nIt was beginning to seem unnecessary to repeat that he might, so he did it immediately. How he managed to do it so often without injuring himself on my knife, I cannot say.\\n\\n\u201cAnd your sister,\u201d he continued, after eating steadily for a while, \u201cwho had the honour of raising you by hand! It is a sad picture, to think that she is no longer capable of fully understanding the honour. May\u2014\u201d\\n\\nI saw he was about to approach me again, and stopped him.\\n\\n\u201cWe\u2019ll drink her health,\u201d I said.\\n\\n\u201cAh!\u201d cried Mr. Pumblechook, leaning back in his chair, completely limp with admiration, \u201cthat\u2019s how you know them, sir!\u201d (I do not know who Sir was, but he certainly was not me, and no third person was present); \u201cthat\u2019s how you recognize the noble-minded, sir!\u201d\"}"
