          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"7922bba9b1664d13d10f6f03\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cDo you play?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think, sir,\u201d said the spy in the most insulting way, turning to Mr. Lorry, \u201cI may appeal to a gentleman of your age and kindness to ask this other gentleman, so much younger than you, whether he can possibly justify playing the Ace he mentioned. I admit that I am a spy, and that spying is considered a disgraceful position\u2014though someone must fill it. But this gentleman is no spy, so why should he lower himself by becoming one?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI play my Ace, Mr. Barsad,\u201d said Carton, answering for himself and checking his watch, \u201cwithout the slightest hesitation, in a few minutes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI should have hoped, gentlemen,\u201d said the spy, still trying to draw Mr. Lorry into the discussion, \u201cthat your respect for my sister\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI could show no greater respect for your sister than by finally freeing her from her brother,\u201d said Sydney Carton.\\n\\n\u201cYou don\u2019t think so, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have made up my mind completely.\u201d\\n\\nThe spy\u2019s smooth manner, strangely at odds with his deliberately rough clothes and probably with his usual behavior, was stopped by Carton\u2019s unreadable expression. Carton was a mystery even to wiser and more honest men than Barsad. The spy faltered and could say no more. While he hesitated, Carton resumed his earlier pose of studying the cards and said:\\n\\n\u201cAnd now that I think of it, I strongly suspect I have another good card here, one I have not yet mentioned. That friend and fellow-Sheep who said he was pasturing in the country prisons\u2014who was he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFrench. You don\u2019t know him,\u201d the spy replied quickly.\\n\\n\u201cFrench, eh?\u201d Carton repeated thoughtfully, seeming not to notice him at all, though he echoed the word. \u201cWell, he may be.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHe is, I assure you,\u201d said the spy, \u201cthough it isn\u2019t important.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThough it isn\u2019t important,\u201d Carton repeated mechanically. \u201cThough it isn\u2019t important\u2014No, it isn\u2019t important. No. And yet I know the face.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t think so. I\u2019m sure you don\u2019t. It can\u2019t be,\u201d said the spy.\\n\\n\u201c\u2018It can\u2019t be,\u2019\u201d Sydney Carton muttered, thinking back, and idly moving his glass\u2014which was fortunately a small one\u2014again. \u201cCan\u2019t be. He spoke good French. Yet I thought he seemed foreign?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cProvincial,\u201d said the spy.\\n\\n\u201cNo. Foreign!\u201d cried Carton, striking his open hand on the table as the truth suddenly became clear. \u201cCly! Disguised, but the same man. We had that man before us at the Old Bailey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNow you are being hasty, sir,\u201d said Barsad, with a smile that tilted his aquiline nose even farther to one side. \u201cThere you have really given me an advantage over you.\u201d\"}"
