          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"859d2e8bf506a1d9d28f0120\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u2018Good heavens!\u2019 the Duchess said to herself. \u2018So he really is some kind of chiropodist after all. How dreadful. I hope he is a foreigner, at least. It wouldn\u2019t seem quite so bad then.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018I must certainly introduce him to you.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Introduce him!\u2019 cried the Duchess. \u2018You don\u2019t mean to say he is here?\u2019 She began looking around for a small tortoiseshell fan and a very tattered lace shawl, so that she would be ready to leave at a moment\u2019s notice.\\n\\n\u2018Of course he is here; I would never dream of giving a party without him. He tells me I have a purely psychic hand, and that if my thumb had been even slightly shorter, I would have become a confirmed pessimist and entered a convent.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Oh, I see!\u2019 said the Duchess, greatly relieved. \u2018He tells fortunes, I suppose?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018And misfortunes too,\u2019 Lady Windermere replied, \u2018in unlimited quantities. Next year, for example, I am in great danger both on land and at sea, so I am going to live in a balloon and have my dinner hauled up in a basket every evening. It is all written on my little finger, or in the palm of my hand\u2014I forget which.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018But surely that is tempting Providence, Gladys.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018My dear Duchess, surely Providence can resist temptation by now. I think everyone should have their hands read once a month, so they will know what not to do. Naturally, one does it anyway, but it is so pleasant to be warned. Now, if someone does not fetch Mr. Podgers at once, I shall have to go myself.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Let me go, Lady Windermere,\u2019 said a tall, handsome young man who stood nearby, listening to the conversation with an amused smile.\\n\\n\u2018Thank you so much, Lord Arthur; but I am afraid you would not recognise him.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018If he is as wonderful as you say, Lady Windermere, I could hardly miss him. Tell me what he looks like, and I will bring him to you at once.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Well, he does not look like a chiromantist at all. I mean, he is not mysterious, esoteric, or romantic-looking. He is a short, stout man with a funny bald head and large gold-rimmed spectacles\u2014something between a family doctor and a country solicitor. I am truly sorry, but it is not my fault. People are so irritating. All my pianists look exactly like poets, and all my poets look exactly like pianists. I remember last season inviting a most dreadful conspirator to dinner, a man who had blown up countless people and always wore a coat of mail and carried a dagger up his sleeve. And do you know, when he arrived, he looked exactly like a pleasant old clergyman and spent the whole evening telling jokes?\u2019\"}"
