          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4164844b66599d5f2012e5b3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was Lady Windermere\u2019s final reception before Easter, and Bentinck House was more crowded than ever. Six Cabinet Ministers had come straight from the Speaker\u2019s Lev\u00e9e, wearing their stars and ribands; all the pretty women were in their finest gowns; and at the far end of the picture-gallery stood the Princess Sophia of Carlsr\u00fche, a massive, Tartar-like woman with minute black eyes and magnificent emeralds, speaking atrocious French at the top of her voice and laughing extravagantly at everything said to her. It was truly a remarkable mixture of people. Splendid peeresses conversed pleasantly with fierce Radicals, fashionable preachers rubbed shoulders with distinguished sceptics, a whole flock of bishops followed a stout prima-donna from room to room, several Royal Academicians, disguised as artists, occupied the staircase, and it was rumoured that the supper-room had at one point been packed with geniuses. Indeed, it was one of Lady Windermere\u2019s most successful evenings, and the Princess remained until nearly half-past eleven.\\n\\nWhen she had departed, Lady Windermere went back to the picture-gallery, where a famous political economist was gravely expounding the scientific theory of music to an outraged Hungarian virtuoso, and began speaking with the Duchess of Paisley. She looked extraordinarily beautiful, with her noble ivory throat, her great blue forget-me-not eyes, and her thick coils of golden hair. _Or pur_ they were\u2014not that pale straw shade which in modern times usurps the gracious title of gold, but gold such as is woven through sunbeams or concealed in curious amber; and they gave her face something of a saint\u2019s setting, with more than a little of a sinner\u2019s allure. She was an intriguing psychological study. In early life she had learned the significant truth that nothing resembles innocence so closely as an indiscretion; and through a succession of reckless adventures, half of them entirely harmless, she had won all the privileges of a personality. She had changed husbands more than once; indeed, Debrett assigned her three marriages; but since she had never changed her lover, society had long since stopped gossiping about her. She was now forty, without children, and possessed of that excessive passion for pleasure which is the secret of staying young.\\n\\nSuddenly she glanced eagerly about the room and said, in her clear contralto, \u2018Where is my cheiromantist?\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Your what, Gladys?\u2019 cried the Duchess, starting involuntarily.\\n\\n\u2018My cheiromantist, Duchess; I cannot do without him just now.\u2019\\n\\n\u2018Dear Gladys! you are always so original,\u2019 murmured the Duchess, trying to recall exactly what a cheiromantist was, and hoping it was not the same thing as a cheiropodist.\\n\\n\u2018He comes to read my hand twice every week without fail,\u2019 Lady Windermere continued, \u2018and is most fascinating about it.\u2019\"}"
