{"request_id":"c9f8f0b34b75c10197fbeb6a","rewritten_text":"You see, I had my handkerchief in my helmet, along with a few other things. But it was the kind of helmet you couldn't remove by yourself. That hadn't occurred to me when I put it there; in fact, I didn't even know it. I thought it would be especially convenient. So now, knowing it was right there, close at hand but impossible to reach, made everything worse and harder to endure. Yes, the thing you can't have is usually the thing you want most; everyone has noticed that. It took my mind away from everything else and fixed it completely on my helmet. Mile after mile, I imagined the handkerchief and pictured it there. It was bitter and maddening to feel salty sweat trickling into my eyes while I couldn't reach it. It may sound like a small matter on paper, but it wasn't small at all; it was a very real misery. I wouldn't say so if it weren't true. I decided that next time I would carry a reticule, no matter how it looked or what people said. Naturally, those iron gentlemen of the Round Table would consider it scandalous and perhaps make a great fuss, but I wanted comfort first and style afterward. So we went on, and every now and then we crossed a stretch of dust. It rose in clouds, filled my nose, and made me sneeze and cry. Naturally, I said things I shouldn't have said; I don't deny it. I'm no better than anyone else.

We couldn't seem to meet anyone in that lonely Britain, not even an ogre. Given my mood then, that was fortunate for the ogre—at least for an ogre who had a handkerchief. Most knights would have thought only of taking his armor, but if I could get his bandanna, he could keep his iron equipment as far as I was concerned.

Meanwhile, it was growing hotter and hotter inside the armor. The sun was beating down, warming the iron more and more. When you're that hot, every little thing irritates you. When I trotted, I rattled like a crate of dishes, and that bothered me. I also couldn't stand the shield slapping and banging, first against my chest and then against my back. If I slowed to a walk, my joints creaked and squealed like a wheelbarrow, and since we made no breeze at that speed, I was nearly roasted inside that oven. Besides, the more slowly you moved, the more heavily the iron settled on you, until every minute you seemed to weigh several tons more."}

