{"request_id":"be87c678811148abc9b2befa","rewritten_text":"It was past two o’clock when, by a sudden turn, I found myself near the lake, beside a cove where a little skiff was moored. It was not far from our house, and I saw my father and aunt walking on the lawn. I jumped into the boat and, well accustomed to such feats, pushed it from shore, using all my strength to row swiftly across. As I approached, dressed in white and covered only by my tartan _rachan_, my hair streaming over my shoulders, and moving with greater speed than anyone could have imagined I could give my boat, my father often told me that I looked more like a spirit than a human girl. He held the boat as I reached the shore; I leapt lightly out and, in a moment, was in his arms.

And now I began to live. Everything around me had changed from dull uniformity into the brightest scene of joy and delight. The happiness I found in my father’s company far exceeded my hopeful expectations. We were always together, and the subjects of our conversations were inexhaustible. He had spent the sixteen years of our separation among nations nearly unknown to Europe; he had wandered through Persia, Arabia, and northern India, and had entered the homes of the natives with a freedom permitted to few Europeans. His accounts of their customs, his anecdotes, and his descriptions of the landscape filled delicious hours when we grew tired of discussing our own plans for the future.

The voice of affection was so new to me that I listened with delight whenever he told me what he had felt for me during those long years of apparent forgetfulness. “At first,” he said, “I could not bear to think of my poor little girl; but afterward, as grief faded and hope returned, I could think only of her. Amid cities and deserts, her little fairy form, as I imagined it, forever flitted before me. The northern breeze, as it refreshed me, seemed sweeter and more fragrant because it appeared to carry some part of your spirit with it. I often thought I would return at once and take you with me to some fertile island where we could live in peace forever. As I came back, my fervent hopes were clouded by so many fears; my impatience became unbearably painful. I dared not imagine that the sun would shine and the moon rise, not upon your living form, but upon your grave. But no, it is not so; I have my Mathilda, my consolation and my hope.”"}

