          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cf153105069a54afe299e9b4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Raymond paced the room in anger. I remained prepared to hear and answer his reproaches. Adrian stood leaning against the wall. \u201cThis is utterly absurd,\u201d he exclaimed; \u201cif you were schoolboys, you could not behave with greater unreasonableness.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou do not comprehend,\u201d said Raymond. \u201cThis is merely one part of a system\u2014a plan of tyranny to which I will never yield. Because I am Protector of England, must I be the sole slave in its empire? My privacy violated, my conduct condemned, my friends insulted? But I will cast off the whole of it at once. Be witnesses,\u201d and he removed from his breast the star, the badge of office, and flung it upon the table. \u201cI renounce my office; I abdicate my power\u2014let whoever can take it assume it!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet him assume it,\u201d cried Adrian, \u201cwho can declare himself, or whom the world will declare, your superior. There is no man in England possessed of sufficient presumption. Know your own worth, Raymond, and your anger will fade; your confidence return. A few months since, whenever we prayed for the prosperity of our country, or for our own, we prayed at the same time for the Protector\u2019s life and welfare, so closely were they bound together. Your hours were spent for our advantage, and your ambition was to win our praise. You adorned our towns with buildings, founded useful institutions for us, and made the soil richly fertile. The powerful and unjust trembled before your judgment-seat, while the poor and oppressed rose like flowers awakened by morning beneath the sunshine of your protection.\\n\\n\u201cCan you wonder that we are all dismayed and grieving when this seems altered? But come, this fit of spleen has already passed; take up your duties again. Your supporters will welcome you, your enemies will be silenced, and our love, honour, and duty will once more be shown to you. Master yourself, Raymond, and the world is subject to your will.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAll this would be excellent sense if it were spoken to someone else,\u201d Raymond replied gloomily. \u201cApply the lesson to yourself, and you, the first peer of the realm, might become its sovereign. You, being good, wise, and just, might govern every heart. But I see, too soon for my happiness and too late for England\u2019s welfare, that I accepted a task beyond my powers. I cannot govern myself. My passions rule me; my slightest impulse is my tyrant. Do you suppose I renounced the Protectorate\u2014and renounced it I have\u2014in a moment of irritation? By the living God, I swear I will never take up that bauble again; never again burden myself with the care and misery whose visible emblem it is.\u201d\"}"
