          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"914b912a15f4b2c4f4900b92\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The winters at Sullivan\u2019s Island\u2019s latitude are rarely harsh, and in autumn it is an unusual thing for a fire to be thought necessary. About mid-October, 18\u2014, there came, however, a day of extraordinary cold. Shortly before sunset I made my way through the evergreens to my friend\u2019s hut, which I had not visited for several weeks\u2014my home then being in Charleston, nine miles from the island, while travel to and from it was far less convenient than in the present day. When I reached the hut, I knocked as usual, and receiving no answer, looked for the key in its accustomed hiding place, opened the door, and entered. A bright fire burned upon the hearth. It was a novelty, and certainly not an unwelcome one. I removed my overcoat, settled into an arm-chair beside the snapping logs, and patiently awaited my hosts\u2019 arrival.\\n\\nThey came soon after dark and welcomed me most warmly. Jupiter, grinning broadly, hurried about getting some marsh-hens ready for supper. Legrand was in one of his fits\u2014what else can I call them?\u2014of excitement. He had discovered an unknown bivalve, belonging to a new genus, and, beyond that, had tracked down and captured, with Jupiter\u2019s help, a _scarab\u00e6us_ he believed entirely new, though he wanted my judgment on it the next day.\\n\\n\u201cAnd why not tonight?\u201d I asked, warming my hands over the fire and wishing the entire tribe of _scarab\u00e6i_ elsewhere.\\n\\n\u201cAh, if only I had known you were here!\u201d said Legrand. \u201cBut it has been so long since I saw you; how could I have guessed that you would visit me on this particular night? On my way home I met Lieutenant G\u2014\u2014, from the fort, and foolishly lent him the insect; so you cannot see it until morning. Remain here tonight, and I will send Jup for it at sunrise. It is the most beautiful thing in creation!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat?\u2014sunrise?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFoolishness! No\u2014the bug. It is a brilliant gold color, about the size of a large hickory-nut, with two jet-black spots near one end of its back, and another, rather longer, at the other. The _antenn\u00e6_ are\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cDey aint no tin in him, Massa Will, I keep a tellin on you,\u201d interrupted Jupiter; \u201cde bug is a goole bug, solid, ebery bit of him, inside and all, sep him wing\u2014neber feel half so hebby a bug in my life.\u201d\"}"
