          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4725443f6fe704f2b8433970\",\"rewritten_text\":\"After joining in general lamentation over the dreadful outcome of this event, which Elizabeth regarded as almost certain, and Miss Bennet could not claim was entirely impossible, the former continued, \u201cBut tell me everything about it that I have not already heard. Give me more details. What did Colonel Forster say? Had they no suspicion of anything before the elopement? They must have seen them together constantly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cColonel Forster admitted that he had often suspected some partiality, especially on Lydia\u2019s side, but nothing that caused him any alarm. I am so sorry for him. His conduct was as attentive and kind as possible. He was coming to us to assure us of his concern before he had any idea that they had not gone to Scotland; when that fear first became known, it hastened his journey.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd was Denny convinced that Wickham would not marry? Did he know they intended to run away? Had Colonel Forster seen Denny himself?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes; but when questioned by him, Denny denied knowing anything about their plan and would not state his true opinion. He did not repeat his belief that they would not marry, and from that I am inclined to hope he may have been misunderstood before.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd until Colonel Forster came himself, not one of you, I suppose, doubted that they were truly married?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow could such an idea have entered our minds? I felt somewhat uneasy\u2014a little afraid for my sister\u2019s happiness with him in marriage, because I knew his conduct had not always been entirely proper. My father and mother knew nothing of that; they only understood how imprudent the match must be. Kitty then admitted, with very natural satisfaction at knowing more than the rest of us, that in Lydia\u2019s last letter she had prepared her for such a step. It seems she had known for many weeks that they were in love with each other.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut not before they went to Brighton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, I believe not.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did Colonel Forster seem to think poorly of Wickham himself? Does he know his true character?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI must admit that he did not speak of Wickham as favorably as he once had. He believed him imprudent and extravagant; and since this sad affair occurred, it is said that he left Meryton deeply in debt. But I hope that may be untrue.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, Jane, if we had been less secretive, if we had told what we knew of him, this could not have happened!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPerhaps it would have been better,\u201d her sister replied.\\n\\n\u201cBut exposing anyone\u2019s former faults without knowing what their present feelings were seemed unjustifiable.\u201d\"}"
