          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"731f498b284e270d856a096f\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cStory!\u201d\\n\\nHe seemed deliberately to misunderstand the word she had repeated. He quickly added, \u201cYes, customers; in banking, we usually call the people connected with us our customers. He was a French gentleman; a learned man; a man of great knowledge\u2014a doctor.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cFrom Beauvais?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, from Beauvais. Like your father, Monsieur Manette, he was from Beauvais. Like your father, he was well known in Paris. I had the honour of knowing him there. Our relationship was business, but confidential. At that time I was working in our French branch, and had been there\u2014oh, twenty years.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAt that time\u2014may I ask when that was, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI mean, miss, twenty years ago. He married an English lady, and I was one of the trustees. His affairs, like those of many other French gentlemen and families, were entirely handled by Tellson\u2019s. In the same way, I am, or have been, trustee for dozens of our customers. These are only business relationships, miss; there is no friendship in them, no special interest, nothing sentimental. During my working life, I have moved from one to another just as I move from one customer to another during my business day. In short, I have no feelings; I am only a machine. To continue\u2014\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut this is my father\u2019s story, sir; and I am beginning to think\u201d\u2014her strangely furrowed forehead was fixed intently on him\u2014\u201cthat when I was left an orphan, because my mother lived only two years longer than my father, it was you who brought me to England. I am almost certain it was you.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Lorry took the small, trusting hand that reached toward his and kissed it with some ceremony. Then he led the young woman back to her chair. Holding the back with his left hand, and using his right at different times to rub his chin, tug at his wig near the ears, or point as he spoke, he looked down at her while she looked up at him.\\n\\n\u201cMiss Manette, it was I. And you will see how honestly I described myself just now, when I said I had no feelings and that all my relationships with other people were merely business, if you consider that I have never seen you since. No; you have been under Tellson\u2019s care ever since, and I have been occupied with Tellson\u2019s other business. Feelings! I have no time for them, and no opportunity for them. I spend my whole life, miss, turning an enormous money-making machine.\u201d\"}"
