          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"330eaa524d3d67ea035af278\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During dinner, Mr. Bennet hardly spoke at all. But once the servants had left, he decided it was time to talk with his guest. He chose a subject on which he expected Mr. Collins to speak well, remarking that he seemed very fortunate in his patroness. Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2019s attention to his wishes and concern for his comfort seemed remarkable. Mr. Bennet could not have chosen a better topic. Mr. Collins praised her at length. The subject made him more solemn than usual, and with a very serious expression he declared that he had never seen such conduct in a person of rank\u2014such friendliness and kindness as he had experienced from Lady Catherine. She had graciously approved both sermons he had already had the honour of preaching before her. She had also invited him to dine at Rosings twice, and had sent for him only the previous Saturday to complete her evening quadrille party. He knew that many people considered Lady Catherine proud, but he had never seen anything in her except friendliness. She had always spoken to him as she would to any other gentleman. She had not objected in the least to his joining the society of the neighbourhood, or to his leaving his parish for a week or two to visit his relations. She had even advised him to marry as soon as possible, provided he chose carefully. Once, she had visited him at his modest parsonage, approved all the changes he had been making, and even suggested some improvements herself\u2014such as shelves in the upstairs closets.\\n\\n\u201cThat is all very proper and polite, I am sure,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet, \u201cand I dare say she is a very pleasant woman. It is a pity that great ladies in general are not more like her. Does she live near you, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe garden surrounding my humble home is separated from Rosings Park, her Ladyship\u2019s residence, by only a lane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you said she was a widow, sir. Has she any children?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe has one daughter only, the heiress of Rosings and of a very large fortune.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d cried Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, \u201cthen she is better provided for than many girls. And what kind of young lady is she? Is she handsome?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe is a most charming young lady, indeed. Lady Catherine herself says that, in true beauty, Miss de Bourgh is far superior to the handsomest women, because there is something in her features that shows the young woman of distinguished birth.\"}"
