{"request_id":"75f1bcaafc0d0d0332332db7","rewritten_text":"I cannot in the least understand why she wished to become intimate with me; yet, were the same circumstances to occur again, I am certain I should once more be deceived. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday; and during the interval I received neither note nor line from her. When she came, it was perfectly clear that she took no pleasure in the visit; she offered a brief, formal apology for not coming sooner, said nothing about wishing to see me again, and was in every way so changed a person, that when she left I was fully determined to continue the acquaintance no longer. I pity her, though I cannot help blaming her. She acted very wrongly in singling me out as she did; I can safely declare that every attempt at intimacy originated with her. Still, I pity her, because she must be conscious of having behaved improperly, and because I am quite convinced that concern for her brother is the reason. I need say no more; and though _we_ know this concern to be entirely needless, yet if she feels it, it readily explains her conduct towards me; and since he is so deservedly dear to his sister, whatever anxiety she may entertain for him is both natural and amiable. I cannot help wondering, however, that she should have such fears now, for if he had ever cared for me, we must have met long, long ago. He knows that I am in town, I am certain, from something she herself said; and yet, from the way she speaks, one would think she wished to persuade herself that he is truly partial to Miss Darcy. I cannot make sense of it. Were I not afraid of judging her too severely, I should almost be inclined to say that there is a strong appearance of duplicity in the whole affair. I will try to dismiss every painful thought, and dwell only on what can make me happy—your affection, and the unfailing kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Write to me very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned something about his never returning to Netherfield, and giving up the house, though she spoke without certainty. We had better not repeat it. I am exceedingly pleased that you have received such agreeable accounts from our friends at Hunsford. Do go to see them, with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

This letter caused Elizabeth some distress; but her spirits revived when she reflected that Jane, at least, would no longer be deceived by the sister. All hope concerning the brother had now entirely disappeared."}

