          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b06ee9212787d4979ae4be97\",\"rewritten_text\":\"In my youth, discontent never entered my mind. If I was ever overcome by boredom, the sight of nature\u2019s beauty or the study of what was excellent and sublime in human creations could always engage my heart and lift my spirits. But I am like a blasted tree; the bolt has struck my soul. I felt then that I would live only to display what I would soon become\u2014a miserable ruin of humanity, pitied by others and unbearable to myself.\\n\\nWe spent a long time at Oxford, walking through its surroundings and trying to identify every place connected with the most inspiring period of English history. Our small journeys of discovery were often extended by the new sights that appeared before us. We visited the tomb of the famous Hampden and the field where that patriot died. For a moment, my soul rose above its degrading and miserable fears as I considered the noble ideas of liberty and self-sacrifice that these places preserved and recalled. For an instant, I dared to throw off my chains and look around me with a free and proud spirit. But the iron had cut into my flesh, and I sank once more, trembling and hopeless, into my miserable self.\\n\\nWe left Oxford reluctantly and went on to Matlock, our next place of rest. The countryside around this village resembled Switzerland more closely, though everything was smaller, and the green hills lacked the distant white Alps that always crown the pine-covered mountains of my native land. We visited the marvelous cave and the small natural-history rooms, where the curiosities were arranged much as they were in the collections at Servox and Chamounix. The latter name made me tremble whenever Henry spoke it, and I hurried to leave Matlock, since that terrible scene had become linked with the place.\\n\\nFrom Derby, still traveling north, we spent two months in Cumberland and Westmorland. I could almost imagine myself among the Swiss mountains. The small patches of snow still clinging to the northern slopes, the lakes, and the rushing rocky streams were all familiar and dear to me. We also made several acquaintances there, who nearly managed to trick me into happiness. Clerval\u2019s pleasure was much greater than mine; among talented people, his mind opened, and he discovered in himself abilities and inner resources greater than he had imagined while keeping company with those beneath him.\"}"
