          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"9a154b2eebdfe980da9821c4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"When I returned, I found the following letter from my father:\\n\\n\u201cMy dear Victor,\\n\\n\u201cYou have probably been waiting impatiently for a letter confirming the date of your return to us; at first, I was tempted to write only a few lines, simply naming the day on which I should expect you. But that would be a cruel kindness, and I dare not do it. What would your surprise be, my son, when, expecting a joyful welcome, you instead found tears and misery? And how, Victor, can I tell you of our misfortune? Absence cannot have made you indifferent to our joys and sorrows; and how can I inflict pain upon my son, absent for so long? I wish to prepare you for the terrible news, but I know it is impossible; even now, your eyes are racing over the page, searching for the words that will bring you the dreadful tidings.\\n\\n\u201cWilliam is dead!\u2014that sweet child, whose smiles delighted and warmed my heart, who was so gentle and yet so cheerful! Victor, he has been murdered!\\n\\n\u201cI will not try to comfort you; I will simply relate what happened.\\n\\n\u201cLast Thursday, May 7th, my niece, your two brothers, and I went for a walk in Plainpalais. The evening was warm and calm, and we continued farther than usual. It was already dusk before we thought of returning; then we discovered that William and Ernest, who had gone ahead, were nowhere to be found. We therefore sat on a bench until they returned. Soon Ernest came and asked whether we had seen his brother. He said he had been playing with him, that William had run away to hide, and that he had searched for him in vain and then waited a long time, but William had not returned.\\n\\n\u201cThis account alarmed us, and we continued searching until night fell, when Elizabeth suggested that he might have gone back to the house. He was not there. We returned once more with torches; I could not rest while thinking that my sweet boy might be lost and exposed to the damp and dew of night, and Elizabeth was suffering terribly as well. At about five in the morning, I found my lovely boy, whom I had seen the previous evening healthy, lively, and blooming, lying on the grass, pale and motionless; the mark of the murderer\u2019s fingers was on his neck.\\n\\n\u201cHe was carried home, and the anguish on my face revealed the secret to Elizabeth. She was desperate to see the body. At first I tried to stop her, but she persisted and entered the room where it lay. She quickly examined the victim\u2019s neck, then clasped her hands and cried, \u2018O God!\"}"
