          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ff0334eb65c9d1fcc58b9972\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cGit up! What you \u2019bout?\u201d\\n\\nI opened my eyes and looked about, trying to figure where I was. The sun was already up, and I\u2019d been sleeping soundly. Pap stood over me, looking sour and sickly besides. He says:\\n\\n\u201cWhat you doing with this gun?\u201d\\n\\nI reckoned he didn\u2019t know a thing about what he\u2019d been up to, so I says:\\n\\n\u201cSomebody tried to get in, so I was waiting for him.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhy didn\u2019t you wake me?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, I tried, but I couldn\u2019t; I couldn\u2019t move you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell, all right. Don\u2019t stand there talking all day, but get out and see whether there\u2019s a fish on the lines for breakfast. I\u2019ll be along directly.\u201d\\n\\nHe unlocked the door, and I hurried up the river-bank. I saw bits of limbs and suchlike drifting down, with a sprinkling of bark; so I knowed the river had started rising. I reckoned I\u2019d have a fine time now if I was over in town. The June rise had always been lucky for me; for as soon as it began, cordwood came floating down, along with pieces of log rafts\u2014sometimes a dozen logs together; and all you had to do was catch them and sell them to the wood-yards and sawmill.\\n\\nI went on up the bank, keeping one eye open for pap and the other for whatever the rise might bring along. Then, all at once, here came a canoe; a real beauty, too, about thirteen or fourteen feet long, riding high as a duck. I plunged head-first from the bank like a frog, clothes and all, and struck out for the canoe. I expected somebody would be lying in it, because folks often did that to fool people, and when a fellow had pulled a skiff nearly up to them, they\u2019d sit up and laugh. But it wasn\u2019t so this time. It was a drift-canoe sure enough, and I climbed in and paddled her ashore. I thought, the old man will be pleased when he sees this\u2014she\u2019s worth ten dollars. But when I reached shore pap still wasn\u2019t in sight, and as I was running her into a little creek like a gully, all covered over with vines and willows, another notion struck me: I figured I\u2019d hide her well, and then, instead of taking to the woods when I ran off, I\u2019d go downriver about fifty miles and camp in one spot for good, so I wouldn\u2019t have such a hard time tramping on foot.\\n\\nIt was pretty near the shanty, and I thought I heard the old man coming every minute; but I got her hidden. Then I stepped out and looked around a clump of willows, and there was the old man a little way down the path, drawing a bead on a bird with his gun.\"}"
