          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"dba2f818de5b14d8901c5fe2\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Once, however, the lieutenant asked why he had come so far across the ice in such a strange vehicle.\\n\\nHis face immediately took on an expression of the deepest gloom, and he replied, \u201cTo seek someone who fled from me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd did the man you pursued travel in the same way?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen I believe we have seen him, for the day before we picked you up, we saw some dogs pulling a sledge, with a man in it, across the ice.\u201d\\n\\nThis caught the stranger\u2019s attention, and he asked many questions about the route the d\u00e6mon, as he called him, had taken. Soon afterward, when he was alone with me, he said, \u201cI have surely aroused your curiosity, as well as that of these good people; but you are too considerate to ask questions.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cCertainly; it would indeed be very impertinent and inhuman of me to trouble you with my curiosity.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd yet you rescued me from a strange and perilous situation; you have kindly restored me to life.\u201d\\n\\nSoon afterward he asked whether I thought the breaking ice had destroyed the other sledge. I replied that I could not say with any certainty, since the ice had not broken until near midnight, and the traveller might have reached safety before then; but I had no way of knowing.\\n\\nFrom that moment, a new spirit of life animated the stranger\u2019s failing body. He showed the greatest eagerness to go on deck and watch for the sledge that had appeared before; but I persuaded him to remain in the cabin, for he is far too weak to endure the raw air. I promised that someone would watch for him and immediately inform him if any new object came into view.\\n\\nThis is my journal of everything connected with this strange event up to the present day. The stranger has gradually recovered some health, but he is very quiet and seems uneasy whenever anyone except me enters his cabin. Yet his manner is so gentle and conciliatory that all the sailors are interested in him, although they have spoken with him very little. For my part, I am beginning to love him like a brother, and his constant, profound sorrow fills me with sympathy and compassion. He must have been a noble person in better days, since even now, amid his ruin, he remains so attractive and kind.\\n\\nI said in one of my letters, my dear Margaret, that I would find no friend on the open ocean; yet I have found a man whom, before misery broke his spirit, I would have been happy to call the brother of my heart.\"}"
