          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"abf2f25df93856f81e10cd47\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cHow long I continued in this condition I cannot possibly say. It must, nevertheless, have been a considerable while, since, when I partly regained a sense of existence, I saw dawn breaking, the balloon at an immense height above a wilderness of ocean, and no sign of land anywhere within the bounds of the enormous horizon. My sensations, however, on recovering were by no means as agonizing as might have been expected. Indeed, there was something almost incipiently mad in the composed inspection I began to make of my circumstances. I raised each of my hands to my eyes in turn, wondering what event could have caused the veins to swell and the fingernails to become horribly black. I then examined my head with care, shaking it again and again and feeling it minutely, until I managed to convince myself that it was not, as I had half suspected, larger than my balloon. Next, with an air of understanding, I felt in both breeches pockets, and, finding a set of tablets and a toothpick case missing, tried to explain their disappearance; failing to do so, I was deeply chagrined. It now came to me that I was suffering considerable discomfort in the joint of my left ankle, and a faint awareness of my situation began to dawn upon me. But, strange to say! I was neither amazed nor filled with horror. If I experienced any emotion, it was a sort of chuckling satisfaction at the ingenuity I was about to show in freeing myself from this predicament; and never for an instant did I regard my eventual safety as doubtful. For several minutes I remained absorbed in the deepest meditation. I distinctly remember repeatedly pressing my lips together, placing my forefinger beside my nose, and employing other gestures and grimaces familiar to men who, comfortably settled in armchairs, ponder over matters of difficulty or importance. Having, as I believed, sufficiently gathered my thoughts, I now, with great care and deliberation, reached my hands behind me and unfastened the large iron buckle attached to the waistband of my inexpressibles. The buckle had three teeth, which, being somewhat rusty, rotated only with great difficulty upon their axis. After some effort, however, I brought them to right angles with the buckle\u2019s body, and was pleased to find that they held firmly in that position. Keeping the instrument thus formed between my teeth, I then set about loosening the knot of my cravat. I was obliged to pause several times before completing this manoeuvre, but at last it was done.\u201d\"}"
