{"request_id":"04693b04321d7a3abf0106cc","rewritten_text":"When I had stopped weeping, reason and memory returned, and I began to think more calmly about what had happened and what I should do. Only a few hours had passed, yet a great change had taken place in me. The natural work of years had been completed since morning: my father was as good as dead to me. For a moment, I felt as if he, with his white hair, lay in his coffin, while I—my youth gone and old age approaching—mourned his timely death. But it was not so. I was still young—far too young—and he was not dead to others. Yet I, most miserable, must never see or speak to him again. I had to flee from him more urgently than from my greatest enemy. Whether in solitude or in cities, I must never see him again. This thought left me breathless with anguish, and, fixed in my imagination, it kept me for a time from following any clear line of thought. I believed I would live forever in the bleakest seclusion. I would go to the Continent and become a nun—not for religion, since I was not Catholic, but so I could be shut away from the world forever. There I would find solitude where I could weep, and the voices of life would never reach me.

But my father—my beloved and most wretched father? Would he die? Would he never overcome the fierce passion that now ruled him without pity? Might he not, many years from now, when age had cooled the burning feelings he now suffered, become a father to me again? This thought smoothed my brow, and I felt—and wept to feel it—a sad half-smile draw away some of the suffering from my lips. I dared hope more for my future. Years would pass, but hope would make them fly; and even if they passed slowly, they would pass, and I would not have lost my father forever. Let him spend another sixteen years wandering in desolation. Let him once again cry out his wild complaints to the vast forests and mighty waterfalls of another land. Let him face terrible dangers and hardships that crushed the spirit. Let the southern sun burn his passion-worn cheeks again, and let the cold night rains fall upon him and chill his blood.

To this miserable life, my father, I devote you! Go! Spend your days among savages and your nights beneath the open sky! Let your limbs grow worn and your heart grow cold, and let all youth die within you!"}

