          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2b481d826a59838f61b41bfa\",\"rewritten_text\":\"His account was that, traveling from his home and passing through Datchet, he was surprised to see smoke rising from the inn\u2019s chimney. Assuming he would find fellow travelers gathered there, he knocked and was admitted. No one was in the house except Lucy and her mother. The older woman had lost the use of her limbs after an attack of rheumatism, and so, one by one, all the remaining inhabitants of the country had set out, leaving them alone. Lucy begged the man to stay with her; her mother would be better in a week or two, and they could then depart, but they would perish if left so helpless and abandoned. The man said that his wife and children were already among the emigrants, and therefore, as he saw it, he could not remain. As a last resort, Lucy gave him a letter for Idris, to deliver wherever he might meet us. At least he carried out this commission, and Idris received the following letter with deep emotion:\u2014\\n\\n\u201cHONOURED LADY,\\n\\n\u201cI am certain you will remember and pity me, and I dare hope that you will help me; what other hope have I? Forgive the way I write\u2014I am so confused. A month ago my dear mother lost the use of her limbs. She is already improving, and in another month, I am sure, she would be able to travel, in the manner you were kind enough to say you would arrange for us. But now everyone is gone\u2014everyone\u2014as they left, each one said that perhaps my mother would recover before we were completely deserted. But three days ago I went to Samuel Woods, who, because of his newborn child, had remained until the last; since they were a large family, I thought I might persuade them to wait a little longer for us, but I found the house deserted. I have not seen a soul since, until this good man arrived. What will become of us? My mother does not know our situation; she is so ill that I have concealed it from her.\\n\\n\u201cWill you not send someone to us? I am sure we must perish miserably if we remain here. If I tried to move my mother now, she would die on the road; and if, when she improves, I were somehow able to discover the roads and travel so many miles to the sea, you would all be in France, with the vast ocean between us\u2014an ocean terrible even to sailors.\u201d\"}"
