          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"27f45eb2cbc898efe24f5c2e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cDo you understand me? I have learned the language of despair; I know it by heart, because I am Despair\u2014and a strange being I am: joyful, triumphant Despair. Yet those words are false, for the wave may be dark, but it is not bitter. We lie down and close our eyes with a gentle good night, and when we wake, we are free. Come, then, no more delay, you who linger! Behold the pleasant potion! Look, I am a spirit of goodness, not a human maiden inviting you; and in winning tones\u2014oh, if only they could win you!\u2014I say, Come and drink.\u201d\\n\\nAs I spoke, I kept my eyes fixed on his face. His exquisite beauty, the heavenly compassion shining from his eyes, and his gentle yet earnest expression of concern and wonder, even before he spoke, changed my strained emotions. They took away all the harshness of despair and filled me only with the softest grief. I saw that his eyes were moist as he took both my hands in his. Sitting down beside me, he said:\\n\\n\u201cThis is a sad act you would lead me toward, dearest friend, and your sorrow must be very deep to fill you with such unhappy thoughts. You long for death, yet fear it, and wish me to be your companion. But I have less courage than you, and even with you beside me, I dare not die. Listen to me, then consider whether you should persuade me to your plan, even if the forceful eloquence of despair could make black death seem so inviting that bright heaven appeared dark. Listen, I beg you, to one who has himself encouraged desperate thoughts and longed impatiently for death, but who at last has trampled that phantom underfoot and crushed its sting. Come: since you have played Despair with me, I will play Una with you and lead you safely from his dark cavern. Listen to me, and let yourself be softened by words untouched by selfish desire.\\n\\n\u201cWe do not know what this wide world means, with its strange mixture of good and evil. But we have been placed here and told to live and hope. I do not know what we are meant to hope for; yet some good lies beyond us, and we must seek it. That is our earthly task. If misfortune comes against us, we must fight it; we must push it aside and continue searching for what it is our nature to desire. I do not know whether this hope of future good prepares us for another existence, or whether we are simply workers in God\u2019s vineyard, helping to smooth the way for those who come after us.\u201d\"}"
