          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ed39889c03dc458e6dc182ed\",\"rewritten_text\":\"They know what a sharp lad he is; he\u2019ll be a lifer. They\u2019ll make the Artful nothing short of a lifer.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by lagging and a lifer?\u201d demanded Mr. Bolter. \u201cWhat\u2019s the use of talking that way to me; why don\u2019t yer speak so I can understand yer?\u201d\\n\\nFagin was just about to translate these puzzling expressions into plain language; and, once interpreted, Mr. Bolter would have learned that they meant the words \u201ctransportation for life,\u201d when the conversation was interrupted by the entrance of Master Bates, his hands in his breeches-pockets and his face twisted into a half-comical expression of misery.\\n\\n\u201cIt\u2019s all up, Fagin,\u201d said Charley, after he and his new companion had been introduced to one another.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey\u2019ve found the gentleman who owns the box; two or three more are coming to \u2019dentify him; and the Artful\u2019s booked for a passage out,\u201d replied Master Bates. \u201cI must have a complete suit of mourning, Fagin, and a hatband, to wisit him in before he starts on his travels. To think of Jack Dawkins\u2014lummy Jack\u2014the Dodger\u2014the Artful Dodger\u2014going abroad for a common twopenny-halfpenny sneeze-box! I never thought he\u2019d have done it for less than a gold watch, chain, and seals. Oh, why didn\u2019t he rob some rich old gentleman of all his walables, and go out like a gentleman, instead of as a common prig, without any honour or glory!\u201d\\n\\nHaving thus expressed his feelings for his unfortunate friend, Master Bates seated himself on the nearest chair, looking chagrined and despondent.\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean by saying he has neither honour nor glory!\u201d exclaimed Fagin, casting an angry glance at his pupil. \u201cWasn\u2019t he always the top-sawyer among you all! Is there one of you who could touch him