          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"594d66eae0bf51174ef18eef\",\"rewritten_text\":\"On the morning appointed for Admiral and Mrs. Croft to see Kellynch Hall, Anne found it most natural to take her almost daily walk to Lady Russell\u2019s and stay out of the way until everything was over; then she found it most natural to regret having missed the opportunity of seeing them.\\n\\nThe meeting between the two parties proved highly satisfactory and settled the entire business at once. Each lady was already well disposed toward an agreement and therefore saw nothing but good manners in the other; and as for the gentlemen, there was such hearty good humour and such open, trusting generosity on the Admiral\u2019s side that it could not fail to influence Sir Walter, who had also been flattered into his finest and most polished behaviour by Mr. Shepherd\u2019s assurances that the Admiral knew him by reputation as a model of good breeding.\\n\\nThe house, grounds, and furniture were approved; the Crofts were approved; the terms, timing, everything, and everyone were right. Mr. Shepherd\u2019s clerks were set to work, without a single preliminary disagreement needing adjustment in all that \u201cThis indenture showeth.\u201d\\n\\nSir Walter declared without hesitation that the Admiral was the best-looking sailor he had ever met, and went so far as to say that, if his own man had been allowed to arrange the Admiral\u2019s hair, he would not have been ashamed to be seen with him anywhere. And the Admiral, with sympathetic warmth, remarked to his wife as they drove back through the park, \u201cI thought we should soon reach an agreement, my dear, despite what they told us at Taunton. The Baronet will never set the Thames on fire, but there seems to be no harm in him.\u201d\u2014mutual compliments that would have been considered nearly equal.\\n\\nThe Crofts were to take possession at Michaelmas; and since Sir Walter proposed moving to Bath during the preceding month, there was no time to lose in making all the necessary arrangements.\\n\\nLady Russell, convinced that Anne would not be permitted to have any use or importance in choosing the house they were about to secure, was very unwilling to have her hurried away so soon. She wanted to make it possible for Anne to remain until she could take her to Bath herself after Christmas; but, having engagements of her own that would keep her away from Kellynch for several weeks, she could not extend the full invitation she wished to offer. And Anne, though she dreaded the possible September heat amid all the white glare of Bath and grieved to surrender all the influence\u2014so sweet and so sad\u2014of the autumn months in the country, did not feel that, all things considered, she wished to stay.\"}"
