          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"16c812d6bdd9577954cc1211\",\"rewritten_text\":\"You must adopt a little of my philosophy. Think only of the past when remembering it gives you pleasure.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI cannot credit you with philosophy of that sort. _Your_ recollections must be entirely free from reproach, so that the satisfaction they bring comes not from philosophy, but, what is far better, from ignorance. With _me_, it is different. Painful memories will intrude, and they cannot, nor ought they, be driven away. I have been selfish all my life in practice, though not in principle. As a child, I was taught what was _right_, but not how to govern my temper. I was given sound principles, yet left to follow them with pride and conceit. Unhappily, as an only son (and for many years an only _child_), I was spoiled by my parents, who, though good themselves\u2014my father especially, being all benevolence and kindness\u2014allowed, encouraged, and almost taught me to be selfish and domineering; to care for no one beyond my own family; to think poorly of the rest of the world; and at least to _wish_ to think their understanding and worth inferior to my own. Such was I from eight to eight-and-twenty, and such I might still have remained but for you, dearest, loveliest Elizabeth! What do I not owe you? You taught me a lesson, difficult indeed at first, but of the greatest benefit. Through you, I was properly humbled. I came to you without doubting that I would be accepted. You showed me how inadequate all my claims were to please a woman deserving of being pleased.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHad you then convinced yourself that I would?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIndeed I had. What will you think of my vanity? I believed you wished for, and expected, my addresses.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy manner must have been at fault, though I assure you it was not intentional. I never meant to mislead you, but my spirits may often have carried me astray. How you must have hated me after _that_ evening!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHated you! I was angry, perhaps, at first, but my anger soon found a more proper object.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am almost afraid to ask what you thought of me when we met at Pemberley. Did you blame me for coming?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, indeed; I felt nothing but surprise.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYour surprise could not have exceeded _mine_ at being noticed by you. My conscience told me I deserved no unusual politeness, and I confess I did not expect to receive _more_ than was my due.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy purpose _then_,\u201d replied Darcy, \u201cwas to show you, through every civility within my power, that I was not so mean as to resent the past; and I hoped to gain your forgiveness and lessen your poor opinion by allowing you to see that I had heeded your reproofs.\u201d\"}"
