{"request_id":"1093864b8a228bb50e1e699e","rewritten_text":"Perdita’s name was not mentioned, yet I could not doubt that his love for her caused the wavering purpose he displayed. And who was more worthy of love than my noble-minded sister? Who better deserved the hand of this self-exalted king than she, whose glance belonged to a queen of nations? She loved him as he loved her, although disappointment had subdued her passion, while ambition struggled fiercely against his.

We went together to the House that evening. Though Raymond knew that his plans and prospects would be discussed and decided in the anticipated debate, he was cheerful and unconcerned. A hum, like that of ten thousand swarming beehives, stunned us as we entered the coffee-room. Groups of politicians had gathered, their brows anxious, their voices loud or grave. The aristocratic party—the wealthiest and most influential men in England—seemed less agitated than the others, since the question was to be debated without their interference. Near the fire stood Ryland and his supporters. Ryland was a man of obscure birth and immense wealth, inherited from his father, who had been a manufacturer. As a young man, he had witnessed the king’s abdication and the union of the two Houses of Lords and Commons; he had sympathized with these popular encroachments, and consolidating and extending them had become his life’s work. Since then, the influence of the landed proprietors had grown; at first Ryland was not displeased to see Lord Raymond’s schemes drawing away many of his opponents’ supporters. But matters had now gone too far. The poorer nobility welcomed the return of sovereignty as an event that would restore their lost power and rights. The half-extinguished spirit of royalty stirred in men’s minds, and they, willing slaves and self-appointed subjects, were ready to bend their necks beneath the yoke. Some upright and manly spirits still remained, pillars of the state; but the word republic had grown stale to common ears, and many—the event would show whether they were the majority—longed for royalty’s glitter and display. Ryland was roused to resistance. He declared that his tolerance alone had allowed this party to grow; but the time for indulgence had ended, and with one sweep of his arm he would clear away the cobwebs blinding his countrymen.

When Raymond entered the coffee-room, his friends greeted him almost with a shout. They gathered around him, counted their numbers, and explained why they were now certain to gain the support of various members who had not yet declared themselves."}

