          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"71387eff4b249c1e04190ce4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"I know she would!\u201d\\n\\nWidow Douglas\u2019s generous hospitality was a tempting offer, and Tom\u2019s arguments soon won. They decided not to tell anyone about their plans for the night. Then Tom thought that Huck might come that very night and give the signal. The idea took much of the excitement out of his expectations. Still, he could not bear to give up the fun at Widow Douglas\u2019s. And why should he, he reasoned? The signal had not come the night before, so why was it more likely to come tonight? The certain pleasure of the evening outweighed the uncertain treasure. Like a boy, he chose what he wanted most and decided not to think about the box of money again that day.\\n\\nThree miles below town, the ferryboat stopped at the mouth of a wooded hollow and tied up. The crowd poured ashore, and soon the distant forest and rocky heights echoed with shouts and laughter. Everyone tried every possible way to get hot and tired. After a while, the explorers wandered back to camp with hearty appetites, and then they began destroying the feast. When they had eaten, they rested and talked in the shade of broad oak trees. Before long, someone shouted:\\n\\n\u201cWho\u2019s ready for the cave?\u201d\\n\\nEveryone was. They gathered bundles of candles and immediately raced up the hill. The cave entrance was partway up the slope, shaped like the letter A. Its huge oak door stood open. Inside was a small chamber, cold as an icehouse, with solid limestone walls covered in a chilly, dewy sweat. It was romantic and mysterious to stand there in the deep darkness and look out at the green valley shining in the sun. But the feeling soon faded, and the rough play began again. Whenever someone lit a candle, everyone rushed at its owner. A struggle and brave defense followed, but the candle was soon knocked down or blown out, bringing delighted laughter and another chase. Yet everything eventually ends. After a while, the group filed down the steep slope of the main passage. Their flickering lights faintly showed the tall rock walls, which nearly met sixty feet above them. The main passage was only eight or ten feet wide. Every few steps, other tall, narrower cracks branched off on both sides. McDougal\u2019s cave was a huge maze of twisting passages that joined one another, separated again, and led nowhere.\"}"
