          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ee194f66173a17bdac3e3495\",\"rewritten_text\":\"During dinner, Mr. Bennet scarcely spoke at all; but once the servants had withdrawn, he decided it was time to converse with his guest. He therefore introduced a subject on which he expected Mr. Collins to shine, remarking that he seemed very fortunate in his patroness. Lady Catherine de Bourgh\u2019s attention to his wishes and concern for his comfort appeared remarkable. Mr. Bennet could not have chosen better. Mr. Collins praised her eloquently. The subject raised him to an even greater solemnity than usual; and, with an extremely important expression, he declared that he had never in his life witnessed such conduct in a person of rank\u2014such affability and condescension as he had experienced from Lady Catherine. She had graciously approved both sermons he had already had the honour of preaching before her. She had also invited him twice to dine at Rosings, and had sent for him only the previous Saturday to complete her quadrille party that evening. Lady Catherine was considered proud by many people, he knew, but he had never seen anything in her except affability. She had always spoken to him as she would to any other gentleman; she had not objected in the least to his joining the society of the neighbourhood, or to his leaving his parish occasionally for a week or two to visit his relations. She had even condescended to advise him to marry as soon as possible, provided he chose wisely; and had once visited him in his humble parsonage, where she had entirely approved the alterations he had been making, and had even been kind enough to suggest some herself\u2014shelves in the upstairs closets.\\n\\n\u201cThat is all very proper and civil, I am sure,\u201d said Mrs. Bennet, \u201cand I dare say she is a very agreeable woman. It is a pity that great ladies in general are not more like her. Does she live near you, sir?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe garden where my humble home stands is separated from Rosings Park, her Ladyship\u2019s residence, only by a lane.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI think you said she was a widow, sir? Does she have any family?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe has only one daughter, the heiress of Rosings and of a very extensive estate.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAh,\u201d cried Mrs. Bennet, shaking her head, \u201cthen she is better provided for than many girls. And what sort of young lady is she? Is she handsome?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cShe is a most charming young lady, indeed. Lady Catherine herself says that, in true beauty, Miss de Bourgh is far superior to the handsomest women of her sex; because there is something in her features that marks a young woman of distinguished birth.\u201d\"}"
