{"request_id":"a87d9241a46eb419e6e80450","rewritten_text":"Yet even now I had not drained the bitter potion to its dregs; I was not yet convinced of my loss; I did not yet feel in every pulse, every nerve, every thought, that I alone remained of my kind—that I was the LAST MAN.

The sky had clouded over, and a drizzling rain began at sunset. Even the eternal heavens weep, I thought; is it shameful, then, for mortal man to spend himself in tears? I remembered the ancient fables in which human beings are said to dissolve through weeping into ever-flowing fountains. Ah, if only that were so; then my fate would in some way resemble the watery death of Adrian and Clara. Oh, grief is fantastical; it weaves a web on which to trace the history of its sorrow through every form and change around it. It joins itself to all living nature; it draws nourishment from every object; like light, it fills all things, and, like light, gives them its own colours.

I had wandered some distance from the place where I had been cast ashore when I reached one of the watch-towers that stand at regular intervals along the Italian coast. I was glad of shelter, glad to find a work made by human hands after gazing so long upon nature’s desolate barrenness. I entered and climbed the rough, winding staircase to the guardroom. So far had fate shown kindness that no distressing trace remained of its former inhabitants. A few planks laid across two iron trestles and covered with dried corn leaves formed the bed offered to me; and an open chest containing some half-mouldered biscuits awakened an appetite that perhaps had existed before, though until then I had not known it. Thirst as well—violent and parching, caused by the seawater I had swallowed and by the exhaustion of my body—tormented me. Kind nature had made the satisfaction of these needs pleasurable, so that I—even I—was refreshed and calmed as I ate this wretched food and drank a little of the sour wine that half-filled a flask left in the abandoned dwelling. Then I stretched myself upon the bed, not to be despised by a shipwrecked man. The earthy scent of the dried leaves soothed my senses after the hateful smell of seaweed. I forgot my loneliness. I looked neither backward nor forward; my senses settled into rest. I fell asleep and dreamed of beloved inland scenes: haymakers, the shepherd’s whistle to his dog when he called for help driving the flock into the fold; sights and sounds belonging to my boyhood’s mountain life, which I had long forgotten."}

