          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"0de8153518dbbff8b4651ff1\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cI wish we had a donkey. We could all come on donkeys\u2014Jane, Miss Bates, and me\u2014with my caro sposo walking beside us. I really must speak to him about buying one. In the country, I think a donkey is almost necessary; for no matter how many resources a woman has, she cannot always stay shut up at home. And long walks, you know\u2014in summer there is dust, and in winter there is mud.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will find neither between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and now it is perfectly dry. Still, come on a donkey if you prefer it. You can borrow Mrs. Cole\u2019s. I would like everything to suit you as much as possible.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am sure you would. Indeed, I give you full credit, my good friend. Beneath that unusually dry, blunt manner, I know you have the warmest heart. As I tell Mr. E., you are a complete humorist. Yes, believe me, Knightley, I fully understand how thoughtful you have been toward me in arranging all this. You have chosen exactly what would please me.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding a table in the shade. He wanted to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the party, and he knew that having any of them sit outside to eat would certainly make him ill. Mr. Woodhouse must not be lured away from his misery under the false promise of a morning drive and an hour or two at Donwell.\\n\\nHe was invited sincerely. There were no hidden dangers waiting to punish his easy trust. He agreed. He had not been to Donwell for two years. \u201cOn some very fine morning, he, Emma, and Harriet could go quite comfortably; and he could sit with Mrs. Weston while the dear girls walked around the gardens. He did not suppose the gardens could be damp now, in the middle of the day. He would be extremely pleased to see the old house again, and happy to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton and any other neighbors. He could see no objection at all to his, Emma\u2019s, and Harriet\u2019s going there on some very fine morning. He thought it very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them\u2014very kind and sensible, and much better than dining out. He was not fond of dining out.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Knightley was lucky that everyone agreed so readily. The invitation was received so warmly everywhere that it seemed, like Mrs. Elton, they all considered the plan a special compliment to themselves. Emma and Harriet declared that they expected great pleasure from it; and Mr. Weston, without being asked, promised to bring Frank if possible\u2014a sign of approval and gratitude that was unnecessary. Mr. Knightley was therefore obliged to say that he would be glad to see him, and Mr. Weston promised to write at once and use every argument to persuade him to come.\"}"
        }
