{"request_id":"217436f82962f8ae4f81cc01","rewritten_text":"The only question that seemed to trouble him was whom to kill; for he understood that murder, like the religions of the Pagan world, needed both a victim and a priest. Since he was no genius, he had no enemies, and he felt this was not the time to satisfy any personal anger or dislike. The mission he had undertaken was one of great and serious importance. He therefore wrote a list of his friends and relatives on a sheet of notepaper and, after careful thought, chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old lady who lived in Curzon Street and was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been very fond of Lady Clem, as everyone called her, and he was wealthy himself, having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property when he came of age. So there was no vulgar financial advantage for him in her death. Indeed, the more he considered the matter, the more she seemed exactly the right person. Feeling that any delay would be unfair to Sybil, he decided to make his arrangements at once.

The first thing to do was, of course, to settle with the cheiromantist. He sat at a small Sheraton writing table near the window, wrote a cheque for £105 payable to Mr. Septimus Podgers, and put it in an envelope. He then told his valet to take it to West Moon Street. After that, he telephoned the stables for his hansom and dressed to go out. As he left the room, he looked back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never let her know what he was doing for her sake. He would keep the secret of his sacrifice hidden in his heart forever.

On the way to the Buckingham, he stopped at a florist’s and sent Sybil a beautiful basket of narcissus, with lovely white petals and bright, staring pheasants’ eyes. When he reached the club, he went straight to the library, rang the bell, and ordered the waiter to bring him a lemon and soda and a book on toxicology. He had decided that poison was the best method for this difficult business. Anything resembling personal violence was deeply unpleasant to him. Besides, he was determined not to murder Lady Clementina in a way that might attract public attention, since he hated the thought of being celebrated at Lady Windermere’s or seeing his name appear in the vulgar society newspapers."}

