          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f7f804c5af89815d21ab3117\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The day passed much as the previous one had. Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley spent part of the morning with the invalid, who continued, though slowly, to recover; and in the evening Elizabeth joined them in the drawing room. The loo table did not appear, however. Mr. Darcy was writing, while Miss Bingley, seated beside him, watched his letter take shape and repeatedly interrupted him with messages for his sister. Mr. Hurst and Mr. Bingley were playing piquet, and Mrs. Hurst was watching their game.\\n\\nElizabeth took up some needlework and was sufficiently entertained by what passed between Darcy and his companion. The lady\u2019s constant praise\u2014of his handwriting, the evenness of his lines, or the length of his letter\u2014together with the complete indifference with which he received it, made a curious dialogue and perfectly matched Elizabeth\u2019s opinion of both.\\n\\n\u201cHow delighted Miss Darcy will be to receive such a letter!\u201d\\n\\nHe did not answer.\\n\\n\u201cYou write remarkably fast.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou are mistaken. I write rather slowly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow many letters you must have to write over the course of a year! Business letters, too! How hateful I should find them!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThen it is fortunate that they fall to me rather than to you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cPlease tell your sister that I long to see her.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI have already told her so once, at your request.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI am afraid you do not like your pen. Let me mend it for you. I mend pens extremely well.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you\u2014but I always mend my own.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cHow do you manage to write so evenly?\u201d\\n\\nHe remained silent.\\n\\n\u201cTell your sister I am delighted to hear of her improvement on the harp, and please let her know that I am absolutely enchanted by her beautiful little design for a table. I think it infinitely better than Miss Grantley\u2019s.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWill you allow me to postpone your raptures until I write again? At present I have no room to do them justice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, it does not matter. I shall see her in January. But do you always write such charmingly long letters to her, Mr. Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThey are generally long; but whether they are always charming is not for me to decide.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cIt is my rule that anyone who can write a long letter with ease cannot write badly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat will not serve as a compliment to Darcy, Caroline,\u201d cried her brother, \u201cbecause he does not write with ease. He thinks too carefully over four-syllable words. Do you not, Darcy?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMy writing style is very different from yours.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh,\u201d cried Miss Bingley, \u201cCharles writes in the most careless manner imaginable.\"}"
