{"request_id":"a0f76d295c18c4e74a959b1f","rewritten_text":"The sole matter that appeared to perplex him was whom he should do away with; for he was not unaware that murder, like the religions of the Pagan world, demands a victim as well as a priest. Since he was no genius, he had no enemies, and he felt, moreover, that this was no occasion for indulging any private resentment or dislike, the mission upon which he was embarked being one of profound and solemn importance. He therefore drew up a list of his friends and relations on a sheet of notepaper, and, after mature reflection, chose Lady Clementina Beauchamp, a dear old lady who resided in Curzon Street and was his second cousin on his mother’s side. He had always been greatly attached to Lady Clem, as everyone called her, and, being himself extremely rich, having inherited all Lord Rugby’s property on coming of age, he could gain no vulgar financial benefit from her death. Indeed, the longer he considered the matter, the more perfectly suitable she seemed, and, feeling that further delay would be unfair to Sybil, he resolved to make his preparations immediately.

The first business, naturally, was to settle with the cheiromantist; so he seated himself at a little Sheraton writing-table beside the window, wrote a cheque for £105 payable to Mr. Septimus Podgers or order, and, putting it into an envelope, instructed his valet to carry it to West Moon Street. He then telephoned the stables for his hansom and dressed to go out. As he passed from the room, he glanced back at Sybil Merton’s photograph and swore that, whatever happened, he would never allow her to discover what he was doing on her behalf, but would forever conceal the secret of his sacrifice within his heart.

On the way to the Buckingham, he paused at a florist’s and sent Sybil a lovely basket of narcissus, their white petals marked by staring pheasants’ eyes. On reaching the club, he went directly to the library, rang the bell, and ordered the waiter to bring him a lemon-and-soda and a book on Toxicology. He had quite made up his mind that poison was the most suitable method for this difficult undertaking. Anything resembling personal violence was intensely repugnant to him; besides, he was particularly anxious not to murder Lady Clementina in any manner likely to draw public notice, for he detested the thought of being lionised at Lady Windermere’s or of seeing his name appear in the columns of vulgar society-newspapers."}

