{"request_id":"a5891780e9235d64480633d9","rewritten_text":"He said he was wasting his time where he was, and that letters from the friends he had made in London urged him to return and finish the negotiations they had begun for his Indian enterprise. He could delay his departure no longer. But since his journey to London might be followed, sooner than he now expected, by his longer voyage, he begged me to give him as much of my company as I could spare. He therefore asked me to leave my lonely island and meet him at Perth, so that we could travel south together. This letter partly brought me back to life, and I decided to leave my island in two days.

Before I left, however, I had one task to complete, and I shuddered to think of it. I had to pack my chemical instruments, which meant entering the room where I had carried out my hateful work and touching the tools whose sight made me sick. The next morning, at daybreak, I gathered enough courage to unlock the laboratory door. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed lay scattered across the floor, and I almost felt as though I had torn apart the living body of a human being. I stopped to steady myself, then entered the room. With a trembling hand, I carried the instruments outside. But I thought I should not leave the remains of my work to horrify and arouse the suspicions of the peasants. So I placed them in a basket with a large number of stones and set it aside, intending to throw it into the sea that night. Until then, I sat on the beach cleaning and arranging my chemical equipment.

The change in my feelings since the night the dæmon appeared could not have been greater. Before, I had viewed my promise with dark despair, as something that had to be fulfilled whatever the consequences. Now I felt as if a veil had been lifted from my eyes and I could see clearly for the first time. The thought of beginning my work again never entered my mind. The threat I had heard still troubled me, but I did not consider that any action of my own could prevent it. I had decided that creating another being like the fiend I had first made would be the most selfish and terrible act imaginable, and I drove from my mind every thought that might lead me to a different decision."}

