          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"213176a496961e46ce566602\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The storm raged violently throughout the night, but nothing noteworthy happened. The next morning, however, when they came down to breakfast, they found the dreadful bloodstain once again on the floor. \u201cI don\u2019t think the Paragon Detergent can be to blame,\u201d said Washington, \u201cbecause I\u2019ve tried it on everything. It must be the ghost.\u201d He therefore scrubbed away the stain a second time, but it reappeared the following morning. It was there on the third morning as well, although Mr. Otis himself had locked the library that night and taken the key upstairs. The entire family was now deeply interested; Mr. Otis began to suspect that he had been too absolute in denying the existence of ghosts, Mrs. Otis announced that she intended to join the Psychical Society, and Washington drafted a long letter to Messrs. Myers and Podmore concerning the Permanence of Sanguineous Stains when Associated with Crime. That night, all doubt about the objective existence of phantoms was removed forever.\\n\\nThe day had been warm and sunny, and in the cool of the evening the whole family went out for a drive. They did not return until nine o\u2019clock, when they had a light supper. The conversation did not turn toward ghosts, so there were not even those basic conditions of receptive expectation that so often precede the appearance of psychic phenomena. The topics discussed, as I later learned from Mr. Otis, were simply the usual subjects of cultivated Americans of the better class: the enormous superiority of Miss Fanny Davenport to Sarah Bernhardt as an actress; the difficulty of obtaining green corn, buckwheat cakes, and hominy even in the finest English houses; the importance of Boston in the development of the world-soul; the advantages of the baggage-check system in railway travel; and the sweetness of the New York accent compared with the London drawl. The supernatural was not mentioned at all, nor was Sir Simon de Canterville referred to in any way. At eleven o\u2019clock the family went to bed, and by half-past, all the lights were out. Some time later, Mr. Otis was awakened by a strange noise in the corridor outside his room. It sounded like metal clanking and seemed to draw nearer with every moment. He rose immediately, struck a match, and checked the time. It was exactly one o\u2019clock. He remained perfectly calm and felt his pulse, which was not at all feverish. The strange noise continued, and with it he clearly heard footsteps. He put on his slippers, took a small, oblong phial from his dressing case, and opened the door.\"}"
