          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"85b9760f1885835b93daa0a7\",\"rewritten_text\":\"She now felt strongly inclined to go to the outer door; she wished to see whether it rained. Why should she suspect herself of any other motive? Captain Wentworth must be out of sight. She rose from her seat and would go; one half of her should not always be so much wiser than the other, or forever suspecting the other of being worse than it was. She would see if it rained. In an instant, however, she was driven back by the entrance of Captain Wentworth himself, with a party of ladies and gentlemen, evidently his acquaintances, whom he must have joined a little below Milsom Street. The sight of her struck and confused him more plainly than she had ever seen before; he looked quite red. For the first time since their renewed acquaintance, she felt that she was betraying less sensibility than he. She had the advantage of him, having been prepared during the last few moments. All the overpowering, blinding, bewildering first effects of strong surprise had passed for her. Still, she had felt enough! It was agitation, pain, pleasure, something between delight and misery.\\n\\nHe spoke to her, then turned away. His manner was marked by embarrassment. She could not have called it cold or friendly, nor anything so definite as embarrassed.\\n\\nAfter a brief interval, however, he approached her and spoke again. Mutual enquiries on ordinary subjects followed: neither of them, probably, much enlightened by what they heard, while Anne remained fully aware that he was less at ease than formerly. By having been so constantly together, they had learned to speak to each other with considerable apparent indifference and composure; but he could not do so now. Time had altered him, or Louisa had altered him. There was some kind of consciousness. He looked very well, not as though his health or spirits had suffered, and he spoke of Uppercross, the Musgroves, even of Louisa, with a momentary return of his own arch significance as he mentioned her; yet Captain Wentworth was not comfortable, not easy, and unable to pretend that he was.\\n\\nIt did not surprise Anne, but it grieved her, to see that Elizabeth would not acknowledge him. She saw that he saw Elizabeth, and that Elizabeth saw him; there was complete inward recognition on both sides. She was convinced that he was ready and expecting to be recognized as an acquaintance, and she suffered the pain of watching her sister turn away with unchanging coldness.\\n\\nLady Dalrymple\u2019s carriage, for which Miss Elliot was becoming very impatient, now drew up; the servant entered to announce it.\"}"
