{"request_id":"645ee857cda22c05343b40ef","rewritten_text":"I uncovered it, and a gentle breeze soon fanned it into a flame. I noticed this too and made a fan from branches, which stirred the embers when they were almost out. When night came again, I was pleased to discover that the fire gave light as well as heat. It also helped me prepare food, for some scraps the travellers had left had been roasted and tasted far better than the berries I gathered from the trees. I therefore tried cooking my food in the same way, placing it on the glowing embers. I found that the berries were ruined by this, while the nuts and roots were greatly improved.

“Food, however, became scarce, and I often spent the entire day searching in vain for a few acorns to ease my hunger. When I realized this, I decided to leave the place where I had been living and search for somewhere my few needs could be met more easily. As I left, I deeply regretted losing the fire I had found by accident, since I did not know how to make it again. I spent several hours seriously considering this problem, but at last I had to give up trying to replace it. Wrapping myself in my cloak, I crossed the woods toward the setting sun. I wandered for three days and finally reached open country. A heavy snowfall had occurred the night before, and the fields were covered in one unbroken sheet of white. The sight was bleak, and my feet grew numb from the cold, wet substance covering the ground.

“It was about seven in the morning, and I longed for food and shelter. At last I saw a small hut on a rise, probably built for the use of a shepherd. This was a new sight to me, and I examined it with great curiosity. The door stood open, so I went inside. An old man sat near a fire, preparing his breakfast. He turned at the sound, saw me, screamed loudly, and ran across the fields. His weakened body seemed hardly capable of such speed. His strange appearance, unlike anything I had seen before, and his flight surprised me somewhat. But I was delighted by the hut. Snow and rain could not enter; the ground was dry; and to me it seemed then as beautiful and heavenly a refuge as Pandæmonium seemed to the demons of hell after their suffering in the lake of fire."}

