          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"8c134ee0174488e798aca7f4\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShucks, Tom,\u201d I said, \u201cI think you could tell a person. What\u2019s a bar sinister?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh, _I_ don\u2019t know. But he has to have one. All the nobility does.\u201d\\n\\nThat was simply his way. If he didn\u2019t feel like explaining something to you, he wouldn\u2019t. You could question him for a week, and it wouldn\u2019t make any difference.\\n\\nHe had finished all that coat-of-arms business, so now he began completing the rest of that part of the work, which was to plan a mournful inscription\u2014Jim had to have one, just as they all did. He composed a lot of them, wrote them on a sheet of paper, and read them aloud:\\n\\n1. _Here a captive heart broke._\\n\\n2. _Here a poor prisoner, forsaken by the world and friends, wore out his sorrowful life._\\n\\n3. _Here a lonely heart broke, and a weary spirit went to its rest, after thirty-seven years of solitary captivity._\\n\\n4. _Here, homeless and friendless, after thirty-seven years of bitter captivity, perished a noble stranger, natural son of Louis XIV._\\n\\nTom\u2019s voice trembled as he read them, and he nearly broke down. When he finished, he couldn\u2019t decide which one Jim should scratch onto the wall, because they were all so good; but finally he said he would let him scratch them all on. Jim said it would take him a year to scratch so much writing onto the logs with a nail, and besides, he didn\u2019t know how to form letters; but Tom said he would outline them for him, and then Jim would only have to follow the lines. Then, after a moment, he said:\\n\\n\u201cCome to think of it, the logs won\u2019t do. They don\u2019t have log walls in a dungeon; we have to carve the inscriptions into a rock. We\u2019ll bring in a rock.\u201d\\n\\nJim said the rock would be worse than the logs; he said it would take him such an awfully long time to carve them into a rock that he would never get out. But Tom said he would let me help him. Then he looked over to see how Jim and I were doing with the pens. It was terribly tedious, difficult, slow work, gave my hands no chance to heal from the sores, and we hardly seemed to be making any progress; so Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cI know how to fix it. We need a rock for the coat of arms and the mournful inscriptions, and we can kill two birds with that same rock.\u201d\"}"
