          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"2de87c81a22650af8daa96ce\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The two ladies were delighted to see their dear friend again, declared that it had been ages since they had met, and repeatedly asked what she had been doing since they parted. They paid little attention to the rest of the family: avoiding Mrs. Bennet as much as possible, saying little to Elizabeth, and nothing at all to the others. They soon left, rising with such energy that their brother was surprised, and hurrying away as though eager to escape Mrs. Bennet\u2019s polite attentions.\\n\\nThe prospect of the Netherfield ball was extremely pleasing to every woman in the family. Mrs. Bennet chose to regard it as a compliment to her eldest daughter, and was especially flattered to receive the invitation from Mr. Bingley himself rather than a formal card. Jane imagined a happy evening in the company of her two friends and their brother\u2019s attentions; Elizabeth looked forward to dancing often with Mr. Wickham and seeing confirmation of everything in Mr. Darcy\u2019s expression and conduct. Catherine and Lydia\u2019s anticipated happiness depended less on any one event or particular person; for although each of them, like Elizabeth, intended to dance half the evening with Mr. Wickham, he was far from the only partner who could satisfy them, and a ball was, in any case, a ball. Even Mary could assure her family that she had no objection to it.\\n\\n\u201cAs long as I can have my mornings to myself,\u201d she said, \u201cthat is sufficient. I do not think it a sacrifice to take part occasionally in evening engagements. Society makes demands upon us all; and I consider intervals of recreation and amusement desirable for everyone.\u201d\\n\\nElizabeth\u2019s spirits were so high that, although she did not often speak unnecessarily to Mr. Collins, she could not help asking whether he intended to accept Mr. Bingley\u2019s invitation and, if so, whether he thought it proper to join in the 