          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"cb84c76ee96fd27bb9353a42\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Weeks passed, and I became more and more absorbed in you. Then something new happened. I had drawn you as Paris in delicate armor, and as Adonis wearing a huntsman\u2019s cloak and carrying a polished boar spear. Crowned with heavy lotus blossoms, you had sat on the prow of Adrian\u2019s barge, gazing across the green, muddy Nile. You had leaned over the still pool of some Greek woodland and seen in its silent silver the wonder of your own face. And it had all been what art should be\u2014unconscious, ideal, and distant. One day, a fatal day, I sometimes think, I decided to paint a magnificent portrait of you as you truly are, not in the costume of vanished ages, but in your own clothes and your own time. Whether it was the realism of the method, or simply the force of your personality, presented to me so directly, without mist or veil, I cannot say. But I know that as I worked, every flake and film of color seemed to reveal my secret. I became afraid that others would discover my idolatry. I felt, Dorian, that I had said too much, that I had put too much of myself into it. Then I resolved never to let the picture be exhibited. You were somewhat annoyed, but you did not understand what it meant to me. Harry, whom I spoke to about it, laughed at me. But I did not care. When the picture was finished and I sat alone with it, I felt that I had been right.... Well, after a few days it left my studio, and once I had escaped the unbearable fascination of its presence, I thought I had been foolish to imagine I had seen anything in it beyond the fact that you were extraordinarily handsome and that I could paint. Even now I cannot help thinking it is a mistake to believe that the passion felt in creation is ever truly visible in the work created. Art is always more abstract than we imagine. Form and color tell us of form and color\u2014that is all. It often seems to me that art conceals the artist far more completely than it reveals him. And so, when I received this offer from Paris, I decided to make your portrait the central work in my exhibition. It never occurred to me that you would refuse. I see now that you were right. The picture cannot be shown. You must not be angry with me, Dorian, for telling you this.\"}"
