          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"d816c54f023906389604deca\",\"rewritten_text\":\"There was a pleasant feeling in our village on Saturday nights: everything seemed to be tidied up, and there was a quiet pause before beginning life again. This encouraged Joe to stay out half an hour later on Saturdays than at other times. When the half-hour ended and the rum and water ran out together, Joe stood up to leave and took me by the hand.\\n\\n\u201cWait half a moment, Mr. Gargery,\u201d said the strange man. \u201cI think I have a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I do, the boy shall have it.\u201d\\n\\nHe found it among a handful of small coins, wrapped it in some crumpled paper, and gave it to me. \u201cYours!\u201d he said. \u201cMind! It\u2019s your own.\u201d\\n\\nI thanked him, staring at him much longer than good manners allowed and holding tightly to Joe. He said good-night to Joe and to Mr. Wopsle, who left with us. To me he gave only a look with his aiming eye\u2014or rather, not a look, since he closed it. Still, strange things can be done with an eye by hiding it.\\n\\nOn the way home, if I had felt like talking, I would have done all the talking, because Mr. Wopsle left us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen. Joe walked the whole way home with his mouth wide open, trying to rinse the rum out with as much air as possible. But I was almost stunned by the return of my old wrongdoing and my old acquaintance, and could think of nothing else.\\n\\nMy sister was not in a very bad mood when we entered the kitchen, and Joe took advantage of this unusual event to tell her about the bright shilling. \u201cA bad one, I\u2019ll be bound,\u201d said Mrs. Joe triumphantly, \u201cor he wouldn\u2019t have given it to the boy! Let\u2019s see it.\u201d\\n\\nI took it from the paper, and it turned out to be genuine. \u201cBut what\u2019s this?\u201d said Mrs. Joe, dropping the shilling and picking up the paper. \u201cTwo one-pound notes?\u201d\\n\\nThey were two large, sweaty one-pound notes that seemed to have spent a great deal of time among all the cattle markets in the county. Joe immediately grabbed his hat and ran back to the Jolly Bargemen to return them to their owner. While he was gone, I sat on my usual stool and stared blankly at my sister, fairly sure that the man would not be there.\\n\\nAfter a while, Joe returned and said the man had gone, but that he had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen about the notes.\"}"
