{"request_id":"ed5f934eaec59047deb35d11","rewritten_text":"He said that he was wasting his time where he was, and that letters from the friends he had made in London urged him to return and complete the negotiations they had begun for his Indian enterprise. He could no longer delay his departure; but since his journey to London might be followed, sooner than he now expected, by his longer voyage, he begged me to give him as much of my company as I could spare. He therefore asked me to leave my solitary island and meet him at Perth, so that we might travel south together. This letter partly brought me back to life, and I resolved to leave my island in two days.

Yet before I departed, I had one task to perform, and I shuddered to think of it: I had to pack my chemical instruments, which meant entering the room where my hateful work had taken place and handling the utensils whose sight sickened me. The next morning, at daybreak, I summoned enough courage to unlock the door of my laboratory. The remains of the half-finished creature I had destroyed lay scattered across the floor, and I almost felt as though I had torn apart the living flesh of a human being. I paused to steady myself, then entered the chamber. With a trembling hand, I carried the instruments out, but I reflected that I could not leave the remnants of my work to arouse the peasants’ horror and suspicion. I therefore placed them in a basket with a large quantity of stones and, setting it aside, resolved to throw it into the sea that very night. Meanwhile, I sat on the beach, cleaning and arranging my chemical apparatus.

Nothing could have been more complete than the change in my feelings since the night the dæmon appeared. Before, I had regarded my promise with bleak despair, as something that had to be fulfilled whatever the consequences; but now I felt as if a veil had been lifted from my eyes and that, for the first time, I could see clearly. The thought of resuming my work did not occur to me for an instant. The threat I had heard weighed on my mind, but I did not consider that any voluntary act of mine could prevent it. I had decided that creating another being like the fiend I had first made would be an act of the basest and most monstrous selfishness, and I drove from my mind every thought that might lead me to any other conclusion."}

