          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"acd482270140e171f4d840b8\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The narrow streets and courtyards finally opened into a large space. Pens for animals and other signs of a cattle market stood scattered around it. Sikes slowed when they reached the place, because the girl could no longer keep up the fast pace they had maintained. Turning to Oliver, he roughly ordered him to take Nancy\u2019s hand.\\n\\n\u201cDo you hear?\u201d Sikes growled when Oliver hesitated and looked around.\\n\\nThey stood in a dark corner, well away from the path used by passers-by.\\n\\nOliver saw clearly that resisting would do no good. He held out his hand, and Nancy gripped it tightly.\\n\\n\u201cGive me the other,\u201d said Sikes, taking Oliver\u2019s free hand. \u201cHere, Bull\u2019s-Eye!\u201d\\n\\nThe dog looked up and growled.\\n\\n\u201cListen, boy!\u201d said Sikes, placing his other hand around Oliver\u2019s throat. \u201cIf he says even the softest word, hold him! Do you understand?\u201d\\n\\nThe dog growled again. Licking his lips, he watched Oliver as though eager to fasten onto his throat at once.\\n\\n\u201cHe\u2019s as willing as a Christian, I swear he is!\u201d said Sikes, looking at the animal with grim, fierce approval. \u201cNow you know what to expect, master, so call out as quickly as you like; the dog will soon stop you. Move on, boy!\u201d\\n\\nBull\u2019s-Eye wagged his tail as if pleased by this unusually affectionate speech. Then, giving Oliver another warning growl, he led the way forward.\\n\\nThey were crossing Smithfield, though Oliver might have thought it was Grosvenor Square, for all he knew. The night was dark and foggy. The shop lights could barely shine through the thick mist, which grew heavier by the moment and covered the streets and houses in gloom. The strange place seemed even stranger to Oliver, and his uncertainty became more miserable and depressing.\\n\\nThey had gone only a few hurried steps when a deep church bell struck the hour. At its first stroke, both his guides stopped and turned toward the sound.\\n\\n\u201cEight o\u2019clock, Bill,\u201d said Nancy when the bell stopped.\\n\\n\u201cWhat\u2019s the point of telling me? I can hear it, can\u2019t I?\u201d Sikes replied.\\n\\n\u201cI wonder whether they can hear it,\u201d said Nancy.\\n\\n\u201cOf course they can,\u201d Sikes answered. \u201cIt was fair time when I was locked up, and there wasn\u2019t a penny trumpet at the fair whose squeaking I couldn\u2019t hear. After they shut me in for the night, the noise outside made that huge old jail so quiet that I could almost have smashed my brains against the iron door.\u201d\"}"
