          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"762e8ce874665fc86e2add1e\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It was the start of February; and Anne, who had been a month in Bath, was becoming very impatient for news from Uppercross and Lyme. She wished to hear far more than Mary had told her. It was three weeks since she had received any news at all. She knew only that Henrietta had returned home; and that Louisa, though thought to be recovering quickly, was still in Lyme. She was thinking of them all very earnestly one evening, when a letter from Mary, thicker than usual, was brought to her; and, to increase the pleasure and surprise, it came with Admiral and Mrs Croft\u2019s compliments.\\n\\nThe Crofts must be in Bath! That was an interesting circumstance. They were people toward whom her heart naturally turned.\\n\\n\u201cWhat is this?\u201d cried Sir Walter. \u201cThe Crofts have arrived in Bath? The Crofts who rent Kellynch? What have they sent you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cA letter from Uppercross Cottage, Sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! Such letters are useful passports. They obtain an introduction. I should have called on Admiral Croft in any case. I know what is owed to my tenant.\u201d\\n\\nAnne could listen no longer; she could not even have said how the poor Admiral\u2019s complexion escaped notice; her letter had completely absorbed her. It had been started several days before.\\n\\n\u201cFebruary 1st.\\n\\n\u201cMY DEAR ANNE,\\n\\nI offer no excuse for my silence, because I know how little people care for letters in a place like Bath. You must be far too happy to concern yourself with Uppercross, which, as you know very well, gives one little to write about. We have had a very dull Christmas; Mr and Mrs Musgrove have not held a single dinner party throughout the holidays. I do not count the Hayters as anybody. The holidays are finally over: I believe no children ever had such a long one. I am certain I never did. The house was emptied yesterday, except for the little Harvilles; but you will be surprised to hear that they have not gone home. Mrs Harville must be a strange mother to keep them away so long. I cannot understand it. They are not at all agreeable children, in my opinion; yet Mrs Musgrove appears to like them quite as much, if not more, than her grandchildren. What terrible weather we have had! It may not matter in Bath, with your excellent pavements; but in the country it is a serious concern. No one has called on me since the second week of January, except Charles Hayter, who had been coming much more often than was welcome. Between ourselves, I think it a great pity Henrietta did not stay in Lyme as long as Louisa; it would have kept her somewhat out of his way.\"}"
