{"request_id":"65791ed899c09df6c4fea9c1","rewritten_text":"I do not understand at all why she wanted to become close to me; but if the same situation happened again, I am sure I would be deceived again. Caroline did not return my visit until yesterday, and in the meantime I received neither a note nor a line. When she came, it was obvious that she took no pleasure in the visit. She offered a brief, formal apology for not calling sooner, said nothing about wanting to see me again, and was so changed in every way that, when she left, I was fully determined not to continue the acquaintance. I pity her, though I cannot help blaming her. She was quite wrong to single me out as she did; I can honestly say that every step toward greater intimacy began with her. But I pity her because she must know that she has behaved badly, and because I am certain that worry about her brother is the reason. I need not explain further; and although _we_ know that this worry is completely unnecessary, if she feels it, it easily explains her behavior toward me. Since he is so deservedly dear to his sister, any concern she feels for him is natural and kind. Still, I cannot understand why she should have such fears now, because if he had cared for me at all, we must have met long ago. I am certain he knows that I am in town, from something she said herself; yet from the way she speaks, it seems she wants to convince herself that he is truly interested in Miss Darcy. I cannot understand it. If I were not afraid of judging her unfairly, I would almost be tempted to say that all this strongly suggests deceit. I will try to put away every painful thought and think only of what will make me happy: your affection and the unfailing kindness of my dear uncle and aunt. Write to me very soon. Miss Bingley mentioned that he might never return to Netherfield, and might give up the house, but she did not speak with any certainty. We had better not mention it. I am very glad that you have received such pleasant news from our friends at Hunsford. Please go to see them with Sir William and Maria. I am sure you will be very comfortable there.

“Yours, etc.”

This letter caused Elizabeth some pain; but her spirits rose again when she reflected that Jane would at least no longer be deceived by the sister. All hope concerning the brother was now completely gone."}

