          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"b68defb299d29519e6a5385c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cWhat is it?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTen o\u2019clock, sir.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhat do you mean? Ten o\u2019clock at night?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, sir. Your honour told me to wake you.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I remember. Very well, very well.\u201d\\n\\nAfter several dull attempts to fall asleep again\u2014which the man skillfully thwarted by constantly stirring the fire for five minutes\u2014he got up, put on his hat, and went out. He turned into the Temple and, having revived himself by walking twice along the pavements of King\u2019s Bench Walk and Paper Buildings, turned into Stryver\u2019s chambers.\\n\\nThe Stryver clerk, who never took part in these conferences, had gone home, and Stryver himself opened the door. He wore slippers and a loose dressing gown, and his throat was bare for greater comfort. Around his eyes was that somewhat wild, strained, weathered look seen in all hard drinkers of his kind, from the portrait of Jeffries onward, and traceable, beneath various artistic disguises, through the portraits of every age of drinking.\\n\\n\u201cYou are a little late, Memory,\u201d said Stryver.\\n\\n\u201cAbout the usual time; perhaps a quarter of an hour later.\u201d\\n\\nThey entered a dingy room lined with books and covered with papers, where a fire blazed. A kettle steamed on the hob, and amid the disorder of papers a table gleamed, set with plenty of wine, brandy, rum, sugar, and lemons.\\n\\n\u201cYou have had your bottle, I see, Sydney.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTwo tonight, I think. I was dining with the day\u2019s client\u2014or watching him dine; it amounts to the same thing!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThat was an excellent point, Sydney, the one you used to establish the identification. How did you think of it? When did it occur to you?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI thought he was rather a handsome fellow, and I thought I would have been much the same sort of fellow if I had had any luck.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Stryver laughed until his prematurely developed belly shook.\\n\\n\u201cYou and your luck, Sydney! Get to work, get to work.\u201d\\n\\nThe jackal, sullen enough, loosened his clothes, went into an adjoining room, and returned with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and a couple of towels. He soaked the towels in the water, partly wrung them out, folded them around his head in a hideous fashion, sat down at the table, and said, \u201cNow I\u2019m ready!\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNot much boiling down to do tonight, Memory,\u201d said Mr. Stryver cheerfully, as he looked through his papers.\\n\\n\u201cHow much?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOnly two sets.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThere they are, Sydney. Fire away!\u201d\\n\\nThe lion then settled himself on his back on a sofa at one side of the drinking table, while the jackal sat at his own proper, paper-covered table on the other side, with the bottles and glasses within easy reach.\"}"
