{"request_id":"acfc7e96de7e84d52ab4ac08","rewritten_text":"You see, I had put my handkerchief in my helmet, along with a few other things; but it was the sort of helmet you cannot remove without help. That had not crossed my mind when I placed it there; indeed, I did not know it. I had assumed it would be especially convenient. And now the knowledge that it was there, so near and easy to reach in appearance, yet impossible to get at, made the matter far worse and more difficult to endure. Yes, what you cannot have is generally what you want most; everyone has observed that. Well, it drew my thoughts away from everything else—completely away—and fixed them inside my helmet; mile after mile they remained there, imagining the handkerchief, picturing the handkerchief. It was bitterly irritating to feel salty sweat trickling into my eyes while I could not reach it. It may sound like a small matter on paper, but it was no small matter at all; it was misery of the most genuine kind. I would not say so if it were not true. I resolved that next time I would carry a reticule, whatever it looked like and whatever people might say. Naturally, those iron gentlemen of the Round Table would consider it scandalous, and perhaps raise Sheol over it; but as for me, comfort first and style afterward. So we jogged on, and from time to time crossed a stretch of dust, which rose in clouds, filled my nose, and made me sneeze and weep; and naturally I said things I should not have said—I do not deny it. I am no better than anyone else.

We seemed unable to meet anyone in that lonely Britain, not even an ogre; and, considering my mood at the time, that was fortunate for the ogre—at least, for an ogre carrying a handkerchief. Most knights would have cared only about taking his armor; but if I could get his bandanna, he might keep the rest of his hardware, for all I cared.

Meanwhile, it was growing hotter and hotter inside. The sun was beating down, heating the iron more and more with every passing moment. When you are hot in that fashion, every minor annoyance becomes irritating. At a trot I rattled like a crate of dishes, which bothered me; besides, I could hardly endure the shield slapping and banging, first against my breast and then around my back. If I slowed to a walk, my joints creaked and squealed in the tiresome manner of a wheelbarrow; and since that pace made no breeze, I was nearly roasted in that stove. Moreover, the more slowly you went, the more heavily the iron settled upon you, until every minute you seemed to weigh additional tons."}

