          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"52b32ff07cf6bd1d3e4ca292\",\"rewritten_text\":\"The storm raged fiercely all night, but nothing unusual happened. The next morning, however, when they came down to breakfast, they found the terrible bloodstain once again on the floor. \u201cI don\u2019t think the Paragon Detergent is to blame,\u201d said Washington, \u201cbecause I\u2019ve tried it on everything. It must be the ghost.\u201d He rubbed out the stain again, but it returned the following morning. It was there on the third morning too, although Mr. Otis himself had locked the library that night and taken the key upstairs. The whole family was now very interested. Mr. Otis began to suspect that he had been too certain in denying the existence of ghosts, Mrs. Otis said she intended to join the Psychical Society, and Washington prepared a long letter to Messrs. Myers and Podmore about the Permanence of Bloodstains Connected with Crime. That night, all doubt about the ghost\u2019s real existence disappeared forever.\\n\\nThe day had been warm and sunny, and in the cool evening the whole family went out for a drive. They did not return until nine o\u2019clock, when they had a light supper. Their conversation did not turn to ghosts, so there were not even the basic conditions of expectant belief that so often come before psychic phenomena. As I later learned from Mr. Otis, they discussed only the usual subjects of cultured, upper-class Americans: the great superiority of Miss Fanny Davenport to Sarah Bernhardt as an actress; the difficulty of getting green corn, buckwheat cakes, and hominy, even in the finest English houses; Boston\u2019s importance in developing the world-soul; the benefits of the railway baggage-check system; and the sweetness of the New York accent compared with the London drawl. They did not mention the supernatural at all, nor did they refer to Sir Simon de Canterville. At eleven o\u2019clock the family went to bed, and by half-past eleven all the lights were out. Some time later, Mr. Otis woke to a strange noise in the corridor outside his room. It sounded like metal clanking and seemed to come closer every moment. He got up immediately, struck a match, and checked the time. It was exactly one o\u2019clock. He was perfectly calm and felt his pulse, which was not at all feverish. The strange noise continued, and he could clearly hear footsteps with it. He put on his slippers, took a small, oblong bottle from his dressing case, and opened the door.\"}"
