          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"6f609b187cacbade0c321317\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Reading over his letter again while replying to it had such a softening effect that Emma found it especially necessary to strengthen Harriet with a few firm words. Harriet was so troubled by the thought of making him unhappy, cared so much about what his mother and sisters would think and say, and was so anxious that they should not consider her ungrateful, that Emma believed the young man would have been accepted after all if he had appeared before her at that moment.\\n\\nThe letter was written, sealed, and sent. The matter was settled, and Harriet was safe. She was rather downcast all evening, but Emma understood her kind-hearted regret. At times she eased it by speaking of her own affection, and at others by mentioning Mr. Elton.\\n\\n\u201cI shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,\u201d Harriet said sadly.\\n\\n\u201cAnd even if you were, I could never bear to part with you, my Harriet. You are far too necessary at Hartfield to be spared to Abbey-Mill.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I am sure I should never want to go there; I am never happy anywhere but at Hartfield.\u201d\\n\\nSome time later, Harriet said, \u201cI think Mrs. Goddard would be very surprised if she knew what had happened. I am sure Miss Nash would be\u2014for Miss Nash thinks her own sister very well married, and she is only married to a linen-draper.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOne ought to be sorry to see greater pride or refinement in a schoolteacher, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would envy you such a chance to marry. Even this achievement would seem valuable to her. As for anything better for you, I suppose she has no idea of it. The attentions of a certain person can hardly yet be part of Highbury gossip. So far, I believe you and I are the only people who have understood his looks and manners.\u201d\\n\\nHarriet blushed and smiled, and said something about being surprised that people should like her so much. The thought of Mr. Elton certainly cheered her; but after a while she again felt tenderly toward the rejected Mr. Martin.\\n\\n\u201cNow he has received my letter,\u201d she said softly. \u201cI wonder what they are all doing\u2014whether his sisters know. If he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope he will not take it too badly.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cLet us think of those absent friends who are more happily occupied,\u201d cried Emma. \u201cAt this moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is showing your picture to his mother and sisters, telling them how much more beautiful the original is, and, after they have asked for it five or six times, allowing them to hear your name\u2014your own dear name.\u201d\"}"
