          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"ded9c817ab34743d13cc6ec3\",\"rewritten_text\":\"\u201cShall I ask them? Or Miss Bates? She is closer by.\u2014And I am not sure that Miss Bates may not understand everyone\u2019s wishes as well as anyone. I think we need a larger council. Suppose I go and invite Miss Bates to join us?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWell\u2014if you like,\u201d said Mrs. Weston, hesitating a little, \u201cif you think she will be useful.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou will get nothing useful from Miss Bates,\u201d said Emma. \u201cShe will be full of delight and thanks, but she will tell you nothing. She will not even listen to your questions. I see no benefit in consulting Miss Bates.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut she is so entertaining, so very entertaining! I am very fond of hearing Miss Bates talk. And I need not bring the whole family, you know.\u201d\\n\\nMr. Weston now joined them, and when he heard the proposal, gave it his firm approval.\\n\\n\u201cYes, do, Frank.\u2014Go and fetch Miss Bates, and let us settle the matter at once. She will enjoy the plan, I am sure; and I do not know a better person to show us how to overcome difficulties. Fetch Miss Bates. We are becoming a little too particular. She is a constant lesson in how to be happy. But fetch them both. Invite them both.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBoth, sir! Can the old lady\u2014?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThe old lady! No, the young lady, of course. I shall think you a great fool, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cOh! I beg your pardon, sir. I did not remember at once. Certainly, if you wish it, I will try to persuade them both.\u201d And away he ran.\\n\\nLong before he returned with the short, neat, quick-moving aunt and her elegant niece, Mrs. Weston, being a kind-hearted woman and a good wife, had looked over the passage again and found its problems much less serious than she had first thought\u2014indeed, very slight. With that, the decision was made. Everything else, at least in theory, was perfectly easy. The smaller arrangements of tables and chairs, lights and music, tea and supper, seemed to arrange themselves; or could be left as minor matters to settle later between Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes.\u2014Everyone invited was certainly coming; Frank had already written to Enscombe asking to stay a few days beyond his fortnight, and this could not possibly be refused. It was to be a delightful dance.\\n\\nWhen Miss Bates arrived, she agreed most warmly that it must be. She was not needed as an adviser; but as an approver, a much safer role, she was very welcome. Her approval, both general and detailed, warm and endless, could not fail to please; and for another half-hour they all walked back and forth between the different rooms, some making suggestions, some listening, and everyone happily enjoying the plans for the future.\"}"
