          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"c973f819bbaba26655dae4d5\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Yet despite all this, the boy became increasingly melancholy, pale, and dejected. She added hot baths, sitz baths, shower baths, and plunges. The boy stayed as gloomy as a hearse. She began supporting the water treatment with a thin oatmeal diet and blister-plasters. She reckoned his capacity as she might a jug\u2019s, and filled him daily with patent cure-alls.\\n\\nBy this time Tom had grown indifferent to persecution. This stage filled the old lady\u2019s heart with alarm. That indifference had to be broken at any cost. Then she heard of Pain-killer for the first time. She ordered a large supply immediately. She tasted it and felt profoundly grateful. It was nothing less than fire in liquid form. She abandoned the water treatment and everything else, placing all her faith in Pain-killer. She gave Tom a teaspoonful and watched anxiously for the effect. Her troubles vanished at once, and her soul was peaceful again, because the \u201cindifference\u201d had been shattered. The boy could not have displayed a livelier, heartier interest if she had set a fire beneath him.\\n\\nTom decided it was time to wake up; this kind of existence might be romantic enough in his ruined condition, but it was acquiring too little sentiment and too much distracting variety. He considered several ways of obtaining relief, and at last settled on pretending to be fond of Pain-killer. He requested it so frequently that he became a nuisance, and his aunt finally told him to help himself and stop troubling her. Had it been Sid, she would have enjoyed the matter without a doubt; but because it was Tom, she secretly kept watch on the bottle. She saw that the medicine truly was being used up, but never suspected that the boy was repairing a crack in the sitting-room floor with it.\\n\\nOne day Tom was dosing the crack when his aunt\u2019s yellow cat approached, purring, eyeing the teaspoon greedily, and begging for a taste. Tom said:\\n\\n\u201cDon\u2019t ask for it unless you want it, Peter.\u201d\\n\\nBut Peter made it clear that he did want it.\\n\\n\u201cYou\u2019d better be certain.\u201d\\n\\nPeter was certain.\\n\\n\u201cNow that you\u2019ve asked for it, I\u2019ll give it to you, because there\u2019s nothing mean about me; but if you discover you don\u2019t like it, you must blame nobody but yourself.\u201d\\n\\nPeter agreed. So Tom pried open his mouth and poured in the Pain-killer. Peter leaped a couple of yards into the air, then gave a war-whoop and raced around the room, crashing into furniture, knocking over flower-pots, and creating general havoc.\"}"
