          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"f52d6859577b4748ee79745b\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Those old and feeble men were always seen by the public bowing, and people commonly believed that after bowing one customer out, they continued bowing in the empty office until they bowed another customer in.\\n\\nThe barrister was perceptive enough to understand that the banker would not have expressed such a strong opinion without something close to moral certainty behind it. Though unprepared for the large pill he had to swallow, he forced it down. \u201cAnd now,\u201d said Mr. Stryver, shaking his lawyerly forefinger at the Temple in general once he had done so, \u201cmy way out of this is to prove that you are all in the wrong.\u201d\\n\\nThis was a piece of Old Bailey courtroom strategy, and it brought him great relief. \u201cYou shall not prove me wrong, young lady,\u201d said Mr. Stryver. \u201cI\u2019ll do that for you.\u201d\\n\\nSo, when Mr. Lorry called that night at ten o\u2019clock, Mr. Stryver appeared, amid books and papers spread out for the purpose, to have nothing on his mind but the morning\u2019s subject. He even seemed surprised to see Mr. Lorry and was completely absent-minded and preoccupied.\\n\\n\u201cWell!\u201d said the good-natured messenger, after half an hour of unsuccessful attempts to bring him back to the matter. \u201cI have been to Soho.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cTo Soho?\u201d Mr. Stryver repeated coldly. \u201cOh, of course! What was I thinking?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cAnd I have no doubt,\u201d said Mr. Lorry, \u201cthat I was right in our conversation. My opinion is confirmed, and I repeat my advice.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI assure you,\u201d replied Mr. Stryver in the friendliest manner, \u201cthat I am sorry for it on your account, and on the poor father\u2019s account. I know this must always be a painful subject for the family; let us say no more about it.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI don\u2019t understand you,\u201d said Mr. Lorry.\\n\\n\u201cI dare say you don\u2019t,\u201d Stryver replied, nodding in a calming, final way. \u201cNever mind, never mind.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cBut it does matter,\u201d Mr. Lorry insisted.\\n\\n\u201cNo, it doesn\u2019t; I assure you it doesn\u2019t. I had imagined there was good sense where there is none, and worthy ambition where there is none. I am glad to have discovered my mistake, and no harm has been done. Young women have made similar foolish choices before, and have often regretted them in poverty and obscurity. From an unselfish point of view, I am sorry the matter has been abandoned, because it would have been bad for me in worldly terms; from a selfish point of view, I am glad it has been abandoned, because it would have been bad for me in worldly terms\u2014it is hardly necessary to say that I could have gained nothing from it.\"}"
