          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"e68a2d04c2bab211d68b2b20\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Even reading about the dead man's luxury was astonishing.\\n\\nThen he turned his attention to embroidery and to the tapestries that served as frescoes in the cold rooms of northern Europe. As he studied the subject\u2014and he always possessed an extraordinary ability to become completely absorbed, for the moment, in whatever he undertook\u2014he was almost saddened by the thought of the ruin time brought upon beautiful and marvelous things. He, at least, had escaped that fate. Summer followed summer, the yellow jonquils bloomed and died many times, and nights of horror repeated the story of their shame, but he remained unchanged. No winter damaged his face or dulled his flowerlike bloom. How different it was with material things! Where had they gone? Where was the great crocus-colored robe, on which the gods battled the giants, woven by brown girls for Athena's pleasure? Where was the enormous velarium Nero had stretched across Rome's Colosseum, that purple Titan's sail depicting the starry sky, with Apollo driving a chariot drawn by white horses with gilded reins? He longed to see the curious table napkins made for the Priest of the Sun, displaying every delicacy and dish that might be desired at a feast; the funeral cloth of King Chilperic, with its three hundred golden bees; the fantastic robes that outraged the Bishop of Pontus and were decorated with \u201clions, panthers, bears, dogs, forests, rocks, hunters\u2014all, in fact, that a painter can copy from nature\u201d; and the coat Charles of Orleans once wore, whose sleeves bore the verses of a song beginning \u201c_Madame, je suis tout joyeux_,\u201d with the words' musical accompaniment worked in gold thread, each note\u2014square-shaped in those days\u2014formed from four pearls. He read about the room prepared at the palace in Rheims for Queen Joan of Burgundy, decorated with \u201cthirteen hundred and twenty-one parrot