          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"1ec2ada581728ccae717ed6c\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Soul and body, body and soul\u2014how mysterious they were! The soul contained something animal, while the body had moments of spirituality. The senses could become refined, and the intellect could deteriorate. Who could say where the physical impulse ended, or the psychological impulse began? How shallow were the arbitrary definitions of ordinary psychologists! And yet how difficult it was to choose between the claims of the various schools! Was the soul a shadow sitting in the house of sin? Or was the body truly within the soul, as Giordano Bruno believed? The separation of spirit from matter was a mystery, and the union of spirit with matter was a mystery as well.\\n\\nHe began to wonder whether psychology could ever become such an exact science that every small source of life would be revealed to us. As things stood, we always misunderstood ourselves and seldom understood others. Experience had no ethical value. It was merely the name people gave to their mistakes. Moralists had generally treated it as a form of warning, claimed that it had some ethical power in shaping character, praised it as something that taught us what to pursue and showed us what to avoid. But experience had no driving force. It was no more an active cause than conscience itself. All it truly proved was that our future would resemble our past, and that the sin we had committed once, with disgust, we would commit many times, with pleasure.\\n\\nIt was clear to him that the experimental method was the only way to reach any scientific analysis of the passions; and certainly Dorian Gray was a subject ready to hand, promising rich and fruitful results. His sudden, mad love for Sibyl Vane was a psychological phenomenon of considerable interest. There was no doubt that curiosity had much to do with it, along with the desire for new experiences, yet it was not a simple passion but a highly complex one. Whatever it contained of the purely sensual instinct of boyhood had been transformed by the workings of imagination, changed into something that seemed to the boy himself far removed from the senses, and was therefore all the more dangerous. It was the passions whose origins we misunderstood that ruled us most powerfully. Our weakest motives were those whose nature we understood. It often happened that when we thought we were experimenting on others, we were actually experimenting on ourselves.\\n\\nAs Lord Henry sat dreaming about these things, there was a knock at the door. His valet entered and reminded him that it was time to dress for dinner.\"}"
