          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"12650a10144a2f58734ce29a\",\"rewritten_text\":\"Not everyone would have behaved so kindly as she did, or refused to accept Jane\u2019s answer; but she firmly declared that she would _not_ write the refusal Jane had asked of her yesterday. She would wait\u2014and, sure enough, by yesterday evening everything was settled: Jane was to go. What a surprise to me! I had not the slightest idea! Jane took Mrs. Elton aside and told her immediately that, after considering the advantages of Mrs. Smallridge\u2019s position, she had decided to accept it. I did not know a thing until it was all settled.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYou spent the evening with Mrs. Elton?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cYes, all of us; Mrs. Elton insisted that we come. It was arranged on the hill, while we were walking with Mr. Knightley. \u2018You _must_ _all_ spend your evening with us,\u2019 she said\u2014\u2018I absolutely must have you _all_ come.\u2019\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMr. Knightley was there too, was he?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cNo, not Mr. Knightley; he declined from the beginning. Although I thought he would come, because Mrs. Elton declared she would not excuse him, he did not\u2014but my mother, Jane, and I were all there, and we had a very pleasant evening. Such kind friends, you know, Miss Woodhouse, must always seem agreeable, though everyone appeared rather exhausted after the morning\u2019s party. Even pleasure, you know, is tiring\u2014and I cannot say that any of them seemed to have enjoyed it very much. Still, _I_ shall always consider it a very pleasant party, and feel extremely grateful to the kind friends who included me.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cMiss Fairfax, I suppose, though you did not know it, had been making up her mind throughout the day?\u201d\\n\\n\u201cI dare say she had.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cWhenever the time comes, it must be unwelcome to her and all her friends\u2014but I hope her engagement will have every possible advantage\u2014I mean, in the character and manners of the family.\u201d\\n\\n\u201cThank you, dear Miss Woodhouse. Yes, indeed, it has everything in the world that could make her happy. Except for the Sucklings and Bragges, there is no other nursery establishment in all Mrs. Elton\u2019s acquaintance so generous and elegant. Mrs. Smallridge is a most delightful woman! Her way of living is almost equal to Maple Grove\u2014and as for the children, except the little Sucklings and little Bragges, there are nowhere such elegant, sweet children. Jane will be treated with such respect and kindness! It will be nothing but pleasure, a life of pleasure. And her salary! I really cannot bring myself to tell you her salary, Miss Woodhouse.\u201d\"}"
