          "text": "{\"request_id\":\"4fffd30166d66bcc7cefd1cb\",\"rewritten_text\":\"It seemed to me that this quaint lie had been told in the simplest and most beautiful way. But I had heard it only once, and that makes a difference; no doubt it had pleased the others when it was still fresh.\\n\\nSir Dinadan the Humorist was the first to wake, and he soon roused the others with a rather poor practical joke. He tied some metal mugs to a dog\u2019s tail and let it loose. The dog raced around the place in terror, while the other dogs chased it, barking and crashing into everything in their path. The result was complete confusion, with a deafening uproar. Every man and woman in the crowd laughed until tears ran down their faces; some even fell from their chairs and rolled on the floor in delight. They were just like children. Sir Dinadan was so proud of his trick that he kept explaining, again and again, how his brilliant idea had come to him, until everyone was tired of hearing it. Like humorists of his kind, he was still laughing at it after everyone else had stopped.\\n\\nHe was so pleased with himself that he decided to give a speech\u2014naturally, a humorous one. I do not think I had ever heard so many worn-out jokes strung together. He was worse than the minstrels, worse than a circus clown. It was especially sad to sit there, thirteen hundred years before my birth, and hear once more the stale, worm-eaten jokes that had given me indigestion when I was a boy, thirteen hundred years later. It nearly convinced me that no new joke could ever be invented. Everyone laughed at these ancient jokes\u2014but people always do; I had noticed that centuries afterward. The scoffer did not laugh, of course\u2014I mean the boy. He scoffed instead; there was nothing he would not mock. He said most of Sir Dinadan\u2019s jokes were rotten, and the rest were petrified. I said \u201cpetrified\u201d was a good word. I myself believed that the only proper way to classify the great ages of some jokes was by geological periods. But the idea meant nothing to the boy, since geology had not yet been invented. Still, I made a note of the remark and planned to educate the whole country up to it if I survived. There was no reason to throw away a good idea merely because the market was not ready for it.\\n\\nThen Sir Kay rose and began to run his history machine, using me as fuel.\"}"
